var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/lenses/#Lenses-API","page":"Lenses","text":"The lens families — two lens kinds, the projections, the terms and their combinators — the lens contract on rays with everything derived from it, the coefficient chart and record, and the batch surfaces. convert_eltype rebuilds a lens, a LogicalIntrinsics, a CameraModel or a grid model in another float type (Float32 for a GPU launch); it is the conversion the batched and fused kernels apply. See the Lens Families and Back-projection and Undistortion guides.","title":"Lenses API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lenses/#Projection-and-Lens-Models","page":"Lenses","text":"","title":"Projection and Lens Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lenses/#The-Lens-Contract,-Coefficients-and-Batches","page":"Lenses","text":"","title":"The Lens Contract, Coefficients and Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lenses/#Index","page":"Lenses","text":"Pages = [\"lenses.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractDistortionTerm","page":"Lenses","text":"AbstractDistortionTerm <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\n\nA distortion term in a projection's frame: a MAP x ↦ f(x) (Radial, Tilt, FieldOfView) or a DELTA x ↦ Δ(x) added to the point (Tangential, ThinPrism) — see Additive. A term is a CoordinateTransformations Transformation, callable on a frame point (t(x), a delta summed with the point, as a lone Additive); terms applied in sequence are its ∘, D₂ ∘ D₁ (D₁ first).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractDistortionTerm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractLensModel","page":"Lenses","text":"AbstractLensModel\n\nBase type for all lens models.\n\nA lens model determines how 3D rays through the optical center map to points on the sensor, including both the projection geometry and any aberrations.\n\nInterface — the lens contract is on rays\n\nA lens is a map between RAYS and NORMALIZED image points, and the pair project(lens, X) (a ray → its image point) and backproject(lens, xd) (an image point → its unit ray) is the whole contract (geometry/cameras/lens_rays.jl): NaN where the lens does not image, generic in the number type, allocation-free. There are two kinds of lens: a forward Lens — a projection onto a frame followed by a distortion in that frame — and a backward LiftLens, whose image point lifts to its ray in closed form (lenses.jl). Everything else is derived from the pair, for every lens: transform_deriv(lens, X), transform_deriv_params(lens, X), project_with_jacobians, the radial profile (projected_radius / view_angle), and the perspective views distort_normalized / undistort_normalized with the functor lens(x).\n\nA lens is a CoordinateTransformations.Transformation, so its derivative is that package's transform_deriv(lens, x) (ForwardDiff through the perspective view), and its back-projection is a map too: inv(lens) is an InverseLens whose call is undistort_normalized and whose transform_deriv is ForwardDiff through it. Compositions inherit both — transform_deriv(K ∘ inv(lens) ∘ K⁻¹, x) is the chain rule. The other generic defaults are imaging_transform(intrinsics, lens) and the capability trait has_distortion(lens). Every lens model here is a central projection (single center of projection); finite-aperture models are downstream.\n\nSubtyping CoordinateTransformations.Transformation makes every imaging chain a ComposedTransformation regardless of lens (the Pinhole and distorted paths used to produce DIFFERENT composition types, breaking structural pattern-matches like pnp_pixel_whitening).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractLensModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractProjection","page":"Lenses","text":"AbstractProjection\n\nA ray → frame-point projection: the first stage of a forward Lens. The five fixed projections (Perspective, Equidistant, Stereographic, Equisolid, Orthographic) each land on a retinal frame (projection_frame); the parametric ones (DoubleSphere, Unified, ExtendedUnified) carry coefficients and land on a frame their coefficients define.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Additive","page":"Lenses","text":"Additive(m, Δ₁, …, Δₙ)\nAdditive(Δ₁, …, Δₙ)\n\nTerms evaluated at the SAME point and summed: m(x) + (Δ₁(x) + … + Δₙ(x)), the deltas left to right; without a leading map, x + (Δ₁(x) + …). OpenCV's projectPoints and COLMAP's Distortion() are of this form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Additive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Division","page":"Lenses","text":"Division(k₁, …, k_Q)\n\nThe division model (Fitzgibbon 2001): x_u = x_d / (1 + k₁ρ² + … + k_Qρ²Q), the lift (x_d, 1 + k₁ρ² + …): LiftLens(Radial((), (k₁, …))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Division"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.DoubleSphere","page":"Lenses","text":"DoubleSphere(xi, alpha) <: AbstractProjection\n\nUsenko, Demmel & Cremers' double-sphere projection (3DV 2018): with d₁ = ‖X‖, z_s = ξ·d₁ + X₃ and d₂ = √(X₁² + X₂² + z_s²), x = X₁₂ / (α·d₂ + (1 − α)·z_s). ξ = α = 0 is the perspective projection. It images the rays out to the fold (or pole) of its radial profile, in closed form — not the paper's conservative field-of-view bound — and the closed-form inverse has the same domain. The coefficients' domain is ξ > −1, α ∈ [0, 1] (parameter_bounds), which LensManifold keeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.DoubleSphere"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Equidistant","page":"Lenses","text":"Equidistant projection r = θ, onto the EquidistantFrame; θ < 180°.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Equidistant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Equisolid","page":"Lenses","text":"Equisolid-angle projection r = 2 sin(θ/2), onto the EquisolidFrame; θ ≤ 180°.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Equisolid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Even","page":"Lenses","text":"The power basis of a Radial factor: Even — ρ², ρ⁴, … (the default); Full — ρ², ρ³, ρ⁴, ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Even"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ExtendedUnified","page":"Lenses","text":"ExtendedUnified(alpha, beta) <: AbstractProjection\n\nThe enhanced unified camera model of Khomutenko et al. (2016) (Kalibr's eucm): with d = √(β(X₁² + X₂²) + X₃²), x = X₁₂ / (α·d + (1 − α)·X₃).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ExtendedUnified"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FieldOfView","page":"Lenses","text":"FieldOfView(omega)\n\nThe field-of-view model of Devernay and Faugeras (2001) (COLMAP FOV, Kalibr fov): x ↦ x·atan(2r·tan(ω/2)) / (r·ω).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FieldOfView"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Full","page":"Lenses","text":"The power basis of a Radial factor: Even — ρ², ρ⁴, … (the default); Full — ρ², ρ³, ρ⁴, ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Full"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.KannalaBrandt","page":"Lenses","text":"KannalaBrandt(k₁, …, k_N)\n\nKannala & Brandt's fisheye (2006) in OpenCV's/COLMAP's form, θ_d = θ·(1 + k₁θ² + … + k_Nθ²ᴺ): Lens(Equidistant(), Radial((k₁, …, k_N))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.KannalaBrandt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Lens","page":"Lenses","text":"Lens(projection[, distortion]) <: AbstractLensModel\n\nA forward lens: the ray is projected onto projection's frame and the frame point is distorted by distortion (nothing, a term, an Additive or terms in sequence, CoordinateTransformations' D₂ ∘ D₁ — each stage a map term or an Additive). Projection is closed form; back-projection inverts the distortion (backproject).\n\nA composed distortion is stored right-associated, whatever association it was built in: Lens(p, D₃ ∘ D₂ ∘ D₁) (Base folds ∘ to the left) and Lens(p, D₃ ∘ (D₂ ∘ D₁)) are one value, with distortion the latter, the form lens_from_record builds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Lens"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LiftLens","page":"Lenses","text":"LiftLens(radial::Radial) <: AbstractLensModel\n\nA backward lens: the image point x_d lifts to the ray (x_d·N(ρ), D(ρ)), ρ = |x_d| — closed-form back-projection; projection solves the image radius as a root on the ray. D crossing zero is the 90° circle and D < 0 the field behind it, so the lens images past 90°.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LiftLens"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Orthographic","page":"Lenses","text":"Orthographic projection r = sin θ, onto the OrthographicFrame; θ ≤ 90°.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Orthographic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Perspective","page":"Lenses","text":"Perspective projection x = X₁₂/X₃ (r = tan θ), onto the GnomonicFrame; θ < 90°.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Perspective"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Pinhole","page":"Lenses","text":"The ideal perspective lens, Lens(Perspective()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Pinhole"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Radial","page":"Lenses","text":"Radial{B, P, Q, T}(k::NTuple{P}, d::NTuple{Q})\nRadial(k, d = ())                  # B = Even\nRadial{Full}(k, d)\n\nThe radial factor F(ρ) = N(ρ)/D(ρ) with N = 1 + Σ kᵢ φᵢ(ρ) and D = 1 + Σ dⱼ φⱼ(ρ), where the basis B sets φᵢ: Even — ρ²ⁱ; Full — ρⁱ⁺¹ (no linear term: the factor is smooth on the axis).\n\nIn a Lens it is the map x ↦ F(|x|)·x in the projection's frame — OpenCV's rational radial factor (k₁…k₃ / k₄…k₆) in the perspective frame, Kannala–Brandt's θ-polynomial in the equidistant frame. In a LiftLens it is the undistortion of the image point, x_u = F(ρ_d)·x_d, represented as the ray (x_d·N, D) — the division model, BabelCalib's rational model, Scaramuzza's polynomial.\n\nCoefficient names: k1…kP then d1…dQ.\n\nThe constructor also stores where the factor's imaged branch ends, in the basis variable u (ρ² for Even): u_fold, the first positive root of the profile's derivative (the fold), and u_end, the smaller of the fold and the pole (the first positive root of D); Inf when there is none. They are not parameters, and a per-point branch test is one comparison against them; every other reader of the branch's end (monotone_radius, view_angle_limit, the inverses' brackets) reads them too.\n\nThe ends follow from the coefficients in the precision they were built in. A WIDENING rebuild — convert_eltype to a wider type, the Duals of a parameter Jacobian and their primal — casts them instead of sweeping again, so a Float32 factor seen at Float64 keeps its own branch (and _primal of a Float64 differentiation of a Float32 lens is _precision(Float64, lens)); such a factor can differ from one constructed at Float64 from the same coefficients by the narrow precision's rounding of the end. A narrowing conversion recomputes them. An integer factor is not a supported stored form: the outer constructors and every rebuild float their coefficients, and only a direct call of the inner Radial{B, P, Q, <:Integer} reaches one; it constructs (the ends stored rounded down, typemax for Inf) but is internal only — the lens contract and the branch readers (_branch_end) take float factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Radial"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Scaramuzza","page":"Lenses","text":"Scaramuzza(a₂, …, a_{N+1})\n\nScaramuzza's omnidirectional model (Scaramuzza, Martinelli & Siegwart 2006; MATLAB's fisheyeIntrinsics), normalized: the lift (x_d, 1 + a₂ρ² + a₃ρ³ + …) — LiftLens(Radial{Full}((), (a₂, …))). The coefficients are named d1, d2, … for a₂, a₃, ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Scaramuzza"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Stereographic","page":"Lenses","text":"Stereographic projection r = 2 tan(θ/2), onto the StereographicFrame; θ < 180°.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Stereographic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Tangential","page":"Lenses","text":"Tangential(p1, p2)\n\nBrown's decentering delta in OpenCV's convention: Δ = (2p₁uv + p₂(r² + 2u²), 2p₂uv + p₁(r² + 2v²)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Tangential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ThinPrism","page":"Lenses","text":"ThinPrism{N, T}(sx::NTuple{N}, sy::NTuple{N})\n\nThe thin-prism delta Δ = (Σ sxᵢ r²ⁱ, Σ syᵢ r²ⁱ): OpenCV's (s₁, s₂; s₃, s₄) is N = 2, COLMAP's (sx₁; sy₁) is N = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ThinPrism"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Tilt","page":"Lenses","text":"Tilt(tx, ty)\n\nOpenCV's tilted-sensor map (CALIB_TILTED_MODEL, τx, τy): the projective map of computeTiltProjectionMatrix(τx, τy) applied to (x, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Tilt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Unified","page":"Lenses","text":"Unified(xi) <: AbstractProjection\n\nThe unified camera model (Geyer & Daniilidis; Mei & Rives 2007; Kalibr's omni, OpenCV's omnidir): x = X₁₂ / (X₃ + ξ·‖X‖).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Unified"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.isdelta-Tuple{AbstractDistortionTerm}","page":"Lenses","text":"isdelta(term) -> Bool\n\nIs the term a delta (added to the point by Additive) rather than a map?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.isdelta"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projection_frame-Tuple{Perspective}","page":"Lenses","text":"projection_frame(projection) -> Type{<:RetinalFrame} | nothing\n\nThe retinal frame a fixed projection lands on; nothing for a parametric projection, whose frame its coefficients define.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projection_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial-Tuple{LiftLens}","page":"Lenses","text":"radial(lens) -> Radial | nothing\n\nThe lens's radially symmetric factor: a lone Radial distortion, the leading Radial of an Additive, the lift of a LiftLens; nothing otherwise. Its folds and poles bound what the lens images (monotone_radius, view_angle_limit), and it is what a regression onto a radially symmetric family fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.InverseLens","page":"Lenses","text":"InverseLens{L} <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\n\nThe back-projection of a lens as a first-class map: inv(lens) returns one, inv(inv(lens)) === lens, its call is undistort_normalized(lens, xd), and its transform_deriv is ForwardDiff through that map (lensrays.jl). Composes like any transformation, so the pixel-domain undistortion of a camera is `inv(distortionmap(model))with the chain rule for its derivative. APinhole` is its own inverse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.InverseLens"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.has_distortion-Tuple{AbstractLensModel}","page":"Lenses","text":"has_distortion(lens) -> Bool\n\nDoes the lens bend rays at all? (false ⇒ undistort_normalized is the identity.) Relation to the framed imaging chain (0.10): has_distortion(lens) == false means the distortion is the identity in the lens's defining frame, so imaging_chain COLLAPSES the ray frame onto DistortedFrame — the chain has no distortion stage and K consumes the ray frame directly (see the collapse rule at DistortedFrame and distortion_frame). Every frame here assumes a single center of projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.has_distortion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.imaging_transform-Tuple{AbstractIntrinsics, AbstractLensModel}","page":"Lenses","text":"imaging_transform(intrinsics, lens) -> ComposedTransformation\nimaging_transform(model::CameraModel)\n\nThe composed camera-space → pixel-space map as a value: affine_part(K) ∘ lens, the lens applied to the ray (its functor is project on 3-vectors). It is project(model, X) for every lens and every ray — past 90° for a lens that images there, NaN where the lens does not (for a Pinhole, every Z ≤ 0). Its derivative is the chain rule through transform_deriv(lens, X). The fully-typed, junction-checked counterpart is imaging_chain, which mints the framed composite {ViewFrame{id} → ImageFrame{id}} through the frame chain documented at DistortedFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.imaging_transform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.monotone_radius-Tuple{AbstractLensModel}","page":"Lenses","text":"monotone_radius(lens) -> Real\n\nThe distorted radius up to which the lens's perspective view is a monotone bijection — the bound a whole-extent perspective undistortion clips to (undistorted_rect, valid_rect, division_bounded). Beyond it the view folds or diverges and project answers NaN. The profile's first fold or pole, whichever comes first, mapped to the distorted radius; both are found exactly, as first positive polynomial roots. Inf for a lens whose profile never folds. A point EXACTLY at this radius can be NaN (it rounds onto the closed end of the branch); a caller that needs a finite value steps inside, as the rect builders' margin does.\n\nthe radial factor (radial) of a forward lens: the fold or pole of its profile r ↦ r·F(r²) in the projection's frame, capped at the frame's edge (a ray cannot leave the sphere); decentering terms do not enter, as in view_angle_limit.\na LiftLens: the first pole of D (the 90° circle) or fold of the lift — 1/√|k| for Division(k).\na composed distortion (CoordinateTransformations' ∘ of stages): its FIRST radial stage (a Radial, or an Additive led by one), wherever it sits — the stage project tests — read as above at that stage's output; the stages around it do not enter (for a stage behind a tilt, the frame's edge caps the stage's input).\na SplineLens: the largest normalized radius about x = 0 (its gauge's principal point, the pixel imaging ẑ) whose disc lies inside the lens's support — its backward area and the lattice cells whose 4 × 4 window holds present nodes only, where the grid is defined (and, for a calibrated grid, one-to-one); 0 when x = 0 is outside it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.monotone_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_part-Tuple{Lens}","page":"Lenses","text":"radial_part(lens) -> AbstractLensModel\n\nThe radially symmetric part of lens: its projection with only its radial factor (radial) — the decentering terms dropped; a lift is its own radial part. What view_angle_limit bounds and what a regression onto a radially symmetric family can represent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_part"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_angle_limit-Tuple{AbstractLensModel}","page":"Lenses","text":"view_angle_limit(lens; cap) -> θ_max\n\nThe largest view angle the lens's radial profile honestly represents: where the profile stops being finite and strictly increasing (a fold, a pole, or the frame's own edge). Closed forms where the lens has one — the fold or pole of a forward lens's radial factor read through its frame, never past the frame's edge (π/2 − 10⁻³ for the perspective frame); a lift's fold; the double sphere's FOV bound — and a monotonicity scan of projected_radius otherwise. A composed distortion (∘) with a radial stage is bounded by that stage, tested at its own input as project tests it: the angle where project first turns NaN, bisected to rounding. When a stage precedes the radial one (a tilt) that angle depends on the azimuth, and the limit is its minimum over azimuths, so every ray within it is imaged; a lens whose bound is radially symmetric reads it at azimuth 0. cap bounds the answer: the generic scan runs to it (default π − 10⁻³); the closed forms clamp to it (default none). A ray projected EXACTLY at the limit can be NaN (a lift's root there is √eps-conditioned); a caller that needs a finite value steps inside, as the valid domain's inscribed radius and the spline lattice's sampler do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_angle_limit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractPanoramaProjection","page":"Lenses","text":"AbstractPanoramaProjection <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\n\nA projection of directions onto a (longitude, vertical) rectangle, the cylindrical family: Equirectangular, Cylindrical, CylindricalEqualArea, Mercator. The call p(X) is project(p, X) (its transform_deriv(p, X) the 2 × 3 Jacobian), and inv(p) is its Backprojection (inv(p)(q) == backproject(p, q), inv(inv(p)) === p). All are kernel-lawful (singletons, no allocation, no throw), so a chain built from them runs in image_warp! and pointwise! on any backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractPanoramaProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Backprojection","page":"Lenses","text":"Backprojection(m) <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\n\nThe back-projection of m as a map: its call is backproject(m, q) and transform_deriv(b, q) its 3 × 2 Jacobian (a CameraModel's or a NoncentralSplineModel's own, last(backproject_with_jacobian(m, q)); any other model's — a panorama projection, a BoundedCamera — by ForwardDiff through its backproject). m is a panorama projection (inv(p) builds it, inv(Backprojection(p)) === p) or a camera model, a pixel → ray map: Backprojection(model) of a CameraModel(...; lens = Lens(Perspective())) is a rectilinear virtual camera, of Lens(Equidistant()) or Lens(Stereographic()) a fisheye one, and LinearMap(R) ∘ Backprojection(model) aims it. Kernel-lawful when m's backproject is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Backprojection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Cylindrical","page":"Lenses","text":"Cylindrical()\n\nThe central cylindrical projection of directions onto the unit cylinder about the up axis, in the camera frame's axes (x right, y down, z forward, up = −y): the longitude about the axis, and the height h = tan φ at which the ray through the origin meets the cylinder of radius 1 (φ the latitude). A cylindrical panorama raster is an affine map of (λ, h): its columns are the longitude, its rows the height, so verticals stay vertical and straight.\n\nproject(Cylindrical(), X) — the direction X (any 3-vector off the up axis; its length does not enter) to SVector(λ, h): the longitude λ = atan(X₁, X₃) ∈ (−π, π] (0 forward, +π/2 right) and h = −X₂ / √(X₁² + X₃²) (+ up). NaN for a zero, non-finite or polar (X₁ = X₃ = 0) vector: the poles have no height.\nbackproject(Cylindrical(), p) — p = (λ, h) to the unit direction (sin λ, −h, cos λ) / √(1 + h²), defined for every finite p (periodic in λ).\n\nIts longitude is Equirectangular's, and h = tan φ of its latitude. An AbstractPanoramaProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Cylindrical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CylindricalEqualArea","page":"Lenses","text":"CylindricalEqualArea()\n\nLambert's cylindrical equal-area projection of directions, in the camera frame's axes (x right, y down, z forward, up = −y): the longitude and v = sin φ of the latitude — the height at which the ray's parallel meets the unit cylinder when projected ALONG the planes of constant height (orthogonally onto the axis' cylinder), so equal areas of the raster are equal solid angles (dΩ = cos φ dφ dλ = dv dλ): a uniform raster's pixels all subtend the same solid angle.\n\nproject(CylindricalEqualArea(), X) — X (any nonzero 3-vector; its length does not enter) to SVector(λ, v), λ = atan(X₁, X₃) and v = −X₂ / ‖X‖ ∈ [−1, 1]. NaN for a zero or non-finite vector; the poles are v = ±1 with atan's longitude.\nbackproject(CylindricalEqualArea(), p) — p = (λ, v) to the unit direction (√(1 − v²) sin λ, −v, √(1 − v²) cos λ); NaN for |v| > 1.\n\nAn AbstractPanoramaProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CylindricalEqualArea"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Equirectangular","page":"Lenses","text":"Equirectangular()\n\nThe equirectangular (latitude/longitude) projection of directions, in the camera frame's axes (x right, y down, z forward, up = −y).\n\nproject(Equirectangular(), X) — the direction X (any nonzero 3-vector; its length does not enter) to SVector(λ, φ) in radians: the longitude λ = atan(X₁, X₃) ∈ (−π, π] (0 forward, +π/2 right) and the latitude φ = atan(−X₂, √(X₁² + X₃²)) ∈ [−π/2, π/2] (+π/2 up). NaN for a zero or non-finite vector. At the poles the longitude is atan's value of the remaining components (0 for an exact pole).\nbackproject(Equirectangular(), p) — p = (λ, φ) to the unit direction (cos φ sin λ, −sin φ, cos φ cos λ), defined for every p (periodic in λ).\n\nA panorama raster is an affine map of (λ, φ) to pixels, and a rotation R with d_world = R·d puts the projection in another frame: project(Equirectangular(), R' * d_world). An AbstractPanoramaProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Equirectangular"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Mercator","page":"Lenses","text":"Mercator()\n\nThe Mercator projection of directions, in the camera frame's axes (x right, y down, z forward, up = −y): the longitude and v = asinh(tan φ) of the latitude — the conformal member of the cylindrical family (angles are kept locally; the scale grows as 1/cos φ).\n\nproject(Mercator(), X) — X (any 3-vector off the up axis; its length does not enter) to SVector(λ, v), λ = atan(X₁, X₃) and v = asinh(−X₂ / √(X₁² + X₃²)). NaN for a zero, non-finite or polar vector.\nbackproject(Mercator(), p) — p = (λ, v) to the unit direction (sin λ / cosh v, −tanh v, cos λ / cosh v), defined for every finite p.\n\nAn AbstractPanoramaProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Mercator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RegionBeyondSupport","page":"Lenses","text":"RegionBeyondSupport(who, region, support) <: Exception\n\nWhat check_region_support throws: who (the fit that refused) was asked to work over region, which reaches beyond support, the source camera's calibrated support (its valid_domain) — a fit there would read the model where it is extrapolated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RegionBeyondSupport"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.check_region_support-Tuple{Union{BoundedCamera, CameraModel, NoncentralSplineModel, PosedCamera}, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"check_region_support(camera, region; who::AbstractString) -> region\ncheck_region_support(support, region; who::AbstractString) -> region\n\nTHE guard of every fit over a camera's region (optical_axis, approximate_camera, refine_camera, …): region back when it lies inside the source's calibrated support — valid_domain(camera), or a support region given directly (a source camera's, when the camera fitted from is a re-gridded copy whose own domain no longer records it) — and a RegionBeyondSupport naming who, the region and the support otherwise. Never a warning: a fit over an extrapolated model is refused. Containment is region ⊆ support, Core's one region containment over a Rect, an Ellipse, their ∩ and the WholePlane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.check_region_support"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.farthest_radius-Tuple{HyperRectangle{2}, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"farthest_radius(region, center) -> Float64\nfarthest_radius(region, center, direction) -> Float64\n\nHow far region reaches from the point center, in the region's units (pixels for an imaged_region).\n\nfarthest_radius(region, center) is the largest distance from center to a point of the region. A Rect has the exact value, its farthest corner. Any other region uses the second form on 720 azimuths, refines the largest by Brent's method between its neighbours (grid_brent_minimum, Core's one 1-D minimizer) and returns the largest value it evaluated.\nfarthest_radius(region, center, direction) is the largest t with center + t·direction in the region, bisected to rounding, in units of ‖direction‖. A direction through a linear map, A·d, gives the radius in the coordinates that A maps to pixels.\n\nThe region is any bounded 2-D region that answers p ∈ region: a Rect, an Ellipse, a Circle, or their ∩. It must contain center and be star-shaped about it, which every intersection of convex parts containing center is. A Rect's farthest point is a corner from any center, inside or not (an image's farthest corner from a distortion centre: division_bounded).\n\nThe sensor clips the result. An imaged_region is the sensor's Rect intersected with the lens coverage, so an image circle larger than the sensor, or cut by it, reaches only as far as the sensor does.\n\nThe whole plane has no farthest point, so it is an ArgumentError (from its Rect2). So is a center outside any other region.\n\nr = imaged_region(camera)\nfarthest_radius(r, principal_point)   # a radial profile's domain: ρ ≤ 1 on every imaged pixel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.farthest_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.image_circle-Tuple{CameraModel, Sensor, FlexUnits.Quantity}","page":"Lenses","text":"image_circle(camera, sensor::Sensor, diameter::Quantity) -> Ellipse{Float64}\nimage_circle(camera::BoundedCamera, diameter::Quantity) -> Ellipse{Float64}\n\nThe image circle of a lens: the region of the sensor it lights, from a lens datasheet's image-circle diameter, a length, carried into pixels by the sensor's pitch — semi-axes diameter / (2 · pitch.width) and diameter / (2 · pitch.height) px about the principal point, axis-aligned, in the camera's pixel frame (the frame of its calibration matrix K; Rect(extent) is the COLMAP-convention extent of the same frame). The datasheet circle is physical, on the sensor plane, and the pitch defines the pixel axes, so K's skew and focal lengths do not enter. A DimensionError when diameter is not a length. A BoundedCamera bounded by its physical Sensor takes the two-argument form; one bounded by a bare extent is an ArgumentError (it has no pitch).\n\nThe image circle is never predicted from the lens model: where the model's formula is valid is valid_domain, a different region. A measured circle (an Ellipse segmented from images) serves wherever this one does. A SplineLens camera's circle is centred on its gauge principal point, the pixel imaging ẑ. Where the camera images is imaged_region(camera).\n\ncam = BoundedCamera(CameraModel(K; lens = KannalaBrandt(-0.135)), sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX250\"))\nq = image_circle(cam, 6.0mm)                # from the datasheet, through the 3.45 μm pitch\nRect(extent(cam)) ∈ q                       # is the whole sensor lit?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.image_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.imaged_region-Tuple{BoundedCamera}","page":"Lenses","text":"imaged_region(camera::BoundedCamera) -> region\n\nThe region of the sensor the camera images, in pixels in its pixel frame: the sensor Rect(extent(camera)) ∩ its lens_coverage (where light reaches the sensor: the lens's image circle, measured or from a datasheet's image_circle, or the whole sensor) ∩ the valid_domain of its model (where the lens formula holds, or where a grid model is defined). The one place the three are combined, so a consumer asks the camera and never carries an image circle of its own. A lens coverage larger than the sensor, or cut by it, is clipped to the sensor.\n\nA lazy RegionIntersection, or a Rect (the sensor's, in its eltype) when the whole sensor is covered and the model has no limit (∩ returns it). The pixel mask of any residual is u ∈ imaged_region(camera); the free parameters of a local model are free_nodes(model, imaged_region(camera)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.imaged_region"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_centre-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, CartesianIndex{2}}} where T<:Real","page":"Lenses","text":"pixel_centre([T = Float64,] I::CartesianIndex{2}) -> SVector{2, T}\n\nThe centre of the pixel at the array index I = (row, col) (Julia's 1-based indices, img[row, col]) in the COLMAP frame, (col − ½, row − ½), in T: the one conversion from an image array's index to a pixel point. Allocation- and branch-free, so a device functor over CartesianIndices calls it at the kernel's element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_centre"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_centres-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Lenses","text":"pixel_centres(region, us, vs) -> Vector{SVector{2, Float64}}\n\nThe COLMAP centres (u − ½, v − ½) of the pixels (u, v), u ∈ us, v ∈ vs (Julia's 1-based pixel indices, as pixel_mask indexes them), that lie in region, v outer and u inner: the pixels of a sub-lattice (a stride, an offset) without the full image's mask. pixel_mask(region, W, H)[v, u] is whether pixel (u, v)'s centre is among pixel_centres(region, 1:W, 1:H).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_centres"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_mask-Tuple{Any, CoordinateTransformations.Transformation, Integer, Integer}","page":"Lenses","text":"pixel_mask(region, frame_map, width, height) -> BitMatrix\n\nThe pixels of a width × height image whose centres, mapped by frame_map into the frame region is stated in, lie in it: mask[v, u] is frame_map((u − ½, v − ½)) ∈ region. For an image on another pixel lattice than the region's — a Bayer plane or channel against a region on the sensor (pixel_mask(imaged_region(camera), bayer_plane_map(L, c), w, h)), a window of a mosaic (raw_frame_map(m)) — the membership is decided in the region's own frame, at each pixel's mapped centre, never by mapping the region. Without a map it is pixel_mask(region, width, height) (the identity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_mask-Tuple{Any, Integer, Integer}","page":"Lenses","text":"pixel_mask(region, width, height) -> BitMatrix\n\nThe pixels of a width × height image whose CENTRES lie in region (any 2-D region answering p ∈ region: an imaged_region, a Rect, an Ellipse, their ∩, the WholePlane), as a height × width BitMatrix in Julia's array layout: mask[v, u] is pixel (u, v), whose centre is the COLMAP point (u − ½, v − ½) — the conversion pixel_centres shares, made in one place.\n\nregion = imaged_region(camera)                # the sensor ∩ its lens coverage ∩ where its lens model is valid\nmask = pixel_mask(region, width, height)      # the pixels the camera images\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pixel_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_domain-Tuple{CameraModel}","page":"Lenses","text":"valid_domain(camera) -> Union{Ellipse{Float64}, WholePlane}\n\nWhere the camera's lens MODEL is valid, in pixels in the camera's own pixel frame (the frame of its calibration matrix K): the image of the cone of view angles θ ≤ θ_max =view_angle_limit(lens), on which the lens's radial profile is finite and strictly increasing, so the model is radially injective. The conic K⁻ᵀ · diag(1, 1, −ρ²) · K⁻¹ with ρ the lens's image radius at θ_max (projected_radius for a radially symmetric lens): an Ellipse centred at the principal point, a circle (equal axes) only when K is isotropic without skew and the pitch square.\n\nThis is a property of the formula, not where light falls on the sensor: a fitted lens's fold can lie well outside the lit disc, or inside the extent where the sensor is dark. Where light falls is the lens's image_circle(camera, sensor, diameter) from its datasheet, or a measured circle; a bounded camera holds either as its lens_coverage.\n\nFor a lens that is not radially symmetric — a composed distortion (∘) with a tilt stage, tangential or thin-prism terms — ρ is the smallest image radius of the θ_max cone over the azimuths (360 samples, the least refined by golden section) less a relative margin of 10⁻⁹, so the ellipse is INSCRIBED: every point inside it is the image of a ray inside the cone.\n\nThe WholePlane when the lens has no limit of its own: view_angle_limit reports only the edge it stops at by construction — the perspective or orthographic chart's edge, or the sphere's π − 10⁻³ (an undistorted equidistant lens, a lens whose profile stays monotone, a lift that never folds). That edge is a cap, not the lens: a conic there would measure the cap constant. A lens WITH a limit — a fold, a pole, the double sphere's bound, a chain's bounded stage — always gets its conic, even one larger than the extent. The rule is generic over AbstractLensModel: any lens with a view_angle_limit (every lens has one, by the generic scan) and a project gets an answer. A PosedCamera or BoundedCamera forwards to its model.\n\nRelated radii, different quantities: horizon_radius is fx times monotone_radius, the radial factor's perspective-monotone bound (decentering ignored, fx only, Inf without a fold) — equal to this ellipse's radius for an isotropic, skew-free K and a radially symmetric folding lens; the imaging domain (where backproject turns NaN) may be wider than a whole-plane answer admits, e.g. r ≤ π·f for an undistorted equidistant lens.\n\ncam = BoundedCamera(CameraModel(K; lens = KannalaBrandt(-0.135)), sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX250\"))\nv = valid_domain(cam)                       # where the lens formula is injective, about the principal point\nRect(extent(cam)) ∈ v                       # is the model valid over the whole sensor?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_domain"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundaryRule","page":"Lenses","text":"BoundaryRule(m, region; order = 2)\n\nThe boundary rule of the lattice model m (a LocalSupport model whose nodes are the CartesianIndices of a 2-D lattice) under the imaged region: its interior nodes (node_mask(m, region), the parameters) and, per dependent node, the linear combination of interior nodes its CORRECTION is, extrapolated outward ring by ring (Chebyshev rings around the interior) with a zero order-th difference across the border:\n\norder = 2 (the default): the natural boundary condition f'' = 0 (Δ²c = 0), the outermost coefficient of BSplineKit's natural recombination (natural_extrapolation, c₀ = 2 c₁ − c₂) — a linear correction is extended exactly;\norder = 3: the curvature-preserving Δ³c = 0, c₀ = 3 c₁ − 3 c₂ + c₃, which carries the slope and the curvature across the border — a quadratic correction is extended exactly.\n\nPer dependent node: along each lattice axis on which the order nodes inward are already defined, averaged over those axes; with none, the same along the diagonals; with none of those either, the mean of the defined neighbours (constant extrapolation). The coefficients of each dependent node sum to one. extend_controls applies the rule; boundary_rule_jacobian is its Jacobian. An ArgumentError when the region meets no node's support or order is not 2 or 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundaryRule"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundaryRule-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Lenses","text":"BoundaryRule(interior::AbstractMatrix{Bool}; order = 2)\n\nThe boundary rule of a lattice whose interior nodes are interior (true), the rest dependent (BoundaryRule(m, region; order) with the mask given).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundaryRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SupportTrait","page":"Lenses","text":"SupportTrait\nGlobalSupport()\nLocalSupport()\n\nHow a model's parameters are supported on the sensor. GlobalSupport (the default for every type): every parameter influences the whole image, so every parameter is free whatever the camera images. LocalSupport: the model is a set of nodes, each influencing a region of the sensor — it implements nodes(m) and support(m, i), and opts in with support_trait(::Type{<:MyModel}) = LocalSupport().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SupportTrait"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.boundary_rule_jacobian-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundaryRule}","page":"Lenses","text":"boundary_rule_jacobian(rule::BoundaryRule) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe Jacobian of extend_controls with respect to the interior controls, per scalar component: row = a node of the lattice (linear index), column = an interior node in linear-index order (findall(rule.interior)). An interior node's row is its unit vector, a dependent node's its coefficients; every row sums to one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.boundary_rule_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_footprint-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"cubic_bspline_footprint(center, spacing) -> Rect2{Float64}\n\nThe footprint of one node of a uniform cubic B-spline lattice: the closed rectangle center ± 2·spacing (two cells on each side, the 4×4 cells the node's basis function is nonzero on), in the lattice's pixel frame. center is the node's position and spacing the lattice's cell size, each a pair (x, y). The one footprint every uniform bicubic lattice model reads for its support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_footprint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.directions-Tuple{Union{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, NoncentralSplineModel}}","page":"Lenses","text":"directions(m) -> AbstractMatrix{<:SVector{3}}\n\nThe direction controls of a SplineLens camera (its backward spline's) or of a NoncentralSplineModel, [gy, gx]: kept as given, not normalized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.directions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.extend_controls-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundaryRule, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Lenses","text":"extend_controls(rule::BoundaryRule, controls, reference) -> matrix\n\nThe full lattice of controls under rule: the interior nodes of controls as given, each dependent node its reference control plus the extrapolated correction, reference[j] + Σ cₖ (controls[k] − reference[k]) over its interior terms. controls and reference are matrices of the lattice's size (the dependent entries of controls are not read) of any control type with +, - and scalar * (3-vectors, scalars, duals). With controls == reference the result is reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.extend_controls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.free_nodes-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"free_nodes(m, region) -> indices\n\nThe parameters of m free under the imaged region (an imaged_region, a Rect, an Ellipse, …): for a LocalSupport model the nodes whose support meets the region, [i for i in nodes(m) if intersects(region, support(m, i))], less any node the model does not hold (a spline model's absent nodes, present_nodes); for a GlobalSupport model every parameter, Colon() — an index that selects them all (θ[free_nodes(m, region)]), the same answer node_mask gives. A consumer that needs to iterate nodes asks a LocalSupport model (support_trait(m) isa LocalSupport).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.free_nodes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.grid_size-Tuple{Union{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, NoncentralSplineModel}}","page":"Lenses","text":"grid_size(m) -> (gx, gy)\n\nThe size of the control grid of a SplineLens camera or a NoncentralSplineModel, (columns, rows) — the grid_size keyword's order, the reverse of size(directions(m)). A window reports its block's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.grid_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lattice_spacing-Tuple{Union{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, NoncentralSplineModel}}","page":"Lenses","text":"lattice_spacing(m) -> (hx, hy)\n\nThe cell size of a SplineLens camera's or a NoncentralSplineModel's lattice in pixels: the calibrated pixel span over its cells, (w / (gx − 3), h / (gy − 3)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lattice_spacing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.node_mask-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"node_mask(m, region) -> BitArray\n\nfree_nodes as a mask in the shape of nodes(m) (true = free), for assembling a Levenberg–Marquardt system. For a GlobalSupport model Colon(), as free_nodes answers: an index meaning \"every parameter\", so θ[node_mask(m, region)] reads the same for every model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.node_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.node_position-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"node_position(m, i) -> Union{Point2, Nothing}\n\nWhere node i of a model sits on the sensor, in pixels (for placing nodes); nothing when the model does not say. A contract generic with that default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.node_position"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.nodes","page":"Lenses","text":"nodes(m) -> indices\n\nThe node ids of a LocalSupport model — CartesianIndices of a lattice, 1:N for scattered nodes — in the shape node_mask returns. A contract generic: a node model implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.nodes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.present_nodes-Tuple{Union{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, NoncentralSplineModel}}","page":"Lenses","text":"present_nodes(m) -> BitMatrix\n\nThe nodes a SplineLens camera or a NoncentralSplineModel holds, [gy, gx] like its controls: true = present. Every node for a model built without a present mask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.present_nodes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.support","page":"Lenses","text":"support(m, i) -> region\n\nThe region of the sensor node i of a LocalSupport model influences, in pixels in the camera's pixel frame: a Rect for a node of a uniform B-spline lattice (cubic_bspline_footprint), WholePlane() for a global basis function. A contract generic: a node model implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.support"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.support_trait-Tuple{Type}","page":"Lenses","text":"support_trait(m) -> SupportTrait\nsupport_trait(::Type{M}) -> SupportTrait\n\nWhether m's parameters have global or local support on the sensor (SupportTrait). GlobalSupport() unless the type opts in; the instance form forwards to the type form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.support_trait"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.window_offset-Tuple{Union{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, NoncentralSplineModel}, Union{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, NoncentralSplineModel}}","page":"Lenses","text":"window_offset(w, m) -> (row, col)\n\nThe 0-based (row, col) of the first control of the window w in the control grid of m, the camera or model it was cut from (w = window(m, rows, cols): rows = row + 1 : …, cols = col + 1 : …), so a control directions(w)[i, j] is directions(m)[row + i, col + j]. Read off the two lattices' areas; an ArgumentError when w does not lie on m's lattice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.window_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject!-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractLensModel, AbstractArray}","page":"Lenses","text":"See project!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distort_normalized!-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractLensModel, AbstractArray}","page":"Lenses","text":"See project!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distort_normalized!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distort_normalized-Tuple{AbstractLensModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"distort_normalized(lens, x::StaticVector{2}) -> Vec2\n\nThe image point of the ray through the perspective point x: project(lens, (x₁, x₂, 1)). The functor lens(x) is this call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distort_normalized"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.project!-Tuple{AbstractArray, Any, AbstractArray}","page":"Lenses","text":"project!(u, m, X; exec = OnBackend(get_backend(u))) -> u\nbackproject!(X, lens, u; exec = OnBackend(get_backend(X))) -> X\ndistort_normalized!(xd, lens, x; exec = OnBackend(get_backend(xd))) -> xd\nundistort_normalized!(x, lens, xd; exec = OnBackend(get_backend(x))) -> x\n\nproject / backproject / distort_normalized / undistort_normalized of every point of a batch, into u / X / … — arrays of static vectors on any backend. m is a lens or anything else project takes (a camera). A point the lens does not image comes back NaN. One kernel on the output's backend, the CPU's included (pointwise!); exec launches it on another backend value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.project!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.project_with_jacobians-Tuple{AbstractLensModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"project_with_jacobians(lens, X::StaticVector{3}) -> (u, ∂u/∂X, ∂u/∂p)\n\nThe image point and both Jacobians — transform_deriv(lens, X) and transform_deriv_params(lens, X) — in ONE ForwardDiff evaluation over (X, p): the entry point for a bundle's or a PnP solver's per-observation residual.\n\nThis is GTSAM's project(point, Dpose, Dpoint, Dcal), with the Jacobians RETURNED rather than written into optional output arguments; the Ceres analogue is what a CostFunction::Evaluate fills.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.project_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projected_radius-Tuple{AbstractLensModel, Real}","page":"Lenses","text":"projected_radius(lens, θ) -> ρ_d\n\nA lens's radial projection function, read off the contract: the image radius of the ray at view angle θ (the classical r(θ) — equidistant r = θ, stereographic 2·tan(θ/2), Kannala–Brandt's θ-polynomial, …), i.e. project at azimuth 0, so it holds past 90° wherever the lens does. NaN where the lens does not image; θ ≥ π — the exact backward ray, a circle rather than a point — never is. Its inverse is view_angle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projected_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_ratio-Tuple{Radial, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"radial_ratio(r::Radial, u) -> N(u)/D(u)\n\nThe radial factor of r at its basis variable u (ρ² for the Even basis): for a LiftLens, the undistortion x_u = x_d · radial_ratio(radial(lens), ρ_d²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort_normalized!-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractLensModel, AbstractArray}","page":"Lenses","text":"See project!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort_normalized!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort_normalized-Tuple{AbstractLensModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"undistort_normalized(lens, xd::StaticVector{2}) -> Vec2\n\nThe perspective point of the ray behind the image point xd — the view of backproject(lens, xd) on the plane Z = 1. NaN where the lens does not image xd, and where its ray has Z ≤ 0 (no perspective point exists).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort_normalized"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_angle-Tuple{AbstractLensModel, Real}","page":"Lenses","text":"view_angle(lens, ρ_d) -> θ\n\nThe view angle of the ray behind the image point at radius ρ_d — the inverse of projected_radius, read off backproject at azimuth 0. NaN where the lens does not image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{AbstractLensModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"backproject(lens::AbstractLensModel, xd::StaticVector{2}) -> Vec3\n\nThe default backproject of a lens that defines only project: Newton's method on project(lens, X(m)) = x_d over the equidistant chart m = θ·(cos φ, sin φ) of the ray X(m) = (sin θ·m̂, cos θ) — so view angles past 90° are reached — started at the pinhole ray's chart point atan(|x_d|)·x̂_d, each step capped at 0.3 rad, a fixed 30 trips (kernel-safe: no throw, no allocation), the 2 × 2 Jacobian by forward-mode differentiation of project. The unit ray, or NaN unless the iterate converged (the residual within the solve tolerance of its precision), the forward map keeps orientation there (det J > 0) and θ < π. Generic in the number type: Float32 and ForwardDiff Duals.\n\nEvery lens of this package has its own inverse (closed form, or a solver that follows the imaged branch), which takes precedence; this one is for a downstream lens type, and agrees with each of them in its domain (tested to 95% of each lens's view_angle_limit; within a few percent of a fold Newton converges too slowly and the answer is NaN).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{Lens, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"backproject(lens, xd::StaticVector{2}) -> Vec3\n\nThe UNIT ray behind the normalized image point xd — the other half of the lens contract, with project(lens, X). NaN where the lens does not image the point (outside its frame's domain, past a fold, a non-converged inverse), never a throw.\n\nWhere the in-plane distortion folds, a point has several preimages, and the answer is the one on the imaged branch: det J > 0 there and every Radial factor before its fold, reached from the axis. Two consequences:\n\nproject tests only the radial factors, so a ray past a fold of the deltas of an Additive or of a composition's Tilt projects to a finite point whose backproject is the imaged branch's preimage (a different ray), or NaN — never that ray.\nThe acceptance test is pointwise. Where the map overlaps itself INSIDE the axis-connected component, a point has several preimages on the branch and backproject returns the first the solver reaches — rare (0.05 % of the interior points of deliberately strong OpenCV chains), but an undistortion map built from backproject may jump between preimages from one pixel to the next there. Whether a lens is injective over a sensor is a property of the LENS, for an optional check over the sensor rectangle, not something tested per pixel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{LiftLens, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"backproject(lens::LiftLens, xd) -> Vec3\n\nThe lift (x_d·N, D), normalized: D = 0 is the 90° ray and D < 0 a ray behind it; only the Euclidean point x_d·N/D has a pole there, not the direction. Past the lift's first fold (where its view angle first stops increasing) the point is not an image of the lens and the ray is NaN — found exactly, so a point past a second fold is NaN too: the same branch project solves on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{Lens, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"project(lens, X::StaticVector{3}) -> Vec2\n\nThe NORMALIZED image point of the ray through X (any positive scale) — half of the lens contract, with backproject(lens, xd). NaN where the lens does not image the ray, never a throw; allocation-free and generic in the number type (Float32 kernels, ForwardDiff Duals).\n\nA Lens projects the ray onto its projection's frame and distorts the frame point there; no perspective hop is involved unless the projection IS the perspective one, whose domain genuinely ends at 90°. A LiftLens solves the image radius as a root on the ray.\n\nA ray past the fold (or pole) of a radial factor is NaN: every Radial factor is tested at its own input — a lone one, an Additive's head, each radial stage of a composed distortion (∘). The other folds are NOT tested forward, since that would cost a Jacobian per projected point: where the deltas of an Additive (tangential, thin prism) or a composition's Tilt fold the map (det J ≤ 0 inside the radial factor's disk), project returns a finite point, and backproject of that point is not the ray — it is the imaged branch's preimage, a different ray, or NaN where the branch does not reach the point. So the round trip backproject(lens, project(lens, X)) ≈ X / ‖X‖ holds on the imaged branch (the region connected to the axis where det J > 0 and every radial factor is before its fold), not past such a fold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{LiftLens, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Lenses","text":"project(lens::LiftLens, X) -> Vec2\n\nThe image point of a ray under the lift, solved ON THE RAY: with (r, z) = (sin θ, cos θ) the image radius ρ is the root of h(ρ) = ρ·N·z − r·D on the lift's monotone branch. NaN past the fold, where the lens images nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LensManifold","page":"Lenses","text":"LensManifold(lens) <: Manifold\n\nThe lens's ParameterManifold: parameter_vector's coordinates as offsets from the anchor lens, each kept inside its parameter_bounds by the chart that table gives (the double sphere's α ∈ [0, 1] through its logit, its ξ > −1 through a log). minimal_manifold(lens) is this manifold; restrict it to a target's free coefficients by mask or by name (restrict(ch, (:k1, :k2))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LensManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ParameterManifold","page":"Lenses","text":"ParameterManifold(x) <: Manifold\n\nThe manifold of a model x with the parameter protocol — a lens, a LogicalIntrinsics, a parametric CameraModel (intrinsics then lens) — anchored at x: parameter_vector's coordinates, each moved by a chart that keeps it inside its parameter_bounds, the one statement of the model's domain:\n\nbound (open ends) chart coordinate v of the value t (anchor t₀)\nnone finite t = t₀ + v\na t = a + (t₀ − a)·exp(v)\nb t = b − (b − t₀)·exp(−v)\na and b t = a + (b − a)·logistic(logit((t₀ − a)/(b − a)) + v)\n\nand a CLOSED endpoint — a value the model takes, such as α = 0 or ω = 0 (parameter_bounds lists them) — is a mirror: the open ends' chart (the plain t₀ + v when it has none) reflected at it, so a value past it folds back inside (with two closed ends, a fold of period 2(b − a)) and the slope keeps its magnitude with its sign flipped per reflection — never the flat residual of a clamp, at which a fixed-chart LM that overshot the end would stall. In range the value is the plain chart's, bit for bit. So no step leaves the domain and every fitter (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's fit_camera, a calibration bundle) keeps the model valid without knowing why, and an anchor at a closed endpoint moves with slope 1, not from the far end of a logit. An anchor on (or past) an OPEN end is moved inside it by a margin of 10⁻⁶ of the interval (of max(1, |a|) for a one-sided bound); an anchor past a closed end is clamped onto it; a mirrored coordinate (ω, whose sign is not part of the map) is anchored at |ω|. ω = 0 is a stationary point of the field-of-view model itself — its map is even in ω, so ∂project/∂ω = 0 there whatever the chart — so a fit of ω starts at a small ω ≠ 0. A zero chart offset is the anchor's value bit for bit, so a coordinate restrict holds fixed never drifts across recenters. restrict it to the free coefficients by mask or by name (restrict(ch, (:k1, :k2)); a camera's restrict(ch, (:f, :cx, :cy, :k1)) fixes its skew and aspect). minimal_manifold(lens) and minimal_manifold(camera) are this manifold. action_jacobian(ch, x) is the model's parameter Jacobian of its action on x through the chart: the lens's transform_deriv_params(lens, x), a camera's ∂project(camera, X)/∂θ (ForwardDiff).\n\nLensManifold is its name for a lens.\n\nA solver's box\n\nParameterManifold(x; box) adds an optimizer's search box — the solver option parameter_bounds leaves out (a prior range such as a focal length between 0.1 and 50, not a model fact): a NamedTuple of (lo, hi) intervals keyed by parameter_names, a name not listed unboxed. The box is intersected with the domain and acts at the step: recenter clamps the stepped free parameters into it (a coordinate restrict holds fixed is never clamped), so an NLLSsolver variable on the manifold (or a restriction of it) never leaves it; retract is the chart's alone. The anchor is taken as given. parameter_box reads the intersected box.\n\nA coordinate's scale\n\nParameterManifold(x; scale) scales coordinates — a NamedTuple of positive factors keyed by parameter_names, a name not listed 1: the coordinate v of a parameter with scale s moves its chart by s·v (t = t₀ + s·v unbounded, t = a + (t₀ − a)·exp(s·v) bounded below, …), so retract_jacobian carries the factor and local_coordinates divides by it. The manifold is the same set; only the units of its coordinates change. An optimizer that damps every coordinate alike (λ·I) needs them comparable: a pixel camera's principal point (pixels, with scale = (; cx = W, cy = H) one coordinate per image width) next to its log-charted focal length and its unit-free lens coefficients. coordinate_scale reads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ParameterManifold"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convert_eltype","page":"Lenses","text":"convert_eltype(::Type{T}, x) where {T <: Real}\n\nx rebuilt with every stored number in T: a lens (its coefficients), a LogicalIntrinsics (K, with K⁻¹ recomputed in T), a CameraModel (both), a BoundedCamera (its camera, the bound kept), a plain EuclideanMap or RotationMap (rotation and translation, representation and frames kept), a RadialHomography (its H, coefficients and centre; T <: AbstractFloat there), a SplineLens or a NoncentralSplineModel (the splines' controls; their areas stay as stored; T <: AbstractFloat there). Use it to move a camera to Float32 before a GPU launch, or back to Float64, or to a ForwardDiff Dual for a Jacobian by forward differentiation (a Dual is a Real, not an AbstractFloat).\n\nA lens's and a grid model's method is also what the batched and fused kernels apply once per launch, so per-element math never promotes (Metal has no Float64): a downstream lens type that adds a method here is converted by them too.\n\nPublic, not exported; a downstream camera or lens type adds its own method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convert_eltype"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coordinate_scale-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ParameterManifold}","page":"Lenses","text":"coordinate_scale(ch::ParameterManifold) -> SVector{N, T} or nothing\n\nThe coordinate scales of a ParameterManifold built with scale, entry by entry over parameter_vector (1 for a name the scale does not list), in the float type of the scales given (Float32 scales keep a retraction of Float32 coordinates in Float32); nothing for a manifold without one (every scale 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coordinate_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.free_parameter_names-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Lenses","text":"free_parameter_names(model, fixed) -> NTuple{N, Symbol}\nfree_parameter_names(names::Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}, fixed) -> NTuple{N, Symbol}\n\nThe parameters a calibration moves: parameter_names(model) (or names), in that order, less :skew and less the fixed ones. A fixed entry is a parameter name (:f, :cx, :k2, :d1) or a GROUP, a coefficient family's stem standing for all of its numbered coefficients (:k for k1…, :d for d1…, :p for the tangential terms, :sx/:sy for the thin prism's). :skew is the gauge, held always, so naming it is accepted and changes nothing (fixed = parameter_names(model) holds everything). An entry that names nothing of the model, or a parameter named twice (directly or through a group), is an ArgumentError.\n\nLensSpec's fixed and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's refine_camera_and_poses resolve their fixed here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.free_parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_from_record-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Lenses","text":"lens_from_record([T = Float64,] d::AbstractDict) -> AbstractLensModel\n\nThe lens of a lens_record, its coefficients in T. Unknown keys, missing or extra coefficients, and a second distortion are refused, naming the offending key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_from_record"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_label-Tuple{Pinhole}","page":"Lenses","text":"lens_label(lens) -> String\n\nA lens's display name — a report's label, a run's name — by the tag rule: a named family is snake_case of its name (\"pinhole\", \"kannala_brandt\", \"division\", \"scaramuzza\"); any other lens is its lens_record's leaf tags in the record's order, joined by _ (a lift is lift_ and its term): Lens(DoubleSphere(ξ, α)) is \"double_sphere\", an OpenCV-14 lens \"perspective_tilt_radial_tangential_thin_prism\". A label, never read back: lens_from_record reads the record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_record-Tuple{Lens}","page":"Lenses","text":"lens_record(lens) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nThe lens as a record: {\"equidistant\": [], \"radial\": [[k₁, …], []]}, {\"perspective\": [], \"add\": [{\"radial\": …}, {\"tangential\": [p₁, p₂]}]}, {\"lift\": {\"radial\": [[], [k₁]]}} — keys are the leaves' tags, arities are array lengths, nothing is named or padded. lens_from_record inverts it exactly: written with shortest round-trip floats, a Float64 lens reads back bitwise identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_bounds","page":"Lenses","text":"parameter_bounds(x) -> (lo::SVector, hi::SVector)\n\nThe interval each coefficient of parameter_vector may take, entry by entry — the model's DOMAIN, where it is defined: (-Inf, Inf) unless the model says otherwise. Any interval (a, b), a < b, either end infinite: the double sphere's ξ > −1 and α ∈ [0, 1], the unified model's ξ > −1, the extended unified model's α ∈ [0, 1] and β ≥ 0, the field-of-view term's ω ∈ [0, π), a LogicalIntrinsics's f > 0 and aspect > 0; a CameraModel's are its intrinsics' then its lens's.\n\nAn endpoint is open unless the model is defined there, as written above: the closed ones are the double sphere's α = 0 and α = 1, the extended unified model's α = 0 (the pinhole), α = 1 and β = 0, and the field-of-view term's ω = 0 (the identity). ω and −ω are one map, so a FieldOfView(ω) with ω < 0 is the lens FieldOfView(|ω|), which is how ParameterManifold anchors it.\n\nThis is the only place a bound is stated: ParameterManifold (a lens's LensManifold) reads it, so no step leaves the domain. An optimizer's box — a prior range a solver searches, such as a focal length between 0.1 and 50 — is not a model fact and is not stated here: it is the solver's option, keyed by parameter_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_box-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ParameterManifold}","page":"Lenses","text":"parameter_box(ch::ParameterManifold) -> (lo::SVector{N, Float64}, hi::SVector{N, Float64}) or nothing\n\nThe search box of a ParameterManifold built with box, entry by entry over parameter_vector and intersected with the model's parameter_bounds (an entry the box does not name is its domain); nothing for a manifold without one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_box"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_names","page":"Lenses","text":"parameter_names(x) -> NTuple{N, Symbol}\n\nThe names of parameter_vector, in its order — what a configuration refers to when it frees or fixes coefficients by name. A lens's are generated from its leaves: k1…, d1… (Radial), p1, p2, sx1…, sy1…, tx, ty, omega, xi, alpha, beta. A LogicalIntrinsics's are (:f, :aspect, :skew, :cx, :cy).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_vector","page":"Lenses","text":"parameter_vector(x) -> SVector\n\nThe coefficients of a model x — a lens, a calibratable target or its calibration, or a LogicalIntrinsics — in the order of parameter_names. Inverse of with_parameters.\n\nA lens's are the projection's, then the distortion's terms depth-first, left to right — for terms in sequence, D₂ ∘ D₁, CoordinateTransformations' order, the outer D₂'s first — each term in its own documented order (Radial: k…, d…; Tangential: p1, p2; ThinPrism: sx…, sy…; …); empty for a lens without coefficients. A PlanarTarget's are its calibration's (empty for Rigid). A LogicalIntrinsics's are f, aspect, skew, cx, cy of K = [f s cx; 0 f·aspect cy; 0 0 1], absolute and in pixels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameters-Tuple{Union{AbstractIntrinsics, AbstractLensModel, CameraModel}}","page":"Lenses","text":"parameters(x) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe model's coefficients by name: parameters(KannalaBrandt(-0.04, 0.01)).k2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_parameters-Tuple{AbstractLensModel, AbstractVector}","page":"Lenses","text":"with_parameters(x, θ::AbstractVector) -> x\n\nThe model rebuilt from the coefficient vector θ (parameter_vector' inverse): the same structure and arities, the number type promoted to θ's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_parameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModelSpec","page":"Lenses","text":"CameraModelSpec(lens::LensSpec; intrinsics::Type{<:AbstractIntrinsics} = LogicalIntrinsics)\nCameraModelSpec(:spline; grid_size = nothing, calib_area = nothing)\nCameraModelSpec(table::AbstractDict)\n\nWhat a calibration is for: an intrinsics kind (the type parameter I: metric LogicalIntrinsics, or the projective ProjectiveIntrinsics of a partial calibration) and a LensSpec or a SplineLensSpec (the field model). The spline form is always metric.\n\ntoml_value(spec) is the table Dict(\"intrinsics\" => \"metric\" | \"projective\", \"lens\" => …, …) (a LensSpec table plus intrinsics) or Dict(\"type\" => \"spline\", \"grid_size\" => […], \"calib_area\" => […]) with the unset keys left out; CameraModelSpec(table) reads it back, and intrinsics defaults to \"metric\" when absent (a spline table may also name it, as \"metric\" only). == compares the specification.\n\nA parameterized intrinsics (LogicalIntrinsics{Float64}) names its kind: it is normalized to the wrapper (LogicalIntrinsics), so the specification round trips equal.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError for an intrinsics other than metric or projective (and toml_value for an intrinsics kind with neither name), for a spline table whose intrinsics is not metric, for an unknown type or key (the grid's type before Core 0.9 included: it names \"spline\"), and for whatever LensSpec or SplineLensSpec refuses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModelSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LensSpec","page":"Lenses","text":"LensSpec(family::Symbol; fixed = (), structure...)\nLensSpec(record::AbstractDict)\nLensSpec(lens::AbstractLensModel)\n\nA lens model specified by family name and term counts, not by a record of zeros: the lens a calibrator starts from (see lens(::LensSpec)) and the coefficients it moves (free_names).\n\nThe families and their structure keywords:\n\n:division, :kannala_brandt: coefficients (an integer ≥ 1), the d1…dn of Division, the k1…kn of KannalaBrandt;\n:brown_conrady: radial (≥ 1) and tangential (a Bool, default false), the k1…kn, p1, p2 of OpenCV's perspective model;\n:rational: numerator (≥ 0) and denominator (≥ 1), OpenCV's rational radial factor k1…k(numerator) over d1…d(denominator);\n:perspective, :equidistant, :stereographic, :equisolid, :orthographic, :double_sphere, :unified, :extended_unified: a projection with no distortion, no keywords.\n\nThe reference values of a family are those of the member that equals its base projection: all-zero coefficients for the polynomial families, and for the parametric projections the parameters that reduce them to perspective (DoubleSphere(0, 0), Unified(0), ExtendedUnified(0, 1)).\n\nfixed lists the coefficients a calibrator HOLDS (the stack's one parameter-holding keyword), each by parameter_names of the lens or by a group (:k for k1…; free_parameter_names); the default () moves all of them, and free_names is the complement. free is refused, naming fixed.\n\nThe record form, LensSpec(Dict(\"record\" => lens_record(lens))), covers every composition the table does not name, and is the only form with numbers: a lens with non-zero starting or held coefficients. LensSpec(lens) is the inverse of lens(spec): the named form only when the lens's structure has a name AND its coefficients are the reference values (compared whatever their element type, so a Float32 lens at the reference values is named; a -0.0 is not the reference 0.0), else the record form, so no value is ever dropped.\n\ntoml_value(spec) is Dict(\"lens\" => \"division\", \"coefficients\" => 3, …) or Dict(\"lens\" => Dict(\"record\" => …)), plus \"fixed\" when not empty; LensSpec(::AbstractDict) reads it back (the keys of the table the spec sits in). == compares the specification, not the lens.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError for an unknown family (listing the known ones), a keyword the family does not take or lacks, a count out of range, a fixed name that is not a parameter name of the lens, a retired free, and a record that is not a lens record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LensSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SplineLensSpec","page":"Lenses","text":"SplineLensSpec(; grid_size = nothing, calib_area = nothing)\n\nThe lattice of a SplineLens camera (the central grid camera: one ray per lattice node, no lens family): grid_size, the (columns, rows) of the control grid, and calib_area, the (x0, y0, x1, y1) pixel rectangle the grid covers; nothing leaves either to the calibrator.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError for a grid_size that is not two positive integers or a calib_area that is not four integers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SplineLensSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.free_names-Tuple{LensSpec}","page":"Lenses","text":"free_names(spec::LensSpec) -> NTuple{N, Symbol}\n\nThe coefficients a calibrator moves, by parameter_names of lens(spec), in that order: every one but spec's fixed (free_parameter_names: an entry names a coefficient or a group, :k for k1…).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.free_names"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lenses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.intrinsics_kind-Union{Tuple{CameraModelSpec{I}}, Tuple{I}} where I","page":"Lenses","text":"intrinsics_kind(spec::CameraModelSpec) -> Type{<:AbstractIntrinsics}\n\nThe intrinsics type spec calibrates: LogicalIntrinsics for a metric calibration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.intrinsics_kind"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#Robust-Estimation","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"note: Division of labor\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore owns the contracts every robust estimator composes through — the problem protocol, the scoring contract, the local-optimization contract — together with the robust-statistics toolkit, the loss family and the baseline scorers. The one generic RANSAC loop, the problems and the core local optimizations live in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.jl; the paper scorers in RANSACScoringDoneRight and the paper local optimizers in LocalOptimizationDoneRight implement the contracts. See The composable RANSAC below.","title":"Robust Estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#Which-function-for-which-task","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"An estimator's name states its kind: a one-step algorithm is called by its algorithm's name; a minimal solver runs through the solve(solver, cs, idx) protocol; iterative fits (fit_*), robust searches (find_*) and refinements from a guess (refine_*) live in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation. Data go in as correspondences (a => b pairs, a Correspondences preferred), with per-correspondence weights as its weights column.\n\nTask Function Kind Package\nHomography from ≥ 4 point correspondences homography_dlt(cs) one-step VGC\nFundamental matrix from ≥ 8 correspondences fundamental_dlt(cs) one-step VGC\nEssential matrix from ≥ 8 bearing pairs essential_dlt(cs) (PoseLib backend) one-step VGC\nRotation from vector pairs (R·a ≈ b) kabsch(cs) one-step VGC\nMean of a set of rotations chordal_mean(Rs) one-step VGC\nFocal lengths from plane homographies zhang_intrinsics(Hs[, pitch]) one-step VGC\nLine through 2D points, with its covariance tls_line(points) one-step VGC\nThe point nearest to N lines (origin => direction pairs) nearest_point(lines) one-step VGC\nEllipse through 2D points (algebraic, always an ellipse) direct_ellipse(points) one-step VGC\nA pure-bending height field through scattered heights (point => height pairs, the plane dropped) HeightField(nodes, points .=> heights; smoothing) one-step VGC\nNormalize points for a solve (centroid, or a known centre) AffineMap(HartleyNormalization(; center), points) normalization VGC\nNormalize a correspondence set for a model class CorrespondenceNormalization(M, cs; center) normalization VGC\nStore image points resolution-independently (pools) AffineMap(DiagonalNormalization(size)) normalization VGC\nHomography from a 4-point sample solve(Homography4pt(), cs, idx) minimal VGC\nRadially distorted homography from 5 points solve(Homography5ptRadial(), cs, idx) minimal VGC\nFundamental matrix from a 7-point sample solve(Fundamental7pt(), cs, idx) minimal VGC\nEssential matrix from 5 bearing pairs solve(Essential5pt(), cs, idx) minimal VGC\nAbsolute pose from 3 point–bearing pairs solve(P3P(), cs, idx) minimal VGC\nCircle through 3 points solve(Circle3pt(), points, idx) minimal VGC\nEllipse through 5 points solve(Ellipse5pt(), points, idx) minimal VGC\nRadial camera from 5 plane–pixel pairs solve(RadialCamera5pt(; center), cs, idx) minimal VGC\nA model from contaminated correspondences find_homography, find_absolute_pose, find_radial_camera, find_circle, find_ellipse, … find (RANSAC) RVG\nRadial camera of one board, whitened iterations fit_radial_camera fit RVG\nDivision camera (focal and distortion) from boards fit_division_camera (over division_camera_step) fit RVG\nA camera model re-expressed in another family (a lens family, a SplineLens and its forward spline) over a region fit_camera fit RVG\nLine through points carrying covariances fit_line fit RVG\nA model refined from a given one (no scorer: the caller's scale or width) refine_homography, refine_absolute_pose, refine_circle, refine_ellipse, … refine RVG\nRobot–world / hand–eye from a guess refine_robot_world_hand_eye refine RVG\n\nThe kind fixes how a call can fail: an algorithm is one deterministic computation and can only be degenerate; a fit can fail to converge; a find is randomized; a refine can end in a local minimum.","title":"Which function for which task"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#find_*-and-refine_*","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Every RVG find_* is RANSAC's sampling around one local-optimization step: it scores hypotheses and ORIGINATES a scale (its result's noise_scale). Its refine_* starts from a given model (guess first, then the data as the find_* takes them) and estimates no scale: it runs no scorer, and its robust loss — optimization_strategy = Triggs{Shape} (the default, Welsch: one robustified LM, no reweighting cycles; an Irls{Shape} is refused) or nothing for least squares — runs at the caller's scale (a number, or an upstream result such as the find_*'s) times the loss's efficiency_constant, or at a given width. It returns the refined model with per-datum loss weights and the scale it ran with. The two take one family of AbstractOptimizationStrategy types under two keywords (local_optimization_strategy on a find_*); the RANSAC-family seeded step, with a scorer and its consensus set, is VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's locally_optimize.","title":"find_* and refine_*"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#The-robust-loss-family","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"One unified family of loss SHAPES (AbstractLossShape) replaces per-package loss-function copies. A loss shape is a normalized scale family ρ_w(r) = w²·ρ₁(r/w): every member shares the quadratic regime at the origin, and its scale parameter w, the loss_width, is the residual scale at which it leaves that regime (LOSSES.md at the repository root states the normalization). The shapes: GaussianShape, WelschShape, GemanMcclureShape, CauchyShape, SmoothTLSShape, TLSShape, HuberShape, and the Barron α-family dial (BarronShape). Traits classify them (liftable, bounded). A shape is paired with the width it runs at by deploy — loss_width = efficiency_constant · ŝ, the width as a multiple of the noise scale — and the resulting DeployedLoss answers the one-argument verbs that read that width: lift_weight, lift_rho, lift_conjugate, lift_cost, lift_zblock. decision_width converts a decision threshold (the residual where a method's inlier/outlier decision flips) into each shape's native width, and is_map_inlier is the MAP decision on a posterior membership weight.\n\nThe GNC surrogate facet supplies the annealed closed form (gnc_tls_weight) and the engine vocabulary (gnc_weight, gnc_target, gnc_mu0), all dispatched on the gnc_surrogate trait.","title":"The robust-loss family"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#The-composable-RANSAC","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"A robust search is a composition of three parts, each behind a contract this package owns: a problem (data, residuals, minimal solver, weighted refit), a scoring (which hypothesis wins, and its consensus set), and a local optimization (how the winner is refined). VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's loop calls only the contract verbs, so a scorer or an optimizer from any package plugs in as a keyword:\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation, LocalOptimizationDoneRight\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore: StudentTUniformScoring, JeffreysPrior, FlatLabelPrior\nfind_homography(cs)       # core default: the class's scorer (MSAC) + Irls{TLSShape} at its decision point\nfind_homography(cs; scoring = StudentTUniformScoring(HomographyProblem, 20.0;   # outlier half-width, pixels\n                        scale_prior = JeffreysPrior(), label_prior = FlatLabelPrior()),\n                    local_optimization_strategy = HQ{WelschShape}())                     # an optimizer (LODR)\n\nEvery dispatch is static: the composed workspace is typed on the scorer's and the optimizer's workspaces, so one RANSAC call compiles per (problem, scoring, local optimization).\n\nThe problem protocol (AbstractConstraintProblem): data_size, codimension, minimal_sample_size and constraint_with_jacobians, from which the signed whitened_residual and the distances mahalanobis_distances! are derived (a problem may supply a faster residual_context / mahalanobis_distance path); optionally solver_covariance for predictive scoring; and the weighted refit refine!(R, problem, θ, w) on refine_workspace(P, n) that local optimizations call.\n\nThe scoring contract (AbstractScoring). A scorer implements four verbs:\n\nverb meaning\nscoring_workspace(scoring, n, d_g) its scratch, built once per composed workspace\nscore!(W, scoring, scores, residuals, n; sample, bound) the score of one model (higher is better)\ninlier_count(scoring, W) the size k* of the consensus set of the last score!\nconsensus_order(scoring, W) an order whose first k* entries are the consensus set\n\nOptional verbs have defaults: is_predictive and scored_model (a scorer that scores a model with its covariance), scale_prior (Jeffreys), outlier_halfwidth (a uniform outlier model, nothing by default), decision_boundary with its static trait has_decision_boundary (the decision point a width-free optimizer follows), score_bound (a bounded-sum scorer may stop a score early) and check_workspace. The derived verbs are stated once and never overridden: consensus_s2, consensus_set (the ConsensusSet, the inlier set and its Student-t scale that local optimization starts from), inlier_probabilities! and score_model!, the one scoring path over a ScoreBuffers. with_width(scoring, w) and width_axis(scoring) rebuild a scorer at another width on its native axis.\n\nThe baseline scorers live here: RansacScoring (inlier count) and MsacScoring (truncated quadratic). MsacScoring(problem; threshold = τ) is a loop scorer at a threshold in the problem's residual unit; the width-free MsacScoring() is the grid family that pool selection sweeps over a width grid. Both are AbstractThresholdScorings: a family at a threshold supplies only its per-point residual_quality (and, where it is proven, its score_bound) and inherits the one threshold score! with its consensus set, so a downstream loss-width family shares the loop.\n\nThe local-optimization contract (AbstractOptimizationStrategy):\n\nlo_workspace(::Type{P}, ::Type{T}, lo, n) -> L     # built once; default nothing\nlocally_optimize!(L, lo, problem, θ, cs::ConsensusSet, buf::ScoreBuffers, scoring)\n    -> lo_estimate(θ′, code, rule; iterations, residuals_current)\n\nOn entry residuals(buf) holds θ's residuals and cs is the consensus set of the score that just ran; the optimizer may overwrite the buffers and call score_model!, but never scores for acceptance (the loop re-scores and keeps the incumbent). A width-carrying optimizer has a width mode, read by lo_width and set by with_width(lo, w): nothing follows the scorer's decision_boundary, EfficiencyConstant(m) anchors the width at m times the consensus scale, and LossWidth(w) is an absolute width. loss_at is the one resolution of a mode into a DeployedLoss, and check_composition refuses, once at entry, an optimizer that follows a scorer with no threshold. The label trait scale_interface is derived from the mode (FollowsScorer, ConsensusAnchored, FixedScale).\n\nExternal refiners. A strategy that runs outside the loop (a third-party solver) declares RefitFamily(lo) = ExternalRefit() and implements external_workspace(lo, problem, frame, scoring), optionally prepare_external!, and refine_external!(ws, lo, problem, θ, width, scoring) -> θ′ | nothing, a pure model-to-model refine at an absolute LossWidth (a FixedS2(s2) noise scale is the width √s2 unless the refiner reads it itself). In-loop strategies are NativeRefit(), the default.\n\nThe chart-rows contract (rows_provider, lift_init, lift_refresh!, lift_jacobian!, lift_retract, lift_model, lift_valid, lift_rows) — what a lifted local optimizer reads of a chart problem: its whitened rows, their Jacobian and per-point norms in a RowsWorkspace. Problems provide it (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation); the half-quadratic lifts and their continuation schedules that consume it are LocalOptimizationDoneRight's.\n\nPool selection over a benchmark pool record (Pools.PoolPair): best_pool_model(scoring, pair, R) is the per-pair argmax of score! over the stored models; select_on_grid!(idxs, P, scoring, grid, staged) the selections at every width of a grid, after selection_staging(scoring) and stage_selection(key, pairs, residuals; backend). The generic method is a CPU fallback (per column with_width, then best_pool_model); a grid family with device kernels (RANSACScoringDoneRight) implements its own staging and selection.\n\nWidth axes. One name per width quantity, each a value carrier and, bare, an axis tag: EfficiencyConstant, LossWidth and OutlierHalfwidth (a scorer's width, never an optimizer's). width_axis(name) / width_axis_name(axis) map the persisted names, and to_native / native_map convert an efficiency constant into an axis's native width at the same decision point.\n\nConfiguration tags and quality gates. A TOML tag that selects a type goes through a TagRegistry, whose tags are snake_case of the type name with the family's suffix removed (TLSShape → \"tls\", StudentTUniform → \"student_t_uniform\"); a renamed tag fails with its successor's name. The quality gates (test_quality_gates) check every registry, every config tag, the stack dependency allow-list, and that a package adds methods to another stack package's functions only through that owner's CONTRACT_GENERICS. The conformance helpers test_scoring_contract, test_local_optimization_contract and test_constraint_problem (loaded with Test) check an implementation.","title":"The composable RANSAC"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#Interfaces-and-loops","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Scoring/LO interface hierarchy (AbstractScoring, AbstractOptimizationStrategy, RefitFamily, AbstractWeightPolicy) — the type hierarchy of the contracts above.\nGeneric block-coordinate-descent loop (block_coordinate_descent!, AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod and its extension verbs) — the model/nuisance/weights loop (EM, IRLS, GNC, Barron-adaptive) stated once, extended by downstream methods.\nChart-rows contract (rows_provider, lift_rows, RowsWorkspace) — what a lifted local optimizer reads of a chart problem: its whitened rows, their Jacobian and per-point norms. The lifts and their continuation schedules are LocalOptimizationDoneRight's.\nThe one LM (lmsolve_chart!, LmStops; static_lm, StaticLmStops) — the stack's Levenberg–Marquardt: NLLSsolver (Marquardt's DᵀD damping) through ONE adapter, lmsolve_chart!, on a linearization state's residual_rows! / residual_jacobian_rows! (a ManifoldLmWorkspace keeps the problem and the solver's internal data), the same robustified per datum, or a multi-block LmProblem (the bundles: heterogeneous variables and residual blocks, each with its own robustifier). Its stop settings have ONE owner, LmStops (configs/default.toml [lm]), and every solve ONE success rule: the stationarity postcondition at the returned point (is_stationary), at every stop that is not a failure or a cap. A kernel's fixed-N solve is the one device LM, static_lm, under StaticLmStops and the same rule. No package calls NLLSsolver's solver API directly: check_duplication fails it. The Schur complement (NLLSsolver pull request #60, still open) is used when the loaded NLLSsolver has it (nlls_features); see \"The Schur complement of the LM driver\" in Getting Started.","title":"Interfaces and loops"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#Model-manifolds-and-covariance","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Model manifolds (Manifold: EssentialManifold5, FundMatManifold7, HomographyManifold8, RigidManifold6, …) provide local parameterizations of the model manifolds with first-order covariance transport between charts and the ambient space (transform_covariance, the ambient chart EuclideanManifold(ch)); implicit_covariance derives the solver covariance by the implicit function theorem — of a minimal solve, and of the whitened least-squares estimator on an overdetermined sample. Image-domain confidence regions (confidence_ellipse, epipolar_band, LineConfidenceBand) turn those covariances into point-transfer ellipses and Hartley–Zisserman epipolar-line bands (Hartley and Zisserman, 2003).","title":"Model manifolds and covariance"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimators/#API-reference","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"See the Robust Estimation API for the complete reference.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/primitives/#Primitives-API","page":"Primitives","text":"Geometric primitives (circles, ellipses, discs, feature frames, hyperplanes, 3D conics) and their traits and operations. See the Primitives guide.","title":"Primitives API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/primitives/#Primitives-and-Feature-Frames","page":"Primitives","text":"","title":"Primitives and Feature Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"api/primitives/#Hyperplanes","page":"Primitives","text":"","title":"Hyperplanes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/primitives/#The-Surface-a-Ray-Meets","page":"Primitives","text":"Two isbits primitives behind one protocol — intersection(s, o, d), normal(s, x), chart(s, x), its inverse surface_point(s, uv) and bounds(s) — generic in the element type (Float32, Float64, ForwardDiff.Dual), allocation-free and usable in a KernelAbstractions kernel. A ray is an origin and a direction in the surface's frame and units, as ray_plane_intersection takes it, and a half-line (t ≥ 0). A Quad is a bounded plane, its intersection ray_plane_intersection's arithmetic bit for bit; a MappedSurface is the surface of a chart map — a deformed posed target's pose ∘ S₃ ∘ lift ∘ C ∘ S₂⁻¹ — its intersection a 2 × 2 root in the chart (the spline models' static LM behind one internal function, the implicit function theorem for Duals, nested ones included). The chart is the DESIGN chart, so a pattern lookup uses nominal coordinates. A ray that misses (parallel, behind the origin, outside the rectangle, or not converging) gives NaN. The normal is geometric, the frame's e_u × e_v; flip it against the ray for a camera-facing one. A posed target's primitive is surface(pt) (Imaging Chain and Stereo API).\n\ns = surface(PosedTarget(target, pose))          # a Quad, or a MappedSurface for a deformed target\nt, x = VisualGeometryToolkitCore.intersection(s, o, d)\nuv = VisualGeometryToolkitCore.chart(s, x)       # the design coordinates of the hit","title":"The Surface a Ray Meets"},{"category":"section","location":"api/primitives/#Conics-and-3D-Primitives","page":"Primitives","text":"","title":"Conics and 3D Primitives"},{"category":"section","location":"api/primitives/#Operations,-Containment,-and-Traits","page":"Primitives","text":"","title":"Operations, Containment, and Traits"},{"category":"section","location":"api/primitives/#Index","page":"Primitives","text":"Pages = [\"primitives.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives","page":"Primitives","text":"Primitives\n\nGeometric primitives submodule for VisualGeometryToolkitCore.\n\nProvides geometric primitive types and operations:\n\nRectangles: Rect constructors from intervals and ranges\nConics: Ellipse and Circle types with conic matrix representations\nHyperplanes: Lines in 2D, planes in 3D (generalized hyperplanes)\nTraits: Conic trait system for dispatch\nOperations: Geometric operations (dilate, intersect, containment)\n\nMain Types\n\nEllipse, Circle, Rect\nHyperplane, Line2D, Plane3D\nConicTrait, CircleTrait, EllipseTrait\n\nMain Functions\n\ndilate, intersects, gradient\nis_ellipse, classify_conic\nsigned_distance, closest_point, normal, offset\n\nExample\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives\n\nellipse = Ellipse(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 5.0, 3.0, π/4)\ncircle = Circle(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 2.0)\ndilated = dilate(circle, 2.0)\n\n# Hyperplanes\nline = Line2D(Point2(0.0, 0.0), Point2(1.0, 1.0))\nplane = Plane3D(Point3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), Vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0))\nd = signed_distance(line, Point2(1.0, 2.0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#GeometryBasics.Circle","page":"Primitives","text":"GeometryBasics.Circle(frame::FeatureFrame, r::Real=1.0) -> Circle\n\nConstruct a Circle from a FeatureFrame at the r-σ contour.\n\nFor isotropic frames (Disc, OrientedDisc), radius = r * σ. For anisotropic frames (EllipticFrame, AffineFrame), radius = r * characteristic_scale (circumscribed circle).\n\nExample\n\ndisc = Disc(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 5.0)\ncircle = Circle(disc, 3.0)  # Circle with radius 15.0 (3σ)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometryBasics.Circle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.AffineFrame","page":"Primitives","text":"AffineFrame{T} <: FeatureFrame{T}\n\nFull affine feature frame with 6 degrees of freedom: translation (2) + general linear (4).\n\nDefines the transformation: x = A·u + center\n\nwhere A is a general invertible 2×2 matrix. This maps the unit circle to an oriented ellipse in image space. Invariant to full affine transformations.\n\nThe extra degree of freedom compared to EllipticFrame is the in-plane rotation that a symmetric matrix cannot represent.\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point2{T}: Center position in image coordinates\nA::SMatrix{2,2,T,4}: Shape factor (general, not necessarily symmetric; shape matrix M = A·Aᵀ)\n\nDecomposition\n\nAny A can be decomposed as A = R(φ)·S where:\n\nS is symmetric positive-definite (the \"pure shape\")\nR(φ) is an in-plane rotation (the extra DOF)\n\nExample\n\nA = @SMatrix [2.0 0.5; -0.5 1.0]  # general shape factor\nframe = AffineFrame(Point2(100.0, 200.0), A)\nT = AffineMap(frame)  # AffineMap: u → A*u + center\n\nSee also: EllipticFrame\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.AffineFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse(frame::FeatureFrame, r::Real=1.0) -> Ellipse\n\nConstruct an Ellipse from a FeatureFrame at the r-σ contour.\n\nFor isotropic frames, returns a circle-equivalent ellipse (a == b). For anisotropic frames, computes semi-axes via SVD of the scaled shape factor.\n\nExample\n\nframe = EllipticFrame(Point2(100.0, 200.0), S)\nell = Ellipse(frame, 2.0)  # Ellipse at 2σ contour\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Tuple{Attributed{<:FeatureFrame, <:Union{CovMat, PrecMat}}}","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse(u::Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}; sigma = 3) -> Ellipse\n\nThe sigma-σ error ellipse of an uncertain frame's centre, for every frame: Ellipse(center(u); sigma), the error ellipse of its point observation (center). (A scaled frame's REGION is region(f, k), a different verb.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame","page":"Primitives","text":"EllipticFrame{T} <: FeatureFrame{T}\n\nAnisotropic feature frame with 5 degrees of freedom: translation (2) + symmetric shape (3).\n\nDefines the transformation: x = S·u + center\n\nwhere S is a symmetric positive-definite 2×2 matrix (the shape factor, shape_factor). The shape matrix is M = S·S' = S² (since S is symmetric, shape_matrix): the region is (x-c)ᵀ M⁻¹ (x-c) ≤ 1.\n\nThis maps the unit circle to an ellipse in image space. Invariant to affine transformations (up to in-plane rotation).\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point2{T}: Center position in image coordinates\nS::SMatrix{2,2,T,4}: Shape factor (symmetric positive-definite)\n\nRelationship to the shape matrix\n\nShape factor: S (what we store)\nShape matrix: M = S² = S·S'\nTo get S from M: S = √M (matrix square root)\n\nExample\n\nS = @SMatrix [2.0 0.5; 0.5 1.0]  # symmetric shape factor\nframe = EllipticFrame(Point2(100.0, 200.0), S)\nT = AffineMap(frame)  # AffineMap: u → S*u + center\nellipse = region(frame, 2.0)  # Ellipse at 2σ contour\n\nSee also: AffineFrame, Distributions.MvNormal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.OrientedDisc","page":"Primitives","text":"OrientedDisc{T} <: FeatureFrame{T}\n\nIsotropic feature frame with orientation: 4 degrees of freedom.\n\nDefines the transformation: x = σ·R(θ)·u + center\n\nwhere R(θ) is a 2D rotation matrix. This maps the unit circle with a marked orientation to an oriented circle in image space. Invariant to full similarity transformations.\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point2{T}: Center position in image coordinates\nσ::T: Characteristic scale\nθ::T: Dominant orientation in radians (measured from +x axis)\n\nExample\n\ndisc = OrientedDisc(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 5.0, π/4)\nT = AffineMap(disc)  # AffineMap with rotation\n\nSee also: Disc, EllipticFrame\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.OrientedDisc"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Paraboloid3D","page":"Primitives","text":"Paraboloid3D{T,P}\n\nQuadratic surface z = a₀ + b'x + x'Cx over a 2D domain.\n\nThe type parameter P encodes the number of free parameters:\n\nP=6: Full anisotropic — b and C are free\nP=4: Isotropic with center — C = cI, b is free\nP=2: Fixed-center isotropic — C = cI, b = 0\n\nStorage is always the full (a₀, b, C) so the geometric interface (shape_matrix, is_peak, EllipticFrame) works uniformly.\n\nFields\n\na₀::T: Intercept (peak value)\nb::SVector{2,T}: Linear gradient\nC::SMatrix{2,2,T,4}: Quadratic Hessian (neg-def for valid peaks)\n\nConstructors\n\nParaboloid3D(θ::SVector{6}): Full anisotropic {T,6}\nParaboloid3D(θ::SVector{4}): Isotropic with center {T,4}\nParaboloid3D(θ::SVector{2}): Fixed-center isotropic {T,2}\nParaboloid3D(d::Disc, a₀): From detection {T,2}\n\nSee also: shape_matrix, is_peak, coeffs, EllipticFrame\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Paraboloid3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Paraboloid3D-Tuple{Disc, Real}","page":"Primitives","text":"Paraboloid3D(d::Disc, a₀::Real) -> Paraboloid3D{T,2}\n\nConstruct a fixed-center isotropic paraboloid from a Disc detection.\n\nThe disc's scale σ maps to the quadratic coefficient c = -1/(2σ²), with zero center offset (b = 0). The intercept a₀ is typically the peak log-intensity z_max = log(max inverted intensity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Paraboloid3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Paraboloid3D-Union{Tuple{Paraboloid3D{T}}, Tuple{Q}, Tuple{T}} where {T, Q}","page":"Primitives","text":"Paraboloid3D{T,Q}(p::Paraboloid3D{T,P}) -> Paraboloid3D{T,Q}\n\nPromote (or demote) a paraboloid to a different parameter count Q. The underlying storage (a₀, b, C) is unchanged — only the type tag changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Paraboloid3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.coeffs-Union{Tuple{Paraboloid3D{T, 6}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"coeffs(p::Paraboloid3D{T,P}) -> SVector{P,T}\n\nReturn the free paraboloid coefficients. The size is determined by P:\n\nP=6: [a₀, bx, by, Cxx, Cxy, Cyy] (full anisotropic)\nP=4: [a₀, bx, by, c] (isotropic with center)\nP=2: [a₀, c] (fixed-center isotropic)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.coeffs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.frame_dof-Tuple{FeatureFrame}","page":"Primitives","text":"frame_dof(::Type{<:FeatureFrame}) -> Int\nframe_dof(f::FeatureFrame) -> Int\n\nThe frame's degrees of freedom, the size of its parameter vector and of an uncertain frame's covariance: PointFrame 2, Disc 3, OrientedDisc 4, EllipticFrame 5, AffineFrame 6. The covariance's coordinates are the centre (x, y) first, then the shape's in the producer's documented chart (a Disc's log σ), so its first 2×2 block is always the centre's (center).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.frame_dof"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_peak-Tuple{Paraboloid3D}","page":"Primitives","text":"is_peak(p::Paraboloid3D) -> Bool\n\nWhether the paraboloid opens downward (its quadratic form is negative definite) — i.e. the fitted surface has a maximum, as expected for a bright-blob response peak. See Paraboloid3D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_peak"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_peak-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2, 2}, T, 2} where T}","page":"Primitives","text":"is_peak(C::StaticMatrix{2, 2}) -> Bool\n\nWhether the quadratic form C of a paraboloid z = a₀ + bᵀx + xᵀCx opens downward (negative definite: C₁₁ < 0, det C > 0): the paraboloid's peak test on its 2×2 coefficients, closed form and kernel-callable — a device fit that holds (C₁₁, C₁₂, C₂₂) tests is_peak(C) and then is_valid_shape(-C; max_aspect_ratio) without building a Paraboloid3D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_peak"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_positive_definite-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Primitives","text":"is_positive_definite(M::StaticMatrix{N, N}) -> Bool\nis_positive_definite(M::AbstractMatrix) -> Bool\n\nPositive-definiteness by Sylvester's criterion: every leading principal minor of the symmetric M is positive. A static N × N matrix tests all N minors, unrolled in plain arithmetic (N ≤ 3: the cofactor expansions; larger N: StaticArrays' det of each leading block); any other matrix tests its leading 2×2 block. At N = 2 the expression is M[1,1] > 0 && M[1,1]·M[2,2] − M[1,2]·M[2,1] > 0, as before. Allocation-free and kernel-callable (no factorization). The test behind is_valid_shape (and, negated, behind is_peak); λ_min(M) > c is is_positive_definite(M − c·I). Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_positive_definite"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_valid_shape-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Primitives","text":"is_valid_shape(S; max_aspect_ratio=DEFAULT_MAX_ASPECT_RATIO) -> Bool\n\nCheck that a 2×2 shape factor is positive-definite and has aspect_ratio √(σ_max/σ_min) ≤ max_aspect_ratio (the frame's, of EllipticFrame(c, S)). Closed form, without building the frame or a decomposition: σ_max·σ_min = |det S| and σ_max² + σ_min² = ‖S‖²_F, so with r = σ_max/σ_min ≥ 1 and k = max_aspect_ratio the test √r ≤ k is r + 1/r ≤ k² + 1/k², i.e. ‖S‖²_F ≤ (k² + k⁻²)·|det S|. Allocation-free and branch-light in the element type: callable inside a kernel on a static 2×2 (a fitted peak's negated quadratic form -C after is_peak).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_valid_shape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc","page":"Primitives","text":"Disc{T} <: FeatureFrame{T}\n\nIsotropic feature frame with 3 degrees of freedom: translation (2) + scale (1).\n\nDefines the transformation: x = σ·u + center\n\nThis maps the unit circle in canonical space to a circle of radius σ in image space. Invariant to similarity transformations (up to rotation).\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point2{T}: Center position in image coordinates\nσ::T: Characteristic scale (not a boundary radius!)\n\nExample\n\ndisc = Disc(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 5.0)\nT = AffineMap(disc)  # AffineMap: u → 5*u + [100, 200]\ncirc = region(disc, 3.0)  # Circle at 3σ\n\nSee also: OrientedDisc, EllipticFrame\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.PointFrame","page":"Primitives","text":"PointFrame{T} <: FeatureFrame{T}\n\nA point feature, the 2-DOF member of the FeatureFrame family: a position and nothing else — no scale, no orientation, no shape. What a detector that locates points without a size estimate returns (a corner, the centre of a rotationally symmetric pattern). Its uncertainty is a 2×2 position covariance, Uncertain(PointFrame(c), CovMat{2}(…)), whose Ellipse is the error ellipse, and whose point observation is center(u).\n\nIt is first-class: every verb every frame has (the FeatureFrame interface table, conventions/feature-frames.md) reaches it through the hoisted code, with no copy of its own, and it costs what a Point2 costs (isbits, the layout of Point2{T}). With no scale, the scaled frames' verbs — scale, shape_factor, shape_matrix, AffineMap, region, Circle, Ellipse(frame, r), characteristic_scale, aspect_ratio, boundary_point, MvNormal, intersects — are absent by design: each is a MethodError whose hint says so.\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point2{T}: the position in image coordinates\n\nExample\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> p = Uncertain(PointFrame(10.0, 20.0), CovMat{2, Float64}(4.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n\njulia> e = Ellipse(p; sigma = 1);   # the 1σ error ellipse\n\njulia> (e.center, e.a, e.b)\n([10.0, 20.0], 2.0, 1.0)\n\njulia> VisualGeometryToolkitCore.center(p) == Uncertain(Point2(10.0, 20.0), CovMat{2, Float64}(4.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0))   # its point observation\ntrue\n\nSee also: Disc\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.PointFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Hyperplane","page":"Primitives","text":"Hyperplane{M,N,T}\n\nAn M-dimensional hyperplane represented by normalized homogeneous coefficients.\n\nThe equation a₁x₁ + a₂x₂ + ... + aₘxₘ + d = 0 is stored as [a₁, ..., aₘ, d] where the normal [a₁, ..., aₘ] is automatically normalized to unit length.\n\nType Parameters\n\nM: Ambient space dimension (2 for lines, 3 for planes)\nN: Number of coefficients (always M+1)\nT: Coefficient type\n\nProperties\n\nnormal(h): Unit normal vector [a₁, ..., aₘ]\noffset(h): Signed distance from origin (-d when normal is unit)\n\nExamples\n\n# 2D line: x - y = 0 (diagonal through origin)\nline = Hyperplane(SVector(1.0, -1.0, 0.0))\nline = Line2D(1.0, -1.0, 0.0)\n\n# 3D plane: z = 5 (horizontal plane at height 5)\nplane = Hyperplane(SVector(0.0, 0.0, 1.0, -5.0))\nplane = Plane3D(Point3(0,0,5), Vec3(0,0,1))\n\n# Point-to-hyperplane signed distance\nd = signed_distance(line, Point2(1.0, 2.0))\n\n# Closest point on hyperplane (orthogonal projection)\np_closest = closest_point(line, Point2(1.0, 2.0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Hyperplane"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D","page":"Primitives","text":"2D line (hyperplane in 2D): ax + by + c = 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D-Tuple{Point2, Point2}","page":"Primitives","text":"Line2D(p::Point2, q::Point2) -> Line2D\n\nConstruct a 2D line through two points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"Primitives","text":"Line2D(ρ, θ) -> Line2D\n\nConstruct a 2D line in Hesse normal form (distance ρ from origin, angle θ). The line equation is: x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ) - ρ = 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D-Union{Tuple{T2}, Tuple{T1}, Tuple{Point2{T1}, Vec2{T2}}} where {T1, T2}","page":"Primitives","text":"Line2D(p::Point2, n::Vec2) -> Line2D\n\nConstruct a 2D line from a point on the line and a normal vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Line2D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D","page":"Primitives","text":"3D plane (hyperplane in 3D): ax + by + cz + d = 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D-Tuple{Point3, Point3, Point3}","page":"Primitives","text":"Plane3D(p1::Point3, p2::Point3, p3::Point3) -> Plane3D\n\nConstruct a 3D plane from three non-collinear points. The normal direction follows the right-hand rule for p1→p2→p3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D-Union{Tuple{T2}, Tuple{T1}, Tuple{Point3{T1}, Vec3{T2}}} where {T1, T2}","page":"Primitives","text":"Plane3D(point::Point3, normal::Vec3) -> Plane3D\n\nConstruct a 3D plane from a point on the plane and a normal vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D-Union{Tuple{}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Plane3D{T}() -> Plane3D{T}\n\nConstruct the canonical z=0 plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Plane3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.clip_to_rect-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Line2D{T}, HyperRectangle{2}}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"clip_to_rect(l::Line2D, rect::Rect2) -> GeometryBasics.Line{2,T} or nothing\n\nClip the infinite 2D line l to the axis-aligned rectangle rect, returning the line segment (as GeometryBasics.Line) of the portion inside. Returns nothing if the line misses the rectangle.\n\nLiang-Barsky clipping on the parametric line p(t) = p₀ + t·(−b, a), where p₀ = (−ac, −bc) is the closest point to the origin (uses ‖(a,b)‖=1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.clip_to_rect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.coeffs-Tuple{Hyperplane}","page":"Primitives","text":"The normalized coefficients (n..., d) of a Hyperplane: n·x + d = 0, |n| = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.coeffs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.intercept-Tuple{Line2D}","page":"Primitives","text":"Y-intercept of line. Returns Inf for vertical lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.intercept"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.ray_plane_intersection-Tuple{Plane3D, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Primitives","text":"ray_plane_intersection(plane, origin, dir_x, dir_y, dir_z)\n    -> (x, y, z, t)\nray_plane_intersection(plane, o_x, o_y, o_z, dir_x, dir_y, dir_z)\n    -> (x, y, z, t)\n\nBroadcasted ray–plane intersection over structure-of-arrays ray bundles: the world-space hit points and ray parameters of the rays origin + t·dir against plane (convention n·x + d = 0). The first form shares one origin::StaticVector{3} across the bundle; the second takes per-ray origin component arrays (finite-aperture cameras, multi-bounce tracers). Rays parallel to the plane divide by zero and produce ±Inf/NaN components — callers mask by validity, the kernel never throws.\n\nThe plane is a CONSTANT of the computation: its scalars are extracted once before the broadcast and the extraction is @non_differentiable under the ChainRulesCore extension; gradients flow through origins and directions only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.ray_plane_intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.side-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{Hyperplane{M, N}, Union{Point{M}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{M}}}}} where {M, N}","page":"Primitives","text":"side(h::Hyperplane, p) -> Int\n\nReturn which side of the hyperplane p is on:\n\n+1 if on positive side (same direction as normal)\n-1 if on negative side\n0 if on the hyperplane (within tolerance)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.side"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.slope-Tuple{Line2D}","page":"Primitives","text":"Slope of line (dy/dx). Returns Inf for vertical lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.slope"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Quad","page":"Primitives","text":"Quad(origin, frame, rect::Rect2, A = I, b = 0)\nQuad{T}(q::Quad)\n\nA bounded plane. frame is a right-handed orthonormal 3 × 3 matrix (a rotation) whose columns are the in-plane basis e_u, e_v and the normal n = e_u × e_v; a point with metric plane coordinates m is origin + m₁ e_u + m₂ e_v. The CHART is c with m = A c + b (A an invertible 2 × 2 matrix, inverted once here), and rect is a rectangle in the chart. A left-handed or non-orthonormal frame (to 64 eps of the frame's element type: 1.4e-14 in Float64, 7.6e-6 in Float32), a singular A or a negative width is an ArgumentError. Quad{T}(q) converts the element type (Float32 for a device).\n\nisbits. intersection is ray_plane_intersection's arithmetic, bit for bit, NaN when t < 0 or the hit's chart is outside rect; chart(q, x) = A⁻¹ (m − b); surface_point its inverse; bounds is rect. normal is the GEOMETRIC normal n on either side: a consumer that wants the normal facing the camera flips it against the ray (shading, not geometry). A rigid or affine-calibrated target's surface is a Quad.\n\nUnder ChainRules (reverse mode) the plane is a constant of ray_plane_intersection, so a Quad's intersection carries no gradient to its pose; ForwardDiff does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Quad"},{"category":"function","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bounds","page":"Primitives","text":"bounds(s) -> Rect2\n\nThe rectangle of chart coordinates the surface s covers (chart).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bounds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.chart","page":"Primitives","text":"chart(s, x) -> SVector{2}\n\nThe chart coordinates (u, v) of the point x of the surface s — the DESIGN chart (for surface(pt), the coordinates of the target's nominal points). For a Quad the projection of x − o on its in-plane basis, then the inverse of its in-plane affine (the print's calibration; the component along the normal is ignored); for a MappedSurface the chart point whose image is x. x is in the surface's frame and units, as for intersection; surface_point is the inverse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.chart"},{"category":"function","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.intersection","page":"Primitives","text":"intersection(s, o, d) -> (t, x)\n\nWhere the ray o + t d, t ≥ 0 (origin o, direction d, both 3-vectors; d need not be unit) meets the surface s: the ray parameter t and the point x. The ray and x are in the surface's frame — for surface(pt) the pose's destination frame F — and its units (metres when the target is physical). A ray that misses — parallel to a plane, hitting behind its origin (t < 0), hitting outside bounds(s), or (a MappedSurface) not converging — returns t and every coordinate of x as NaN, never an error. Methods: Quad (closed form, the arithmetic of ray_plane_intersection) and MappedSurface (a 2 × 2 root in its chart). Allocation-free and generic in the element type, so it runs on the GPU path and under ForwardDiff (a MappedSurface's root by the implicit function theorem).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.intersection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.surface","page":"Primitives","text":"surface(pt::PosedTarget{<:PlanarTarget}) -> Quad | MappedSurface\n\nThe surface a ray meets on the posed planar target pt, in the pose's destination frame, its CHART the design chart (so chart of a hit is the nominal coordinates it shows). Its rectangle, in that chart, is Rect(board(t)) when the target has a Board, else the design bounding box — either grown by 16 eps of its coordinates, the margin within which PlanarTarget accepts a point on its board, so a ray at a point on the edge is a hit (up to the chart's own rounding).\n\nA planar affine calibration (Rigid, the factors EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew or any of them) gives a Quad: the target's TargetFrame placed by pose(pt) (origin its translation, e_u, e_v and the normal the rotation's columns), the calibration its affine (placed: A p + b, b = s β − (A − I) o).\nAny other — a HeightField, an in-plane ThinPlateSpline, either after the affine factors (HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew) — gives the MappedSurface of the composed chart map pose ∘ S₃ ∘ lift ∘ C ∘ S₂⁻¹ (the pose, the placement S₃ and its in-plane part S₂, the calibration C, the lift (c, 0) of a planar one), built by MappedSurface(pose, calibration, placement, rect). Heights are along TargetFrame +z.\n\nA physical target (metres) gives a primitive in metres, plain numbers. An uncertain pose or calibration gives its value. A PointTarget has no surface (a MethodError). The kind is chosen by the calibration's type, so a rigid target's ray caster never compiles the root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.surface"},{"category":"function","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.surface_point","page":"Primitives","text":"surface_point(s, uv) -> SVector{3}\n\nThe point of the surface s at chart coordinates uv (the inverse of chart): a Quad's in-plane affine applied and placed in its frame, a MappedSurface's chart map. In the surface's frame and units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.surface_point"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.MappedSurface","page":"Primitives","text":"MappedSurface(map, frame::Quad)\n\nA surface given by its CHART MAP: the points map(p) (3-vectors in the surface's frame) of the chart points p in the rectangle bounds(s) = bounds(frame). map is any isbits CoordinateTransformations Transformation of a 2-vector to a 3-vector with a transform_deriv (its point Jacobian, 3 × 2) — for a posed target's surface, the composition pose ∘ S₃ ∘ lift ∘ C ∘ S₂⁻¹ of its pose, its placement, its calibration and the placement's inverse on the design chart, so the chart is the DESIGN chart. frame is the plane the map deforms (the posed target's z = 0 plane, its rectangle the chart's bounds): it seeds the solves and orients the normal.\n\nintersection solves for the chart point of the hit, p, such that map(p) lies on the ray: the two components of map(p) − o across the ray (u₁, u₂, an orthonormal pair perpendicular to d) vanish — a 2 × 2 least-squares root through transform_deriv, seeded at the chart coordinates of the frame's plane hit and bounded to the chart's rectangle — and the hit is x = map(p), t = (x − o)·d / (d·d). A ray misses (NaN) when the plane hit does not exist, the solve does not converge within the rectangle, or t < 0. chart(s, x) is the same root for the residual map(p) − x, surface_point(s, p) = map(p), and normal the unit ∂map/∂p₁ × ∂map/∂p₂ at the point's chart point (geometric: on the side of the frame's normal for a chart map of positive orientation).\n\nGeneric in the element type (Float32, Float64, ForwardDiff Duals, nested ones included) and allocation-free: under ForwardDiff the root is solved on the values and differentiated by the implicit function theorem, one layer at a time — never by differentiating the iteration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.MappedSurface"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.MappedSurface-Tuple{EuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}, Union{TargetCalibration, TargetCalibration}, Union{Nothing, SimilarityMap{3, T, S, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, S<:Real, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}, HyperRectangle{2}}","page":"Primitives","text":"MappedSurface(pose, calibration, placement, rect::Rect2) -> MappedSurface\n\nThe deformed surface of a posed planar target from its parts — THE builder: surface(pt) builds it from the target's stored parts. The chart map is the composition\n\npose ∘ S₃ ∘ lift ∘ C ∘ S₂⁻¹        (design chart → the pose's frame)\n\nof the pose (an EuclideanMap{3}, plain numbers), the placement S₃ (a SimilarityMap{3} or nothing, see placement) and its in-plane part's inverse S₂⁻¹, the calibration C (any kind or composition, plain numbers) and, for a planar calibration, the lift (c, 0). rect is the design chart's rectangle. Each member is a CoordinateTransformations map with its transform_deriv; the composition's derivative is CT's chain rule.\n\nThis form checks its parts (the calibration's composition, the placement, the pose's frame), on the host. A device caller — a kernel that rebuilds a HeightField or ThinPlateSpline from new nodal values by with_parameters on its adapted solver — calls the unchecked inner builder VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras._mapped_surface(pose, calibration, placement, rect) with parts it has checked once on the host; it keeps the parts' element type (an unplaced target gets the identity placement in that type).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.MappedSurface"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_parameters-Tuple{MappedSurface, AbstractVector}","page":"Primitives","text":"with_parameters(s::MappedSurface, θ::AbstractVector) -> MappedSurface\n\nThe surface s with its calibration rebuilt from the parameters θ: the chart map's calibration member C (a HeightField, a ThinPlateSpline, or a composition with the affine factors) replaced by with_parameters(C, θ) — θ in C's parameter_names order, in the numbers the surface was built in (plain metres for a physical target; a thin-plate kind projects them onto its pure-bending subspace) — and every other member (the pose, the placement, the lift) and the frame kept as they are. A surface on the device stays on the device (the kind's with_parameters on its adapted solver), and θ may be of any element type (Float32, ForwardDiff Duals): a warped board's surface at new nodal heights inside a kernel, with no rebuild from the target's parts (the checked MappedSurface(pose, calibration, placement, rect)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_parameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#GeometryBasics.Circle-2","page":"Primitives","text":"GeometryBasics.Circle(det::AbstractDetection, cutoff::Real=1.0) -> Circle\n\nConstruct a unitless GeometryBasics.Circle from a detection with radius cutoff * σ (σ the frame's characteristic_scale), through the checked unit strip. The geometry is the detection's feature_frame, so a detection of an Uncertain frame gives the circle of its frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometryBasics.Circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#GeometryBasics.Circle-Tuple{HomCircleMat}","page":"Primitives","text":"Circle(Q::Union{HomCircleMat, HomEllipseMat, Conic}) -> Circle\n\nThe circle of a conic matrix. A HomCircleMat is one by its class; an ellipse's or a general conic's matrix is checked by THE circle predicate, the HomCircleMat class check (isotropy within √eps · ‖A‖, relative, so a large circle is read as a small one is), with its ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometryBasics.Circle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-2","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse{T}\n\nSemantic ellipse representation with center, semi-major axis a, semi-minor axis b, and orientation angle θ in radians.\n\nThe constructor automatically ensures a ≥ b by swapping axes and adjusting the angle if needed.\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point2{T}: Center coordinates (cx, cy)\na::T: Semi-major axis length (always ≥ b)\nb::T: Semi-minor axis length (always ≤ a)\nθ::T: Orientation angle in radians [0, π)\n\nConstructors\n\n# Basic constructor with Point2 center\nellipse = Ellipse(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0, 2.0, π/4)\n\n# Constructor with vector center (automatically converted to Point2)\nellipse = Ellipse([1.0, 2.0], 3.0, 2.0, π/4)\n\n# Mixed numeric types are automatically promoted\nellipse = Ellipse([1, 2], 3.0f0, 2, π/4)\n\nIntegration with GeometryBasics\n\n# Generate boundary points using GeometryBasics.coordinates\npoints = GeometryBasics.coordinates(ellipse, 64)\n\n# Access geometric properties\ncenter = GeometryBasics.coordinates(ellipse)  # Returns ellipse.center\nmajor_radius = GeometryBasics.radius(ellipse)  # Returns max(a, b)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Tuple{Any, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse(center, A::AbstractMatrix)\n\nRefused: a plain 2×2 matrix is ambiguous — it could be a shape matrix M ((x-c)ᵀ M⁻¹ (x-c) = 1, the 1σ contour of a covariance M) or a shape factor A with M = A·A', and for symmetric input the two interpretations silently differ by a squaring. Say which one you mean:\n\nshape matrix: Ellipse(center, Symmetric(M))\nshape factor (e.g. Baumberg adaptation): Ellipse(center, Symmetric(A * A'))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Tuple{Any, LinearAlgebra.Symmetric{var\"#s549\", S} where {var\"#s549\"<:Real, S<:(AbstractMatrix{<:var\"#s549\"})}}","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse(center, Σ::Symmetric) -> Ellipse\n\nConstruct an ellipse from its 2×2 shape matrix (symmetric PD; a covariance's 1σ contour). The boundary is {x : (x-c)ᵀ Σ⁻¹ (x-c) = 1}.\n\nSemi-axes are √λᵢ where λᵢ are eigenvalues of Σ.\n\nΣ = Symmetric([4.0 1.0; 1.0 9.0])\ne = Ellipse(Point2(100.0, 200.0), Σ)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Tuple{Uncertain{<:AbstractVector, <:Union{CovMat{2, T, M, C} where {T, M<:Union{LinearAlgebra.Diagonal{T, StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, T}}, ScaledIdentity{2, T}, StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{2, 2, T}}, C<:Frame{2}}, PrecMat{2, T, M, C} where {T, M<:Union{LinearAlgebra.Diagonal{T, StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, T}}, ScaledIdentity{2, T}, StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{2, 2, T}}, C<:Frame{2}}}}}","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse(d::Uncertain{<:AbstractVector, <:Union{CovMat{2}, PrecMat{2}}}; sigma=3) -> Ellipse\n\nThe sigma-σ confidence ellipse of an uncertain 2D point: Ellipse(center, Σ::Symmetric) at its covariance cov(d) (a stored precision is inverted once), semi-axes scaled by sigma.\n\nΣ = CovMat{2,Float64}(4.0, 1.0, 1.0, 9.0)\ne = Ellipse(Uncertain(Point2(123.4, 56.7), Σ); sigma=3)\nΛ = PrecMat{2,Float32}(2.0f0, 0.1f0, 0.1f0, 3.0f0)\ne = Ellipse(Uncertain(Point2(123.4, 56.7), Λ); sigma=3)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Union{Tuple{Circle{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse(c::Circle) -> Ellipse\n\nConvert a Circle to an Ellipse with equal semi-axes.\n\nExample\n\ncircle = Circle(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 5.0)\nellipse = Ellipse(circle)  # Ellipse with a=b=5.0, θ=0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Union{Tuple{Union{HomEllipseMat{T}, HomCircleMat{T}}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse(Q::Union{HomEllipseMat{T}, HomCircleMat{T}}) -> Ellipse{T}\n\nExtract Ellipse from conic matrix Q using robust eigenvalue decomposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.gradient-Tuple{HomEllipseMat, AbstractVector}","page":"Primitives","text":"gradient(Q::HomEllipseMat{T}, p::Point2{T}) -> Vec2{T}\n\nCompute the gradient of the conic implicit function at point p.\n\nFor conic equation x^T Q x = 0, the gradient is ∇f(x) = 2Qx (homogeneous form). Returns the 2D Euclidean gradient [∂f/∂x, ∂f/∂y] as a Vec2 — it's a direction (steepest-ascent), not a position: the point-on-conic constraint's observation gradient (constraint_with_jacobians(::Conic, p)'s G_x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.gradient"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_ellipse-Tuple{Any}","page":"Primitives","text":"is_ellipse(Q) -> Bool\n\nWhether Q — a conic matrix, an Ellipse or a Circle — is a real, non-degenerate ellipse: classify_conic answering EllipseTrait or CircleTrait, so the two can never disagree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.is_ellipse"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D","page":"Primitives","text":"Circle3D{T}\n\nA planar circle embedded in 3D space.\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point3{T}: Center point of the circle\nradius::T: Radius of the circle\nu::Vec3{T}: First basis vector in circle's plane (unit length)\nv::Vec3{T}: Second basis vector in circle's plane (unit length, perpendicular to u)\n\nProperties\n\nThe circle boundary is parameterized as:\n\np(θ) = center + radius * (cos(θ) * u + sin(θ) * v)\n\nThe normal is cross(u, v).\n\nExample\n\n# Circle centered at origin, in xy-plane, radius 3\nc3d = Circle3D(Point3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), 3.0)\n\n# Transform with rigid motion\nE = EuclideanMap(R, t)\nc3d_transformed = E(c3d)\n\n# Sample boundary point\nθ = π/4\np = centroid(c3d) + c3d.radius * (cos(θ) * c3d.u + sin(θ) * c3d.v)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D-Union{Tuple{Circle{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Circle3D(c::Circle) -> Circle3D\n\nEmbed 2D circle into 3D in the xy-plane at z=0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D-Union{Tuple{T2}, Tuple{T1}, Tuple{Point3{T1}, T2}} where {T1, T2}","page":"Primitives","text":"Circle3D(center::Point3, radius) -> Circle3D\n\nConstruct a circle in the xy-plane (normal = +z).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D-Union{Tuple{T3}, Tuple{T2}, Tuple{T1}, Tuple{Point3{T1}, T2, Vec3{T3}}} where {T1, T2, T3}","page":"Primitives","text":"Circle3D(center::Point3, radius, normal::Vec3) -> Circle3D\n\nConstruct a circle from center, radius, and normal vector.\n\nAutomatically generates orthonormal basis vectors u, v in the circle's plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Circle{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Circle3D(c::Circle, E::EuclideanMap{3}) -> Circle3D\n\nEmbed a 2D circle into 3D using a Euclidean frame.\n\nThe EuclideanMap E defines how the local xy-plane maps to world coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Circle3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D-Union{Tuple{Circle3D{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse3D(c::Circle3D) -> Ellipse3D\n\nConvert a Circle3D to an Ellipse3D (axisa = radius*u, axisb = radius*v).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.AbstractConic3D","page":"Primitives","text":"AbstractConic3D{T}\n\nAbstract supertype for planar conic sections embedded in 3D space.\n\nSubtypes must have a center::Point3{T} field and implement:\n\n_axis_a(c)::SVector{3,T} — first axis vector (direction × length)\n_axis_b(c)::SVector{3,T} — second axis vector (perpendicular to first)\n\nThe boundary is parameterized as: p(θ) = center + cos(θ)*axis_a + sin(θ)*axis_b\n\nSee also: Ellipse3D, Circle3D\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.AbstractConic3D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse3D{T}\n\nA planar ellipse embedded in 3D space, represented by center and axis vectors.\n\nFields\n\ncenter::Point3{T}: Center point of the ellipse (3 DOF)\naxis_a::Vec3{T}: Semi-major axis vector (3 DOF)\naxis_b::Vec3{T}: Semi-minor axis vector, perpendicular to axis_a (2 DOF)\n\nProperties\n\nThe ellipse boundary is parameterized as:\n\np(θ) = center + cos(θ) * axis_a + sin(θ) * axis_b\n\nExample\n\n# Ellipse centered at origin, in xy-plane, semi-axes 3 and 2\ne3d = Ellipse3D(\n    Point3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n    Vec3(3.0, 0.0, 0.0),  # semi-major along x\n    Vec3(0.0, 2.0, 0.0)   # semi-minor along y\n)\n\n# Transform with rigid motion\nE = EuclideanMap(R, t)\ne3d_transformed = E(e3d)\n\n# Sample boundary point\nθ = π/4\np = centroid(e3d) + cos(θ) * e3d.axis_a + sin(θ) * e3d.axis_b\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D-Tuple{Ellipse3D}","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse3D(e::Ellipse) -> Ellipse3D\nEllipse3D(c::Circle) -> Ellipse3D\n\nEmbed 2D shape into 3D in the xy-plane at z=0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Circle{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse3D(c::Circle, E::EuclideanMap{3}) -> Ellipse3D\n\nEmbed a 2D circle into 3D using a Euclidean frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Ellipse{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}} where T","page":"Primitives","text":"Ellipse3D(e::Ellipse, E::EuclideanMap{3}) -> Ellipse3D\n\nEmbed a 2D ellipse into 3D using a Euclidean frame.\n\nThe EuclideanMap E defines how the local xy-plane (containing the 2D ellipse) maps to world coordinates:\n\nE.R rotates the xy-plane to its 3D orientation\nE.t translates the local origin to its 3D position\n\nExample\n\n# Place ellipse in xy-plane at z=5\nE = EuclideanMap(I, SVector(0, 0, 5))\ne3d = Ellipse3D(ellipse_2d, E)\n\n# Place ellipse in a tilted plane\nR = RotX(π/4)  # tilt 45° around x-axis\nE = EuclideanMap(R, SVector(0, 0, 0))\ne3d = Ellipse3D(ellipse_2d, E)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse3D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc-Tuple{Distributions.AbstractMvNormal}","page":"Primitives","text":"Disc(d::AbstractMvNormal) -> Disc\n\nConvert an MvNormal to a Disc using the equivalent isotropic scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame-Tuple{Distributions.AbstractMvNormal}","page":"Primitives","text":"EllipticFrame(d::AbstractMvNormal) -> EllipticFrame\n\nConvert an MvNormal to an EllipticFrame for geometric operations.\n\nThe frame is the Gaussian's 1σ contour: its shape matrix is the covariance, shape_matrix(frame) = Σ, and its shape factor S = √Σ (symmetric, by eigendecomposition).\n\nExample\n\nd = MvNormal(SVector(100.0, 200.0), SMatrix{2,2}(25.0, 5.0, 5.0, 16.0))\nframe = EllipticFrame(d)\nellipse = region(frame, 3.0)  # 3σ contour\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bbox-Tuple{Any}","page":"Primitives","text":"bbox(shape; scale=1.0, snap_to_grid=false) -> SpatialIndexing.Rect\n\nGeneric shape bounding box (Circle, Ellipse, …) via the shape's Rect2 extent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bbox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bbox-Tuple{FeatureFrame}","page":"Primitives","text":"bbox(f::FeatureFrame; scale=1.0, snap_to_grid=false) -> SpatialIndexing.Rect\n\nBounding box of a feature frame at its scale-σ contour, optionally snapped outward to the pixel grid, through the coordinate compute boundary (a frame in quantities is stripped: pixels in px, lengths in mm). Every frame, on its DOF trait: a PointFrame's is the point (no extent at any scale), an isotropic frame's center ∓ scale·σ (allocation-free), a shape-factor frame's its region(f, scale) ellipse's exact box.\n\nExample\n\nd = Disc(100.5, 200.5, 5.0)\nrect = bbox(d; scale=3.0, snap_to_grid=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bbox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.best_iou_match-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Primitives","text":"best_iou_match(query, candidates) -> (idx, iou_value)\n\nThe candidate with the highest iou against query, as (index, iou); (0, 0.0) when candidates is empty or no pair overlaps. Works for any pair iou accepts (ellipses, circles, mixed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.best_iou_match"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.iou_below-Tuple{Ellipse, Ellipse, Real}","page":"Primitives","text":"iou_below(e1::Ellipse, e2::Ellipse, t) -> Bool\n\ntrue only when iou(e1, e2) < t is PROVEN by a bound that needs no exact intersection, so a pair whose IoU could reach t is never declared below it (false means \"not proven\", not \"at least t\"). Three upper bounds on IoU = |A∩B| / |A∪B|, each from |A∪B| ≥ max(|A|, |B|): the area ratio (|A∩B| ≤ min(|A|, |B|)); disjoint circumscribed discs (each ellipse lies in the disc of its semi-major axis about its centre); and those discs' lens (|A∩B| ≤ lens, the two Circles' overlap_area). Each is compared with a relative slack of 1e-6, above the exact IoU's rounding. A pre-filter before iou over many pairs (BlobBoards' guided search).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.iou_below"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.homogeneous_w-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Primitives","text":"homogeneous_w(H::StaticMatrix{3, 3}, p::StaticVector{2}) -> Real\n\nThe homogeneous coordinate H * [p; 1] produces — the chirality reference for to_euclidean's guarded form and maps_within. Evaluate it once at a point known to be on the visible side (e.g. the plane's own center) and supply it as w_ref.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.homogeneous_w"},{"category":"method","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.maps_within-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2} where T, HyperRectangle{2}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, Real}","page":"Primitives","text":"maps_within(H::StaticMatrix{3, 3}, rect::Rect, p::StaticVector{2}, w_ref::Real) -> Bool\n\nDoes p map through the homography H inside rect (closed bounds), on the same side of the plane's horizon as w_ref? The one predicate behind \"does this image point back-project onto this board/plane region\": points beyond the horizon flip the homogeneous sign and would wrap into the rectangle from behind the camera — w_ref (see homogeneous_w) keeps them out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.maps_within"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.RegionIntersection","page":"Primitives","text":"RegionIntersection{A, B}\n\nThe intersection a ∩ b of two 2-D regions, held lazily: a point is in it when it is in both (p ∈ s ⟺ p ∈ s.a && p ∈ s.b), and its bounding box is the intersection of the two bounding boxes, Rect2(s) == Rect2(s.a) ∩ Rect2(s.b) (so bbox works on it). Built by Base.intersect (∩) of a Rect, an Ellipse or another intersection, in any order and nesting (a Circle enters as its Ellipse):\n\ndomain = Rect(0, 0, 1920, 1200) ∩ Ellipse(Point2(960.0, 600.0), 700.0, 700.0, 0.0)\nPoint2(960.0, 100.0) ∈ domain    # true: inside both\nPoint2(10.0, 10.0) ∈ domain      # false: in the rect, outside the ellipse\nRect2(domain)                    # the rect clipped to the ellipse's box\n\nThe identity of ∩ is WholePlane: r ∩ WholePlane() === r (what valid_domain answers for a lens with no limit of its own).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.RegionIntersection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/primitives/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.WholePlane","page":"Primitives","text":"WholePlane()\n\nThe whole plane as a 2-D region: the identity of ∩. Every point is in it (p ∈ WholePlane()), every rectangle lies inside it (r ∈ WholePlane()), and a ∩ WholePlane() === a for any region a (in either order). It has no bounding box: Rect2(WholePlane()) is an ArgumentError. It is what valid_domain answers for a lens with no limit of its own, so Rect(extent) ∩ valid_domain(camera) and Rect(extent) ∈ valid_domain(camera) read the same for every lens, and what imaged_region is given when no image circle is known.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.WholePlane"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Conventions","page":"Conventions","text":"This is the stack's canonical convention text, kept once, in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's conventions/ folder. VisualGeometryToolkitCore, VisualGeometryToolkitIO, VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitEval, VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation, RANSACScoringDoneRight, LocalOptimizationDoneRight, PoseLib.jl and BlobBoards all follow it; each package's CLAUDE.md points here instead of carrying a copy, and adds only how a rule lands in that package.\n\nEvery rule below exists because breaking it produced a defect that a green test suite did not catch. Where that history matters it is stated, because a convention whose reason is lost gets \"simplified\" away by the next cleanup.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Naming:-the-verb-states-the-kind","page":"Conventions","text":"An estimator's name states its KIND — how it computes, and so how it can fail — and the object names WHAT IS ESTIMATED. Which family a function belongs to is decided by its input.\n\nCorrespondences or a model in → decide in this order:\n\nIt needs a starting guess → refine_*.\nIt samples hypotheses and verifies them (RANSAC) → find_*.\nIt is one deterministic algebraic step — a minimal sample, or least squares with FIXED weights → an ALGORITHM NAME, no verb (homography_dlt, kabsch, zhang_intrinsics, tls_line).\nOtherwise (iterative, reweighted, repeated, staged) → fit_*.\n\nKind Input Needs a guess Decides inliers Returns Must not Lives in\nminimal solver a minimal sample: solve(solver, cs, idx) no no every solution (FixedModels) iterate, weigh, reject VGC (Homography4pt, P3P, RadialCamera5pt)\none-step solver (algorithm name) an overdetermined set taken as inliers, optionally with FIXED weights no no the model, plus any statistic a consumer reads iterate, reweight, reject VGC (homography_dlt, fundamental_dlt, kabsch, chordal_mean, zhang_intrinsics, tls_line)\nfit an overdetermined set taken as inliers no — it seeds itself soft weights at most (an IRLS) one model with residual statistics sample hypotheses RVG (fit_division_camera, fit_radial_camera, fit_camera, fit_line)\nfind contaminated data no yes: hypothesize, verify, locally optimize model + inlier set require a guess RVG (find_homography, find_absolute_pose, find_radial_camera)\nrefine a model and its observations yes, required no; continuous reweighting at most the polished model, same family re-estimate from scratch; change the family RVG (refine_robot_world_hand_eye); PoseLib.jl's refine_* bindings; BlobBoards' board bundle\n\nThe kind fixes how a call can fail: an algorithm is one deterministic computation and can only be degenerate; a fit can fail to converge; a find is randomized; a refine can end in a local minimum. That is the point of naming by kind rather than by what comes back — the caller's error handling follows the verb.\n\nThe name follows the DESIGN, not a knob: fit_radial_camera(iterations = 1) is still a fit. find is RESERVED for the sample-and-verify search. solve is ONLY the minimal-solver protocol — VGC's solve(solver, cs, idx) and RVG's solve(problem, idx), both returning every root — never an estimator's name. The RANSAC local-optimization step is a refine: refine!(ws, problem, θ, weights) starts from the current model θ (a linear problem runs its one-step algorithm under the weights and ignores θ). The noise-model estimators (VGC's fit_scale, VisualGeometryToolkitEval's fit_prior) are outside this rule.\n\nDetection, correspondence, observation — the terminology taxonomy (owner, 2026-11-13), enforced by check_naming (R14).\n\nA detection is a detector's output in one image, unassociated: Attributed{Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}}, a PointFrame as first-class as any scaled frame (feature-frames). A keypoint IS a detection; with a descriptor it is VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' DescribedFeature.\nA correspondence is a hypothesised pairing (Correspondence, Correspondences, correspondences). A match comes from descriptors; an inlier is a verified correspondence.\nAn observation is a detection associated with a model entity (a target point, a landmark), in the form an estimator consumes: TargetObservations, VisualGeometryToolkitIO's observations kind (its entity target_point or landmark), and a detection's point observation center(u). A track is the observations of one entity.\nmeasured is a provenance adjective and value only (SensorSource.measured, \"measured\": the source of a sensor-model component such as a ChannelBlack, a covariance's), never a bound name. No identifier — function, type, field, keyword, local, constant, quoted symbol, TOML key — holds the word \"measurement\", the old pairing abbreviation, \"sighting\" or \"keypoint\" (R14); each old public name is in RETIRED_NAMES with its replacement (R9). A published artifact's name that spells an old word is data: it is listed in FOREIGN_FORMAT_TOKENS and accepted only in string literals, quoted TOML values and data fixtures.\n\nIterative-estimator vocabulary — no exceptions, enforced by a quality test in every stack package.\n\nAn iteration is one cycle of block_coordinate_descent! through every block. Its counters are iter, max_iter and iterations; its hook is post_iteration!.\nA block is one component of the iteration state the loop overwrites: the model, the residuals, the nuisance and each of its parts (scale, inlier prior, ν, α), and the weights.\nupdate_<block>! overwrites that one block in place and nothing else. Every name that begins update_ or _update_ ends in ! and writes exactly one block; a composite block's update (update_nuisance!) only calls its parts' updates. A function that writes two blocks is split.\nnext_<block> (or _next_<block>) is pure: it returns a block's new value and mutates nothing.\n<fit>_step (<fit>_step! if it mutates) is the public model-block update of the fit fit_<fit>, with weights and nuisance held fixed; that fit's update_model! calls it. fit_<fit> must exist. No other identifier contains the word step, except Manopt.jl's step_solver! (one cycle of block_coordinate_descent!).\n<method>_increment is a local model's Δ (_newton_increment, _lm_increment).\nA fixed number of reweight-and-solve cycles is iterations; a repetition of a whole estimator after changing its inputs is an outer_iteration (outer_iterations for the count). A random hypothesis draw — a RANSAC or PROSAC sample-and-verify cycle, a sampler draw, a random LO inner sample — is a trial (trial, trials, max_trials, min_trials, n_trials; its cap is the trial budget), never an iteration, and a deterministic cycle is never a trial; the one exception is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's FitResult.iterations field and its iterations(result) accessor, which for a RANSAC count its trials. A test, gate or check that holds succeeds (succeeded, n_succeeded), and one that does not fails; a caller supplies an argument. No other word is used for any of these: the banned words and phrases are listed once, in VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_naming, which every stack package's quality test runs.\nThe hooks of block_coordinate_descent! are exactly init_state!, update_model!, update_residuals!, update_nuisance!, update_weights!, post_iteration!, stopping_criterion, stop_reason and block_coordinate_result; its steps carry Manopt.jl's names, initialize_solver!, step_solver! and stop_solver!, and its termination tests are StoppingCriterion values with Manopt's Stop… names.\nThe words are matched as whole _-separated tokens (CamelCase split into words) of every identifier the stack binds: functions, types, fields, keywords, locals and constants, in src, ext, test, experiments, scripts and docs/src. A method added to a function the stack does not own keeps the owner's name (Base's step(r)). Retired names — listed in check_naming — may not appear anywhere, including comments, docstrings and docs, except a CHANGELOG.md, whose job is to record what each release removed (banned words are still banned there).\n\nWidth vocabulary — one name per quantity, enforced by check_naming (R10). A robust loss is a LOSS (AbstractLossShape, DeployedLoss), never a \"kernel\"; \"kernel\" means only a GPU, convolution, minimal-solver, null-space or probability-density kernel. A width is efficiency_constant (the width as a multiple of the estimated scale ŝ — not a quantile), loss_width (a loss shape's scale parameter, the residual scale where it leaves the shared quadratic regime; sq_loss_width is its square, and a scorer's threshold is its loss's loss_width) or outlier_halfwidth (the half-width of the Student-t marginal's uniform outlier component); the noise scale stays scale / s2. A single letter or abbreviation (k, a, c, τ, thr, a *_ref or *_grid short form) is never a keyword, field, type parameter, argument, TOML key, sidecar key or CLI flag for them; a paper's symbol appears once, in the paper project's symbol map. decision_width(m, boundary) is the one conversion to the native width whose inlier/outlier decision flips at a given residual (its inverse, and a scorer's current one, is decision_boundary), and is_map_inlier(posterior_weight) the one MAP rule (posterior > ½). Each scorer takes its own width and only it: a threshold scorer (RANSAC, MSAC, the loss-width families) a threshold, the Student-t + uniform scorer an outlier_halfwidth — it has no threshold, and no name, key, comment or doc calls its half-width one. R12 flags that word close to the scorer's type or its TOML tag (six tokens); the contract's decision point is decision_boundary (a threshold scorer's constant τ; the Student-t scorer's MAP crossover at the current consensus scale, re-estimated with every score).\n\nImages or a session in → a task verb, naming the job: find_boards (per board the answer IS a RANSAC's model and inliers), track_boards, detect_*, extract_*, load_*. A pipeline composes the estimators above and does no estimation of its own; an estimate from scratch buried inside a refine (a P3P inside a polish) is a violation to move, not to name around.\n\nNouns are accessors, constructors and algorithm names only — centroid(frame), blobs(bundle), cutoff(bundle), PatternConfig(...), kabsch(cs) — never pipelines: a pipeline's result's natural variable name is the noun, and detections = detections(img) makes the name local inside a function (an UndefVarError) and rebinds the global at top level. An algorithm name carries no such hazard: the result of kabsch(cs) is named R, not kabsch.\n\nPackage aliases: one short name per stack package. The registered names carry the full VisualGeometryToolkit prefix (General already holds other VisualGeometry* packages). Code that qualifies a stack package's name binds its alias once per module — import VisualGeometryToolkitCore as VgtkCore — and writes VgtkCore.foo, never the long form. The aliases are exactly VgtkCore, VgtkIO, VgtkFeatures, VgtkEval and VgtkEstimation; PoseLib keeps its name. The long name stays where Julia or a reader needs the real one: Project.toml, using/import paths (using VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools), a package's references to itself in its own src/ and docs/make.jl, URLs, and user-facing install snippets. A package's own tests use its alias like any other code. \"Vgtk\" is internal shorthand only, never a registered name.\n\nSee Robust Estimation for which function to reach for.","title":"Naming: the verb states the kind"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Signatures:-the-model-leads,-then-its-data,-then-parameters","page":"Conventions","text":"One rule: the model or shape first, then the data it acts on or is measured against, then parameters.\n\nCall Model or shape Data Parameters\nproject(camera, X) camera X \nsampson_distance(F, u₁, u₂) F u₁, u₂ \nsigned_distance(plane, p), closest_point(plane, p) plane p \nis_in_front(camera, X) camera X \nundistort(model, img, rect; scale) model img rect, scale\nboundary_point(ellipse, θ) ellipse  θ\nfocal_length(sensor, θ) sensor  θ\ncentroid(ellipse) ellipse  \n\nThe first argument names what the function belongs to, in the math's order (P X, H x, mul!(Y, A, X)). Measuring data against a model is the same question whether the model is F or a plane, so it takes the same order.\n\nWhat counts as the model:\n\nA given model leads, in task verbs too. The camera handed to find_absolute_pose(camera, pairs), the guess handed to refine_relpose(pose, K1, K2, x1, x2): estimated or given, it is applied to the data.\nA required tool leads; a policy with a default stays last. A metric, a descriptor network and gallery, the method or scorer being evaluated: pairwise_matrix(metric, d1, d2), find_boards(gallery, network, img), evaluate_sample_budget(methods, pairs, T). But match(d1, d2, strategy = MutualNN()): a defaulted strategy is a parameter. When several model arguments lead, they keep their relative order.\n\nMutation follows Julia's style guide exactly (\"Write functions with argument ordering similar to Julia Base\"; the model-first rule never overrides it):\n\nA function that modifies a Julia object argument ends in ! — a workspace, buffer, state object, or a memo cache it fills (Base's get!(f, d, k)). Not mutation, per the manual and Base: advancing an RNG (a RANSAC sampler's state counts as RNG state), writing to an IO or to files (write, mkpath, rm carry no !), and a caller-supplied scratch buffer given as a keyword whose contents don't matter afterwards (sort(v; scratch)). A positional scratch buffer becomes such a keyword; an output the caller reads back is either returned or the leading argument of a ! function (score_residuals!(sws, q, scoring, residuals, n), refine!(ws, problem, θ, weights)).\nBase's order: (1) a function argument, (2) an IO stream — an RNG takes this slot (Random: \"accept an optional AbstractRNG object as first argument\"), (3) the input(s) being mutated, (4) a type, (5) inputs not mutated, (6) key, (7) value, (8) everything else, (9) varargs, (10) keywords. The model-first rule above orders the inputs within (5).\nSo only a function argument, an IO or an RNG may precede what a ! call mutates; a strategy or method object may not: map!(f, dst, src), rand!(rng, A), draw_sample!(rng, indices, problem), undistort!(dst, model, src), block_coordinate_descent!(state, likelihood, method, prob, θ₀). The in-place and allocating forms agree on the order of the rest.\nA type argument takes slot (4), positionally — precision and element types, frame tags and problem types alike, never a keyword. In a ! call it follows the mutated inputs: undistort!(dst, [T = Float64], model, src), rand!(rng, A, S). In a non-mutating call it leads, after any function or RNG: zeros(Float32, n), rand(rng, Float32, dims), image_warp(Float32, math, rect), evaluate_sample_budget(problem_type, methods, pairs), relabel(Src, Dst, m) (like convert(T, x)). An optional type is an optional leading positional, as in zeros([T = Float64], dims).\nAn RNG leads (the IO slot): rand(rng, …), tune!(rng, method, spec, n).\n\nThree exceptions:\n\nA method added to a function the package does not own keeps the owner's order: p ∈ ellipse is Base's in(item, collection).\nA symmetric relation takes either order: intersects(a, b), iou(a, b).\nVisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's RANSAC family leads with the problem, which carries the data, and puts the hypothesis after it: solve(problem, idx), test_model(problem, model, idx), mahalanobis_distances!(d, problem, model), locally_optimize(problem, θ₀), and wrappers that hold a problem or a per-pair context (refit_imc(lo, ctx, seed)).\n\n(VisualGeometryToolkitCore 0.27.0 replaced seven role-based rules with this one: ACTION vs QUERY put sampson_distance(F, u) and distance(p, plane) in opposite orders, and an image-first rule reversed the lens between the batched point undistortion and undistort!.)\n\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore's src/base/interface_functions.jl repeats the rule beside the shared stubs.","title":"Signatures: the model leads, then its data, then parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Data-arguments:-correspondences-in,-dicts-for-groups","page":"Conventions","text":"An estimator's data is a vector of correspondences, cs::AbstractVector{<:Correspondence} (Correspondence = Pair; correspondences(sources, targets) builds the columnar StructArray), never parallel arrays (x1, x2) or index tuples.\nPer-correspondence data (prior scores, weights, covariances) are attribute COLUMNS (withattrs(cs; weights = w)), never keywords; per-correspondence outputs come back the same way.\nGrouped data is an AbstractDict{K, <:AbstractVector{<:Correspondence}} keyed by the identity of the group's unknown (a marker id, a (frame, board)): fit_robot_world_hand_eye(markers), fit_division_camera(radial_cameras, views). Block order is sort(collect(keys(groups))), stored on the result; results come back keyed by K, and a joint covariance is ordered [shared | per-group in key order].\nGiven per-group models lead, keyed by the same K; the key sets must match (ArgumentError).\nNest only an observation with its evidence: A_i => correspondences(X, u) (a rig pose => that frame's features) in refine_robot_world_hand_eye.\nKeywords are configuration only.\nA value carried between stages carries its per-datum attributes as Attributed(x, attrs) (Attributed(H, (; weight)), Attributed(R_ij, (; weight))): a stage reads the weight with weight_of (1.0 for a bare value) and the value with value_of, never from a parallel weights vector or keyword. Attributed is for the data, not for wrapping results.\nA fit returns a FitResult; its extras go in its metadata. Several fits come back keyed by what was fitted (a Dict). Per-datum outputs are index-aligned in the metadata, and callers attach them to their data with withattrs.\nMinimal-solver kernels stay positional internals (public, not exported); the public entry is solve(solver, cs, idx).","title":"Data arguments: correspondences in, dicts for groups"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Coordinate-frames-and-normalization:-frame-in-frame-out","page":"Conventions","text":"A public function (exported or public) returns results in the frame and units its inputs came in — models, residuals, covariances, inlier sets, scales: pixels in, pixels out. No public signature takes or returns normalized coordinates or a normalization, and no result or metadata carries one.\nNormalization is private conditioning owned by ONE estimator (fit_*, find_*, refine_*, or a one-step algorithm's public form such as homography_dlt(cs)): data mapped into the normalized frame once at entry, models composed back and covariances propagated back once at exit. The strategy is chosen in one place, CorrespondenceNormalization(M, cs; center), by model class.\nThe inner loop never normalizes — minimal kernels, *_step updates, workspace ! forms, RANSAC problem internals and their validity gates. They take normalized data plus the estimator's CorrespondenceNormalization; a frame = nothing keyword that falls back to normalizing is a bug.\nPublic thresholds and tolerances are in the caller's units, converted inside by the normalization's scale (similarity_scale).\nThe one exception is the benchmark pool. Its record, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Pools.PoolPair, stores correspondences and models in NormalizedImageFrame (image centre, half diagonal: corners at unit radius) together with the per-image maps that put them there. VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation builds a record from a pixel-frame problem (PoolPair(rng, problem, raw)) and owns the record's pool-frame RANSAC problems (HomographyProblem(pair), FundMatProblem(pair), EssentialProblem(pair)); VisualGeometryToolkitEval reads and writes pools; RANSACScoringDoneRight's pool scorers score them in that frame. Every API where pool data enters or leaves names that frame (pool_frame_maps, pool_pixel_scale, pixel_correspondences); nothing converts silently.\n\nLayer Examples Normalizes\ntask verb find_boards, track_boards never; forwards pixels\nestimator, one-step public form find_homography, fit_division_camera, homography_dlt(cs) once in, once out\ninner loop homography_dlt!, division_camera_step, refine!(ws, problem, θ, w), test_model never; takes the estimator's\nminimal kernel homography_4pt, essential_5pt, solve(solver, cs, idx) never\npool PoolPair (VGC Pools), VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's pool-frame problems, VisualGeometryToolkitEval's pool IO, RANSACScoringDoneRight's pool scorers stored in NormalizedImageFrame, declared\n\nfunction homography_dlt(cs)                       # pixels in\n    N = CorrespondenceNormalization(HomographyMat, cs)    # the one strategy, private\n    return homography_dlt!(A, N, cs)              # inner loop: uses N, never builds one\nend                                               # H back in pixels\nτ_n = similarity_scale(destination_map(N)) * τ_px # a pixel tolerance, inside\n\nNames: the strategies HartleyNormalization(; center) (centroid, or a known centre; mean distance √2) and DiagonalNormalization(size; center) (the pool frame), applied as AffineMap(strategy, points); the two-sided value CorrespondenceNormalization, whose two sides (source_map, destination_map) move a model by COMPOSITION — D ∘ M ∘ inv(S) into the normalized frame and inv(D) ∘ M ∘ S back, with S = source_map(N), D = destination_map(N), M a map or a bare model matrix (a HomographyMat comes back a HomographyMat, an F/E a FundMat; an EpipolarMap stays one) — never by a dedicated verb (the retired frame-change verbs are listed in check_naming). *Frame names frame TAGS only (ImageFrame, NormalizedImageFrame); a normalization is never called a frame. Retired: hartley_normalize, diag_normalize, rms_normalize, HartleyFrame, coord_normalization, normalization_map.\n\nStrip where you normalize. Physical units leave a value at the same place as its frame. The estimator that owns the normalization strips its physical inputs into its private working unit once at entry, with the whole value's ustrip(u, X), and reattaches the unit once at exit, where it composes the model back. Every layer below it sees plain numbers. A task verb forwards quantities as it forwards pixels, and a minimal kernel never sees a unit. See \"Units\" (units.md) for the layering and its four enforcement points.\n\nCalibrated geometry (E, relative pose, PnP) is on unit bearings, never the z = 1 plane, and takes no normalization: see \"Calibrated geometry is on bearings\" (bearings.md).\n\nEach package states how the rule lands in it in its own CLAUDE.md (\"In this package\", \"Frames here\").","title":"Coordinate frames and normalization: frame in = frame out"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Why-this-rule-is-worth-its-cost","page":"Conventions","text":"A tolerance is a number in a frame, and neither the type system nor a test suite carries the frame with it. One constant in this stack — the coincidence tolerance that rejects duplicate detections — was read in three frames by three consumers, none of which names the helper, so grepping its call sites finds none of them: as metres in P3P's world points (a 10 cm radius), as a spread-normalized unit (≈17 px at full frame), and in a calibrated chart (120 px at f = 1200, rejecting 22% of clean minimal samples).\n\nNone of those showed up as a failure. An over-strict gate makes RANSAC discard more samples, so the suites go quieter, never red. The question that finds this class of bug is: what units is my data in, at every problem I construct?","title":"Why this rule is worth its cost"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#The-frames-of-a-rig,-its-members-and-a-capture","page":"Conventions","text":"A Rig is posed in ONE frame F (a body BodyFrame{:camera_rig}, the world, a tracker's room, or a member's own frame — the gauge). Each member is a (model, pose) pair and names its own frame:\n\na sensor's is ViewFrame{id}; its pose is its extrinsics, F → ViewFrame{id};\na target's is TargetFrame{id}; its pose (PosedTarget) is TargetFrame{id} → F, stored unlabeled and minted on access. The target's geometry — points(target), a PlanarTarget's plane z = 0 (or the 3-vectors a HeightField calibration lifts off it) or a PointTarget's points — is in TargetFrame{id} and independent of the pose, so one target can sit in several rigs.\n\nA capture's per-frame values keep the same frames: a Trajectory maps one body's frame into one frame over time (BodyFrame{:board_rig} → WorldFrame), TargetPoses map TargetFrame{id} → F per frame (F the camera's frame), and TargetObservations{F} are pixels in a declared PixelFrame or points in a declared 3D frame, the dimension the frame's (one container for both since 0.10). An observation names a target point by (target id, point index) — in TargetObservations, per frame and target, index => observation — resolved against the target's definition, the rig member.","title":"The frames of a rig, its members and a capture"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#A-planar-target's-frame:-one-orientation-for-every-design","page":"Conventions","text":"Every planar target's TargetFrame{id} has the same axes: x right and y up as the target is seen from its viewing side, and +z towards the viewer, so the design lies on z = 0 and a camera that sees it has its centre at z > 0 (signed_distance to the plane > 0). The viewing side is the printed side of a print; a target that is seen from behind (backlit, transparent, a mirror) states its viewing side, and every cheirality test reads that side rather than assuming one.\n\nThe ORIGIN is the design's own and is documented with it: an AprilGrid's is tag 0's first corner (Kalibr's frame, point for point), a SchoepsPattern's the pattern's lower-left corner, a BlobBoards board's its centre. An origin is a translation, never a rotation, so the orientation, the side and every cheirality test read the same for every design; where the printed page sits relative to the origin is the target's Canvas.\n\nA tool's own target frame is converted once, at the IO adapter that reads its files, by a fixed rigid map, and nothing inside the stack sees it: Schöps' KnownGeometry (y down: a half turn about x and a shift of one square), and OpenCV's and MATLAB's checkerboard frames (z into the board, the camera at z < 0). Kalibr's AprilGrid frame is this one.\n\nWhy one orientation. A front end that built its own page frame (y down, from texture pixels) and searched both row directions found two labellings of one scene — rows along −y with the camera behind is rows along +y rotated 180° about x — scored them alike, and let rounding choose: Julia 1.12 and 1.13 chose differently (RadiometricCamera, 2026-11-05). With the axes fixed by the convention there is one labelling, and cheirality removes the one remaining wrong pose, the camera behind the target.","title":"A planar target's frame: one orientation for every design"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Pixel-origins","page":"Conventions","text":"Three canonical conventions, no aliases:\n\n:opencv — top-left pixel centre at (0, 0)\n:colmap — top-left pixel centre at (0.5, 0.5)\n:julia — top-left pixel centre at (1, 1)\n\n:vlfeat, :makie and :matlab were removed as redundant aliases; do not reintroduce them.\n\nGeometric operations default to COLMAP: Rect(sensor) has its origin at (0, 0) with widths equal to the resolution, and the image-display functions (imshow, CampixelImshow) and the other geometric APIs follow. Conversion between conventions is an EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{A}, PixelFrame{B}) — see Coordinate Frames. Persisted files declare their convention; a reader returns typed data rather than a bare array, so the frame travels with the numbers instead of living in a comment.","title":"Pixel origins"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Calibrated-geometry-is-on-bearings","page":"Conventions","text":"Calibrated two-view and pose geometry — the essential matrix, relative pose, P3P/PnP, their RANSAC problems, refiners, scoring and benchmark pools — works on unit bearings (unit rays on S², typed StaticVector{3}), never on the affine z = 1 plane (GnomonicFrame, the chart).\n\nThe reason is not tidiness: a ray at or beyond 90° has no coordinate on that plane, so any K⁻¹u dehomogenized to a 2-vector, any silent [x, y, 1] lift and any scoring through F = K⁻ᵀEK⁻¹ excludes the fisheye and omnidirectional cameras this stack calibrates. Calibrated solvers and problems are typed Correspondence{<:StaticVector{3},<:StaticVector{3}} so a 2-vector is a MethodError rather than a silent narrowing.\n\nThe type is a necessary condition, not a guarantee. A three-vector still on the z = 1 plane, (x, y, 1), satisfies the signature, and because the epipolar algebra is scale-invariant per endpoint it even returns the right answer for every ray the chart can represent. What it cannot represent is a ray at or past 90°, so the fisheye exclusion survives the type and merely stops being visible in it. The guarantee comes from where bearings are built — the camera's back-projection, defined for every ray the camera can see — and the essential problem checks unit norm once, at construction. A unit-norm check inside the solver was considered and deliberately not taken: it would be a per-sample tolerance in a hot loop, checking data the caller built. At the boundary it is a structural discriminator rather than a tuned constant, since a z = 1 point has norm ≥ 1 (≈ 1.41 at 45° off axis) and coincides with a unit bearing only on the optical axis, where the two are the same vector.\n\nConsequences worth stating:\n\nBearings come from the camera's own back-projection, backproject_with_jacobian(camera, u) -> (f, M), whose Jacobian is tangent to the sphere (fᵀM = 0).\nThe residual is a tangent-plane (spherical) Sampson distance whitened by the pixel covariance propagated through that Jacobian, which keeps thresholds in pixel σ where the caller set them.\nCalibrated data takes no normalization: only rotations preserve the essential structure, and unit bearings need no conditioning.\nThe tangency assertion fᵀM = 0 is load-bearing, not decorative. Swapping the two views' Jacobians moves the residual by under 2% (0.19% at equal focal lengths) but its Jacobian by 341%: a swap destroys the descent direction, not the cost, and is invisible to Monte Carlo calibration averages. Only tangency, checked per view, catches it — and a test of per-view wiring needs two different focal lengths to be able to fail at all.\n\nThree declared pinhole islands, one fact with three instances. A matrix cannot express a general camera's nonlinear back-projection, so (1) the mapped-homography path, where K⁻¹ declares the calibration as observation maps, and (2) VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's relative-pose bundle, which holds K matrices rather than camera models, are pinhole by construction. (3) LocalOptimizationDoneRight's vendored RansacLib baseline is third-party and exempt. Making (1) or (2) camera-general means carrying camera models instead of matrices — a separate decision, not a rider on this rule. Separately, the Monte Carlo harness's epipolar band trial keeps its query in the z = 1 chart (an epipolar line is a calibrated-plane object), so it cannot validate an essential matrix's covariance at a fisheye periphery: a green band trial is not fisheye coverage.\n\nWhen checking whether something \"runs on bearings\", trace the data PREPARATION path, not just the kernel. The five-point kernel had taken bearings all along, while the affine-plane assumption was found in five other places by five different routes: a pool generator dehomogenizing K⁻¹u, a 2-vector lift the solver registry documented as \"the caller's frame convention\", a cheirality test building (x/z, y/z, 1), a helper named for bearings that returned plane points, and the Monte Carlo harness's own interface — the last of which no test could catch, because the harness is what the tests are written in.","title":"Calibrated geometry is on bearings"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Observation-noise-lives-on-the-observations","page":"Conventions","text":"A correspondence residual is a constraint g(θ; x₁, x₂) = 0 whitened by the noise of its observations. That noise is a property of each OBSERVATION, stated where the observation is built, never a property of a problem type:\n\nEndpoint Covariance Spelled\nplain point Σ = I, unit isotropic noise in its own frame p\nuncertain point its own Σ (or precision) Uncertain(p,CovMat(Σ)), Uncertain(p,PrecMat(Λ))\nexact point Σ = 0 exact(p)\n\nWith G_x = ∂g/∂(x₁, x₂) = [G₁ G₂], the constraint covariance is the first-order propagation of both endpoints and the residual is the constraint whitened by it:\n\nΣ̃ = G₁·Σ₁·G₁ᵀ + G₂·Σ₂·G₂ᵀ,    r = L⁻¹·g,    L·Lᵀ = Σ̃.\n\npair_covariance is the 4×4 block diagonal over (x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂) that whiten and whitening_factor take as Σ_x. A correspondence whose two endpoints are both exact is an error (ArgumentError): Σ̃ = 0 leaves nothing to whiten, and no residual unit exists.\n\nTwo layers, two distances. The noise never enters the Sampson distance: sampson_distance(M, x…) is GEOMETRIC, the first-order distance from the observations to the model's variety in their own units, whatever covariance they carry. Its residual in the constraint's normal basis, sampson_residual_with_normal(M, x…) = (s_N, N), is what the noise layer whitens: r =whiten(s_N, N, Σ) = L_N⁻¹·s_N, L_N L_Nᵀ = NᵀΣN, which is exactly L⁻¹·g above (G_x = L_g·Nᵀ, so Σ̃ = L_g·NᵀΣN·L_gᵀ). mahalanobis_distance(M, x…) is ‖r‖ with Σ read from the observations by this rule: for plain points it equals the Sampson distance.\n\nWhat a threshold means. r is in units of the noise. For plain image points that is the pixel: a 3 px threshold means 3 px, exactly as before this rule was written down. With real covariances r is in SIGMAS — r² ~ χ²(d_g) — and a threshold is a number of standard deviations. An exact source with a plain target is the plain pixel case on one side: the residual is the transfer error in the target image.\n\nPlain is bit-for-bit what it always was. A plain set carries no covariance columns, every reader takes its Σ = I path unchanged, and the stack's equivalence goldens are identical across the change. Image↔image matches, pool pairs and F/E/H estimation on them do not move.","title":"Observation noise lives on the observations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Why-the-old-formulation-was-wrong-—-a-worked-example","page":"Conventions","text":"Until 2026-10 every correspondence problem hard-coded Σ₁ = Σ₂ = I over (x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂), i.e. assumed both endpoints were noisy points measured in the SAME unit. A calibration board breaks that silently.\n\nTake a 5 cm board in METRES seen at 0.3 m by an f = 800 px camera. The board → image Jacobian is about f/Z ≈ 2670 px/m (measured: singular values 2500–2600 at the board centre). For the homography's transfer form G_x = [A −I], A = ∂u₂/∂x₁, so\n\nΣ̃ = A·Aᵀ + I ≈ 2600²·I,    r ≈ e / 2600,\n\nwith e the transfer error in pixels: the residual comes out in BOARD METRES. A 0.2 px inlier scores ≈ 7e-5 and a 40 px mismatch ≈ 0.015. Both are far below a 3 px threshold, MSAC counts everything as an inlier, and the fit lands tens of pixels off (25–40 px across draws of the regression test, VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's metre-unit board).\n\nWith the board exact, Σ₁ = 0: Σ̃ = I, r = e in pixels, and the 40 px mismatch is rejected at 3 px. The fit is then the classical reprojection estimator for a known target.\n\nWhy it went unnoticed. Before the K+ refactor the default scorer was a scale-estimating marginal (Student-t + uniform): it estimated the residual scale from the data, so a residual in metres simply produced a scale in metres and the classification came out right. MSAC's FIXED pixel threshold, made the find_* default in K+, had no scale to absorb the unit and exposed it. A scale-estimating scorer still hides a unit error; it does not make one correct — its outlier half-width is still stated in the residual's unit.","title":"Why the old formulation was wrong — a worked example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#When-to-use-which","page":"Conventions","text":"Image ↔ image matches (detections in two images, pool pairs): plain.\nBoard, model, world or map points — anything known by construction rather than measured: exact. A board → image fit is exact.(board) .=> pixels.\nDetections with a covariance (a detector's per-point uncertainty, a propagated estimate): Uncertain, in the caller's frame.\n\nA problem class whose observation model already treats one side as exact is consistent with the rule as it stands: absolute pose takes plain 3D points and whitens only the image point; the radial homography and radial camera score the reprojection distance in the target image, accept their board side exact (or plain, its long-standing meaning there) and refuse any other covariance. A problem class whose every residual route (scoring, refit rows, lift rows) does not yet read covariances refuses Uncertain/exact endpoints with an ArgumentError rather than scoring one function and descending another (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation: F and E today).","title":"When to use which"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Normalization","page":"Conventions","text":"Covariances are in the CALLER's frame, like the points (frame in = frame out). An estimator's private normalization moves them with the points: an affine side map x ↦ A·x + b takes Σ to A·Σ·Aᵀ (s²·Σ for a similarity of scale s); normalize(N, cs) of a CorrespondenceNormalization does it for the correspondence set's covariance columns, and a projective observation map uses its Jacobian at each point. The Mahalanobis residual is invariant to the move, so thresholds stay in the caller's units — pixels for plain pixel data, sigmas for covariances — and nothing downstream converts them.","title":"Normalization"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Storage","page":"Conventions","text":"noise_columns turns Uncertain endpoints into two attribute columns of the correspondence set, Σ₁ (sources) and Σ₂ (targets), static 2×2 matrices in the caller's frame; a side whose endpoints are all plain has NO column (unit isotropic). An estimator's constructor calls it once; the inner loop reads the columns (pair_covariance(cs, i), the batch rows whitened_residuals! / whitened_jacobian_rows!) and never an Uncertain value. A RANSAC problem hands its point's covariance to the scorers through ONE hook, observation_covariance(problem, i) (I by default), never as a slot of its constraint tuple: constraint_with_jacobians(problem, model, i) is (g, G_x, G_θ) for every problem, as for every model. exact is an ordinary zero CovMat: its four numbers per point cost nothing past the constructor.","title":"Storage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Feature-frames:-one-interface,-every-verb-once","page":"Conventions","text":"A detection is a FeatureFrame, plain or Uncertain, in an Attributed with its metadata. The family is ordered by degrees of freedom (frame_dof): PointFrame 2 (a centre), Disc 3 (+ σ), OrientedDisc 4 (+ θ), EllipticFrame 5 (a symmetric shape factor), AffineFrame 6 (a general one). The DOF level is a trait, frame_scale: PointShape(), IsotropicScale(), ShapeFactorScale().\n\nEvery verb is implemented ONCE, on FeatureFrame or on its trait, from the per-type primitives: the centre (center), the local shape where a frame has one (shape_factor; σ for the isotropic frames), and two one-line constructors per type (its frame from mapped lengths, its similarity image). A PointFrame has only the centre, so it gets the whole interface through the hoisted code — no verb has a copy per type. It is isbits with the size and layout of Point2{T}, and its verbs cost what the Point2 verbs cost (tested: no allocation, no Union or boxing in the typed code, a KernelAbstractions kernel on the CPU and the GPU over a StructArray of detections, and a benchmark against Uncertain{Point2}).","title":"Feature frames: one interface, every verb once"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#The-verbs-every-frame-has","page":"Conventions","text":"Verb Every frame (PointFrame included)\ncentre center(f) (also centroid, GeometryBasics.origin)\nthe point observation of a detection center(u::Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}), center(d::Attributed{<:Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}}) → Uncertain{Point2}: the centre and the first 2×2 block of the frame's covariance. THE one conversion: no code takes a centre and a covariance block apart by hand\nsimilarity action S(f) for a SimilarityMap{2}, an EuclideanMap{2}, a rotation or uniform-scaling LinearMap: exact, the class kept\ntransfer through any point map transfer(m, f): the centre by m; a scaled frame's shape by m's Jacobian at the centre (an AffineFrame, first order), exact through a similarity\npropagate propagate(m, u::Uncertain{PointFrame}) through every framed map an uncertain point propagates through (EuclideanMap, SimilarityMap, HomographyMap, FramedAffine), the frame kept; for a scaled frame, propagate(m, center(u))\nerror ellipse Ellipse(u::Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}; sigma): its centre's\nbounding box extrema(f), Rect2(f), Rect2{Int}(f) (the 1σ box), bbox(f; scale, snap_to_grid) (the scale-σ box, unit-stripped; a point's is the point at any scale)\nunits ustrip(u, f), f * s, f / s (a change of unit or length scale; angles kept)\ntranslation f + v, f - v\nGPU isbits values: adapt is the identity, a StructArray of (uncertain, attributed) frames is columnar\ncodec VisualGeometryToolkitIO's one detection codec: center and the shape, covariance over the frame's parameters, centre first (frame_dof; persisted-geometry)","title":"The verbs every frame has"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#The-verbs-only-the-scaled-frames-have","page":"Conventions","text":"Verb Disc, OrientedDisc EllipticFrame, AffineFrame PointFrame\nscale(f) σ — (read characteristic_scale) absent\nshape_factor(f), shape_matrix(f) σI, σR(θ); σ²I exactly S, A; F Fᵀ absent\ncharacteristic_scale, aspect_ratio σ, 1 `√ det F\nAffineMap(f), boundary_point(f, θ) the canonical → image map the same absent\nregion(f, k), Circle(f, r), Ellipse(f, r) the k·σ contour the same absent\nMvNormal(f) N(c, σ²I) N(c, F Fᵀ) absent\ninv(f) Disc: (-c/σ, 1/σ); OrientedDisc absent the class kept: (-F⁻¹c, F⁻¹) absent\nintersects(p, q, k) Disc only absent absent\n\nAn absent verb is absent BY DESIGN, not a stub: calling it on a PointFrame is a MethodError whose hint names the interface and the point's own verbs.","title":"The verbs only the scaled frames have"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Residuals-and-distances:-the-prefix-is-the-currency,-the-noun-is-the-shape","page":"Conventions","text":"Every name in the residual and distance family states two choices, each in a fixed place.\n\nThe currency is the prefix.\n\nsampson_ is GEOMETRIC: the first-order distance to the model's variety in the data's own units, whatever noise the observations carry.\nwhitened_ and mahalanobis_ are divided by the observation noise: in σ units, which for plain points is the pixel (\"Observation noise lives on the observations\", observation-noise.md).\n\nThe noise comes from the observations (plain, Uncertain, exact), never from a type switch on one name and never from a whitening the caller supplies. One name for two currencies is what \"Derivatives and Jacobians\" forbids (jacobians.md), and for the same reason: for plain points the two currencies agree, so a caller who confuses them stays green on every plain test and is wrong on the first covariance.\n\nThe shape is the noun. With d_g the constraint's codimension:\n\nNoun Value Sign\nresidual the vector of d_g components signed\ndistance its norm ‖r‖ ≥ 0\nsigned distance the residual when d_g = 1: one number signed\n\nsigned_distance(plane, p) is the geometric signed distance (VisualGeometryToolkitCore 0.5.7). distance(hyperplane, p) was removed in 0.7 because it returned a signed value under an unsigned name.\n\nA square is named as one. ‖r‖² is the SQUARED distance: gᵀΣ̃⁻¹g is the squared Mahalanobis distance (~ χ²(d_g) under H₀), never \"the Mahalanobis distance\". A threshold on a distance and a threshold on its square differ by a square root, so a square under the plain name is a threshold off by a power.\n\nPrecedent. Statistics: a residual eᵢ is signed, the Mahalanobis distance √(rᵀΣ⁻¹r) is ≥ 0 (R's mahalanobis returns the square under the plain name, the confusion this rule avoids). Ceres: residuals are signed vectors and the cost is ½‖r‖². GTSAM: whitenedError and unwhitenedError are signed vectors and error() is the scalar.\n\nThe names, by currency and shape:\n\nCurrency Residual (signed vector) Distance (≥ 0) Signed distance (d_g = 1)\ngeometric sampson_residual, sampson_residuals! sampson_distance, sampson_distances! signed_distance\nwhitened whitened_residuals! (model level (r, M, xs) and problem level (r, problem, model)), whitened_residual(problem, ctx, i) (one point) mahalanobis_distance (model level (M, x…) and problem level (problem, ctx, i)), mahalanobis_distances!(d, problem, model) the whitened residual, d_g = 1","title":"Residuals and distances: the prefix is the currency, the noun is the shape"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#The-RANSAC-protocol","page":"Conventions","text":"The constraint-problem protocol (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's robust/problem.jl) follows the same table. What a scorer reads per point is a DISTANCE: mahalanobis_distances!(d, problem, model) fills d[i] ≥ 0 from the per-point hook mahalanobis_distance(problem, ctx, i), ctx the problem's residual_context(problem, model) (the model by default). The signed residual is whitened_residual(problem, ctx, i), in the constraint's shape (a number at d_g = 1, its d_g components otherwise), and whitened_residuals!(r, problem, model) stacks its rows. The squares are sq_distances and square_distances!. Scores read d², which the residual's sign does not move. Until the 0.7 wave the per-point scalar was a signed distance at d_g = 1 and a distance otherwise, under the word \"residual\"; a whitened_residual override written then returns a scalar at d_g ≥ 2, and test_constraint_problem refuses its shape.","title":"The RANSAC protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Persisted-geometry:-uncertainty-and-storage","page":"Conventions","text":"How geometry is written to and read from files (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's vgio/<kind>/v<major>.<minor> JSON kinds and any other stack format). It continues the observation-noise rule above: the same three cases, now on disk.","title":"Persisted geometry: uncertainty and storage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Uncertainty-mirrors-the-type","page":"Conventions","text":"Every geometry-carrying record, whether a primitive (a 2D or 3D point) or a pose (a rigid transform), stores uncertainty exactly as the in-memory type states it:\n\nIn memory On disk Read back as\nplain T no covariance field plain T\nUncertain{T} (Attributed{T, CovMat}) covariance present Uncertain{T} with that CovMat\nexact(p) (zero covariance) covariance all zeros Uncertain{T} with a zero CovMat (= exact(p))\n\nA writer emits covariance if and only if the value is Uncertain. The key is covariance in every kind and every body, in one of the three storages below; no kind spells it otherwise (a flat vector, a cov with a separate size).\nAn absent covariance is stated by its producer. A record whose producer has no finite covariance omits the key and states a per-record covariance_status (for example \"unrefined\") with its reason. A null covariance is refused, and a reader never substitutes a default for an omitted one (the producer owns its observation policy).\nA reader returns the matching type, so a value round-trips exactly, type included.\nA precision-form Uncertain (PrecMat) is written as its covariance and read back in covariance form.\nPlain means \"no covariance stated\", not \"unit noise\". The consumer applies its own documented default. For an image point that default is the observation-noise rule's Σ = I; other consumers document theirs.\nexact means zero, and it is written as zeros.\nAn estimator states its uncertainty. A file written from an estimate (a hand-eye calibration, a fitted pose) carries Uncertain values. A writer that has a covariance and drops it is a bug.","title":"Uncertainty mirrors the type"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#One-encoding-per-storage","page":"Conventions","text":"The covariance states the storage the value's CovMat holds, so it round-trips as that storage:\n\nIn memory (parent(Σ)) On disk\nSMatrix (full) a d×d array of rows (d arrays of d numbers), symmetric\nSDiagonal (diagonal) {\"diagonal\": [d numbers]}\nScaledIdentity (isotropic, λ·I) {\"isotropic\": λ}\n\nAn isotropic covariance over entries of different length dimensions (a pose's angles and lengths) has no single number in the file's units, so it is written, and read back, as its diagonal. The storage is told from the JSON's shape, never from the numbers. Every form is in the primitive's own tangent order and units:\n\nPoints: (x, y) or (x, y, z), in the file's unit squared. That is px² in a file that declares a pixel_frame, and the declared length_unit squared otherwise.\nPoses: 6×6 over [δω; δt], rotation first: rad² for the rotation block, the file's length_unit squared for the translation block, and their product for the cross block.\nThe chart is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's RigidExpManifold6: R ↦ exp(δω)·R and t ↦ t + δt. The rotation is perturbed on the LEFT, in the pose's destination (to) frame, and the translation additively in the same frame. This is the chart VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation reports estimator covariances in.\nRelation to GTSAM. The order [ω; v] is GTSAM's Pose3 tangent order (Pose3::Expmap: w = xi.head<3>(), v = xi.tail<3>()).\nGTSAM's chart is different. It is the RIGHT (body-frame) retraction T·Exp(ξ), so a GTSAM marginal needs the adjoint, not a relabel.\nRelation to Sophus. Sophus' SE3 tangent is [υ; ω], translation first.\nROS PoseWithCovariance. It is row-major 6×6 over (x, y, z, rot X, rot Y, rot Z), with fixed-axis rotations in the parent frame and the translation in the parent frame. That is this chart with its blocks swapped.\nThe conversion is the permutation P = [0 I₃; I₃ 0]: Σ_ros = P·Σ·Pᵀ.\nVisualGeometryToolkitIO's ros_pose_covariance and pose_covariance_from_ros implement it.\n\nUnits: memory in base units, files in the declared unit. In memory a covariance is in base units, like the value it belongs to: m² for a 3D point, and for a pose rad² in the rotation block, m² in the translation block and m·rad in the cross block. On disk it is in the file's declared length_unit. A writer converts each entry with VisualGeometryToolkitIO's write_length rule, the same decimal shift as the value, raised to the entry's power of length, and a reader converts back the same way. A file in mm therefore holds mm² and mm·rad entries, and a covariance read from it is in m² and m·rad again. Pixel covariances are px² and are never converted.\n\nA reader refuses a covariance of the wrong size, a non-finite one, or an asymmetric one. It does not test positive semi-definiteness (an exact zero is valid). Every matrix a file holds (a covariance, a shape matrix, an affine shape factor) is written in rows, the stack's MATRIX_LAYOUT.\n\nAn uncertain feature frame's covariance is in the frame's parameter order, the centre first. A detection's FeatureFrame is written as its center and its shape parameters (frame_dof of them), and its covariance is over exactly those parameters, (x, y) first: a PointFrame's is the uncertain point's 2×2, so the point frame is the 2-DOF case of the one frame codec, never a codec of its own. The point observation of a detection is its first 2×2 block (center(::Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame})), read by that function only.","title":"One encoding per storage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#One-units-object,-one-conventions-object","page":"Conventions","text":"Every file states its units ONCE, in one file-level units object, and its conventions once, in one conventions object; neither has a default, and a file without either, or without one of their keys, is refused:\n\n\"units\": {\"length\": \"mm\", \"time\": \"s\", \"angle\": \"rad\", \"pixel\": \"colmap\",\n          \"density\": \"dpi\", \"radiance\": \"relative\", \"length_roles\": {…}},\n\"conventions\": {\"index_base\": 1, \"matrix_layout\": \"rows\", \"rotation_encoding\": \"quaternion_wxyz\", …}\n\nA field's unit is a power of a declared base, stated by the schema, never by the file. A blur covariance is length² on a print and px² on a sensor; a focus surface is length⁻¹. No block carries its own \"unit\" string.\nTime values are in units.time; a time_origin is ISO 8601 with its UTC offset.\nAngles are radians, declared as the fixed token \"rad\".\nSizes are objects. Every Size2 (an image size, a board, canvas or texture size, a pixel pitch, a lattice's grid_size, the identity tree's sizes) is {\"width\": w, \"height\": h}, never a positional pair. A rectangle (area) and the positional primitives (an ellipse, a circle) stay documented arrays, described field by field in the kind's JSON Schema.\nEvery index is 1-based, Julia's, declared once as \"index_base\": 1 in the conventions object: a point index, a lattice's present runs, a frame index. Only a foreign format's importer and exporter (Schöps, COLMAP, OpenCV, all 0-based) convert, at their own boundary; a stack reader and writer hold no ±1.","title":"One units object, one conventions object"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Versions:-a-major-and-a-minor","page":"Conventions","text":"A schema tag is vgio/<kind>/v<major>.<minor>. A reader accepts any minor of its major: a minor version adds optional keys its schema declares, and an unknown, undeclared key stays refused. A major version changes a meaning. The versions a reader accepts are derived from its migration steps, never kept as a list by hand.","title":"Versions: a major and a minor"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Storage:-JSON-now,-a-binary-payload-past-a-threshold","page":"Conventions","text":"Every kind is a single JSON file today. A kind keeps that form while its bulk numeric arrays stay small.\n\nOnce a kind's arrays exceed about 10 MB, it follows glTF's JSON + bin pattern:\n\nThe JSON header stays. It holds the schema tag, the declared conventions, the frames, the sensor and the provenance.\nThe bulk arrays move to an Apache Arrow sidecar. The header references the sidecar under the reserved top-level key \"payload\": {\"file\": \"<name>.arrow\", \"sha256\": \"<hex>\"}.\n\nEvery kind reserves \"payload\" now, so a sidecar can be added later without a schema break. No kind implements it yet. Until one does, a reader refuses a file that carries \"payload\".","title":"Storage: JSON now, a binary payload past a threshold"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Every-stored-kind-is-a-VGC-type","page":"Conventions","text":"A persisted kind is the DIRECT serialization of a VisualGeometryToolkitCore type: the reader returns the VGC value, the writer takes it, and the round trip is exact by type (plain ↔ plain, Uncertain ↔ Uncertain, labels ↔ frame names). No format-side record type stands between the file and the value; a foreign format (COLMAP, an OpenCV or MATLAB export) keeps an adapter type that converts to and from VGC types.\n\nKind (VisualGeometryToolkitIO) VGC value\ncameras Dict{Symbol, …} of unposed BoundedCameras of a CameraModel (name → camera)\ntransforms a Vector of labeled EuclideanMap{3}s (Uncertain allowed)\nrig Rig: sensors, PosedTargets, or both\ntarget an AbstractTarget (PointTarget, PlanarTarget)\ntrajectory Trajectory\ntarget_poses TargetPoses\nobservations TargetObservations (pixels or 3D points, by its frame; each set names its entity, target_point or landmark). The 0.9 target_detections, target_points2d and target_points3d kinds are read through version steps into it\ndetection a detection, Attributed{Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}} (the one frame codec, every frame type)\nsensor_characterisation SensorCharacterisation: the body and, per ISO, one body per present component (an absent one absent), each with its source (measured, estimated, specified, file)\ncapture_settings CaptureSettings, Attributed with its identities' sources\n\nFile names state the kind, never the time. A file's name is a convention for people, not a format: every loader reads the file's schema and never its name. VisualGeometryToolkitIO's docs (\"Kinds and the I/O Surface\", File names) state it once.\n\nThe observation kind is COLMAP's decomposition: an observation ≈ COLMAP's images.txt points2D with their POINT3D_ID — here the pair (target id, point index) — and the target rig ≈ points3D, kept as per-target frames (each target's points in its own frame, placed by its pose) with no stored tracks.\n\nMetadata: envelope for the file, attributes for the value. The file's envelope holds what is about the FILE — the schema tag, the declared conventions (the units and conventions objects), the generator (the writing package and its version), the reserved payload, and the free-form provenance, which is written from a keyword and never read back — and none of it is part of the value. A fact about the VALUE that must round-trip is part of the VGC value: a field (a trajectory's per-frame images and times, an observation's target identities) or an Attributed attribute — a covariance as Uncertain, a camera's calibration block as Attributed(camera, (; calibration = (; tool, source, date, reprojection_rms, n_images, n_points, note, device))) (device = (; body, lens), the DeviceIdentitys of the hardware it was made for: values are math, identity is an attribute, so a camera model holds no names), a target's as Attributed(target, (; calibration_record = (; …))) (the same closed set of keys; the target's own calibration is its TargetCalibration, part of the value, so the record is named apart).\n\nA target's calibration is part of its record. A PlanarTarget's TargetCalibration is written in its body as one calibration record (vgio/target/v4, vgio/rig/v7): its type, the kind's tag in TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS; its parameters in parameter_names order, lengths in the file's unit; nodes for a thin-plate kind; for a composition (the affine print error Aspect ∘ Skew, HeightField ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew, HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline, …), written by its structure, {\"type\": \"composition\", \"compose\": [outer, …, inner]}, the members in CoordinateTransformations' order, the outer map first; and a covariance over every parameter, in that order, iff it is Uncertain. A lens record lists its stages in sequence under the same key, compose, in the same order. A list whose order changes changes its key: the retired calibration_chain / members and lens chain listed the same members in application order, and a reader refuses them by name in a current record rather than read them in the new order (a file of the version that held them is read through that version's migration). A target with a board (the physical board it is printed on) writes it as {\"size\": {\"width\", \"height\"}, \"origin\": [u, v]}, lengths in the file's unit.\n\nA referenced design is stored by its reference. A PlanarTarget whose design is content-addressed (a BlobBoards board uid) is written by its reference and its calibration; its nominal points are the design's owner's and are not restated. A reader returns it unresolved unless the caller supplies the owner's resolver.\n\nA parametric design is stored by its layout. A PlanarTarget built from a layout (PlanarTarget(layout; …), an AbstractTargetLayout: AprilGrid, SchoepsPattern) records it (layout(t)) and is written by it (vgio/target/v4, vgio/rig/v7): a layout record whose type is the layout's tag in TARGET_LAYOUT_TAGS, its whole design (tag family, ids) and its lengths in the file's unit; its points follow from the layout and are not restated. Its identity is of its points, as a target given by them: the layout is not hashed.\n\nAn identity does not change when the file format does. An observation names the rig that defines its targets by content_id: the SHA-256 of a canonical tree built from the in-memory rig, serialized by RFC 8785 (JCS, its number formatting), under the current names, specified once in VisualGeometryToolkitIO's docs, in base units (each length normalised to the Float64 its shortest round-tripping decimal in METRES reads back to), its targets in a fixed order, with no schema version and no envelope provenance. A rig, every file written from it in any unit, and a migration of that file to the next record version have one identity; a test pins it for a rig of every target kind.\n\nA published file keeps loading. Every earlier schema version a published file holds is READ, and only the current version is written: a reader that meets an older version migrates it in memory, version by version, to the current record (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's migrate_schema steps, run by migrate_record; a schema's owner adds the steps of its own kinds), silently. The reason is the published data: a schema change must never force an archive on Zenodo or Hugging Face to be uploaded again. Rewriting a file once (vgio_migrate) is optional, and runs the same steps. A version is refused only where no published file holds it, or where it lacks what the current record needs (transforms v1/v2's free-form frame names).\n\nPersisted rotations are quaternions. A rotation is stored as a Hamilton quaternion (w-x-y-z, w ≥ 0; a stored w < 0 is refused on read, never flipped) and READ AS ONE: the reader builds a EuclideanMap{3, Float64, QuatRotation{Float64}, …} from the stored unit quaternion without renormalizing it, so file → value → file is exact and a value read from a file round-trips bit for bit. The writer accepts any Rotation: a QuatRotation is written as it is (its sign made w ≥ 0 by exact negation, the same rotation); any other — a RotMatrix an estimator computed — is converted to a quaternion on write and reads back as a QuatRotation-valued map (equal to the stored matrix to the conversion's rounding). Code that consumes a persisted pose therefore takes any Rotation, never a RotMatrix specifically; one that needs a matrix converts explicitly.","title":"Every stored kind is a VGC type"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Derivatives-and-Jacobians","page":"Conventions","text":"One word, jacobian; the suffix says what comes back. deriv survives only where an upstream package owns the name.\n\nForm Meaning Example\ntransform_deriv(f, x), transform_deriv_params(f, x) a map's Jacobian w.r.t. input / parameters CoordinateTransformations' API, kept\n<f>_with_jacobian (value, J) backproject_with_jacobian(camera, u)\n<f>_with_jacobians value plus several, documented project_with_jacobians(lens, X) -> (u, J_X, J_p)\nconstraint_with_jacobians (g, G_x, G_θ) for an implicit constraint constraint_with_jacobians(E, f₁, f₂; M₁, M₂)\nconstraint_jacobians the Jacobians alone constraint_jacobians(M, v, cs)\n<thing>_jacobian the Jacobian alone, named after what it differentiates retract_jacobian, action_jacobian, observation_gradient_jacobian (∂G_x/∂θ of a constraint)\n\nEach docstring names its Ceres or GTSAM equivalent, and says so explicitly where they disagree:\n\nretract_jacobian(ch, v) is ∂vec(retract(ch, v))/∂v — Ceres' Manifold::PlusJacobian. An inverse form would be local_coordinates_jacobian (Ceres' MinusJacobian).\nleft_jacobian(SO3, ω) is Sophus' SO3::leftJacobian. GTSAM's Rot3::ExpmapDerivative is its transpose, the right Jacobian, because GTSAM retracts by right multiplication — measured here, not assumed (left identity residual 5.8e-13; the right identity fails at 1.8e-6).\nexp_so3/log_so3/exp_se3/log_se3 keep their names: Manifolds.jl's exp(M, p, X) is the base-point exponential while ours is the Lie-group one (exp_lie(G, X)), so exp(SO3, ω) would read as the base-point form with the point dropped.\nbackproject keeps its name: GTSAM's nearest counterpart, Cal3_S2::calibrate, collides with our task verb calibrate (estimating the camera).\nImplicit constraints follow Förstner's A = ∂g/∂θ, B = ∂g/∂x, since neither Ceres nor GTSAM has the concept. Their Sampson residual has ONE implementation (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's base/sampson.jl): the residual, sampson_residual_with_jacobian's exact model row (from G_θ and the model's observation_gradient_jacobian) and the √w rows over a set are built there and nowhere else; a frozen whitening is whitening_factor.\n\nThe stack's Levenberg–Marquardt protocol (VisualGeometryToolkitCore lm_core.jl) is residual_rows! and residual_jacobian_rows!, mirroring Ceres' CostFunction::Evaluate; its driver is NLLSsolver (lmsolve_chart!, one adapter), and the protocol is independent of the driver. They are deliberately NOT named whitened_residuals!: the RANSAC protocol's whitened_residuals!(r, problem, model) is unweighted and in data units, while LM rows are √w-weighted in chart coordinates. One name for two currencies is what the frame rule forbids, so the currency is in the name.","title":"Derivatives and Jacobians"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Units","page":"Conventions","text":"Units are FlexUnits.jl: no stack quantity type or dimension set. The stack's unit code lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's src/base/units.jl: its own unit registry, StackUnits, built with FlexUnits' RegistryTools exactly as FlexUnits documents for a registry of another transform type — a PermanentDict of Units{Dimensions{FixRat32}, AffineTransform{Rational{Int}}} filled by register_unit! and exposed by @generate_registry_exports — the unit constants, base_quantity, and the stack's one guarded parse. Parsing (ud\"…\", uparse, qparse), printing, conversion and dimension errors are FlexUnits'.\n\nScales are exact rationals. Every registered scale is written as an exact ratio of base units and handed to register_unit! as data: mm = 1//1000, cm = 1//100, μm = 1//1000000, km = 1000, inch = 127//5000 (25.4 mm), ft = 12 inch, pt = inch/72, px and pd dimensionless with scale 1, dpi = px/inch, and the SI base units (m, kg, s, A, K, cd, mol) at 1. The registry holds those and nothing else. FlexUnits' registry_defaults! is not called: in a Rational registry it converts its Float64 scales to their binary values (Rational(0.001) = 1152921504606847//1152921504606846976). So a length built from an integer is exact — 142mm is 71//500 m, whose Float64 is the decimal-nearest 0.142 (FlexUnits' Float64 mm gave 0.14200000000000002) — and a float literal is FlexUnits' promotion, 142.0mm = 142.0 * 0.001, correctly rounded since FlexUnits 0.6.16 (Deduction42/FlexUnits.jl#131: a float times a rational scale as x * num / den) — which rounds the BINARY value of the float, so a decimal read from text can still land one ulp from the decimal's nearest double; VisualGeometryToolkitIO's read_length/write_length (decimal-exact, by exponent shift) remain the stack's one documented exception for that case. An exact quantity compares exactly: ustrip(m, 142mm) == 0.142 is false (the double 0.142 is not 71/500), while ustrip(m, base_quantity(142mm)) === 0.142.","title":"Units"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#The-layering:-units-at-the-boundary,-plain-numbers-inside","page":"Conventions","text":"Units live at the public boundary and plain numbers live inside. A value moves from one to the other where the frames rule already conditions data: once at an estimator's entry and once at its exit.\n\nLayer Holds\nPublic values (held by users, handed between packages, persisted): EuclideanMap translations, PlanarTarget and PointTarget points, Rig target poses, Trajectory, TargetPoses, a 3D TargetObservations, sensor and board sizes FlexUnits quantities for every physical length, in metres; a dimensionless quantity where a public value has no unit\nIO a file declares its length_unit; a reader returns quantities, and a writer writes numbers in the declared unit\nEstimator, renderer and solver entry and exit (fit_*, find_*, refine_*, a one-step algorithm's public form) one strip into the private working unit at entry, one reattachment at exit\nInner loop (minimal kernels, *_step updates, ! workspace forms, RANSAC internals, LM rows and charts, GPU kernels) plain numbers of eltype T (Float64, Float32, a ForwardDiff Dual)\n\nPixel coordinates keep their frame tags, because PixelFrame is their unit. px quantities stay for sizes and densities.\n\nA physical value has two forms, and both are bits types:\n\nForm Element type Where\nbase form, in metres Quantity{T, Dimensions{FixRat32}} public values: poses, points, sizes\nplain T (Float64, Float32, Dual) inner loops and GPU kernels\n\nThe plain form of a value is the whole value's ustrip(u, X), for a pose, a target, a rig or a capture. It is never a value rebuilt from stripped components. A unit is attached by construction, EuclideanMap(R, t .* m), or by with_unit(X, u), which reads a plain value — a map included, its rotation kept exactly — as being in u. One exception is forced by its type: NoncentralSplineModel holds its line points as plain metres, because its spline of SVector{3, T} controls cannot hold quantities, and its docstring says so. Because a plain extrinsics is \"in the caller's unit\", a PosedCamera of that model refuses one (ArgumentError, naming t .* m); its plain form is ustrip(m, camera) only.\n\nUnits are allowed in a type but not required by it, so the layering is held at four points:\n\nVisualGeometryToolkitIO's readers always return quantities, through read_length.\nIts writers refuse a plain translation or point for a physical kind (rig, trajectory, target_poses, transforms, target, a 3D observations, and the object points of correspondences). The ArgumentError names the fix, t .* m.\nA public estimator that takes physical values strips them in metres at entry (ustrip(m, x)) and reattaches the unit at exit, so unitful input gives unitful output. Plain input gives plain output, in the caller's own unit, as before units: estimators are not where plain data is refused, the IO writers of point 2 are. A model that cannot record a unit — a plane homography, a radial or division camera, the up-to-scale relative pose of refine_relative_pose — refuses unitful input instead, with an ArgumentError naming the fix (strip the plane points in metres, the unit the stack's boundaries use). This supersedes the plan's earlier \"a plain physical input is refused\" (ruling of 2026-10-25, Task 9): refusing plain input would break every existing caller, so a later audit must not \"fix\" the code back to it.\ncheck_naming rule R11 flags a ustrip(…) fed into an EuclideanMap or a target constructor, and a power-of-ten scale in a definition that names a file's length_unit. Unit names are read only by parse_unit.\n\nA pixel is dimensionless. FlexUnits' dimensions are the seven SI ones, so px cannot be told from rad or a ratio, and 3px + 1.0 is allowed. Pixel coordinates keep their safety through PixelFrame tags; only pixel-sized quantities lose it. A function that takes a pixel-sized quantity accepts a plain number or a dimensionless quantity, and refuses a length with DimensionError.\n\nUnit names from outside (a file, a QR code, Python) are read with parse_unit(s) — one registered symbol, or with compound = true a product, quotient or power of them (\"mm/px\") — and written with unit_string(u), FlexUnits' printing of a registered unit. uparse evaluates what it parses, so nothing else calls it. A dimension check is dimension(q) == dimension(u). check_naming enforces this everywhere (rule R11): no package outside the units module calls uparse/qparse/register_unit, builds a unit or a registry (Units, AffineTransform, PermanentDict, register_unit!, registry_defaults!), reaches into the registry, writes a table of unit names, converts with convert(Quantity{…}, q) or a bare ubase(q) in source (base_quantity(q)), branches x isa Quantity ? ustrip(…) (strip_or_bare(u, x)) or writes a conversion factor (25.4, 0.0254, 304.8, 0.3048, 127//5000); what FlexUnits lacks, or arithmetic that must stay bit-identical to stored values, is allowed by file with its reason (_UNIT_RULE_ALLOW). The one hand-rolled conversion is VisualGeometryToolkitIO's read_length (below), until FlexUnits rounds a rational scale once.\n\nValues are stored in base units. A length is held in metres, a pixel count as a plain dimensionless value, so a bare ustrip(q) of a length is in metres. Code that wants a unit names it: ustrip(mm, q), ustrip(px, q), ustrip(dpi, q). A bare ustrip is correct only on a pixel quantity or a dimensionless ratio. Feature geometry (Disc, Circle, …) strips through one boundary that names its units: pixels in px, lengths in mm.\n\nDynamic quantities in structs. The exported constants (px, mm, cm, μm, m, inch, ft, pt, dpi) are the registry's dynamic units, so 10.0mm, 3.0px and 300.0dpi share FlexUnits' base-form type Quantity{Float64, Dimensions{FixRat32}}, a bits type; 10mm (an integer times an exact scale) is a Quantity{Rational{Int}, …} until it is stored. A struct that holds quantities stores the Float64 form, named once in VisualGeometryToolkitCore as the computed constant BaseQuantity = typeof(ubase(1.0mm)) — FlexUnits' stored form, not a stack quantity type: no signature takes it, every boundary takes any Quantity — and needs no unit type parameter, and a mixed-dimension container is concrete. Static units (u\"…\") are not used in struct fields.\n\nBoundaries accept any quantity and convert. A quantity combined with a unit keeps that unit (5.86μm / px is a Quantity{Float64, Units{…}}), and uconvert returns that form too. Public signatures therefore take FlexUnits' Quantity, and a constructor that stores one converts it with base_quantity(q) = float(ubase(q)) (base_quantity.(s) for a Size2, which FlexUnits broadcasts over as it does ustrip.(u, s)), or by converting into a BaseQuantity field. A Rational never reaches a struct field or a kernel: the stack's strips (strip_metres, strip_or_bare) return floats. A pixel–length crossing is a product with a density (q * 300dpi is in px); there is no density-taking conversion verb. A dimension mismatch raises DimensionError (arithmetic) or ConversionError (ustrip/uconvert into a unit of the wrong dimension). Angles are plain radians.","title":"The layering: units at the boundary, plain numbers inside"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Files-keep-human-units","page":"Conventions","text":"Memory holds metres. A file holds the unit that reads naturally for its quantity: focal lengths and board sizes in mm, pixel pitch in μm, room-scale poses in m. The file declares its unit, once per file (length_unit) or per field. VisualGeometryToolkitIO's default file unit is mm.\n\nNever convert a file length by multiplying by a scale. The rounding comes from the multiplication, not from metres. 62.8 * 0.001 is 0.0628, but 0.0628 / 0.001 is 62.79999999999999, and the same happens for a few percent of typical millimetre values. VisualGeometryToolkitIO converts every file length with one pair, read_length(x, u) and write_length(q, u). For a power-of-ten unit (mm, cm, μm, km) the pair shifts the decimal exponent, which is one correctly rounded parse. For another unit (inch, ft, pt) it reads by the unit's scale and writes the shortest decimal that reads back to the identical value. A side benefit is that memory values are correctly rounded: 13.9 mm reads as 0.0139 m.\n\nWhat is exact. A file that VisualGeometryToolkitIO wrote is rewritten byte for byte when its numbers have at most 15 significant digits. A full-precision number (16 or 17 digits) may move by at most 2 ulp on its first rewrite, and it stays fixed after that. This is inherent to metres in memory and millimetres in files: the two grids differ, so some 17-digit millimetre numbers share their nearest metre value with a neighbour, and one of the two cannot come back. An identity follows the value, not the bytes: VisualGeometryToolkitIO's content_id of a rig hashes a canonical tree of the loaded value in metres, so a file, the rig loaded from it and the same rig written in another unit name the same targets.\n\nOne quantity form. A JSON record holds each quantity as a number in the unit it declares (\"units\": {\"world\": \"mm\", \"density\": \"dpi\"}), through the pair above; a record whose lengths need more than one unit declares each further one as a length role (\"lengths\": {\"pattern\": \"pt\"}), each number decimal-exact in its own. The {\"value\": v, \"unit\": u} objects that VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures once wrote, and that BlobBoards' older boards hold, are never a current record's: a file of an earlier schema version that holds them is read by its owner's version step for that version, on load (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's migrate_schema and migrate_record), and vgio_migrate rewrites it once if its owner wants it rewritten. Every earlier version a published file holds stays readable, and only the current one is written: a schema change must not force published data (Zenodo, Hugging Face) to be uploaded again.\n\nA float length built in code has already rounded. [62.8, 1.3] .* mm multiplies by the Float64 of the scale when it is stored in metres. 1.3mm is held as 0.0013000000000000002 m, and a file written from it holds the 17-digit text 1.3000000000000003. An integer length is exact (142mm, 210mm). To build an exact length from a decimal in code, read it the way a file is read: VisualGeometryToolkitIO.read_length(1.3, mm).\n\nCovariances follow the same split. In memory a covariance is in base units: for a pose, rad² in the rotation block, m² in the translation block and m·rad in the cross block. On disk it is in the file's declared length_unit, converted by the same decimal shift raised to each entry's power of length (mm² for a translation entry, mm·rad for a cross entry in a millimetre file).","title":"Files keep human units"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#Stack-contracts:-tags,-dependencies-and-package-boundaries","page":"Conventions","text":"Six rules hold across the stack. Each is a function in VisualGeometryToolkitCore (src/quality/) that a paper project's suite can call directly, and a gate of VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_quality_gates, which every stack package's quality test runs.\n\nThe tag rule. A configuration file names a type by its tag, and every tag is snake_case of the type's name, with the family's declared suffix stripped from its end when present. snake_case has exactly two rules, then lowercases: an _ goes before an uppercase letter that follows a lowercase letter or a digit, and before the last uppercase letter of an uppercase run when a lowercase letter follows it. A digit stays with its letters: FixedS2 → fixed_s2, WelschHQNewton → welsch_hq_newton, StudentTUniform → student_t_uniform, SimFitPP → sim_fit_pp. A tag that selects nothing is spelled none.\n\nOne registry. TagRegistry(family, suffix, \"tag\" => Type, …; retired, paths) is the only structure a config loader may use to map a tag to a type:\n\nconst MODEL_UPDATES = TagRegistry(:model_update, \"\",\n    \"keep_model\" => KeepModel, \"weighted_refine\" => WeightedRefine, \"none\" => nothing;\n    retired = (\"keep\" => \"keep_model\",),\n    paths = [r\"local_optimization_strategy\\.model_update\"])\n\nMODEL_UPDATES[\"keep_model\"]        # KeepModel\nMODEL_UPDATES[\"keep\"]              # ArgumentError: tag \"keep\" was renamed \"keep_model\"\ntag_of(MODEL_UPDATES, KeepModel)   # \"keep_model\" (the writer side)\n\nAn unknown tag is an ArgumentError that lists the known ones. paths are the TOML key paths (dotted, an array element [i] counted from 1, matched whole) whose string values are tags of the family. Construction does not check the rule — a package's load never fails on a spelling; tag_rule_violations does.\n\nThe dependency allow-list. configs/stack_dependencies.toml in VisualGeometryToolkitCore has one row per project: the stack packages its Project.toml may name in [deps], [weakdeps], [extras] or the test target. A stack package is any key of that table, and a project missing from it is an error. stack_dependency_violations(project_toml_path, name) checks one project. The table lists the published packages and paper projects only; a project kept elsewhere supplies its own row through the extra keyword (a TOML file in the same format, or a dictionary), and the quality gate's stack_extra. An extra row adds a project and never replaces a published one.\n\nPublic names only, across a package boundary. A name imported from, or accessed qualified in, another package is public there (ExplicitImports' check_all_explicit_imports_are_public and check_all_qualified_accesses_are_public), and no name reached from another stack package begins with _ (private_name_violations, a source scan).\n\nExtension points are declared. A package adds methods to another stack package's function only when the owner lists it in its CONTRACT_GENERICS (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's: the block-coordinate-descent hooks, the chart-LM row protocol, the RANSAC/LO interface, the minimal-solver backends, the TOML config hooks, the accessors other packages implement for their own types, the parameter protocol, the camera maps project and backproject, convert_eltype, the Manifold protocol with ambient_coordinates, and the plotting stubs). Any stack package may declare its own; contract_generic_violations(M) lists the methods that land elsewhere. Functions owned by Base, Core, the standard library or a package outside the stack are exempt.\n\nAgent instructions carry nothing private. CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and the tracked .claude/ files ship with every package. agent_doc_violations(root) checks them against AGENT_DOC_FORBIDDEN (absolute home-directory paths, private keys and access tokens); every stack package's suite runs it with extra = get(ENV, \"AGENT_DOC_PATTERNS\", nothing), a file of further patterns that release tooling supplies.\n\nOne copy of the conventions. This text lives once, in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's conventions/ folder (VisualGeometryToolkitCore.conventions_dir()). A package's CLAUDE.md points at the folder and carries no copy of it; each package's agent-docs test checks that its CLAUDE.md names the folder and holds none of the shared sections.\n\nGate keyword Checks Function\ntag_rules every TagRegistry constant of the package obeys the tag rule tag_rule_violations\ntag_tables no Dict{String, <:Type} table or \"tag\" => SomeType pair outside a TagRegistry(...) call in src/ (a heuristic scan; comments, docstrings and Base/Core converter types such as \"cutoff\" => Float32 are skipped) tag_table_violations\nconfig_tags every string at a registry's paths in the TOML files under config_dirs (default: the package's configs/) is a current tag config_tag_violations\nstack_dependencies the Project.toml against the package's row (stack_name) stack_dependency_violations\npublic_names the two ExplicitImports public checks and the private-name scan private_name_violations\ncontract_generics methods on other stack packages' functions land on their contracts contract_generic_violations\n\nEach gate takes a mode: :enforce (@test that it finds nothing), :report (each finding logged with @warn and the gate recorded as broken; a gate that finds nothing is an ordinary test) or :off. Every gate defaults to :report until the stack conforms; a package turns one to :enforce by supplying the keyword.","title":"Stack contracts: tags, dependencies and package boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/conventions/#See-also","page":"Conventions","text":"Coordinate Frames — the Frame{N} hierarchy and the tags\nRobust Estimation — which estimator for which task\nEpipolar Forms — model forms and their composition\nDesign: Attributed{V,A} — composition and attribute columns","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#Epipolar-Forms-API","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"The slot-labeled epipolar forms: which form a model is in, and how forms compose; see the Epipolar Forms guide. The solvers that estimate them are in the Solvers API.","title":"Epipolar Forms API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#Index","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"Pages = [\"epipolar_forms.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EpipolarForm","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"EpipolarForm{S1<:Frame{2}, S2<:Frame{2}, M<:AbstractFundMat}\n\nSlot-labeled epipolar form: a bilinear form with two contravariant point arguments, wrapping a frame-free FundMat or EssentialMat.\n\nSlot convention (pinned here; every other method defers to this): slot 1 takes points of frame S1 (view 1), slot 2 takes points of frame S2 (view 2), and the wrapped matrix M satisfies\n\nx₂ᵀ M x₁ = 0\n\nfor corresponding x₁ ∈ S1, x₂ ∈ S2 (homogeneous). Slot 1 is the RIGHT argument of the matrix, matching the u₂ᵀ F u₁ convention used throughout the solver layer. In slot-monogram terms the form is M_{S2,S1} — first index left slot, second index right slot.\n\nThe frame tags label the slots of a form, not the endpoints of a map: an EpipolarForm is not a Transformation, does not compose with ∘, and has no inv (deliberately — see the note below). Its point ↦ line action is epipolar_map, and a frame change is composition on that map: EpipolarForm(H₂ ∘ epipolar_map(f) ∘ inv(H₁)) (see ∘ on EpipolarMap).\n\nConstruction is the labeled Mat → form lift; unlabeled lifts are allowed for gradual adoption (both slots UnlabeledFrame{2}):\n\nform = EpipolarForm{ImageFrame{:a}, ImageFrame{:b}}(F)   # labeled lift\nform = EpipolarForm(F)                                 # unlabeled lift\n\nEvaluating the form applies the slot convention operationally: form(x₁, x₂) returns the scalar x̃₂ᵀ M x̃₁ (zero for an exact correspondence). The wrapper does NOT enforce the rank-2 (or E-manifold) constraint — that discipline belongs to the wrapped Mat type and the solvers that produce it.\n\nWhy there is no inv\n\nThe epipolar correlation is rank 2: its kernel is the epipole e₁ (M e₁ = 0), every point on one epipolar line maps to the SAME line in the other view, and the image is not all lines but the 1-parameter pencil through the other epipole e₂. A 2-parameter point domain collapsing onto a 1-parameter family of lines has no inverse — inv(::EpipolarForm) is deliberately left undefined (a MethodError), and transpose (swap the slots) is the only involution the form supports. Use epipoles to make the degeneracy explicit.\n\nSee also epipolar_map, EpipolarMap, epipoles, sampson_distance, ImageFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EpipolarForm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EpipolarForm-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"(f::EpipolarForm)(x₁, x₂) -> scalar\n\nEvaluate the bilinear form on a point per slot: x̃₂ᵀ M x̃₁ with x̃ = [x; 1]. Zero (up to noise) iff (x₁, x₂) satisfy the epipolar constraint. x₁ feeds slot 1 (frame S1), x₂ slot 2 (frame S2) — the convention pinned at EpipolarForm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EpipolarForm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EpipolarMap","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"EpipolarMap{S1, S2, M}\n\nTyped callable returned by epipolar_map: the rank-2 correlation of an EpipolarForm, taking a point in frame S1 to its epipolar line in frame S2; EpipolarMap(F) / EpipolarMap{S1, S2}(F) lift a Mat directly. Not a Transformation — it has no inverse (see the rank-2 note at EpipolarForm) — but it composes with collineations on either side (∘ below), which is how it changes frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EpipolarMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap-Union{Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TransferForm{Src, Dst}}, Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}} where {Src, Dst}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"HomographyMap(f::TransferForm{Src, Dst}) -> HomographyMap{Src, Dst}\nTransferForm(h::HomographyMap{3, T, M, Src, Dst}) -> TransferForm{Src, Dst}\n\nA transfer form's tags are endpoints, so its homography IS a framed HomographyMap with the same two frames, and its frame change is that map's composition (frame-checked by the map's own junction rule): with D₁, D₂ carrying the current endpoints into the new ones,\n\nTransferForm(D₂ ∘ HomographyMap(f) ∘ inv(D₁))   has   H′ = D₂ · H · D₁⁻¹.\n\nAn estimator that solves in a model frame holds OBSERVATION maps Cᵢ carrying data-frame points into the model frame (x̃ᵢ = Cᵢ xᵢ; for calibrated homography Cᵢ = Kᵢ⁻¹), so moving the solved model back to the data frame is inv(C₂) ∘ HomographyMap(f) ∘ C₁. inv on a HomographyMap is an O(1) swap of its two stored matrices.\n\nA residual is EQUIVARIANT, not invariant: g = π(H x̃₁) − x₂ is a displacement in the destination image, so a frame change moves it by that frame's local Jacobian — exactly (not to first order) for an affine D₂, whose Jacobian is constant. What IS frame-independent is the ZERO SET (an exact correspondence stays exact in every frame). Measured over 2000 random draws with κ(H), κ(D₁), κ(D₂) ≤ 100 (pinned, seed and all, in test/transfer_composition_tests.jl), as a relative error against max(|value|, 1):\n\nquantity relation under the composition measured max\ntransferred point π(H x̃₁) commutes: π(H′ b̃₁) = D₂(π(H ã₁)) 8.2e-14\nalgebraic c = x̃₂ × H x̃₁ c′ = (λ₁λ₂ det D₂)·D₂⁻ᵀ c 3.5e-14\ntransfer residual g = π(H x̃₁) − x₂, affine dst g′ = A₂ g (A₂ = D₂'s linear part) 3.2e-13\n\n(The same grid puts the WRONG direction — the observation maps composed uninverted — no closer than 4.5e-2 on the first row.)\n\nPublic, not exported (as TransferForm is).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TransferForm","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"TransferForm{Src, Dst}(H::HomographyMat)\nTransferForm(H::HomographyMat)\n\nThe TRANSFER form of a homography constraint: the residual is measured in the image, g = π(H·ũ₁) − u₂, as against the ALGEBRAIC (cross-multiplied) form that constraint_with_jacobians(H, u₁, u₂) returns and the DLT minimizes.\n\nThe two tags are ENDPOINTS, not slots (see the note above): Src is u₁'s frame, Dst is the frame of u₂ and of the residual. The bare constructor lifts to UnlabeledFrame{2} endpoints, the gradual-typing default used by the Maps and by EpipolarForm.\n\nPublic, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TransferForm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.compose_jacobian-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"compose_jacobian(outer, m, inner) -> SMatrix{9, 9}\n\nThe derivative of the composition outer ∘ m ∘ inner in the model's matrix, ∂vec(M′)/∂vec(M) (column-major vec), for m an EpipolarMap or a HomographyMap{3} and outer/inner collineations (a HomographyMap{3} or a CoordinateTransformations affine). The composition is linear in M, so this is the constant Kronecker factor of its law (_conjugation_jacobian, the one ∂vec(A·M·B)/∂vec(M)), B = inner's matrix and A = outer's:\n\ncorrelation, M′ = A⁻ᵀ·M·B: kron(Bᵀ, A⁻ᵀ);\ncollineation, H′ = A·H·B: kron(Bᵀ, A).\n\nWhat a constraint evaluated on the composed model needs to pull its model gradient back into the model's own coordinates: ∂g/∂vec(M) = Jᵀ·∂g/∂vec(M′) for a scalar constraint's 9-vector, G_θ·J for a 2×9. Frame-checked like ∘; in the promoted element type of the model and the maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.compose_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{EpipolarForm, Any, Any}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(f::EpipolarForm, x₁, x₂) -> (g, G_x, G_θ)\n\nThe epipolar constraint of the wrapped Mat, with the slot convention of EpipolarForm — x₁ feeds slot 1 (frame S1), x₂ slot 2 (frame S2). So a form has everything a constraint gives: sampson_distance, sampson_residual, mahalanobis_distance.\n\nThe delegation is CHECKED where a frame is available: an Uncertain observation whose CovMat/PrecMat carries a frame label must meet the slot it feeds (standing wildcard truth table — a labeled mismatch is a MethodError; plain points and unlabeled matrices are the wildcard), before the Sampson layer strips the covariance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TransferForm, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(f::TransferForm, u₁, u₂) -> (g, G_x, G_θ)\n\nThe transfer constraint of the wrapped homography and its Jacobians: g = π(H·ũ₁) − u₂, the transfer error in the destination image, with G_x = [Jπ·H₁₂  −I₂] (2×4, over (x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂)) and G_θ = Jπ·(ũ₁ᵀ ⊗ I₃) (2×9, ∂g/∂vec(H) column-major), Jπ the perspective derivative at H·ũ₁ and H₁₂ the first two columns of H.\n\nThe ALGEBRAIC counterpart is constraint_with_jacobians(H, u₁, u₂) on the bare Mat — same verb, same return shape, different residual. Which one you get is decided by whether the model is wrapped in a TransferForm, not by a second function name. Generic in the element type; device-safe. Image points only: u₁, u₂ are 2-vectors, and a 3-vector (a bearing) is a MethodError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TransferForm, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(f::TransferForm, f₁, f₂; M₁, M₂) -> (g, G_x, G_θ)\n\nThe transfer constraint of the wrapped homography on UNIT BEARINGS — the infinite homography of a calibrated camera (under a pure rotation H = R): g = P·(H f₁/‖H f₁‖), the transferred ray in the tangent plane of f₂ (P the 2×3 orthonormal basis of s2_tangent_basis(f₂)), zero when f₂ ∥ H f₁ — the constraint f₂ × H f₁ = 0 at codimension 2. Its Jacobians:\n\nG_x = [D·H·M₁  −P·M₂] (2×4) in PIXEL coordinates, M₁/M₂ the 3×2 back-projection Jacobians of backproject_with_jacobian and D = P·(I − ĥĥᵀ)/‖H f₁‖, the derivative of g along the joint observation (both images' pixels), so that at Σ = I (assumed by the rows; detection covariances are a planned extension) the Sampson denominator G_x G_xᵀ = P(H̄M₁M₁ᵀH̄ᵀ + M₂M₂ᵀ)Pᵀ (H̄ the normalized transfer, R for a rotation) is the Sampson covariance in f₂'s tangent plane. The f₂ block is the first-order derivative at ĥ = f₂ (Sampson's linearization at the observation), exact there.\nG_θ = D·(f₁ᵀ ⊗ I₃) (2×9, ∂g/∂vec(H) column-major) — the pixel form's _transfer_params_jacobian with D in the place of Jπ.\n\nThe pixel sibling constraint_with_jacobians(f, u₁, u₂) is the same constraint in the z = 1 chart. An antipodal pair (ĥ·f₂ < 0) also gives a small g; a scorer must refuse it separately (the hemisphere of the transferred ray).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.epipolar_map-Union{Tuple{EpipolarForm{S1, S2, M}}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{S2}, Tuple{S1}} where {S1, S2, M}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"epipolar_map(f::EpipolarForm{S1, S2}) -> EpipolarMap{S1, S2}\n\nThe honest epipolar correlation: a callable taking a point x₁ in slot 1's frame S1 and returning its epipolar LINE in slot 2's frame S2,\n\nℓ₂ = M x̃₁\n\nas a Line2D — VGC's 2D Hyperplane, i.e. the covector of the incidence constraint ℓ₂ᵀ x̃₂ = 0. The returned line is itself a one-slot form (a linear form on S2-frame points), which is why a correlation lands on Line2D and not on a point type: point ↦ line, not point ↦ point.\n\nepipolar_map(transpose(f)) goes the other way (S2 points to S1 lines, via Mᵀ). There is no inverse map — the correlation is rank 2 (see EpipolarForm); the two degenerate directions are exposed by epipoles.\n\nnote: Note\nSince 0.8 this name refers ONLY to the form action. The former epipolar_map(source::PosedCamera, target::PosedCamera, extrinsics) — a unit-depth point-TRANSFER closure, not an epipolar correlation — is renamed depth_transfer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.epipolar_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.epipoles-Tuple{EpipolarForm}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"epipoles(f::EpipolarForm{S1, S2}) -> (e₁, e₂)\n\nThe two kernels of the rank-2 form, labeled by their frames: e₁ is the epipole in slot 1's frame S1 (right kernel, M e₁ ≈ 0) and e₂ the epipole in slot 2's frame S2 (left kernel, Mᵀ e₂ ≈ 0), returned as a NamedTuple (e₁ = ..., e₂ = ...) of homogeneous SVector{3} (unit norm, from the SVD null vectors — homogeneous because an epipole can lie at infinity; dehomogenize with to_euclidean when e[3] ≠ 0).\n\nThese are the degenerate directions that make the correlation one-way: epipolar_map sends e₁ to the zero \"line\", and every returned line goes through e₂ (the pencil property). Computed by SVD of the wrapped matrix; for a noisy or rank-3 matrix the returned vectors are the nearest-kernel directions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.epipoles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/epipolar_forms/#Base.transpose-Tuple{EpipolarForm}","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"transpose(f::EpipolarForm{S1, S2}) -> EpipolarForm{S2, S1}\n\nSwap the slots of the form: wraps Mᵀ, so the constraint reads x₁ᵀ Mᵀ x₂ = 0 — the same incidence relation with the argument order exchanged. An involution (transpose(transpose(f)) recovers f), and the ONLY inversion-like operation the form supports — the rank-2 correlation has no inv (see EpipolarForm). The wrapped Mat type is preserved: the transpose of a fundamental matrix is fundamental, and Eᵀ remains on the essential manifold.\n\nadjoint (f') is the same operation (real coefficients).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.transpose"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#Back-projection-and-Undistortion","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"Every lens answers two questions, and only two: which image point a ray lands on, and which ray is behind an image point. project(lens, X) and backproject(lens, x_d) are that contract, on rays — so a fisheye that images 190° answers for every ray it images, past 90° included. Everything else is derived from the pair, for every lens, with no per-lens code.","title":"Back-projection and Undistortion"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#The-contract","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"project(lens, X::StaticVector{3}) -> Vec2 — the normalized image point of the ray X (any positive multiple of it).\nbackproject(lens, x_d::StaticVector{2}) -> Vec3 — the UNIT ray behind the normalized image point x_d.\nBoth return NaN where the lens does not image (past a fold, outside a model's field of view), never throw, never allocate, and are generic in the number type: Float32 for GPU kernels, ForwardDiff Duals for Jacobians in the point and in the lens parameters.\nA DoubleSphere lens's domain is the fold of its radial profile, in closed form: the rays out to the angle where the image radius stops increasing (a fold) or runs to infinity (a pole) — wider than the paper's conservative field-of-view bound (ξ = −0.6, α = 0.6: 105.2° against 95.1°), and never past the fold, where two rays would share a point. project and backproject invert each other on the whole of it.\n\nA Lens — a projection plus a distortion in the projection's frame — gets both maps from one generic body; a LiftLens (the division family, the rational lift, Scaramuzza's polynomial) lifts the image point to its ray in closed form and projects by a root on the ray. See Lens Families.\n\nA downstream lens type that defines only project gets a default backproject: Newton's method over the equidistant chart of the ray (so past 90°), a fixed 30 trips from the pinhole ray, NaN unless it converged where the map keeps orientation.","title":"The contract"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#Derived-for-every-lens","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"Perspective views. distort_normalized(lens, x) = project(lens, (x, 1)) — also the functor lens(x) — and undistort_normalized(lens, x_d), the perspective point of backproject(lens, x_d): NaN where that ray has Z ≤ 0, which no perspective image contains. inv(lens) is an InverseLens whose call is undistort_normalized.\nJacobians. transform_deriv(lens, X), transform_deriv_params(lens, X) and project_with_jacobians(lens, X) on rays; transform_deriv(lens, x) and transform_deriv(inv(lens), x_d) on the perspective views — ForwardDiff through the maps, exact at the solved roots.\nRadial profile. projected_radius(lens, θ) and view_angle(lens, ρ).\nCameras. project(model, X), backproject(model, u), unproject(model, u, depth) (Z-depth: NaN where the ray has Z ≤ 0; a point at distance d along any ray is d · backproject(model, u)), and imaging_transform(model) = affine_part(K) ∘ lens, the lens applied to the ray.","title":"Derived for every lens"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#Inside-the-maps","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"The inverse of an in-plane distortion is a solve, matched to the model's structure and private to the maps:\n\ndistortion solve\na lone Radial factor (Kannala–Brandt, COLMAP RADIAL, …), any frame Newton on the ratio g = ρ/ρ_d — no square root, exact on the axis — safeguarded on the factor's branch\nan Additive led by a Radial (OpenCV's models, COLMAP's thin-prism fisheye) OpenCV's fixed point x ← (x_d − ΣΔ(x))/F(r²), finished by trust-region Newton where it stops contracting\nan Additive of deltas, a lone delta Picard peel x ← x_d − ΣΔ(x), trust-region Newton if it does not certify\nFieldOfView, Tilt closed form\na composition (∘ of stages), anything else trust-region 2-D Newton\na LiftLens project: the root of ρ·N·z − r·D on the ray, nothing divided by z; backproject: closed form\nthe parametric projections closed forms both ways, with the papers' validity bounds\n\nEvery solve stops by its convergence order — a Newton step at the solve tolerance of the iterate's scale, a contraction's update at rounding level — under a trip cap, and is certified (residual, and the branch where it matters); a solve that does not certify is NaN. For Dual inputs the derivative comes from one Newton step at the converged primal root (the implicit function theorem), so first derivatives are exact.","title":"Inside the maps"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#Folds-and-the-imaged-branch","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"A distortion that folds gives one image point several preimages. The lens images the imaged branch: the region connected to the axis where the map keeps orientation (det J > 0) and every Radial factor is before its fold or pole. backproject returns the preimage on it, or NaN. The two directions do not test the same things:\n\nproject NaNs a ray past a radial factor's fold, every radial factor at its own input (a lone one, an Additive's head, each radial stage of a composition). It does not test the determinant, which would cost a Jacobian per projected point. So where the deltas of an Additive (tangential, thin prism) or a composition's Tilt fold the map, a ray past that fold projects to a finite point, and backproject of that point is the branch's preimage — a different ray — or NaN. The round trip backproject(lens, project(lens, X)) ≈ X/‖X‖ holds on the imaged branch, not past such a fold. An image undistortion samples through project, so it repeats branch content there instead of filling.\nThe branch test is pointwise. Where the map overlaps itself inside the axis-connected component, a point has several preimages on the branch and backproject returns the first one its solver reaches. This is rare (0.05 % of the interior points of deliberately strong OpenCV chains), but a per-pixel undistortion map can jump between preimages there. Whether a lens is one-to-one over a sensor is a property of the lens: it belongs in an optional check of the lens over the sensor rectangle, not in a per-pixel test.","title":"Folds and the imaged branch"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#Batches","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"A batch of points goes through the same per-point maps by broadcasting: project!(u, lens, X), backproject!, distort_normalized! and undistort_normalized! are pointwise! of project, backproject, …: a SIMD loop on a host array (split across threads above a measured length, or as the exec policy says) and one compiled kernel on a GPU array. A camera is a per-point map too: project!(u, camera, X). A point the lens does not image comes back NaN. See Batches.","title":"Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#Adding-a-lens-model","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"A new term or projection is a leaf: its coefficients as a tuple, how to rebuild it from one, their names, its record tag, and its map — a term's _distort (or _delta, with isdelta), a projection's _project_ray / _lift_point. Everything above follows. A lens of a new kind implements project(lens, X) and backproject(lens, x_d) directly.","title":"Adding a lens model"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/undistortion/#See-also","page":"Back-projection and Undistortion","text":"Lens Families — the lens kinds, projections, terms and records\nBatches — one per-point function, every backend\nThe Framed Imaging Chain — the framed, direction-checked stage wrappers\nLenses API","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/intrinsics/#Intrinsics-and-Sensors-API","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"A camera's intrinsics (the logical, projective and pixel forms) and its sensor. The cameras that hold them are on the Cameras API page.","title":"Intrinsics and Sensors API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/intrinsics/#Index","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"Pages = [\"intrinsics.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"CameraCalibrationMat{T} <: StaticMatrix{3, 3, T}\n\nThe pinhole calibration matrix\n\n[fx  s  cx]\n[ 0 fy  cy]\n[ 0  0   1]\n\nstored as its five parameters (fx, fy, s, cx, cy): indexing returns the full matrix, its structural entries exactly 0 and 1. Every constructor from a 3×3 matrix checks that structure (row 3 = 0 0 1 and K[2, 1] = 0, within atol = 1e-6; the transpose named as the likely fix) — the storage cannot hold anything else; check = false skips only the positive-focal check. Arithmetic on it gives an SMatrix; inv(K) is analytic, an SMatrix with exact structural zeros (K⁻¹ is not a calibration matrix). Isbits and eltype-generic (Float32, ForwardDiff Dual).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"CameraCalibrationMat(f::Quantity, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}) -> CameraCalibrationMat{Float64}\nCameraCalibrationMat(f::Tuple{T,T}, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}) where T<:Quantity -> CameraCalibrationMat{Float64}\n\nConstruct camera intrinsics matrix K from focal length(s) and principal point in pixel units.\n\nUnits are enforced at construction but stored internally as unitless Float64 for performance. A pixel is dimensionless, so f and pp are dimensionless quantities (800.0px); a length (16.0mm, a metric focal length without its pitch) is a DimensionError.\n\nArguments\n\nf: Focal length in pixels - either scalar for isotropic or tuple (fx, fy) for anisotropic\npp: Principal point in pixels as 2D vector [cx, cy]\n\nReturns\n\n3×3 unitless Float64 camera intrinsics matrix in column-major order:\n\n[fx  0  cx]\n[ 0 fy  cy]\n[ 0  0   1]\n\nExamples\n\n# Isotropic focal length (fx = fy)\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\n\n# Anisotropic focal length (fx ≠ fy)\nK = CameraCalibrationMat((800.0px, 805.0px), [320.0px, 240.0px])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity, Size2{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"CameraCalibrationMat(f::Quantity, pitch::Size2{<:Quantity}, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}) -> CameraCalibrationMat{Float64}\n\nConstruct camera intrinsics matrix K from physical focal length, pixel pitch, and principal point.\n\nUnits are enforced at construction but stored internally as unitless Float64 for performance.\n\nThis constructor converts from metric units to pixel units using the factorization:\n\nK = [1  0  cx] [1/px   0   0] [f  0  0]\n    [0  1  cy] [0   1/py  0] [0  f  0]\n    [0  0   1] [0    0    1] [0  0  1]\n\nwhere f is in mm, px/py are pixel pitch in mm/px, and (cx,cy) are in pixels.\n\nArguments\n\nf::Quantity: Focal length in physical units (e.g., mm)\npitch::Size2{<:Quantity}: Pixel pitch (e.g., Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px))\npp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}: Principal point in pixels [cx, cy]\n\nReturns\n\n3×3 unitless Float64 camera intrinsics matrix with focal length in pixels:\n\n[αx  0  cx]\n[ 0  αy cy]   where αx = f/px, αy = f/py\n[ 0  0   1]\n\nExamples\n\n# From sensor datasheet and lens specification\nf = 16.0mm                                    # Lens focal length\npitch = Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px)  # Sensor pixel pitch\npp = [320.0px, 240.0px]                       # Principal point (image center)\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(f, pitch, pp)\n# K[1,1] ≈ 2730.38 (focal length in pixels)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat-Union{Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Real","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"CameraCalibrationMat(M::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}; check=true) -> CameraCalibrationMat\n\nConstruct from a 3×3 matrix (e.g., from OpenCV calibration JSON).\n\nValues are stored unitless (COLMAP convention: pixel centers at 0.5). An integer, rational or irrational matrix is promoted to Float64; any other concrete real element type is kept, so a matrix of ForwardDiff Duals gives a Dual calibration matrix that automatic differentiation runs through. The pinhole structure (upper-triangular, last row [0, 0, 1]) is always checked, and names the transpose when the rows are swapped; check adds the positive focals.\n\nExample\n\n# From OpenCV JSON (row-major nested arrays)\nKm = [[800, 0, 320], [0, 800, 240], [0, 0, 1]]\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(stack(Km)')\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat-Union{Tuple{LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Real","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"CameraCalibrationMat(s::LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling)\n    -> CameraCalibrationMat\n\nIdentity shortcut: CameraCalibrationMat(I) returns CameraCalibrationMat{Float64} with K = SMatrix{3,3,Float64}(I).  Supply Float32(1)*I to choose the eltype. Only the identity: α·I with α ≠ 1 has K₃₃ = α, not a calibration matrix's structure (ArgumentError; the calibration of focal α is CameraCalibrationMat(Diagonal(SVector(α, α, 1)))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"LogicalIntrinsics{T}\n\nCamera intrinsics containing only the calibration matrix K (dimensionless), parametric in storage element type T <: Real. Defaults to Float64 for all existing call sites; supply a Float32 CameraCalibrationMat to get single-precision storage end-to-end (useful for GPU pipelines).\n\nUsed when physical parameters cannot be disentangled from K, such as when K is recovered from autocalibration, Zhang's method, or other calibration techniques that don't provide metric information about the sensor.\n\nFields\n\nK::CameraCalibrationMat{T}: 3×3 calibration matrix\nK_inv::SMatrix{3,3,T,9}: Precomputed inverse of K for efficient backprojection\n\nExamples\n\n# From autocalibration (defaults to Float64 storage)\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\nintrinsics = LogicalIntrinsics(K)              # LogicalIntrinsics{Float64}\n\n# Float32 path for GPU pipelines\nK32 = CameraCalibrationMat(Float32[1 0 0; 0 1 0; 0 0 1])\nintrinsics32 = LogicalIntrinsics(K32)          # LogicalIntrinsics{Float32}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"LogicalIntrinsics(f::Quantity, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}) -> LogicalIntrinsics\n\nConvenience constructor for LogicalIntrinsics from focal length and principal point (both in pixels).\n\nThis constructor builds the calibration matrix K internally. Use this when you have calibrated intrinsics but no physical sensor parameters.\n\nExample\n\nlogical = LogicalIntrinsics(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity, Size2{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"LogicalIntrinsics(f::Quantity, pitch::Size2{<:Quantity}, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}) -> LogicalIntrinsics\n\nPhysical-spec construction: build the calibration matrix from a metric focal length and pixel pitch, then store ONLY K.  The physical scale is construction-boundary metadata — it belongs to the Sensor record, not the intrinsics (the former PhysicalIntrinsics carried it redundantly and doubled the camera dispatch table; demoted 2026-07, audit #15).\n\nExample\n\nf = 16.0mm\npitch = Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px)\npp = [320.0px, 240.0px]\nintrinsics = LogicalIntrinsics(f, pitch, pp)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tuple{T, T}, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}} where T<:FlexUnits.Quantity","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"LogicalIntrinsics(f::Tuple{T,T}, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}) where T<:Quantity -> LogicalIntrinsics\n\nConvenience constructor for LogicalIntrinsics with anisotropic focal lengths (fx ≠ fy).\n\nExample\n\n# Different focal lengths in x and y\nlogical = LogicalIntrinsics((800.0px, 805.0px), [320.0px, 240.0px])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogicalIntrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length-Tuple{LogicalIntrinsics}","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"focal_length(intrinsics::LogicalIntrinsics) -> Tuple{Float64, Float64}\n\nGet focal lengths (fx, fy) from LogicalIntrinsics K matrix (unitless, in pixels).\n\nReturns a tuple (fx, fy) extracted from the calibration matrix K. For square pixels, fx ≈ fy.\n\nThe other forms answer the focal length a FULL field of view θ implies: focal_length(resolution::Size2, θ; dimension = :horizontal) in pixels and focal_length(sensor::Sensor, θ; dimension = :horizontal) as a length (see their docstrings); angle_of_view is their inverse for a camera.\n\nExample\n\nintrinsics = LogicalIntrinsics(K)\nfx, fy = focal_length(intrinsics)  # Returns (Float64, Float64) in pixels\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length"},{"category":"type","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ProjectiveIntrinsics","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"ProjectiveIntrinsics{T <: Real}(center)\nProjectiveIntrinsics(center)\n\nThe intrinsics of a projective (\"partially calibrated\") camera: the principal point center (Point2, COLMAP pixels) and nothing else. Its ray frame is PROJECTIVE: centred pixels u − center, defined only up to the plane homography that absorbs the focal length. There is no focal-length field, and none is implied — in particular it is not f = 1. Anything that needs a metric ray has no method and raises a MethodError (focal_length, calibration_matrix, unproject, P3P, absolute pose), except the field of view, which is refused with an ArgumentError: angle_of_view and fov_radius, and a finite max_fov in undistorted_rect (use max_fov = Inf).\n\nCameraModel(ProjectiveIntrinsics(c), lens) projects, back-projects and undistorts exactly as CameraModel(LogicalIntrinsics(K), lens) with the unit focal K = [1 0 cx; 0 1 cy; 0 0 1] does: both go through the same code, which reads the pixel matrix at _K / _K_inv.\n\nProjectiveIntrinsics{T}(center) stores the centre in T (a floating-point or AD eltype; an integer or rational T is an ArgumentError); ProjectiveIntrinsics(center) takes T from center (integers become Float64). See principal_point, parameter_names ((:cx, :cy)), convert_eltype and convert_frame.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> ProjectiveIntrinsics(Point2(2120.0, 1416.0))\nProjectiveIntrinsics(cx = 2120.0, cy = 1416.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ProjectiveIntrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.principal_point-Tuple{ProjectiveIntrinsics}","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"principal_point(intrinsics::ProjectiveIntrinsics{T}) -> Point2{T}\nprincipal_point(intrinsics::LogicalIntrinsics{T}) -> Point2{T}\n\nThe principal point, in COLMAP pixels: a projective camera's center, a logical camera's (K[1, 3], K[2, 3]) — the one accessor for both kinds of intrinsics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.principal_point"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CMOS_SENSORS","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"CMOS_SENSORS\n\nDatabase of common CMOS image sensors indexed by manufacturer and model.\n\nCMOS_SENSORS[\"Sony\"][\"IMX174\"]      # 1920×1200 @ 5.86μm\nCMOS_SENSORS[\"Sony\"][\"IMX250\"]      # 2448×2048 @ 3.45μm\nCMOS_SENSORS[\"OmniVision\"][\"OV9281\"] # 1280×800 @ 3.0μm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CMOS_SENSORS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Sensor","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"Sensor{TR, TP}\n\nImage sensor specification with resolution and pixel pitch.\n\nFields\n\nresolution::Size2{TR}: Pixel count (width × height in px; dimensionless, a length is a DimensionError)\npitch::Size2{TP}: Pixel pitch (physical size per pixel, e.g., μm/px; a length, px being dimensionless)\n\nConstructors\n\nSensor(Size2(width=1920px, height=1200px), 5.86μm)\nSensor(Size2(width=1920, height=1200), 5.86μm)  # Integers → px\n\nSee Also\n\nCMOS_SENSORS: Pre-defined sensor database\naspect_ratio\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Sensor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/intrinsics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sensor-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Intrinsics & Sensors","text":"sensor(manufacturer, model) -> Sensor\n\nLook up a sensor from the CMOS_SENSORS database.\n\nsensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")  # equivalent to CMOS_SENSORS[\"Sony\"][\"IMX174\"]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sensor"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Geometric-Primitives","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore provides 2D geometric primitives with homogeneous matrix representations.","title":"Geometric Primitives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Points-and-Rectangles","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"","title":"Points and Rectangles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Points","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"using GeometryBasics\n\n# 2D points (from GeometryBasics, re-exported)\np = Point2(100.0, 150.0)\np = Point2f(100.0, 150.0)  # Float32 version\n\n# 3D points\np3d = Point3(100.0, 50.0, 0.0)","title":"Points"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Rectangles","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"# Create rectangle from origin and size\norigin = Point2(10.0, 20.0)\nsize = Vec2(100.0, 50.0)\nrect = Rect(origin, size)\n\n# Or from intervals\nusing IntervalSets\nx_range = ClosedInterval(10.0, 110.0)\ny_range = ClosedInterval(20.0, 70.0)\nrect = Rect(x_range, y_range)\n\n# Utility functions\nc = centroid(rect)         # Get center point","title":"Rectangles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Circles","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"# Create circle\ncenter = Point2(100.0, 150.0)\nradius = 25.0\ncircle = Circle(center, radius)\n\n# Scale (dilate) circle\nlarger_circle = dilate(circle, 2.0)  # 2× larger radius, same center","title":"Circles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Homogeneous-Circle-Matrix","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"Circles can be represented as 3×3 symmetric matrices:\n\n# Convert circle to matrix form\nQ = HomCircleMat(circle)\n\n# Transform circle with HomographyMap\nH = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\nQ′ = H(circle)       # a Conic; Ellipse(Q′) once classify_conic(Q′) isa EllipseTrait\n\n# Or work with conic matrices directly via matrix multiplication\nH_mat = SMatrix{3,3}(HomographyMat(camera))\nQ_transformed = inv(H_mat)' * SMatrix{3,3}(Q) * inv(H_mat)","title":"Homogeneous Circle Matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Ellipses","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"# Create ellipse\ncenter = Point2(100.0, 150.0)\na = 30.0  # Semi-major axis\nb = 20.0  # Semi-minor axis\nθ = π/4   # Rotation angle\nellipse = Ellipse(center, a, b, θ)\n\n# From a homogeneous matrix of unknown class: a Conic (no class check), then\n# classify — HomEllipseMat(M) itself throws for anything but a real ellipse\nQ = Conic(M)\nif is_ellipse(Q)\n    ellipse = Ellipse(Q)\nend\n\n# Dilate ellipse\nlarger_ellipse = dilate(ellipse, 2.0)  # 2× larger, same center","title":"Ellipses"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Ellipse-Properties","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"c = centroid(ellipse)      # Center point\ngradient(ellipse, point)   # Gradient at a point on the ellipse","title":"Ellipse Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Geometric-Queries","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"","title":"Geometric Queries"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Containment-and-Intersection","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"# Check if point is inside a shape\npoint ∈ circle    # Returns true/false\npoint in ellipse  # Same using 'in' operator\n\n# Check intersection between shapes\nintersects(circle1, circle2)\nintersects(ellipse, rect)\nintersects(rect1, rect2)","title":"Containment and Intersection"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Conic-Classification","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"# Check if a conic matrix represents an ellipse (a Conic: any class; a\n# HomEllipseMat is one by construction, its checked constructor refuses the rest)\nQ = Conic(M)\nis_ellipse(Q)  # a real, non-degenerate ellipse?\n\n# Classify the conic\ntrait = classify_conic(Q)\n# For a conic matrix: EllipseTrait(), ParabolaTrait(), HyperbolaTrait() or\n# DegenerateTrait(); CircleTrait() comes from classify_conic(::Circle)","title":"Conic Classification"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Transformations","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"","title":"Transformations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Homogeneous-Representations","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"All primitives can be converted to homogeneous matrix form:\n\n# Circles → 3×3 symmetric matrix\ncircle = Circle(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 10.0)\nQ_circle = HomCircleMat(circle)\n\n# Ellipses → 3×3 symmetric matrix\nellipse = Ellipse(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 20.0, 10.0, 0.0)\nQ_ellipse = HomEllipseMat(ellipse)","title":"Homogeneous Representations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Transforming-Shapes","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"Use HomographyMap to transform shapes:\n\n# Create homography and wrap for callable syntax\nH_mat = HomographyMat(camera)\nH = HomographyMap(H_mat)\n\n# Transform circles. A general homography returns a `Conic` — an ellipse\n# for a circle in view, a hyperbola for one crossing the line at infinity.\nQ = H(circle)\nif classify_conic(Q) isa EllipseTrait\n    ellipse = Ellipse(Q)  # valid ellipse in view\nend","title":"Transforming Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Affine-Transforms","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"using Rotations: RotMatrix\nusing StaticArrays: SVector\n\n# A similarity: 2× scaling, a 45° rotation, a translation (x ↦ s·R·x + t)\nS = SimilarityMap(2.0, RotMatrix{2}(π/4), SVector(10.0, 20.0))\n\n# Apply to shapes - a similarity preserves circles\ntransformed_circle = S(circle)  # Returns Circle (radius doubled)","title":"Affine Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Working-with-Units","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"Unit constants (mm, μm, px, dpi, …) and the quantity machinery are exported from the main module (FlexUnits-based; see Getting Started). Geometric storage is unitless (lengths in mm, pixels in px); units are enforced at API boundaries (e.g. sensor pitches, focal lengths, metric tolerances) by stripping in a named unit (ustrip(mm, q)) or comparing dimension(q) == dimension(mm). A pixel is dimensionless, so a pixel-sized parameter can refuse a length but not an angle.","title":"Working with Units"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#Feature-Frames","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"The transformation-based feature primitives — Disc (center + σ), OrientedDisc (+ orientation), EllipticFrame (+ aspect/shape), AffineFrame — represent detections geometrically; each defines AffineMap(frame) mapping canonical coordinates to its image-space support. Paraboloid3D is the quadratic surface used for refinement fits. The detectors that produce them live in VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.","title":"Feature Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/primitives/#See-Also","page":"Geometric Primitives","text":"Primitives API Reference\nTransforms Guide - Transforming primitives\nCameras Guide - Projecting primitives with cameras","title":"See Also"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#Lens-Families","page":"Lens Families","text":"Every lens is one of two kinds, built from a handful of parts. The parts — projections and terms — are leaves, structs of numbers and tuples of numbers; everything a lens is asked (its coefficients, their names, its zero, its record, its manifold, every Jacobian, every batch) is derived from their fields by one walk, never tabulated per model. A coefficient is named by its field — a tuple field k as k1, k2, … — and a leaf's record tag is its name in snake case (DoubleSphere → double_sphere, FieldOfView → field_of_view; the Full radial basis is radial_full).","title":"Lens Families"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#Two-kinds-of-lens","page":"Lens Families","text":"Lens(projection, distortion) — forward: the ray is projected onto the projection's frame, and the frame point is distorted there, x_d = distortion(projection(X)). Projection is closed form; back-projection inverts the distortion.\nLiftLens(radial) — backward: the image point lifts to its ray in closed form, (x_d·N(ρ), D(ρ)); projection is a root on the ray. D crossing zero is the 90° circle and D < 0 the field behind it.","title":"Two kinds of lens"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#Projections","page":"Lens Families","text":"projection radius frame\nPerspective r = tan θ GnomonicFrame\nEquidistant r = θ EquidistantFrame\nStereographic r = 2 tan(θ/2) StereographicFrame\nEquisolid r = 2 sin(θ/2) EquisolidFrame\nOrthographic r = sin θ OrthographicFrame\nDoubleSphere(xi, alpha) Usenko et al. (2018) model-defined\nUnified(xi) Mei and Rives (2007) (Kalibr omni) model-defined\nExtendedUnified(alpha, beta) Khomutenko et al. (2016) (Kalibr eucm) model-defined\n\nLens(projection) alone is the pure projection; Pinhole is Lens(Perspective()).\n\nThe whole sphere of directions onto the (longitude, latitude) rectangle — a panorama raster, a sky map — is Equirectangular: not a lens (it has no radial profile), a projection of its own with project/backproject. Its cylindrical family shares the longitude and changes the vertical coordinate: Cylindrical (tan φ, the central projection onto the unit cylinder), CylindricalEqualArea (sin φ: equal raster areas are equal solid angles) and Mercator (asinh(tan φ), conformal). Each is a Transformation whose inv is its Backprojection, so a panorama raster's pixel → ray map composes into an image_warp chain; an azimuthal view needs no type of its own — it is Backprojection(camera) of a virtual camera with the view's lens (Lens(Perspective()), Lens(Equidistant()), Lens(Stereographic())).","title":"Projections"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#Terms-and-how-they-combine","page":"Lens Families","text":"term kind function of the frame point x, r² = ‖x‖² names\nRadial(k, d) map x·N/D, N = 1 + Σ kᵢ r²ⁱ, D = 1 + Σ dⱼ r²ʲ k1…, d1…\nTangential(p1, p2) delta (2p₁uv + p₂(r² + 2u²), 2p₂uv + p₁(r² + 2v²)) p1, p2\nThinPrism(sx, sy) delta (Σ sxᵢ r²ⁱ, Σ syᵢ r²ⁱ) sx1…, sy1…\nTilt(tx, ty) map OpenCV's tilted-sensor projective map tx, ty\nFieldOfView(omega) map x·atan(2r tan(ω/2))/(rω) omega\n\nThe arity of a term is its tuple lengths — a type parameter — so a four-coefficient Kannala–Brandt and a six-coefficient one are the same family. Radial{Full} uses the powers ρ², ρ³, … instead of ρ², ρ⁴, … (Scaramuzza's lift).\n\nAdditive(m, Δ₁, …) — every term at the same point, summed: m(x) + (Δ₁(x) + …), the deltas left to right. OpenCV's projectPoints and COLMAP's Distortion() are of this form.\nCoordinateTransformations' ∘ — in sequence, (D₂ ∘ D₁)(x) = D₂(D₁(x)) (a term is a Transformation): Aria's Fisheye624 (COLMAP RAD_TAN_THIN_PRISM_FISHEYE) applies its tangential and thin-prism terms to the radially distorted point, Additive(Tangential(…), ThinPrism(…)) ∘ Radial(…); OpenCV's tilt follows its additive model, Tilt(τx, τy) ∘ Additive(…). Each stage is a map term or an Additive. A composition's coefficients are in CT's order, the outer stage's first, and its record lists the stages the same way (\"compose\", the outer first; the retired \"chain\", which listed them in application order, is refused).\n\nWhether a lens is radially symmetric about the principal point — what RadialCamera(::PosedCamera) needs — is the trait RadialSymmetry, read off the lens's type: the projections, Radial, FieldOfView and LiftLens are, and an Additive, a ∘ composition or a Lens when every piece is. It fails closed: a lens type defined downstream is asymmetric until it declares itself, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialSymmetry(::Type{MyLens}) = VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadiallySymmetric().","title":"Terms and how they combine"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#Familiar-models","page":"Lens Families","text":"model lens\npinhole Pinhole() = Lens(Perspective())\nOpenCV 4/5/8/12 coefficients Lens(Perspective(), Additive(Radial((k1, k2, k3), (k4, k5, k6)), Tangential(p1, p2), ThinPrism((s1, s2), (s3, s4)))) — only the terms in use\nOpenCV 14 (tilted) Lens(Perspective(), Tilt(τx, τy) ∘ Additive(…))\nOpenCV fisheye, COLMAP OPENCV_FISHEYE KannalaBrandt(k1, k2, k3, k4) = Lens(Equidistant(), Radial((k1, k2, k3, k4)))\nCOLMAP SIMPLE_RADIAL, RADIAL Lens(Perspective(), Radial((k,))), Radial((k1, k2))\nCOLMAP THIN_PRISM_FISHEYE Lens(Equidistant(), Additive(Radial((k1, …, k4)), Tangential(p1, p2), ThinPrism((sx1,), (sy1,))))\nCOLMAP FOV Lens(Perspective(), FieldOfView(ω))\ndivision (Fitzgibbon), COLMAP DIVISION Division(k…) = LiftLens(Radial((), (k…)))\nBabelCalib rational LiftLens(Radial((a…), (b…)))\nScaramuzza / MATLAB fisheye Scaramuzza(a2, a3, a4) = LiftLens(Radial{Full}((), (a2, a3, a4)))\ndouble sphere (Kalibr ds) Lens(DoubleSphere(ξ, α))\nKalibr omni-radtan Lens(Unified(ξ), Additive(Radial((k1, k2)), Tangential(r1, r2)))\n\nThe adapters that read and write these conventions — and reproduce each convention's function exactly — live in VisualGeometryToolkitIO.","title":"Familiar models"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#Coefficients","page":"Lens Families","text":"parameter_vector(lens) is the coefficient vector — projection first, then the distortion's terms depth-first, left to right; parameter_names names it, parameter_bounds states its domain (unbounded, but the double sphere's ξ > −1 and α ∈ [0, 1], the unified models' ξ > −1, α ∈ [0, 1] and β ≥ 0, the field-of-view ω ∈ [0, π)), parameters pairs the names and values in a NamedTuple, and with_parameters rebuilds the same structure from a vector of any number type. The same functions describe a calibratable target, its TargetCalibration, a LogicalIntrinsics (f, aspect, skew, cx, cy, with f, aspect > 0) and a parametric CameraModel — the intrinsics' coordinates followed by the lens's. ParameterManifold (a lens's LensManifold, minimal_manifold(lens) or minimal_manifold(camera)) moves the coefficients inside their bounds — a log for a one-sided bound, a scaled logit for an interval — and restrict(ch, names) frees a subset by name: a camera's restrict(minimal_manifold(camera), (:f, :cx, :cy, :k1)) fixes its skew and aspect. An optimizer's search box is not a model fact; it stays the solver's option, keyed by parameter_names.\n\nA caller holds parameters with ONE keyword, fixed: the parameters held, by name or by group (:k for k1, k2, …), () by default (everything is refined; a camera's skew, the gauge, is held by construction, so naming it changes nothing). LensSpec(family; fixed) and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's refine_camera_and_poses take it, and free_parameter_names resolves it to the names that stay free — free_parameter_names((:f, :aspect, :skew, :cx, :cy, :k1, :k2), (:aspect, :k)) is (:f, :cx, :cy). A name or group that names nothing of the model, or a parameter named twice, is an ArgumentError, and so is the retired free (the complement), naming fixed; the refines refuse fix, fixed_params and hold alike.\n\nradial(lens) is the lens's radially symmetric factor — its folds and poles bound what the lens images (view_angle_limit, monotone_radius) — and radial_part the lens with only that factor.","title":"Coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#The-valid-domain-and-the-image-circle","page":"Lens Families","text":"Two regions of the sensor, easy to confuse and never interchangeable:\n\nvalid_domain(camera) is where the lens MODEL is valid: the image of the cone of view_angle_limit through the radial projection and K, inside which the lens's radial profile is finite and strictly increasing. It is a property of the formula and says nothing about light: a fitted Kannala–Brandt profile can fold far outside the lit disc.\nimage_circle(camera, diameter) is where light falls, from a lens datasheet's image-circle diameter through the sensor's pitch. A circle measured from images serves equally; it is never predicted from the model.\n\nBoth are an Ellipse in pixels about the principal point (an ellipse proper for an anisotropic K or pitch), so the region algebra treats them alike.\n\nFor a lens that is not radially symmetric (a composed distortion with a tilt stage, or tangential terms) the valid domain is an inscribed bound.\nA lens with no limit of its own (a perspective lens, an undistorted equidistant lens: view_angle_limit reports only its chart's or the sphere's edge, a cap) has the WholePlane as its valid domain, the identity of ∩.\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore: Pinhole, KannalaBrandt\ns = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX250\")                            # 2448 × 2048 @ 3.45 μm\nk = -4 / (3π^2)                                         # r = θ(1 + kθ²) folds at 90°, r = (2/3)θ\nf = ustrip(px, 6.0mm / (2 * s.pitch.width)) / (π / 3)   # a 180° lens drawing a 6 mm circle\ncam = BoundedCamera(CameraModel(; focal = f, pp = (1224.0, 1024.0), lens = KannalaBrandt(k)), s)\nv = valid_domain(cam)                                   # where the lens formula holds\n(centroid(v), v.a, v.b)\n([1224.0, 1024.0], 869.5652165217391, 869.5652165217391)\n\nq = image_circle(cam, 6.0mm)                            # where light falls: the datasheet, through the pitch\n(v.a - q.a, Rect(extent(cam)) ∈ q, rad2deg(angle_of_view(cam)), rad2deg(angle_of_view(cam; dimension = :diagonal)))\n(-8.695652695678291e-7, false, 179.9999998199235, 179.9999998199235)\n\nHere the lens was chosen so the two coincide; for a fitted lens they need not. angle_of_view is the angle of view the camera images — its extent bounded by its lens coverage, clipped by the model's valid domain; field_of_view is the size that angle spans at a distance.\n\nrcam = BoundedCamera(CameraModel(; focal = 2000.0, pp = (1224.0, 1024.0), lens = Pinhole()), s)\n(valid_domain(rcam), rad2deg(angle_of_view(rcam)), rad2deg(angle_of_view(rcam; dimension = :diagonal)))\n(VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.WholePlane(), 62.933264526160734, 77.17464549362784)\n\nA local lens model — a grid of nodes rather than a formula — has observations only where light falls: the camera's imaged_region, the sensor ∩ its lens coverage (here the datasheet's circle) ∩ where the lens model is valid:\n\ndomain = imaged_region(with_lens_coverage(cam, q))         # a grid lens's domain\nnodes = [Point2(x, y) for x in range(0.0, 2448.0; length = 25), y in range(0.0, 2048.0; length = 21)]\nlit = filter(n -> n ∈ domain, nodes)\n(length(lit), length(nodes) - length(lit))              # lit, outside\n(225, 300)\n\nThe whole example, runnable, is examples/image_circle.jl.","title":"The valid domain and the image circle"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#Which-parameters-a-camera-can-see","page":"Lens Families","text":"A camera images a region of its sensor, imaged_region(camera) = Rect(extent) ∩ lens_coverage(camera) ∩ valid_domain(camera), its lens coverage (the image circle it carries, lens_coverage); pixel_mask(region, width, height) rasterizes it, the pixels whose centres lie in it. Each parameter of a model has a support on the sensor; it is free when its support meets the imaged region and frozen otherwise. A global model (a parametric lens, K) has the whole plane as every parameter's support, so free_nodes answers Colon() (all of them); a node model such as a SplineLens camera is LocalSupport and only the nodes whose footprint meets the lens coverage are free.\n\nfish = CameraModel(; focal = 250.0, pp = (640.0, 480.0), lens = KannalaBrandt(k))\ng = CameraModel(SplineLens, fish, Size2(1280, 960); grid_size = (20, 16))\ncircle = Ellipse(Point2(640.0, 480.0), 250.0, 250.0, 0.0)   # a measured image circle, 250 px\nR = imaged_region(BoundedCamera(fish, Size2(1280, 960); lens_coverage = circle))\nmask = node_mask(g, R)                                  # in the grid's shape\nlit = pixel_mask(R, 1280, 960)                          # the pixels it images: 960 × 1280, ≈ π · 250² of them\n(size(mask), count(mask), length(mask) - count(mask), free_nodes(fish, R), count(lit))   # free, frozen; a lens: all\n((16, 20), 96, 224, Colon(), 196364)\n\nA lattice model declares its supports through two contract generics: support_trait(::Type{<:MyLattice}) = LocalSupport(), then VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nodes(m) (the node ids, CartesianIndices of the lattice) and VisualGeometryToolkitCore.support(m, i) (the node's region on the sensor in pixels — for a uniform cubic B-spline lattice cubic_bspline_footprint(center, spacing)); free_nodes and node_mask then work unchanged.","title":"Which parameters a camera can see"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#A-spline-lens-in-a-refinement","page":"Lens Families","text":"A refinement of a SplineLens camera reads it through a few primitives of its lattice. The controls of a lens may be kept off the sphere (unit_controls = false): backproject normalizes the spline value, so a control's norm is an interpolation weight, not a gauge — an optimizer stepping in each control's tangent plane (direction_tangents) leaves them there. The lens's backward is the raw spline, a ChartSpline on the normalized plane x = K⁻¹u (generic in the element type, Float32 on a device), continued past its area by the outermost cells' cubic pieces; a per-tile cost block evaluates a window — the camera of a block of controls, possibly replaced, which evaluates as the camera wherever its windows lie in the block; spline_window gives the 4 × 4 controls and weights a point reads, for the Jacobian's fan-out onto the controls; and spline_value lays the same lattice under any control type (a scalar field over the image).\n\ninit = :quasi_interpolate builds a camera from any source — a camera or a function pixel -> direction (nothing where it images nothing) — by the local 4th-order quasi_interpolant on a lattice extended one node past the area, linearly extrapolating per axis where the source images nothing and inward from the area's centre where its domain ends inside the area. A camera source keeps its own K; a function source takes the gauge of its sampled spline (the principal point is the pixel imaging ẑ). Where light falls is the bound's, not the lens's: BoundedCamera(cam, extent; lens_coverage = circle) carries a supplied image circle, and free_nodes(cam, imaged_region(bc)) are the nodes that can reach a pixel it images.\n\nA lattice already known — Schöps et al.'s intrinsics0.yaml, a stored grid record — is built from its controls: CameraModel(SplineLens, controls, size; calib_area, intrinsics, present) lays the direction controls ([gy, gx]) over the calibrated pixel area as the reference does, so every pixel backprojects as its grid does; K is the given one (skew 0) or the lattice's gauge, and an absent node's control is never read. It is the one constructor: the source constructor and spline_lattice build through it.\n\nA lens need not hold the other nodes. Built with with_present(cam, node_mask(cam, region)), it keeps the same lattice (spacing, basis, the dense control matrix) and holds only those nodes; every pixel of the region reads them alone, so the camera evaluates the region as the full one. present_nodes is the mask; past the fully present windows backproject is NaN and project gives a NaN pixel, as outside the area. with_directions(cam, D) replaces the direction controls (the same K, lattice and mask; the forward spline, which inverted the old directions, is dropped). Both rebuild a BoundedCamera too, keeping its bound, lens coverage and pose.\n\nA spline's ẑ is a gauge: wherever its first calibration's frame put it, which may be far from the lens's optical axis. axis_gauged(cam, R, K′) turns the camera frame by R (every control R·c, so every ray R·d, exactly) and takes the skew-0 K′ as its gauge (no ray changes). Turn the poses by the same R with axis_gauged(extrinsics, R); a posed camera turns its own. Every image is then kept. With R taking the optical axis onto ẑ and K′ the radially symmetric camera fitted to the lens (its principal point the axis's pixel), the principal point is the optical axis.\n\ncam = CameraModel(SplineLens, fish, Size2(1280, 960); grid_size = (35, 25), init = :quasi_interpolate)\nw = window(cam, 5:14, 15:24)                       # rows (y) × cols (x) of the grid\nbackproject(w, SVector(700.0, 400.0)) ≈ backproject(cam, SVector(700.0, 400.0))","title":"A spline lens in a refinement"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lenses/#The-lens-record","page":"Lens Families","text":"lens_record writes a lens as a self-describing record, the form VisualGeometryToolkitIO stores and configurations name: every key a leaf tag or a combinator, every value a coefficient array whose length is the arity.\n\nlens_record(KannalaBrandt(-0.043, 0.011))\n# {\"equidistant\": [], \"radial\": [[-0.043, 0.011], []]}\nlens_record(Division(-0.31, 0.024))\n# {\"lift\": {\"radial\": [[], [-0.31, 0.024]]}}\nlens_record(Lens(Perspective(), Additive(Radial((-0.28,)), Tangential(1.0e-3, -5.0e-4))))\n# {\"perspective\": [], \"add\": [{\"radial\": [[-0.28], []]}, {\"tangential\": [0.001, -0.0005]}]}\n\nlens_from_record inverts it exactly: the record determines the lens type, and with shortest round-trip floats a Float64 lens reads back bitwise.","title":"The lens record"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/batched/#Batches","page":"Batches","text":"A batch of points goes through the same per-point function a single point does, by broadcasting it. pointwise!(f, out, xs...) is out .= f.(xs...): a SIMD loop on a host array and one compiled kernel on a GPU array (a CuArray, an MtlArray), so a lens's project, a camera, or a framed stage is the kernel body — there is no per-model kernel code, and a batch can be any composition of per-point maps (K ∘ lens ∘ pose), fused into one evaluation per point.\n\nThe lens surfaces are project!(u, m, X), backproject!, distort_normalized! and undistort_normalized!. A lens is converted once to the output's number type, so a Float32 batch computes in Float32 end to end (Metal has no Float64).","title":"Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/batched/#One-kernel","page":"Batches","text":"A batch runs as ONE KernelAbstractions kernel on the backend that owns the output, the CPU's included: the same per-point function, bit for bit, on every backend. The exec keyword, OnBackend(backend; workgroupsize), launches it on a backend value the caller gives, so its compiler options hold (CUDABackend(; always_inline = true)), with a fixed workgroup size. On the CPU the launch has finished on return; on a device it does not wait: synchronize the backend before reading. OnBackend is the stack's one kernel-launch policy: every stack kernel is instantiated through it, OnBackend(backend; workgroupsize)(kernel), the workgroup per backend (the backend's choice; one work item per workgroup on the CPU and one warp on a device; or a size as given). (The host's broadcast policies, Serial, Threaded and Auto, are retired: the owner's one-kernel policy, 2026-11-11.)","title":"One kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/batched/#Failure","page":"Batches","text":"A point the map does not image comes back NaN — the per-point function's own answer, never a throw. Its validity is all(isfinite, out[i]).","title":"Failure"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_statistics/#Robust-Statistics-and-Solvers-API","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"The robust-statistics toolkit and the generic block-coordinate-descent loop: the shared machinery under the contracts of the Robust Estimation API page. The chart-rows contract and the chart LM are on the Chart LM API page.","title":"Robust Statistics and Solvers API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_statistics/#Robust-Statistics-Toolkit","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"The consensus scale, the Student-t + uniform mixture and its posterior-weight state, shared by the scorers, the local optimizers and the mixture fits.","title":"Robust-Statistics Toolkit"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_statistics/#Block-Coordinate-Descent","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"","title":"Block Coordinate Descent"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_statistics/#Index","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"Pages = [\"robust_statistics.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IGPrior","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"IGPrior(alpha0, beta0)\n\nInverse-Gamma prior on σ²: π(σ²) ∝ (σ²)^{-(α₀+1)} exp(-β₀/σ²). Conjugate to the Gaussian inlier likelihood, so the marginal reduces to a closed-form sweep update.\n\nThe Jeffreys prior π(σ²) ∝ 1/σ² is the limiting α₀ = β₀ = 0 case, built by JeffreysPrior() (or IGPrior()); one struct handles both.\n\nFields\n\nalpha0::Float64 — shape hyperparameter (0 ⇒ Jeffreys)\nbeta0::Float64  — scale hyperparameter (0 ⇒ Jeffreys)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IGPrior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.JeffreysPrior-Tuple{}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"JeffreysPrior() -> IGPrior\n\nThe Jeffreys scale prior π(σ²) ∝ 1/σ²: IGPrior(0.0, 0.0), the default prior of every scale estimator and scorer in the stack. A function returning the value, not a second name of the type — test alpha0 == 0 rather than dispatching on a name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.JeffreysPrior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fit_scale-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IGPrior, Real, Real}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"fit_scale(prior::IGPrior, rss, dof) -> s2\n\nFit the squared scale ŝ² as the inverse-gamma MAP, (rss + 2β₀)/(dof + 2α₀).\n\nReturns ŝ², the SQUARED scale — never a linear σ̂.\n\nŝ² is PER COMPONENT: rss sums squared NORMS of dg-vector residuals, so dof counts components — `νdata = (k − modeldof)·dgfor a hard partition, ordg·Σπᵢin the IRLS M-step (updatescale!). The2α₀/2β₀pseudo-counts make a refinement inherit the scoring prior (β₀floors ŝ² at low inlier counts);IGPrior(0, 0)(Jeffreys) givesrss/dof`.\n\nThe first argument is THE ESTIMATOR: IGPrior selects the inverse-gamma MAP, its prior being that estimator's parameters. The residuals are TAKEN as inliers, no initial guess is needed, and no inlier decision is made here.\n\nThe result is floored at eps(), so a zero rss under Jeffreys returns eps(), not a NaN. A caller that would rather HOLD its previous scale in that case must test the dof itself at the call site.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fit_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.partition_rss-Tuple{AbstractVector{Int64}, AbstractVector, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"partition_rss(order, residuals, k) -> Float64\n\nThe residual sum of squares of the partition order[1:k]: Σ_{j ≤ k} residuals[order[j]]², from UNSQUARED residuals (signed scalars or norms), accumulated in Float64 whatever their storage eltype. order is a scorer's consensus_order (or a copy of it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.partition_rss"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.partition_student_t-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IGPrior, Real, Integer}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"partition_student_t(prior::IGPrior, rss, k; model_dof, codimension = 1) -> (ν, ŝ²)\n\nThe Student-t of a hard partition of k points with residual sum of squares rss. It is the scoring marginal's posterior predictive over it under prior, and THE seed of every Student-t in the stack: counted dof ν_data = (k − model_dof)·codimension, then\n\nν  = max(ν_data + 2α₀, 2),     ŝ² = fit_scale(prior, rss, ν_data).\n\nThe ν ≥ 2 clamp keeps the seed's variance finite (a Student-t has one only for ν > 2) at a thin partition, and fit_scale floors the QUOTIENT at eps. One rule for every caller: both PosteriorWeightState seeds, the partition mixture of posterior_weights!/posterior_inlier_probs!, the consensus scale consensus_s2 and a RANSAC result's σ̂. model_dof is the caller's: the sweep's minimal_sample_size for the scoring marginal, one more for a local-optimization seed that also estimates the scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.partition_student_t"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.square_distances!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector, Int64}} where T","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"square_distances!(sq, r, n) -> sq\n\nsq[i] = r[i]² for i ≤ n, squared in sq's OWN precision (x = eltype(sq)(r[i]), then x·x): a Float64 buffer squares a Float32 residual exactly. THE one residual-squaring loop of the stack — the refill of a PosteriorWeightState's sq_distances, the per-point scores a scorer sweeps, and the pool staging.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.square_distances!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_rss-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"weighted_rss(w, sq_distances, n) -> Float64\n\nThe weighted residual sum of squares Σ_{i ≤ n} wᵢ·rᵢ² of SQUARED residuals, accumulated in Float64 left to right from 0.0. A zero-weight term contributes exactly 0: an excluded point may carry an infinite squared residual (the Sampson degeneracy rule — a vanishing projected variance), and 0·Inf = NaN would poison the sum and everything read from it (the ŝ² M-step of update_scale!, an IRLS cost and its stop rule).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_rss"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_sq-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"weighted_sq(w, sq_r) -> w·sq_r, exactly 0 at w = 0\n\nOne term of weighted_rss, for a caller summing weights it computes on the fly rather than holding in a buffer (an ECM scale over a loss's own IRLS weights). A zero weight contributes exactly 0 even against an infinite squared residual, where 0·Inf = NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_sq"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GauInlier","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"GauInlier(s2; nu = Inf, gamma = 1/2, d_g)\n\nEverything the Gaussian-uniform mixture needs EXCEPT the outlier half-width — the half both directions of the boundary share.\n\ns2   inlier variance sigma^2\nnu   effective dof: Inf is the Gaussian inlier, a finite value the        scale-marginalised (Student-t) one. The Gaussian IS the nu -> Inf        limit, and the two closed forms below agree there.\ngamma the label prior Pr(z_i = 1); a general gamma adds        log(gamma/(1-gamma)) to the boundary.\nd_g  the codimension, REQUIRED. It enters the outlier density as the        EXPONENT (2·outlier_halfwidth)^-d_g, so no value of it is a safe        default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GauInlier"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StudentTMixture(ν, s_hat_sq, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, d_g[, inlier_prior = 1/2])\nStudentTMixture{T}(m::StudentTMixture)\n\nThe Student-t inlier + uniform outlier mixture of one E-step, with every per-residual constant precomputed. Its parameters are ν degrees of freedom, squared scale ŝ² = s_hat_sq, outlier support of half-width outlier_halfwidth per axis (log_two_outlier_halfwidth = log(2·outlier_halfwidth), density p_out = (2·outlier_halfwidth)^-d_g), inlier mixing proportion π = inlier_prior. The loggammas are evaluated HERE, once.\n\nAn immutable isbits value, so it can be an argument of a KernelAbstractions kernel, and the three scalar functions of it — inlier_posterior, posterior_weight, mixture_nll — are allocation-free plain functions of a squared residual, callable inside a kernel as VGC's ρ is. StudentTMixture{Float32}(m) converts the constants for a Float32 kernel.\n\nThese are THE formulas of the mixture: soft_posterior_weights! (the E-step over a buffer) and posterior_weights!/posterior_inlier_probs! (over a partition) build one of these and call the same scalar kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Real, Real, Real, Integer}, Tuple{Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, F}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StudentTMixture{T}(ν, s_hat_sq, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, d_g[, inlier_prior = 1/2]; lgamma = loggamma)\n\nThe mixture built IN T (every constant computed in T, not in Float64 and rounded), with the log-gamma function supplied: kernel-lawful, so a device kernel whose ν and ŝ² change per work item builds one per item and calls inlier_posterior / posterior_weight on it. lgamma is the device's (CUDA's __nv_lgamma, Core's lgamma_lanczos elsewhere); the host default is SpecialFunctions' loggamma. StudentTMixture(…) is StudentTMixture{Float64}(…).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_boundary-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GauInlier, Real}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"decision_boundary(m::GauInlier, outlier_halfwidth) -> Float64\n\nThe residual at which the MAP rule flips between inlier and outlier: z* = 1 iff gamma*f_in > (1-gamma)*f_out holds exactly when norm(r) is below it.\n\nReturns 0.0 when the outlier component already explains every residual at least as well (the mixture admits nothing), rather than a negative root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_boundary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_width-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GauInlier, Real}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"decision_width(m::GauInlier, boundary) -> outlier_halfwidth\n\nThe mixture's method of decision_width, and the inverse of decision_boundary: the per-axis outlier half-width that puts the MAP boundary at boundary. A HALF-WIDTH and not a radius — the outlier support is a box, a product of intervals, never a ball, which is why the codimension enters as the exponent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_posterior-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"inlier_posterior(m::StudentTMixture, sq_r) -> P(inlier | r)\n\nThe posterior probability that a residual with squared norm sq_r is an inlier, π·t / (π·t + (1−π)·p_out). Scalar and kernel-callable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_posterior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mixture_nll-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"mixture_nll(m::StudentTMixture, sq_r) -> −log(π·t_ν(r; ŝ²) + (1−π)·p_out)\n\nThe negative log-density of the mixture at one residual — the per-point summand of the mixture NLL. Computed against the outlier term in log space, −(log((1−π)p_out) + softplus(log π·t − log((1−π)p_out))), so it is finite for an infinite residual (it is the outlier term's) and for an exact inlier. Scalar and kernel-callable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mixture_nll"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.next_nu-Tuple{Float64, AbstractVector, Float64, AbstractVector, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"next_nu(ν, sq_distances, s_hat_sq, w_fixed, n; d_g=1, kwargs...) -> ν_new\n\nM-step: Newton update for ν maximizing the EM Q-function with fixed E-step weights.\n\nMaximizes Q(ν) = Σ wi · log t{dg}(ri; ν, ŝ²) w.r.t. ν, where wi are the fixed posterior inlier probabilities from the E-step (not recomputed), ri² the squared NORM of a d_g-vector residual and ŝ² the per-component scale:\n\nlog t_d(r; ν, ŝ²) = logΓ((ν+d)/2) − logΓ(ν/2) − (d/2)·log(νπŝ²)                       − ((ν+d)/2)·log(1 + z),     z = r²/(νŝ²)\n\n∂Q/∂ν = Σ wi · [½ψ((ν+d)/2) − ½ψ(ν/2) − d/(2ν)                      − ½log(1 + zi) + (ν+d)zi/(2ν(1+zi))]\n\n∂²Q/∂ν² = Σ wi · [¼ψ₁((ν+d)/2) − ¼ψ₁(ν/2) + d/(2ν²)                        + zi/(2ν(1+zi)) − zi(ν + 2d + d·zi)/(2ν²(1+zi)²)]\n\nwith z_i the SCALE-NORMALISED squared residual.\n\nUp to max_newton Newton steps from the current ν, each clamped to [ν_min, ν_max]. Newton is taken only where Q is concave (∂²Q < 0), where its step is uphill; elsewhere the step is geometric, uphill along the gradient — ν ← 2ν if ∂Q/∂ν > 0, ν ← ν/2 otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.next_nu"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.next_nu_from_sums-Union{Tuple{T}, NTuple{6, T}} where T<:Real","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"next_nu_from_sums(ν, dQ, d2Q_data, sum_w, ψ1_half_νdg, ψ1_half_ν; d_g = 1, ν_min = 2, ν_max = 1e4) -> (ν_new, converged)\n\nOne safeguarded Newton update of next_nu from the reduced sums dQ = Σwᵢ·dqᵢ, d2Q_data = Σwᵢ·d2q_dataᵢ (nu_point_terms) and sum_w = Σwᵢ, with ψ1_half_νdg = ψ₁((ν + d_g)/2) and ψ1_half_ν = ψ₁(ν/2) the caller's trigammas. ∂²Q = sum_w·(¼ψ₁((ν+d)/2) − ¼ψ₁(ν/2) + d/(2ν²)) + d2Q_data; Newton is taken only where Q is concave (∂²Q < 0, its step then uphill), elsewhere the step is geometric along the gradient (2ν or ν/2); the result is clamped to [ν_min, ν_max]. converged is true when the weights vanish (sum_w < 1e-10, ν returned unchanged) or |dQ/sum_w| < 1e-8. Generic in the float type, allocation-free: the ν update of a device kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.next_nu_from_sums"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nu_point_terms-Union{Tuple{T}, NTuple{4, T}} where T<:Real","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"nu_point_terms(z, ν, ψ_half_νdg, ψ_half_ν; d_g = 1) -> (dq, d2q_data)\n\nOne point's terms of next_nu's Q-derivatives at ν (its docstring states both): dq its summand of ∂Q/∂ν and d2q_data the data part of its summand of ∂²Q/∂ν² (the ν-only part is the sum's, Σwᵢ times a constant, added by next_nu_from_sums), at the scale-normalised squared residual z = r²/(ν·ŝ²), with ψ_half_νdg = ψ((ν + d_g)/2) and ψ_half_ν = ψ(ν/2) the caller's digammas. Generic in the float type and allocation-free: a device kernel sums wᵢ·dq and wᵢ·d2q_data over its points (a workgroup reduction) and hands the sums to next_nu_from_sums, the same arithmetic as the host loop of next_nu.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nu_point_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_weight-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"posterior_weight(m::StudentTMixture, sq_r) -> w\n\nThe IRLS weight of the mixture at sq_r: P(inlier | r) · (ν + d_g)/(ν·ŝ² + r²), the responsibility times the Student-t's own weight. Scalar and kernel-callable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_weight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_weight_derivative-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"posterior_weight_derivative(m::StudentTMixture, sq_r) -> dw/d(sq_r)\n\nThe slope of posterior_weight in the squared residual, −w/(ν·ŝ² + r²) · (1 + (ν + d_g)/2 · (1 − P(inlier | r))) — the Student-t weight's own decay plus the responsibility's. w is strictly decreasing in sq_r, so this is negative wherever w > 0; it is what a Newton inversion of the weight (a tabulated half-quadratic conjugate, LocalOptimizationDoneRight's TabulatedLift) needs. Scalar and kernel-callable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_weight_derivative"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.soft_posterior_weights!","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"soft_posterior_weights!(w, inlier_posterior, sq_distances, ν, s_hat_sq, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, n, d_g=1[, inlier_prior=1/2])\n\nE-step: compute posterior inlier probability and combined IRLS weight.\n\nStores inlier_posterior[i] = P(inlier | r_i) separately for the ν M-step, and w[i] = inlier_posterior[i] · u_i (combined weight for the θ solve).\n\nd_g is the constraint codimension (1 for scalar residuals, 2 for 2D).\n\nThe separation is critical: the ν Newton update must use fixed E-step weights (not recomputed from the changing ν), otherwise ν and inlier_posterior couple in a positive feedback loop that drives ν → ∞.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.soft_posterior_weights!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusDof","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"ConsensusDof()\n\nThe update policy of a Student-t dof HELD at the value its consensus seed gave it: the posterior-predictive dof of the scorer's consensus set, ν = 2α₀ + (k* − model_dof)·d_g (partition_student_t, with its ν ≥ 2 floor), where α₀ is the scale prior's shape (0 under Jeffreys). The seed sets it once; the ν block never moves it afterwards.\n\nWhy hold it there: a Gaussian whose scale carries an inverse-gamma posterior marginalizes to a Student-t with exactly that dof — ν measures how well the consensus set DETERMINES σ, not the shape of the inlier tail. Estimating ν instead lets the fit trade one for the other: on a contaminated set a heavy inlier tail (ν → 2) absorbs the outliers — the identifiability collapse this policy prevents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusDof"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Estimated","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"Estimated()\n\nThe update policy of a nuisance part that is ESTIMATED: its M-step block runs (update_scale!(pws, n), update_inlier_prior!(pws, n), update_nu!(pws, n)). One value for every part.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Estimated"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedNu","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"FixedNu(ν)\n\nThe update policy of a Student-t dof HELD at ν (positive, finite): the ν block writes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedNu"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedPrior","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"FixedPrior(inlier_prior)\n\nThe update policy of an inlier prior HELD at inlier_prior ∈ (0, 1): the π block writes it (through the E-step's one clamp, [1e-3, 1 − 1e-3]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedS2","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"FixedS2(s2)\n\nThe update policy of a scale HELD at the squared scale s2 (positive, finite): the ŝ² block writes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedS2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"PosteriorWeightState\n\nMutable state for computing robust IRLS weights from a Student-t inlier + uniform outlier mixture model. Holds the sufficient statistics (ν, ŝ², logtwooutlierhalfwidth, the inlier prior π) and per-point buffers (w, inlierposterior, r²).\n\nThe Posterior in the name is the posterior over the inlier indicator: the weights w/inlier_posterior are P(inlier | r). The noise scale σ² also has a posterior, but it is marginalized away, so it is held here only as the scalar ν/ŝ², never as a posterior.\n\nThe per-point buffers are supplied by the caller (typically a workspace's); construction-time allocation is performed only by the n-constructor and median_seeded_posterior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState-Tuple{AbstractVector{Float64}, Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"PosteriorWeightState(sq_distances, n, model_dof, k; order, prior, w, inlier_posterior, sq_r,\n                     log_two_outlier_halfwidth, codimension = 1)\n\nThe state seeded from a hard partition: the first k entries of order (a scorer's consensus_order over these same squared residuals), with no fresh sort. The seed is the partition's Student-t under prior (partition_student_t), so it equals the scoring predictive over order[1:k]; the E-step runs once.\n\nPrecondition: order[1:k] is a partition of sq_distances[1:n] and k > model_dof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"PosteriorWeightState(n; ν, s_hat_sq, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, prior, codimension = 1, inlier_prior = 1/2)\n\nA state with FRESH length-n buffers at the given mixture, for a caller that fills the residuals itself and runs the E-step (e.g. mixture_ecm!) before reading any weight. The buffers are uninitialised; ŝ² is floored at eps and the inlier prior clamped to [1e-3, 1 − 1e-3] (the one clamp, update_inlier_prior!'s). The scale prior prior (an IGPrior) is required: the stack's default prior is a configuration's, not this constructor's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StudentTMixture(pws::PosteriorWeightState)\n\nThe mixture the state's E-step evaluates: its current ν, ŝ², outlier support, codimension and inlier prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTMixture"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_prior-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"inlier_prior(pws::PosteriorWeightState) -> Float64\n\nThe mixing proportion π the E-step weighs with: the prior probability that a residual is an inlier (1/2 unless estimated or held elsewhere).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.median_seeded_posterior-Tuple{AbstractVector{Float64}, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"median_seeded_posterior(sq, n; outlier_halfwidth, ν, prior, codimension = 2) -> PosteriorWeightState\n\nA PosteriorWeightState seeded from the MEDIAN residual, for a caller with no partition to seed from. Every point starts an inlier candidate at the scale ŝ² = mad_scale² — the median residual norm over the median of χ_d (mad_scale, on the norms √sq), floored at eps — at the caller's start ν (large for a near-Gaussian start) under the scale prior prior, both required (a configuration's values, not this function's), and the E-step run once. Allocates its own buffers (sq[1:n] is copied). codimension is 1 or 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.median_seeded_posterior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mixture_ecm!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, AbstractVector, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"mixture_ecm!(pws, r, n)\n\nOne ECM cycle of the Student-t + uniform mixture with the mixing proportion ESTIMATED (ν held fixed), from the residuals r[1:n] (unsquared). It squares them into sq_distances(pws), then runs the E-step (update_weights!), the scale block (update_scale!) and the inlier-prior block (update_inlier_prior!), in that order. Each block is an exact conditional maximisation, so the mixture NLL (mixture_nll) never rises. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mixture_ecm!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mixture_nll-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, AbstractVector, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"mixture_nll(pws::PosteriorWeightState, r, n) -> Float64\n\nThe mixture negative log-likelihood Σ_{i ≤ n} −log[π·t_ν(rᵢ; ŝ²) + (1−π)·p_out] of the residuals r[1:n] (UNSQUARED) at the state's current (ν, ŝ², π). It sums the per-point mixture_nll(m, rᵢ²) of StudentTMixture(pws) in Float64. Reads the state; writes nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mixture_nll"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_inlier_probs!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"posterior_inlier_probs!(w, sq_distances, order, k; model_dof, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, prior,\n                        codimension = 1)\n\nPosterior inlier probabilities w[i] ∈ [0, 1] of the hard partition order[1:k], without the IRLS weight factor — the same mixture as posterior_weights!, so that is this times uᵢ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_inlier_probs!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_weights!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"posterior_weights!(w, sq_distances, order, k; model_dof, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, prior,\n                   codimension = 1)\n\nStudent-t posterior weights w[i] = P(inlier | rᵢ) · uᵢ of the hard partition order[1:k] of the squared residuals: the partition's Student-t (partition_student_t under prior: ν = (k − model_dof)·d_g + 2α₀, at least 2, and ŝ² = (RSS + 2β₀)/((k − model_dof)·d_g + 2α₀)) against the uniform outlier support, uᵢ = (ν + d_g)/(ν·ŝ² + r²ᵢ). Per point this is posterior_weight of that StudentTMixture. Fills every entry of w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.posterior_weights!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reset_posterior!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"reset_posterior!(pws; ν, s_hat_sq, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, prior, codimension, inlier_prior) -> pws\n\nPosteriorWeightState(n; …) IN PLACE: the same parameters (ŝ² floored at eps, π clamped) on pws's existing buffers, whose contents are left as they are — that constructor's buffers are uninitialized, so nothing may read them before writing. A keyword left out keeps the state's CURRENT value (its ν, ŝ², outlier support, scale prior, codimension and inlier prior), so a caller re-seeding one parameter states only that one (the floor and the clamp, idempotent, are applied as to a given value).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reset_posterior!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.s_hat_sq-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"s_hat_sq(pws::PosteriorWeightState) -> Float64\n\nThe current squared scale estimate ŝ² of the Student-t inlier density (a SQUARE; see fit_scale).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.s_hat_sq"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.seed_posterior!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, AbstractVector{Float64}, Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"seed_posterior!(pws, sq_distances, n, model_dof, k; order, prior, log_two_outlier_halfwidth,\n                codimension = 1) -> pws\n\nThe partition seeding of PosteriorWeightState(sq_distances, n, model_dof, k; …) IN PLACE, on pws's own buffers: the same squared residuals, partition Student-t and first E-step, the inlier prior back at 1/2. What a caller that keeps one state across calls (a local optimizer's workspace) re-seeds with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.seed_posterior!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sq_distances-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"sq_distances(pws::PosteriorWeightState) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe squared Mahalanobis distances the E-step weighs — the buffer update_scale! and update_weights! read, refilled by the caller between iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sq_distances"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.student_t_dof-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"student_t_dof(pws::PosteriorWeightState) -> Float64\n\nThe Student-t degrees of freedom ν of the inlier density — not the model's DOF (minimal_sample_size), and not Distributions' dof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.student_t_dof"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_inlier_prior!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, Int64, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Estimated}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_inlier_prior!(pws, n, policy) -> π\n\nThe inlier-prior block under its update policy: Estimated() is update_inlier_prior!(pws, n); FixedPrior holds π.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_inlier_prior!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_inlier_prior!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_inlier_prior!(pws, n)\n\nM-step, inlier-prior block: overwrite the mixing proportion π with the mean inlier posterior of the E-step, and nothing else. The mean Σπᵢ/n is clamped to [1e-3, 1 − 1e-3] (the E-step reads it through log((1 − π)/π)). Call after update_weights! filled the posteriors it reads. Returns π.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_inlier_prior!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_nu!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, Int64, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Estimated}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_nu!(pws, n, policy) -> ν\n\nThe ν block under its update policy: Estimated() is update_nu!(pws, n); FixedNu holds ν; ConsensusDof keeps the seed's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_nu!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_nu!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_nu!(pws, n)\n\nM-step, ν block: overwrite the Student-t dof ν with next_nu (the safeguarded Newton MLE) from the E-step's inlier posteriors, the current squared residuals and the CURRENT ŝ², and nothing else. Call after update_scale!, which it reads. Returns ν.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_nu!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_scale!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, Int64, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Estimated}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_scale!(pws, n, policy) -> ŝ²\n\nThe scale block under its update policy. Estimated() is update_scale!(pws, n); FixedS2 holds ŝ².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_scale!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_scale!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_scale!(pws, n)\n\nM-step, scale block: overwrite ŝ² from the E-step's weights and the current squared residuals, and nothing else. Call after the residual block fills sq_distances(pws); the ν block is update_nu!, called after this.\n\nŝ² is the ECM step of the d_g-variate Student-t with scale ŝ²·I:\n\nŝ²_new = Σ πᵢ uᵢ rᵢ² / (d_g · Σ πᵢ),     uᵢ = (ν + d_g)/(ν + rᵢ²/ŝ²),\n\nwith πᵢ the inlier posteriors and rᵢ² squared NORMS. The E-step's weight is precision-scaled, wᵢ = πᵢ·(ν + d_g)/(νŝ² + rᵢ²) = πᵢuᵢ/ŝ², so the numerator is ŝ²·Σwᵢrᵢ² at the ŝ² that E-step used, and the inverse-gamma prior enters as fit_scale(prior, ŝ²·Σwᵢrᵢ², d_g·Σπᵢ). Returns ŝ².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_scale!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_weights!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeightState, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_weights!(pws, n)\n\nE-step: recompute posterior inlier probabilities and combined IRLS weights from current ν, ŝ², and squared residuals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_weights!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.truncated_log_normaliser-Tuple{AbstractLossShape, Float64, Float64, Float64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"truncated_log_normaliser(shape, sq_loss_width, depth, r_max) -> Float64\n\nlog Z̃ = log ∫_{−r_max}^{r_max} exp(−depth·ρ(shape, r²/sq_loss_width, 1)) dr — the normaliser of the density a loss shape induces on the truncated domain [−r_max, r_max], as log 2∫₀^{r_max} (the integrand is even), by adaptive Gauss–Kronrod (QuadGK) at rtol 1e-12. The integrand is a well of width c = √sq_loss_width on a flat floor, so for c ≪ r_max an unsplit rule never samples the well: the domain is split at c·4ᵏ < r_max, which holds the result to ≲1e-12 relative over every width and depth in use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.truncated_log_normaliser"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod\n\nAbstract type for the problem side of block_coordinate_descent!: the model block, its residuals, and the per-iteration bookkeeping.\n\nEach concrete subtype defines extension points:\n\ninit_state!(method, prob, θ₀) — build the state and compute the initial r/σ/w. It carries ! because a method may fill buffers its own workspace holds (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's posterior IRLS seeds method.ws's residual buffers, which the iterations then read); the method is the mutated argument\nupdate_model!(state, method, prob, θ) — the model block: one weighted update of θ (weighted least squares, LM, Newton), or θ unchanged; nothing when there is no update to make (the run stops with :solve_failed)\nupdate_residuals!(state, method, prob, θ) — compute residuals + sq_distances from θ\npost_iteration!(state, method, prob, θ, iter) — per-iteration bookkeeping (best-tracking, counters the stopping criteria read)\nstopping_criterion(method) — the method's own StoppingCriterion (default StopNever)\nblock_coordinate_result(state, method, prob, θ, stop_reason, final_iter, stop_rule) — package result\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Recorded","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"Recorded(state)\n\nA block_coordinate_descent! state that also keeps a per-iteration trace: iterates (the model each iteration produced), cost (the state's current_cost after post_iteration!), s2 (the likelihood's current_s2, the squared scale, NaN where it holds none) and reason (a Ref to the stop reason). Give it in place of the state; the driver runs every block and every criterion on the wrapped state, so a recorded run is the bare run, iterate for iterate. The bare state never records — off by default, nothing to branch on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Recorded"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopAfterIteration","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StopAfterIteration(n)\n\nStops with :max_iterations once n iterations have run (before the first, when n ≤ 0). block_coordinate_descent! adds it, LAST, from its max_iter, so on the final iteration a method's own reason wins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopAfterIteration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopNever","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StopNever()\n\nNever stops: the default stopping_criterion of a method that runs its whole budget.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopNever"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopWhenAny","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StopWhenAny(criteria::Tuple)\na | b\n\nStops at the first of criteria, in order, whose stop_reason is not :continue, with that reason. a | b builds one and flattens nested ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopWhenAny"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopWhenSolveFailed","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StopWhenSolveFailed()\n\nStops with :solve_failed when the model block returned nothing (a singular or non-finite solve, an underdetermined refit): checked right after update_model!, before any other block runs. block_coordinate_descent! adds it FIRST.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopWhenSolveFailed"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StoppingCriterion","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"StoppingCriterion\n\nWhen a block_coordinate_descent! run ends, and why. A criterion is an immutable, concretely typed value; it answers through stop_reason(criterion, state, method, prob, θ, θ_old, iter), which returns :continue or the reason the run ends (:converged, :max_iterations, :solve_failed, or any instance's own Symbol such as :left_basin). Nothing is stored in the criterion: the counters a criterion reads live in the solver state, written by post_iteration!.\n\nCriteria compose with | into a StopWhenAny, evaluated left to right; the first reason other than :continue wins. The tuple is part of the type, so the composed check compiles to the chain of branches it stands for.\n\nThe Manopt.jl correspondence: Manopt's criteria are mutable functors that store their reason; these are pure, and the reason is the return value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StoppingCriterion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_coordinate_descent!-Tuple{AbstractWeightPolicy, AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"block_coordinate_descent!(likelihood, method, prob, θ₀; max_iter) -> result\n\nBlock coordinate descent on a negative log-likelihood, over three blocks:\n\nmodel — update_model! (the method): weighted least squares, LM, Newton, or nothing at all (a fixed-model method such as a seed EM, which returns θ unchanged);\nnuisance — update_nuisance! (the likelihood): scale, mixing proportion, ν, Barron α, or nothing;\nweights — update_weights! (the likelihood): EM responsibilities, loss weights, or GNC surrogate weights.\n\nupdate_residuals! is bookkeeping between the model and nuisance blocks: it recomputes the residuals block from the new model and descends on nothing. One iteration (step_solver!) is one cycle through every block, in the order model → residuals → nuisance → weights, then post_iteration!. See update_nuisance! for why the nuisance block is not named \"scale\".\n\nStopping\n\nThe run's criterion is\n\nStopWhenSolveFailed() | stopping_criterion(method) | StopAfterIteration(max_iter)\n\nevaluated in that order by stop_solver! after every iteration: a failed model block ends the run at once (:solve_failed, no other block run); otherwise the method's own criterion is read after post_iteration!, and only then the budget — on the last iteration a method's reason wins over :max_iterations. max_iter ≤ 0 runs no iteration. The result is block_coordinate_result(state, method, prob, θ, reason, iterations, rule) with θ the last model the model block produced (θ₀ if none).\n\nMonotonicity\n\nInstances: EM (under the Neal–Hinton free-energy view EM is coordinate ascent on the free energy, i.e. descent on its negative); IRLS / half-quadratic (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's Irls{K} LO strategy is one — the strategy, not this loop); Barron-adaptive; GNC.\n\n\"Descent\" is on the negative log-likelihood, and monotonicity is each INSTANCE's claim, not the loop's: the best iterate is kept (a method tracks it in post_iteration! with keep_best and returns it from block_coordinate_result), but the loop never rejects a step. An instance proves monotonicity through its post_iteration! trace. GNC is the declared exception — it changes its objective between iterations.\n\nThe Manopt.jl names\n\nThe loop is Manopt's solve! loop; the names are public, not exported, so a future bridge extends Manopt's functions rather than clashing with them:\n\nManopt.jl here\nAbstractManoptSolverState the method's state (any type; RVG's *State)\ninitialize_solver! initialize_solver!\nstep_solver! step_solver!: one BCD cycle, the four blocks + post_iteration!\nstop_solver! stop_solver!\nStoppingCriterion, get_reason StoppingCriterion, stop_reason (pure; the reason is returned)\nStopAfterIteration, StopWhenAny (criteria or-ed) StopAfterIteration, StopWhenAny (criteria or-ed)\nRecordSolverState Recorded\nget_solver_result block_coordinate_result\n\nArgument order is Julia Base's: the inputs the loop mutates come first (state, in the method below, then likelihood), then the inputs it only reads (the method, the problem, the seed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_coordinate_descent!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_coordinate_descent!-Tuple{Any, AbstractWeightPolicy, AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"block_coordinate_descent!(state, likelihood, method, prob, θ₀; max_iter) -> result\n\nBlock coordinate descent with pre-allocated state (skips init_state!). state may be a Recorded one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_coordinate_descent!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_coordinate_result","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"block_coordinate_result(state, method::AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, prob, θ, stop_reason, final_iter, stop_rule)\n\nPackage the run of block_coordinate_descent! as the method's result (a FitResult for the stack's fits), from the final state, model, stop_reason and iteration count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_coordinate_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.current_cost-Tuple{Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"current_cost(state) -> Float64\n\nThe objective a state tracks at its current iterate, for Recorded. NaN unless the state's type implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.current_cost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.current_s2-Tuple{Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"current_s2(likelihood) -> Float64\n\nThe squared scale ŝ² a weight policy currently holds, for Recorded. NaN unless the policy's type implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.current_s2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.init_state!","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"init_state!(method::AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, prob, θ₀) -> state\n\nBuild the solver state of block_coordinate_descent! and compute the initial residuals, scale and weights at θ₀. A method may fill buffers its own workspace holds, hence the !. An extension point: the method's package adds the method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.init_state!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.initialize_solver!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StoppingCriterion}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"initialize_solver!(state, likelihood, method, prob, θ₀, criterion) -> (reason::Symbol, rule::StopRule)\n\nStart a block_coordinate_descent! run: clears a Recorded trace, and returns the reason the run ends before its first iteration (:max_iterations for a non-positive budget) or :continue. The state itself is built beforehand — by init_state! or the caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.initialize_solver!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.keep_best-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{Attributed{M, @NamedTuple{cost::Float64}}, Any, Real}} where M","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"keep_best(best, θ, cost) -> (best′, improved::Bool)\n\nBest-tracking for a block_coordinate_descent! instance that claims descent. The loop never rejects an iterate, so such an instance keeps its best one as best = Attributed(θ, (; cost)) (cost a Float64): its post_iteration! offers every iterate here, and its block_coordinate_result returns best.value. Returns (Attributed(θ, (; cost)), true) when cost is STRICTLY below best.cost, and (best, false) otherwise. Pure and allocation-free: the caller stores the result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.keep_best"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.post_iteration!-Tuple{Any, AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"post_iteration!(state, method::AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, prob, θ, iter) -> nothing\n\nPer-iteration bookkeeping of block_coordinate_descent!, after the weight block: best-model tracking and the counters a StoppingCriterion reads. The default does nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.post_iteration!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.step_solver!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"step_solver!(state, likelihood, method, prob, θ, iter) -> θ′ or nothing\n\nOne iteration — one cycle through the blocks, in the frozen order: update_model! (the model block; nothing ends the cycle there, with no other block run), update_residuals!, update_nuisance!(likelihood), update_weights!(likelihood), post_iteration!. Returns the new model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.step_solver!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_reason","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"stop_reason(criterion, state, method, prob, θ, θ_old, iter) -> Symbol\n\n:continue, or the reason the run ends after iteration iter, which moved θ_old to θ (θ is nothing when the model block failed). Pure: it reads the state and writes nothing. Implement it for a new StoppingCriterion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_reason"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_rule-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StoppingCriterion, Any}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"stop_rule(criterion::StoppingCriterion, method) -> StopRule\n\nThe StopRule a criterion's stop records, for the method it stopped: NoRule by default; Core's own criteria StopAfterIteration → IterationCap, StopWhenSolveFailed → NonFinite (a failed model block). A package adds a method for each criterion it defines (and may specialise on the method). block_coordinate_descent! asks it of the member of a StopWhenAny that fired — the first whose stop_reason is not :continue, in evaluation order — and hands it to block_coordinate_result, so no consumer re-evaluates the criteria to learn which stopped the run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_rule"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_solver!-Tuple{Any, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StoppingCriterion, Any, Any, Any, Any, Int64}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"stop_solver!(state, criterion, method, prob, θ, θ_old, iter) -> (reason::Symbol, rule::StopRule)\n\nThe run's stop_reason after iteration iter (θ is nothing when its model block failed), with the stop_rule of the criterion that fired (NoRule while the run continues); the reason recorded on a Recorded state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_solver!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stopping_criterion-Tuple{AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod}","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"stopping_criterion(method::AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod) -> StoppingCriterion\n\nThe method's own termination test, evaluated after post_iteration! and before the iteration budget (StopAfterIteration). Default StopNever: the run ends on the budget or a failed solve only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stopping_criterion"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_model!","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_model!(state, method::AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, prob, θ) -> θ′ | nothing\n\nThe model block of block_coordinate_descent!: one weighted update of θ at fixed weights and nuisance (a weighted least-squares solve, an LM step, Newton), or θ unchanged. nothing means there is no update to make, and the run stops with :solve_failed. A fit fit_<fit>'s method calls its <fit>_step here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_model!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_statistics/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_residuals!","page":"Robust Statistics & Solvers","text":"update_residuals!(state, method::AbstractBlockCoordinateMethod, prob, θ) -> state\n\nThe residual block of block_coordinate_descent!: writes the residuals and squared residuals of θ into the state, and nothing else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_residuals!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#Optimization-Strategies-API","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"The optimization strategies a fit, a refine and a local optimization take (Irls{K}, Triggs{K}, their efficiency constants and the strategy registry), and the local-optimization and external-refit contracts. The problem and scoring contracts they work with are on the Robust Estimation API page.","title":"Optimization Strategies API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#Optimization-Strategies","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"","title":"Optimization Strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#Local-Optimization-and-External-Refit-Contracts","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"","title":"Local-Optimization and External-Refit Contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#Index","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"Pages = [\"optimization_strategies.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LOSS_TAGS","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"LOSS_TAGS\n\nThe loss-shape registry (a TagRegistry, family suffix Shape): TOML loss tag → shape type, read off the one loss-identity table (_LOSS_TABLE).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LOSS_TAGS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.STRATEGY_TAGS","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"STRATEGY_TAGS\n\nThe core optimization strategies' registry (a TagRegistry built by @lo_registry): \"irls\" → Irls, \"triggs\" → Triggs. A package with strategies of its own merges it with its registry (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's OPTIMIZATION_STRATEGY_TAGS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.STRATEGY_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Irls","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"Irls{K<:AbstractLossShape} <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nSTATIC IRLS at a frozen loss width — direct iteratively-reweighted least squares on loss K, no lift, no graduation. Irls{K} is an LO STRATEGY: one instance of VisualGeometryToolkitCore's generic block_coordinate_descent! loop, which is not itself \"IRLS\" (it also runs EM, the half-quadratic lifts and GNC). Runs the posterior-weight iteration (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's posterior_irls!) — the PosteriorIrlsMethod / PosteriorIrlsState, same weighted refine! M-step (the problem's chart LM), same best-cost tracking and stop rule — with only the per-iteration weight functor swapped per loss:\n\nIrls{WelschShape}: wᵢ = exp(−rᵢ²/loss_width²), loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ (the paper's Welsch-IRLS arm; 1.5 was the pre-anchoring point)\nIrls{HuberShape}: wᵢ = min(1, loss_width/|rᵢ|), loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ (the elbow; 1.345 is the classical value) — the static-tail control (influence constant, never vanishing, past the elbow)\nIrls{GemanMcclureShape}: wᵢ = (loss_width²/(loss_width²+rᵢ²))², loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ — the GM static-reweighting arm\nIrls{GaussianShape}: unit weights, no scale and NO efficiency constant — plain least squares over all points, the coverage extreme. It takes no efficiency_constant; supplying one is an ArgumentError (a width it would silently ignore)\n\nThe loss width is frozen at the start of the call, set by the width mode W (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's loss_at) — no per-iteration scale refit:\n\nefficiency_constant = m (EfficiencyConstant(m)): m·ŝ at the consensus set's Student-t scale ŝ — m the plain multiple, for every loss;\nloss_width = w (LossWidth(w)): the absolute width w;\nneither (nothing, the default): the scorer's decision point (decision_boundary) for a shape with a decision width — MSAC with Irls{TLSShape} refits at MSAC's threshold —, and for one without (Huber) the table's efficiency_constant(K, d) times the consensus scale ŝ, d the scorer's codimension (a shape with neither is an ArgumentError).\n\nFields\n\nmax_iter::Int=25: max IRLS iterations per hypothesis\nwidth::W: the width mode, nothing | EfficiencyConstant{Float64} | LossWidth{NTuple{2,Float64}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Irls"},{"category":"type","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Triggs","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"Triggs{K<:AbstractLossShape} <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nThe ROBUSTIFIED LEVENBERG–MARQUARDT strategy: from a guess θ, ONE NLLSsolver.jl LM over all the data minimizing Σᵢ ρ(‖rᵢ‖²), robustified per datum by loss K through loss_robustifier. Triggs{K} is a strategy (configuration) type only: it selects the loss shape and its width; how the robustified LM is formed is NLLSsolver's. There is no reweighting loop and no inlier set inside the solve: θ is the initial guess and the loss decides each datum's influence. A RANSAC problem's implementation (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation) runs it as the local optimization of every chart problem.\n\nThe loss width is loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ (the plain multiple, as for Irls), frozen at the start of the call: ŝ is the Student-t marginal scale of θ's residuals — the Sampson errors of all the data — over θ's consensus partition (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's partition_student_t, the scorer's scale_s2), the scale every consensus-anchored strategy reads. With no efficiency_constant (Triggs{K}()) inside a RANSAC the width follows the scorer's decision_boundary, as for Irls; in a refine (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's refine_*) it is efficiency_constant(K, d) times the caller's scale: a refine reads its scale from the caller, never from a consensus set. An absolute loss_width is an ArgumentError from a keyword or a TOML table. An absolute width reaches it only through with_width, for fixed_width's freeze — with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, s2)), the loss the consensus-anchored strategy would deploy at ŝ² = s2 (the role-isolated controls).\n\nK is any shape Irls admits — the zero-argument shape tags: Welsch (the pipelines' default), Geman–McClure, Cauchy, smooth TLS, TLS, Huber and Gaussian; Barron, whose shape carries its α, is refused by both. Triggs{GaussianShape} is the plain chart LM over every datum: like Irls{GaussianShape} it has no width and takes no efficiency_constant.\n\nFields\n\nwidth: the width mode, set by efficiency_constant = c (EfficiencyConstant), or nothing (no constant stated; always for Triggs{GaussianShape})\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Triggs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fixed_width-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy, Real}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"fixed_width(lo, s2) -> LossWidth\n\nThe absolute width mode of a strategy deployed at EfficiencyConstant(m) with the consensus scale held at s2: LossWidth(m, s2) — so with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, s2)) deploys exactly the loss the consensus-anchored strategy would at ŝ² = s2 (the role-isolated controls: solo CV, oracle scale, the anchoring ablation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fixed_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_absent-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Val{F}}} where {T, F}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_absent(T, Val(f); defaults)\n\nField f of strategy T when the caller omits it: the strategy's defaults[lo_tag(T)] key lo_key(T, Val(f)), converted by lo_field_from_toml; with no such key the field is required (UndefKeywordError). The width field defaults to nothing (follow the scorer). A downstream method is per field (::Val{:x}): a method on Val{F} for every field of its type is ambiguous with the Val{:width} catch-all below.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_absent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_admits-Tuple{Type, Nothing}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_admits(T, K) -> nothing\n\nConstruction-time admission of strategy type T, called before any field is read. K is the type parameter of a loss-parametric family — the loss K of Irls{K}, or the inner strategy of a wrapper (LocalOptimizationDoneRight's Gnc{S}) — and nothing for any other strategy. The default admits every non-parametric strategy and checks a family's shape with lo_admits_shape; a family with further conditions adds a method (LocalOptimizationDoneRight's half-quadratic lift and GNC surrogate checks). Throws ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_admits"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_admits_shape-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Type{K}}} where {T, K}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_admits_shape(T, K) -> nothing\n\nConstruction-time admission of loss shape K by strategy type T (named in the message): K must be a zero-argument shape tag, so a shape the mechanism cannot run is refused when the strategy is built, rather than surfacing mid-RANSAC as a MethodError from a VisualGeometryToolkitCore internal the caller never wrote (BarronShape() for a shape that carries its α). The admissible set is read off VisualGeometryToolkitCore's own traits, never re-listed. The default of lo_admits for a loss-parametric family; a family with further conditions (a half-quadratic lift, a GNC surrogate) calls it first. An ArgumentError when K is not admitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_admits_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_construct-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractOptimizationStrategy","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_construct(T; defaults = lo_defaults(T), kw...) -> T\n\nTHE keyword constructor of every registered strategy (@lo_registry defines T(; kw...) as this call): each field is its keyword or, omitted, its defaults value (lo_absent); the width is spelled efficiency_constant or loss_width (both is an ArgumentError; neither follows the scorer). The hooks lo_admits, lo_field and lo_validate state the per-strategy exceptions. defaults is the [optimization_strategy] table, keyed by tag. An unknown keyword is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_construct"},{"category":"function","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_defaults","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_defaults(T) -> AbstractDict\n\nThe [optimization_strategy] table of the defaults file of the package that registered T (keyed by tag; @lo_registry's defaults): what lo_construct reads an omitted field from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_defaults"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_efficiency_constant_shape-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_efficiency_constant_shape(T) -> Type{<:AbstractLossShape}\n\nThe loss shape a TOML efficiency_constant of strategy T is read for (and a named one, \"cross_validated\", tuned for): a loss-parametric family's strategy_loss, a fixed-loss arm's @lo_registry shape column. An ArgumentError for a strategy that has neither.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_efficiency_constant_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_field-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Val{F}, Any}} where {T, F}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_field(T, Val(f), v)\n\nField f of strategy T from a value the caller supplied by keyword. Default: convert(fieldtype(T, f), v); the width field goes through lo_width_value. A downstream method is per field (::Val{:x}): a method on Val{F} for every field of its type is ambiguous with the Val{:width} catch-all below.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_field"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_field_from_toml-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Val{F}, Any}} where {T, F}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_field_from_toml(T, Val(f), v)\n\nField f of strategy T from a TOML value — a key of the caller's table or of the defaults table. Default: VisualGeometryToolkitCore's convert_field(fieldtype(T, f), v). A field whose TOML spelling differs from its value adds a method (LocalOptimizationDoneRight's GNC entry, \"r_max\" for NaN).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_field_from_toml"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_has_default-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Symbol, AbstractDict}} where T","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_has_default(T, f::Symbol, defaults::AbstractDict) -> Bool\n\nWhether strategy T's table of defaults (keyed by its lo_tag) holds a default for field f (at its key lo_key).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_has_default"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_key-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Val{F}}} where {T, F}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_key(T, Val(f)) -> String\n\nThe key of the defaults table field f of strategy T defaults from. Default: VisualGeometryToolkitCore's toml_key(T, f) (Greek spelled ASCII). A strategy whose field defaults from another key adds a method (LocalOptimizationDoneRight's smooth-TLS lift, max_iter_smooth_tls).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_loss-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet, Any, Any}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_loss(lo, cs::ConsensusSet, scoring, buf) -> DeployedLoss\n\nThe loss a loss-parametric strategy (Irls{K}, and downstream families with a strategy_loss) deploys: its shape at the width its mode resolves to (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's loss_at: the consensus scale times its multiple, an absolute width, or the scorer's decision point). The least-squares arm has no scale and no width: it deploys at s2 = Inf, efficiency_constant = 1 (its weights are unit whatever the width).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_loss"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_parametric-Tuple{Type}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_parametric(T) -> Bool\n\nWhether T is a loss-parametric family (Irls{K}): its TOML table names its loss (loss key, LOSS_TAGS) and its type names the loss through strategy_loss. false unless its @lo_registry row says so.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_parametric"},{"category":"function","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_tag","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_tag(T) -> String\nlo_tag(lo) -> String\n\nThe TOML tag of a registered strategy type or loss-parametric family (its @lo_registry row): the type a config spells it by and the [optimization_strategy.<tag>] table its keyword defaults read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_tag"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_takes_width-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_takes_width(T) -> Bool\n\nWhether strategy type T takes a width (the efficiency_constant / loss_width keys): iff it has a width field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_takes_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_validate-Tuple{Any}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_validate(lo) -> lo\n\nCross-field checks on a freshly built strategy; returns it or throws. Default: none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_validate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_width_value-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_width_value(w) -> w\n\nA local optimization's width mode, checked: nothing (follow the scorer), EfficiencyConstant(m) or LossWidth(w) — never an outlier half-width (a scorer's). Anything else is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_width_value"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.strategy_loss-Union{Tuple{Type{<:Irls{K}}}, Tuple{K}} where K","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"strategy_loss(lo_or_type) -> Type{<:AbstractLossShape}\n\nThe loss-shape tag of a loss-parametric strategy (Irls{K}; downstream families add methods: LocalOptimizationDoneRight's HQ{K}, Gnc{S}). Read off the TYPE, so a wrapper recurses into its inner strategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.strategy_loss"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.@lo_registry-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"@lo_registry rows defaults = table [retired = (old => new, …)]\n\nRegister the local-optimization strategies of rows and return their VisualGeometryToolkitCore TagRegistry (family :optimization_strategy, the TOML path local_optimization_strategy.type): const OPTIMIZATION_STRATEGY_TAGS = @lo_registry ROWS defaults = …. Each row is\n\n(T, tag, path, scale_interface, loss_family, label, shape, parametric)\n\nwith nothing for a fact that is derived rather than stated. defaults is an expression evaluated, in the calling module, each time a default is read: the [optimization_strategy] table of the caller's defaults file, keyed by tag. retired lists retired tags with their successors; none = true adds the tag \"none\" → nothing (no local optimization; this package's registry holds it, so a merge with a downstream one holds it once).\n\nThe expansion is one call, made at load time in the calling module. Per row it defines lo_tag, lo_defaults, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's toml_skip_fields and, for a stated path, optimization_path (the width is written as its two keys, never as width); for a parametric row lo_parametric; for a stated scale interface, loss family or label the Core accessor; for a stated shape lo_efficiency_constant_shape and the loss family of that shape; and, for a strategy with fields, the keyword constructor T(; kw...) (T{K}(; kw...) for a parametric family) as lo_construct. A macro rather than a function because it defines methods in the caller's module. Every method it adds is on a declared contract generic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.@lo_registry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LoInfo","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"LoInfo\n\nThe metadata of one local-optimization refit (lo_estimate): residuals_current, whether the optimizer left the refined model's residuals in the scoring buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LoInfo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_composition-Tuple{AbstractScoring, Union{Nothing, AbstractOptimizationStrategy}}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"check_composition(scoring, lo) -> nothing\n\nRefuse, once at entry, a local optimizer that follows the scorer's decision boundary (lo_width(lo) === nothing) composed with a scorer that defines none (has_decision_boundary). A strategy without a width mode is accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_composition"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.efficiency_constant-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy, Integer}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"efficiency_constant(lo::AbstractOptimizationStrategy, d::Integer) -> Float64\n\nThe efficiency constant strategy lo multiplies a noise scale by at the residual's codimension d (loss_width = c × scale, c always the plain multiple): the strategy's own (its width mode EfficiencyConstant(c)), else the table's efficiency_constant(K, d) of the shape K a strategy's constant is read for (lo_efficiency_constant_shape: Irls{K}'s and Triggs{K}'s K, a fixed-loss arm's registry shape). An ArgumentError for a shape with no constant (a truncation: TLS, Le–Zach) when the strategy states none, and for an absolute width in the strategy (LossWidth, which no scale multiplies). The one rule of every stage that deploys a strategy at a given scale (Estimation's refines and fits, PoseLib's refiners).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.efficiency_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.external_workspace-Tuple{Union{Nothing, AbstractOptimizationStrategy}, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"external_workspace(lo, problem, frame, scoring) -> ws\n\nThe per-(pair, arm) workspace of an external refiner lo (see refine_external!): whatever the solver reads beyond the problem — from the two-view frame (frame.Kc1, frame.Kc2, frame.cs; see the contract above) a point matrix, the solver's calibrations, a consensus workspace — built once and reused by every refit of that pair. Default nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.external_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_estimate-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{M, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.T, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopRule}} where M","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_estimate(model, code::ReturnCode.T, rule::StopRule; iterations = 0, residuals_current = false) -> FitResult{M, LoInfo}\nlo_estimate(model, retcode::Symbol, rule::StopRule; iterations = 0, residuals_current = false)\n\nThe lean result of one local-optimization refit — what locally_optimize! and a block_coordinate_result return. estimate is the refined model; its termination record (the FitResult core's retcode and stop_rule) is why the refit stopped. No LO ran (the strategy nothing): (Success, ClosedForm), the seed returned as it is; the consensus set has no residual dof, so no LO ran: (InitialFailure, NoRule); a refit that failed (the incumbent returned): (Failure, NonFinite); otherwise the optimizer's record. A loop that reports a legacy Symbol supplies it with its success rule, retcode_from_symbol(retcode, rule) (the one map; the second form). The refit computes no residual summary, so rms is NaN and n is 0. Zero heap allocation when M is isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_estimate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_width-Tuple{Union{Nothing, AbstractOptimizationStrategy}}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_width(lo) -> nothing | EfficiencyConstant{Float64} | LossWidth{NTuple{2,Float64}}\n\nThe width mode of a width-carrying local optimizer: nothing (follow the scorer's decision_boundary), EfficiencyConstant(m) (the loss width m·ŝ at the consensus scale) or LossWidth (an absolute width). The owner of the strategy implements it; a strategy with no width mode (the seed, a self-scaling optimizer) keeps the default missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_workspace-Tuple{Type, Type, Union{Nothing, AbstractOptimizationStrategy}, Int64}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"lo_workspace(::Type{P}, ::Type{T}, lo, n::Int) -> L\n\nThe local optimizer's own scratch and per-call state for problems of type P with up to n points at storage precision T, built ONCE per composed workspace and reset by each call. Default nothing (an optimizer that needs none; also nothing, no local optimization).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_workspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.locally_optimize!","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"locally_optimize!(L, lo, problem, θ, cs::ConsensusSet, buf::ScoreBuffers, scoring) -> FitResult\n\nRefine the hypothesis θ of problem with the strategy lo on its workspace L (lo_workspace), seeded by the consensus set cs of the score that just ran on buf. Returns lo_estimate(θ′, code, rule; iterations, residuals_current): θ′ === θ when nothing was refit; residuals_current when residuals(buf) holds θ′'s residuals on exit (the loop then skips recomputing them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.locally_optimize!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_at-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy, Any, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet, Any, Any}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"loss_at(lo, shape, cs::ConsensusSet, scoring, buf::ScoreBuffers) -> DeployedLoss\n\nTHE resolution of a width-carrying local optimizer's loss, statically dispatched on its width mode lo_width:\n\nnothing (follow the scorer): for a shape with a decision_width method (Welsch, Geman–McClure, Cauchy, smooth TLS, TLS, Barron), deploy(shape, w²; efficiency_constant = 1) with w = decision_width(shape, decision_boundary(scoring, buf)) — the loss's decision boundary at the scorer's (the identity for TLSShape): a threshold scorer's constant threshold, or the Student-t + uniform scorer's MAP crossover at the current consensus scale and its outlier half-width, re-estimated with every score; for a shape without one (Huber), deploy(shape, ŝ²; efficiency_constant = efficiency_constant(shape, codimension(scoring))) — the table's constant at the consensus scale; a shape with neither is an ArgumentError;\nEfficiencyConstant(m): deploy(shape, ŝ²; efficiency_constant = m), ŝ² = max(scale_s2(cs), eps()) — the consensus-anchored width, m always the plain multiple (loss_width = m·ŝ) whatever the shape;\nLossWidth((m₀, s2)): deploy(shape, max(s2, eps()); efficiency_constant = m₀) — the absolute width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.prepare_external!-Tuple{Any, Union{Nothing, AbstractOptimizationStrategy}, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"prepare_external!(ws, lo, problem, θ, width::LossWidth, scoring) -> Bool\n\nThe part of an external refit that runs OUTSIDE its timing bracket, before it (a consensus-scale bootstrap). false means no refit is made — the call is neither timed nor counted, and the refit falls back. Default true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.prepare_external!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine_external!","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"refine_external!(ws, lo, problem, θ, width, scoring) -> θ′ | nothing\n\nThe ExternalRefit member of RefitFamily: refine the model θ of problem with the external solver lo at the absolute loss width width::LossWidth (loss_width(width)), on its workspace ws (external_workspace). A fixed noise scale FixedS2(s2) is the loss width √s2 unless the refiner states otherwise. Returns the refined model (the problem's model type) or nothing when the solver produced none. A pure model → model refine: no seed sweep, no re-score. The owner of the strategy implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine_external!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine_width-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Integer}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"refine_width(lo, scale, width, d::Integer) -> (; width, efficiency_constant, input)\n\nThe refine contract's loss width, the one rule of every refiner that deploys a strategy from its scale / width keywords (Estimation's refine_*, fit_* and bundles, PoseLib's refiners), at the residual's codimension d:\n\nlo === nothing (plain least squares) takes neither keyword (an ArgumentError names the one given): (NaN, NaN, nothing).\notherwise EXACTLY ONE of them, read by scale_input into input:\nscale = σ (or an upstream result): width = c·σ with c = efficiency_constant(lo, d) (the strategy's own, else the table's; refused for a strategy with an absolute width, or a truncation without one);\nwidth = w: the width itself, c = 1, refused for a strategy that states its own width mode (an efficiency constant, which w would override, or an absolute width, which w would contradict).\n\nThe strategy itself is the caller's to admit first (each refiner takes its own set).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_s2-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"width_s2(lo, cs::ConsensusSet) -> Float64\n\nThe squared scale a width-carrying strategy's mode runs at — the consensus scale under EfficiencyConstant, the absolute s2 under LossWidth, both floored at eps; the INITIAL estimate of a self-scaling strategy is scale_s2(cs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_s2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/optimization_strategies/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_scale_interface-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Optimization Strategies","text":"width_scale_interface(w) -> ScaleInterface\n\nThe ScaleInterface label of a width mode: EfficiencyConstant → ConsensusAnchored(), LossWidth → FixedScale(), nothing → FollowsScorer() — what a width-carrying strategy's scale_interface returns, so the label has one source of truth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_scale_interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/frames/#Frames-API","page":"Frames","text":"Coordinate-frame tags: the open Frame{N} hierarchy carried by EuclideanMap/HomographyMap (Src/Dst), by CovMat/PrecMat (the trailing F), and by the framed imaging-chain and batched stages. See the Coordinate Frames guide.","title":"Frames API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/frames/#Frame-Tags-and-Image-Frames","page":"Frames","text":"","title":"Frame Tags and Image Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"api/frames/#Index","page":"Frames","text":"Pages = [\"frames.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BodyFrame","page":"Frames","text":"BodyFrame{id} <: Frame{3}\n\nThe 3D frame of a SPECIFIC rigid body, tagged by an instance identity: id::Symbol names which body — the ViewFrame instance-identity idiom applied to things that are tracked or mounted as a unit (a motion-capture rigid body, a camera rig, a board rig carrying several targets). A body is one frame, and a trajectory is its pose over time: EuclideanMap{..., Src = BodyFrame{id}, Dst = WorldFrame} per instant, never a new frame per instant.\n\nWhat is mounted on a body is posed in it: a target on a board rig is EuclideanMap{..., Src = TargetFrame{m}, Dst = BodyFrame{:board_rig}}, a camera on a camera rig EuclideanMap{..., Src = BodyFrame{:camera_rig}, Dst = ViewFrame{id}} (extrinsics: rig coordinates into the camera's), so a hand-eye chain is junction-checked by dispatch.\n\nSee also TargetFrame, ViewFrame, WorldFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BodyFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CameraFrame","page":"Frames","text":"CameraFrame <: Frame{3}\n\nThe camera-centered coordinate frame (origin at the optical center, +Z along the optical axis in the CV convention). Source frame of a camera POSE, destination frame of camera EXTRINSICS (X_CW ≙ EuclideanMap{..., Src = WorldFrame, Dst = CameraFrame}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CameraFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Frame","page":"Frames","text":"Frame{N}\n\nAbstract supertype for coordinate-frame tags of dimension N.\n\nA frame tag names a coordinate frame (3D, or a 2D image frame) at the type level; EuclideanMap carries a source and destination frame as trailing type parameters so that composition and inversion are frame-checked by dispatch. In Drake monogram notation, X_AB (pose of B in A, maps B-coordinates to A-coordinates) corresponds to EuclideanMap{..., Src = B, Dst = A}.\n\nThe hierarchy is open: downstream packages declare their own frames (struct LidarFrame <: Frame{3} end) and participate in frame-checked composition with zero changes to VisualGeometryToolkitCore.\n\nSee also UnlabeledFrame, WorldFrame, CameraFrame, TargetPlaneFrame, PixelFrame, unlabel, relabel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Frame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TargetFrame","page":"Frames","text":"TargetFrame{id} <: Frame{3}\n\nThe 3D body frame of a SPECIFIC calibration target (board) instance, tagged by an instance identity: id::Symbol names which target — the ViewFrame instance-identity idiom applied to the target side of a calibration. Target-local geometry lives in this frame (for planar boards, the pattern plane is z = 0); the target's POSE in a common frame F is EuclideanMap{..., Src = TargetFrame{id}, Dst = F} (target-local coordinates into F).\n\nFor content-addressed targets (e.g. BlobBoards board uids) the symbol is the content hash, so the frame tag doubles as the geometry reference. Minted from a target rig's keys by target_frames.\n\nSee also ViewFrame, TargetPlaneFrame (the 2D frame of the board plane), Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TargetFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.UnlabeledFrame","page":"Frames","text":"UnlabeledFrame{N} <: Frame{N}\n\nThe gradual-typing default frame: \"no frame recorded\".\n\nEvery EuclideanMap constructor that does not state frames produces Src = Dst = UnlabeledFrame{N}, so pre-frame code compiles and behaves exactly as before. UnlabeledFrame is the composition wildcard: it composes with anything, the junction is unchecked, and known endpoints are kept as-is (the truth table at Base.:∘ for EuclideanMap; the rule is _junction_ok, below). This anti-virality rule means frame labels never force themselves onto code that does not opt in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.UnlabeledFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ViewFrame","page":"Frames","text":"ViewFrame{id} <: Frame{3}\n\nThe 3D camera-centered frame of a SPECIFIC view, tagged by an instance identity: id::Symbol names which camera (ViewFrame{:left} ≠ ViewFrame{:right}). This extends the PixelFrame{:colmap} idiom from convention-identity to instance-identity — the symbol parameter distinguishes coexisting instances of the same kind of frame, so that a labeled junction between two different cameras' frames is a MethodError instead of a silent CameraFrame collision.\n\nAxis conventions are those of CameraFrame (origin at the optical center, +Z along the optical axis); the tag adds WHICH camera, not a new convention. In Drake monogram terms a two-view relative pose is X_{V_b V_a} ≙ EuclideanMap{..., Src = ViewFrame{:a}, Dst = ViewFrame{:b}}.\n\nGradual as always: nothing forces view identities — UnlabeledFrame (and the singleton CameraFrame) remain the defaults everywhere. StereoRig mints per-view identities on request (see view_frames).\n\nSee also ImageFrame (the 2D image frame of a view), CameraFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ViewFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WorldFrame","page":"Frames","text":"WorldFrame <: Frame{3}\n\nThe world (scene) coordinate frame. Destination frame of a camera POSE (X_WC ≙ EuclideanMap{..., Src = CameraFrame, Dst = WorldFrame}), source frame of camera EXTRINSICS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WorldFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DistortedFrame","page":"Frames","text":"DistortedFrame{id} <: Frame{2}\n\nThe post-lens, pre-calibration 2D frame of view id: the frame a lens model's forward distortion lands on, one framed K-affine short of pixels. Completes the imaging-frame vocabulary:\n\nframe meaning\nGnomonicFrame{id} gnomonic ray frame (calibrated, undistorted): x̂ = π(K⁻¹ũ)\nEquidistantFrame{id} equidistant ray frame (radius θ): the omni models' home\nStereographicFrame{id} stereographic ray frame (radius 2·tan(θ/2), conformal)\nEquisolidFrame{id} equisolid ray frame (radius 2·sin(θ/2), equal-area)\nOrthographicFrame{id} orthographic ray frame (radius sin θ; folds at 90°)\nDistortedFrame{id} post-lens, pre-K: where forward distortion lands\nImageFrame{id} canonical pixel frame of the view (:colmap convention)\nPixelFrame{Origin} pixel ORIGIN conventions — orthogonal to view identity\n\nThe ray-frame rows are all frames on the same viewing sphere S² (the RetinalFrame family): the gnomonic frame (perspective plane) is ONE frame on S², degenerate at 90° and blind to Z ≤ 0 rays; the equidistant and stereographic frames cover θ ∈ [0, π), the equisolid frame the whole sphere, and the orthographic frame only the closed forward hemisphere (it folds at the equator). \"Perspective precedes lens\" is therefore a property of perspective-plane MODELS, not a law of the imaging chain — each lens model's distortion is labeled with the frame it is actually defined in (distortion_frame), and the typed chain of imaging_chain reads\n\nViewFrame{id} ─ ray-frame projection → Gnomonic/EquidistantFrame{id}\n              ─ lens distortion      → DistortedFrame{id}\n              ─ framed K-affine      → ImageFrame{id}\n\nwith every junction frame-checked. When a lens's distortion is the identity in its defining frame (has_distortion == false, e.g. Pinhole; also the pure Lens(Equidistant()) on its equidistant frame), the distortion stage is omitted and the ray frame meets K directly — the two frames COLLAPSE rather than being separated by a manufactured identity stage.\n\nAnonymous view id. The id axis is optional: the reserved symbol :_ is the designated ANONYMOUS view identity, and every stage mint defaults to it. Frame KIND then still does the junction checking — a lone CameraModel with no view identity gets GnomonicFrame{:_} → DistortedFrame{:_} stages whose distort-vs-undistort direction is checked by kind — while rig-derived chains mint real ids (imaging_chain(model; id = ids(rig)[1])). The wildcard rules for UnlabeledFrame are unchanged; :_ is a real label, not a wildcard.\n\nSee also GnomonicFrame, EquidistantFrame, ImageFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DistortedFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EquidistantFrame","page":"Frames","text":"EquidistantFrame{id} <: RetinalFrame{id}\n\nThe equidistant-azimuthal 2D ray frame of view id: the frame on the viewing sphere S² whose point for a ray at polar angle θ (from the optical axis) and azimuth ψ is θ·(cos ψ, sin ψ) — radial coordinate equal to the ANGLE, azimuth preserved. Well-defined for θ ∈ [0, π) (every ray except the exact backward direction), so it represents fields of view past 90° where the gnomonic GnomonicFrame is degenerate.\n\n\"Equidistant\" (radial coordinate r = θ) names this frame unambiguously within the fisheye family, whose classical frames are all functions of the polar angle — equidistant r = θ, stereographic r = 2·tan(θ/2), equisolid r = 2·sin(θ/2), orthographic r = sin θ — so \"the angle frame\" would not single one out. The other radial profiles slot under RetinalFrame beside this one (0.13): StereographicFrame, EquisolidFrame, OrthographicFrame.\n\nThis is the DEFINING frame of the omnidirectional distortion models: KannalaBrandt is the radial polynomial θ_d = θ·(1 + k₁θ² + …) on this frame, the pure Lens(Equidistant()) is the IDENTITY on it (the \"distortion\" of a pure equidistant fisheye is entirely the frame choice), and COLMAP's THIN_PRISM_FISHEYE applies its additive terms to this frame's coordinates. The framed projection onto this frame is FramedEquidistantProjection (imaging_chain stage 1 for equidistant-frame lenses); see the frame table at DistortedFrame for how it slots into the imaging chain.\n\nNot to be confused with Manifold: that machinery parameterizes MODEL manifolds (essential matrices, homographies) for estimation; EquidistantFrame is a coordinate frame on the viewing sphere of one camera.\n\nSee also GnomonicFrame, DistortedFrame, ImageFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EquidistantFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EquisolidFrame","page":"Frames","text":"EquisolidFrame{id} <: RetinalFrame{id}\n\nThe equisolid-angle 2D ray frame of view id: the frame on the viewing sphere S² whose point for a ray at polar angle θ and azimuth ψ is 2·sin(θ/2)·(cos ψ, sin ψ) — the Lambert azimuthal equal-area projection. Well-defined on the WHOLE sphere θ ∈ [0, π] (the only retinal frame that reaches the antipode), with bounded radius r ∈ [0, 2]; equal areas on the sphere map to equal areas in the frame, which is why equisolid (\"equal-area fisheye\") lenses are the standard for sky photometry and illuminance observation. The antipode θ = π maps to radius 2 under the pole azimuth convention ψ = 0.\n\nThe defining frame of the pure equisolid fisheye projection: Lens(Equisolid()) is the IDENTITY on it, and the framed projection onto this frame is FramedEquisolidProjection (imaging_chain stage 1). Transitions to the other retinal frames are minted by frame_transition; see the family table at RetinalFrame and the chain table at DistortedFrame.\n\nSee also StereographicFrame, OrthographicFrame, EquidistantFrame, GnomonicFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EquisolidFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrameDependence","page":"Frames","text":"FrameDependence\n\nHoly trait classifying a 2D frame TAG by what DEFINES the frame it names — the retina's geometry alone, or a model's continuous parameters:\n\nGeometryDefined(): the frame is fixed by geometry and conventions — every RetinalFrame kind (gnomonic / equidistant / stereographic / equisolid / orthographic), ImageFrame, NormalizedImageFrame (fixed by the image size), PixelFrame{Origin}, TargetPlaneFrame.\nModelDefined(): the frame is pinned down by a model's continuous parameters — DistortedFrame (which frame it IS depends on the view's lens coefficients).\n\nThe trait encodes a COMMENSURABILITY rule: points in GeometryDefined frames of the same kind and view are always commensurable — the tag alone determines the frame, so a model-free frame_transition between two such frames exists and the sphere sections/lifts behind it are defined. Points in ModelDefined frames are commensurable only under the one-lens-per-view invariant that makes DistortedFrame{id} honest, and no model-free transition into or out of them exists — the model-carrying maps (distort_stage/undistort_stage, or a fused model-carrying projection stage) are the only bridges.\n\nDeliberate boundary: the trait classifies the KIND of a frame tag; it cannot and does not verify parameter equality between two ModelDefined uses — parameter values are a runtime question, exactly as rank lives on the manifold rather than in the matrix type. Query with frame_dependence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrameDependence"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GeometryDefined","page":"Frames","text":"Frame fixed by the retina's geometry/conventions alone. See FrameDependence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GeometryDefined"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GnomonicFrame","page":"Frames","text":"GnomonicFrame{id} <: RetinalFrame{id}\n\nThe calibrated (normalized) 2D image frame of view id: the frame whose points are gnomonic coordinates x̂ = π(K⁻¹ũ) — pixel coordinates with the intrinsics removed, i.e. the perspective projection of the view's unit-focal image plane. EssentialMat-backed epipolar forms are honest on this frame (x̂₂ᵀ E x̂₁ = 0); ImageFrame remains the PIXEL frame (canonical :colmap convention) and is never overloaded for gnomonic coordinates.\n\nFormally, this is the GNOMONIC chart of the view's ray sphere S² — the central projection of directions onto the unit-focal plane. Like every gnomonic chart it covers only the forward hemisphere: it is degenerate at 90° from the optical axis and cannot represent rays with Z ≤ 0. It is the defining frame of the perspective projection's distortions (OpenCV's radial-tangential-thin-prism models); omnidirectional models define their distortion on the EquidistantFrame instead — see the frame table at DistortedFrame. The framed projection onto this frame is FramedPerspective (imaging_chain stage 1 for perspective-plane lenses).\n\nThe calibrated → pixel collineation of view id is the labeled lift of the view's intrinsics, HomographyMap{GnomonicFrame{id}, ImageFrame{id}}(K) — the slot maps whose composition with the correlation computes F = K₂⁻ᵀ E K₁⁻¹ with the frame labels verifying that each K met the slot it was meant for. Minted by essential_form(rig) / EpipolarForm(rig) on a StereoRig.\n\nSee also EquidistantFrame, DistortedFrame, ImageFrame, ViewFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GnomonicFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ImageFrame","page":"Frames","text":"ImageFrame{id} <: Frame{2}\n\nThe 2D image frame of view id — the per-view pixel frame used to label the argument slots of epipolar forms (see EpipolarForm) and the endpoints of inter-view 2D maps.\n\nDeliberate division of labor with PixelFrame: PixelFrame{Origin} tags a pixel-origin CONVENTION (:colmap/:julia/:opencv) — a property of how coordinates are written; ImageFrame{id} tags WHICH VIEW an image point belongs to — a property of what the coordinates refer to. The two are orthogonal, and ImageFrame does not encode an origin: convert origin conventions FIRST (via the EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{A}, PixelFrame{B}) frame maps, landing on the canonical :colmap convention), then label the points' frame with their view. An ImageFrame{id} point is therefore always read in the canonical :colmap convention.\n\nSee also ViewFrame (the 3D frame of the same view), GnomonicFrame (the calibrated frame of the same view), image_frames, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ImageFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ModelDefined","page":"Frames","text":"Frame pinned down by a model's continuous parameters. See FrameDependence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ModelDefined"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NormalizedImageFrame","page":"Frames","text":"NormalizedImageFrame{id} <: Frame{2}\n\nThe diagonal-normalized 2D image frame of view id: ImageFrame coordinates moved to the image centre and divided by the half-diagonal s = hypot(width, height) / 2, so the image corners sit at unit radius. Pixel-scale constants are sensor-independent here: a focal length is the dimensionless f / s (1 for a 90° diagonal field of view) and a pixel threshold τ becomes τ / s. Mint the map with the factory FramedAffine(ImageFrame{id}, NormalizedImageFrame{id}, sz) (or from a PixelFrame{Origin} source); its inv de-normalizes.\n\nNot to be confused with GnomonicFrame, the CALIBRATED frame K⁻¹·pixel: this frame needs no intrinsics, only the image size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NormalizedImageFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OrthographicFrame","page":"Frames","text":"OrthographicFrame{id} <: RetinalFrame{id}\n\nThe orthographic 2D ray frame of view id: the frame on the viewing sphere S² whose point for a ray at polar angle θ and azimuth ψ is sin θ·(cos ψ, sin ψ) — parallel projection of the sphere straight down onto the equatorial plane, the image formed by an orthographic (\"sine law\") fisheye lens.\n\nHonest domain: θ ∈ [0, π/2] ONLY — the frame FOLDS at the equator. r = sin θ increases to 1 at θ = π/2 and then DECREASES again: past 90° every radius value is taken twice, so the projection is non-injective on any domain crossing the equator and the frame is restricted to the closed forward hemisphere. This is the poster child for the honest-domain discipline of the retinal frames — a ray with Z < 0 is not \"a large radius\" in this frame, it is OUTSIDE the frame, and the section/lift machinery throws a DomainError rather than silently aliasing it onto a forward ray (the lift always selects the forward sheet cos θ = +√(1 − r²)). Radius is bounded, r ∈ [0, 1].\n\nThe defining frame of the pure orthographic fisheye projection: Lens(Orthographic()) is the IDENTITY on it, and the framed projection onto this frame is FramedOrthographicProjection (imaging_chain stage 1). Transitions to the other retinal frames are minted by frame_transition; see the family table at RetinalFrame and the chain table at DistortedFrame.\n\nSee also StereographicFrame, EquisolidFrame, EquidistantFrame, GnomonicFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OrthographicFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PixelFrame","page":"Frames","text":"PixelFrame{Origin} <: Frame{2}\n\n2D pixel frame of an image, tagged by its origin convention: Origin::Symbol names the pixel-origin placement. The registered origins:\n\nframe first-pixel center corner offset\nPixelFrame{:opencv} (0, 0) (-0.5, -0.5)\nPixelFrame{:colmap} (0.5, 0.5) (0, 0)\nPixelFrame{:julia} (1, 1) (0.5, 0.5)\n\n(x right / y down throughout; :julia is additionally read row-major (row, col) at the array boundary — that permutation is image_warp's RCSwap, not part of the frame.)\n\nOrigin conversion is a labeled rigid map between two pixel frames (identity rotation, constant translation), built by the constructor-as-factory on the frame TYPES:\n\nT = EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia})\nT isa EuclideanMap{2, …, PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia}}  # true\nT.t == Vec2(0.5, 0.5)                                              # true\npt_julia = T(pt_colmap)\n\nThe map composes (frame-checked) with other labeled 2D maps and fuses in image_warp chains like any EuclideanMap{2}. This is the ONLY origin conversion API: the 0.6-era ImageOrigin value surface (COLMAP_ORIGIN, JULIA_ORIGIN, Translation(::ImageOrigin, ...), Translation(::Symbol, ::Symbol)) is removed in 0.7.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PixelFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RetinalFrame","page":"Frames","text":"RetinalFrame{id} <: Frame{2}\n\nAbstract supertype of the canonical 2D ray frames of view id — the coordinate systems on the view's SPHERICAL RETINA, the sphere S² of rays through the center of projection (the manifold-level object of the Geyer–Daniilidis unified imaging model). Every central camera has a spherical retina; what differs between camera models is the FRAME its distortion is expressed in, so frames — not retinas — are the type currency of the imaging chain. The \"planar retina\" of the perspective camera is not a different manifold but the gnomonic frame on this one (GnomonicFrame); the equidistant frame (EquidistantFrame) covers the fields of view the gnomonic frame cannot.\n\nSubtypes share the azimuth and differ in the radial profile — the five classical azimuthal frames of the fisheye-projection family, each a function of the polar angle θ with its own honest domain:\n\nframe radial profile domain\nGnomonicFrame r = tan θ θ ∈ [0, π/2)\nEquidistantFrame r = θ θ ∈ [0, π)\nStereographicFrame r = 2·tan(θ/2) θ ∈ [0, π)\nEquisolidFrame r = 2·sin(θ/2) θ ∈ [0, π]\nOrthographicFrame r = sin θ θ ∈ [0, π/2] ONLY\n\n(the orthographic frame FOLDS at the equator — see its docstring). The transition maps between them are pure radial reparameterizations, minted as first-class framed stages by frame_transition. A point of the retina written in no 2D frame needs no 2D tag — it is a unit 3-vector in ViewFrame{id}.\n\nimaging_chain stage 1 (the ray-frame projection family) maps ViewFrame{id} → RetinalFrame{id}; distortion_frame(lens) names which subtype a lens model's distortion is defined in. DistortedFrame is deliberately NOT a subtype: it is defined by the lens map's image, not as a canonical frame of the retina.\n\nSee also GnomonicFrame, EquidistantFrame, StereographicFrame, EquisolidFrame, OrthographicFrame, DistortedFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RetinalFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StereographicFrame","page":"Frames","text":"StereographicFrame{id} <: RetinalFrame{id}\n\nThe stereographic 2D ray frame of view id: the frame on the viewing sphere S² whose point for a ray at polar angle θ (from the optical axis) and azimuth ψ is 2·tan(θ/2)·(cos ψ, sin ψ) — the classical stereographic projection of the sphere from its backward pole onto the tangent plane at the forward pole. Well-defined for θ ∈ [0, π) (every ray except the exact antipode), and CONFORMAL — the only angle-preserving member of the fisheye-projection family, which is why stereographic (\"fisheye of least visual distortion\") lenses are prized in panorama work. Radius is unbounded as θ → π.\n\nThe defining frame of the pure stereographic fisheye projection: Lens(Stereographic()) is the IDENTITY on it, and the framed projection onto this frame is FramedStereographicProjection (imaging_chain stage 1). Transitions to the other retinal frames are minted by frame_transition; see the family table at RetinalFrame and the chain table at DistortedFrame.\n\nSee also EquidistantFrame, EquisolidFrame, OrthographicFrame, GnomonicFrame, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StereographicFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TargetPlaneFrame","page":"Frames","text":"TargetPlaneFrame{id} <: Frame{2}\n\n2D frame of a calibration target's plane, the target named by its id (a Symbol, as TargetFrame{id} names its 3-D frame): target-local (x, y) on the plane z = 0 of TargetFrame{id}. A file names it \"target:<id>\" (VisualGeometryToolkitIO: a blur on a print, a target homography's source). Equal coordinates in two targets' planes are different points: the frames differ by their id.\n\nThe bare TargetPlaneFrame (no id) is the plane of a target the value does not name — the source of a pinhole camera's plane-induced homography, HomographyMap(camera::PosedPinholeCamera), Src = TargetPlaneFrame, Dst = PixelFrame{:colmap} (monogram H_PB).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TargetPlaneFrame"},{"category":"function","location":"api/frames/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.frame_dependence","page":"Frames","text":"frame_dependence(C::Type{<:Frame{2}}) -> FrameDependence\n\nWhat defines the frame named by tag C: GeometryDefined for the RetinalFrame family, ImageFrame, NormalizedImageFrame, PixelFrame, and TargetPlaneFrame; ModelDefined for DistortedFrame. Downstream packages minting their own frame tags add a method; there is no default — an unclassified frame tag is a MethodError, not a silent guess. See FrameDependence for the commensurability rule the trait encodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.frame_dependence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/stack_contracts/#Configuration-Tags-and-Stack-Contracts-API","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"TagRegistry is the one TOML tag → type map; CONTRACT_GENERICS and the dependency allow-list are what the quality gates (test_quality_gates) check across the stack; the conformance helpers test an implementation of the contracts of the Robust Estimation API page (their methods load with Test).","title":"Configuration Tags and Stack Contracts API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/stack_contracts/#Quality-gates","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"The checks every stack package's quality items run: the naming rules, the rows Jacobian check, the gate bundle, the agent-docs policy and the one copy of the conventions. check_naming and test_quality_gates load with Test (the Test extension).","title":"Quality gates"},{"category":"section","location":"api/stack_contracts/#Index","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"Pages = [\"stack_contracts.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"TagRegistry(family::Symbol, suffix::AbstractString, pairs::Pair{<:AbstractString}...;\n            retired = (), paths = Regex[])\nTagRegistry(family::Symbol, suffix::AbstractString, pairs::AbstractVector{<:Pair};\n            retired = (), paths = Regex[])\n\nThe ONLY structure a configuration loader uses to map a TOML tag to a type (or, rarely, a value). registry[tag] returns the type; a retired tag throws ArgumentError(\"tag \\\"keep\\\" was renamed \\\"keep_model\\\"\"), and an unknown tag throws an ArgumentError that lists the known tags.\n\nfamily names the family (:optimization_strategy, :loss) in messages.\nsuffix is the family's common type-name suffix (\"Shape\" for TLSShape), stripped before the spelling rule; \"\" for none.\npairs are tag => type, as arguments or one vector (a registry built from a table: no splat of a data-length collection, check_splat). The one value that is not a type is nothing, whose tag is \"none\".\nretired are old => new renames: the old tag no longer loads, and the error names its successor.\npaths are the TOML key paths whose string values are tags of this family, matched whole against the dotted path of a value (\"lo.arms[2].type\"; an array element is [i], counted from 1). The config gate reads them.\n\nThe tag rule: a type's tag is snake_case of its name with suffix stripped from its end when present (the whole name otherwise), and nothing is \"none\". Construction does not check it — a package's load never fails on a spelling — tag_rule_violations does, and the quality gate runs it over every registry constant. A duplicate tag, or a retired tag that is also current, is a construction error.\n\nkeys, values, haskey, get, length and iteration (tag => type pairs, in declaration order) cover the current tags only; tag_of is the reverse lookup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#Base.merge-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry, Vararg{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry}}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"merge(a::TagRegistry, b::TagRegistry...) -> TagRegistry\n\nThe registry of one family that two packages share — the core's tags and a downstream package's (merge(VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.OPTIMIZATION_STRATEGY_TAGS, LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS)): every current and retired tag of each, and their key paths. The family and its suffix must agree, and a tag declared by two of them (or current in one and retired in another) is an ArgumentError, as within one registry. There is no global registry: a caller that loads several packages' tags merges them explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.merge"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.config_tag_violations-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"config_tag_violations(registries, config_dirs) -> Vector{String}\n\nParse every .toml under config_dirs (recursively); every string value at a key path one of the TagRegistrys claims (tag_paths, matched whole) must be a current tag of that registry — not a retired tag and not an unknown one. Each finding is \"file:path: message\". A file that does not parse is a finding. registries is a collection of registries or of name => registry pairs (the form tag_registries returns).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.config_tag_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.expected_tag-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry, Any}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"expected_tag(registry::TagRegistry, value) -> String\n\nThe tag the rule gives value in registry: \"none\" for nothing, otherwise snake_case of its type name with the registry's suffix stripped from the end when present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.expected_tag"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retired_tags-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"retired_tags(registry::TagRegistry) -> Vector{Pair{String, String}}\n\nThe old => new renames of a TagRegistry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retired_tags"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.snake_case-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"snake_case(name::Symbol) -> String\nsnake_case(name::AbstractString) -> String\n\nThe tag spelling of a CamelCase name, by exactly two rules, then lowercase:\n\nan _ goes before an uppercase letter that follows a lowercase letter or a digit;\nan _ goes before the last uppercase letter of an uppercase run when a lowercase letter follows it.\n\nA digit stays with its letters.\n\nsnake_case(:FixedS2)              # \"fixed_s2\"\nsnake_case(:SmoothTLS)            # \"smooth_tls\"\nsnake_case(:WelschHQNewton)       # \"welsch_hq_newton\"\nsnake_case(:StudentTUniform)      # \"student_t_uniform\"\nsnake_case(:TerminalPlacement)    # \"terminal_placement\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.snake_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_family-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"tag_family(registry::TagRegistry) -> Symbol\n\nThe family a TagRegistry names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_family"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_of-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry, Any}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"tag_of(registry::TagRegistry, x) -> String\n\nThe writer side of a TagRegistry: the tag of x. x is a registered value (nothing → \"none\"), a type registered as such, a subtype of a registered type (Irls{2} under a registered Irls; the most specific registered supertype wins) or an instance of one. An ArgumentError when nothing matches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_paths-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"tag_paths(registry::TagRegistry) -> Vector{Regex}\n\nThe TOML key paths whose string values are tags of registry's family.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_paths"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_registries-Tuple{Module}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"tag_registries(M::Module) -> Vector{Pair{String, TagRegistry}}\n\nEvery TagRegistry constant of M and its submodules, as \"Module.NAME\" => registry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_registries"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_rule_violations-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TagRegistry}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"tag_rule_violations(registry::TagRegistry) -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery way registry breaks the tag rule (see TagRegistry): a tag that is not its value's expected_tag, and a retired tag whose successor is not a current tag. Empty when the registry obeys it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_rule_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_table_violations-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"tag_table_violations(dir) -> Vector{String}\n\nA heuristic source scan of the .jl files under dir (a package's src/) for tag tables built outside a TagRegistry: a Dict{String, <:Type}-style table (keyed by a string or symbol, valued by Type, DataType or UnionAll), or a \"tag\" => SomeType pair — a string literal paired with a CamelCase name that is not called and is not a Base or Core type (\"cutoff\" => Float32 is a key's converter, not a tag). TagRegistry(...) calls, comments and triple-quoted strings are not scanned. Each finding is \"file:line: text\", relative to dir.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tag_table_violations"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CONTRACT_GENERICS","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"CONTRACT_GENERICS\n\nThe generic functions of VisualGeometryToolkitCore that another package may add methods to across a package boundary — its extension points. Any stack package may declare its own CONTRACT_GENERICS (a tuple or vector of functions); the quality gate (contract_generic_violations) requires every method a package adds to another stack package's function to land on one of that owner's contracts. Functions owned by Base, Core, the standard library or any package outside the stack are exempt.\n\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore's, by group:\n\nthe block-coordinate-descent hooks and readers (update_model!, …, current_s2);\nthe chart-LM row protocol (residual_rows!, residual_jacobian_rows!) and its reductions (residual_cost!, residual_normal_equations!);\nthe RANSAC / local-optimization interface, the constraint-problem protocol, the scoring and local-optimization contracts, pool selection and the chart-rows contract;\nthe minimal-solver backends PoseLib.jl implements;\nthe FitResult property forwarding and the TOML config hooks;\nthe geometry and camera accessors other packages implement for their own types (centroid, intrinsics, lens, model, division_coeffs, …);\nthe parameter protocol (parameter_vector, parameter_names, parameter_bounds, with_parameters);\nthe target-calibration protocol (regularizer, is_planar) and the target accessors calibration and design;\nthe surface protocol (intersection, normal, chart, surface_point, bounds) and surface, which builds a posed target's primitive;\nthe camera maps project and backproject, and convert_eltype;\nthe Manifold protocol: tangent_size, retract and recenter (required), local_coordinates, retract_jacobian, action_jacobian, zero_tangent, restrict, tangent_part and transform_covariance (optional), and minimal_manifold and ambient_coordinates, which a downstream model type answers (a model that is not a static array or a tuple of them adds ambient_coordinates rather than overriding retract_jacobian);\nthe plotting stubs the Makie extensions implement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CONTRACT_GENERICS"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.contract_generic_violations-Tuple{Module}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"contract_generic_violations(M::Module) -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery function owned by another loaded stack package (a key of stack_dependencies) that has a method defined in M or one of its submodules but is not in its owner's CONTRACT_GENERICS (a package without one has no contracts). Constructors of another package's types are not counted (Aqua's piracy check covers them). Each finding is \"Owner.f (file:line)\", located at the first such method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.contract_generic_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.private_name_violations-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"private_name_violations(dir, name) -> Vector{String}\n\nA heuristic source scan of the .jl files under dir (a package's src/ and ext/) for names that begin with _ and are reached from another stack package (a key of stack_dependencies other than name): imported (using Pkg: _x, import Pkg.Sub: _x) or accessed qualified (Pkg._x, Pkg.Sub._x, also through a const P = Pkg or import Pkg as P alias). Comments and string literals are not scanned. Each finding is \"file:line: Pkg._x\", relative to dir.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.private_name_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stack_dependencies-Tuple{}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"stack_dependencies(; extra = nothing) -> Dict{String, Vector{String}}\n\nThe stack's dependency allow-list (configs/stack_dependencies.toml in VisualGeometryToolkitCore): each project's name → the stack packages its Project.toml may name. A stack package is any key of this table.\n\nThe table lists the published packages and paper projects only. A project kept elsewhere supplies its own row as extra: a path to a TOML file of rows in the same format, or a dictionary name → packages. Extra rows add projects; one that repeats a row of the table is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stack_dependencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stack_dependency_violations-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"stack_dependency_violations(project_toml_path, name; extra = nothing) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe stack packages the Project.toml at project_toml_path names — in [deps], [weakdeps], [extras] or the test target — that are not in name's row of stack_dependencies (with the extra rows, as there), one \"section: Package\" per finding. A project absent from the table is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stack_dependency_violations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_constraint_problem","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_constraint_problem(problem, θ)\n\nThe constraint-problem protocol on problem at the model θ, as tests: data_size/codimension/minimal_sample_size integers, mahalanobis_distances! type-stable, allocation-free after warm-up, ≥ 0, and equal to mahalanobis_distance at every point to rounding: rtol = 1e-11 of the point's distance or 1e-14 of the largest finite distance (a non-finite one identical); and whitened_residual in the constraint's shape at every point — a number at d_g = 1, d_g components otherwise, so a scalar override at d_g ≥ 2 (a distance under the residual's name, the pre-0.7 hook) is refused — and with its norm equal to the point's distance to the same rounding (a non-finite distance, a gate's, matched by a non-finite residual), so the two hooks describe one point. The rounding is of the observations, not of the residual: a reprojection residual is a difference of ~1000 px coordinates, so two paths differ by ~1000·eps px whatever the residual (PnP measured 4.8e-14 px on a 1.08 px residual, E 5.7e-14 px; 2026-11). Not bit for bit: the batch loop and a single call may contract StaticArrays' muladds into FMAs differently once the loop vectorizes, so a bitwise rule held for a problem only by forbidding inlining (PnP's @noinline, 15–22% per point) — result accuracy, not bit accuracy. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_constraint_problem"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_local_optimization_contract","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_local_optimization_contract([T = Float64,] lo, problem, θ, scoring; allocations = true)\n\nThe local-optimization contract on lo for problem from the hypothesis θ under scoring, in the element type T (the workspace's and score buffers'), as tests: lo_workspace built once, locally_optimize! type-stable, returning a lo_estimate result of the problem's model type whose termination record satisfies test_termination_record, honest about residuals_current (the buffers then hold the refined model's residuals), allocation-free after warm-up (allocations = true). Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_local_optimization_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_manifold_contract","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_manifold_contract(m::Manifold; atol = 1e-12, allocations = true)\n\nThe manifold contract on m, as tests — what an optimizer that holds m as its variable relies on: the tangent size a constant of the type (zero_tangent inferred, static); recenter inferred to typeof(m) for a step of m's own float type in any static container (SVector, MVector; a plain Vector returns that type too) and allocation-free (allocations = false records a known allocation as @test_broken); retract_jacobian at the anchor and at a step equal to ForwardDiff through retract within atol; and the chart's own invariants (the essential chart's translation on the unit sphere after a step). Run it on every manifold family a package defines. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_manifold_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_scoring_contract","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_scoring_contract(scoring, residuals; n = length(residuals), d_g = 1, sample = nothing)\n\nThe scoring contract on scoring over one residual vector, as tests: score! type-stable and allocation-free after warm-up and deterministic; inlier_count in 0:n; consensus_order a permutation of 1:n; the derived consensus_set and consensus_s2 inferred; check_workspace accepting the scorer's own workspace and refusing a foreign one; decision_boundary a Float64 where has_decision_boundary; and, for a bounded-sum scorer (score_bound), the early exit tie-safe: a bound the score beats or ties never changes it, and an exit returns a value at or below its bound. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_scoring_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_termination_record","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_termination_record(result)\n\nThe termination record of one fit result, as tests: retcode a ReturnCode ReturnCode.T, stop_rule a StopRule, converged a Bool and retcode_symbol(result) a Symbol, each inferred; converged(result) (and the property result.converged) is successful_retcode(retcode(result)), and retcode_symbol(result) is retcode_symbol(code, rule). test_local_optimization_contract runs it on the refit it checks; a producer's suite runs it on one result of each of its verbs and paths (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's and PoseLib's termination-record items cover every fit_*, find_* and refine_*). Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_termination_record"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NamingViolation","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"NamingViolation\n\nOne finding of check_naming: the file (relative to the package directory), the line, the offending name or line of text, and the rule it breaks (:R3 … :R12, or :parse for a file that does not parse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NamingViolation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_naming","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"check_naming(pkgdir) -> Vector{NamingViolation}\n\nThe stack's naming rules, checked over a package directory. Implemented by the VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt extension: using Test loads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_naming"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.naming_scope","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"naming_scope(dir) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe files check_naming reads under dir. Implemented by the VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt extension: using Test loads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.naming_scope"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RECORDED_NESTED_STATES","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"RECORDED_NESTED_STATES\n\nThe names of the nested state types test_recorded compares field by field by default (the posterior weights, the division camera's IRLS state, the GNC ladder); every other non-bits field of a state is a workspace it points into.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RECORDED_NESTED_STATES"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReadmeExample","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"ReadmeExample(page, lang, row, illustrative, code)\n\nOne fenced code block of a documentation page (readme_examples): the page (relative to the package root), its lang (\"julia\", \"python\", \"bash\"), the install row its first line names (# Install row: <row>; \"\" when it names none), whether it is illustrative (its first line # Illustrative, the row on the second: a block that cannot run as written) and its code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReadmeExample"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_rows_jacobian","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"check_rows_jacobian(lm, θ, m; atol = 0, rtol = 0, reference = ForwardDiff.jacobian,\n                    jacobian = Matrix{Float64}(undef, m, length(θ))) -> Bool\n\nDoes the analytic residual_jacobian_rows! of lm at θ (m rows) match a reference Jacobian of residual_rows! to maximum|J − J_ref| ≤ atol + rtol·max(1, maximum|J_ref|)? reference(f, θ) returns the Jacobian of f at θ: ForwardDiff by default; where a Dual cannot flow through the rows, a finite difference such as (f, θ) -> FiniteDiff.finite_difference_jacobian(f, θ, Val(:central)). jacobian is the buffer the analytic Jacobian is written into — a dense matrix by default; a state whose method takes another representation (a fixed-pattern SparseMatrixCSC) is checked in that one. On a miss the two errors are logged. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_rows_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_lines","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"install_lines(root, page) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe packages of every pkg> add … line of the page at root/page (each line's text after add), in page order: what a README test compares with an install row's install_packages. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_lines"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_packages","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"install_packages(entry) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe packages of the pkg add  line in an install table's entry; empty when it holds none. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_packages"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_table","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"install_table(path, tasks) -> Dict{String, String}\n\nThe install table of the page at path: each row | task | entry | … | whose task is one of tasks, as task => entry. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.julia_blocks","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"julia_blocks(path) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe code of every julia fenced block of the page at path, in page order (a block's opening fence exactly julia, so a julia-repl block — an install line — is not one; the code without its fences). What a README test compares between the README and the landing page, and runs. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.julia_blocks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loaded_packages","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"loaded_packages(code) -> Set{String}\n\nThe packages Julia code loads (using/import lines, a trailing comment ignored), the standard library left out: what an install line must add for the code to run. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loaded_packages"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.page_examples","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"page_examples(examples, page; lang = \"julia\") -> Vector{String}\n\nThe code of the lang examples of one page, in page order, from readme_examples's records: what a README test compares between the README, the landing page and a guide (\"one example, shown on every page\"). Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.page_examples"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.readme_environment","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"readme_environment(root, packages; developed = Dict(), setup = \"\") -> String\n\nA fresh environment holding exactly packages, as a reader's install line builds it: the package at root and those named in developed (name => path, the stack's unregistered checkouts) by path, the rest from the registries; setup runs after the adds (a runtime pin), then the environment is precompiled. Built in a child Julia (run_readme_child); @test that it builds. Returns its directory. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.readme_environment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.readme_examples","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"readme_examples(root, pages; langs = (\"julia\", \"python\", \"bash\")) -> Vector{ReadmeExample}\n\nThe fenced code blocks of langs in each of pages (paths relative to root), in page order, as ReadmeExamples. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.readme_examples"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rows_cost","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"rows_cost(lm, θ, m) -> Real\n\nThe chart-LM cost Σ rᵢ² of a linearization state lm at chart point θ, its m rows filled by residual_rows!. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rows_cost"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.run_readme_child","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"run_readme_child(env, args...) -> Int\n\nRun Julia with args (a script, or \"-e\", code) in the environment env, a child Julia with the test run's flags (Base.julia_cmd(): --depwarn=error, coverage) and a load path of the project and the standard library only, so no default environment supplies a package the install line does not add. Returns its exit code. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.run_readme_child"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sensor_fixture","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"sensor_fixture(; body, iso = 100, exposure = 1.0s, black = nothing, black_std_error = nothing, dark = nothing,\n               gain = nothing, gain_std_error = nothing, read_noise = nothing, read_noise_std_error = nothing,\n               relative_noise = nothing, defects = nothing, source = SensorSource.specified)\n    -> (; characterisation, capture, model)\nsensor_fixture(characterisations::SensorCharacterisation...) -> SensorCharacterisation\n\nTHE builder of sensor-model fixtures for tests (owner ruling D13): a test never constructs a sensor component, a SensorCharacterisation or a SensorModel itself (checkduplication's `:sensorcomponentpattern; the absent modelSensorModel()excepted), it describes one here in plain numbers. The slots, each absent when its keywords arenothing`:\n\nblack: black (a 4-tuple per colour index) → ChannelBlack (with black_std_error); dark = (; offset, variance, exposure_band, frames) → a Dark over that channel black (the FileBlack without black);\nnoise: read_noise → ReadNoise (with read_noise_std_error); with a gain → PoissonGaussian (gain_std_error and read_noise_std_error its standard errors); relative_noise → RelativeNoise;\ndefects: defects = (; x, y, onset, size) → DefectList.\n\nsource is one SensorSource.T for every slot or a named tuple by slot ((; black = SensorSource.measured), specified where it names none). Returns the characterisation of body with that one entry at iso, a one-frame capture (frame 1 at iso and exposure, f/8, 14 mm) and the frame's model, resolved by sensor_model. The second form merges characterisations of one body (their ISOs) into one. The components' own ArgumentErrors reach the caller. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sensor_fixture"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_docs","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_docs(M::Module; exceptions = Dict{String,String}(), backlog = Set{String}(),\n          signature_exceptions = Set{String}(), dirs = (\"src\",)) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe docstring gates the Documenter build cannot run (its checkdocs = :public sees only docstrings that exist), over package M, as tests:\n\nevery public name of M and its submodules has a docstring, except the exceptions (name => reason: undocumented on purpose) and the backlog (names still to document; the list only shrinks — a name documented must leave it); a stale exception is a failure;\nno docstring under M's dirs (relative to its package directory; a missing one is skipped) is detached from its definition — a bare string statement the parser did not attach, after a blank or comment line;\nevery signature line of a docstring is accepted by a method of its name (methods a test item defined under Main are not the package's), and a docstring that shows a sibling's signature leaves the sibling documented on its own, except the signature_exceptions (stale ones fail).\n\nA name in a submodule is Sub.name. The exceptions are logged with @info. Returns the findings, (; undocumented, detached, mismatches). Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_docs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_duplication","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_duplication(M::Module; kwargs...)\n\nThe stack's no-duplication rule (check_duplication) over package M, as a test: every finding printed, and @test that there is none. The keywords go to check_duplication (the stack repositories compared come from VGTK_STACK_PATHS by default). Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_duplication"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_naming","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_naming(M::Module)\n\nThe stack's naming rules (check_naming) over M's package directory, as a test: every finding printed, and @test that there is none. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_naming"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_quality_gates","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_quality_gates(M::Module; jet_bound, treat_as_own = [], stale_ignore = (),\n                   qualified_ignore = nothing, persistent_tasks = true,\n                   tag_rules = :enforce, tag_tables = :enforce, config_tags = :enforce,\n                   config_dirs = nothing, registries = (), stack_dependencies = :enforce,\n                   stack_name = string(nameof(M)), stack_extra = nothing,\n                   public_names = :enforce, third_party_public = :report,\n                   contract_generics = :enforce, splat = :report)\n\nThe stack's static quality gates over package M, as one test set — RATCHETS: each bound is the value the package meets today and only ever moves down.\n\nAqua: undefined exports, unbound type parameters, stale deps, deps compat, project extras, persistent tasks (persistent_tasks = false skips it), no method ambiguities involving a method of M (pairs touching a test item's Main methods are dropped and logged), and no piracy in M or any of its submodules beyond treat_as_own.\nExplicitImports: no implicit imports, explicit imports via their owners, no stale explicit imports beyond stale_ignore; with qualified_ignore (a tuple of names) also every qualified access public, those names excepted.\nJET: report_package(M) counts at most jet_bound reports, not counting KernelAbstractions LAUNCH reports (a kwcall(::NamedTuple{(:ndrange,)}, ::Kernel, …) no-method report, which JET raises at every launch site because the backend is known only at run time). The filter is checked against a real launch and two decoys every run.\nStack contracts, each gate in a mode — :enforce (@test that it finds nothing), :report (every finding logged with @warn and the gate recorded @test_broken; a gate that finds nothing is an ordinary test) or :off:\ntag_rules: every TagRegistry constant of M and its submodules obeys the tag rule (tag_rule_violations);\ntag_tables: no tag table outside a TagRegistry in M's src/ (tag_table_violations, a heuristic scan);\nconfig_tags: every string at a registry's key path in the TOML files under config_dirs (default: M's configs/, when it exists) is a current tag of M's registries and of the extra registries (config_tag_violations);\nstack_dependencies: M's Project.toml names only the stack packages in stack_name's row of the allow-list (stack_dependency_violations); a project kept outside the published stack gives its own row as stack_extra (the extra of stack_dependencies);\npublic_names: no _-prefixed name reached from another stack package (private_name_violations over src/ and ext/);\nthird_party_public (default :report): ExplicitImports' check_all_explicit_imports_are_public and check_all_qualified_accesses_are_public (with qualified_ignore) — a non-public name of ANY module, the stack's or a third party's (ForwardDiff's Dual, StaticArraysCore's StaticArray);\ncontract_generics: every method M adds to another stack package's function lands on that owner's CONTRACT_GENERICS (contract_generic_violations);\nsplat (default :report): no call splats a collection whose length is data — a Vector, a generator, an abstract type — which compiles the callee once per length (check_splat over M and its loaded extensions; exceptions are DUPLICATION.toml's [[splat]] entries).\n\nImplemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreQualityExt: using Test, Aqua, ExplicitImports, JET loads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_quality_gates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_readme_example","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_readme_example(root; index = \"docs/src/index.md\", install_pages = (), developed = Dict(), checks = \"\")\n\nThe README's one example, as tests: README.md holds one Julia block and one pkg> add line; the landing page index shows the same block, it and each of install_pages the same line; the line adds exactly the packages the block loads (loaded_packages); and the block runs in a fresh environment built from that line (readme_environment), expression by expression, every expression whose source ends # true returning true, then checks — code run with readme the example's module. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_readme_example"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_readme_row","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_readme_row(env, task, blocks; preamble = \"\", after = \"\")\n\nRun each Julia example of blocks (code strings) in the environment env, each in a module of its own, so each is shown complete on its own; a block whose last line ends # true must return true. preamble is top-level code run first (an import the assertions need); after is code run in the same child after the examples, mods their modules in order (the row's assertions). @test that the child succeeds. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_readme_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_recorded","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_recorded(make; nested = RECORDED_NESTED_STATES) -> Int\n\nThat a Recorded run of block_coordinate_descent! IS the bare run, as tests: make() returns a fresh (state, likelihood, method, problem, θ₀, max_iter) each call (fresh workspaces too), whose result has estimate, retcode and iterations. The recorded run returns the bare run's estimate, retcode and iteration count and leaves the same state; it records one iterate per good iteration (a :solve_failed run one fewer), with as many costs and scales; and the bare run truncated after every k has the recorded run's k-th cost — iterate for iterate. A state is compared by what a run moves: its numbers, arrays and best iterates (Attributed(θ, (; cost))), and the fields of the nested state types whose names are in nested; anything else it points into is skipped. Returns the number of recorded iterates. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_recorded"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_splat","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"test_splat(M::Module; mode = :enforce, extensions = true)\n\nThe stack's splat rule (check_splat) over package M, as a test in a gate mode: :enforce (@test that there is no finding), :report (each finding logged with @warn and the test recorded @test_broken; none found, an ordinary test) or :off. extensions goes to check_splat. A package on test_quality_gates gets the same check as its splat gate. Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_splat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.thrown_message","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"thrown_message(f; shown = false) -> String\n\nThe message of the ArgumentError that f() throws, \"\" when it throws nothing; any other exception is rethrown. With shown = true, any exception is caught and its shown text (sprint(showerror, e), its type included) is returned. For a test that reads what a refusal says: @test occursin(\"frame\", thrown_message(() -> load(T, path))). Implemented by VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt (using Test loads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.thrown_message"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AGENT_DOC_FORBIDDEN","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"AGENT_DOC_FORBIDDEN\n\nThe patterns no agent instruction file of a stack package may match (agent_doc_violations): absolute paths into a user's home directory, private keys and access tokens.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AGENT_DOC_FORBIDDEN"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.agent_doc_violations-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"agent_doc_violations(root; forbidden = AGENT_DOC_FORBIDDEN, extra = nothing) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe lines of the agent instruction files under the package directory root — CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and anything under .claude/, the files git tracks when root is a checkout — that match a pattern of forbidden or extra, one \"file:line matches pattern\" per finding, plus \"CLAUDE.md: missing\" when the package has none. These files ship with the package: they carry its conventions and nothing private.\n\nextra adds patterns: a Regex, a collection of them, or the path to a text file of one regular expression per line (blank lines and # comments skipped; an empty path adds none). The stack's tests call it with extra = get(ENV, \"AGENT_DOC_PATTERNS\", nothing), so release tooling supplies its own list by setting that variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.agent_doc_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.convention_files-Tuple{}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"convention_files() -> Vector{String}\n\nThe absolute paths of the convention files in conventions_dir, in reading order (the order of the docs' Conventions page).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.convention_files"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.conventions_dir-Tuple{}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"conventions_dir() -> String\n\nThe conventions/ folder of this VisualGeometryToolkitCore installation: the one canonical copy of the conventions every stack package follows (naming, signatures, data arguments, coordinate frames and normalization, pixel origins, bearings, observation noise, residuals and distances, persisted geometry, Jacobians, units, stack contracts). A downstream package finds the installed text here rather than carrying a copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.conventions_dir"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DuplicationViolation","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"DuplicationViolation\n\nOne finding of check_duplication: the check that found it (:name, :similar, :file, :pattern, :backend_branch, :twin_name or :stale_exception), the file (relative to the repository root) and line, the text found (a name, a function, a code line or an exception entry), and other — the upstream owner, the matching function or file elsewhere (<Repository>:<path>:<line> outside this repository, a monorepo's member as <Member>:<path>:<line>), or the owner a retired pattern names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DuplicationViolation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_duplication","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"check_duplication(M::Module; kwargs...) -> Vector{DuplicationViolation}\ncheck_duplication(dir; stack = <ENV[\"VGTK_STACK_PATHS\"]>, similarity = ((0.6, 60), (0.85, 25)),\n                  shingle = 8, min_file_tokens = 25) -> Vector{DuplicationViolation}\n\nThe stack's no-duplication rule, checked over a package (M, its directory pkgdir(M)) or a directory dir. Six checks:\n\nName ownership (the Module form only). Every binding M (or a submodule of it) owns — defined there, not imported, a function or type whose parentmodule is that module; method extensions of another package's function do not count — whose name an upstream stack package also owns, private _names included (Julia's per-module eval, include and __init__, and the per-package CONTRACT_GENERICS, are each package's own). Upstream is every package in M's dependency closure (its loaded Project.toml [deps], transitively) that is VisualGeometryToolkitCore or depends on it: Core < IO, Features < Estimation < Eval < the downstream repositories.\nFunction-body similarity. Every function and macro definition in the Julia files is tokenized with Julia's own tokenizer (JuliaSyntax), its comments and whitespace dropped, each identifier mapped to one symbol and each string piece to another, and cut into shingle-token shingles. Two definitions in different files are a finding when their shingle sets' Jaccard index reaches a threshold of similarity, each a pair (jaccard, min_tokens) that both bodies' token counts must reach: by default J ≥ 0.6 with at least 60 tokens, or J ≥ 0.85 with at least 25. (A shingle shared by more than 40 definitions — a common idiom — is not counted towards any pair.) Compared within the repository and against stack.\nWhole files. A Julia file whose token text, comments and whitespace dropped (at least min_file_tokens tokens), equals another file's.\nRetired patterns. A line of code (comments and docstrings left out) that spells a pattern whose owner lives elsewhere: NLLSOptions( (Core's LmStops, mapped in lmsolve_chart!), acos(clamp((tr(… (Rotations' rotation_angle), the sRGB transfer constants (Features' src/linear_image.jl), and a \\newcommand writer (Eval's src/reproduce/number_macros.jl). The failure names the owner.\nBackend branches (:backend_branch). A GPU branch's CPU branch is the SAME KernelAbstractions kernel on the CPU backend, so a definition that tests the backend to run different code is a finding: backend isa CPU (KA.CPU, GPU), get_backend(x) isa … (or ==), x isa CuArray (AbstractGPUArray and the other GPU array types), == CPU(); and a pair of methods of one function, one on a CPU type (::CPU) and one on a GPU type (::GPU, ::Backend, ::CuArray). Allowed, so not flagged: the same kernel launched on whatever get_backend returns, and the transfer and allocation helpers, by their WHOLE names: every _-separated word of the name is a transfer or allocation word (an upload or download, to_host, to_device, on_device, zeros, ones, fill, an allocation, a synchronization, adapt, a copy, the device queries), so zeros_on is a helper and fill_sqrt_weights! or transfer_ellipse is not.\nCPU/GPU twins (:twin_name). Two bindings of the package whose names differ only by a CPU/GPU marker: _cpu/_gpu, _host/_device, cpu_/gpu_, or _cuda beside the plain name (the transfer helpers' whole names excepted).\n\nThe repository is the git checkout holding dir. Inside a monorepo the other packages' files are compared too (the package's own files are the ones checked; a pair or a file found twice is reported by each package). stack lists other stack repositories' root directories (by default the paths in the environment variable VGTK_STACK_PATHS, separated by :, or ; on Windows); those absent are skipped with a note, so a CI checkout checks itself only. Files are the ones naming_scope reads (vendored trees out).\n\nExceptions live in the repository root's DUPLICATION.toml, each with its reason; an entry about this package that matches nothing is itself a finding:\n\n[[name]]                  # a binding M owns that an upstream package also owns\nmodule = \"MyPackage\"\nname = \"foo\"\nreason = \"…\"\n\n[[similar]]               # two functions, `<path>::<name>` (another repository's `<Repository>:<path>::<name>`)\nfunctions = [\"src/a.jl::f\", \"test/b.jl::g\"]\nreason = \"…\"\n\n[[file]]                  # two files (paths as above)\nfiles = [\"test/a.jl\", \"OtherRepo:test/a.jl\"]\nreason = \"…\"\n\n[[pattern]]               # a retired pattern in one file\nuse = \"rotation_angle\"\nfile = \"src/a.jl\"\nreason = \"…\"\n\n[[backend]]               # a backend branch in one function of one file\nfile = \"src/a.jl\"\nfunction = \"f\"\nreason = \"…\"\n\n[[twin]]                  # two names that differ by a CPU/GPU marker\nnames = [\"f_cpu\", \"f_gpu\"]\nreason = \"…\"\n\nImplemented by the VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt extension: using Test loads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_duplication"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RawDecodingViolation","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"RawDecodingViolation\n\nOne finding of check_raw_decoding: the file (relative to the scanned directory), the line, its code text, and the use found — :rawpy, :libraw (a LibRaw C call), :dcraw, :postprocess (rawpy's), :demosaic, :cfa_interpolation (a Bayer/CFA interpolation by another name) or :png_raw_output (a decoded RAW output re-read as an image, PNGFiles.load or Gray.(…)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RawDecodingViolation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_raw_decoding","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"check_raw_decoding(dir; allow = ()) -> Vector{RawDecodingViolation}\n\nThe stack's one-RAW-path rule, checked over the code under dir (the files naming_scope reads, less prose: Julia, Python, shell, TOML and YAML files, with comments and docstrings left out). Flags any use, outside VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' raw module, of rawpy, a LibRaw C call (libraw_…), dcraw, rawpy's postprocess(, a demosaic, a Bayer/CFA interpolation, or PNGFiles.load/Gray.(…) on a decoded RAW output (a line naming linear, decoded, prepared, bin2, green or raw_ images). Line-level matching: a finding is a line to port, read by a person. The raw module's own files (VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' src/raw_image.jl, its LibRawjll extension and their tests; VisualGeometryToolkitCore's src/raw/ sensor image and its test) are the one allowed place; they are recognized when dir is the owning package's directory, so scan each package of a monorepo from its own directory (from the repository root they are flagged). The Features API asked for its display demosaic, `loadraw(…; mode = :demosaic)orLinearRaw(…; mode = :demosaic)`, is the approved display path in every repository and is not flagged: the mode keyword inside that call's own parentheses, over every line the call spans; a demosaic by any other route, on the same line or not, is.\n\nallow lists a repository's genuine exceptions, each a path relative to dir or a Regex over such paths (one exception may be given bare). They should be rare: a file is allowed only when it cannot go through the Features raw module (say, a third-party script kept verbatim), never as a way to keep a second decoding path.\n\nImplemented by the VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt extension: using Test loads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_raw_decoding"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.githooks_dir-Tuple{}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"githooks_dir() -> String\n\nThe githooks/ folder of this VisualGeometryToolkitCore installation: the one copy of the stack's git hooks (sync-manifest, and post-checkout, post-merge, post-rewrite linking to it), which install_githooks installs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.githooks_dir"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_githooks","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"install_githooks(repo = pwd(); hooks = githooks_dir()) -> String\n\nPoint the git working tree repo's core.hooksPath at hooks (default: this VisualGeometryToolkitCore's githooks/, whose sync-manifest re-resolves the repository's root Manifest through sync_manifest whenever a checkout, merge or rebase changes a Project.toml). Run once per clone; the hooks keep the path on the resolved VisualGeometryToolkitCore afterwards. Returns the hooks path set. The git config is the repository's, so it holds in every worktree of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_githooks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sync_manifest","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"sync_manifest(mode = \"update\"; io = stdout, repo = nothing) -> NamedTuple{(:created, :moved, :seconds)}\n\nRe-resolve the active project after git moved a Project.toml: mode = \"update\" runs a full Pkg.update() (an untracked Manifest: a targeted update fails on an illegal Manifest), mode = \"instantiate\" a Pkg.instantiate() (a tracked Manifest: download only). Prints to io what moved and returns created (no Manifest held a direct dependency before), moved (each direct dependency whose version changed, (name, before, after), before === nothing for a new one) and seconds. With repo (a git working tree whose core.hooksPath install_githooks set to an installed VisualGeometryToolkitCore's hooks), the hooks path is moved to the newly resolved VisualGeometryToolkitCore's, so a Core update never leaves the repository on a copy that Pkg.gc may delete.\n\nThe git hooks run it (githooks/sync-manifest) by including this file by path, without loading the package; it is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's for every other caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sync_manifest"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gpu_problem-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"gpu_problem(prefix::AbstractString; min_free_bytes::Integer = 20 * 2^30) -> Union{Nothing, String}\n\nOn a GPU-tier worker: nothing when the runner decided to run the tier (<prefix>_GPU_DECIDED=1, gpu_tier) and the accelerator is still functional with min_free_bytes free; otherwise the reason the GPU items cannot run, as a sentence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gpu_problem"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gpu_tier-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"gpu_tier(prefix::AbstractString; min_free_bytes::Integer = 20 * 2^30) -> Bool\n\nThe test runner's ONE decision whether to run a package's GPU tier: true when an accelerator is the default device (default_device is not KA.CPU(), the backend require_gpu hands the items, so the vendor package — CUDA — must be loaded and functional before it is first resolved), the environment does not opt out (<prefix>_TEST_GPU=0) and the device has at least min_free_bytes free (device_free_bytes). A yes is handed to the GPU tier's worker processes as <prefix>_GPU_DECIDED=1 (set here, in ENV); a no logs why (@info) and clears it (a verdict inherited from an earlier process is not this one's). The GPU tier runs its items on ONE worker per device (RadiometricCamera's rule: two processes on one card fail spuriously), so each worker's re-check of the free memory sees the device as the runner did. Called once, in test/runtests.jl, before the GPU tier; each GPU item then starts with require_gpu(prefix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gpu_tier"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.require_gpu-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"require_gpu(prefix::AbstractString; min_free_bytes::Integer = 20 * 2^30) -> KA.Backend\n\nThe first line of every GPU test item: the accelerator's backend (default_device) when gpu_problem(prefix) finds none, and an error with the reason otherwise — never a silent skip, so a GPU tier cannot succeed on the CPU path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.require_gpu"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SplatViolation","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"SplatViolation\n\nOne finding of check_splat: the check that found it (:splat; :unknown, a splat of a value inferred Any, with unknown = true; or :stale_exception for a [[splat]] entry of DUPLICATION.toml that matches nothing), the file (relative to the repository root) and line of the call, the function whose method holds it (closures and keyword bodies by their enclosing function's name; the name an exception entry gives), the callee, and the splatted argument's inferred type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SplatViolation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_splat","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"check_splat(M::Module; extensions = true, unknown = false,\n            root = <the git checkout holding pkgdir(M)>) -> Vector{SplatViolation}\n\nEvery call in package M (its submodules, and with extensions its LOADED package extensions) that splats an argument whose inferred type is not <:Union{Tuple, NamedTuple, StaticArray, Pair}: a Vector, a generator, a Dict, a ValueIterator, a String, an abstract type. Such a splat compiles its callee once per LENGTH of the data (zip(v...) is a new zip for every length(v)), and the length is the data's.\n\nTyped, not syntactic: each method whose module is one of M's is inferred at its own signature (code_typed(…; optimize = false): the calls as written, nothing inlined) and every Core._apply_iterate — the compiler's form of every f(x...), [x...], (x...,) — is checked argument by argument. So a bare name (f(rows...)) is found, and a call returning a tuple (f(_pad(k, 2)...)) is not a finding. A splat into a callee compiled once whatever its arity — a builtin such as Core.apply_type, or Expr — is not a finding either (Core.tuple is: its tuple carries the length).\n\nA value inferred Any (an untyped argument, inferred at the method's own signature) may be a tuple at every call: such a splat is reported, as check :unknown, only with unknown = true. Type the argument (a Tuple, an SVector) or hand over the collection whole.\n\nThe fixes: a method that takes the collection (linkaxes!(axes), collect(T, rows), convert(Vector{T}, xs)), reduce/mapreduce (mapreduce(typeof, typejoin, xs), reduce(vcat, parts)), vcat of collections, SVector{N}(v) for a length fixed by contract.\n\nA module that is not a package (a script's) is checked with root, the directory its files' paths and its DUPLICATION.toml are relative to.\n\nExceptions live in the repository root's DUPLICATION.toml (the file check_duplication reads), each with its reason; an entry about a file this run checked that matches nothing is itself a finding:\n\n[[splat]]                 # every splat in one function of one file\nfile = \"src/a.jl\"         # relative to the repository root\nfunction = \"f\"            # the name the finding prints\nreason = \"…\"\n\nImplemented by the VisualGeometryToolkitCoreTestExt extension: using Test loads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_splat"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/stack_contracts/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SCALE_INTERFACE_TAGS","page":"Configuration Tags & Stack Contracts","text":"SCALE_INTERFACE_TAGS\n\nThe scale-interface labels as TOML tags (a TagRegistry of ScaleInterface values' types): \"consensus_anchored\", \"fixed_scale\", \"follows_scorer\", \"self_scaling\", \"no_scale\", \"unspecified_scale\" — snake_case of the type's name. \"fixed\" and \"partition_anchored\" are retired. A config that names an interface (an ablation's delivered interface) reads it here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SCALE_INTERFACE_TAGS"},{"category":"section","location":"api/termination/#Termination-Record","page":"Termination Record","text":"Why a fit stopped is its termination record: the verdict, a ReturnCode (ReturnCode.T, SciMLBase's names and success rule), read by retcode, and the rule that fired, a StopRule, read by stop_rule. Both are in the result's core, so every producer records them. converged is strict, successful_retcode of the code: a cap or a stall is not converged. Whether a RANSAC found a model is found_model.","title":"Termination Record"},{"category":"module","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode\n\nWhy a fit stopped: the standard verdict, modelled on SciMLBase.ReturnCode (SciMLBase 3.58; the Julia ecosystem's return-code interface). The enum type is ReturnCode.T and each value a module property (ReturnCode.Success), as with SciMLBase's EnumX enum; the names, integer values and success rule (successful_retcode) are SciMLBase's, so a later SciMLBase bridge is a reinterpretation. Only the values the stack produces are defined; their meaning is SciMLBase's:\n\nReturnCode.Default — the solving process has not been run (SciMLBase's default): a record of a solve that never ran, such as a RANSAC result's local-optimization record when no local optimization ran. Not successful.\nReturnCode.Success — the solve succeeded: a tolerance or stop rule ended an iteration (an LM's step-size or rejection stop once its stationarity postcondition holds), a closed-form solve, a RANSAC that reached its confidence or drew every sample. Successful.\nReturnCode.Terminated — a user callback ended the solve (NLLSsolver's callback flags). Successful, as in SciMLBase.\nReturnCode.MaxIters — the iteration cap was reached with no tolerance met on the last iteration; for a RANSAC, the trial budget was drawn without reaching the confidence. Not successful.\nReturnCode.MaxTime — the time cap. Not successful.\nReturnCode.Stalled — an LM stopped (step size, rejections) at a point that fails the stationarity postcondition. Not successful.\nReturnCode.InitialFailure — the start was unusable: too few data or a degenerate configuration before any iteration, or a non-finite cost or gradient at the start point. Not successful.\nReturnCode.InternalLinearSolveFailed — a linear system inside the fit (a weighted least-squares or normal-equation solve) could not be solved. Not successful.\nReturnCode.Failure — the solve failed otherwise: a non-finite cost, step or gradient during it, a failed model block, or a RANSAC that found no model. Not successful.\n\nCompare with successful_retcode, never == ReturnCode.Success; the rule that fired is the result's stop_rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.T","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.T: the ReturnCode enum type (SciMLBase's ReturnCode.T).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.T"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Default","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.Default: a ReturnCode — the solving process has not been run (SciMLBase's default). Not successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Default"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Success","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.Success: a ReturnCode — the solve succeeded (a tolerance or stop rule, a closed form, a RANSAC's confidence or every sample). Successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Success"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Terminated","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.Terminated: a ReturnCode — a callback (or LODR's lifted mixture leaving its basin) ended the solve. Successful, as in SciMLBase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Terminated"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.MaxIters","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.MaxIters: a ReturnCode — the iteration cap, or a RANSAC's trial budget, with no tolerance or confidence met. Not successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.MaxIters"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.InitialFailure","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.InitialFailure: a ReturnCode — the start was unusable: too few data, a degenerate configuration, a non-finite start point. Not successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.InitialFailure"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Failure","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.Failure: a ReturnCode — the solve failed otherwise (non-finite values, a failed model block, no RANSAC model). Not successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Failure"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.MaxTime","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.MaxTime: a ReturnCode — the time cap. Not successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.MaxTime"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Stalled","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.Stalled: a ReturnCode — an LM stopped at a point that fails the stationarity postcondition. Not successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.Stalled"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.InternalLinearSolveFailed","page":"Termination Record","text":"ReturnCode.InternalLinearSolveFailed: a ReturnCode — a linear system inside the fit could not be solved. Not successful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReturnCode.InternalLinearSolveFailed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.successful_retcode","page":"Termination Record","text":"successful_retcode(code::ReturnCode.T) -> Bool\n\nWhether code is a success state — SciMLBase's rule over the values the stack defines: ReturnCode.Success and ReturnCode.Terminated. The one success test; converged reads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.successful_retcode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopRule","page":"Termination Record","text":"StopRule\n\nWhich rule ended a fit — the detail behind its ReturnCode (the verdict), read by stop_rule:\n\nNoRule — no rule fired: a run that never iterated (a degenerate start, a non-finite start point);\nClosedForm — a one-shot solve (no iteration), or a selection among fixed candidates;\nCostDecrease — the cost decrease fell below its tolerance (NLLSsolver's relative or absolute cost stop; an IRLS outer loop's robust cost; an EM's negative log-likelihood under a held model);\nIncrementSize — the update fell below its tolerance (NLLSsolver's step stop; an IRLS fit's parameter change);\nRejectionCap — NLLSsolver's consecutive cost increases (maxfails);\nParameterChange — the EM's ParameterChangeStop;\nNoImprovement — the EM's NoImprovementStop (the incumbent's cost did not fall);\nExactFit — every residual is zero;\nToleranceMet — a search met its accuracy tolerance (a spline spacing search);\nLadderConverged — a graduated-non-convexity ladder converged (LODR);\nLeftBasin — the iterate left the seed's basin of attraction (LODR);\nShapeGate — the fitted shape failed a validity gate (BlobBoards' blob fit: not a peak, or an aspect above its maximum); its code is Failure;\nIterationCap — the iteration cap;\nTimeCap — the time cap;\nNonFinite — a non-finite cost, step or gradient, or a failed model block;\nCallback — a user callback;\nTrialBudget — a RANSAC drew its whole trial budget (the cap, or every sample of an exhaustive sampler);\nAdaptiveCount — a RANSAC drew its adaptive trial count (its confidence was reached);\nSeedKept — a RANSAC's score guard kept the seed: the refined model scored below it (recorded with the local optimization's own code).\n\nAn @enum (isbits): a metadata that carries it stays concretely typed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopRule"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoRule","page":"Termination Record","text":"NoRule: a StopRule — no rule fired: the run never iterated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoRule"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ClosedForm","page":"Termination Record","text":"ClosedForm: a StopRule — a one-shot solve, or a selection among fixed candidates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ClosedForm"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CostDecrease","page":"Termination Record","text":"CostDecrease: a StopRule — the cost decrease fell below its tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CostDecrease"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IncrementSize","page":"Termination Record","text":"IncrementSize: a StopRule — the update fell below its tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IncrementSize"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RejectionCap","page":"Termination Record","text":"RejectionCap: a StopRule — NLLSsolver's consecutive cost increases (maxfails).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RejectionCap"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ParameterChange","page":"Termination Record","text":"ParameterChange: a StopRule — the EM's ParameterChangeStop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ParameterChange"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoImprovement","page":"Termination Record","text":"NoImprovement: a StopRule — the EM's NoImprovementStop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoImprovement"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ExactFit","page":"Termination Record","text":"ExactFit: a StopRule — every residual is zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ExactFit"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ToleranceMet","page":"Termination Record","text":"ToleranceMet: a StopRule — a search met its accuracy tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ToleranceMet"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LadderConverged","page":"Termination Record","text":"LadderConverged: a StopRule — a graduated-non-convexity ladder converged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LadderConverged"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LeftBasin","page":"Termination Record","text":"LeftBasin: a StopRule — the iterate left the seed's basin of attraction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LeftBasin"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ShapeGate","page":"Termination Record","text":"ShapeGate: a StopRule — the fitted shape failed a validity gate (its code is Failure).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ShapeGate"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IterationCap","page":"Termination Record","text":"IterationCap: a StopRule — the iteration cap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IterationCap"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TimeCap","page":"Termination Record","text":"TimeCap: a StopRule — the time cap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TimeCap"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NonFinite","page":"Termination Record","text":"NonFinite: a StopRule — a non-finite cost, step or gradient, or a failed model block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NonFinite"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Callback","page":"Termination Record","text":"Callback: a StopRule — a user callback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Callback"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TrialBudget","page":"Termination Record","text":"TrialBudget: a StopRule — a RANSAC drew its whole trial budget.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TrialBudget"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AdaptiveCount","page":"Termination Record","text":"AdaptiveCount: a StopRule — a RANSAC drew its adaptive trial count (its confidence was reached).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AdaptiveCount"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SeedKept","page":"Termination Record","text":"SeedKept: a StopRule — a RANSAC's score guard kept the seed (the refined model scored below it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SeedKept"},{"category":"function","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_rule","page":"Termination Record","text":"stop_rule(r::FitResult) -> StopRule\n\nWhich rule ended the fit (StopRule), the detail behind its retcode.\n\n\n\n\n\nstop_rule(criterion::StoppingCriterion, method) -> StopRule\n\nThe StopRule a criterion's stop records, for the method it stopped: NoRule by default; Core's own criteria StopAfterIteration → IterationCap, StopWhenSolveFailed → NonFinite (a failed model block). A package adds a method for each criterion it defines (and may specialise on the method). block_coordinate_descent! asks it of the member of a StopWhenAny that fired — the first whose stop_reason is not :continue, in evaluation order — and hands it to block_coordinate_result, so no consumer re-evaluates the criteria to learn which stopped the run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stop_rule"},{"category":"function","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.found_model","page":"Termination Record","text":"found_model(r::FitResult) -> Bool\n\nWhether the fit returned an estimate — a RANSAC's \"a model was found\" (estimate(r) !== nothing), whatever its retcode: a search that drew its whole trial budget without reaching its confidence still found one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.found_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retcode_symbol","page":"Termination Record","text":"retcode_symbol(code::ReturnCode.T, rule::StopRule) -> Symbol\nretcode_symbol(r::FitResult) -> Symbol\n\nThe legacy Symbol spelling of a termination record (a log line, a persisted record that names the Symbol; retcode_symbol(r) of a result) — the one map from a record to a Symbol: Success is :solved for a ClosedForm rule and :converged otherwise; Terminated :converged; MaxIters :max_iterations; MaxTime :max_time; Stalled :stalled; InitialFailure :degenerate, or :solve_failed for a NonFinite start; InternalLinearSolveFailed :solve_failed; Failure :no_model when a RANSAC's trial rule ended it (TrialBudget, AdaptiveCount) and :solve_failed otherwise; Default (not run) :not_run, and any code with the SeedKept rule :guard_kept_seed.\n\n\n\n\n\nretcode_symbol(r::FitResult) -> Symbol\n\nThe legacy Symbol spelling of the result's record, retcode_symbol(retcode(r), stop_rule(r)) (:converged, :solved, :max_iterations, :degenerate, :solve_failed, :stalled, :max_time, :no_model): for a log line or a persisted record that names the Symbol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retcode_symbol"},{"category":"function","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retcode_from_symbol","page":"Termination Record","text":"retcode_from_symbol(retcode::Symbol, rule::StopRule) -> (ReturnCode.T, StopRule)\n\nThe termination record of a loop that reports a legacy Symbol — the inverse of retcode_symbol, so retcode_symbol(retcode_from_symbol(s, rule)...) === s. rule is the rule a SUCCESSFUL stop of that loop fired (its tolerance: CostDecrease, IncrementSize, ParameterChange, …), also the one whose postcondition a :stalled stop failed:\n\nretcode ReturnCode StopRule\n:converged Success rule\n:solved Success ClosedForm\n:stalled Stalled rule\n:max_iterations MaxIters IterationCap\n:max_time MaxTime TimeCap\n:solve_failed Failure NonFinite\n:degenerate InitialFailure NoRule\n:not_run Default NoRule\n:guard_kept_seed Success SeedKept\n:no_lo Success ClosedForm (read back as :solved)\n:thin_partition InitialFailure NoRule (read back as :degenerate)\n\nThe last four are a RANSAC's former local-optimization statuses (its lo_retcode before it was a record). Any other Symbol (a RANSAC's :no_model) is an ArgumentError: its producer states its record itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retcode_from_symbol"},{"category":"function","location":"api/termination/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.worst_retcode","page":"Termination Record","text":"worst_retcode(a::Tuple{ReturnCode.T, StopRule}, b::Tuple{ReturnCode.T, StopRule}, more...) -> Tuple{ReturnCode.T, StopRule}\n\nThe termination of a fit that ran several solves (stages, rounds, the cycles of an outer loop — its own cap counted as one), from each solve's (code, rule): the most severe code, in the order InitialFailure > InternalLinearSolveFailed > Failure > MaxTime > MaxIters > Stalled > Terminated > Success, with that solve's rule. On a tie the LATER solve's pair is kept (the final solve's rule is the informative one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.worst_retcode"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Core-API","page":"Core","text":"The package docstring, units and quantities, utilities, the benchmark pool record, and the visualization stubs. The core composition and uncertainty types are on the Core Types API page, synthetic data and the Monte Carlo calibration on the Synthetic Data API page.","title":"Core API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#The-Package","page":"Core","text":"","title":"The Package"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Units-and-Quantities","page":"Core","text":"","title":"Units and Quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Utilities","page":"Core","text":"Drawing into an array without Makie (masks, debug frames; public, not exported):","title":"Utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#TOML-Configuration","page":"Core","text":"The config-of-record machinery shared by the package family's config types (public, not exported — import by name).","title":"TOML Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Benchmark-Pools","page":"Core","text":"The pool record and the raw-pair interface (using VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools). The pool is the stack's one declared exception to \"frame in = frame out\": its data is stored in NormalizedImageFrame, and every accessor that crosses the boundary names it.","title":"Benchmark Pools"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Spatial-Queries","page":"Core","text":"Allocation-free queries on SpatialIndexing.jl's R-tree (using VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries): the intersection query with early exit and the branch-and-bound nearest neighbour, with a reusable state for batches, and the in-place OMT bulk load. bbox gives a shape's query rectangle.","title":"Spatial Queries"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Visualization-Stubs","page":"Core","text":"Stubs implemented by the Makie/GLMakie package extension (load a Makie backend to activate them); see the Visualization guide.","title":"Visualization Stubs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Index","page":"Core","text":"Pages = [\"core.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.VisualGeometryToolkitCore","page":"Core","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\nGeometry, camera models and the robust-estimation core of the visual-geometry stack. VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitIO, VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation and BlobBoards build on it.\n\nSubmodules\n\nPrimitives: conics and feature frames (Ellipse, Circle, Disc, OrientedDisc, EllipticFrame, AffineFrame), hyperplanes (Line2D, Plane3D), 3-D conics (Ellipse3D, Circle3D) and Paraboloid3D.\nCameras: sensors (CMOS_SENSORS), intrinsics, lens models (Lens, LiftLens, Pinhole, KannalaBrandt, Division, …), PosedCamera, CameraModel, Rig, projection, calibration fits and P3P.\nSynthetic: ground-truth generators and metrics for benchmarking estimators.\n\nKey types in the parent module\n\nTransform maps carry the class: EuclideanMap ⊂ SimilarityMap ⊂ CoordinateTransformations' affines ⊂ HomographyMap; the algebraic matrices are HomographyMat, FundMat, EssentialMat, CameraCalibrationMat (five parameters) and the symmetric conics.\nFrame-tagged maps: EuclideanMap, SimilarityMap and HomographyMap carry Src/Dst tags from the open Frame hierarchy (ViewFrame, PixelFrame, GnomonicFrame, …); composition and inv are frame-checked.\nMinimal solvers (homography_4pt, p3p, the 5-point essential and the fundamental-matrix solvers) and the manifolds their models are refined on.\nThe robust-loss family (AbstractLossShape, deploy), the block-coordinate-descent/LO interface shared with VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation, and FitResult, the one fit return type.\nNumerics: the B-spline kernels, the thin-plate spline (ThinPlateSplineSystem, tps_coefficients, tps_evaluate, tps_gradient) and the spherical spline (SphericalSplineSystem), public, not exported.\nUnits: FlexUnits as shipped, plus the registered px, pd, dpi and pt (mm, px, dpi, Size2).\nThe sensor image: RawMosaic (a decoded Bayer RAW, an AbstractMatrix of its photosites that knows its colour phase and frame), its channel-first photosite view (a method of ImageCore's channelview), and LinearRaw, its exact linear values on the 2×2 cells, with the exact maps onto the sensor (raw_frame_map, bayer_plane_map). Decoding a file is VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' (LibRaw).\n\nPlotting loads as a package extension with any Makie backend.\n\nSee ARCHITECTURE.md (module graph, include order, extension points) and LOSSES.md (the robust-loss family) at the repository root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.VisualGeometryToolkitCore"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cm","page":"Core","text":"cm\n\nThe centimetre, 1//100 m exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cm"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dpi","page":"Core","text":"dpi\n\nDots per inch, px / inch: the density a length crosses to pixels by (210mm * 300dpi is 2480.3… px).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dpi"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ft","page":"Core","text":"ft\n\nThe foot, 12inch exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ft"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inch","page":"Core","text":"inch\n\nThe international inch, 127//5000 m (25.4 mm) exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inch"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.m","page":"Core","text":"m\n\nThe metre, the base unit every length is stored in: a bare ustrip(q) of a length is in metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.m"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mm","page":"Core","text":"mm\n\nThe millimetre, 1//1000 m exactly: 142mm is 71//500 m. A length is stored in metres, so read one back in millimetres with ustrip(mm, q).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mm"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pt","page":"Core","text":"pt\n\nThe typographic point, inch / 72 exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pt"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.px","page":"Core","text":"px\n\nThe pixel, a dimensionless count (FlexUnits' dimensions are the seven SI ones, so px cannot be told from a radian or a ratio). Pixel COORDINATES keep their frame through PixelFrame tags; px is for sizes and densities. A length becomes pixels through a density: q * 300dpi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.px"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.μm","page":"Core","text":"μm\n\nThe micrometre, 1//1000000 m exactly; the unit of a sensor's pixel pitch (5.86μm / px).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.μm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BaseQuantity","page":"Core","text":"BaseQuantity\n\nFlexUnits' concrete stored form of a base quantity, computed once as typeof(ubase(1.0mm)) (today Quantity{Float64, Dimensions{FixRat32}}): the type a struct field holds, so a container of mixed dimensions is concrete. It is NOT a stack quantity type — public signatures take any FlexUnits Quantity and convert with base_quantity — and it is named here only so the stack spells FlexUnits' stored form once instead of restating its parameters (see conventions/units.md).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BaseQuantity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.base_quantity-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity}","page":"Core","text":"base_quantity(q::Quantity) -> Quantity\n\nq in FlexUnits' base form with a floating-point value: float(ubase(q)). The ONE conversion a constructor applies to a quantity it stores. A quantity built from an integer (142mm, 3px) holds an exact Rational (71//500 m); its stored form is the correctly rounded Float64, 0.142 m, the decimal-nearest value. A float value keeps its type (Float64, Float32, a ForwardDiff Dual), so base_quantity(1.0mm) is ubase(1.0mm). A Rational never reaches a struct field or a kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.base_quantity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_units-Tuple{Real}","page":"Core","text":"has_units(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x holds FlexUnits quantities — a physical value (a pose's translation, a target's points, a rig's mounts, in metres) — rather than plain numbers, the inner loop's form. Defined for numbers, arrays, and every carrier of a physical length (EuclideanMap, Uncertain values, targets, mounts, rigs, cameras, trajectories, target poses and 3D target points).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_units"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.parse_unit-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"parse_unit(s::AbstractString; compound = false) -> Units\n\nThe unit a serialized name s spells, resolved by the stack's registry (StackUnits) and its generated uparse — guarded, since uparse evaluates its parse. By default s must be ONE registered unit symbol (\"mm\", \"inch\", \"px\", \"dpi\", …); compound = true also admits a product, quotient or power of them (\"mm/px\", \"px/mm\", \"mm^2\"), checked on the parsed expression before anything is evaluated. Anything else is an ArgumentError, never a default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.parse_unit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.strip_metres-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity}","page":"Core","text":"strip_metres(x) -> plain numbers\n\nA length, or a vector of lengths, as plain metres: the one strip at a public boundary that takes physical values and computes on plain numbers (a pose's translation, a 3D point, a threshold given beside physical data). It refuses plain numbers where a length is meant — a unit the caller did not state — with FlexUnits' DimensionError, as the arithmetic would raise; a quantity of another dimension is FlexUnits' ConversionError. A static vector stays static. Values are stored in metres, so the strip is exact; a quantity built from an integer (2m, exactly rational) comes out as its correctly rounded float.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.strip_metres"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.strip_or_bare-Tuple{FlexUnits.Units, Real}","page":"Core","text":"strip_or_bare(u::Units, x)\n\nx as plain numbers in unit u: a quantity, or a struct of quantities with a ustrip(u, ·) method (Size2, the feature frames), stripped in u; plain numbers returned as they are. A stripped quantity is a float (float of an exact Rational from 3px). For the producers that emit either (the ASIFT frames are plain Float64, the detectors' are px). A quantity of another dimension is FlexUnits' ConversionError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.strip_or_bare"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_string-Tuple{FlexUnits.Units}","page":"Core","text":"unit_string(u::Units) -> String\n\nThe serialized name of a registered unit: FlexUnits' own printing, string(u), which for a registry unit is its symbol. A unit the registry has no symbol for (mm/px, 1000mm) is an ArgumentError: its printing is no name parse_unit reads back. The inverse of parse_unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_string"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_unit","page":"Core","text":"with_unit(x, u::Units)\n\nThe inverse of ustrip(u, x) on the stack's carriers: x's plain lengths read as being in the unit u and stored as FlexUnits base-form quantities (metres) — an Uncertain pose's covariance rescaled with them. An ArgumentError when x already has units (has_units). A plain EuclideanMap takes its unit this way too, its rotation kept exactly (construction, EuclideanMap(rotation(E), translation(E) .* u), re-stores a non-RotMatrix rotation as one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_unit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Size2","page":"Core","text":"Size2{T}\n\nWidth/height container with (optionally unit-carrying) elements for image and canvas sizes.\n\nwidth: columns (x-axis)\nheight: rows (y-axis)\n\nConstructors:\n\nSize2(; width, height) keyword constructor\nSize2(matrix) from a matrix's (width, height)\n\nExamples:\n\nSize2(width=800, height=600)\nSize2(width=210mm, height=297mm)\nSize2(width=800px, height=600px)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Size2"},{"category":"module","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StackUnits","page":"Core","text":"StackUnits\n\nThe stack's unit registry: FlexUnits' RegistryTools over Units{Dimensions{FixRat32}, AffineTransform{Rational{Int}}}, every scale an exact rational in base units. It holds the units the stack reads and writes and nothing else:\n\nunit scale (base units) \nm, kg, s, A, K, cd, mol 1 the SI base units\nkm, cm, mm, μm (um) 1000, 1//100, 1//1000, 1//1000000 m\ninch (in), ft, pt 127//5000, 381//1250, 127//360000 m (the international inch; pt = inch/72)\npx, pd 1, dimensionless a pixel count; pd the board JSONs' print dot\ndpi 5000//127 px/inch, of dimension 1/m\n\nIts generated uparse, qparse and @ud_str are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's; a name from outside is read with parse_unit, never uparse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StackUnits"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RetiredKeyword","page":"Core","text":"RetiredKeyword()\nrefuse_retired_keyword(value, old, new) -> nothing\n\nThe stack's one refusal of a retired keyword, never an alias: a function keeps the retired keyword old with the default RetiredKeyword() and calls refuse_retired_keyword(old_value, :old, successor) first, which throws ArgumentError(\"the keywordoldis retired: givenew\") for any value a caller supplies and returns nothing for the default. new names what replaces it (a keyword, or the argument that now carries it). LensSpec refuses free this way (it takes fixed), every find_*, ransac and locally_optimize refuses local_optimization (it takes local_optimization_strategy), every refine_* and fit_* the strategy spellings (the owner's ruling, 2026-11-11); a downstream package refuses its own retired keywords through the same pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RetiredKeyword"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.filter_kwargs-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Core","text":"filter_kwargs(T::Type; kwargs...) -> (matched::NamedTuple, dropped::NamedTuple)\n\nSplit keyword arguments into those that match fieldnames of type T and those that don't.\n\nThis is useful for forwarding a subset of kwargs to a struct constructor while forwarding the remainder to another function.\n\nArguments\n\nT::Type: Type whose fieldnames determine which kwargs are \"matched\"\nkwargs...: Keyword arguments to filter\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple of two NamedTuples:\n\nmatched: kwargs whose names are fields of T\ndropped: kwargs whose names are not fields of T\n\nExamples\n\nBase.@kwdef struct Config\n    width::Int = 100\n    height::Int = 100\nend\n\nmatched, dropped = filter_kwargs(Config; width=200, extra=true)\n# matched = (width = 200,)\n# dropped = (extra = true,)\n\nconfig = Config(; matched...)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.filter_kwargs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refuse_retired_keyword-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RetiredKeyword, Any, Any}","page":"Core","text":"refuse_retired_keyword(value, old, new) -> nothing\n\nnothing for RetiredKeyword(), the retired keyword's default; for any other value an ArgumentError naming the retired keyword old and its successor new.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refuse_retired_keyword"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.require_extension-Tuple{Any, Module, Symbol}","page":"Core","text":"require_extension(missing_error, parent::Module, extension::Symbol) -> nothing\nrequire_extension(missing_error, f, args::Tuple, parent::Module, extension::Symbol)\n\nThe stack's one check for a package extension a call needs. missing_error is a zero-argument function returning the exception to throw when the extension is not loaded: the caller's own message, naming what to install and load (using X), built only on that path.\n\nrequire_extension(missing_error, parent, extension): nothing when parent's extension extension is loaded (Base.get_extension(parent, extension)), else throw(missing_error()). A function calls it first, before any work that would end in the extension.\nrequire_extension(missing_error, f, args, parent, extension): the fallback method of a function f whose methods live in the extension, called with the arguments args it received. Without the extension it throws missing_error(). With the extension loaded the arguments fit none of its methods, so it throws the MethodError(f, args) the call would have raised, at the caller's world age (Base.tls_world_age()), so that a call compiled before the extension loaded keeps Julia's \"method too new\" hint.\n\n_fit(args...; kwargs...) = require_extension(_fit, args, @__MODULE__, :MyPackageEstimationExt) do\n    ArgumentError(\"_fit needs `using VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation` (its package extension is not loaded)\")\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.require_extension"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.saturation_bounds-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Core","text":"saturation_bounds(::Type{T}) -> (lo, hi)\nsaturation_bounds(::Type{<:Colorant}) -> (lo, hi)\nsaturation_bounds(img::AbstractMatrix) -> (lo, hi)\n\nThe open interval of pixel values that are NOT clipped by the sensor or the encoding, as Float64s: (1e-6, 1 - 1e-6) for float images, one code inside typemin/typemax for integer ones, one eps inside for fixed-point ones. A pixel outside it carries no intensity information (it is saturated) and must not enter a photometric fit. Colorants defer to their element type; an image to its eltype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.saturation_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sha256_hex-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"sha256_hex(s::AbstractString) -> String\nsha256_hex(bytes::AbstractVector{UInt8}) -> String\nsha256_hex(io::IO) -> String\n\nLower-case hex SHA-256 — the one hash behind every content identity in the stack (sha256_hex(canonical_json(tree)), canonical_json) and every file digest (a pinned model's, a data artifact's). A string is hashed as its bytes (never opened as a path); a stream is read to its end. A file's digest is the stream form, open(sha256_hex, path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sha256_hex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stable_seed-Tuple{Integer, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Core","text":"stable_seed(seed, keys...) -> UInt64\n\nThe seed derivation of every random stream in the stack: the first eight bytes (big-endian) of the SHA-256 of seed and keys printed as text, separated by the unit separator \\x1f. A fixed algorithm, unlike Base.hash (which changes across Julia versions), and keyed by NAME, unlike seed + i: a scene's draw depends on that scene's name only, so adding, removing or reordering other scenes leaves it unchanged, and no arithmetic coincidence gives two key tuples one stream. Keys are told apart by their printed text (string): 2, \"2\" and Int32(2) are one key, and a key whose text holds \\x1f can collide with two keys; integers and plain names are always distinct. Seed a StableRNGs StableRNG with it (StableRNG(stable_seed(seed, \"scene\", i))), whose stream is fixed across Julia versions too.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitCore: stable_seed\n\njulia> stable_seed(7, \"budget\", 2, 1)\n0xf5154d7e8f0e4d1c\n\njulia> stable_seed(1000, 1, 3) == stable_seed(0, 2, 3)\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stable_seed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.validate_dir-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core","text":"validate_dir(dir; create=false) -> String\n\nValidate a directory path and optionally create it.\n\nArguments\n\ndir: Directory path (String or AbstractString)\ncreate::Bool=false: If true, create the directory (and parents) if it doesn't exist\n\nReturns\n\nThe validated directory path as a String.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError if dir is not a directory and create=false\nArgumentError if dir exists but is not a directory (e.g., it's a file)\n\nExamples\n\n# Validate existing directory\npath = validate_dir(\"/tmp\")\n\n# Create directory if needed\npath = validate_dir(\"/tmp/mydir\"; create=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.validate_dir"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.canonical_json-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"Core","text":"canonical_json(io::IO, tree)\ncanonical_json(tree) -> String\n\nThe RFC 8785 (JCS) serialization of a lowered JSON tree (see the file header): members sorted by their names' UTF-16 code units, no whitespace, minimal string escapes, every number in ECMAScript's form of its double (1.0 is 1, 1e-5 is 0.00001, 1.0e21 is 1e+21). Throws on a non-finite number, an Int64 past ±2^53 (not exactly a double) and any value outside the lowered vocabulary — a tree that cannot be encoded must fail loudly, never hash to something. Core.sha256_hex(canonical_json(tree)) is a content identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.canonical_json"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#GeometryBasics.Rect-Union{Tuple{Size2{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Core","text":"Rect(s::Size2{T}) where T\n\nCreate a Rect from a Size2 with origin at (0,0) and widths filled from the size dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometryBasics.Rect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Size2-Tuple{HyperRectangle}","page":"Core","text":"Size2(r::Rect{N,T}) where {N,T}\n\nCreate a Size2 from a Rect using its widths field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Size2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#FlexUnits.ustrip-Tuple{FlexUnits.Units, HyperRectangle}","page":"Core","text":"ustrip(u, r::HyperRectangle)\n\nRemove units from HyperRectangle, expressing magnitudes in unit u.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlexUnits.ustrip"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.area-Tuple{Size2}","page":"Core","text":"area(s::Size2)\n\nWidth × height (unit-carrying if the fields carry units).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.area"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.aspect_ratio-Tuple{Size2}","page":"Core","text":"aspect_ratio(s::Size2)\n\nWidth/height ratio.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.aspect_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.canonical_basis-Union{Tuple{Type{StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}} where {N, T}","page":"Core","text":"canonical_basis(::Type{SVector{N,T}}) where {N,T}\n\nGenerate all N canonical basis vectors for ℝⁿ as a tuple.\n\nReturns a tuple containing all canonical basis vectors e₁, e₂, ..., eₙ where eᵢ has 1 in the i-th position and 0 elsewhere. This is type-stable and compile-time optimized using StaticArrays.\n\nArguments\n\nSVector{N,T}: Type of vectors to generate (N dimensions, element type T)\n\nReturns\n\nAn N-tuple of SVector{N,T} containing all canonical basis vectors\n\nExamples\n\n# 3D basis\ne1, e2, e3 = canonical_basis(SVector{3,Float64})\n# e1 = [1.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n# e2 = [0.0, 1.0, 0.0]\n# e3 = [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]\n\n# 2D basis with Float32\nex, ey = canonical_basis(SVector{2,Float32})\n\n# Use in transformation\nR = RotMatrix(...)  # 3×3 rotation matrix\ne1, e2, e3 = canonical_basis(SVector{3,Float64})\nx_axis = R * e1  # Transform basis vectors\ny_axis = R * e2\nz_axis = R * e3\n\nSee also: VisualGeometryToolkitCore.basis_vector (unexported)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.canonical_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.centroid-Tuple{HyperRectangle}","page":"Core","text":"centroid(r::HyperRectangle)\n\nGet the centroid (geometric center) of a HyperRectangle (Rect/Rect2).\n\nNote: This returns the midpoint, not the origin. For the corner/origin, use r.origin or GeometryBasics.origin(r).\n\nExamples\n\nrect = Rect2(0.5, 0.5, 1920.0, 1200.0)\nc = centroid(rect)  # [960.5, 600.5]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.centroid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.default_device-Tuple{}","page":"Core","text":"default_device() -> KA.Backend\n\nThe device every KernelAbstractions-generic entry point in the package family uses when the caller supplies none: an accelerator when one is loaded AND functional, else KA.CPU(). The accelerator's backend value is built with the compiler options its extension chooses (the CUDA extension's always_inline = true); on_device treats it as the same memory as the backend of an array on that device.\n\nMemoized: resolution asks the vendor package whether it is functional, which is not free and cannot change within a session. _DEVICE_RESOLVER[] overrides it (including back to KA.CPU()); reset_default_device! clears the memo so the next call resolves again.\n\nSupplying device = explicitly always wins — this only decides what \"unspecified\" means, and it is deliberately NOT the cheapest device (see the file header).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.default_device"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_free_bytes-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core","text":"device_free_bytes(dev) -> Int\n\nFree memory on the KernelAbstractions backend dev, in bytes: the host's for KA.CPU(), the accelerator extension's query for its backend (CUDA's free_memory), else typemax(Int) with a one-time warning (a caller sizing chunks from it then does not chunk).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_free_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_names-Tuple{}","page":"Core","text":"device_names() -> Vector{String}\n\nThe names of the accelerator devices this process sees (the extension's query: CUDA's devices when CUDA is functional); none without an accelerator extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_names"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_resident_threads-Tuple{KernelAbstractions.Kernel, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Core","text":"device_resident_threads(kernel, args...; ndrange) -> Int\n\nHow many threads of kernel can be resident on its device at once: the work its device runs in one wave. kernel is a KernelAbstractions kernel instance — OnBackend(dev)(f!) or f!(dev[, workgroupsize]), the one the caller launches as kernel(args...; ndrange) — and args, ndrange that launch's: the count is of THAT kernel compiled for those arguments, at the workgroup size the launch takes.\n\nA GPU (the accelerator extension's backend; CUDA): register- and shared-memory-aware. The compiled kernel's occupancy (CUDA's active_blocks at the launch's block size, the size the backend picks for a dynamic workgroup) times the block size times the multiprocessors. Never the device's raw maximum of threads per multiprocessor: a kernel that uses 255 registers a thread fits about an eighth of it.\nThe host (KA.CPU()): Threads.nthreads(), the threads its kernels run on.\nAnother backend: an ArgumentError (no query; a caller must not guess a capacity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_resident_threads"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_zeros-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Vararg{Integer}}} where T","page":"Core","text":"device_zeros(T, device, dims...) -> array\n\nA zero array of element type T and size dims on the KernelAbstractions backend device (an Array on KA.CPU()), as ka_upload puts one there: the stack's one spelling of it (KernelAbstractions' own zeros is not public).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.device_zeros"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.free_device_buffers!-Tuple{AbstractArray, Vararg{AbstractArray}}","page":"Core","text":"free_device_buffers!(x::AbstractArray, xs::AbstractArray...) -> nothing\n\nRelease each device array's memory NOW (GPUArrays' unsafe_free!), rather than when the collector finalizes its few-byte host wrapper — which, with no host memory pressure, it has no reason to do while the device fills. A host array is left alone. Only for buffers the caller allocated and never reads again: a freed array must not be used (GPUArrays throws on its next use). An array derived from another (a reshape) shares its memory, which is released when the last of them is freed. KernelAbstractions' unsafe_free! is not used: CUDA.jl does not extend it, so it freed nothing on the GPU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.free_device_buffers!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ka_upload-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, KernelAbstractions.CPU, AbstractArray}} where T","page":"Core","text":"ka_upload(T, device, A) -> array\n\nA copy of A with element type T on device: one Array{T} on the host (Array{T}(A), which also downloads a device array), else a KA.allocated array filled by one copyto! — from A's own memory when it is a dense array of T or a host wrapper of T that is a dense array's memory in order (a reinterpret, a reshape, a contiguous view: no host copy), else from T.(A) (a strided view, an adjoint, another eltype). An integer image (UInt8, UInt16, N0f8, N0f16: an integer or fixed-point eltype narrower than a float T) crosses as it is and is converted ON the device by one KernelAbstractions kernel, T(x) per element, on every backend (the CPU's included): bit for bit the host's T.(A), at sizeof(eltype(A)) bytes a pixel moved instead of sizeof(T).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ka_upload"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.on_device-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, AbstractArray}} where T","page":"Core","text":"on_device([T = eltype(A)], device, A) -> array\n\nA itself when it already lives on device with element type T, else one ka_upload(T, device, A). Also the way back: a result handed back in the caller's array kind is on_device(get_backend(input), result).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.on_device"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reclaim_device!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core","text":"reclaim_device!(dev) -> nothing\n\nHand the memory dev's backend pool holds back to the device (CUDA.jl's reclaim), so an allocator outside that pool (ONNX Runtime's) can use it; nothing for the host or a backend without a pool query.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reclaim_device!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reset_default_device!-Tuple{}","page":"Core","text":"reset_default_device!() -> nothing\n\nForget the memoized default so the next default_device() re-resolves — for tests, and for a session that changed _DEVICE_RESOLVER[] after the first resolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reset_default_device!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.primal_float-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Core","text":"primal_float(T::Type) -> Type\n\nThe real type under every ForwardDiff Dual layer of T (Float64 for a Dual{Tag, Dual{Tag2, Float64, 1}, 2}, T itself for a non-Dual type): the precision a branch on primal values computes in. A type-level recursion, so it folds at compile time inside a kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.primal_float"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.huggingface_token-Tuple{}","page":"Core","text":"huggingface_token() -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nThe user's Hugging Face token: ENV[\"HF_TOKEN\"], else the file hf auth login writes ($HF_HOME/token, HF_HOME defaulting to ~/.cache/huggingface), else nothing. install_artifact sends it to huggingface.co only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.huggingface_token"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_artifact-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"install_artifact(name, toml) -> String\n\nInstall artifact name of toml (download, verify sha256 and git-tree-sha1, unpack into the depot) unless present, and return its tree. A Hugging Face tarball is downloaded with the user's Hugging Face token when there is one (huggingface_token), so a private dataset installs for whoever has access to it. A placeholder URL (an UPPER_CASE token in place of the Zenodo id or the Hugging Face revision: the record is not published or uploaded) and a failed download (an unpublished Zenodo draft, a private dataset without a token) are refused with the way out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.install_artifact"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.draw_ellipse!-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"Core","text":"draw_ellipse!(img, center, S, cutoff, color; n = 180, w = 2)\n\nDraw the cutoff·σ boundary of the elliptic frame (center, S) (image coordinates): boundary point = center + cutoff · S · [cos t, sin t], n samples around the ring, each stamped w pixels thick.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.draw_ellipse!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.draw_polyline!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Core","text":"draw_polyline!(img, pts, color; n = 300, w = 2)\n\nDraw the closed polyline through pts (each an (x, y) tuple or vector), sampling every edge n times so no Bresenham is needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.draw_polyline!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.draw_projected_ellipse!-Tuple{Any, AbstractMatrix, Any, Any, Real, Any}","page":"Core","text":"draw_projected_ellipse!(img, H, center, S, cutoff, color; n = 180, w = 2)\n\nDraw the cutoff·σ boundary of a WORLD-plane elliptic frame after projecting each boundary point through the world→image homography H (the map before the shape it maps, after the canvas it draws on). The projection of an ellipse is only approximately an ellipse under perspective, so points are mapped one by one rather than through a shape matrix; samples beyond the plane's horizon are skipped (chirality-guarded to_euclidean), never wrapped into view.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.draw_projected_ellipse!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.allowed_keys-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Vararg{Any}}} where T","page":"Core","text":"allowed_keys(::Type{T}, extra...) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe keys a [T] table may carry: the same field walk toml_table emits, plus extra (a type discriminator, say). Derived, never hand-listed, so a field is readable and writable together — emitting a key the reader then refuses is exactly the asymmetry this avoids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.allowed_keys"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_keys-Tuple{AbstractDict, Any, AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"check_keys(d::AbstractDict, allowed, section::AbstractString; retired = (), renamed = ())\n\nRefuse unknown keys in table d with an ArgumentError naming the section, the offending keys and the allowed set. A typo'd knob that silently does nothing is the failure a config loader exists to prevent — a config that looks like it set max_trials but spelled it max_trial would otherwise run at the default and report success.\n\nretired is a collection of exact (section, key) pairs: knobs removed because nothing read them, which recorded run sidecars (immutable provenance) legitimately still contain. Those load with a one-time warning; every other unknown key throws. Retirement is section-scoped on purpose — the same key in another table is a typo and must still fail.\n\nrenamed is a collection of (section, key) => new_key pairs: keys whose knob lives on under a new name. Each is refused with an ArgumentError naming its new key (there is no alias: the old spelling never loads), before the unknown keys are checked; section-scoped like retired.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_keys"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.convert_field-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Core","text":"convert_field(::Type{T}, v)\nconvert_field(::Type{T}, v, key)\n\nA TOML value → the field type (Float64(v), Int(v), Bool(v), Symbol(v)): the one converter behind a typed present_kwargs spec.\n\nThe keyed form checks the value's shape and names key in every error (an ArgumentError): a table where a value belongs is refused; a Tuple field takes an array of exactly its length (any length for a Vararg tuple such as Tuple{Vararg{Float64}}), converted element by element; a Vector field an array, converted element-wise; any other array field (an SVector) an array, converted by its constructor as the unkeyed form does; any other value for a tuple or array field (a string would convert character by character) is refused; a value that does not convert (MethodError, InexactError, ArgumentError, DimensionMismatch) is reported as not a T. read_table's default converter is the keyed form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.convert_field"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_finite_real-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core","text":"is_finite_real(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is a number a record may hold: a Real, not a Bool, finite, and finite as a Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_finite_real"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.parse_defaults-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"parse_defaults(path::AbstractString) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nParse a package's defaults file (its configs/default.toml) and record it as a precompilation dependency of the package being precompiled, so editing the file recompiles it. Call it at a module's top level with an absolute path: const _TOML_DEFAULTS = parse_defaults(default_config_path()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.parse_defaults"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.present_kwargs-Tuple{AbstractDict, Vararg{Pair{String}}}","page":"Core","text":"present_kwargs(d::AbstractDict, spec::Pair{String}...; fallback = nothing, field_of = Symbol)\n    -> Vector{Pair{Symbol, Any}}\n\nTyped per-key overrides. For each \"key\" => convert in spec, emit field_of(\"key\") => convert(d[\"key\"]) when d has the key, else the same from fallback when THAT has it, else nothing at all — so an absent key falls through to the struct's own default rather than to a parallel literal here. field_of maps a TOML spelling to a field name; the default Symbol keeps the key as the field. A loader of structs with Greek fields (:μ read from an ASCII \"mu\") supplies field_of =toml_field_name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.present_kwargs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.read_table-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, AbstractDict}} where T","page":"Core","text":"read_table(::Type{T}, d::AbstractDict; skip = (), from_toml) -> Vector{Pair{Symbol, Any}}\n\nThe reader's half of toml_table: the keyword overrides of a [T] table d, one f => from_toml(T, Val(f), d[toml_key(T, f)]) for every field f of T whose key is PRESENT in d, in field order — the same fieldnames / toml_skip_fields / toml_key walk the writer emits, minus the fields in skip the caller reads itself. An absent key yields nothing, so the field falls through to the struct's own default: T(; read_table(T, d)...). from_toml(T, Val(f), v) converts one value (a package's per-field hook); the default is convert_field to the field's type. Unknown keys are check_keys' job, with allowed_keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.read_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.required_key-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"required_key(d, key, where) -> value\n\nd[key], or an ArgumentError naming where (the file and the table) and the missing key, rather than a KeyError from deep inside a parsed record. The one reader of a key a record or a config must hold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.required_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_default-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"toml_default(defaults::AbstractDict, table::AbstractString, key::AbstractString)\n\nLook a default up in a parsed defaults file. table is a dotted path (\"ransac\", \"optimization_strategy.hq\"; \"\" is the top level). Throws a KeyError naming the missing table or key BY DESIGN: a keyword default that is not in the file would be a silent second source of truth, which is what the mechanism exists to prevent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_default"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_field-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{Any, Val{F}}} where F","page":"Core","text":"toml_field(c, ::Val{F})\n\nThe TOML value of field F of c; default toml_value(getfield(c, F)). Override for the non-mechanical cases only — a nested config struct (toml_table(getfield(c, F))), a tagged union (type = ... discriminator), a sentinel (typemax(Int) written as \"unbounded\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_field"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_field_name-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Core","text":"toml_field_name(key::AbstractString) -> Symbol\n\nThe field name a TOML key spells: the inverse of toml_key's Greek spelling (\"mu\" → :μ), Symbol(key) for every other key. A loader whose structs carry the Greek fields hands it to present_kwargs as field_of = toml_field_name; it is not that function's default, since a struct whose fields are themselves ASCII (nu, gamma, alpha) must not be renamed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_field_name"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_key-Tuple{Type, Symbol}","page":"Core","text":"toml_key(::Type, f::Symbol) -> String\ntoml_key(c, f::Symbol) -> String\n\nThe TOML spelling of field f of this type; default String(f), except that a Greek field name is spelled ASCII (:μ → \"mu\", :α_min → \"alpha_min\", the table behind toml_field_name) so a config is typeable everywhere. Dispatched on the TYPE so the reader's allowed-key list and the writer share one mapping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_skip_fields-Tuple{Type}","page":"Core","text":"toml_skip_fields(::Type) -> Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}\n\nFields the walker must not emit for this type: data supplied at load (a camera), a property derived from the inputs (a polarity), a scale interface that is not a config key. Default: none. Declared per type so the reason lives next to the type it applies to.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_skip_fields"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_table-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core","text":"toml_table(c) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nc as a TOML table: fieldnames(typeof(c)) minus toml_skip_fields, keyed by toml_key, valued by toml_field. Julia's TOML stdlib serialises Dicts only, so the walk is ours — but it walks fieldnames, which means a new field appears in the file with no edit and cannot be forgotten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_value-Tuple{Union{AbstractString, Real}}","page":"Core","text":"toml_value(x)\n\nA leaf in TOML-representable form: numbers, booleans and strings are returned unchanged; symbols become strings; vectors, tuples and dicts recurse. Anything else is handed to the TOML writer as is (which refuses what it cannot represent).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.toml_value"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.write_toml-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractDict}","page":"Core","text":"write_toml(path, body::AbstractDict; header = \"\") -> path\n\nWrite body as sorted TOML, creating the directory, with header (one or more # comment lines, no trailing newline needed) first. The one writer behind every package's write_config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.write_toml"},{"category":"module","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools","page":"Core","text":"Pools\n\nThe benchmark pool record and the raw-pair interface (using VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools):\n\nPoolPair: one image pair of a scoring pool — correspondences, minimal-sample models and their pose errors, ground truth — in NormalizedImageFrame, with the per-image maps it was normalized by;\nits accessors: num_points, num_models, pool_imc, gt_pose, calibration_matrices, pool_normalization, pool_frame_maps, pool_frame_calibrations, pool_pixel_scale, pixel_correspondences, pixel_residuals and rig;\nStereoRawPair: what a dataset reader's pair provides before a pool is drawn from it (pixel correspondences, calibration, image sizes, GT).\n\nThe record holds no residuals: a pool's residual matrices are a separate file, bound to the record by a content key, read by the evaluation package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair","page":"Core","text":"PoolPair{M}\n\nOne image pair of a scoring pool: the light record — everything about the pair except the per-model residual matrix, which is a separate file bound to the record by a content key.\n\nThe pool is the one place in the stack that stores data normalized: every point datum and model is in NormalizedImageFrame (image centre, half-diagonal unit), through the per-image maps the record carries. See \"Coordinate frame\" below.\n\nType Parameters\n\nM: Model type, expressed in NormalizedImageFrame (FundMat{Float64} for the E and F pools, HomographyMat{Float64} for the H pools).\n\nFields\n\ncorrespondences: Correspondences in NormalizedImageFrame — NOT pixels, stored as the columnar correspondences(sources, targets) of SVector{2,Float64} sides (any vector of 2-vector pairs is converted to it at construction). Each side is mapped pixel → normalized by its own map (source_map, destination_map). pixel_correspondences is the exact way back to pixels.\nK1::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}, K2::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}: the pixel calibration of the source and target cameras (calibration_matrices).\nR_gt::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}, t_gt::SVector{3,Float64}: the GT relative motion, source → target (X₂ = R_gt·X₁ + t_gt; gt_pose).\nmodels::Vector{M}: Precomputed minimal-sample models, expressed in NormalizedImageFrame (e.g. fundamentals conjugated by the per-image maps).\nsample_indices::Matrix{Int}: (m, num_models) — 1-based sample indices per model.\nerrors_r::Vector{Float64}: (num_models,) — rotation error [deg] per model vs GT.\nerrors_t::Vector{Float64}: (num_models,) — translation error [deg] per model vs GT.\ngt_inliers::BitVector: (n,) — GT inlier mask (from bundle adjustment), one entry per correspondence (checked at construction).\ngt_variance::Float64: σ̂²p = RSS/ν of the residuals under the oracle pose (from the GT R, t) on the SfM-track detections — NOT a DLT refit. `ν = n·dg` (oracle: no fitted DOF). NaN if too few tracks.\nsource_map::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}, destination_map::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}: the per-image maps T (pixel → NormalizedImageFrame) the data is stored in — the stored truth of the pool's frame, and the two sides of its pool_normalization (source_map(N), destination_map(N)); framed, they are pool_frame_maps.\nsnn::Union{Nothing,Vector{Float32}}: (n,) — the per-correspondence second-nearest-neighbour ratio the pool's hypotheses were sampled by, where the generator's is known; nothing otherwise.\n\nCoordinate frame\n\nAll point data (correspondences, models, and the separate residuals) lives in NormalizedImageFrame: each image is normalized by its own T (source_map/destination_map), isotropically — translate to the image centre and scale BOTH axes by 1/s, s = ½√(W²+H²) HALF the image diagonal: (ũ,ṽ) = (u−W/2, v−H/2)/s, which puts the image corners at unit radius (DiagonalNormalization(size) per side: pool_normalization(size1, size2), what the pool generator writes). Every width on pool data — the outlier half-width, the loss width, the IG scale prior — is in this half-diagonal unit. The stored maps are the truth of the frame: every conversion goes through them and none is reconstructed from the image size.\n\nIsotropy is the key property. Feature noise is isotropic in pixels (Σ_px = σ²·I); a single scale maps it to Σ_pool = (σ²/s²)·I, still isotropic, so the scalar σ², the single global outlier half-width, the IG scale prior, and the Sampson distance all stay correctly specified. (A per-axis box normalization would make Σ_pool anisotropic and force a full 2×2 noise covariance through the scoring.) Normalizing by image size — not focal — also keeps the outlier half-width resolution/FOV-uniform: the K⁻¹ calibrated CHART — the z = 1 plane, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's GnomonicFrame — would not (extent ≈ tan(fov/2), so a single outlier half-width drifts ~4× with focal across PhotoTourism), which is why the pool frame is the scoring frame even for the calibrated (essential) problem.\n\nRe-solving / local-optimization applications\n\nBecause the pool-frame maps T are stored, the record is self-contained for re-solving and LO: the metric frame is recovered EXACTLY (no reconstruction from image size or principal point). With T and the pixel K, the pool-frame calibration is Kc = T·K (pool_frame_calibrations), and:\n\npool frame → the calibrated z = 1 CHART (GnomonicFrame, not a unit ray): x_chart = inv(Kc) · x_pool, a unit BEARING being x_chart / ‖x_chart‖;\na pool-frame fundamental F → metric essential: EssentialMat(F, Kc₁, Kc₂);\nscoring an essential E in the pool frame: FundMat(E, Kc₁, Kc₂) Sampson.\n\nThis is exact, so the global pool-frame outlier half-width needs no per-pair conversion. A pixel tolerance enters the pool frame only through pool_pixel_scale.\n\nConstruction\n\nPoolPair(; correspondences, K1, K2, R_gt, t_gt, models, sample_indices, errors_r,\n         errors_t, gt_inliers, gt_variance, source_map, destination_map, snn = nothing)\nPoolPair(; correspondences, …, gt_variance, normalization::CorrespondenceNormalization, snn = nothing)\nPoolPair{M}(; …)\n\nThe keyword form is the one to build a record with (every field named; M from models unless given): the maps come either as source_map and destination_map or as the normalization whose sides they are, never both. The positional forms below take the fields in declaration order.\n\nPoolPair{M}(correspondences, K1, K2, R_gt, t_gt, models, sample_indices,\n            errors_r, errors_t, gt_inliers, gt_variance,\n            source_map, destination_map, snn)\nPoolPair{M}(correspondences, K1, K2, R_gt, t_gt, models, sample_indices,\n            errors_r, errors_t, gt_inliers, gt_variance,\n            N::CorrespondenceNormalization, snn)\n\n(M is inferred from models when omitted.) The maps, or the pool_normalization whose sides they are, are REQUIRED: a record without them is refused with an ArgumentError, never read as the identity — an identity default once silently read a map-less record, a pool from a generator that did not persist its maps, as if it were already in the pool frame. Synthetic data built directly in the pool frame supplies the identity EXPLICITLY. Each map must be affine (last row [0 0 1], else ArgumentError), and the columns must agree in length (DimensionMismatch): per point, gt_inliers and snn (when present) have one entry per correspondence; per model, sample_indices has one column and errors_r, errors_t one entry. Every pool on disk satisfies this (all are format v4, loaded through these checks). The record is built by the loader from plain data; it is never serialized as a struct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.best_model_index-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Integer}} where F","page":"Core","text":"best_model_index(score, M::Integer) -> Int\n\nThe one pool argmax: the index of the best of models 1:M under score(j). The FIRST strictly best model wins; a non-finite score (NaN, ±Inf) never wins; 0 (never a valid index) means no model scores a finite value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.best_model_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.calibration_matrices-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"calibration_matrices(pair::PoolPair) -> (K1, K2)\n\nThe PIXEL calibration matrices of the source and target cameras, as SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}. The pool-frame calibrations are pool_frame_calibrations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.calibration_matrices"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.check_residual_matrix-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Core","text":"check_residual_matrix(pair::PoolPair, R::AbstractMatrix) -> nothing\n\nThe pool record's residual-matrix contract: R is num_points(pair) × num_models(pair), its rows the pair's correspondences and its columns the stored models. Any other shape is a DimensionMismatch, so a matrix of another pair is refused rather than read. Every scorer entry that reads a pair's residuals calls it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.check_residual_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.gt_pose-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"gt_pose(pair::PoolPair) -> (R_gt, t_gt)\n\nThe ground-truth relative pose (source → target, X₂ = R_gt·X₁ + t_gt) as (SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}, SVector{3,Float64}). t_gt is the stored (unnormalized) translation; pose-error callers use it direction-only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.gt_pose"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.num_models-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"num_models(pair::PoolPair) -> Int\n\nThe number of stored minimal-sample models: the residual matrix's column count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.num_models"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.num_points-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"num_points(pair::PoolPair) -> Int\n\nThe number of correspondences of the pair: the residual matrix's row count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.num_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pixel_correspondences-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"pixel_correspondences(pair::PoolPair) -> StructArray\n\nThe pair's correspondences back in PIXELS: each side through the inverse of its stored map (pool_frame_maps), so the result is in the pixel frame the pool's maps were built in (the generator's input frame). The exact inverse of the pool's normalization up to rounding; the way out of the pool frame for a consumer that works in pixels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pixel_correspondences"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pixel_residuals-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"pixel_residuals(pair::PoolPair) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nThe image-plane Sampson residuals of the pair's stored models in the PIXEL frame, num_points × num_models. Pixels are the frame the SIMFIT++ reference works in, so its pixel bounds apply to them without any scalar approximation (the pool frame scales the two images independently, so pool_pixel_scale is a bound on the scale, not a conversion). One n×M scan of the light record: the correspondences come back to pixels through the stored maps (pixel_correspondences), each model by composition with the pool normalization's two sides, inv(D) ∘ M ∘ S (a pool F → T₂ᵀ F T₁, a pool H → T₂⁻¹ H T₁). A model type with neither law (a bare matrix) has no sampson_distance and is a MethodError.\n\nIt never reads the pool's stored residual matrix, so it does not depend on that matrix's residual kind. The stored residuals of an essential pool are TANGENT Sampson distances (the pool generator's residual_kind = :tangent_sampson), those of an F or H pool image-plane Sampson (:sampson); this exit is image-plane Sampson for every pool, the quantity SIMFIT++'s pixel bounds are stated in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pixel_residuals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_frame_calibrations-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"pool_frame_calibrations(pair::PoolPair) -> (Kc1, Kc2)\n\nThe POOL-FRAME calibration matrices Kc = T·K of the source and target cameras: the pixel calibrations of calibration_matrices moved into the pool frame by the stored maps source_map, destination_map through reframed_calibration (each map an isotropic scale and a translation; any other affine map is an ArgumentError), which map pool-frame coordinates ↔ the calibrated z = 1 chart (GnomonicFrame) exactly. What a problem built on the stored pool data takes as its K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_frame_calibrations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_frame_maps-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"pool_frame_maps(pair::PoolPair) -> (S, D)\n\nThe stored per-image maps as frame-typed affine maps, S::FramedAffine{ImageFrame{:cam1}, NormalizedImageFrame{:cam1}} and D::FramedAffine{ImageFrame{:cam2}, NormalizedImageFrame{:cam2}}: pixels of each view in, the pool frame out; inv goes back. The frame lives on the maps in memory only — the stored maps are plain matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_frame_maps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_imc-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair, Integer}","page":"Core","text":"pool_imc(pair::PoolPair, j::Integer) -> Float64\n\nThe stored selection pose error [deg] of pool model j — the \"imc\" every pool evaluation reports for the model it selects — dispatched on the pool's model type:\n\na homography pool (HomographyMat): the rotation error errors_r[j] alone — a homography's translation is known only up to the plane's scale, so its stored errors_t is not part of the selection error;\nevery other pool (FundMat, EssentialMat): max(errors_r[j], errors_t[j]).\n\nThe one definition: the scorers' staged pairs bake it in, and so every staged grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_imc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_normalization-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"pool_normalization(pair::PoolPair) -> CorrespondenceNormalization\npool_normalization(size1, size2) -> CorrespondenceNormalization\npool_normalization(raw::StereoRawPair) -> CorrespondenceNormalization\n\nThe pool's two-sided normalization (pixel → NormalizedImageFrame per image), with its inverses. Of a record: its stored maps, pair.source_map and pair.destination_map. Of two image sizes: the map a pool of such images is written in, DiagonalNormalization per side (image centre, half diagonal, corners at unit radius) — THE construction of the pool frame from an image size; a raw pair's is that of its two image sizes; a record built from it (PoolPair{M}(…, N, snn)) stores its sides. A stored pool model M comes back to pixels by composition with the two sides, inv(D) ∘ M ∘ S with S, D = source_map(N), destination_map(N), and goes the other way by D ∘ M ∘ inv(S). It returns a normalization, not a frame: *Frame names frame tags only. The same maps, frame-typed, are pool_frame_maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_normalization"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_pixel_scale-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"pool_pixel_scale(pair::PoolPair) -> Float64\n\nPool units per PIXEL of the pair: the isotropic scale (similarity_scale) of its pixel → NormalizedImageFrame maps, the larger of the two views (a gate takes one tolerance; the two half-diagonals differ by a few percent, so the denser view's 0.1 px reads as ≈ 0.1 px in the other). THE entry of a pixel tolerance into the pool frame: τ_pool = pool_pixel_scale(pair) · τ_px.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pool_pixel_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.rig-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Core","text":"rig(pair::PoolPair) -> StereoRig\n\nThe GT stereo rig of the pair, built on access from the stored pixel calibrations and GT pose (camera 1 at the origin, camera 2 at the GT pose; view ids :cam1, :cam2, the ids of pool_frame_maps). The record stores plain matrices, not the rig: a change to the rig type never makes a stored pool unreadable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.rig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair","page":"Core","text":"StereoRawPair\n\nAbstract supertype of a dataset reader's raw image pair: the input a pool pair is drawn from, in PIXELS. Read through its accessors, whose defaults read the field of the same name (a reader that stores a quantity differently overrides the accessor):\n\nAccessor Default field Meaning\npair_correspondences correspondences pixel correspondences x₁ => x₂\npair_snn snn per-correspondence second-nearest-neighbour ratio (low = good)\npair_K1, pair_K2 K1, K2 pixel calibration matrices\npair_size1, pair_size2 size1, size2 image sizes (Size2)\npair_R_gt, pair_t_gt R_gt, t_gt GT relative motion, source → target\npair_gt_inliers gt_inliers GT inlier mask\npair_scene — the pair's source-scene label, nothing unless overridden\n\nIts pool frame is pool_normalization(raw), the normalization of its two image sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_K1-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_K1(raw::StereoRawPair): the source camera's pixel calibration matrix (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_K1"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_K2-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_K2(raw::StereoRawPair): the target camera's pixel calibration matrix (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_K2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_R_gt-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_R_gt(raw::StereoRawPair): the GT relative rotation, source → target (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_R_gt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_correspondences-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_correspondences(raw::StereoRawPair): the pixel correspondences x₁ => x₂ (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_correspondences"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_gt_inliers-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_gt_inliers(raw::StereoRawPair): the GT inlier mask of the correspondences (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_gt_inliers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_scene-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_scene(raw::StereoRawPair): the pair's source-scene label, nothing unless the reader overrides it (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_scene"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_size1-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_size1(raw::StereoRawPair): the source image's size, a Size2 (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_size1"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_size2-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_size2(raw::StereoRawPair): the target image's size, a Size2 (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_size2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_snn-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_snn(raw::StereoRawPair): the per-correspondence second-nearest-neighbour ratio, low = good (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_snn"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_t_gt-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.StereoRawPair}","page":"Core","text":"pair_t_gt(raw::StereoRawPair): the GT relative translation, source → target (see StereoRawPair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pair_t_gt"},{"category":"module","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries","page":"Core","text":"SpatialQueries\n\nAllocation-free queries on a SpatialIndexing.jl RTree (using VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries):\n\nforeach_intersecting: call a function on every element whose bounding box intersects a query Rect, with early exit;\nnearest_neighbor: the element nearest a point (branch-and-bound), within an optional squared radius;\nnearest_neighbor! with a NearestNeighborState: the same query with its state supplied, so a batch of queries allocates nothing;\nbulk_load!: SpatialIndexing's OMT bulk load, sorting the caller's elements in place.\n\nThe trees are SpatialIndexing's own (RTree, SpatialElem, Rect); build them with bulk_load!, or SpatialIndexing's insert! or load!. VisualGeometryToolkitCore's bbox gives a shape's Rect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.NearestNeighborState","page":"Core","text":"NearestNeighborState(tree::RTree)\n\nThe reusable state of nearest_neighbor!: build one per querying task and every query through it is allocation-free. It is overwritten by each query, so a state is never shared between threads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.NearestNeighborState"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.bulk_load!-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{SpatialIndexing.RTree{T, N, V}, Vector{V}}} where {T, N, V}","page":"Core","text":"bulk_load!(tree::RTree{T,N,V}, elems::Vector{V}) -> tree\n\nBulk-load elems into the empty tree by SpatialIndexing's OMT method (Lee & Lee, \"OMT: Overlap Minimizing Top-down Bulk Loading Algorithm for R-tree\"), sorting elems in place: the caller hands the vector over and must not rely on its order afterwards. The tree is the one SpatialIndexing.load!(tree, elems) builds, node for node, without the copies load! sorts at every level.\n\ntree = RTree{Float64, 2}(Int, Nothing)\nbulk_load!(tree, [SpatialElem(SpatialIndexing.Rect(p, p), j, nothing) for (j, p) in enumerate(points)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.bulk_load!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.foreach_intersecting-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, SpatialIndexing.RTree{T, N, V}, SpatialIndexing.Rect{T, N}}} where {F, T, N, V}","page":"Core","text":"foreach_intersecting(f, tree::RTree{T,N}, rect::SpatialIndexing.Rect{T,N}) -> Bool\n\nCall f(elem) on every element of tree whose bounding box intersects rect (closed boxes: touching counts). f returns true to stop the query early and false to continue. Returns true when f stopped it.\n\nThe elements are SpatialIndexing's (SpatialElem: SpatialIndexing.id(elem), SpatialIndexing.mbr(elem)), visited in tree order. Allocation-free when f is: unlike intersects_with, no iterator state is built per element.\n\nhits = 0\nforeach_intersecting(tree, SpatialIndexing.Rect((0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 1.0))) do elem\n    hits += 1\n    false                                   # keep going\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.foreach_intersecting"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.nearest_neighbor!-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.NearestNeighborState{V, F}, SpatialIndexing.RTree{T, N, V}, NTuple{N, Number}}} where {T, N, V, F}","page":"Core","text":"nearest_neighbor!(state::NearestNeighborState, tree::RTree{T,N}, point::NTuple{N,<:Number}; max_sqdist = Inf) -> (id, sqdist) | nothing\n\nnearest_neighbor with its state supplied: allocation-free. state comes from NearestNeighborState(tree) (or from any tree of the same element type) and is overwritten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.nearest_neighbor!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.nearest_neighbor-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{SpatialIndexing.RTree{T, N}, NTuple{N, Number}}} where {T, N}","page":"Core","text":"nearest_neighbor(tree::RTree{T,N}, point::NTuple{N,<:Number}; max_sqdist = Inf) -> (id, sqdist) | nothing\n\nThe element of tree nearest point: its SpatialIndexing.id and its squared Euclidean distance, measured to the element's bounding box (for point elements, the point-to-point distance). nothing when no element lies within max_sqdist (inclusive) or the tree is empty. Ties keep the element visited last in tree order.\n\nDepth-first branch-and-bound: a subtree whose bounding box is farther than the best distance so far (seeded by max_sqdist) is skipped, so a query touches only the neighbourhood of point. Allocates its one state; a batch of queries supplies one to nearest_neighbor! instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SpatialQueries.nearest_neighbor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CampixelImshow","page":"Core","text":"Create an LScene spec with campixel! camera for SpecApi composition. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CampixelImshow"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrameSpec","page":"Core","text":"Create a coordinate frame as a Lines PlotSpec. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrameSpec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Frustum","page":"Core","text":"Create a camera frustum wireframe as a Lines PlotSpec. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Frustum"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LScene3D","page":"Core","text":"Create a 3D scene with Camera3D controls. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LScene3D"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.camera!","page":"Core","text":"Add camera visualization to an LScene using SpecApi. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.camera!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cameraplot","page":"Core","text":"Visualize a camera with center marker, axes, and frustum. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cameraplot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.campixel_imshow","page":"Core","text":"Display an image using an LScene with campixel! camera. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.campixel_imshow"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.campixel_imshow!","page":"Core","text":"Add an image to an existing LScene with campixel! camera. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.campixel_imshow!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.closeall","page":"Core","text":"Close all GLMakie screen windows. Requires GLMakie (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreGLMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.closeall"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ellipses!","page":"Core","text":"Batched elliptical-boundary overlay — see the Makie extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ellipses!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.frameplot","page":"Core","text":"Visualize a coordinate frame with RGB axes. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.frameplot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.imshow","page":"Core","text":"Display an image with correct orientation using a standard Makie Axis. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.imshow"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.imshow!","page":"Core","text":"Add an image to an existing Axis. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.imshow!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.matches","page":"Core","text":"Visualize point correspondences on a single axis. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.matches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.plot_rectification","page":"Core","text":"Raw | rectified panels through one camera, or one panel per camera for comparison. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.plot_rectification"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.poly!","page":"Core","text":"Add a polygon/shape to an LScene. Not exported to avoid Makie conflicts. Requires a Makie backend (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.poly!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.window!","page":"Core","text":"Display a Figure in a new named GLMakie window. Requires GLMakie (implementation lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCoreGLMakieExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.window!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Transforms-API","page":"Transforms","text":"Framed maps — the geometric objects, whose type is their transform class — the dense homography matrix, coordinate conversions, uncertainty propagation, and the SO(3)/SE(3) helpers. Region maps and image warping are on the Warping API page. See the Transforms and Coordinate Frames guides.","title":"Transforms API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Framed-Maps","page":"Transforms","text":"A map acts on a primitive in its class: an EuclideanMap or a SimilarityMap keeps a circle a Circle (and a feature frame its type), an AffineMap gives a HomEllipseMat, a HomographyMap a Conic (the actions are on the Warping API page).","title":"Framed Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#The-Homography-Matrix","page":"Transforms","text":"","title":"The Homography Matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Coordinate-Conversions","page":"Transforms","text":"","title":"Coordinate Conversions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Uncertainty-Propagation","page":"Transforms","text":"","title":"Uncertainty Propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Sampson-Distance-and-Whitening","page":"Transforms","text":"The Sampson residual is geometric (a model and its observation, no noise); whiten divides it by the observation's covariance along the normal.","title":"Sampson Distance and Whitening"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Observation-Noise","page":"Transforms","text":"A plain point is unit isotropic noise, an Uncertain point carries its covariance, an exact point has none (the Conventions page, \"Observation noise\").","title":"Observation Noise"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Lie-Group-Helpers-and-Rotation-Fitting","page":"Transforms","text":"","title":"Lie-Group Helpers and Rotation Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/transforms/#Index","page":"Transforms","text":"Pages = [\"transforms.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CameraExtrinsics","page":"Transforms","text":"CameraExtrinsics\n\nFrame-pair alias for camera EXTRINSICS (world-to-camera transform): CameraExtrinsics{T, R, V} = EuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, WorldFrame, CameraFrame} (monogram X_CW). The typed counterpart of CameraPose; inv(::CameraExtrinsics) -> CameraPose via the frame swap. Not a wrapper — a CameraExtrinsics IS an EuclideanMap.\n\nCameraExtrinsics(m::EuclideanMap{3}) labels m as extrinsics (an explicit relabel, overriding whatever frames m carried). PosedCamera(model, ::CameraExtrinsics) stores it directly in the extrinsics slot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CameraExtrinsics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CameraPose","page":"Transforms","text":"CameraPose\n\nFrame-pair alias for a camera POSE (camera-to-world transform): CameraPose{T, R, V} = EuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, CameraFrame, WorldFrame} (monogram X_WC). Not a wrapper — a CameraPose IS an EuclideanMap, so .R/.t, point application, and composition all apply directly.\n\nCameraPose(m::EuclideanMap{3}) labels m as a pose (an explicit relabel, overriding whatever frames m carried).\n\nThe typed algebra subsumes the former wrapper machinery: inv(::CameraPose) -> CameraExtrinsics (and back) via the frame swap, and PosedCamera(model, ::CameraPose) inverts internally into the extrinsics slot, so handing a pose where extrinsics are expected is unrepresentable.\n\nSee also CameraExtrinsics, lookat_pose, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CameraPose"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap","page":"Transforms","text":"EuclideanMap{N,T,R<:Rotation{N,T},V<:StaticVector{N},Src<:Frame{N},Dst<:Frame{N}} <: Transformation\n\nRigid transformation x ↦ R*x + t in N dimensions, combining rotation and translation.\n\nThis represents a Euclidean (rigid body) transformation that preserves distances and angles. It consists of a rotation R followed by a translation t.\n\nThe translation's element type is free. A PHYSICAL map — a camera or target pose, a rig member — holds FlexUnits' base-form quantities, Quantity{T, Dimensions{FixRat32}} in metres (UnitfulEuclideanMap); an inner-loop map holds plain numbers — Float64, Float32, a ForwardDiff Dual — beside a rotation of its own eltype T (PlainEuclideanMap). has_units tells them apart, ustrip(u, E) gives the plain map in the unit u, and attaching a unit is construction, EuclideanMap(rotation(E), translation(E) .* u). A unitful map meeting plain numbers — applied to a plain point, composed with a plain map — is FlexUnits' DimensionError, never a promotion.\n\nThe trailing Src/Dst parameters are coordinate-Frame tags: the map consumes Src-coordinates and produces Dst-coordinates (Drake monogram: X_AB ≙ Src = B, Dst = A). All constructors that do not state frames produce Src = Dst = UnlabeledFrame{N} — labels are opt-in and never viral. inv swaps the frames; ∘ checks the junction frame between two labeled maps (see the truth table at that method) and throws a MethodError on a labeled mismatch. Use relabel/unlabel as explicit escape hatches.\n\nFields\n\nR::Rotation{N,T}: Rotation component (from Rotations.jl)\nt::StaticVector{N}: Translation vector — plain numbers, or lengths in metres\n\nConstructors\n\n# From rotation and translation vector\nR = RotMatrix{2}(π/4)  # 45° rotation\nt = [2.0, 1.0]         # translation\neuclidean = EuclideanMap(R, t)\n\n# From MRP (Modified Rodrigues Parameters) for 3D\nmrp = MRP(0.1, 0.2, 0.3)\neuclidean_3d = EuclideanMap(mrp, [1.0, 2.0, 3.0])\n\n# A physical pose: the translation a length (stored in metres)\npose = EuclideanMap(mrp, SVector(62.8, 0.0, 1200.0) .* mm)\n\n# From tuple\neuclidean = EuclideanMap((R, t))\n\nUsage\n\n# Apply transformation to points\npoint = Point2f(1.0, 0.0)\ntransformed = euclidean(point)\n\n# Compose transformations\ncombined = euclidean2 ∘ euclidean1\n\n# Inverse transformation\ninverse_euclidean = inv(euclidean)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap-NTuple{7, Real}","page":"Transforms","text":"EuclideanMap(qw, qx, qy, qz, tx, ty, tz) -> EuclideanMap{3,Float64,…}\nEuclideanMap(quat::AbstractVector, t::AbstractVector) -> EuclideanMap{3,Float64,…}\n\nCOLMAP-style 3D rigid transform from a unit quaternion (qw, qx, qy, qz) and a 3-vector translation (tx, ty, tz).  Used by SfM-format readers (ETH3D, IMC PhotoTourism, ScanNet++, custom COLMAP exports) where pose data arrives as raw scalars on a per-line basis.\n\nRotations.QuatRotation auto-normalizes the quaternion, so callers don't need to pre-normalize — the resulting rotation is always a valid SO(3) element.\n\nExamples\n\n# Per-scalar form (typical when parsing line-by-line)\nT = EuclideanMap(0.998, 0.0, 0.064, 0.0, 1.5, 2.5, 3.0)\n\n# Vector form (typical when receiving npz arrays)\nT = EuclideanMap([qw, qx, qy, qz], [tx, ty, tz])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Rotations.Rotation{N, T}, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}} where {N, T}","page":"Transforms","text":"EuclideanMap(R::Rotation{N}, t::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}; check = true)\n\nA PHYSICAL map: every component of t a length, in any length unit (a quantity of another dimension is a DimensionError), stored as FlexUnits' base-form quantities — metres (base_quantity). A RotMatrix, QuatRotation or Angle2d rotation is stored as given; any other Rotation as a RotMatrix of its own eltype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Rotations.Rotation{N, T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, <:Real, 1}}} where {N, T}","page":"Transforms","text":"EuclideanMap(R::Rotation{N,T}, t::StaticVector{N,<:Real}; check=true) -> EuclideanMap{N,T,...}\n\nElement-type-preserving constructor that PRESERVES the input static-vector subtype and eltype.  Vec3, Point3, SVector, MVector, FieldVector are all bit-identical at the data level — accept whatever the caller hands in. The translation's eltype is independent of the rotation's: a Float32 or ForwardDiff Dual translation beside a Float64 rotation stays what it is (an integer translation becomes its float type).\n\nIf a typed container (e.g. MVector{4, Extrinsics3} or FixedModels{4, Extrinsics3}) needs a specific translation type, the implicit Base.convert defined below performs the (zero-cost) bitcast at the storage boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3, T, 9}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}} where T","page":"Transforms","text":"EuclideanMap(R::SMatrix{3,3,T,9}, t::StaticVector{3};\n             check=true, project=false) -> EuclideanMap\n\nConstruct a rigid-body transform directly from a 3×3 SMatrix (not already a Rotation type).  This is the boundary constructor — the entry point at which raw 3×3 numerical data crosses into the typed EuclideanMap.  All other constructors take a typed Rotation{N,T} whose invariants are enforced by Rotations.jl.\n\nValidation policy\n\ncheck project semantics typical caller\ntrue false strict — must already be a rotation math you trust\ntrue true snap-to-nearest then verify dataset loaders\nfalse false trusted — skip everything internal algebra\nfalse true snap, no verify very dirty inputs\n\nproject=true snaps R to the nearest proper rotation via SVD (U·Vᵀ, with a sign flip if the result has determinant −1).  Floating-point drift from text-file roundtrip or naive inv(T_B)·T_A composition typically sits at 1e-6-ish, well within the snap-then-verify regime.\n\nNarrowed to SMatrix{3,3,T,9} (rather than StaticMatrix{3,3,T}) to avoid dispatch ambiguity with the Rotation-typed constructor: RotMatrix{3,T} is a subtype of both Rotation and StaticMatrix{3,3,T}, but is not an SMatrix, so narrowing here keeps the two paths disjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Extrinsics3","page":"Transforms","text":"Extrinsics3\n\nConcrete LABELED CameraExtrinsics alias: CameraExtrinsics{Float64, RotMatrix{3,Float64,9}, Vec{3,Float64}}, i.e. EuclideanMap{3, Float64, …, Src = WorldFrame, Dst = CameraFrame} (monogram X_CW, applies as x_cam = R·X_world + t).\n\nThe model currency of the absolute-pose solvers: p3p returns FixedModels{4, Extrinsics3} and P3P <: MinimalSolver{Extrinsics3}. isbitstype(Extrinsics3) == true — stored inline in FixedModels and RansacWorkspace, zero heap allocation on update.\n\nReplaces the deprecated Pose3 alias (removed in 0.7): same layout and element types, but the world-to-camera direction is now a dispatch fact instead of a docstring caveat — an Extrinsics3 IS a CameraExtrinsics, so inv yields a CameraPose and labeled composition is frame-checked. Solver code that composes models against other labeled maps must have the junction frames agree (or unlabel explicitly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Extrinsics3"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap","page":"Transforms","text":"PlainEuclideanMap{N}\nPlainEuclideanMap{N, T}\n\nAn EuclideanMap whose translation is plain numbers (V <: StaticVector{N, <:Real}: Float64, Float32, a ForwardDiff Dual) — the map the inner loop takes: minimal kernels, charts and manifolds, solvers. A unitful map handed to a function typed on it is a MethodError. T is the rotation's eltype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.UnitfulEuclideanMap","page":"Transforms","text":"UnitfulEuclideanMap{N}\nUnitfulEuclideanMap{N, T}\n\nAn EuclideanMap whose translation is FlexUnits quantities — a physical map, its translation in metres (base form). See PlainEuclideanMap, has_units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.UnitfulEuclideanMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#FlexUnits.ustrip-Tuple{FlexUnits.Units, Uncertain{<:EuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}}","page":"Transforms","text":"ustrip(u::Units, X::Uncertain{<:EuclideanMap{3}}) -> Uncertain\n\nThe plain form of an uncertain physical pose: the map stripped in u (ustrip(u, E)) and its 6×6 covariance (or precision) — rotation first, the RigidExpManifold6 chart — rescaled with it: the translation block in u², the rotation–translation block in rad·u. The inverse is with_unit(X, u).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlexUnits.ustrip"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#FlexUnits.ustrip-Union{Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{FlexUnits.Units, EuclideanMap{N, T, R, V, Src, Dst}}} where {N, T, R, V, Src, Dst}","page":"Transforms","text":"ustrip(u::Units, E::EuclideanMap) -> EuclideanMap\n\nThe plain map of a physical E: its translation in the length unit u, the rotation and the frames unchanged. An ArgumentError on a map that is already plain (has_units); with_unit(E, u) attaches one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlexUnits.ustrip"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.angle_between-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"angle_between(u, v) -> Real\n\nThe angle between two vectors of 2 or 3 entries, in radians in [0, π]: θ = atan(‖u × v‖, u · v) (for 2-vectors ‖u × v‖ = |u₁v₂ − u₂v₁|). Neither vector need be unit: the form is scale-free. Accurate at every angle, where the acos of the normalized dot product loses half the digits near 0 and π (its derivative is unbounded there): two unit vectors 1e-8 rad apart have a dot product that rounds to 1, so acos returns 0 (or 1.5e-8 a few ulps away), and this form returns the angle to full precision. 0 when either vector is zero; NaN in, NaN out. Generic in the element type (ForwardDiff duals flow through away from θ = 0, where ‖u × v‖ has no derivative). The angle between two bearings, two rotation axes, a predicted and an observed ray; for two translations up to scale, lengths included, translation_angle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.angle_between"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, Float64, var\"#s548\", var\"#s546\", Src, Dst} where {var\"#s548\"<:Rotations.Rotation{3, Float64}, var\"#s546\"<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, Float64, 1}, Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, Float64, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, Float64, 1}}","page":"Transforms","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(pose::EuclideanMap{3}, X::SVector{3}, ray::SVector{3})\n    -> (g::SVector{2}, G_x::SMatrix{2,5}, G_θ::SMatrix{2,6})\n\nCompute the reprojection constraint and its derivatives for a 3D rigid pose.\n\nThe constraint is the perspective-projected residual in calibrated coordinates:   g = π(R·X + t) − π(ray)\n\nwhere π is perspective division [x,y,z] → [x/z, y/z] and ray is the backprojected observation (encodes camera calibration).\n\nThe model Jacobian G_θ uses the left-perturbation convention with a 6-DOF parameterization δ = (δω₁, δω₂, δω₃, δt₁, δt₂, δt₃):   ∂g/∂δ = Jπ · [−[X_cam]×  I₃]\n\nwhere Jπ is the 2×3 perspective division Jacobian and [v]× is the skew-symmetric (cross-product) matrix of v.\n\nReturns:\n\ng::SVector{2,T}      — reprojection residual (calibrated coordinates)\nG_x::SMatrix{2,5,T}  — ∂g/∂(X, ray₁, ray₂) observation Jacobian\nG_θ::SMatrix{2,6,T}  — ∂g/∂(δω, δt) pose perturbation Jacobian\n\nwarning: PINHOLE ONLY — this residual lives in the gnomonic chart\nπ divides by the third component, so this is the z = 1 chart (GnomonicFrame) and it exists only on the forward hemisphere. It is degenerate at 90° from the optical axis and cannot represent a ray with Z ≤ 0 at all. That is correct for a PINHOLE camera and wrong for a fisheye or omnidirectional one, whose rays routinely go past 90°.It is legitimate where it is used — VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's Pinhole PnP LM — and must not become the general path. The camera-general route is the ray-domain pixel residual (constraint_with_jacobians(model, pose, X, u) in cameras/camera_models.jl), which projects through the camera's own lens and therefore never assumes the chart exists. Calibrated two-view geometry takes the same view: it acts on unit bearings (unit rays, StaticVector{3}), never on this chart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.map_frames-Union{Tuple{EuclideanMap{N, T, R, V, Src, Dst}}, Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}} where {N, T, R, V, Src, Dst}","page":"Transforms","text":"map_frames(X) -> (Src, Dst)\n\nThe source and destination frame types of a frame-labeled map — an EuclideanMap, a RotationMap or a HomographyMap, plain or Uncertain — read off its type. The labels relabel sets: relabel(map_frames(X)..., Y) gives Y the frames of X.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.map_frames"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.relabel-Union{Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Type{Src}, Type{Dst}, EuclideanMap{N, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {Src<:Frame{N}, Dst<:Frame{N}}}} where {N, T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{N, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T), Src<:Frame{N}, Dst<:Frame{N}}","page":"Transforms","text":"relabel(Src::Type{<:Frame{N}}, Dst::Type{<:Frame{N}}, m::EuclideanMap{N}) -> EuclideanMap\n\nReturn m with its frame tags replaced by Src/Dst (map consumes Src-coordinates, produces Dst-coordinates). Zero-cost: the rotation and translation are reused bit-for-bit; only the type changes.\n\nThis is a DELIBERATE escape hatch: it overrides whatever frames m carried, with no check. Use it at trust boundaries (dataset loaders, solver outputs) where the caller — not the type system — knows the frames. Attaching a frame of the wrong dimension (e.g. a Frame{2} to an N = 3 map) is a type error (MethodError).\n\nSee also unlabel, Frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.relabel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rotation-Tuple{EuclideanMap}","page":"Transforms","text":"rotation(E::EuclideanMap) -> Rotation\n\nThe rotation R of the rigid map x ↦ R·x + t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rotation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.skew-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"skew(v::AbstractVector) -> SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}\n\nSkew-symmetric (cross-product) matrix [v]_× such that [v]_× w = v × w for all w ∈ R³.  Used in essential-matrix construction E = [t]_× R and in pose-perturbation Jacobians (see constraint_with_jacobians).\n\nGTSAM spells this skewSymmetric; Sophus spells it SO3::hat (with vee for the inverse).\n\nErrors if length(v) != 3.\n\nExample\n\nt = SVector{3,Float64}(1.0, 2.0, 3.0)\nS = skew(t)        # 3×3 SMatrix\nS * SVector(0.0, 1.0, 0.0) ≈ cross(t, SVector(0.0, 1.0, 0.0))   # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.skew"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.translation-Tuple{EuclideanMap}","page":"Transforms","text":"translation(E::EuclideanMap) -> StaticVector\n\nThe translation t of the rigid map x ↦ R·x + t: lengths in metres (FlexUnits base-form quantities) for a physical map, plain numbers for an inner-loop one (has_units).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.translation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.translation_angle-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"translation_angle(t1, t2) -> Float64\n\nAngular distance (radians) between two translation directions — plain vectors, or lengths (compared in metres).\n\nSince translation is only recoverable up to scale from epipolar geometry, this compares directions, scale-free: angle_between(t₁, t₂), atan(‖t₁ × t₂‖, t₁ · t₂), accurate at every angle. 0 when either vector is (numerically) zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.translation_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unlabel-Union{Tuple{EuclideanMap{N, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{N, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T), Src<:Frame{N}, Dst<:Frame{N}}}, Tuple{N}} where N","page":"Transforms","text":"unlabel(m::EuclideanMap{N}) -> EuclideanMap{..., UnlabeledFrame{N}, UnlabeledFrame{N}}\n\nErase the frame tags of m (both endpoints become UnlabeledFrame{N}). Zero-cost, deliberate escape hatch — the result composes with anything under the wildcard rule. Inverse direction of relabel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unlabel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_unit-Tuple{EuclideanMap, FlexUnits.Units}","page":"Transforms","text":"with_unit(E::EuclideanMap, u::Units) -> EuclideanMap\n\nThe physical map of a plain E: its translation read as being in the length unit u and stored in metres, the rotation (of any parameterization) and the frames kept exactly. The inverse of ustrip(u, E); an ArgumentError on a map that already has units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_unit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap","page":"Transforms","text":"SimilarityMap{N,T,S,R<:Rotation{N,T},V<:StaticVector{N},Src<:Frame{N},Dst<:Frame{N}} <: Transformation\n\nThe similarity transformation x ↦ s·R·x + t of N-space: a positive scale s, a rotation R (a Rotations.jl Rotation{N}) and a translation t.\n\nS = SimilarityMap(2.0, RotMatrix{2}(π / 6), SVector(1.0, -3.0))\nS(circle)                  # a Circle: centre mapped, radius · 2\nS ∘ inv(S)                 # a SimilarityMap (the identity, up to rounding)\nSimilarityMap(E)           # an EuclideanMap, s = 1 (also a rotation LinearMap, a Translation, a uniform scaling)\n\nAccessors scale, rotation, translation. ∘ with a similarity, an EuclideanMap or a Translation (either order) is a SimilarityMap, and so is inv, both computed from the parts. It acts on primitives and feature frames in its class (the class table of the Transforms guide); to_homogeneous is [s·R t; 0 1].\n\nPlain numbers only. No consumer holds a physical similarity, so a quantity translation or scale (or a unitful EuclideanMap converted or composed with one) is refused with an ArgumentError naming the fix: strip it in metres first, ustrip(m, t) or ustrip(m, E). check = true checks the rotation and that s is positive and finite.\n\nThe trailing Src/Dst parameters are coordinate-Frame tags, as for EuclideanMap: constructors that state none give UnlabeledFrame{N}, inv swaps them, ∘ checks a labeled junction (MethodError on a mismatch), relabel/unlabel are the escape hatches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap-Union{Tuple{EuclideanMap{N, T, R, V, Src, Dst}}, Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}} where {N, T, R, V, Src, Dst}","page":"Transforms","text":"SimilarityMap(E::EuclideanMap) -> SimilarityMap\n\nThe rigid map as a similarity of scale 1, its frame tags kept. A physical (unitful) map is refused: strip it first, SimilarityMap(ustrip(m, E)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Number, Rotations.Rotation{N, T}, AbstractVector}} where {N, T}","page":"Transforms","text":"SimilarityMap(s::Real, R::Rotation{N}, t::AbstractVector{<:Real}; check = true)\n\nThe similarity x ↦ s·R·x + t. A RotMatrix, QuatRotation or Angle2d is stored as given, any other Rotation as a RotMatrix; an integer scale or translation becomes its float type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RotationMap","page":"Transforms","text":"RotationMap{N, T, R <: Rotation{N, T}, Src <: Frame{N}, Dst <: Frame{N}} <: AbstractMatrixMap\n\nA rotation about the origin, x ↦ R·x, in N dimensions, carrying the frame tags an EuclideanMap carries: it consumes Src-coordinates and produces Dst-coordinates. Its one field is the rotation R, so it has no translation at all, rather than a zero one: it poses a camera that turns about its centre (OrientedCamera), and it holds no length, so it is the same map plain and physical (has_units is false).\n\nRotationMap(R::Rotation{N}; check = true), unlabeled. A RotMatrix, a QuatRotation or an Angle2d is stored as given, any other Rotation as a RotMatrix of its own eltype, by EuclideanMap's rule. RotationMap(R::StaticMatrix{N, N}; check = true) takes a raw matrix as a RotMatrix. check verifies R·Rᵀ = I, det R = 1.\nrelabel(Src, Dst, M) and unlabel(M) set and erase the tags.\nM(x) is R·x, for a vector or a point, plain or physical.\ninv(M) is the inverse rotation with the tags swapped.\nA ∘ B checks the junction (B's Dst meets A's Src, the unlabeled frame a wildcard). Two rotations compose to a RotationMap. A rotation and an EuclideanMap, in either order, compose to an EuclideanMap: R_A·(R_B·x + t_B) = (R_A R_B)·x + R_A t_B and R_A R_B·x + t_A.\nrotation(M) is R. transform_deriv(M, x) is R. to_homogeneous(M) is the (N + 1) × (N + 1) block [R 0; 0 1]. convert_eltype(T, M) rebuilds the rotation in T and keeps the tags.\n== compares the rotations as matrices and the tags. isapprox compares the rotations' matrices, and the tags must be equal.\n\nIt is isbits, so it is a device value as it is. A persisted rotation is a Hamilton quaternion, as for poses (the stack's persisted-geometry convention); no kind writes a RotationMap yet.\n\nusing Rotations: RotY\nRk = relabel(CameraFrame, WorldFrame, RotationMap(RotY(0.3)))   # a frame's orientation: camera → world\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, Rk)            # an OrientedCamera; stores the extrinsics inv(Rk)\npose(camera) == Rk\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RotationMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap","page":"Transforms","text":"HomographyMap{N,T,M<:StaticMatrix{N,N,T},Src<:Frame,Dst<:Frame} <: Transformation\n\nProjective transformation with cached inverse for efficient covariant (points) and contravariant (lines, conics) transformations.\n\nA HomographyMap is ALWAYS projective in meaning, whatever its matrix: a circle or an ellipse maps to a Conic. The sub-projective classes are their own maps, acting in their class (the class table of the Transforms guide, \"Maps Carry the Transform Class\").\n\nType Parameters\n\nN: Matrix dimension (3 for 2D projective, 4 for 3D projective)\nT: Element type\nM: Matrix type — a HomographyMat (a lifted homography matrix) or a plain SMatrix (every other lift and every mixed composition)\nSrc/Dst: coordinate-Frame tags of the point FRAMES the map connects — Frame{N-1}, since the N×N matrix acts on (N-1)-dimensional frames (a 3×3 homography is 2D↔2D projective: Frame{2} on both sides). Same design as EuclideanMap: the map consumes Src-frame coordinates and produces Dst-frame coordinates (Drake monogram H_AB ≙ Src = B, Dst = A), every frame-free constructor defaults both tags to UnlabeledFrame{N-1}, inv swaps the tags, and ∘ checks a labeled junction (identical wildcard truth table — see Base.:∘ below).\n\nHomographyMat stays frame-FREE algebra; frames are declared at the Mat → Map lift:\n\nH = HomographyMap{TargetPlaneFrame, PixelFrame{:colmap}}(H_mat)   # labeled lift\nH = HomographyMap(H_mat)                                    # unlabeled lift\n\nrelabel/unlabel are the same explicit escape hatches as for EuclideanMap.\n\nTransformation Rules (Hartley & Zisserman conventions)\n\nPoints (covariant): p' = H·p\nLines (contravariant): l' = H^{-T}·l\nConics (contravariant): Q' = H^{-T}·Q·H^{-1}\n\nDimension Convention\n\nN×N matrix operates on (N-1)-dimensional Euclidean space\nPoint{N-1} → Euclidean input, lifted via to_affine\nPoint{N} → Homogeneous input, used directly\n\nExamples\n\n# Lift a homography matrix, or any structured map (its analytic inverse is stored)\nh = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\nh = HomographyMap(EuclideanMap(RotMatrix{2}(θ), t))\nh = HomographyMap(AffineMap(A, b))\n\n# Transform points (Euclidean → homogeneous output)\np′ = h(Point2(100, 200))  # → Point3 (homogeneous)\n\n# Transform points (homogeneous → homogeneous output)\np′ = h(Point3(100, 200, 1))  # → Point3\n\n# Transform conics: always a Conic (classify it, or apply the structured map itself)\nh(circle)   # Conic\nh(ellipse)  # Conic\n\n# Transform lines\nl′ = h(Line2D(1.0, -1.0, 0.0))  # → Line2D\n\n# Composition\nh_composed = h1 ∘ h2\n\n# Inverse is O(1) - just swaps stored matrices\nh_inv = inv(h)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap-Tuple{Circle}","page":"Transforms","text":"(h::HomographyMap{3})(c::Circle) -> Conic\n\nTransform a circle through a homography map: a Conic, whatever the map's matrix (a HomographyMap is projective in meaning). An EuclideanMap, a SimilarityMap, a translation, rotation or uniform scaling keeps a circle a Circle, and an affine map gives a HomEllipseMat: apply them directly.\n\nThe returned conic could be:\n\nAn ellipse (most common for camera projections of visible circles)\nA hyperbola (circle partially behind camera)\nA parabola (circle tangent to plane at infinity)\nA degenerate conic (point or lines)\n\nUse classify_conic() to determine the conic type, then construct the appropriate geometric primitive:\n\nH = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\ncircle = Circle(Point2(100.0, 100.0), 50.0)\nQ′ = H(circle)  # a Conic under a camera homography\n\ntrait = classify_conic(Q′)\nif trait isa EllipseTrait\n    ellipse = Ellipse(Q′)  # Construct geometric primitive\nelseif trait isa HyperbolaTrait\n    # Handle hyperbola case\nend\n\nExample: Filter to valid ellipses\n\nH = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\nconic_matrices = H.(circles)\nellipses = [Ellipse(Q) for Q in conic_matrices if classify_conic(Q) isa EllipseTrait]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap-Tuple{Ellipse}","page":"Transforms","text":"(h::HomographyMap{3})(e::Ellipse) -> Conic\n\nTransform an ellipse through a homography map: a Conic, which could be any conic type (an affine map applied directly gives a HomEllipseMat). Use classify_conic() to determine the type and construct appropriately.\n\nExample\n\nH = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\nQ′ = H(ellipse)\nif classify_conic(Q′) isa EllipseTrait\n    ellipse′ = Ellipse(Q′)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap-Tuple{Union{AffineMap, CoordinateTransformations.LinearMap, Translation}}","page":"Transforms","text":"HomographyMap(a) -> HomographyMap{3, T, SMatrix{3, 3, T, 9}}\n\nAny structured planar map — a Translation, LinearMap or AffineMap, an EuclideanMap{2}, a SimilarityMap{2} — lifted to a homography: the forward matrix to_homogeneous(a), the stored inverse to_homogeneous(inv(a)) (the map's own analytic inverse, never a 3×3 inversion). An EuclideanMap's or SimilarityMap's frame tags ride along. The lift is projective in meaning: it maps a circle to a Conic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap-Union{Tuple{Hyperplane{K, N, S}}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}} where {N, T, M, K, S}","page":"Transforms","text":"(h::HomographyMap{N})(hp::Hyperplane{M}) -> Hyperplane{M}\n\nTransform a hyperplane through a homography map.\n\nHyperplanes transform contravariantly (like lines): π' = H^{-T}·π\n\nExample\n\nH = HomographyMap(AffineMap(A, b))\nline = Line2D(1.0, -1.0, 0.0)  # x - y = 0\nline′ = H(line)\n\nplane = Plane3D(Point3(0,0,0), Vec3(0,0,1))  # z = 0\n# For 4×4 homography map:\n# plane′ = H(plane)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer_ellipse-Tuple{CoordinateTransformations.Transformation, Union{Ellipse, Circle}}","page":"Transforms","text":"transfer_ellipse(T::Transformation, shape::Union{Circle, Ellipse}) -> Ellipse | nothing\n\nThe ellipse-PRESERVING transfer of a circle/ellipse through any 2-D map of the warping paradigm. A homography carries the conic exactly (the method above); every other map is replaced by its local affinity at the shape's centre — c′ = T(c), Σ′ = J·Σ·Jᵀ with J = transform_deriv(T, c) — the same linearisation a detection's shape gets when it is carried across an undistortion. A ComposedTransformation transfers stage by stage, so the exact stages stay exact and only the non-projective ones linearise: for a lens camera, distortion_map(model) ∘ HomographyMap(pinhole camera) pushes the conic through the plane homography exactly and applies the lens's affinity at the transferred centre (see transfer_ellipse(::PosedCamera, _)). nothing where the map is not defined or not finite at the centre (a lens fold, the line at infinity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer_ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer_ellipse-Tuple{HomographyMap{3, T, M} where {T, M<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}}, Union{Ellipse, Circle}}","page":"Transforms","text":"transfer_ellipse(H::HomographyMap, shape::Union{Circle, Ellipse}) -> Ellipse | nothing\n\nPush a circle/ellipse through the homography and classify: the transferred conic when it IS an ellipse, nothing when the map carries it across the line at infinity (hyperbola/parabola/degenerate). Downstream code spelled Q = H(region(...)); is_ellipse(Q) || continue; Ellipse(Q) at six call sites — this is that pattern, once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer_ellipse"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractMatrixMap","page":"Transforms","text":"Parent for CT.Transformations that have a homogeneous matrix form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractMatrixMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMat","page":"Transforms","text":"HomographyMat{T} <: StaticMatrix{3, 3, T}\n\nA planar projective homography as a dense 3×3 matrix — algebra (a solver's, a DLT's output), frame-free. The geometric object is its HomographyMap, which carries the inverse and the frame tags. The product and the inverse of homographies are homographies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_affine-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}} where {N, T}","page":"Transforms","text":"to_affine(v::StaticVector{N,T}) -> StaticVector{N+1,T}\n\nConvert euclidean coordinates to affine coordinates by appending 1 (setting w=1). Embeds points in the affine hyperplane of projective space.  vcat preserves the input wrapper (Point2 → Point3, Vec2 → Vec3, SVector{2} → SVector{3}).\n\nFor a non-static AbstractVector fallback, returns SVector{N+1,T}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_affine"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_euclidean-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, Real}","page":"Transforms","text":"to_euclidean(v::StaticVector{3}, w_ref::Real) -> Union{SVector{2}, Nothing}\n\nChirality-guarded perspective division: returns nothing unless the homogeneous coordinate is finite, nonzero, and on the SAME SIDE as w_ref (the w a reference point on the visible side produced — see homogeneous_w). Points beyond a plane's horizon flip w's sign and would otherwise wrap into view from behind the camera; the throwing one-arg form cannot express that, so callers hand-rolled this guard (three copies in BlobBoards, one of which forgot it — the 2026-09 audit's training-mask bug).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_euclidean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_euclidean-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Transforms","text":"to_euclidean(v::AbstractVector{T}) -> SVector{N-1,T}\n\nConvert homogeneous coordinates to euclidean by perspective division. Removes the last coordinate after dividing by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_euclidean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.affine_part-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Transforms","text":"affine_part(M::StaticMatrix{3,3}) -> AffineMap\n\nExtract the 2×2 linear part and 2D translation from a 3×3 homogeneous matrix. The bottom row is not read; for a camera calibration matrix K it is the pixel affine of the imaging chain, affine_part(K) ∘ lens.\n\nWorks with any 3×3 static matrix: a CameraCalibrationMat, a plain SMatrix{3,3} (to_homogeneous of an affine map), etc.\n\nExample\n\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\nam = affine_part(K)  # AffineMap([800 0; 0 800], [320, 240])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.affine_part"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_homogeneous-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N, N}, T, 2} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T}} where N","page":"Transforms","text":"to_homogeneous(map) -> SMatrix{N+1, N+1, T}\nto_homogeneous(A::StaticMatrix{N,N}, t::StaticVector{N}) -> SMatrix{N+1, N+1, T}\n\nThe homogeneous matrix [A t; 0 1] of an affine-family map of N-space, as a plain SMatrix — for algebra and interop. Every map has one: a Translation, a LinearMap (a rotation, a UniformScaling — 2-D — a Diagonal, any matrix), an AffineMap, an EuclideanMap, a SimilarityMap, a RotationMap, a composition a ∘ b (the product), and a HomographyMap (its forward matrix). Exact: the map's entries copied, the last row the constant 0 … 0 1.\n\nThe matrix carries no class: the map's TYPE does. Geometric operations take the map (S(circle), a ∘ b, inv(a)) and convert inside, once.\n\nExample\n\nM = @SMatrix [2.0 0.0; 0.0 1.5]  # scaling\nt = SVector(1.0, 2.0)            # translation\nH = to_homogeneous(M, t)         # 3×3 homogeneous matrix\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_homogeneous"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate-Tuple{Any, LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling{Bool}}","page":"Transforms","text":"propagate(J, Σ)\n\nPropagate covariance Σ through the linear map with Jacobian J:\n\npropagate(J, Σ) = J * Σ * Jᵀ\n\nSpecializations:\n\nΣ = I  (UniformScaling{Bool}): returns J * Jᵀ\nΣ = λI (UniformScaling): returns λ * (J * Jᵀ)\nΣ = diag(...) (Diagonal): returns J * Σ * Jᵀ via column scaling\nGeneral matrix: full sandwich product J * Σ * Jᵀ\nStatic J with a dense Σ (Matrix, Symmetric, Diagonal{Vector}): Σ is converted once to its static size, so the result is static like J\n\nOne of three covariance verbs: propagate goes THROUGH A MAP (its Jacobian, or a framed map carrying its frames), transform_covariance BETWEEN CHARTS of one model, implicit_covariance of an ESTIMATE.\n\nGTSAM equivalent: a function's OptionalJacobian H, with the caller forming H·Σ·Hᵀ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitening_factor-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{dg, nx}, T, 2}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{nx}, Tuple{dg}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{dg, nx}, T, 2}, Any}} where {dg, nx, T}","page":"Transforms","text":"whitening_factor(G_x::SMatrix{dg,nx}, Σ_x = I) -> SMatrix{dg,dg}\n\nThe inverse Cholesky factor L⁻¹ of the propagated constraint covariance Σ_g = G_x·Σ_x·G_xᵀ = L·Lᵀ: whitened rows are L⁻¹·g and L⁻¹·G_θ, the factor frozen once (at a seed, or at the previous iteration's model of an iteratively reweighted fit) where whiten re-derives it per call — the Gauss–Newton currency of a constraint whose LM row holds its whitening fixed. A scalar constraint's gradient (an SVector) gives the number 1/√(G_x·Σ_x·G_xᵀ). A degenerate Σ_g (its relative rank verdict) is a ZERO factor — its rows drop out of a solve; NaN propagates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitening_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{Sc}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{SimilarityMap{N, T, Sc, R, V, Src, Dst}, CovMat{N, S, L, C}}} where {N, T, Sc, R, V, Src<:Frame{N}, Dst<:Frame{N}, S, L, C<:Frame{N}}","page":"Transforms","text":"propagate(m::SimilarityMap{N}, Σ::CovMat{N})  -> CovMat{N, ..., Dst}\npropagate(m::SimilarityMap{N}, Λ::PrecMat{N}) -> PrecMat{N, ..., Dst}\npropagate(m::SimilarityMap{N}, u::Uncertain)  -> Uncertain\n\nAs for an EuclideanMap (the same junction check, the same two laws), with the constant Jacobian J = s·R: Σ ↦ s²·RΣRᵀ, Λ ↦ RΛRᵀ / s².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{EuclideanMap{N, T, R, V, Src, Dst}, CovMat{N, S, L, C}}} where {N, T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{N, T}, V<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1}, Src<:Frame{N}, Dst<:Frame{N}, S, L, C<:Frame{N}}","page":"Transforms","text":"propagate(m::EuclideanMap{N}, Σ::CovMat{N})  -> CovMat{N, ..., Dst}\npropagate(m::EuclideanMap{N}, Λ::PrecMat{N}) -> PrecMat{N, ..., Dst}\n\nChecked, frame-labeled propagation of a statistical form through a framed rigid map. The map's action on points is affine with constant Jacobian J = R, so no evaluation point is needed. The form's frame must meet the map's Src under the standing wildcard truth table (a labeled mismatch is a MethodError; UnlabeledFrame on either side is unchecked), and the result carries the map's Dst as-is.\n\nThe law dispatches on the REPRESENTATION — this is the point of the types:\n\ncovariance, a (2,0) tensor, pushes forward: Σ ↦ J Σ Jᵀ\nprecision, a (0,2) bilinear form, pulls back: Λ ↦ J⁻ᵀ Λ J⁻¹\n\n(For a rigid map the two conjugations coincide numerically because R is orthogonal; the code follows the honest law per type so the invariant propagate(m, inv(Σ)) == inv(propagate(m, Σ)) holds by construction, not by coincidence.)\n\nThe raw-Jacobian layer propagate(J, Σ) is unchanged and unlabeled — use it when no framed map is in hand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{HomographyMap{3, T, M, Src, Dst}, Attributed{P, <:CovMat{2, S, <:Any, C}}}} where {T, M, Src<:Frame{2}, Dst<:Frame{2}, S, C<:Frame{2}, P<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T)}","page":"Transforms","text":"propagate(h::HomographyMap{3}, u::Uncertain{<:StaticVector{2}}) -> Uncertain\n\nTransport an uncertain 2D point through a framed collineation. The projective point action x ↦ π(H·x̃) is nonlinear, so the Jacobian is evaluated at the point — which is why the HomographyMap overloads take the observation (Uncertain), not a bare statistical form. Frame checking and labeling follow the framed-map contract (frame meets Src under the wildcard truth table; result carries Dst), and the law dispatches on the representation: covariance pushes forward Σ ↦ JΣJᵀ, precision pulls back Λ ↦ J⁻ᵀΛJ⁻¹ with the 2×2 point Jacobian J.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{EuclideanMap{N, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{N, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T), Src<:Frame{N}, Dst<:Frame{N}}, Uncertain{V, <:Union{CovMat{N, T, M, C} where {T, M<:Union{LinearAlgebra.Diagonal{T, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}}, ScaledIdentity{N, T}, StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{N, N, T}}, C<:Frame{N}}, PrecMat{N, T, M, C} where {T, M<:Union{LinearAlgebra.Diagonal{T, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}}, ScaledIdentity{N, T}, StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{N, N, T}}, C<:Frame{N}}}}}} where {N, V}","page":"Transforms","text":"propagate(m::EuclideanMap{N}, u::Uncertain) -> Uncertain\n\nTransport an observation — geometry AND uncertainty — through a framed rigid map in one checked call: the value moves by the map's point action m(x), the statistical form by its own law (see the two-argument methods above, which also perform the frame check). The result's uncertainty carries the map's Dst frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Transforms","text":"constraint(M, x...; kw...) -> g\n\nThe constraint value g(M; x...) alone — the <f> of the constraint_with_jacobians pair (conventions/jacobians.md): its first return, so a caller that keeps only g names the verb rather than discarding two Jacobians. A model may add a method that skips work the Jacobians need (the bearing TransferForm's g needs no backprojection Jacobians).\n\ng is ALGEBRAIC: it scales with the model's homogeneous scale and with the observations' units (a fundamental matrix scaled by 10⁶ scales g by 10⁶), so it is not a distance and a threshold on it means nothing in pixels. The geometric distance is sampson_distance; the noise-whitened one mahalanobis_distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_keywords-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Transforms","text":"constraint_keywords(M, xs) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe attribute columns of an observation set that the constraint of M takes as keywords, by keyword name, read per element by the batched Sampson functions: (; M₁ = xs.M₁, M₂ = xs.M₂) — the back-projection Jacobians — for the bearing forms of an EssentialMat and a TransferForm (xs of 3-vectors), none otherwise. A missing column is an ArgumentError naming it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_keywords"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_rounding_scale-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Transforms","text":"constraint_rounding_scale(M, x...; kw...) -> NamedTuple{(:gradient, :value)} | nothing\n\nThe rounding scales of the constraint g(M; x...) at an observation — the one input the Sampson degeneracy rule needs beyond (g, G_x): gradient, below which the observation gradient ‖G_x‖ is rounding (DEGENERATE), and value, below which |gᵢ| is rounding (the constraint VANISHES; a scalar, or one per component). Relative to the model's and the points' own magnitudes, so a model's homogeneous scale never decides. sampson_residual_with_normal consumes it; the default, nothing, makes only an exactly zero gradient degenerate and an exactly zero g vanishing. The epipolar models return their entries' rounding bounds (|F|·|s|, |d|ᵀ·|F|·|s|), a homography its rows' term-by-term bound, a conic, an ellipse, a circle and a radial camera theirs. The default is RIGHT, not missing, where a gradient cannot vanish to rounding: a Line2D's is its unit normal, and a TransferForm's blows up (its transfer goes to infinity) rather than vanishing, its rank verdict being the relative Cholesky test. A RANSAC problem answers it per point, constraint_rounding_scale(problem, model, i) (default nothing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_rounding_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mahalanobis_distance-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Transforms","text":"mahalanobis_distance(M, x...; kw...) -> T\n\nThe first-order Mahalanobis distance of the observations x under the model M: the norm of whiten(sampson_residual_with_normal(M, x...)..., Σ), Σ the observations' own covariance per the observation-noise rule — a plain point unit isotropic (so a plain correspondence's Mahalanobis distance IS its sampson_distance), an Uncertain one its covariance or precision, an exact one zero (not every observation of a constraint: ArgumentError). In noise units: d² ~ χ²(dg). A correspondence may be given as a Pair; keywords are forwarded to the constraint.\n\nGTSAM's noiseModel::Gaussian::mahalanobisDistance is the same (not squared); its squaredMahalanobisDistance is the square.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mahalanobis_distance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.observation_gradient_jacobian","page":"Transforms","text":"observation_gradient_jacobian(M, x...; kw...) -> SMatrix{dg·nx, nθ}\n\nThe derivative of a constraint's observation gradient G_x (constraint_with_jacobians's second return, dg × nx) along the model coordinates of its G_θ: ∂vec(G_x)/∂θ (column-major vec; nx × nθ for a scalar constraint), the mixed second derivative ∂²g/∂x∂θ. It is what the Sampson residual's model Jacobian adds to G_θ: the normalizer L (LLᵀ = G_x·G_xᵀ) moves with the model (sampson_residual_with_jacobian). A model implements it beside its constraint — FundMat and EssentialMat on image-plane points and EssentialMat on bearings (with M₁, M₂), an EpipolarForm (its matrix's), HomographyMat, a TransferForm, Circle, RadialCamera, Conic, Ellipse. GTSAM and Ceres have no counterpart: they differentiate an explicit residual, not an implicit constraint's gradient; it is the term the FNS-type exact Sampson Jacobians (Chojnacki et al.) carry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.observation_gradient_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_distance-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Transforms","text":"sampson_distance(M, x...; kw...) -> T\n\nThe Sampson distance of the observations x under the model M: the norm of its sampson_residual, the first-order geometric distance to the model's variety in the observations' own units — for the epipolar constraint u₂ᵀ F u₁ = 0 the familiar |u₂ᵀ F u₁| / sqrt((Fu₁)₁² + (Fu₁)₂² + (Fᵀu₂)₁² + (Fᵀu₂)₂²), for an ellipse the distance to its boundary to first order. Any model with a constraint_with_jacobians has one: FundMat, EssentialMat (pixel plane, or bearings with M₁, M₂), HomographyMat, an EpipolarForm, a TransferForm, a Conic, an Ellipse, a Circle, a Line2D. A correspondence may be given as a Pair.\n\nGeometric only: an observation's covariance does not enter. The noise-whitened (Mahalanobis) distance is mahalanobis_distance. Degenerate observations follow sampson_residual_with_normal's rule (0 or Inf).\n\nNOT SQUARED: this is ‖δ‖, the length of the first-order correction. Hartley & Zisserman's \"Sampson error\" and OpenCV's cv::sampsonDistance are its square, ‖δ‖² = gᵀ(G_x G_xᵀ)⁻¹g.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_distances!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"sampson_distances!(R::AbstractMatrix, models, xs) -> R\n\nThe length(xs) × length(models) matrix of sampson_distances, model j in column j, cast to R's element type at storage: a pool's residual matrix (Float32). models is iterated once and has a length; a model's constraint_keywords columns (M₁, M₂ for an essential matrix on bearings) are read from xs. The distance of each element through the one path — one KernelAbstractions kernel per model column on the backend that owns R, the CPU's included; a buffer of another size is a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_distances!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_jacobian_rows!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Any, Union{Tuple, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N, M}, T, 2} where {N, M, T}}, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"sampson_jacobian_rows!(J, M, directions, xs) -> J\n\nThe model rows of sampson_residuals!: element i's EXACT derivative along the chart directions (an nθ × N matrix, retract_jacobian, or the models dM = (∂M/∂θ₁, …) of retract_directions) — sampson_residual_with_jacobian's — in rows dg(i−1)+1 … dg·i. GEOMETRIC and UNWEIGHTED, as its residuals: neither a noise nor a sqrt_weights column is read. The whitened rows are whitened_jacobian_rows!, the √w-weighted ones weighted_whitened_jacobian_rows!. A degenerate element has zero rows. One body: one KernelAbstractions kernel on the backend that owns J, the CPU's included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_jacobian_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residual-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Transforms","text":"sampson_residual(M, x...; kw...) -> SVector{dg}\n\nThe Sampson residual of sampson_residual_with_normal alone: signed, smooth, one entry per constraint component — the residual an LM descends. only(sampson_residual(F, u₁, u₂)) is the signed epipolar Sampson distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residual"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residual_with_jacobian-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Transforms","text":"sampson_residual_with_jacobian(M, x...; directions = nothing, kw...)\n    -> (s_N::SVector{dg}, J::SMatrix{dg, N})\n\nThe Sampson residual (sampson_residual) and its EXACT derivative along the model: with g, G_x, G_θ from constraint_with_jacobians and ∂G_x/∂θ from observation_gradient_jacobian, s_N = L⁻¹g (LLᵀ = G_x·G_xᵀ) differentiates as\n\n∂s_N/∂θ_q = L⁻¹·∂g/∂θ_q − Φ(L⁻¹·∂P/∂θ_q·L⁻ᵀ)·s_N,   ∂P/∂θ_q = 2 sym(∂G_x/∂θ_q·G_xᵀ),\n\nΦ the lower triangle with its diagonal halved (the Cholesky factor's derivative) — for a scalar constraint (G_θ − (g/‖G_x‖²)·(∂G_x/∂θ)ᵀG_x)/‖G_x‖. The normalizer moves with the model and its derivative is carried, so a Gauss–Newton or LM step descends exactly the residual that is scored (a whitening frozen at an anchor descends a different function away from it). Carried along the chart directions (nθ × N, e.g. retract_jacobian(ch, a), or the models dM = (∂M/∂θ₁, …) of retract_directions; nothing keeps the model's own coordinates, N = nθ). A degenerate observation (the rule of sampson_residual_with_normal) has ZERO rows: a least-squares row cannot carry ±Inf. Keywords are forwarded to the constraint (M₁, M₂ for an EssentialMat on bearings). Element-type generic and device-safe; sampson_jacobian_rows! runs it over an observation set.\n\nCeres and GTSAM differentiate an explicit residual (CostFunction::Evaluate, a factor's unwhitenedError Jacobians); this is the Jacobian of the Sampson residual of an implicit constraint, as Chojnacki et al.'s FNS differentiate the Sampson cost, which neither library forms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residual_with_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residual_with_normal-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Transforms","text":"sampson_residual_with_normal(M, x...; kw...) -> (s_N::SVector{dg}, N::SMatrix{nx, dg})\n\nThe Sampson residual of the observations x under the model M and the orthonormal basis of the constraint's normal space it is expressed in: with g, G_x from constraint_with_jacobians(M, x...),\n\ns_N = L⁻¹g,   N = G_xᵀL⁻ᵀ,   LLᵀ = G_x·G_xᵀ,\n\nthe first-order displacement onto the model's variety (‖s_N‖ is the sampson_distance), and N the directions it is measured along (NᵀN = I; one column for a scalar constraint, dg for a vector one). Always an SVector{dg}, dg the constraint's component count (1 for F, E, a conic, an ellipse, a circle or a line; 2 for a homography). The noise layer, whiten(s_N, N, Σ), takes both.\n\nGEOMETRIC: a covariance attached to an observation (Uncertain, exact) is ignored here; the observation's point is used. A correspondence may be given as a Pair. Keywords are forwarded to the constraint (an EssentialMat on bearings takes its backprojection Jacobians M₁, M₂).\n\nDEGENERATE (the observation gradient vanishes to rounding, at the model's constraint_rounding_scale — both points at the epipoles, a point a homography sends to the line at infinity, an ellipse's centre): each component is 0 when the constraint vanishes with it and ±Inf (the sign of g) when it does not, and N is zero. NaN propagates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residual_with_normal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residuals!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"sampson_residuals!(r, M, xs) -> r\n\nThe Sampson residuals of the model M over the observation set xs (a PointSet or vector of points, a Correspondences or vector of Pairs): element i's sampson_residual in rows dg(i−1)+1 … dg·i of r. GEOMETRIC: a noise column is not read. UNWEIGHTED, like whitened_residuals!: a sqrt_weights column is not read either; the √w-weighted least-squares rows are weighted_whitened_residuals! (which, on a set without noise columns, are these rows times √wᵢ). The constraint's keyword columns (constraint_keywords: M₁, M₂ on bearings) are read per element. A degenerate element is ZERO (the row an LM can take; its distance is ±Inf under the rule). One body: one KernelAbstractions kernel on the backend that owns r, the CPU's included. Reductions compose: the residual sum of squares is sum(abs2, r). The noise- whitened rows are whitened_residuals!; the model rows sampson_jacobian_rows!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sampson_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_whitened_jacobian_rows!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Any, Union{Tuple, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N, M}, T, 2} where {N, M, T}}, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"weighted_whitened_jacobian_rows!(J, M, directions, xs) -> J\n\nThe model rows of weighted_whitened_residuals!: whitened_jacobian_rows! times √wᵢ from the set's sqrt_weights column — the chart LM's residual_jacobian_rows! of a Sampson-constraint problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_whitened_jacobian_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_whitened_residuals!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"weighted_whitened_residuals!(r, M, xs) -> r\n\nwhitened_residuals! times √wᵢ from the set's sqrt_weights column (absent: 1): the least-squares rows of a weighted fit — the chart LM's residual_rows! of a Sampson-constraint problem (conventions/jacobians.md: LM rows are √w-weighted, the RANSAC rows are not). Its model rows are weighted_whitened_jacobian_rows!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weighted_whitened_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whiten-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where {N, T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N, M}, T, 2} where {N, M, T}, LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling{Bool}}","page":"Transforms","text":"whiten(s_N, N, Σ) -> SVector{dg}\n\nThe noise layer: the Sampson residual s_N in the normal basis N (sampson_residual_with_normal) whitened by the observation covariance Σ (over the constraint's observations, nx × nx),\n\nr = L_N⁻¹s_N,   L_N L_Nᵀ = NᵀΣN,\n\nthe first-order Mahalanobis residual — equal to the constraint whitened by its propagated covariance, L⁻¹g with LLᵀ = G_x·Σ·G_xᵀ. Σ = I returns s_N unchanged; r² is χ²(dg) under Gaussian noise of covariance Σ.\n\nDEGENERATE: NᵀΣN losing rank to rounding relative to its diagonal (a observation exact along the normal) leaves the residual's zero components 0 and its others ±Inf; a degenerate Sampson residual (zero N) is returned unchanged. NaN propagates.\n\nThe least-squares rows of the whitened residual, exact (the factor differentiated with the model), are whitened_jacobian_rows!'s. GTSAM's noiseModel::Gaussian::whiten(v) is the same operation on an explicit residual (R·v, RᵀR = Σ⁻¹); here the residual is the Sampson residual of an implicit constraint and the noise is the observations' along its normal, so Σ enters through NᵀΣN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whiten"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_jacobian_rows!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Any, Union{Tuple, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N, M}, T, 2} where {N, M, T}}, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"whitened_jacobian_rows!(J, M, directions, xs) -> J\n\nThe model rows of whitened_residuals!: sampson_jacobian_rows! with the observations' noise columns, the Cholesky factor of G_x·Σᵢ·G_xᵀ differentiated with the model (exact, not frozen). Unweighted, as its residuals and as sampson_jacobian_rows!; weighted_whitened_jacobian_rows! is the √w form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_jacobian_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_residuals!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"whitened_residuals!(r, M, xs) -> r\n\nsampson_residuals! whitened by the observations' noise columns (Σ of a point set, Σ₁/Σ₂ of a correspondence set — conventions/ observation-noise.md): element i's constraint L⁻¹gᵢ, LLᵀ = G_x·Σᵢ·G_xᵀ, in σ units (‖rᵢ‖² ~ χ²(dg); an exact source with a plain target is the transfer error in the target's units). UNWEIGHTED: a sqrt_weights column is not read — the same currency as the problem-level whitened_residuals!(r, problem, model); the √w-weighted rows a least-squares solver takes are weighted_whitened_residuals!. Without noise columns it is sampson_residuals! (both unweighted). GTSAM's NoiseModelFactor::whitenedError is this per factor (and unwhitenedError the constraint); a degenerate element is zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exact-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}} where {N, T<:Real}","page":"Transforms","text":"exact(p::StaticVector) -> Uncertain\n\nThe observation p known EXACTLY: p with a ZERO covariance, the third case of the observation-noise rule (a plain point is unit isotropic noise, an Uncertain carries its own covariance). For data with no observation error in its frame — a calibration board's points in metres, a world point. Board → image correspondences built as exact.(board) .=> pixels score the transfer error in the image's pixels, whatever the board's units. A physical p (lengths) stays in metres, and its zero covariance is in m².\n\nA correspondence needs one noisy endpoint: an estimator refuses a correspondence whose two endpoints are both exact (nothing to whiten).\n\nThe zero covariance is an ordinary CovMat — no second representation. It is read once, where an estimator's constructor turns endpoints into its covariance columns (noise_columns); the per-point matrices cost 4 numbers each at that boundary and nothing in the inner loop, where the columns are what is read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exact"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.noise_columns-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Transforms","text":"noise_columns(sources, targets) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe covariance columns of a correspondence set whose endpoints may be Uncertain (or exact): (; Σ₁, Σ₂), each a vector of static 2×2 covariances in the endpoints' own frame, with a side whose endpoints are all plain LEFT OUT (an absent column is unit isotropic noise) — so a plain set gives (;) and its estimator runs exactly as without. Merge them into the geometry-only set with withattrs(cs; noise_columns(s, t)...); the endpoints themselves are geom of these.\n\nThrows ArgumentError for a correspondence whose two endpoints are both exact, or a covariance that is not finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.noise_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pair_covariance-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling, LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling}","page":"Transforms","text":"pair_covariance(Σ₁, Σ₂)\npair_covariance(cs, i)\n\nThe 4×4 observation covariance of a correspondence over (x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂): the block diagonal of its endpoints' 2×2 covariances, I (the UniformScaling) when both are unit isotropic — the shape whiten and whitening_factor take as Σ_x. With a correspondence set cs, the endpoints' covariances are its Σ₁/Σ₂ columns (noise_columns), an absent column unit isotropic; a plain set returns I for every i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pair_covariance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SO3","page":"Transforms","text":"SO3\n\nThe rotation group, as a type tag — the group a Lie-group helper is being asked about, in the slot a type argument takes (left_jacobian(SO3, ω)). Carries no data. Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SO3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exp_se3-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, <:Real}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, <:Real}}","page":"Transforms","text":"exp_se3(ω::SVector{3}, ρ::SVector{3}) -> EuclideanMap\n\nSE(3) exponential of the twist (ω, ρ): (exp_so3(ω), left_jacobian(SO3, ω)·ρ) as an EuclideanMap (constructed with check = false; the Rodrigues result is orthonormal to floating-point precision by construction).\n\nGTSAM's Pose3::Expmap (log_se3 is Pose3::Logmap), Sophus' SE3::exp, Manifolds.jl's exp_lie. Note the twist ORDER differs between libraries — ours is (ω, ρ), rotation first.\n\nGeneric over Real: the two parts are promoted to one float type, so ForwardDiff Duals flow through it (a pose retraction exp_se3(ω, ρ) ∘ T differentiated by an NLLS solver), as through exp_so3 and left_jacobian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exp_se3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exp_so3-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Real","page":"Transforms","text":"exp_so3(ω::SVector{3,T}) -> SMatrix{3,3,T,9}\n\nRodrigues exponential map so(3) → SO(3): R = I + a·[ω]× + b·[ω]×² with the second-order Taylor branch below the per-type small-angle threshold.\n\nPlain SMatrix arithmetic — safe inside Zygote-tracked code (unlike RotMatrix(RotationVec(ω...)), which reverse-mode AD cannot penetrate) and generic over Real, so ForwardDiff Duals flow through it too. See also skew, left_jacobian, exp_se3.\n\nGTSAM spells this Rot3::Expmap (and log_so3 is Rot3::Logmap), Sophus SO3::exp / SO3::log. Manifolds.jl's name is exp_lie(G, X) / log_lie: its exp(M, p, X) is the exponential at a BASE POINT, while this is the Lie-group exponential at the identity — which is why the group is not a first argument here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exp_so3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.left_jacobian-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SO3}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, T}}} where T<:Real","page":"Transforms","text":"left_jacobian(SO3, ω::SVector{3,T}) -> SMatrix{3,3,T,9}\n\nLeft Jacobian of SO(3): J = I + a·[ω]× + b·[ω]×² with a = (1−cosθ)/θ², b = (θ−sinθ)/θ³. Maps a rotation-vector perturbation to the translation part of the SE(3) exponential: t = J(ω)·ρ. Not provided by Rotations.jl. Below θ = 1/4, where the closed forms cancel, a and b are their six-term Taylor series (within 4 eps of the exact value); above it the closed forms.\n\nThis is Sophus' SO3::leftJacobian. GTSAM's Rot3::ExpmapDerivative is its TRANSPOSE, the RIGHT Jacobian Jr(ω) = Jl(ω)ᵀ = Jl(−ω), because GTSAM retracts by right multiplication — do not equate the two. Measured here (2026-09-22): the left perturbation identity exp(ω+δ) = exp(Jl(ω)·δ)·exp(ω) holds to 5.8e-13 while the right-multiplied one is off by 1.8e-6, and Jl(ω) = Jl(−ω)ᵀ to 0.0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.left_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.log_se3-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}","page":"Transforms","text":"log_se3(E::EuclideanMap) -> (ω, ρ)\n\nSE(3) logarithm of a rigid transform: ω = log_so3(R), ρ = left_jacobian(SO3, ω) \\ t. Inverse of exp_se3. Element type follows the map's rotation eltype. NOT AD-safe under Zygote (see log_so3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.log_se3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.log_so3-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3, T, 9}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Real","page":"Transforms","text":"log_so3(R::StaticMatrix{3,3,T}) -> SVector{3,T}\n\nRotation vector (axis · angle, angle ∈ [0, π]) of R — the SO(3) logarithm: the unit quaternion (w, v) of R by Shepperd's method (the largest of w, x, y, z computed from the diagonal, so every branch, the near-π one included, divides by a number ≥ ½), sign fixed to w ≥ 0, then 2·atan(‖v‖, w)·v/‖v‖ — with its Taylor series (2/w)(1 − ‖v‖²/(3w²))·v below the per-type small-angle threshold, so the derivative at the identity is exact. Generic over Real: ForwardDiff Duals flow through (within 2.5 ulp of Rotations' RotationVec, measured 2026-09-22).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.log_so3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reproject_so3-Union{Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Transforms","text":"reproject_so3(R::AbstractMatrix) -> SMatrix{3, 3}\n\nThe rotation a drifted rotation matrix R stands for, by its unit quaternion (Rotations' QuatRotation of it, back to a matrix), in R's element type: the ONE re-anchoring policy of every chart with an SO(3) part (recenter of the rigid and essential charts), and the one a caller applies to a rotation that walked through many retractions or was read from a file before building an EuclideanMap — whose rotation check refuses a matrix past √eps off orthonormal. On an orthonormal R it changes it by rounding only. Retractions do not re-project; only re-anchoring does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reproject_so3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.chordal_mean-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2} where T}}","page":"Transforms","text":"chordal_mean(Rs) -> RotMatrix{3, T}\n\nChordal L2 mean of a set of rotations: the rotation minimizing Σᵢ ‖R − Rᵢ‖²_F, i.e. Rotations.nearest_rotation of the elementwise sum. Suited to averaging rotations within a moderate spread (the chordal mean and the geodesic mean agree to second order); it is NOT a robust estimator.\n\nThrows ArgumentError on an empty collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.chordal_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.kabsch-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Pair{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:FlexUnits.Quantity, 1}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:FlexUnits.Quantity, 1}}}}","page":"Transforms","text":"kabsch(cs) -> RotMatrix{3, T}\n\nKabsch/Wahba: the rotation minimizing Σᵢ ‖bᵢ − R·aᵢ‖² over the vector pairs cs (aᵢ => bᵢ, StaticVector{3} each side; any sources/targets container, a Correspondences preferred): one closed-form step, Rotations.nearest_rotation of the cross-covariance Σ bᵢaᵢᵀ (an SVD with the determinant-sign correction). A weights attribute column (withattrs(cs; weights = w), data-arguments.md) weighs the pairs, Σᵢ wᵢ‖bᵢ − R·aᵢ‖² and the cross-covariance Σ wᵢbᵢaᵢᵀ (Wahba's problem): fixed, finite, non-negative weights, one per pair (an ArgumentError otherwise); a zero weight drops its pair. The vectors are used as given, NOT centred: centre them at the call site when aligning point clouds rather than directions.\n\nPhysical pairs (lengths on both sides) are stripped in metres at entry; the rotation is unit-free, so it comes back the same either way.\n\nThrows ArgumentError on fewer than 2 pairs, or fewer than 2 of positive weight (a single pair leaves a one-parameter family of solutions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.kabsch"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mirror-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Transforms","text":"mirror(pose, plane_point; normal = SVector(0., 0., 1.)) -> EuclideanMap\n\nThe second branch of the planar two-fold pose ambiguity: for a PLANAR target (world-plane normal normal, any point plane_point on it — conventionally the target center), the pose whose plane normal is reflected about the viewing ray to plane_point. Under weak perspective this mirror pose predicts image positions that agree with pose to second order, so the two tie on reprojection consensus; the rotation between them is twice the viewing tilt. Total: at a frontal view the reflection is (numerically) the identity and the returned pose coincides with pose — gate on rotation_angle(RotMatrix{3}(mirror.R' * pose.R)) if the degenerate case must be skipped.\n\nTranslation is carried over unchanged (same center ray and apparent size ⇒ same depth to first order); the result is a first-order INIT for the second basin, to be refined before any comparison.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mirror"},{"category":"method","location":"api/transforms/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spectral_rotation_averaging-Union{Tuple{AbstractDict{<:Tuple{K, K}}}, Tuple{K}} where K","page":"Transforms","text":"spectral_rotation_averaging(relative) -> Dict{K, RotMatrix{3, Float64}}\n\nSpectral chordal rotation averaging (Singer 2011; Arie-Nachimson et al. 2012): the absolute rotations of a set of frames from their relative rotations, relative an AbstractDict keyed by frame pairs (i, j) whose value R_ij takes frame i's directions into frame j's (f_j = R_ij·f_i; with d = R_k·f_k a direction in the common frame, R_i = R_j·R_ij). The 3n × 3n symmetric block matrix with blocks w_ij·R_ij at (j, i) and its transpose at (i, j), identity diagonal blocks, normalized by the frames' weighted degrees (D^{-1/2} A D^{-1/2}), has the stacked R_kᵀ in its three leading eigenvectors; each 3 × 3 block of them is projected onto SO(3) (Rotations.nearest_rotation, the eigenvectors' sign chosen so most blocks are proper), and the result is gauged so the first of the sorted frames is the identity (the common frame is that frame's). Symmetric in the frames, exact on consistent relative rotations, one eigendecomposition — the seed of a robust rotation averaging, not a robust estimator. A pair carries its non-negative weight as Attributed(R_ij, (; weight)), read with weight_of; a bare R_ij weighs 1. A zero-weight pair contributes nothing. The pairs of positive weight must connect every frame: the caller checks it on its graph (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's fit_rotation_averaging refuses a split one through its ViewGraph); on a split graph the leading eigenspace is degenerate and the rotations returned are meaningless (a chain with one zero-weight bridge came back 180° off).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spectral_rotation_averaging"},{"category":"section","location":"api/spline_lens/#Spline-Lens-API","page":"Spline Lens","text":"The grid cameras of Schöps et al. (CVPR 2020): a uniform cubic B-spline of rays over the image, central (a SplineLens of a CameraModel) or non-central (NoncentralSplineModel), and the lattice they share. Split from the Cameras API page. See the Lenses guide.","title":"Spline Lens API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/spline_lens/#The-central-grid-as-a-lens","page":"Spline Lens","text":"The central grid camera as an ordinary CameraModel(K, SplineLens(…)): a backward lens on the K-normalized image plane, K a gauge whose principal point is the pixel imaging ẑ. Its optional forward spline maps the equidistant chart about ẑ to the image plane. The spline lattice's shared helpers (the grid size, the quasi-interpolant, the window of a block of controls, the chart a control moves in) live beside it.","title":"The central grid as a lens"},{"category":"section","location":"api/spline_lens/#The-non-central-grid-on-the-lens's-directions","page":"Spline Lens","text":"NoncentralSplineModel: the central grid's directions (a SplineLens's backward spline) and a point spline over the same rectangle of the normalized plane, one observation line per pixel.","title":"The non-central grid on the lens's directions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/spline_lens/#The-lattice:-a-spline-on-a-chart","page":"Spline Lens","text":"The lattice of both, as a primitive: ChartSpline, a uniform cubic B-spline over a rectangle of a 2-D chart, its window and span weights, and the equidistant direction chart a forward spline is laid on.","title":"The lattice: a spline on a chart"},{"category":"section","location":"api/spline_lens/#Index","page":"Spline Lens","text":"Pages = [\"spline_lens.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{Type{SplineLens}, AbstractMatrix{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}, Size2}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"CameraModel(SplineLens, controls::AbstractMatrix{<:StaticVector{3}}, size; calib_area,\n            intrinsics = nothing, present = nothing, unit_controls = false)\n    -> CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}\n\nTHE constructor of a SplineLens camera (the source constructor and spline_lattice build through it): the camera of a pixel lattice given by its direction controls, as the reference implementation (Schöps et al.'s intrinsics0.yaml) and the stored grid records state one: controls ([gy, gx]; an absent node's control is never read, not even by the gauge) laid over the calibrated pixel area calib_area (a Rect of whole pixels, the closed rectangle of pixel corners [min, max + 1]; default Rect(size), the whole image) of an image of size, the nodes present held (nothing: every node). Its K is intrinsics (skew 0, positive focal lengths, else an ArgumentError) or, without one, the lattice's gauge. Its backward area is K⁻¹ of the calibrated rectangle, so each pixel's lattice coordinate is the reference's and the camera backprojects as the pixel grid does, whatever the K. The controls are kept as given (unit_controls = true normalizes each one unless it is unit to 1e-12, the reference reader's rule).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{Type{SplineLens}, Union{Function, CameraModel}, Size2}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"CameraModel(SplineLens, source, size; grid_size, calib_area, init = :interpolate, sampling_margin = 0.97)\n    -> CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}\n\nThe SplineLens camera initialized from source — a CameraModel (its backprojection) or a function pixel -> direction (nothing or a non-finite vector where it images nothing) — over an image of size (a Size2): a lattice of grid_size = (columns, rows) controls (default default_spline_grid) over the calibrated pixel area calib_area (a Rect of whole pixels, the closed rectangle its pixels cover; default Rect(size), the whole image).\n\ninit = :interpolate (default) solves the cubic-prefilter tridiagonal system per axis so the spline INTERPOLATES the source's backprojection at the grid nodes, sampling a camera inside sampling_margin times its lens's valid domain. init = :quasi_interpolate is the 4th-order local quasi-interpolant on a lattice extended one node past the area (quasi_interpolant), with its border rule for a source whose domain ends near or inside the area; a function source takes it. The prefilter's controls are re-normalized (each unit unless it is unit to 1e-12), the quasi-interpolant's kept as it forms them.\n\nA camera source keeps its own K (skew ≠ 0 is an ArgumentError); any other source takes the gauge of its sampled spline: the principal point is the pixel imaging ẑ (the calibrated area's centre when ẑ is not imaged inside it) and fx, fy the inverse local scale of d_xy/d_z there, skew 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ControlPatchManifold","page":"Spline Lens","text":"ControlPatchManifold(directions[, points]) <: Manifold\n\nA patch of a spline lattice's controls as a manifold (a SplineLens's, a NoncentralSplineModel's): a PX × PY block of its direction controls (an SMatrix of 3-vectors, in the caller's layout: the coordinates follow its column-major order) and, for a non-central model, the matching block of its point controls — the block a per-tile refinement moves (an NLLSsolver variable through the manifold adapter). A direction control d moves on its own sphere by the reference's chart,\n\n‖d‖ · normalize(d + δ₁t₁ + δ₂t₂),   (t₁, t₂) = direction_tangents(d / ‖d‖),\n\n2 coordinates that keep its norm (the norm is a weight of the spline's blend, not a gauge) and whose derivative at δ = 0 is (t₁, t₂). A point control moves additively, p + (Δx, Δy, Δz). The coordinates are per control, column-major over the block: (δ₁, δ₂) each, (δ₁, δ₂, Δx, Δy, Δz) with points — restrict them by mask to hold some fixed (a pupil-plane model's Δz). model(ch) is (; directions, points) (points is nothing for a central patch); retract_jacobian(ch) is block diagonal, (t₁ t₂) per direction and the identity per point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ControlPatchManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SplineLens","page":"Spline Lens","text":"SplineLens(backward::ChartSpline{3}; forward = nothing, present = nothing) <: AbstractLensModel\n\nThe central grid camera's lens (Schöps et al., CVPR 2020) as a backward lens of a CameraModel: the normalized image point x = K⁻¹u maps to the unit direction normalize(backward(x)), backward a uniform cubic B-spline of 3-vectors over the closed rectangle backward.area of the normalized plane (ChartSpline; controls [row = y, column = x], size − 3 cells per axis). backproject(lens, x) is NaN outside the area's closed box [origin, origin + widths] and where the point's 4 × 4 window holds an absent node; it never throws.\n\nforward (a ChartSpline{2} from the equidistant chart about ẑ to x, or nothing) is the optional fitted forward map. present says which nodes the lens holds: nothing (every node) or a Bool matrix of size(backward.controls) (true = present); an all-true mask is stored as nothing, an all-false one is an ArgumentError. An absent node's control is never read.\n\nA camera of this lens has skew 0 and positive focal lengths (an ArgumentError wherever one is built otherwise), and its K is a gauge: the principal point is the pixel imaging ẑ (CameraModel(SplineLens, source, size)). Where ẑ is not imaged inside the calibrated area, the gauge falls back to the area's centre, a pixel that does not image ẑ: the methods that read the principal point as the optical axis — undistort and undistort! onto the gauge pinhole, distortion_map, valid_rect, undistorted_rect, angle_of_view, horizon_radius and monotone_radius, image_circle(camera, sensor, d), view_overlap — then throw an ArgumentError naming the off-axis gauge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SplineLens"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.axis_gauged-Tuple{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, Rotations.Rotation{3}, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"axis_gauged(camera::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, rotation::Rotation{3}, K′) -> CameraModel\naxis_gauged(camera::Union{PosedCamera, BoundedCamera}, rotation, K′)\naxis_gauged(extrinsics::Union{EuclideanMap{3}, RotationMap{3}}, rotation::Rotation{3})\n\nThe same SplineLens camera in its camera frame turned by rotation (d′ = R·d), its K the skew-0 K′ (a 3 × 3 matrix or a CameraCalibrationMat). With R taking the lens's optical axis onto ẑ and K′ the radially symmetric camera fitted to it (its principal point the pixel imaging that axis, its focal lengths and aspect the fit's), the principal point images ẑ and ẑ is the optical axis.\n\nIt is an exact re-gauge, not a fit.\n\nEvery backward control is turned, c ↦ R·c (with_directions), so each pixel's ray is R times its old ray, to rounding (the normalization commutes with R).\nK becomes K′: its principal point and its focal lengths, so its aspect, are K′'s. This is CameraModel(camera, K′): any skew-0 K is an exact gauge of the lattice, so no ray changes.\nThe forward spline is dropped, since it inverts the old rays.\nThe presence mask is kept.\n\nThe poses turn with the frame.\n\nA world → camera map (extrinsics, X_c = R_p·X + t) becomes (R·R_p, R·t), its frame tags kept (the third form). Every projection is then kept.\nA posed camera turns its extrinsics with its model.\nA bounded camera keeps its image and lens coverage, and turns its camera as above: a posed one's model and extrinsics together.\n\nA spline that is never gauged this way keeps its source's ẑ: one read from a record, or one built by CameraModel(SplineLens, source, size).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.axis_gauged"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_prefilter_taps-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"cubic_bspline_prefilter_taps(K::Integer) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe one-sided taps h[0], …, h[K] of the cubic B-spline interpolation prefilter on an unbounded uniform grid: the inverse of the sampled B-spline (z + 4 + z⁻¹)/6 is √3·z₁^|k| with z₁ = √3 − 2 (Unser, Aldroubi & Eden 1991), truncated at |k| ≤ K and renormalised so h[0] + 2Σₖ h[k] = 1 (a constant is reproduced exactly). The controls c[j] = Σₖ h[|k|]·f[j + k] make the spline INTERPOLATE the samples f away from any border: the prefilter of _prefilter_matrix, the tridiagonal system of a finite lattice with its boundary rows, as a local filter a kernel can apply in a window (the dropped tail is 2√3·|z₁|^(K+1)/(1 − |z₁|) of a unit sample: 2.6e-6 at K = 10). VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' continuous DoG response reads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_prefilter_taps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.default_spline_grid-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"default_spline_grid(width, height) -> (gw, gh)\n\nThe control grid the reference lays over a width × height pixel area: one node every 64 px plus the three border nodes the cubic window needs (ceil(width / 64) + 3, likewise in y; at least 4). Apply it to the CALIBRATED AREA, not the frame, when the two differ — a circular fisheye's lit field is smaller than its sensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.default_spline_grid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.direction_tangents-Tuple{Any}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"direction_tangents(d) -> (t1, t2)\n\nThe reference's orthonormal tangent basis of the unit direction d — the 2-dof chart a grid control moves in (normalize(d + δ₁t1 + δ₂t2)), and the plane a direction residual is measured in. Generic in the element type: the basis is in d's type (a Float32 direction gives Float32 tangents, a ForwardDiff.Dual direction dual tangents), so a device kernel or a differentiated residual is never promoted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.direction_tangents"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.forward_chart-Tuple{Type{SplineLens}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"forward_chart(SplineLens) -> chart\n\nThe chart of every SplineLens's forward spline: the equidistant chart about ẑ (equidistant_chart(ẑ)), fixed in the camera frame, so no K moves it (a re-gauge maps the forward's output by the normalized plane's affinity and keeps every projection exactly). THE one derivation: project evaluates a forward on it and a fit of a forward is made on it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.forward_chart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.has_spline_gauge-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}, Size2}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"has_spline_gauge(controls::AbstractMatrix{<:StaticVector{3}}, size; calib_area, present = nothing,\n                 unit_controls = false) -> Bool\n\nWhether the pixel lattice of CameraModel(SplineLens, controls, size; calib_area, present, unit_controls) gives its own gauge K: whether its 4 × 4 window at the gauge's pixel (the pixel imaging ẑ, else the calibrated area's centre) holds only present nodes. Where it does not, that constructor refuses to build without a supplied intrinsics; a reader that can supply one asks this first. The lattice is the constructor's own (_spline_lattice: the controls normalized as it does, NaN in the absent slots), so the two agree whatever the absent slots hold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.has_spline_gauge"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.quasi_interpolant-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"quasi_interpolant(samples::AbstractMatrix) -> controls\n\nThe separable 4th-order quasi-interpolant of uniform cubic B-splines: node samples f (a matrix of values with + and scalar *, laid out [gy, gx] as a spline lattice's controls) become controls c_j = (−f_{j−1} + 8 f_j − f_{j+1}) / 6, along x (the second index) first, then along y; at the border the outermost sample is replicated. Exact for cubics at interior nodes, local (each control reads three samples per axis), no linear solve. The stencil of init = :quasi_interpolate, where the samples extend one node past the grid on every side so that no grid control reads a replicated sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.quasi_interpolant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_lattice-Tuple{Type{SplineLens}, CameraModel, Size2}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"spline_lattice(SplineLens, source::CameraModel, size; grid_size, calib_area) -> CameraModel\n\nThe layout a SplineLens camera built from the camera source over an image of size (a Size2) takes — the same grid_size, calib_area and K (the source's own), with the same defaults, as CameraModel(SplineLens, source, size) — WITHOUT sampling the source: every control is the optical axis (0, 0, 1). What a fit that solves for every control needs (the lattice, the gauge), with none of the node interpolation. A function source has no K without its sampled spline: build the camera.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_lattice"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_domain-Tuple{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"valid_domain(camera::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}) -> Rect2{Float64}\n\nWhere a SplineLens camera is defined, in pixels: its backward area mapped through K (the calibrated pixel area [min, max + 1]) when every node is present; under a presence mask, the bounding rectangle of the lattice cells whose 4 × 4 window holds present nodes only — where backproject can be finite. An ArgumentError when no window is fully present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.window-Tuple{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, AbstractUnitRange{<:Integer}, AbstractUnitRange{<:Integer}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"window(camera::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, rows, cols; directions = nothing) -> CameraModel\n\nThe block rows × cols (unit ranges into the lens's controls [gy, gx], at least 4 × 4) of a SplineLens camera as a SplineLens camera of its own: the same K, the block of the backward spline's controls (or directions, an equal-size matrix of 3-vectors, kept as given) over the rectangle of the normalized plane they span, and the block of the presence mask; no forward spline. It images a point exactly when the point's 4 × 4 window lies inside the block (and is present), and there it evaluates as the camera holding those controls, to the rounding of the lattice coordinate. It is what a per-tile cost block of a refinement evaluates: a few hundred controls instead of the whole lattice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.window"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_directions-Tuple{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"with_directions(camera::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, D::AbstractMatrix{<:StaticVector{3}}) -> CameraModel\n\nThe SplineLens camera with the backward direction controls D ([gy, gx], kept exactly as given, in the controls' element type): the same K, lattice and presence mask. The forward spline, which inverts the old directions, is dropped (project seeds without it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_directions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_present-Tuple{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"with_present(camera::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, present::AbstractMatrix{Bool}) -> CameraModel\n\nThe SplineLens camera holding the nodes present ([gy, gx], true = present): the same K, lattice and forward map, the controls of the present nodes kept and NaN in the absent ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_present"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, <:Real, 1}}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"backproject(camera::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, pixels::AbstractVector{<:StaticVector{2}};\n            device = default_device()) -> rays\n\nBatched backprojection on the KernelAbstractions backend device (default_device() by default): the scalar backproject of the lens at K⁻¹u per pixel, one kernel (the pixels uploaded once, the lens's device copy too, the lattice slopes formed once), the rays back in the pixels' array kind — bit for bit the scalar value on the CPU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}, AbstractVector}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"project(camera::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}, rays::AbstractVector{<:StaticVector{3}},\n        p₀s::AbstractVector; iters = 10, tol = 1.0e-6, device = default_device()) -> (; px, ok)\n\nBatched projection on the KernelAbstractions backend device (default_device() by default: an accelerator when one is functional, else KA.CPU()): the scalar project's solve per ray, warm-started at the pixel p₀s[i] (a non-finite seed: the scalar project's own start) and given at most iters iterations (a lens with a forward spline reads no seed). The rays and seeds are uploaded once (a device copy of the lens too), and both outputs come back in the rays' array kind (an Array gives Arrays, a device array stays on its device with no copy). Returns the pixels and the mask of the imaged ones (converged below tol onto a present window); a pixel not imaged is NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{SplineLens, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"project(lens::SplineLens, X::StaticVector{3}) -> SVector{2}\n\nThe normalized image point of the ray X (K maps it to the pixel), NaN where the lens does not image it. With a forward spline (lens.forward), its value at the ray's point of the equidistant chart about ẑ (fixed in the camera frame, so no choice of K moves it), NaN outside the forward's fitted chart (more than one knot cell past its area) or where the backward spline does not image the point. Without one, Levenberg–Marquardt on the backward spline's direction mismatch (100 iterations, converged below a 1e-6 residual) from the ray's pinhole point (X₁/X₃, X₂/X₃) where the lens images that point, else from the backward area's centre; NaN unless it converges onto a point whose window is present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.NoncentralSplineModel","page":"Spline Lens","text":"NoncentralSplineModel(K::LogicalIntrinsics, directions::ChartSpline{3}, points::ChartSpline{3};\n                      present = nothing) <: AbstractCamera\nNoncentralSplineModel(source, size; grid_size, calib_area, init, sampling_margin)\n\nThe non-central grid camera (Schöps et al., CVPR 2020): the pixel u observes the LINE through points(x) along normalize(directions(x)), x = K⁻¹u the normalized image point, both splines laid over the same rectangle of the normalized plane with the same lattice (ChartSpline; controls [row = y, column = x]). backproject returns a Ray3; project is Levenberg–Marquardt on the reference's tangent-plane point-to-line residual. With an all-zero point spline the model is exactly its central_model, a SplineLens camera.\n\nK is the gauge of a SplineLens camera (skew 0 and positive focal lengths, else an ArgumentError). present says which nodes the model holds, as a SplineLens's (nothing, or a Bool matrix of the grid's size; an all-true mask is nothing).\n\nThe points are plain metres in the camera frame. This is an exception to the units layering that the type forces: its controls are interpolated in the inner loop and cannot hold quantities, so the unit is stated here instead. A reader or writer of the model converts at its boundary. A PosedCamera of the model therefore takes physical extrinsics (EuclideanMap(R, t .* m), or with_unit(E, mm) of a map in mm) and projects scene points that are lengths; a plain extrinsics is an ArgumentError. ustrip(m, camera) is the plain camera, in metres only.\n\nThe model carries no image extent: a stored or rig camera holds it in its BoundedCamera.\n\nFrom a source (a camera or a function pixel -> direction): the directions of CameraModel(SplineLens, source, size; …) (the same keywords and K), the points all zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.NoncentralSplineModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Ray3","page":"Spline Lens","text":"Ray3(origin, direction)\n\nA 3D ray: origin + t·direction, direction unit by contract. The unprojection currency of NoncentralSplineModel — a non-central pixel observes a LINE in the camera frame, not a direction through a common center. Its numbers are any Real: Float64, Float32 in a kernel, a ForwardDiff Dual through a back-projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Ray3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject_with_jacobian-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"backproject_with_jacobian(m::NoncentralSplineModel, u) -> (f, M)\n\nThe unit DIRECTION f of the pixel u's observation line and its Jacobian M = ∂f/∂u (3×2): the central_model's. The line's point is not differentiated: backproject gives it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject_with_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.central_model-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"central_model(m::NoncentralSplineModel) -> CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics, <:SplineLens}\n\nThe central camera of a non-central one's directions — what a point at infinity sees: the same K, direction spline and present nodes as a SplineLens camera; the line points dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.central_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_domain-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"valid_domain(m::NoncentralSplineModel) -> Rect2{Float64}\n\nWhere the model is defined, in pixels: its central_model's valid domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.window-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel, AbstractUnitRange{<:Integer}, AbstractUnitRange{<:Integer}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"window(m::NoncentralSplineModel, rows, cols; directions = nothing, points = nothing) -> NoncentralSplineModel\n\nThe block rows × cols (unit ranges into the controls [gy, gx], at least 4 × 4) of the model as a model of its own, as window of a SplineLens camera: the same K, the block of both splines' controls (or directions, points, equal-size matrices of 3-vectors, kept as given) over the rectangle they span, and the block of the presence mask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.window"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_directions-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"with_directions(m::NoncentralSplineModel, D::AbstractMatrix{<:StaticVector{3}}) -> NoncentralSplineModel\n\nThe model m with the direction controls D ([gy, gx], kept exactly as given), every other part kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_directions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_points-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"with_points(m::NoncentralSplineModel, P::AbstractMatrix{<:StaticVector{3}}) -> NoncentralSplineModel\n\nThe model m with the line-point controls P ([gy, gx], plain metres in the camera frame, kept exactly as given), every other part kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_present-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel, AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"with_present(m::NoncentralSplineModel, present::AbstractMatrix{Bool}) -> NoncentralSplineModel\n\nThe model m holding the nodes present ([gy, gx], true = present): the same K and lattice, the controls of the present nodes kept and NaN in the absent ones, in both splines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_present"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"backproject(m::NoncentralSplineModel, u::StaticVector{2}) -> Ray3{T}\n\nThe observation LINE of the pixel u: the direction of the central_model (normalized) and the point spline's value (plain metres in the camera frame). A ray of NaN origin and direction where the direction lens does not image u (outside the area, or a window holding an absent node): the lens contract's form, never nothing.\n\nT is the promoted number type of the pixel and the model: Float64 for a Float64 model, Float32 for convert_eltype(Float32, m) at a Float32 pixel, a ForwardDiff Dual at a Dual pixel. It allocates nothing, never throws and has no branch of another type, so a KernelAbstractions kernel runs it on the model's device copy (Adapt.adapt(backend, m)) on every backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{NoncentralSplineModel, Any}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"project(m::NoncentralSplineModel, X) -> SVector{2}\nproject(m::NoncentralSplineModel, X, p₀) -> SVector{2}\n\nProject the camera-frame point X — plain metres, the point spline's unit — to a pixel: Levenberg–Marquardt in the normalized plane on the reference's tangent-plane point-to-line residual, so a converged pixel's observation line goes through X. The two-argument form starts at the point's pinhole point (X₁/X₃, X₂/X₃) where the model images that point, else at the area's centre; the three-argument form at the seed pixel p₀ (a good seed matters for a non-central model; positional, per-call data). NaN when the solve does not converge onto a point the model images (inside the area, its window present).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ChartSpline","page":"Spline Lens","text":"ChartSpline(area::Rect2, controls::AbstractMatrix{<:StaticVector{K}})\n\nA tensor-product uniform cubic B-spline from a 2-D chart to ℝᴷ: the controls laid over the closed rectangle area of the chart (controls[row, column], the row along the chart's second coordinate, size − 3 cells per axis, so the knot spacing along axis i is widths(area)[i] / (nᵢ − 3)). Evaluated with spline_value, continued past area by the outermost cell's cubic pieces.\n\nA CoordinateTransformations.Transformation: cs(c) is the value at the chart point c, transform_deriv(cs, c) its K × 2 Jacobian (the differentiated cubic pieces, no automatic differentiation), and cs(points) on a vector of chart points evaluates the batch with a KernelAbstractions kernel on the points' own backend (the controls are uploaded beside them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ChartSpline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_weight_derivatives-Tuple{Any}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"cubic_bspline_weight_derivatives(u) -> (NTuple{4}, NTuple{4})\n\nThe first and second derivatives in u of the four cubic_bspline_weights at the span's local coordinate u: ((−(1 − u)², 3u² − 4u, −3u² + 2u + 1, u²) / 2, (1 − u, 3u − 2, −3u + 1, u)), the same cubic pieces differentiated once and twice, in the same local coordinate and Horner form as the weights. Kernel-lawful, in u's own type (a Float32 coordinate gives Float32 derivatives). The spline lattices' partials (spline_weights, spline_window_sums) and VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' continuous DoG response read it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_weight_derivatives"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_weights-Tuple{Any}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"cubic_bspline_weights(u) -> NTuple{4}\n\nThe four weights of a uniform cubic B-spline span at its local coordinate u ∈ [0, 1): the value on the span is w₁c₁ + w₂c₂ + w₃c₃ + w₄c₄ over its four consecutive controls,\n\n((1 − u)³, 3u³ − 6u² + 4, −3u³ + 3u² + 3u + 1, u³) / 6,\n\na partition of unity. Outside [0, 1) the same cubics continue (extrapolate). Kernel-lawful: in u's own type (a Float32 coordinate gives Float32 weights, a ForwardDiff.Dual dual ones), no allocation and no branch, so a device kernel can call it. Evaluated in Horner form in u, which keeps Float32 within 1e-7 of the exact weights. The weights a spline lattice's windows (spline_window) and ChartSpline read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.cubic_bspline_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.equidistant_chart-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"equidistant_chart(axis::StaticVector{3}) -> Lens(Equidistant()) ∘ LinearMap(R)\n\nThe equidistant direction chart about axis (any nonzero 3-vector, normalized here): a ray at angle θ from the axis lands at radius θ (radians) in the chart plane, in the direction of its azimuth; singular only at −axis. R is the rotation whose rows are (e₁, e₂, axis), with e₁ = normalize(ŷ × axis) (x̂ × axis when the axis is within 1e-6 of ±ŷ) and e₂ = axis × e₁ — right-handed, so the chart is the camera's own equidistant lens when axis = ẑ. The chart a SplineLens's forward spline is laid on (about ẑ), and the one a direction field over a cap of the sphere (a panorama's vignetting) reads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.equidistant_chart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_value-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, HyperRectangle{2}, Any}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"spline_value(controls, area::Rect2, point)\n\nThe uniform cubic B-spline surface of controls (any matrix of values with + and scalar *: scalars, 3-vectors) laid over the closed rectangle area ([row = y, column = x], cells = size − 3 per axis), at point, continued past the area as spline_window: a ChartSpline's value, for a lattice of any control type (a per-position aperture radius, a field over the image).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_value"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_weights-Tuple{ChartSpline, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, <:Real, 1}}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"spline_weights(cs::ChartSpline, x) -> (ix, iy, w, (∂w₁, ∂w₂))\n\nThe weights of the 16 controls a point x of the chart reads, and their partials: w[r, c] multiplies cs.controls[iy - 3 + r, ix - 3 + c] (r the second coordinate's tap, c the first's; sum(w .* window controls) is cs(x)), and ∂wᵢ = ∂w/∂xᵢ, all SMatrix{4, 4}. The window is the point's own (continued past the area, as spline_window). In the point's precision. For a SplineLens camera's backward spline, x is the normalized point K⁻¹u: the pixel partials are ∂w₁ / fx, ∂w₂ / fy (skew 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_window-Tuple{HyperRectangle{2}, Tuple{Integer, Integer}, Any}","page":"Spline Lens","text":"spline_window(area::Rect2, grid_size, point) -> (ix, iy, wx, wy)\n\nThe window point reads on the uniform cubic lattice of grid_size = (rows, cols) controls laid over the closed rectangle area (cells = size − 3 per axis): the controls [iy - 3 + r, ix - 3 + c], r, c ∈ 1:4, and the four weights per axis (wx[c] along x, wy[r] along y; w[r, c] = wy[r] * wx[c]). Outside the area the outermost cell's cubic pieces continue — the same continuation spline_value evaluates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_window"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spline_lens/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_window_sums-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{C, Vararg{Any, 5}}, Tuple{C, Vararg{Any, 6}}, Tuple{C, Vararg{Any, 7}}} where C","page":"Spline Lens","text":"spline_window_sums(control, zero, wx, wy, dwx, dwy[, ddwx, ddwy]) -> (S, S₁, S₂[, S₁₁, S₁₂, S₂₂])\n\nTHE 4 × 4 tensor-product sweep of a uniform cubic B-spline window: the value S = Σᵣ Σ_c wy[r]·wx[c]·control(r, c) and its partials in the window's two lattice coordinates (S₁ through dwx, the first coordinate, along c; S₂ through dwy, along r), and with the second-derivative weights ddwx, ddwy the second partials S₁₁, S₁₂, S₂₂. control(r, c) returns the window's control at row tap r and column tap c (1…4), zero the accumulators' zero (a number or a static vector); the weights are cubic_bspline_weights and cubic_bspline_weight_derivatives of the span's local coordinates. One sweep, rows outer and columns inner, in plain arithmetic in that one order, so every caller sums the same bits; kernel-lawful. The spline lattices' value and partials read it (the lattice → area slope applied by the caller), as does VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' continuous DoG response.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_window_sums"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Coordinate-Transforms","page":"Transforms","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore provides 2D transforms as maps, coordinate mappings, and image warping utilities.","title":"Coordinate Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Maps-Carry-the-Transform-Class","page":"Transforms","text":"A transform is a MAP, and its type is its class: Euclidean ⊂ similarity ⊂ affine ⊂ projective. Each map acts on a primitive exactly as its class allows, and composes and inverts within it.\n\nclass maps Circle → Ellipse → feature frames\nEuclidean EuclideanMap{2}, Translation Circle Ellipse same type\nsimilarity SimilarityMap{2}, LinearMap of a rotation or a UniformScaling Circle Ellipse same type\naffine AffineMap, any other LinearMap HomEllipseMat HomEllipseMat —\nprojective HomographyMap Conic Conic MethodError\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\nusing CoordinateTransformations: Translation, LinearMap, AffineMap\nusing LinearAlgebra: Diagonal, I\nusing Rotations: RotMatrix\nusing StaticArrays: SVector\n\nE = EuclideanMap(RotMatrix{2}(π/4), SVector(10.0, 20.0))    # rotation + translation\nS = SimilarityMap(2.0, RotMatrix{2}(π/4), SVector(10.0, 20.0))  # x ↦ s·R·x + t\nL = LinearMap(Diagonal(SVector(2.0, 1.5)))                  # 2× in x, 1.5× in y\n\ncircle = Circle(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 10.0)\nS(circle)          # a Circle: centre mapped, radius 20\nL(circle)          # a HomEllipseMat (an affine image of a circle is an ellipse)\nS ∘ E              # a SimilarityMap, from the parts (s, R, t) — exact\ninv(S)             # a SimilarityMap: (1/s, Rᵀ, −Rᵀt/s)","title":"Maps Carry the Transform Class"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Homographies","page":"Transforms","text":"HomographyMap is projective in meaning: it maps a circle or an ellipse to a Conic, whatever its matrix (possibly a hyperbola: the circle may cross the line at infinity). Any structured map lifts to one — HomographyMap(S) stores to_homogeneous(S) and, as its inverse, to_homogeneous(inv(S)), never a 3×3 inversion — and a mixed composition (E ∘ H, H ∘ AffineMap(…)) is a HomographyMap. To keep an ellipse an ellipse under an affinity, apply the affine map itself rather than its lift.\n\nH_proj = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\nQ = H_proj(circle)                                     # a Conic\nellipse = classify_conic(Q) isa EllipseTrait ? Ellipse(Q) : nothing\n\np = Point2(100.0, 150.0)\np_mapped = (H_proj ∘ E)(p)                             # homogeneous Point3","title":"Homographies"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Coordinate-Representations","page":"Transforms","text":"","title":"Coordinate Representations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Homogeneous-Affine-Euclidean","page":"Transforms","text":"to_homogeneous(map) is a plain SMatrix ([A t; 0 1], the last row the constant 0 … 0 1) of every map: for algebra and interop. It carries no class — the map does — so geometric code takes the map and converts inside.\n\nmat_hom = to_homogeneous(E)          # 3×3 SMatrix\np_hom = to_affine(p)                 # [x, y, 1]\np_transformed = to_euclidean(mat_hom * p_hom)","title":"Homogeneous ↔ Affine ↔ Euclidean"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Coordinate-Mappings","page":"Transforms","text":"Map between geometric regions via constructors on AffineMap. Each primitive's AffineMap is the map from canonical [-1, 1]² to that primitive's frame; compose / invert as needed.","title":"Coordinate Mappings"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Rectangle-to-Rectangle","page":"Transforms","text":"# Source and target rectangles\nsource = Rect(Point2(0.0, 0.0),  Vec2(100.0, 50.0))\ntarget = Rect(Point2(10.0, 20.0), Vec2(200.0, 100.0))\n\n# Compose source → canonical → target\nmap = AffineMap(target) ∘ inv(AffineMap(source))\n\n# Apply to point\np_source = Point2(50.0, 25.0)   # Center of source\np_target = map(p_source)         # Center of target","title":"Rectangle to Rectangle"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Circle/Ellipse-to-Canonical-Square","page":"Transforms","text":"# Map circle to canonical square [-1, 1]² is the inverse of canonical → circle\ncircle = Circle(Point2(100.0, 150.0), 25.0)\nmap = inv(AffineMap(circle))\n\n# Point on circle boundary → point on square boundary\np_circle = Point2(125.0, 150.0)  # Right edge of circle\np_canonical = map(p_circle)","title":"Circle/Ellipse to Canonical Square"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Image-Warping-API","page":"Transforms","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore exposes a single adapter image_warp that wraps a math-space Transformation into a BoundedMap callable from Julia (row, col) array indices.  VGC math is always COLMAP convention (matching camera-intrinsics math); image_warp bakes the fixed COLMAP↔Julia bridges + row/col swap via composition.  No kwargs.\n\nThe user composes the math chain themselves with the two-step pattern: build the math transform → wrap with image_warp.\n\ncanonical_map(geom, n; bound, align_corners) — Cartesian-patch math.\nlogpolar_map(geom, n, inner_radius; align_corners) — log-polar math.\nimage_warp(math, n) — final wrap; no kwargs.\n\nFor advanced use (non-square or custom patch-domain), both math builders also accept an explicit HyperRectangle{2} instead of an integer size, and image_warp accepts a Rect{2} for the output region.","title":"Image Warping API"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Basic-Image-Warping","page":"Transforms","text":"image_warp!(dst, src, bm; fillvalue) resamples src into dst through the warp: for each (row, col) of dst it bilinearly samples src at bm((row, col)), running one KernelAbstractions kernel on the backend that owns dst (supply CuArrays for GPU execution).\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\n# img :: Matrix{Float32}\n\n# Create a warp from geometry\nmath = canonical_map(ellipse, 256; bound=1.0)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 256)\n\n# Resample into a pre-allocated patch\npatch = Matrix{Float32}(undef, 256, 256)\nimage_warp!(patch, bm, img)","title":"Basic Image Warping"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Reusing-a-warp","page":"Transforms","text":"The returned BoundedMap is a plain value — keep it and apply it to many images:\n\nmath = canonical_map(ellipse, 256; bound=1.0)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 256)\nimage_warp!(patch1, bm, img1)\nimage_warp!(patch2, bm, img2)\n\nimage_warp_batch_to! samples one image through a vector of warps into a 3D stack. A multi-plane image whose planes are sampled at different points (a Bayer image's R, G and B) is warped plane by plane, each through its own warp: image_warp!(dst, warps, src) with dst and src H × W × P and warps a tuple of P warps, each composing its plane's frame map, image_warp(T, inv(plane_map) ∘ math, rect).","title":"Reusing a warp"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Fillvalue-Conventions","page":"Transforms","text":"Supply fillvalue (the out-of-bounds tap value, in the float accumulation domain) as a keyword argument:\n\nimage_warp!(patch, bm, img; fillvalue=1.0f0)  # white out of bounds","title":"Fillvalue Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Patch-Extraction","page":"Transforms","text":"","title":"Patch Extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Canonical-Patches","page":"Transforms","text":"Extract patches in canonical (normalized) coordinates:\n\n# From an ellipse - maps ellipse interior to square patch\nellipse = Ellipse(Point2(100.0, 150.0), 30.0, 20.0, π/4)\nmath = canonical_map(ellipse, 64; bound=1.0)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 64)\npatch = image_warp!(Matrix{Float32}(undef, 64, 64), bm, img)\n\n# From geometric primitives\nmath = canonical_map(circle, 64; bound=1.0)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 64)","title":"Canonical Patches"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Feature-Frame-Patches","page":"Transforms","text":"Extract patches from feature frames (Disc, EllipticFrame, AffineFrame). The math builders take a Circle or Ellipse, so convert the frame first:\n\n# From a Disc (isotropic feature)\ndisc = Disc(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 5.0)\nmath = canonical_map(Circle(disc), 31; bound=1.0)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 31)\npatch = image_warp!(Matrix{Float32}(undef, 31, 31), bm, img)\n\n# From an EllipticFrame (anisotropic feature)\nframe = EllipticFrame(center, S)  # S is the shape factor (shape matrix S²)\nmath = canonical_map(Ellipse(frame), 31; bound=1.0)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 31)","title":"Feature Frame Patches"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Log-Polar-Patches","page":"Transforms","text":"Extract rotation-invariant patches using log-polar sampling:\n\nlp_patch = Matrix{Float32}(undef, 256, 256)\n\n# Log-polar patch from a disc\nmath = logpolar_map(Circle(disc), 256, 0.01)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 256)\nimage_warp!(lp_patch, bm, img)\n\n# Custom inner radius (avoids singularity at center)\nmath = logpolar_map(Circle(disc), 256, 0.05)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 256)\nimage_warp!(lp_patch, bm, img)\n\n# From ellipse\nmath = logpolar_map(ellipse, 256, 0.01)\nbm    = image_warp(math, 256)\nimage_warp!(lp_patch, bm, img)\n\nLog-polar sampling maps:\n\nx-axis (columns) → radius (logarithmically scaled)\ny-axis (rows) → angle (0 to 2π)\n\nThis makes the patch invariant to rotation (becomes translation in y) and scale (becomes translation in x).","title":"Log-Polar Patches"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Math-side-patch-composition","page":"Transforms","text":"When you need to compose further math on the bare patch transform (e.g., detector-side compositions that chain patch → input → level), bypass the image-warp builders and use the math builders directly:\n\n# Bare math: patch coords → input image coords\npatch_to_input  = canonical_map(geometry, patch_size; bound=1.0, align_corners=true)\n\n# Compose with additional math\nscale = 1.0 / step\ninput_to_level  = AffineMap(SMatrix{2,2}(scale, 0.0, 0.0, scale),\n                            SVector(1.0 - scale, 1.0 - scale))\npatch_to_level  = input_to_level ∘ patch_to_input    # still bare math\n\n# Bake conventions at the boundary when you're ready to warp:\nbm = image_warp(patch_to_level, patch_size)\npatch = image_warp!(Matrix{Float32}(undef, patch_size, patch_size), bm, level_img)","title":"Math-side patch composition"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#HomographyMap-and-Perspective-Division","page":"Transforms","text":"When the inner transform is a HomographyMap (whose output is in homogeneous coordinates), image_warp automatically composes a PerspectiveMap into the chain so the warp sees inhomogeneous (x, y) output ready for sampling:\n\n# HomographyMap output needs perspective division (handled internally)\nH  = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(matrix))\nbm = image_warp(H, 256)\npatch = image_warp!(Matrix{Float32}(undef, 256, 256), bm, img)","title":"HomographyMap and Perspective Division"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Basis-Vectors","page":"Transforms","text":"Canonical basis for coordinate systems:\n\n# 2D canonical basis\ne1, e2 = canonical_basis(SVector{2,Float64})  # ([1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0])\n\n# A single basis vector (internal, not exported)\ne1 = VisualGeometryToolkitCore.basis_vector(SVector{2,Float64}, 1)  # [1.0, 0.0]","title":"Basis Vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Camera-Homographies","page":"Transforms","text":"Project between world plane and image:\n\nusing Rotations\n\n# Create camera\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, EuclideanMap(R, t))\n\n# Homography from world plane (z=0) to image\nH_mat = HomographyMat(camera)\nH = HomographyMap(H_mat)  # Wrap for callable syntax\n\n# Map world point to image\np_world = Point2(100.0, 50.0)  # On z=0 plane\np_image = H(p_world)\n\n# Transform circles (a Conic under a general homography)\ncircle_world = Circle(Point2(100.0, 50.0), 25.0)\nQ_image = H(circle_world)  # a Conic; Ellipse(Q_image) once classify_conic(Q_image) isa EllipseTrait","title":"Camera Homographies"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Full-Image-Warping-Pipeline","page":"Transforms","text":"# Create camera viewing a planar surface\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, pose)\n\n# Get homography and wrap in HomographyMap\nH = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\n\n# Get output size from sensor\nimage_rect = Rect(sensor)\nw_size = widths(image_rect)\n\n# Create warp (handles coordinate conventions and perspective division)\nbm = image_warp(inv(H), image_rect)\n\n# Resample the board image (a Matrix{Float32}) into the sensor frame\nwarped_img = Matrix{Float32}(undef, Int(w_size[2]), Int(w_size[1]))  # (height, width)\nimage_warp!(warped_img, bm, board_image; fillvalue=1.0f0)","title":"Full Image Warping Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#Units-in-a-Pose","page":"Transforms","text":"A physical EuclideanMap, such as a camera pose, a target pose or a rig member, holds its translation as lengths in metres (UnitfulEuclideanMap). An inner loop works on a plain map, whose translation is plain numbers (PlainEuclideanMap). The two forms meet only at an estimator's entry and exit, the same place where it normalizes its data (see the units layering on the Conventions page).\n\nusing StaticArrays, Rotations\nimport VisualGeometryToolkitCore as VgtkCore\nX = EuclideanMap(one(RotMatrix{3, Float64}), SVector(0.1, 0.0, 1.5) .* m)  # a pose, in metres\nVgtkCore.has_units(X)         # true\nXp = ustrip(mm, X)            # the plain map, translation in millimetres\nXm = EuclideanMap(Xp.R, Xp.t .* mm)   # a unit attached by construction\nX(SVector(0.0, 0.0, 1.0) .* m)                           # a unitful map acts on lengths\n\nStrip the whole map with ustrip(u, X) rather than rebuilding it from a stripped translation. A unitful map that meets plain numbers (applied to a plain point, or composed with a plain map) raises FlexUnits' DimensionError instead of promoting. Where a plain-only object meets a unitful map — a homography (HomographyMap(E), E ∘ H, E ∘ a model matrix, to_homogeneous(E)), a SimilarityMap (SimilarityMap(E), E ∘ S) or a planar primitive's action (E(circle)) — the error is an ArgumentError naming the fix, ustrip(m, E).","title":"Units in a Pose"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/transforms/#See-Also","page":"Transforms","text":"Transforms API Reference\nCoordinate Frames - The Src/Dst frame tags carried by EuclideanMap and HomographyMap, and the checked composition rules\nBatched Maps - The structure-of-arrays transform protocol\nPrimitives Guide - Transforming geometric shapes\nCameras Guide - Camera-based transformations","title":"See Also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/raw/#Camera-RAW-API","page":"Camera RAW","text":"The sensor image: a decoded Bayer RAW as RawMosaic (an AbstractMatrix{UInt16} of its photosites that knows its colour phase, its levels and where it sits on the sensor), its photosites channel-first as a method of ImageCore's channelview (a view, on the mosaic's device), and LinearRaw, its exact linear values on the 2×2 cells, with the maps of every plane and channel onto the sensor. Decoding a file is VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' (load_raw_mosaic, LibRaw), and so is the display demosaic. A cell mode's frame map is also read without an image, raw_frame_map(LinearRaw{:green_cells}), and its provenance record, raw_provenance(LinearRaw{:green_cells}), for a frame with no RAW behind it. See the Raw images guide.","title":"Camera RAW API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/raw/#The-mosaic-and-its-photosite-views","page":"Camera RAW","text":"","title":"The mosaic and its photosite views"},{"category":"section","location":"api/raw/#Linear-values-on-the-22-cells","page":"Camera RAW","text":"","title":"Linear values on the 2×2 cells"},{"category":"section","location":"api/raw/#The-sensor-model:-the-black-level,-the-noise-and-the-defects","page":"Camera RAW","text":"Three levels: the geometric Sensor (part of a camera), a SensorCharacterisation (ONE body's components per ISO, held beside the camera like CaptureSettings) and a SensorModel (ONE frame's black, noise and defect components). Each slot's component is a type, absence included (FileBlack, NoNoise, NoDefects), and each present one states its source (SensorSource: measured, estimated, specified, file). The resolver sensor_model is a lookup: the frame's body and ISO pick an entry, and with none the absent model SensorModel() linearises bit for bit as the RAW alone. LinearRaw(mosaic; mode, sensor_model) is THE one black subtraction; under a PoissonGaussian or RelativeNoise its image carries each value's variance. The estimators build a characterisation's components from frames, offline: photon_transfer, master_dark, defect_onsets.","title":"The sensor model: the black level, the noise and the defects"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BayerCellFrame","page":"Camera RAW","text":"BayerCellFrame{C} <: Frame{2}\n\nThe COLMAP pixel frame of one colour plane C of the half-resolution grid of 2×2 Bayer cells: pixel (row, column) of the plane is centred at (column − ½, row − ½) of its frame. The planes are indexed alike but sampled at different points of the cell, so each is a frame of its own:\n\n:R, :B — a cell's red and blue photosite (a plane of a :bin2 LinearRaw, a channel of a mosaic's channelview);\n:G1, :G2 — a cell's two green photosites, in the cell's column-major order (the two green channels of channelview(m));\n:G — the mean of the two greens, which sits at the cell centre in every phase: the cell grid itself (the :green_cells image), whose map is raw_frame_map.\n\nbayer_plane_map maps each onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, and a composition that joins one plane's frame to another's is refused (MethodError), as at every frame junction of Core.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BayerCellFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearRawView","page":"Camera RAW","text":"LinearRawView{T, A, D, L} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nThe lazy linear view of a mosaic's photosites on the raw grid, linearview(m): element (i, j) is the linear value of the held array's code there, with its channel's black level and span, computed when read. Under a sensor model whose noise has a variance law (law: a PoissonGaussian or a RelativeNoise) the element is an Uncertain{Float64}, the value with its scalar variance computed on the same read (owner ruling D14: the default uncertain image is this lazy one; copy materialises it as a StructArray); otherwise T is Float64. It holds the photosites (parent, the mosaic's held array, a device array too), the four channels' levels and, from a sensor model's Dark, the offset map over the visible raw (dark, on the same device; nothing without one) read at the mosaic's window_origin; similar, a broadcast and copy are the held array's kind (a broadcast over a device view runs on the device), adapt moves it, and a KernelAbstractions kernel reads it as it is (its indexing is two parities, the one black rule and one linearisation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearRawView"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PhotositeColors","page":"Camera RAW","text":"PhotositeColors <: AbstractMatrix{UInt8}\n\nThe colour index of every photosite of a mosaic, lazily, as an isbits value: the size, the colour pattern (at most 4 × 4, in an NTuple) and its phase at the mosaic's first photosite. Indexing is the one phase rule; nothing is stored per photosite, so it is the same on the host and inside a GPU kernel or a fused broadcast with a device mosaic's arrays (it holds no array, and a device array in the same broadcast decides where it runs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PhotositeColors"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RawMosaic","page":"Camera RAW","text":"RawMosaic{A <: AbstractMatrix{UInt16}, M <: NamedTuple} <: AbstractMatrix{UInt16}\n\nA RAW file decoded and nothing more: the visible area of the sensor, as the decoder reports it (VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' load_raw_mosaic, LibRaw). It IS its photosite matrix: size, indexing and parent are the held array's, so an ordinary array or image function reads it unchanged, and what such a function makes (similar, copy, a broadcast, a reduction) is a plain array of the held array's kind — the colour pattern and the metadata stay with the mosaic. copy, ==, isequal and isapprox are the held array's own (no element read through the wrapper, so a device mosaic compares on the device), and equality is the photosite matrix's: two mosaics with the same photosites are == whatever their colour pattern, window origin or metadata (compare photosite_colors, window_origin and metadata for those), and a mosaic is == to its held array. It is read-only (no setindex!): write to parent(m). The held array may be a device array (adapt(CuArray, m), or adapt(backend, m) with a KernelAbstractions backend, moves the photosites and keeps the rest); a kernel receives parent(m), never the wrapper (nor, on a GPU, the nested view channelview(m): read its photosites by its index rule).\n\nA unit-range index m[rows, columns] (a copy) or view(m, rows, columns) (no copy) is a window: a mosaic of its own whose colours are the parent's at those photosites (the colour phase shifts with an odd start, as a circshift of the pattern would) and whose raw_frame_map is its offset into the visible raw. Any other index is a plain array.\n\nraw::A — the photosites, raw[row, column], in the sensor's orientation (no EXIF flip): pixel (row, column) is the raw's COLMAP pixel centred at (column − ½, row − ½) — the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, the frame every calibration of the raw is in (a window's pixels are offset by window_origin).\npattern::Matrix{UInt8} — the smallest repeating colour pattern, square, at most 4 × 4, from the full raw frame's origin (LibRaw's COLOR read from there), in LibRaw's colour indices: 0 R, 1 G, 2 B, 3 the second G of an RGBG pattern. Each photosite's index is photosite_colors(mosaic), the pattern tiled from that origin.\nblack::NTuple{4, Float64} — the black level per colour index (the common and per-channel parts summed, LibRaw's adjust_bl).\nwhite::Float64 — the white level (LibRaw's maximum): the code at which a photosite is saturated.\nvisible_origin::NTuple{2, Int} — (top_margin, left_margin), the visible area's offset into the full raw frame, which is raw_size ((height, width)).\nmetadata::NamedTuple — what the decoder read: the EXIF fields (VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures types them: exposure_settings, lens_settings, body_identity, lens_identity, capture_time), the daylight white balance, the camera's declared linear-response limit, the decoder and its library's version. Core reads none of it except in raw_provenance.\nwindow_origin::NTuple{2, Int} — (rows, columns), the offset of raw[1, 1] from the visible area's first photosite: (0, 0) as decoded (the keyword of the constructor), a window's start less one otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RawMosaic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SaturationView","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SaturationView{A} <: AbstractMatrix{Bool}\n\nThe lazy saturation flags of a mosaic's photosites on the raw grid, saturationview(m): element (i, j) is raw[i, j] ≥ white, read when indexed. The match of LinearRawView: the same grid, the same held array, and the same device behaviour (similar, a broadcast and copy are the held array's kind; adapt moves it; a kernel reads it as it is).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SaturationView"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#ImageCore.channelview-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"channelview(m::RawMosaic) -> AbstractArray{UInt16, 3}\n\nImageCore's channelview, for a Bayer mosaic: the photosites channel-first, 4 × H ÷ 2 × W ÷ 2, without a copy. The Bayer interpretation of \"channels\": each 2×2 cell is a pixel and its four photosites — spatially interleaved, each at its own position on the sensor — are its channels, so v[k, i, j] is photosite k of cell (i, j), the cell's photosites in column-major order (k = di + 2dj + 1 for photosite (2i − 1 + di, 2j − 1 + dj)): the mosaic's own phase order, whose colours are bayer_channels(m) (R, G1, G2, B for an RGGB cell; another phase permutes them, since reordering channels is not a view). It is built as ImageCore builds a view: a reshape of the photosite matrix (H × W → 2 × H/2 × 2 × W/2), its dimensions permuted (→ 2 × 2 × H/2 × W/2, a PermutedDimsArray) and reshaped (→ 4 × H/2 × W/2), views of parent(m): writing through it writes the mosaic's held array, a device mosaic's view is a view of its device array (KernelAbstractions' get_backend is the device's, adapt(Array, v) moves it whole), and a broadcast or a Cartesian copy over a whole mosaic's view runs on the device. An odd last row or column (no 2×2 cell) is left out, as in LinearRaw.\n\nIn a GPU kernel read the photosites by the view's index rule instead, parent(m)[2i − 1 + di, 2j − 1 + dj] (or the 4-D parent(v)[di + 1, dj + 1, i, j]): Base's ReshapedArray indexing compiles in a CUDA kernel only over a whole contiguous array, at about a third of the held matrix's bandwidth (509 against 1391 GB/s on a B200, 2848 × 4256), and not at all over a window or an odd-sized mosaic (an InvalidIRError); benchmark/raw_channelview.jl measures it, and LinearRaw's cell kernel reads the held matrix.\n\nA plain AbstractMatrix{UInt16} keeps ImageCore's own method (a gray image's channel view is the array itself): this method is the mosaic's, whose phase says which photosite is which. Channel k's pixel frame is BayerCellFrame{C} (C its colour), and bayer_plane_map(m, k) maps it onto the visible raw, exactly, for every Bayer phase and either parity of a window's origin. Its inverse is photositeview. The linear values channel-first are channelview(linearview(m)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImageCore.channelview"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bayer_channels-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"bayer_channels(m::RawMosaic) -> NTuple{4, Symbol}\n\nThe colour of each channel of channelview(m): :R, :G1, :G2, :B, in the channels' order, the cell's photosites column-major ((0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1) from the cell's top-left). (:R, :G1, :G2, :B) for an RGGB cell, (:G1, :B, :R, :G2) for GRBG; the greens are numbered in that order. The cell's phase is the mosaic's own at its first photosite, so a window with an odd start reads another phase (from the same pattern, the one phase rule of photosite_colors). A pattern that is not a 2×2 Bayer cell (one R, two G, one B) is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bayer_channels"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bayer_plane_map-Tuple{RawMosaic, Integer}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"bayer_plane_map(m::RawMosaic, k::Integer) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, BayerCellFrame{C}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nThe map of channel k of channelview(m) onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}: pixel (i, j) of the channel, at (j − ½, i − ½) of its frame BayerCellFrame{C} (C = bayer_channels(m)[k]), goes onto its photosite's centre, (2j − 1 + dj − ½, 2i − 1 + di − ½) plus a window's offset, exactly, for every Bayer phase and either parity of the visible or window origin. It is the cell grid's map x ↦ 2x after the photosite's translation from the cell centre ((±¼, ±¼) cell pixels): the rule the planes of a LinearRaw share, so the R channel's map IS the R plane's of the mosaic's :bin2 image. An isbits value, usable inside a kernel. A channel past the fourth is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bayer_plane_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cfa_string-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"cfa_string(mosaic::RawMosaic) -> String\n\nThe colour pattern as letters, row by row (\"RGGB\" for [0 1; 3 2]): LibRaw's indices read through \"RGBG\", as convert_raw.py records it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cfa_string"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.linearview-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"linearview(m::RawMosaic; sensor_model::SensorModel = SensorModel()) -> LinearRawView\n\nThe mosaic's linear values on the raw grid, lazily, as ImageCore's normedview reads codes as numbers without a copy: element (i, j) is (raw − black[c]) / (white − black[c]) of photosite (i, j), c its colour — the one linearisation, the one LinearRaw's cell kernel applies. Not clipped, no gain. It composes with ImageCore's channelview: channelview(linearview(m)) is the linear values channel-first (4 × H/2 × W/2, the channels of channelview(m)), so the green-cell value of cell (i, j) is the mean of its two green channels, bit for bit (LinearRaw(m; mode = :green_cells).image[i, j, 1], at gain 1). An odd last row or column is in the view (its photosites' colours by the same rule) and left out of the channel view.\n\nNot normedview(N0f16, …) itself, whose code / 65535 ignores the black levels, nor a MappedArrays.mappedarray of the codes, whose function sees the value alone: the black level and span depend on the photosite's position (its colour in the phase), so the view reads its index — one parity rule, the cell sites' (k = di + 2dj + 1).\n\nsensor_model, the frame's SensorModel, sets the levels as it does LinearRaw's, by the same resolution: its black slot's per-colour-index black (the span white − black[c]), and a Dark's offset in place of the black per photosite. Its defects are not values: they join LinearRaw's mask, not this view. Its noise is: under a PoissonGaussian or a RelativeNoise each element is an Uncertain{Float64}, the value and its scalar variance (Statistics.var(x), computed on the read by the law LinearRaw's kernel writes per plane, one site at gain 1: owner ruling D14, the default uncertain image of the photosites; the binned cell image, LinearRaw's, is materialised once by its kernel and never re-derived by a consumer), and copy of the view is the materialised form, a StructArray of a value plane and a variance plane (on the view's device). The layout is the raw grid, H × W; channelview of it is the 4 × H/2 × W/2 cells, uncertain alike. With the absent model, the default, the view is the mosaic's own levels, plain values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.linearview"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.photosite_colors-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"photosite_colors(mosaic::RawMosaic) -> PhotositeColors <: AbstractMatrix{UInt8}\n\nEach photosite's colour index (LibRaw's: 0 R, 1 G, 2 B, 3 the second G), the size of mosaic: the pattern tiled from the full raw frame's origin, so photosite (row, column) reads pattern[mod(top + row − 1, n) + 1, mod(left + column − 1, n) + 1] with (top, left) = visible_origin .+ window_origin. Lazy and isbits (PhotositeColors): no matrix is built, and it broadcasts with a device mosaic's arrays on the device, fused, so a photosite mask is one broadcast with no host round-trip:\n\ncol = photosite_colors(m)\nvalid = ((col .== 0x01) .| (col .== 0x03)) .& (parent(m) .!= 0) .& .!saturationview(m)\n\n(collect gives a Matrix{UInt8}.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.photosite_colors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.photositeview-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"photositeview(v::AbstractArray{T, 3}) -> AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nThe inverse of a mosaic's channelview, in the role ImageCore's colorview plays for an image's (a name of its own: colorview makes colorants of channels, and this makes the photosite matrix of cells): a channel-first array of 2×2 cells (4 × h × w, each column a cell's photosites in column-major order) as the 2h × 2w photosite matrix, without a copy. Of a mosaic's channelview it is the mosaic's (even-sized) held array again; of any other array (per-photosite values computed channel-first, say) it is a view in the mosaic's layout, photositeview(v)[2i − 1 + di, 2j − 1 + dj] == v[di + 2dj + 1, i, j]. A first dimension other than 4 is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.photositeview"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_frame_map-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"raw_frame_map(m::RawMosaic) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nThe map of the mosaic's pixels onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}: the identity as decoded, a window's offset (x ↦ x + (columns, rows) of its window_origin) for a window.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_frame_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.saturationview-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"saturationview(m::RawMosaic) -> SaturationView\n\nThe mosaic's saturation flags on the raw grid, lazily: true at a photosite whose code is at or above the white level, raw ≥ white with no margin — THE saturation rule, the one LinearRaw's flags apply per cell (a cell is flagged when any photosite its planes read is; under a sensor model holding a DefectList LinearRaw also masks those photosites). The Bool match of linearview(m): the same grid, and channelview of it is the flags channel-first. A mask of the usable photosites of one colour is a plain broadcast with photosite_colors:\n\ncol = photosite_colors(m)\nusable_green = ((col .== 0x01) .| (col .== 0x03)) .& .!saturationview(m)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.saturationview"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearRaw","page":"Camera RAW","text":"LinearRaw\n\nA RAW image as linear values: (raw − black[c]) / (white − black[c]) × gain (each photosite's linearview value), camera raw colour, white balance 1, 1.0 = the white level (before the gain). Not clipped: a value above 1 (a gain above 1) or below 0 (noise under the black level) is kept.\n\nimage — image[row, column, plane], on the mosaic's device (an Array on the host); a plane's frame is asked of the image with its index (bayer_plane_map(L, c)). Under a sensor model whose noise defines a variance (PoissonGaussian, RelativeNoise) it is a StructArray of Uncertain{Float64} values (owner ruling D11): its value component the Float64 values, its attributes component each value's variance as a 1×1 CovMat (cov(L.image[i, j, p])[1]), both planes from the one kernel. This cell image is the MATERIALISED uncertain image (owner ruling D14, (a)): its binned variances are computed once, in the one kernel, from the one variance formula, and a consumer never re-derives them; the photosites' uncertain image, linearview(m; sensor_model), is the lazy default. Otherwise (no noise, a ReadNoise alone) it is a plain AbstractArray{Float64, 3}. A consumer dispatches on the image's element type.\nsaturated::AbstractMatrix{Bool} (a BitMatrix on the host) — the MASKED pixels: those whose value includes a photosite at the white level (raw ≥ white) or, under a sensor model holding a DefectList, a defective photosite: in :bin2 a cell is masked when ANY of its four photosites is, so a cell whose two greens straddle the white level is flagged rather than averaged into a plausible value; in :green_cells when either green is. Masked, never interpolated.\ngeometry — where the samples are, (; plane_maps, green_offsets, plane_sites):\nplane_maps, per plane, its map onto the visible raw (bayer_plane_map, which reads it);\ngreen_offsets, the cell's two green photosites, (Δx, Δy) from the cell centre in RAW pixels, in the cell's column-major order (G1, G2 of bayer_channels; green_cell_sites);\nplane_sites, per plane, the photosites its value is the mean of, each (; color, dn): its CFA colour index (LibRaw's 0 R, 1 G, 2 B, 3 the second G; the index of a per-colour component's entry, σ[color + 1]) and its white − black[c], the codes that span 0 … 1 (before the gain) — what a value's photon and read noise scale by. The green plane's are in the order of green_offsets. black[c] is the channel's constant (the sensor model's black slot's) also when a Dark replaces it per photosite: the span is a scale, the same for every photosite of the channel, so a value is still codes / dn above its own black.\nnothing for :demosaic, whose pixels are interpolated, not samples.\ngain (positive and finite, ArgumentError otherwise) and the mosaic's metadata (the decoder and its library's version among it).\n\nThe mode is the type's first parameter, LinearRaw{:green_cells}, LinearRaw{:bin2} or LinearRaw{:demosaic}: the geometric accessors are methods of the cell images, and the display image's are its refusals. raw_provenance gives the mode, the gain, the metadata and the frame map as plain data for a result to record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearRaw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearRaw-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"LinearRaw(mosaic::RawMosaic; mode::Symbol, gain::Real = 1, sensor_model::SensorModel = SensorModel()) -> LinearRaw\n\nThe mosaic's linear values, (raw − black[c]) / (white − black[c]) × gain, on the half-resolution grid of 2×2 Bayer cells (H ÷ 2 × W ÷ 2; an odd last row or column is dropped), arranged by mode (required: there is no neutral choice between them):\n\n:green_cells — THE image for geometry and radiometry, one plane: each cell's two green photosites' mean. The greens of a cell are always diagonal, so in every Bayer phase their mean sits at the cell centre (offset (0, 0)), and the cell grid maps onto the raw exactly by raw_frame_map (x ↦ 2x in COLMAP coordinates). The footprint of a value is its two photosites, at (±½, ∓½) raw pixels from the cell centre for an RGGB or BGGR cell, (±½, ±½) for GRBG or GBRG (green_cell_sites).\n:bin2 — the same cells with their red and blue, × 3 planes: R, the mean of the two greens (the :green_cells image, bit for bit), B. R and B sit at their photosites, (±¼, ±¼) cell pixels from the centre: each plane's map onto the raw is bayer_plane_map.\n\nEvery value is from the mosaic's linearview, read channel-first (channelview(linearview(mosaic)), the mosaic's channelview): the green-cell value of cell (i, j) is the mean of the view's two green channels there, times the gain, bit for bit. :demosaic (LibRaw's interpolation, for display figures) is VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures': load_raw(path or mosaic; mode = :demosaic).\n\nThe values are those convert_raw.py writes (--mode bin2), in the same Float64 arithmetic and order, before its clip to [0, 1] and 16-bit rounding.\n\nTHE black subtraction is here, once, for every caller: sensor_model, the frame's SensorModel (sensor_model resolves it from a characterisation), each slot by its component:\n\nblack: FileBlack (the absent model's) the mosaic's own per-channel mosaic.black — with the absent model SensorModel(), the default, bit for bit the values without the keyword; ChannelBlack its values (the span white − black[c]); a Dark its per-photosite offset in place of the channel constant in the subtraction ((raw − offset) / (white − black[c]), black its channel black: the per-channel scalar is the constant-map case of the same kernel path);\ndefects: a DefectList's photosites join the saturation flags (saturated is then \"masked: saturated or defective\"); NoDefects adds none;\nnoise: PoissonGaussian or RelativeNoise make image an uncertain image, each value with its variance from the same kernel (D11): g·max(x, 0)/(m²K)·Σ 1/d + g²/m²·Σ s/d² for a value x of m photosites (spans d, gain g, s the photosite's σ_r[c]², or under a Dark its dark variance times 1 + 1/frames: its read noise and dark current, and the error of the offset subtracted), (σ_rel·x)² under a RelativeNoise; NoNoise and ReadNoise leave it plain (a read-noise variance alone underestimates a well-exposed pixel's, ruling A).\n\nThe maps cover the visible raw and are read at the mosaic's window_origin (DimensionMismatch when they do not reach the mosaic's photosites); they are moved to the mosaic's device.\n\nSaturation is raw ≥ white with no margin: on the Sony ARW sets this was written for, codes pile up exactly at the white level (16383) while the histogram runs smoothly through the camera's declared linear limit (15360, metadata.linear_max), so any margin would be a tuned constant. A caller who wants that stricter limit thresholds mosaic.raw itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearRaw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bayer_plane_map-Tuple{LinearRaw, Integer}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"bayer_plane_map(L::LinearRaw, c::Integer) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, BayerCellFrame{C}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nThe Bayer transform of plane c of the image: the map of the plane's own frame (BayerCellFrame{C}, C its colour — :R, :G, :B for :bin2, :G for :green_cells) onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}. It is the plane's translation onto the cell grid (where its samples sit from the cell centre: (±¼, ±¼) cell pixels for R and B, none for G) composed with raw_frame_map(L), so pixel (row, column) of the plane, at (column − ½, row − ½) of its frame, goes onto the centre of the photosites its value is the mean of — R's or B's one, the two greens' midpoint — exactly, for every Bayer phase and either parity of the visible origin. The green plane's map IS raw_frame_map(L), and the R and B planes' maps are the R and B channels' of the mosaic's channelview (bayer_plane_map(m, k)): one rule.\n\nA warp reads plane c of an image through inv(bayer_plane_map(L, c)) ∘ math, math landing in the raw's pixels, like any other map (image_warp; one warp per plane: image_warp!(dst, warps, L.image) with a tuple of them). A composition that joins two planes' frames is refused (MethodError). A plane past the image's, or a :demosaic image, is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bayer_plane_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.green_cell_sites-Tuple{LinearRaw, Integer, Integer}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"green_cell_sites(L::LinearRaw, row::Integer, column::Integer) -> NTuple{2, SVector{2, Float64}}\n\nThe footprint of the green-cell value of pixel (row, column): the centres of its two green photosites in the visible raw's COLMAP frame, G1 then G2 (the cell's column-major order). They are diagonal, at (±½, ∓½) raw pixels from the cell centre raw_frame_map(L)((column − ½, row − ½)) in an RGGB or BGGR cell and (±½, ±½) in a GRBG or GBRG one, and the value is the mean of the two — what a renderer models to predict it exactly. Each is the green channel's bayer_plane_map of the mosaic at that pixel. A :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.green_cell_sites"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.green_plane-Tuple{LinearRaw}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"green_plane(L::LinearRaw) -> AbstractMatrix{Float64}\n\nThe image's green cells, a view: each cell's two greens' mean — L.image's plane whose frame IS the cell grid (raw_frame_map(L)): a :green_cells image's one plane, a :bin2 image's second (G). The plane every green-cell consumer reads (a detector, VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' variance_stabilize); its photosites' colours and spans are L.geometry.plane_sites of that plane. An uncertain image's green plane is uncertain too (a StructArray view: values and variances). A :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.green_plane"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.poisson_slope-Tuple{LinearRaw, SensorModel}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"poisson_slope(L::LinearRaw, model::SensorModel) -> NTuple{P, Float64}\n\nPer plane of L (L.image[:, :, p]), the Poisson slope α of a value's variance under model: the α of α·x + σ², gain/(m²K)·Σ 1/d over the plane's m photosites (L.geometry.plane_sites[p]), 0 without a Poisson term (a model whose noise is not PoissonGaussian). The one expression the kernel writes the variance plane with; the generalized Anscombe transform reads it beside the variance plane (VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' variance_stabilize). A :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError).\n\npoisson_slope(L::LinearRaw, model::SensorModel, plane) -> Float64\n\nThe slope of one plane: plane its index p, or its colour, :R, :G or :B (:G the green plane, green_plane(L)'s; a :green_cells image has :G alone). A colour the image does not hold is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.poisson_slope"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_coordinates-Tuple{Union{LinearRaw, RawMosaic}, Any}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"raw_coordinates(img, x) -> SVector{2, Float64}\n\nWhere the point x of an image's pixel frame (COLMAP, (column − ½, row − ½) the centre of pixel (row, column); any 2-vector or tuple) lies on the sensor: in the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, the frame of every calibration of the raw. THE accessor of an image's sensor-pixel coordinates, raw_frame_map(img)(x):\n\na RawMosaic: the identity as decoded, a window's offset added for a window;\na LinearRaw cell image (:green_cells, :bin2): the cell grid's map, x ↦ 2x (and a window's offset) — a detection on the green cells reports its position through it, never as raw pixels unmapped (half the scale); a position on another plane of a :bin2 image is bayer_plane_map(L, c)(x), and on a channel of a mosaic's channelview(m) it is bayer_plane_map(m, k)(x);\na :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError), and a plain matrix, which states no frame, has no method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_frame_map-Tuple{LinearRaw}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"raw_frame_map(L::LinearRaw) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, BayerCellFrame{:G}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nTHE map of the image's pixel frame (the cell grid, which is its green plane's frame) onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap} (the frame of RawMosaic.raw and of every calibration of the raw): x ↦ 2x, exactly, for every Bayer phase and either parity of the visible origin (the cells start at the visible area's first photosite; an odd margin changes only the cell's colour phase); for an image made from a window of the mosaic, x ↦ 2x then the window's offset. Its inverse is inv(raw_frame_map(L)), and a calibration matrix moves with it through Core, reframed_calibration(raw_frame_map(L), K_cells), K_cells in the cell image's pixels (BayerCellFrame{:G}), giving K in the raw's pixels (and back with the inverse; K carries no frame label, so which frame it is in is the caller's statement). A :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError): it is for display.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_frame_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_frame_map-Tuple{Type{<:LinearRaw{:green_cells}}}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"raw_frame_map(::Type{LinearRaw{mode}}) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, BayerCellFrame{:G}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nThe frame map of a mode image made from a whole mosaic (no window), without an image: x ↦ 2x, exactly, for :green_cells and :bin2 alike (the cell grid; a map that depends on the mode alone, not on the photograph, its Bayer phase or its visible origin). THE source of the cell images' map: every LinearRaw is made with this map after its mosaic's own (raw_frame_map(mosaic), a window's offset), so raw_frame_map(LinearRaw(m; mode)) == raw_frame_map(m) ∘ raw_frame_map(LinearRaw{mode}) bit for bit, and for an unwindowed mosaic the two are equal. For a frame with no RAW behind it (a decoded image on the cell grid) whose map is needed, e.g. scale(raw_frame_map(LinearRaw{:green_cells})) == 2. LinearRaw{:demosaic} is refused (ArgumentError), as its images are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_frame_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_provenance-Tuple{LinearRaw}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"raw_provenance(L::LinearRaw) -> NamedTuple\n\nHow the image was made, as plain data a result records: decoder, libraw_version (from the mosaic's metadata; nothing where it states none), mode, gain and frame_map ((; from, to, scale, translation) of raw_frame_map(L), nothing for :demosaic). Its mode is the mode's own record's (raw_provenance(LinearRaw{mode})), and both records' frame_map are written by one rule from the map, so an image of a whole mosaic records the mode's frame_map; a window's records its offset in translation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_provenance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_provenance-Tuple{Type{<:LinearRaw{:green_cells}}}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"raw_provenance(::Type{LinearRaw{mode}}) -> (; mode, frame_map)\n\nThe part of raw_provenance(L) that the mode alone determines, without an image: mode (\"green_cells\", \"bin2\", \"demosaic\") and frame_map, the record of raw_frame_map(LinearRaw{mode}) ((; from, to, scale, translation); nothing for :demosaic, which holds no geometry). An image made from a whole mosaic (no window) records the same mode and frame_map, == field for field, whatever its Bayer phase or visible origin. For a result whose frames have no RAW behind them that records the frame they are on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.raw_provenance"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DarkOutOfBand","page":"Camera RAW","text":"DarkOutOfBand: a ModelReason — this body at this ISO, but the frame's exposure is outside the Dark's band: its ChannelBlack, the entry's other components.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DarkOutOfBand"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Matched","page":"Camera RAW","text":"Matched: a ModelReason — an entry of this body at the frame's ISO gave the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Matched"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoCharacterisation","page":"Camera RAW","text":"NoCharacterisation: a ModelReason — no characterisation was given; the absent model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoCharacterisation"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OtherBody","page":"Camera RAW","text":"OtherBody: a ModelReason — the characterisations given are of other bodies; the absent model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OtherBody"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.UnmatchedISO","page":"Camera RAW","text":"UnmatchedISO: a ModelReason — this body is characterised, but not at the frame's ISO; the absent model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.UnmatchedISO"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BlackComponent","page":"Camera RAW","text":"BlackComponent, NoiseComponent, DefectComponent\n\nThe three slots of a SensorModel and of a SensorCharacterisation entry: what a code's black level is (FileBlack, ChannelBlack, Dark), its noise (NoNoise, ReadNoise, PoissonGaussian, RelativeNoise) and which photosites are defects (NoDefects, DefectList).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BlackComponent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ChannelBlack","page":"Camera RAW","text":"ChannelBlack(values, std_error = nothing; source::SensorSource.T)\n\nA per-channel black level: values per CFA colour index (LibRaw's 0 R, 1 G, 2 B, 3 the second G: values[c + 1], RawMosaic.black's layout), in codes, finite; std_error its standard error per channel (nonnegative), or nothing where none is stated (a specified value). source is measured, estimated or specified (the RAW's own is FileBlack). Every value is below the white level where it is used (ArgumentError there).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ChannelBlack"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Dark","page":"Camera RAW","text":"Dark(black, offset, variance, exposure_band, frames; source::SensorSource.T)\n\nA master dark of one body at one ISO, for one exposure-time band — the black slot's per-photosite form (D2: all dark information in ONE component):\n\nblack — its per-channel black (ChannelBlack or FileBlack): the span white − black[c] of every photosite of the channel, and the black a frame whose exposure the band does not serve is linearised with;\noffset — per photosite of the visible raw (raw[row, column], the size of the decoded mosaic), the mean code of the lens-capped frames (the black, the DSNU and the dark current together): LinearRaw's kernel subtracts it in place of the channel's black;\nvariance — its temporal variance (codes²: the read noise with the rounding, and the dark current's shot noise), the Gaussian part of a PoissonGaussian model's variance at that photosite;\nexposure_band = (shortest, longest), time quantities, the exposures it serves (stored in seconds);\nframes, the number of frames averaged (at least 2).\n\nMade by master_dark (SensorSource.measured). Moved to a device by Adapt.adapt, as LinearRawView is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Dark"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DefectComponent","page":"Camera RAW","text":"BlackComponent, NoiseComponent, DefectComponent\n\nThe three slots of a SensorModel and of a SensorCharacterisation entry: what a code's black level is (FileBlack, ChannelBlack, Dark), its noise (NoNoise, ReadNoise, PoissonGaussian, RelativeNoise) and which photosites are defects (NoDefects, DefectList).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DefectComponent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DefectList","page":"Camera RAW","text":"DefectList(x, y, onset; size, source::SensorSource.T)\n\nThe defective photosites of a sensor: photosite (x[i], y[i]) — x the column and y the row of the visible raw, 1-based (raw[y, x]) — is a defect at every exposure of at least onset[i] (a time quantity, stored in seconds: the shortest exposure at which it is hot). size = (rows, columns) is the visible raw's (every site inside it, each listed once). Made from per-exposure masks by defect_onsets. sensor_model keeps the sites whose onset is at most the frame's exposure, and LinearRaw masks them (masked, never interpolated). Moved to a device by Adapt.adapt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DefectList"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FileBlack","page":"Camera RAW","text":"FileBlack()\n\nThe black slot's absent default: the RAW file's own per-channel black level (RawMosaic.black, LibRaw's), today's behaviour bit for bit. Its source is SensorSource.file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FileBlack"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ModelReason","page":"Camera RAW","text":"ModelReason\n\nWhy sensor_model gave a frame the model it did (an isbits @enum, SensorModel.reason):\n\nMatched: a characterisation of this body holds an entry at the frame's ISO; its components are the model's (an absent slot of the entry is the absent type);\nNoCharacterisation: none was given (also the absent default SensorModel());\nUnmatchedISO: characterisations of this body exist, none at this ISO (no extrapolation across ISO);\nOtherBody: the characterisations given are of other bodies;\nDarkOutOfBand (Core 0.10.2): the body and the ISO matched, but the frame's exposure is outside the entry's Dark's exposure band, so the model's black is the Dark's ChannelBlack (the entry's other components kept): the fallback is visible, never silent.\n\nNoCharacterisation, UnmatchedISO and OtherBody give the absent model; Matched and DarkOutOfBand the entry's components. A caller that matches the values exhaustively handles DarkOutOfBand too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ModelReason"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoDefects","page":"Camera RAW","text":"NoDefects()\n\nThe defect slot's absent default: no photosite is masked beyond the saturated ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoDefects"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoNoise","page":"Camera RAW","text":"NoNoise()\n\nThe noise slot's absent default: no noise is stated. A LinearRaw image is plain, and each detector applies its own documented policy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoNoise"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoiseComponent","page":"Camera RAW","text":"BlackComponent, NoiseComponent, DefectComponent\n\nThe three slots of a SensorModel and of a SensorCharacterisation entry: what a code's black level is (FileBlack, ChannelBlack, Dark), its noise (NoNoise, ReadNoise, PoissonGaussian, RelativeNoise) and which photosites are defects (NoDefects, DefectList).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoiseComponent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PoissonGaussian","page":"Camera RAW","text":"PoissonGaussian(gain, σ, std_error = nothing; source::SensorSource.T)\n\nThe Poisson–Gaussian noise: gain K, electrons per code (positive, finite), and the read noise σ per CFA colour index, in codes, the rounding included; a photosite's variance about its black-subtracted mean μ (codes) is μ/K + σ[c+1]². std_error is (; gain, σ) (a number and a 4-tuple) or nothing. source is measured, estimated (photon_transfer) or specified. Under it a LinearRaw image carries each value's variance (an Uncertain image).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PoissonGaussian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReadNoise","page":"Camera RAW","text":"ReadNoise(σ, std_error = nothing; source::SensorSource.T)\n\nThe read noise alone: σ per CFA colour index (σ[c + 1]), in codes, the rounding included (nonnegative, finite), and its standard error per channel or nothing. Stored and recorded, but it defines no pixel variance: a read-noise variance alone badly underestimates the noise of a well-exposed pixel, so a LinearRaw image under it is plain, as under NoNoise (ruling A).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ReadNoise"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RelativeNoise","page":"Camera RAW","text":"RelativeNoise(σ_rel; source::SensorSource.T)\n\nA noise proportional to the value: a value x has variance (σ_rel·x)² (σ_rel positive, finite). Its source is SensorSource.specified only, stated by a person (owner ruling on D14): no estimator or measurement makes one, so any other source is an ArgumentError naming it — the one rule, at the source (an IO reader of a stored one meets it here too). The model of a processed (tone-mapped) intensity, not of the photosites' physics; a LinearRaw image under it carries each value's variance (an Uncertain image), with no read-noise or dark term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RelativeNoise"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorCharacterisation","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorCharacterisation(body::DeviceIdentity; entries)\n\nThe description of ONE physical sensor, host-side: body, the key (serial if known, else make and model), held beside the camera like CaptureSettings, never in a camera or lens model; and entries, per ISO its components — an iterable of iso => (; black, noise, defects), each slot optional (an absent slot IS the absent type: FileBlack, NoNoise, NoDefects), so a partial characterisation is a mix of components. c.entries is a Dict{Float64, SensorEntry} (ISO → the three slots). An ISO listed twice, a slot not among black, noise and defects (a dark map is the black slot's Dark), or a non-positive ISO is an ArgumentError.\n\nsensor_model resolves a frame's model from it. Written and read by VisualGeometryToolkitIO's vgio/sensor_characterisation kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorCharacterisation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorEntry","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorEntry = @NamedTuple{black::BlackComponent, noise::NoiseComponent, defects::DefectComponent}\n\nOne ISO's entry of a SensorCharacterisation: its three slots, each a component (an absent slot its absent type).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorEntry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorModel","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorModel(black::BlackComponent, noise::NoiseComponent, defects::DefectComponent;\n            reason::ModelReason = Matched, specified_override::Bool = false)\nSensorModel()\n\nThe sensor model of ONE frame: the black, noise and defects components sensor_model picked for its ISO and exposure (a Dark only when its band serves the exposure, a DefectList holding only the sites whose onset is at most the exposure), reason why (a ModelReason) and specified_override, whether a specified component displaced a measured one (the run record notes the override).\n\nSensorModel() is the absent model, SensorModel(FileBlack(), NoNoise(), NoDefects(); reason = NoCharacterisation): LinearRaw and linearview under it are bit for bit the RAW's own linearisation. A model of isbits components is isbits; one holding a dark map or a defect list moves to a device with Adapt.adapt. content_digest is its identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.component_sources-Tuple{SensorModel}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"component_sources(m::SensorModel) -> NamedTuple{(:black, :noise, :defects, :reason, :specified_override)}\n\nWhere each of a frame's sensor components came from — the provenance a run record states (ruling A): black, noise and defects each the component's SensorSource.T (a FileBlack's is file), or nothing for an absent slot (NoNoise, NoDefects); reason why the frame got this model (a ModelReason: Matched, NoCharacterisation, UnmatchedISO, OtherBody, or DarkOutOfBand when the entry's Dark did not serve the frame's exposure and its ChannelBlack stands in); specified_override whether a specified component won over a measured one. The one public view of a model's provenance: a consumer reads it, never the components' fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.component_sources"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.content_digest-Tuple{SensorModel}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"content_digest(model::SensorModel) -> String\n\nThe identity of a frame's sensor model: the SHA-256 (lower-case hex) of the canonical JSON (canonical_json, the stack's one content-identity encoding) of a tree of its components — each component's type, values, standard errors and source, a map (a Dark's offset and variance) by the SHA-256 of its Float32 values' bytes and its size, a DefectList by its sites — its reason and specified_override. Equal models have equal digests, on any device (a device map is read to the host); a cache keyed by a model (VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' decode cache) keys on it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.content_digest"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sensor_model-Tuple{Union{Nothing, SensorCharacterisation}, CaptureSettings, Integer}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"sensor_model(characterisation, capture::CaptureSettings, t::Integer) -> SensorModel\n\nTHE resolver of frame t's SensorModel: a LOOKUP (owner ruling D1; it never estimates — photon_transfer makes an estimated entry offline). characterisation is a SensorCharacterisation, a vector or tuple of them, or nothing; the frame's body is capture.body, its ISO and exposure capture[t].exposure.\n\nThe characterisations of this body (serial if both state one, else make and model; DeviceIdentity), and their entries at exactly the frame's ISO (no extrapolation across ISO).\nPer slot, independently, the component those entries hold; where several hold one, the specified one over the measured over the estimated (D5), and specified_override records a specified one displacing a measured one; two of the same source are an ArgumentError, as are two different bodies matching a capture that states no serial.\nA Dark only when its exposure band serves the frame's exposure (otherwise its channel black, and the reason DarkOutOfBand); a DefectList cut to the sites whose onset is at most the exposure (none left: NoDefects).\n\nWith no characterisation (NoCharacterisation), none of this body (OtherBody) or none at this ISO (UnmatchedISO) the result is the absent model SensorModel(FileBlack(), NoNoise(), NoDefects()) with that ModelReason: today's linearisation, bit for bit. Otherwise its reason is Matched, or DarkOutOfBand when the entry's Dark does not serve the frame's exposure. A frame the capture does not hold is an ArgumentError. An explicit model a caller holds is used as it is: this resolver is where a characterisation becomes one, never a substitute for one given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sensor_model"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoiseEstimateFailed","page":"Camera RAW","text":"NoiseEstimateFailed(reason)\n\nThe exception photon_transfer and master_dark throw when the data cannot give the estimate (too few usable samples, a degenerate fit), reason a sentence. The resolver sensor_model never runs an estimator (a lookup): a characterisation without the estimate lacks the component, and a frame resolves to its absent type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoiseEstimateFailed"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PhotonTransferMetadata","page":"Camera RAW","text":"PhotonTransferMetadata\n\nThe FitResult metadata of photon_transfer(groups, darks) (its estimate a PoissonGaussian), read through the result's forwarded properties:\n\nlevel_range::NTuple{2, Float64} — the fit's lowest and highest point (block means, codes above black); level_fraction the same as fractions of the white level above black (the greens' span);\nintercept::Float64 — σ₀² (codes²), the variance at zero signal, fixed from the darks at each group's own exposure (the median over the fitted points);\ncurvature, curvature_t — the μ² coefficient c of v = σ₀² + μ/K + cμ² over the range and its t (c over its standard error);\ntests, test_level — the μ² tests the lower end's search can perform (m, the candidate lower ends) and each test's two-sided level, significance / m (Bonferroni: the search's family-wise rate is significance); rollover_tests, rollover_level the same for the upper end's rollover tests (one-sided);\nbirge_ratio — R = √(χ²/dof) of the fitted points about the line (each in its median's standard deviation from the counts; dof the points less the one slope): the scatter the counts explain is R ≤ 1; when R > 1, K's standard error is multiplied by R (the Birge ratio: never shrunk);\npoints_offered — the points before the range search (the result's n is the points fitted).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PhotonTransferMetadata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.defect_onsets-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Pair{<:FlexUnits.Quantity, <:AbstractMatrix{Bool}}}}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"defect_onsets(masks; source::SensorSource.T = SensorSource.measured) -> DefectList\n\nONE DefectList from per-exposure defect masks: masks is a vector of exposure => mask (a time quantity and a Bool matrix over the visible raw, master_dark's defects with the shortest exposure of its band, say), all masks of one size. Each photosite flagged in any mask is listed once, its onset the shortest exposure whose mask flags it (the resolver masks it at every exposure from there on: a site flagged at a short exposure and missed at a longer one is a defect at both). The sites are in column-major order of the visible raw. An empty vector or masks of different sizes are an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.defect_onsets"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.master_dark-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:RawMosaic}}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"master_dark(frames::AbstractVector{<:RawMosaic}; exposure_band, false_defects = 0.01)\n    -> (; dark::Dark, read_noise::ReadNoise, defects::BitMatrix)\n\nTHE dark-frame estimator: the lens-capped frames of one body at one ISO and one exposure band (whole mosaics of one size, at least 2) into the components of a characterisation's entry, all SensorSource.measured:\n\ndark — the Dark: per photosite the temporal mean (the offset: black, DSNU and dark current) and sample variance, one KernelAbstractions work item each, on the frames' device; its channel black (dark.black) a ChannelBlack, per colour index (LibRaw's 0–3) the mean offset of its photosites (defects left out), with its standard error from their spread and count;\nread_noise — a ReadNoise: per colour index, the root mean temporal variance (the read noise with the rounding, and at a long exposure the dark current's shot noise), its standard error from the χ² spread of the frame count and the photosite count (√(2/(n − 1)/N)·σ² for the mean variance, by first order);\ndefects — a BitMatrix, the photosites whose offset leaves their channel's median by more than z robust standard deviations (the median absolute deviation over the normal's, mad_scale's rule for codimension 1, about the channel's median), z the normal quantile at which false_defects defect-free photosites are expected to be flagged over the whole sensor of N photosites (1 − false_defects / (2N), two-sided, for offsets that are Gaussian about their channel's black). The masks of several exposures make one DefectList by defect_onsets.\n\nA colour index the pattern lacks (a pattern with one green index) has black and read noise 0 (no photosite reads it).\n\nexposure_band is (shortest, longest), time quantities, the exposures the dark serves. A frame of another size, or fewer than 2, is an ArgumentError; a channel without a usable photosite throws NoiseEstimateFailed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.master_dark"},{"category":"method","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.photon_transfer-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Camera RAW","text":"photon_transfer(groups::AbstractDict{<:Quantity, <:AbstractVector{<:RawMosaic}},\n                darks::AbstractDict{<:Quantity, <:ReadNoise}; sensor_model::SensorModel = SensorModel(),\n                block = 8, normalize = true, significance = 0.001) -> FitResult{PoissonGaussian, PhotonTransferMetadata}\nphoton_transfer(mosaic::RawMosaic; sensor_model::SensorModel = SensorModel(), block = 8, significance = 0.001)\n    -> PoissonGaussian\n\nTHE estimator of a sensor's Poisson–Gaussian noise for a characterisation's entry, built offline (owner ruling D1: the resolver sensor_model never estimates): the conversion gain K (electrons per code), by a photon-transfer fit of variance against mean, v = σ₀² + μ/K.\n\nThe photon-transfer series (groups, exposure => the frames at that exposure, at least 2 each, of one body at one ISO; darks, exposure => the ReadNoise of master_dark over the darks of that exposure), a MEASURED PoissonGaussian (SensorSource.measured, a dedicated capture) in a FitResult (its estimate), its metadata a PhotonTransferMetadata: per green photosite its TEMPORAL variance over the group's frames, which cancels the scene and every fixed pattern, about its mean μ. Pairs are the case of 2 frames. With normalize (the default) each frame is first scaled to its group's mean (its greens' black-subtracted mean over the group's: a source that flickers between frames adds no μ² term). Per block, the photosites' means and their pooled variance give a point, v̂/v following χ²_{p(N−1)}/(p(N − 1)) over a whole block of p photosites and N frames; every group gives its first N frames, N the smallest group's, so every block follows that one law.\n\nTHE INTERCEPT is fixed at each group's own exposure t: σ₀²(t), the read noise and dark-current noise of the darks at that exposure, the greens' σ[c + 1]² of darks[t]. A group whose exposure has no entry in darks (equal in seconds) is an ArgumentError: never interpolated, never substituted. The fit is the slope alone, v − σ₀²(t) = μ/K.\nTHE RANGE is derived from the data, at least 3 points: the upper end below the variance's rollover (the top point dropped while it lies significantly below the line fitted to the points under it, one-sided), then the lower end, the lowest point from which the μ² test on the line succeeds (the lowest point dropped until it does). Each search runs its tests at significance / m, m the tests it can perform (Bonferroni: the search's family-wise false-alarm rate is significance); the range (in codes and as fractions of the white level), m and the levels are in the result's metadata.\nTHE STANDARD ERROR of K is the fitted line's from the counts, multiplied by the Birge ratio R = √(χ²/dof) of the fitted points when R > 1 (the points scatter more than the counts explain); when R ≤ 1 it is unchanged, never shrunk. R is in the result's metadata.\nThe result's σr is √σ₀² of the fitted points' darks (their median), its standard error not the fit's (0); a characterisation states σr per colour from master_dark's ReadNoise.\n\nOne frame (mosaic), in the line of Foi et al. (2008), an ESTIMATED PoissonGaussian (SensorSource.estimated): unreliable on flats, scheduled for removal in the next breaking wave; use the multi-frame method. A single flat frame cannot certify its own K (its brightness range gives the μ² test little power); for flat fields use the multi-frame photon-transfer method. Each 2×2 cell's two greens see the same light, so their difference d carries no scene where the scene is flat across the cell, and Var d = 2(μ/K + σ_r²) about their black-subtracted mean μ. Per block, the cells' mean and the difference's sample variance give a point (μ̂, v̂ = s²/2), v̂/v following χ²_{n−1}/(n − 1) over a whole block of n cells; the line's intercept σr² is fitted, over every point, with its standard error (σr stated for every colour index, the greens'). A fixed pattern between the two greens (their gain or response mismatch) does NOT cancel in d: it adds a term in μ², which the μ² test REFUSES.\n\nBoth: per block of block × block cells (one KernelAbstractions work item each, on the frames' device), a sample with a photosite at the white level, or under the model's defect mask, is left out (CLIPPED, never modelled), and only whole blocks (the most usable samples any block has) enter the fit. The blocks are sorted by μ̂ into points of equal count (min(32, blocks ÷ 8); the series': per exposure, so a point never mixes two exposures' intercepts, an exposure of fewer than 8 whole blocks left out); each point's median μ̂ and median v̂ / q (q that law's median, so the ratio is median-unbiased for v). The line is fitted by weighted least squares, each point weighted by the inverse of its median's variance from the counts, v²/(4 m f(q)² q²) over a point of m blocks (f the law's density: the sample median's asymptotic variance), with v the line's own prediction (iterated to a fixed point from a start weighted at each point's own value). The median is robust to the blocks a scene edge raises. The standard errors are the fitted line's covariance from those counts; K's by first order.\n\nTHE μ² TEST: the points fitted again with a μ² term, v = … + μ/K + cμ², at the same weights, and c significant when |ĉ / se(ĉ)| exceeds the two-sided normal quantile at the test's level (one frame: significance, 3.29 at the default 0.001, over every point; the series: the lower end's search). It never returns a K biased by a μ² term it detects.\n\nsensor_model gives what is subtracted and left out, as it does for LinearRaw: its black slot's per-colour black (a Dark's offset per photosite in its place) and its defect slot's photosites; its noise slot is not read. Throws NoiseEstimateFailed with the reason when fewer than 16 whole blocks of at least 8 usable samples remain (a saturated frame), the points hold one level only (no slope), one frame's μ² term is significant, the series' μ² test fails on every contiguous range of at least 3 points, or the fit is not physical (K, or one frame's σ_r², not positive). A group of fewer than 2 frames, frames of another size, pattern or white level, or a group's exposure without darks, is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.photon_transfer"},{"category":"module","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorSource\n\nWhere a sensor component's values came from, SensorSource.T (an isbits @enum): SensorSource.measured (a record of dark frames, flats or a photon-transfer capture), SensorSource.estimated (fitted by an estimator, photon_transfer, with its standard error), SensorSource.specified (stated by a person: a datasheet, a config, TOML literals) or SensorSource.file (the RAW file's own metadata: FileBlack's). string(s) is its tag (\"measured\", …). Each present component holds one in its source field. A specified component wins over a measured one at the same slot (sensor_model).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.T","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorSource.T: the SensorSource enum type, a sensor component's source field (an isbits @enum).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.T"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.measured","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorSource.measured: a SensorSource — the values were measured (dark frames, flats, a photon-transfer capture).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.measured"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.estimated","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorSource.estimated: a SensorSource — the values were fitted by an estimator, with their standard errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.estimated"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.specified","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorSource.specified: a SensorSource — the values were stated by a person (a datasheet, a config, TOML literals).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.specified"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/raw/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.file","page":"Camera RAW","text":"SensorSource.file: a SensorSource — the values are the RAW file's own metadata (a FileBlack's).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SensorSource.file"},{"category":"section","location":"api/synthetic/#Synthetic-Data-API","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Synthetic scenes, evaluation metrics and augmentation (the Synthetic submodule), and the Monte Carlo calibration of the uncertainty propagation.","title":"Synthetic Data API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/synthetic/#Synthetic-Data","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"","title":"Synthetic Data"},{"category":"section","location":"api/synthetic/#Monte-Carlo-calibration-of-the-uncertainty-propagation","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Public, not exported (Synthetic.run_calibration, or import by name): the scenes, the per-model trials and the coverage tests the stack's propagation calibration items run.","title":"Monte Carlo calibration of the uncertainty propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/synthetic/#Index","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Pages = [\"synthetic.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Synthetic\n\nSynthetic data generation for benchmarking geometric estimators.\n\nProvides ground-truth scene generators with configurable noise and outlier contamination for conic fitting, and evaluation metrics.\n\nConic Points\n\npts, mask = ellipse_points(; n=100, outlier_frac=0.3, sigma=0.5, seed=42)\n\nEvaluation\n\nerr = imc_pose_error(F_est, K, R_gt, t_gt)\nscore = mean_average_accuracy(errs; max_error = 10)   # mAA@10°: the threshold is required\n\nSee also: VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures for correspondence generators (synthetic_stereo_rig, stereo_correspondences, planar_correspondences).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.ellipse_points-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"ellipse_points(; cx=50.0, cy=50.0, a=20.0, b=10.0, angle=0.3,\n                n=100, outlier_frac=0.0, sigma=0.0, seed=42)\n\nGenerate synthetic points on an ellipse with optional noise and outliers.\n\nPoints are uniformly sampled in angle around the ellipse. Outliers are uniformly distributed within the bounding box of the ellipse (expanded by 2×).\n\nReturns\n\npoints::Vector{Point2{Float64}}: Point set (inliers + outliers, shuffled)\nis_inlier::BitVector: Mask indicating true inlier points\n\nArguments\n\ncx, cy: Ellipse center\na, b: Semi-major and semi-minor axes\nangle: Rotation angle (radians)\nn::Int=100: Number of inlier points\noutlier_frac::Real=0.0: Fraction of total that are outliers\nsigma::Real=0.0: Gaussian noise std added to points\nseed::Int=42: RNG seed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.ellipse_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.imc_pose_error-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"imc_pose_error(F_est, K, R_gt, t_gt) -> Float64\n\nImage Matching Challenge pose error metric (DEGREES).\n\nDecomposes E = KᵀFK into 4 possible (R, t) solutions, computes max(rotation_error, translation_error) for each, and returns the minimum. This is the coherent (single-decomposition) counterpart to optimistic_pose_error, which takes per-axis minima from possibly different decompositions; both report in degrees.\n\nThis is the standard metric from the IMC benchmark: it measures how well an estimated fundamental matrix recovers the true relative pose.\n\nArguments\n\nF_est: Estimated 3×3 fundamental matrix\nK: Shared 3×3 calibration matrix (or K1, K2 for different intrinsics)\nR_gt: Ground-truth 3×3 rotation matrix\nt_gt: Ground-truth 3-vector translation\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Pose error in degrees (max of rotation/translation angular errors, minimized over the 4 essential matrix decompositions)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.imc_pose_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.imc_pose_error-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"imc_pose_error(E, R_gt, t_gt) -> Float64\n\nIMC pose error (degrees) of an already-metric essential matrix E against the ground-truth pose — decomposes E directly via motion(E), no calibration. Use the (F, K…) overloads when starting from a fundamental matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.imc_pose_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.imc_pose_error-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, StereoRig}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"imc_pose_error(F_est, rig::StereoRig) -> Float64\n\nIMC pose error using a StereoRig for ground-truth pose and calibration.\n\nExtracts K from the source camera and (R, t) from the rig's extrinsics. Assumes shared intrinsics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.imc_pose_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.maa-Tuple{Any, Real}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"maa(errors, t) -> Float64\n\nAccuracy at threshold t: the fraction of errors strictly below t. Non-finite entries (failed estimates) never succeed and therefore count as failures in the denominator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.maa"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.maa_score-Tuple{Any}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"maa_score(e; max_error) -> Float64\n\nPer-item contribution to the continuous mean_average_accuracy: max(0, 1 - min(e, T)/T) with T = max_error, and 0 for non-finite e. Use this per-sample form for bootstrap resampling; mean(e -> maa_score(e; max_error), errors) == mean_average_accuracy(errors; max_error). max_error is required, as for the aggregate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.maa_score"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.mean_average_accuracy-Tuple{Any}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"mean_average_accuracy(errors; max_error) -> Float64\n\nTHE canonical mAA@max_error (≡ AUC): the normalized area under the empirical CDF of errors from 0 to T = max_error — (1/T)∫₀ᵀ F(t)dt, equivalently mean_p max(0, 1 - min(e_p, T)/T). Non-finite entries (failed estimates) contribute 0 but stay in the denominator. This is the EXACT AUC; the former discrete threshold-average variant is retired — it is strictly coarser (it cannot resolve errors below T/#thresholds). All consumers use this continuous statistic.\n\nmax_error is required, in the errors' own units: the threshold is a protocol parameter of the caller's benchmark (the IMC pose-error mAA takes max_error = 10 degrees), never a hidden default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.mean_average_accuracy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.optimistic_pose_error-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"optimistic_pose_error(E::AbstractMatrix, R_gt, t_gt) -> (Δr_deg, Δt_deg)\n\nLower-bound (per-axis-optimistic) pose error of an essential matrix against a ground-truth pose.  Decomposes E into its 4 sign-ambiguous candidates and returns the per-axis minimum errors:\n\nΔr_min = min over decomps k of  rad2deg(rotation_angle(RotMatrix{3}(R_kᵀ R_gt)))\nΔt_min = min over decomps k of  rad2deg(translation_angle(t_k, t_gt))\n\nThe two minimums may come from different decompositions — the returned tuple does not correspond to any single (R, t) pair, and is therefore unrealizable as a coherent single-decomposition output.  It bounds from below what any one decomposition could achieve per axis.\n\nUsed by the Shekhovtsov RANSAC-Scoring protocol (per-axis independent min, see RANSAC-Scoring/model_H.py:438-452) and by the pool record Pools.PoolPair, which stores errors_r / errors_t as separate columns.\n\nFor a coherent single-decomposition error: select the desired decomposition externally (e.g. via cheirality on real correspondences, or argmin_k max(Δr_k, Δt_k) against the oracle) and call pose_error(R, t, R_gt, t_gt) directly.\n\nSee also: pose_error, imc_pose_error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.optimistic_pose_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.optimistic_pose_error-Tuple{HomographyMat, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"optimistic_pose_error(H::HomographyMat, K1, K2, R_gt, t_gt) -> (Δr_deg, Δt_deg)\n\nThe pose error of a PIXEL-space homography with intrinsics K1 (source view) and K2 (target view): calibrated as inv(K2)·H·K1, then the calibrated form above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.optimistic_pose_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.optimistic_pose_error-Tuple{HomographyMat, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"optimistic_pose_error(H::HomographyMat, R_gt, t_gt) -> (Δr_deg, Δt_deg)\n\nPer-axis-optimistic pose error (DEGREES) of a calibrated homography against a ground-truth pose: decomposes H via Malis–Vargas motion(H) and returns the per-axis minima over the (up to 4) (R, t, n) candidates, exactly as the essential-matrix overload does (the two minima may come from different candidates — a per-axis lower bound).  Returns (180.0, 180.0) when the decomposition is empty (singular H).\n\nH must already be calibrated; for a pixel-space H_px use the intrinsics form below.  Translation error uses translation_angle, which is 0 for a zero-translation (pure-rotation) candidate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.optimistic_pose_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.perturbed_pose-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Any, Any, Real}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"perturbed_pose(rng, R_gt, t_gt, δ) -> (RotMatrix, Vec3)\n\nThe protocol's δr = δt = δ perturbation of a ground-truth relative pose: rotation by δ [rad] about a seeded random axis plus a magnitude-δ translation jitter (t re-normalized). The draw order is fixed (axis first, then translation jitter), so a caller-seeded rng reproduces the seed bit-for-bit (ladder convention: one StableRNG(stable_seed(seed, scene, pair, δ)) per (pair, δ), so every arm sees the identical perturbed seed, on every Julia version).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.perturbed_pose"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.pose_error-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"pose_error(R_est, t_est, R_gt, t_gt) -> (rot_err, trans_err)\n\nRotation and translation angular errors in radians.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.pose_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.projective_distance-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"projective_distance(A, B) -> Float64\n\nDistance between two models known only up to a nonzero scale and a sign (E, F, H, a radial camera's rows, a homogeneous vector): the Euclidean distance between the unit-normalized entries, with B taken in A's sign,\n\n‖â − sign(â·b̂)·b̂‖ = min(‖â − b̂‖, ‖â + b̂‖),   â = vec(A)/‖A‖.\n\nIt lies in [0, √2], is 0 exactly when A ∝ B, and is symmetric. For small values it is the angle between the two representatives: 1 − |â·b̂| = d²/2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.projective_distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.normalize_map-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"normalize_map(cx, cy, hx, hy) -> SMatrix{3, 3, Float64}\n\nThe world→normalised affine map sending centre (cx, cy) to the origin and half-extents (hx, hy) to 1 (guarded against a zero extent).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.normalize_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.perspective_augmentation-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3}, Any}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"perspective_augmentation(rng, H_wi::SMatrix{3, 3}, extent;\n                         strengthen_max = 1.0, jitter_max = 0.1) -> SMatrix{3, 3, Float64}\n\nAn image→image homography that strengthens H_wi's perspective and adds a random-direction tilt. extent = (cx, cy, hx, hy) is the target's world bounding box (centre + per-axis half-extent). Strengthening scales the projective row's in-plane entries (the vanishing line) by 1 + γ, γ ~ U(0, strengthen_max), leaving the centroid fixed; the jitter is a tilt of magnitude ~ U(0, jitter_max) in a uniformly random direction, in the normalised frame. Returns the identity on any degeneracy (a singular H_wi), i.e. no augmentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.perspective_augmentation"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.F_NORM_SCALE","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Calibration frame for the F cells: pixel coordinates scaled by s = 1/25 about the principal point. The Bartoli–Sturm orthonormal chart is near-GAUGE at both natural frames — raw pixels drive σ₂/σ₁ → ~1e-4 (U/V near-co-rotation gauge; chart-coordinate calibration is numerically meaningless there — REPORTED as a finding by the pixel-frame driver cell), and K-normalized coordinates drive σ → 1 exactly (F → E, the near-essential gauge Bartoli–Sturm §5.1.4). s = 1/25 puts the scene family's σ₂/σ₁ in ~[0.04, 0.95] (median 0.22): generically mid-gauge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.F_NORM_SCALE"},{"category":"type","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.CalibrationResult","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Result of one MC calibration cell.\n\nnees_mean, nees_se: sample mean of the NEES and its standard error (χ² theory: sd(mean) = √(2·dof/N) under calibration).\ncoverage: empirical fraction of trials with NEES ≤ χ²(dof) quantile at the levels (0.5, 0.9, 0.95).\nemp_cov, mean_pred: empirical covariance of the tracked chart errors vs the mean predicted covariance (for eigen-ratio diagnostics).\nnfail: trials discarded. For the tracking cells this is the DIVERGENCE count (chart-Newton continuation failed to converge — genuinely ill-conditioned at that noise level) plus the rare degenerate-anchor trials (singular ∂v g at θgt); for refit cells it is fit failures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.CalibrationResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.ContainmentResult","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Result of one output-domain containment cell: rates[i] is the empirical containment fraction of statistic i (H: the 95% transfer ellipse; E/F: [pointwise χ²₁ band, whole-line χ²₂ band]); nfail is the divergence/degeneracy count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.ContainmentResult"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.containment_nominal-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.ContainmentResult, Int64}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"containment_nominal(r, i; level=0.95, z=4.0) -> Bool\n\nBinomial gate: statistic i's empirical containment within z binomial standard errors of level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.containment_nominal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.epipolar_band_trial-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"epipolar_band_trial(model_class::Symbol; σ_px, f=600.0, regime=:generic,\n                    aniso=false, img2_scale=1.0, level=0.95, nspan=40,\n                    npts=minimal, estimate=nothing, covariance=nothing)\n\nCriminisi-protocol output-domain trial for the epipolar models (model_class ∈ (:E, :F)): per trial, perturb the minimal computation correspondences in both images, TRACK the ground-truth-anchored solution on the perturbed constraints, perturb a query point x in image 1, form the MC epipolar line l̂ = θ̂·x̃q, and test it against the analytic H–Z envelope band at θgt whose Σl composes the model term ((x̃ᵀ⊗I₃)·Σθ·(x̃⊗I₃)ᵀ) with the query term (F·σ²Σq̃·Fᵀ). Two statistics per trial: `(pointwise, wholeline)— pointwise at one random station on the nominal line against the χ²₁ band; whole-line over the visible span against the χ²₂ band (the H–Z whole-line convention). Returnsnothingon tracking divergence / degenerate anchor / query line missing the view.npts(default 7 for F, 5 for E),estimate,covariance`: the hooks (see the chart-domain section header).\n\ncameras = (cam1, cam2) (:E only) makes the trial camera-aware: the COMPUTATION correspondences are projected through two real cameras, perturbed in PIXELS with σ_px, and handed to the estimate and the covariance (the hooks, or the defaults) as bearings carrying M₁/M₂. See essential_trial for why the noise model must move with them, and use two different focal lengths.\n\nThe QUERY does not move. An essential matrix's epipolar line is a calibrated-plane object, so the query point, the line and the band stay there, and the query's noise is carried into that plane through view 1's own linear part. Two σ in one trial is not frame MIXING — it is the rule applied once per frame, which is what \"σ moves with the data\" requires of a trial spanning two. The default path uses an identity carrier, so it reduces to the original expression exactly — which is what shows the generalisation did not change the case it generalises.\n\nWhat this trial still CANNOT validate, and why that is a choice\n\nA query point and a band expressed in the z = 1 chart cannot represent a ray at or past 90° from the optical axis — precisely the case the bearing work exists for. So the ESTIMATION side of this trial is now camera-general while its QUERY side remains pinhole-shaped BY CONSTRUCTION, and the harness therefore cannot validate E's output-domain covariance at a fisheye periphery. It is scoped that way deliberately, but it is exactly the kind of gap someone later assumes has been closed because the rest of the trial went camera-aware.\n\nThis is the FIFTH site of the calibrated-plane assumption in the stack — after _mapped_bearing, the deleted 2-vector _bearing lift, cheiral_pose_fast and this harness's own correspondence interface — and the first one kept on purpose rather than removed. The generalisation (a spherical band on the bearing's tangent plane) is a post-release item; until it lands, a fisheye claim about this band is unsupported by anything here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.epipolar_band_trial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.essential_trial-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"essential_trial(; σ_px, f=600.0, regime=:generic, aniso=false, img2_scale=1.0,\n                npts=5, estimate=nothing, covariance=nothing, cameras=nothing)\n\nContinuation-tracking calibration trial for the 5-dof so(3)⊕T_tS² essential chart covariance.\n\nDefault (cameras = nothing): works in K-NORMALIZED coordinates, pixel noise σ_px entering as σ = σ_px/f, as the F and H trials do.\n\nCamera-aware (cameras = (cam1, cam2)): the scene is projected through two real CameraModels, perturbed in PIXELS with σ_px itself, and handed to the estimate and the covariance as BEARING correspondences carrying the M₁/M₂ pixel Jacobians from backproject_with_jacobian — what an essential-matrix problem takes. The default estimate and covariance read those columns (the tangent form of constraint_with_jacobians), so the hooks are optional here as everywhere. The returned covariance is scaled by σ_px², so the noise model moves into the same frame as the data rather than being left behind in calibrated units.\n\nUse two DIFFERENT focal lengths. A per-view M₁/M₂ swap is nearly norm-preserving: measured, it moves a residual by 1.8% at f = 1200/600 but only 0.19% at equal focals, where it would hide inside anything one would call noise.\n\nnpts, estimate, covariance: the hooks (see the section header).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.essential_trial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.fundmat_norm_xform-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"Similarity upx → ucal for the F calibration frame (3×3 homogeneous).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.fundmat_norm_xform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.fundmat_trial-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"fundmat_trial(; σ, regime=:generic, aniso=false, frame=:calibration,\n              img2_scale=1.0, f=600.0, npts=7, estimate=nothing, covariance=nothing)\n\nContinuation-tracking calibration trial for the Bartoli–Sturm 7-dof F chart covariance. frame = :calibration (default) runs tracking + provider + chart NEES in the mid-gauge frame fundmat_norm_xform with noise σ·s; frame = :pixel runs raw — kept to DOCUMENT the pixel-frame chart degeneracy (expected miscalibrated at every σ). npts, estimate, covariance: the hooks (see the section header); f the focal length of the scene.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.fundmat_trial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.h_transfer_trial-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"h_transfer_trial(; σ, regime=:generic, aniso=false, img2_scale=1.0,\n                 level=0.95, npts=4, estimate=nothing, covariance=nothing)\n\nCriminisi Table 4.1 for the homography, containment form: per trial, perturb the 4 computation correspondences IN BOTH IMAGES (known per-endpoint covariances), compute the unique minimal H of the perturbed sample, perturb a query point, transfer it, and test the transferred point against the first-order level ellipse whose covariance composes the model term (transform_deriv_params(H, x) propagating the hypothesis covariance at θgt, transported to the ambient space) with the query term (`Jx·σ²Σq·Jxᵀ). Returns(contained::Bool,)ornothing(degenerate solve).npts,estimate,covariance`: the hooks (see the chart-domain section header).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.h_transfer_trial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.homography_trial-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"homography_trial(; σ, regime=:generic, aniso=false, img2_scale=1.0,\n                 npts=4, estimate=nothing, covariance=nothing)\n\nChart-domain calibration trial for the 8-dof sl(3) homography chart covariance: base 4-point sample with θgt as its exact interpolant, known per-point perturbation, then the UNIQUE minimal solution of the perturbed sample (the 4-point problem is single-valued — no tracking device needed), expressed in the chart anchored at θgt and compared against the implicit-function covariance J·Σ̃x·Jᵀ at θgt. npts, estimate, covariance: the hooks (see the section header).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.homography_trial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.is_calibrated-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.CalibrationResult}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"is_calibrated(r::CalibrationResult; z=4.0) -> Bool\n\nStatistical gate: NEES mean within z standard errors of dof AND every empirical coverage within z binomial standard errors of nominal. z = 4 keeps the false-alarm rate negligible at harness trial counts while still catching a Σ that is wrong by tens of percent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.is_calibrated"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.make_point_covs-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Int64}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"make_point_covs(rng, k; aniso=false, img2_scale=1.0) -> Vector{SMatrix{4,4}}\n\nUnit-scale 4×4 covariance shapes, one per correspondence, block-diagonal over the TWO endpoint slots — noise is injected in BOTH images, each endpoint with its own independent 2×2 covariance (the real-data structure: per-endpoint precision, e.g. RoMa matcher covariances). aniso=true draws per-ENDPOINT rotated anisotropic 2×2 blocks with trace 2, drawn INDEPENDENTLY for the two endpoints (so the two images' covariances differ; trace 2 keeps the total variance of I₂ and σ retains its meaning); aniso=false returns isotropic blocks. img2_scale multiplies the image-2 endpoint's standard deviation (e.g. img2_scale = 3 → σ₂ = 3σ₁, the unequal-image cells).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.make_point_covs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.make_query_cov-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"make_query_cov(rng; aniso=false) -> SMatrix{2,2}\n\nUnit-scale covariance shape for the QUERY point of the output-domain cells (trace-2 rotated-anisotropic when aniso, else I₂) — the observation-point noise of the Criminisi Table 4.1 protocol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.make_query_cov"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.noise_ladder-Tuple{Any, Random.AbstractRNG, Int64, Int64, Any}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"noise_ladder(make_trial, rng, ntrials, dof, σs) -> Vector{Pair{Float64,CalibrationResult}}\n\nRun one calibration cell per noise level: make_trial(σ) must return a trial closure. Reports every rung — including the ones past the first-order breakdown (the first rung failing is_calibrated marks it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.noise_ladder"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.perturb_pairs-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Any, Float64, Any}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"perturb_pairs(rng, cs, σ, shapes) -> Vector{Pair}\n\nAdd δ ~ N(0, σ²·shapes[i]) to each correspondence: to both endpoints of a two-view pair (4×4 shapes), or to the image point of a pose correspondence X => u (2×2 shapes; the 3D point is exact).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.perturb_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.pnp_trial-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"pnp_trial(; σ_px, npts, f=600.0, c=250.0, aniso=false, estimate=nothing, covariance=nothing)\np3p_trial(; σ_px, kwargs...) = pnp_trial(; σ_px, npts = 3, kwargs...)\n\nChart-domain calibration trial for the 6-dof pose covariance: a sample_pose_scene, per-point image noise (unit-scale 2×2 shapes, make_query_cov's law), and the estimate in the chart anchored at θgt. Defaults: the minimal P3P solution TRACKED from θgt (the P3P problem is multi-rooted; tracking keeps the trial single-valued, as for E and F) against the implicit-function covariance; with npts > 3, the tracked reprojection Gauss–Newton solution against implicit_covariance's sandwich. f sets the field of view (f = 2000 narrow, f = 150 wide at c = 250). The hooks as for the two-view trials; estimate(ch, cs_noisy) returns an EuclideanMap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.pnp_trial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.reprojection_trial-Tuple{}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"reprojection_trial(; σ_px, npts=3, f=600.0, c=250.0, aniso=false, level=0.95,\n                   estimate=nothing, covariance=nothing)\n\nCriminisi-protocol output-domain trial for the pose: estimate the pose from npts noisy image points (as pnp_trial), then reproject a HELD-OUT 3D point and test its reprojection against its own noisy observation, inside the first-order level ellipse composing the model term (J·σ²Σ̃_v·Jᵀ, J the reprojection's derivative in the pose chart at θgt) with the query term (`σ²Σq). Returns(contained::Bool,)ornothing` (divergence / degenerate anchor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.reprojection_trial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.run_calibration-Tuple{Any, Random.AbstractRNG, Int64, Int64}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"run_calibration(trial, rng, ntrials, dof; cond_cap=Inf) -> CalibrationResult\n\nRun ntrials MC trials of trial(rng) -> nothing | (v̂, Σ_pred) and gather NEES / coverage statistics against χ²(dof). See the module header for the trial-closure contract.\n\ncond_cap: with a finite value, the NEES is evaluated on the eigen-subspace of Σpred with λ ≥ λmax/cond_cap and compared against χ²(effective dof) — per trial. This is a numerical safeguard for problems whose predicted chart covariances are anisotropic beyond Float64 reach (predicted standard deviations in the stiffest directions below what the arithmetic of the tracked solve can realize — there the full-dof NEES measures ROUNDOFF, not statistics). The capped statistic states calibration on the numerically representable uncertainty subspace; the discarded-direction count is visible as dof < declared dof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.run_calibration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.run_containment-Tuple{Any, Random.AbstractRNG, Int64}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"run_containment(trial, rng, ntrials) -> ContainmentResult\n\nAggregate ntrials output-domain MC trials of trial(rng) -> nothing | NTuple{K,Bool} (contained / not, per statistic).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.run_containment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.sample_homography_scene-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"sample_homography_scene(rng; regime=:generic, npts=4) -> (H::HomographyMat, cs)\n\nGround-truth homography + npts source points (the first 4 well-spread, the rest uniform) in a nominal 500×500 image. Regimes: :generic (strong projective part), :near_affine (projective row ~1e-8 — the near-degenerate regime for the 8-dof chart's projective directions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.sample_homography_scene"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.sample_pose_scene-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Int64}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"sample_pose_scene(rng, n; f = 600.0, c = 250.0) -> (pose::EuclideanMap{3}, cs_nm, cs_px)\n\nA world→camera pose and n world points imaged by K = [f 0 c; 0 f c; 0 0 1]: the first ≤ 9 points one per cell of a 3×3 grid over the 2c × 2c image (as sample_twoview_scene stratifies, and for the same reason), depths 4–8, the rotation uniform. cs_nm pairs X => u with u calibrated (K⁻¹), cs_px with u in pixels. f sets the field of view: f = 2000 is narrow (14° at c = 250), f = 150 wide (118°).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.sample_pose_scene"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.sample_twoview_scene-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Int64}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"sample_twoview_scene(rng, k; regime=:generic, f=600.0, c=250.0)\n    -> (R, t, F::FundMat, E::EssentialMat, cs_px, cs_norm, cs_bear)\n\nTwo calibrated views (shared K = diag(f, f, 1) + principal point c) observing k random 3D points; returns pixel correspondences (for F), K-normalized GNOMONIC correspondences, and UNIT BEARING correspondences (Vec3 each side, for E).\n\ncs_bear is the calibrated currency of this stack: E, relative pose and PnP act on bearings, never on the z = 1 chart. cs_norm is the chart and is kept only for the Monte-Carlo cells that have not yet moved to the tangent Sampson; those cells migrate with the E gates, and it goes with them. New code should take cs_bear.\n\nAppended rather than substituted on purpose: Julia's destructuring ignores trailing elements, so every existing six-way call site keeps working and nothing silently changes meaning under a positional unpack. Regimes: :generic — baseline 1, depths 4–8, rotation ~10°; :short_baseline — ‖t‖ = 0.02 vs depths 4–8 (near-degenerate E); :near_planar — depth variation 1e-3 about a plane (near-degenerate F); :forward — translation along camera 1's optical axis (t ∝ (0.1·n₁, 0.1·n₂, ±1)), both epipoles inside the 2c × 2c image (geometries resampled until they are), and a point whose PARALLAX — its displacement from the rotation-only image π(K·R·K⁻¹·ũ₁) — is under 1 px resampled: near the epipole a correspondence carries no epipolar information, and a trial built on one fails for reasons that are not the covariance's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.sample_twoview_scene"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.second_order_moments-Tuple{Any, Any, Int64, AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"second_order_moments(res, out, D, x0, q0, Σδ) -> (Σ1, μ2, Σ2)\n\nFirst- and second-order propagated moments of the output functional out([v; q]) under the implicitly defined chart solution v(x) of res([v; x]) = 0 (D equations, res([0; x0]) = 0 at the anchor), with Gaussian input δ = (x − x0, q − q0) ~ N(0, Σδ).\n\nReturns the first-order output covariance Σ1 = J·Σδ·Jᵀ, the second-order mean shift μ2[c] = ½·tr(Q_c·Σδ), and the second-order covariance Σ2[c,d] = Σ1[c,d] + ½·tr(Q_c·Σδ·Q_d·Σδ) (Gaussian fourth-moment identity), where Q_c is the full second derivative of output component c with respect to δ, assembled from the implicit first/second derivatives of v(x) (implicit function theorem, differentiated twice) and the ForwardDiff Hessians of res and out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.second_order_moments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/synthetic/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.track_manifold_solution-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Synthetic Data","text":"track_manifold_solution(ch, cs_clean, cs_noisy;\n                     stages=4, tol=1e-11, maxiter=50, min_dt=1/64)\n    -> SVector{tangent_size(ch)} | nothing\n\nDamped chart-Newton CONTINUATION of the local constrained solution: the observations are moved from cs_clean (on the constraint variety — the anchor of ch solves g exactly there) to cs_noisy along the linear data homotopy, each stage solved by damped Newton on g(v, xt) = 0 warm-started at the previous stage's solution. The stage size starts at 1/stages and is BISECTED adaptively whenever a stage fails to converge (standard continuation practice — strong constraint curvature shrinks the per-stage Newton basin). Returns the tracked chart coordinates of the local solution of g(v, xnoisy) = 0, or nothing when a stage still fails at min_dt — the DIVERGENCE event reported by the calibration tables: at that noise level the perturbation left the continuation radius of this sample's geometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic.track_manifold_solution"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lm_core/#Chart-LM-API","page":"Chart LM","text":"The chart-rows contract and the stack's one Levenberg–Marquardt (NLLSsolver, through one adapter): its stop settings (LmStops, with the damping strategy and the data's residual resolution), the chart, robust and multi-block solves (lmsolve_chart!, LmProblem), the one success rule, and the device LM (static_lm, StaticLmStops).","title":"Chart LM API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lm_core/#Chart-Rows-and-the-LM-Core","page":"Chart LM","text":"","title":"Chart Rows and the LM Core"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lm_core/#Index","page":"Chart LM","text":"Pages = [\"lm_core.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RowsWorkspace","page":"Chart LM","text":"RowsWorkspace{D, C}\nRowsWorkspace{D, C}(n)\n\nThe whitened residual rows of a D-dof chart with codimension-C residuals for n points: the stacked rows r and their Jacobian J (C·n × D, row-contiguous), the per-point residual norms rn (the codim-2 currency), the per-iteration participation mask valid and a unit √weight vector onev for the row kernels. rows_workspace builds one per (task, size).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RowsWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_init","page":"Chart LM","text":"The chart at the model model (a provider's starting anchor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_init"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_jacobian!","page":"Chart LM","text":"Fill the provider's rows' Jacobian at the chart ch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_jacobian!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_model","page":"Chart LM","text":"The model at the chart ch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_refresh!","page":"Chart LM","text":"Fill the provider's rows (and, at codimension 2, the per-point norms) at the chart ch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_refresh!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_residuals","page":"Chart LM","text":"The provider's residual currency: the rows at codimension 1, the per-point norms at 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_residuals"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_retract","page":"Chart LM","text":"The chart re-anchored at the tangent step Δθ from ch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_retract"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_rows","page":"Chart LM","text":"The provider's rows, a RowsWorkspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_size","page":"Chart LM","text":"The provider's point count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_valid-Tuple{Any}","page":"Chart LM","text":"The provider's per-iteration participation mask, or nothing (every point participates).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_valid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.point_norms!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Int64, Int64}","page":"Chart LM","text":"point_norms!(rn, r, n, d) -> rn\n\nPer-point norms of d-row residual groups: rn[i] = ‖r[d(i−1)+1 : d·i]‖.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.point_norms!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rows_provider","page":"Chart LM","text":"rows_provider(problem) -> prov\n\nThe chart-rows provider of a chart problem for one local-optimization call (see the contract at the head of this file). The problem's owner implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rows_provider"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rows_workspace-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{Val{D}, Val{C}, Int64}} where {D, C}","page":"Chart LM","text":"rows_workspace(::Val{D}, ::Val{C}, n) -> RowsWorkspace{D, C}\n\nThe rows of n points under the one reuse policy (task_buffers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rows_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.task_buffers-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Chart LM","text":"task_buffers(alloc, key)\n\nThe buffer set stored under key in the current task's local storage, built by alloc() on first use. A workspace keyed by its type and sizes is then built once per (task, size) and reused by every later call of that size on that task — task-local, so threaded callers (Threads.@threads bodies are tasks) never share one. The one memo every per-call scratch workspace of the stack draws from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.task_buffers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.task_buffers-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{Type{V}, Any, K}} where {V, K}","page":"Chart LM","text":"task_buffers(::Type{V}, alloc, key) -> V\n\nThe typed form: the buffer set of type V stored under key (a tuple of sizes, any isbits value) in the current task, built by alloc() on first use. Allocation-free on reuse: the task's storage is keyed by a singleton per (V, typeof(key)) and holds a Dict{typeof(key), V}, so no key is boxed. The form every per-call scratch with a size in its key uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.task_buffers"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Eliminable","page":"Chart LM","text":"Eliminable{E}(value)\n\nAn NLLSsolver variable — a Core Manifold, an SVector, any value with NLLSsolver's nvars/update — that the solver may ELIMINATE (E = true): the Schur-complement linear system eliminates every such variable (a bundle's structure points, a rotation bundle's track directions) before it solves for the rest. nvars and update forward to value (an update re-wraps the updated value); iseliminable is declared only where the installed NLLSsolver has the Schur system (nlls_features().schur), and Eliminable{false} is the same variable left in the full sparse LDL — what a solver without the Schur system takes, and a fallback test forces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Eliminable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LevenbergDamping","page":"Chart LM","text":"MarquardtDamping()\nLevenbergDamping()\n\nThe damping of the host Levenberg–Marquardt step, a strategy TYPE of LmStops and LmProblem (dispatch, not a flag), each NLLSsolver's own public iterator:\n\nMarquardtDamping() (the default): λ·DᵀD, DᵀD the running maximum of diag(JᵀJ) floored at 1e-8 of its largest entry — NLLSsolver's :levenbergmarquardt_dtd; Core 0.10's damping, bit for bit;\nLevenbergDamping(): λ·I — NLLSsolver's :levenbergmarquardt (RadiometricCamera 0.8.0's).\n\nMeasured (Core 0.10.2, 2026-11-14, six iterations of RC's fisheye, Tamron and grid refinements and a non-central origin fit): neither wins everywhere. λ·DᵀD reaches a lower cost on the fisheye case (1711 against 1776), while λ·I converges where DᵀD's weak columns stall (the non-central fit: a cost ratio of 1.7e-18 against DᵀD's 1.2e-8 after 25 iterations, 9.0e-9 after 125), and on the Tamron and grid cases it reaches a slightly lower cost 1.6–1.8× faster (λ·DᵀD rejects more candidate steps). An UNFLOORED running max (Moré's scaling, NLLSsolver's DᵀD without its floor) was measured and rejected: a column whose diag(JᵀJ) is zero leaves H + λDᵀD singular (the grid and non-central factorizations fail), and it is no faster than the floored DᵀD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LevenbergDamping"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LmDamping","page":"Chart LM","text":"MarquardtDamping()\nLevenbergDamping()\n\nThe damping of the host Levenberg–Marquardt step, a strategy TYPE of LmStops and LmProblem (dispatch, not a flag), each NLLSsolver's own public iterator:\n\nMarquardtDamping() (the default): λ·DᵀD, DᵀD the running maximum of diag(JᵀJ) floored at 1e-8 of its largest entry — NLLSsolver's :levenbergmarquardt_dtd; Core 0.10's damping, bit for bit;\nLevenbergDamping(): λ·I — NLLSsolver's :levenbergmarquardt (RadiometricCamera 0.8.0's).\n\nMeasured (Core 0.10.2, 2026-11-14, six iterations of RC's fisheye, Tamron and grid refinements and a non-central origin fit): neither wins everywhere. λ·DᵀD reaches a lower cost on the fisheye case (1711 against 1776), while λ·I converges where DᵀD's weak columns stall (the non-central fit: a cost ratio of 1.7e-18 against DᵀD's 1.2e-8 after 25 iterations, 9.0e-9 after 125), and on the Tamron and grid cases it reaches a slightly lower cost 1.6–1.8× faster (λ·DᵀD rejects more candidate steps). An UNFLOORED running max (Moré's scaling, NLLSsolver's DᵀD without its floor) was measured and rejected: a column whose diag(JᵀJ) is zero leaves H + λDᵀD singular (the grid and non-central factorizations fail), and it is no faster than the floored DᵀD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LmDamping"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LmProblem","page":"Chart LM","text":"LmProblem(variables::AbstractVector, blocks::AbstractVector; fixed = nothing, damping = MarquardtDamping())\n\nAn NLLSsolver problem as the stack's one LM adapter (lmsolve_chart!) solves it: variables (any NLLSsolver variables — Core Manifolds, SVectors, Eliminables — of one type or several) and the residual blocks (NLLSsolver AbstractResiduals, of one type or several, each with its own varindices and its robustkernel: NLLSsolver.NoRobust(), or a frozen loss through loss_robustifier). The problem is built once, with NLLSsolver's typed OptimizeCache (the linear system's layout — the Schur system where the variables allow it), and kept: a later solve on the same structure (a RANSAC refit's bundle) rebuilds neither.\n\np.variables is the problem's variable vector: set the start in place before a solve, read the solution after it (NLLSsolver swaps its buffers, so read it through p, never through a saved reference). p.blocks are the blocks; the residual statistics of a solve are nlls_rows_and_jacobian(p.blocks, p.variables).\n\nfixed holds variables: the indices into variables (an Integer or a collection of them) of those the solve keeps as constants the blocks read (NLLSsolver's unfixed mask); the stationarity postcondition reads the free variables' columns only. nothing (the default) holds none, the problem as without the keyword, bit for bit. An index outside variables, or every variable held, is an ArgumentError.\n\ndamping (MarquardtDamping, the default, or LevenbergDamping) is the damping the problem's kept NLLSsolver cache is built for: solve it with the same LmStops(; damping) (another damping is correct but rebuilds the cache, as a changed problem does).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LmProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LmStops","page":"Chart LM","text":"LmStops(; max_iter, ftol, xtol, stationarity_tol, max_time = nothing, damping = MarquardtDamping(), residual_resolution = 0)\n\nTHE stop settings of every host Levenberg–Marquardt fit of the stack — the one owner of its knobs, which lmsolve_chart! maps onto NLLSsolver's options in one place. Every keyword defaults to Core's configuration of record (configs/default.toml [lm]); a fit overrides one only from its own configuration table.\n\nknob meaning NLLSsolver\nmax_iter the iteration cap ([lm] 1000) maxiters: outer iterations, one ACCEPTED step each; an iteration's damping loop may reject ~8–10 candidate steps first, so the cap bounds accepted steps, not work\nftol relative cost tolerance ([lm] 1e-10): stop once a step lowers the cost by less than ftol·F (a relative tolerance: never the exact-fit test, whose threshold is the run's own, _exact_fit_threshold) reldcost\nxtol step tolerance ([lm] 1e-10) dstep: ABSOLUTE, the step's max-norm in chart units (max|pᵢ| < xtol)\nstationarity_tol the stationarity postcondition's column cosine ([lm] 0.05) — (the stack's own test, is_stationary)\nmax_time wall-clock cap in seconds; nothing (the default) or Inf: none maxtime\nresidual_resolution the residual norm the DATA resolve (0, the default: the run's own resolution alone). The success rule's exact-fit test reads it — a cost below its square is zero to what the data resolve, where the rows' rounding points in no direction and the cosine says nothing — and nothing else: the iteration's stops are unchanged. A caller states it DERIVED (the data's noise in the residual's units), never tuned — (the stack's own test; StaticLmStops' residual_resolution)\ndamping the step's damping, a strategy type: MarquardtDamping (the default, λ·DᵀD) or LevenbergDamping (λ·I) iterator: :levenbergmarquardt_dtd / :levenbergmarquardt\n— absolute cost decrease absdcost = 0: the stack has no such knob\n— consecutive failed iterations maxfails = 3, NLLSsolver's default\n\nThe initial λ is NLLSsolver's (1e-6), not a knob. Single-threaded (numthreads = 1): a fit runs inside the caller's threads. There is no gradient tolerance: success is the stationarity postcondition's (lmsolve_chart!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LmStops"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ManifoldLmWorkspace","page":"Chart LM","text":"ManifoldLmWorkspace{N}\nManifoldLmWorkspace(n, N)\n\nThe buffers of the chart LM (lmsolve_chart!) at n residual rows and an N-dimensional chart: the per-row √weights the caller's refit fills, the residual and n × N Jacobian buffers the rows are evaluated into, the stall postcondition's scratch, and the NLLSsolver problem the adapter builds on first use (and keeps, with NLLSsolver's internal data, for every later refit on a linearization state of the same type) — allocated once per problem instead of per LM iteration. N is the model manifold's tangent size (essential 5, fundamental 7, homography 8 in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation). The residual SEMANTICS come entirely from the caller's linearization state — Sampson distance for E/F, whitened transfer error for H, calibrated reprojection for P3P — which is why the type is named for the manifold, not the distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ManifoldLmWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MarquardtDamping","page":"Chart LM","text":"MarquardtDamping()\nLevenbergDamping()\n\nThe damping of the host Levenberg–Marquardt step, a strategy TYPE of LmStops and LmProblem (dispatch, not a flag), each NLLSsolver's own public iterator:\n\nMarquardtDamping() (the default): λ·DᵀD, DᵀD the running maximum of diag(JᵀJ) floored at 1e-8 of its largest entry — NLLSsolver's :levenbergmarquardt_dtd; Core 0.10's damping, bit for bit;\nLevenbergDamping(): λ·I — NLLSsolver's :levenbergmarquardt (RadiometricCamera 0.8.0's).\n\nMeasured (Core 0.10.2, 2026-11-14, six iterations of RC's fisheye, Tamron and grid refinements and a non-central origin fit): neither wins everywhere. λ·DᵀD reaches a lower cost on the fisheye case (1711 against 1776), while λ·I converges where DᵀD's weak columns stall (the non-central fit: a cost ratio of 1.7e-18 against DᵀD's 1.2e-8 after 25 iterations, 9.0e-9 after 125), and on the Tamron and grid cases it reaches a slightly lower cost 1.6–1.8× faster (λ·DᵀD rejects more candidate steps). An UNFLOORED running max (Moré's scaling, NLLSsolver's DᵀD without its floor) was measured and rejected: a column whose diag(JᵀJ) is zero leaves H + λDᵀD singular (the grid and non-central factorizations fail), and it is no faster than the floored DᵀD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MarquardtDamping"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StaticLmStops","page":"Chart LM","text":"StaticLmStops(scale::SVector{N, T}; acceptance = Inf, residual_scale = 1, cost_floor = 0, residual_resolution = 0, max_iter, stationarity_tol)\n\nTHE stop settings of the device LM static_lm (isbits: a kernel takes it as an argument), owned with the host LM's (LmStops): every keyword has a default of record — max_iter configs/default.toml [lm] device_max_iter, acceptance [lm] device_acceptance, stationarity_tol [lm] stationarity_tol (the ONE success rule's cosine, the host's), and the floors' neutral values.\n\nscale: the problem's unit per coordinate, in which the step tolerance (1e-10·(eps(T)/eps(Float64)) ./ scale, RELATIVE to the coordinate) and the DᵀD clamp ([1e-12, 1e16]·scale²) are read.\nacceptance: the cost a solution must be below, F < acceptance — a root-finding caller's residual bound; by default [lm] device_acceptance, Inf: success by the stationarity rule alone.\nresidual_scale: the residual's scale fs, so the absolute cost and gradient floors are what T resolves of it, (16 eps(T) fs)².\ncost_floor: the absolute floor under those (0: resolved to rounding; the spline lenses' reference routine stops at eps(Float64)).\nresidual_resolution: the residual norm the DATA resolve, when they carry less precision than T (a Float32 ray or Float32 controls in a Float64 solve: 16 eps(Float32) of a unit residual). The success rule's exact-fit test reads it — a cost below its square is zero to what the data resolve, where the rows' rounding points in no direction and the cosine says nothing — and nothing else: the iteration's stops stay T's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StaticLmStops"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.finite_cost-Tuple{Float64}","page":"Chart LM","text":"finite_cost(c::Float64) -> Float64\n\nA cost as the stack's NLLSsolver fits read it: c when finite, +Inf otherwise. NLLSsolver (≥ 5.3.1) accepts a trial at a step below xtol whatever its cost and asserts cost == cost_ per iteration, which NaN fails; +Inf compares equal and is rejected like NaN everywhere else. Every NLLSsolver residual block of the stack returns its cost through it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.finite_cost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_stationary-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Chart LM","text":"is_stationary(J::AbstractMatrix, f::AbstractVector; tol, ftol) -> Bool\n\nThe stall postcondition of lmsolve_chart! for a fit the chart LM does not run (a block-structured NLLSsolver problem, nlls_termination's postcondition): whether the point at which the residual f and its Jacobian J were evaluated is a stationary point of F = ‖f‖² — F < ftol (an exact fit, whose residual points in no particular direction), or every column Jⱼ numerically orthogonal to f, max_j |Jⱼᵀ f| / (‖Jⱼ‖ ‖f‖) ≤ tol (scale-invariant in the residuals and in each unknown; an all-zero column is skipped, a NaN fails). A fit whose solver stopped without this holding stopped where its damping grew, not at a minimizer: its retcode is :stalled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_stationary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_stationary-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{Bool}}","page":"Chart LM","text":"is_stationary(blocks, variables, free::AbstractVector{Bool}; tol, ftol) -> Bool\n\nThe same stall postcondition for an NLLSsolver problem's residual blocks at its variables, in the chart coordinates of the variables free marks (a held variable's columns are left out, as the solver held them): the cost NLLSsolver minimizes, F = Σₖ ρ(‖rₖ‖²) (ρ each block's robustkernel, the identity for a plain block), is stationary when Σₖ ‖rₖ‖² < ftol (or Σₖ ρ′ₖ‖rₖ‖² < ftol with some ρ′ₖ > 0), or when every free column of the √ρ′-weighted system is numerically orthogonal to its rows, max_j |gⱼ| / (√wⱼ ‖√ρ′ r‖) ≤ tol with gⱼ = Σₖ ρ′ₖ Jₖⱼᵀ rₖ and wⱼ = Σₖ ρ′ₖ ‖Jₖⱼ‖² — the test the matrix method above makes of the stacked rows √ρ′ₖ rₖ and Jacobian √ρ′ₖ Jₖ, and exactly the robust chart LM's (lmsolve_chart!): ρ′ clamped at 0, the unweighted cost below ftol an exact fit, and a plateau (every block past a redescending loss's cutoff, ρ′ = 0 throughout) NOT stationary. The rows and their Jacobian (NLLSsolver.computeresjac of each block with all its variables, as nlls_rows_and_jacobian evaluates them, a held variable's columns skipped) are accumulated block by block into g, w and F: the problem's Jacobian is never held.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_stationary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lmsolve_chart!-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ManifoldLmWorkspace{N}, L}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ManifoldLmWorkspace{N}, L, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LmStops}} where {N, L}","page":"Chart LM","text":"lmsolve_chart!(ews::ManifoldLmWorkspace{N}, lm, stops::LmStops = LmStops())\n    -> (x::SVector{N}, F, converged, iterations, (code, rule))\nlmsolve_chart!(ews::ManifoldLmWorkspace{N}, lm, robustifier, ::Val{C}, stops::LmStops = LmStops())\n    -> (x::SVector{N}, F, converged, iterations, (code, rule))\nlmsolve_chart!(p::LmProblem, stops::LmStops = LmStops(); callback = nothing)\n    -> (; code::ReturnCode.T, rule::StopRule, iterations::Int, cost::Float64)\n\nTHE stack's host Levenberg–Marquardt: NLLSsolver (Marquardt's DᵀD damping) under the stop settings stops (LmStops, the one owner of the knobs), on a kept problem and NLLSsolver cache, with ONE success rule.\n\nTHE CHART LM minimizes ‖r(x)‖², r the residual_rows! of the linearization state lm (its Jacobian residual_jacobian_rows!, both read through the state's two reductions), from the chart origin x = 0.\nTHE ROBUSTIFIED CHART LM minimizes Σᵢ ρ(‖rᵢ(x)‖²) from the chart origin, rᵢ datum i's C consecutive rows (C the codimension) and ρ the NLLSsolver robustifier — a stack loss through loss_robustifier(shape, loss_width), applied per datum (NLLSsolver's robustified Hessian), so one solve is the robust M-estimate. F is Σᵢ ρ(‖rᵢ‖²) at x; on return the workspace's r holds the unweighted rows at x.\nTHE MULTI-BLOCK LM solves an LmProblem — heterogeneous variables and residual blocks, each block with its own robustifier — in place in p.variables; cost is Σₖ ρ(‖rₖ‖²) (‖r‖² without robustifiers), callback an NLLSsolver iteration callback (nothing: none). An indefinite or singular normal system in NLLSsolver's step solve (PosDefException, SingularException, ZeroPivotException, LAPACKException) is a failed solve, (ReturnCode.Failure, NonFinite), the variables as the solver left them.\n\nThe chart forms keep the problem and NLLSsolver's OptimizeCache in the workspace (one caching path, plain and robust alike), so a refit rebuilds neither. x is the solution chart vector, F the final cost, converged the stop code (nlls_stop_code's, or -2 for a stop the postcondition fails), iterations NLLSsolver's count (one accepted step each; a start at a stationary point takes one zero step, and only a start whose cost or gradient is not finite stops at 0, :solve_failed), and (code, rule) the termination record, a ReturnCode and a StopRule (nlls_termination's decode).\n\nTHE SUCCESS RULE. A stop is a success only at a STATIONARY point: for the cost-decrease stop, the step-size stop and the rejection cap alike, stationarity is TESTED at the returned point, and a stop that fails it is ReturnCode.Stalled (converged = -2) with the rule that fired. A tiny cost decrease or a tiny step proves nothing: a run whose damping grew until no step could lower the cost (a wrong-sign Jacobian; a NaN wall the minimizer lies behind) stops there. Stationary means the cost is zero by the exact-fit test — below the run's own resolution of its starting cost F₀, F < m·ε·F₀ over m residuals, or below the square of the residual norm the data resolve, stops.residual_resolution (0 by default; stops.ftol is the relative cost-decrease stop, never this test). A run that STARTS at an exact fit (a restart from a solve's minimum, F₀ itself rounding) is resolved only by the latter: with the default it is Stalled. Otherwise stationary means every column of the Jacobian is numerically orthogonal to the residual, max_j |Jⱼᵀr| / (‖Jⱼ‖ ‖r‖) ≤ stops.stationarity_tol (√ρ′-weighted per datum or block when robust: is_stationary). The iteration and time caps are MaxIters and MaxTime, a failed solve Failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lmsolve_chart!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_robustifier-Tuple{AbstractLossShape, Real}","page":"Chart LM","text":"loss_robustifier(shape::AbstractLossShape, loss_width::Real)\n\nNLLSsolver's robustifier (an NLLSsolver.AbstractRobustifier) for the loss shape at loss_width. NLLSsolver hands a robustifier the squared residual norm cost and reads robustify(k, cost) = ρ and robustifydcost(k, cost) = (ρ, ρ′, ρ″); here ρ is Core's ρ(shape, cost, loss_width²) in the stack's convention ρ′(0) = w_max (no factor ½), ρ′ its weight and ρ″ the derivative of that weight (Core has no closed ρ″ per shape; ForwardDiff differentiates the weight).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_robustifier"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nlls_features-Tuple{}","page":"Chart LM","text":"nlls_features() -> (; schur::Bool)\n\nWhether the loaded NLLSsolver has the Schur-complement linear system for eliminable variables (NLLSsolver.MultiVariateLSschur, NLLSsolver.iseliminable; upstream pull request #60, open). Until #60 merges a registered NLLSsolver lacks it and the bundle takes NLLSsolver's sparse LDL over every unknown — the same optimum, slower; the stack's fork has it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nlls_features"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nlls_rows_and_jacobian-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Chart LM","text":"nlls_rows_and_jacobian(blocks, variables) -> (r, J::SparseMatrixCSC)\n\nThe rows of NLLSsolver residual blocks at variables (an NLLSsolver problem's variable vector) and their sparse Jacobian in the variables' chart coordinates — NLLSsolver.computeresjac of every block with all its variables free — the rows in block order, each variable's NLLSsolver.nvars columns in variable order (a run-time size included). What nlls_termination's stall postcondition reads, and a covariance or a GCV linearization of the same fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nlls_rows_and_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nlls_stop_reasons-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Chart LM","text":"nlls_stop_reasons(bits::Integer) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nEvery reason NLLSsolver's termination bits (result.termination) state, in bit order: :cost_infinite (0), :cost_nan (1), :relative_cost_decrease (2, below reldcost), :absolute_cost_decrease (3, below absdcost), :delta_infinite (4), :delta_nan (5), :delta_size (6, the LM update δ below dstep), :max_consecutive_rejections (7, maxfails iterations whose cost exceeded the best), :max_iterations (8), :max_time (9), :cost_disagreement (10, the residual and the residual-Jacobian evaluations disagree), :nonfinite_start (11, the start point's cost or gradient is not finite: no step is tried), :nonfinite_gradient (12, the gradient at an accepted point is not finite: the solve stops there, its best point kept), :callback (16). A report of the stop; nlls_stop_code is the one decision read from the same bits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nlls_stop_reasons"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_cost-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Chart LM","text":"reduce_cost(partials::AbstractMatrix) -> ½‖r‖²\n\nresidual_cost!'s value for a device state: half the sum of row 1 of its per-work-item partial sums (the layout of reduce_normal_equations; only row 1 is read, so a cost kernel may write a 1 × W matrix), summed on the array's backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_cost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_cost-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Vararg{Any, M}}} where {F, M}","page":"Chart LM","text":"reduce_cost(row!, args...; partials) -> ½‖r‖²\n\nA device LM state's residual_cost! in one call: row!(partials, w, args...) for every work item w as one kernel on partials' backend (as reduce_normal_equations's row form), then reduce_cost(partials) (row 1: each work item's Σ ‖rᵢ‖²). partials is overwritten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_cost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_normal_equations-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Val{N}, Vararg{Any, M}}} where {F, N, M}","page":"Chart LM","text":"reduce_normal_equations(row!, ::Val{N}, args...; partials) -> (½‖r‖², Jᵀr::SVector{N}, JᵀJ::SMatrix{N, N})\n\nA device LM state's residual_normal_equations! in one call: row!(partials, w, args...) for every work item w (each column of partials) as one kernel on partials' backend — the stack's one row launch, KernelAbstractions on every backend, the CPU's included, so the sums are the same bits on a device — then the columns reduced (reduce_normal_equations(partials, Val(N))). row! writes work item w's share into column w in that layout (1 + N + N² rows). partials is the state's scratch (its column count the work items), overwritten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_normal_equations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_normal_equations-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Val{N}}} where N","page":"Chart LM","text":"reduce_normal_equations(partials::AbstractMatrix, ::Val{N}) -> (½‖r‖², Jᵀr::SVector{N}, JᵀJ::SMatrix{N, N})\n\nresidual_normal_equations!'s value for a device state, from its per-work-item partial sums. Column w of partials is work item w's share over its rows, 1 + N + N² entries: Σ ‖rᵢ‖², then the N of Σ Jᵢᵀrᵢ, then the N × N of Σ JᵢᵀJᵢ, column-major. The columns are summed where they are, on the array's backend (sum(partials; dims = 2)), and only the 1 + N + N² totals come to the host. The result is isbits. The cost alone is reduce_cost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.reduce_normal_equations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_cost!","page":"Chart LM","text":"residual_cost!(r, lm, θ) -> ½‖r(θ)‖²\nresidual_normal_equations!(r, J, lm, θ::SVector{N}) -> (½‖r‖², Jᵀr::SVector{N}, JᵀJ::SMatrix{N, N})\n\nTHE CHART LM'S TWO REDUCTIONS of the rows: what lmsolve_chart! reads.\n\nresidual_cost! gives the cost at a trial point.\nresidual_normal_equations! gives the cost, the gradient and the Gauss–Newton Hessian at a linearization. It also feeds the stall postcondition, whose cosine |Jⱼᵀr| / (‖Jⱼ‖‖r‖) reads g and diag(H).\n\nThe defaults evaluate residual_rows! and residual_jacobian_rows! into the caller's buffers r and J, and reduce on the host.\n\nA state whose rows live on a DEVICE overrides both with one kernel that evaluates every row and its Jacobian and reduces them where they are. The rows never reach the host; r and J go unused. Only the N × N step is the host's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_cost!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_jacobian_rows!","page":"Chart LM","text":"residual_rows!(r, lm, θ) -> r\nresidual_jacobian_rows!(J, lm, θ) -> J\n\nTHE CHART-LM PROTOCOL — one pair for every model: the residual and Jacobian rows the chart LM, NLLSsolver, minimizes in lmsolve_chart!.\n\nlm is the per-call LINEARIZATION STATE a refit builds once: the anchor the chart is centred on, the whitening frozen there, the data views and the √weights. θ is the CHART vector, not a model. The rows are √w-WEIGHTED and live in chart coordinates — a different currency from a RANSAC problem's UNWEIGHTED whitened_residuals!(r, problem, model) in data units. Two names, because one name with two currencies is what the frame-in = frame-out rule exists to prevent.\n\nCeres analogue: together they are what a CostFunction::Evaluate fills — residual_rows! the residuals buffer, residual_jacobian_rows! the jacobians — with the manifold's retract_jacobian (Ceres' PlusJacobian) already folded into the chart directions. GTSAM's nearest equivalent is a factor's linearize(values), which returns the same rows and Jacobian as a JacobianFactor at the linearization point rather than filling caller buffers.\n\nBoth write into caller-owned buffers and return them; neither allocates. Methods live with the models (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's problems).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_jacobian_rows!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_normal_equations!","page":"Chart LM","text":"residual_cost!(r, lm, θ) -> ½‖r(θ)‖²\nresidual_normal_equations!(r, J, lm, θ::SVector{N}) -> (½‖r‖², Jᵀr::SVector{N}, JᵀJ::SMatrix{N, N})\n\nTHE CHART LM'S TWO REDUCTIONS of the rows: what lmsolve_chart! reads.\n\nresidual_cost! gives the cost at a trial point.\nresidual_normal_equations! gives the cost, the gradient and the Gauss–Newton Hessian at a linearization. It also feeds the stall postcondition, whose cosine |Jⱼᵀr| / (‖Jⱼ‖‖r‖) reads g and diag(H).\n\nThe defaults evaluate residual_rows! and residual_jacobian_rows! into the caller's buffers r and J, and reduce on the host.\n\nA state whose rows live on a DEVICE overrides both with one kernel that evaluates every row and its Jacobian and reduces them where they are. The rows never reach the host; r and J go unused. Only the N × N step is the host's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_normal_equations!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_rows!","page":"Chart LM","text":"residual_rows!(r, lm, θ) -> r\nresidual_jacobian_rows!(J, lm, θ) -> J\n\nTHE CHART-LM PROTOCOL — one pair for every model: the residual and Jacobian rows the chart LM, NLLSsolver, minimizes in lmsolve_chart!.\n\nlm is the per-call LINEARIZATION STATE a refit builds once: the anchor the chart is centred on, the whitening frozen there, the data views and the √weights. θ is the CHART vector, not a model. The rows are √w-WEIGHTED and live in chart coordinates — a different currency from a RANSAC problem's UNWEIGHTED whitened_residuals!(r, problem, model) in data units. Two names, because one name with two currencies is what the frame-in = frame-out rule exists to prevent.\n\nCeres analogue: together they are what a CostFunction::Evaluate fills — residual_rows! the residuals buffer, residual_jacobian_rows! the jacobians — with the manifold's retract_jacobian (Ceres' PlusJacobian) already folded into the chart directions. GTSAM's nearest equivalent is a factor's linearize(values), which returns the same rows and Jacobian as a JacobianFactor at the linearization point rather than filling caller buffers.\n\nBoth write into caller-owned buffers and return them; neither allocates. Methods live with the models (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's problems).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sqrt_weights-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ManifoldLmWorkspace}","page":"Chart LM","text":"The per-row √weight buffer of a ManifoldLmWorkspace, filled by the caller's refit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sqrt_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.static_lm-Union{Tuple{B}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{FJ}, Tuple{FJ, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StaticLmStops{N, T}}, Tuple{FJ, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StaticLmStops{N, T}, B}} where {FJ, N, T<:AbstractFloat, B<:Union{Nothing, T}}","page":"Chart LM","text":"static_lm(fj, x₀::SVector{N, T}, lb, ub, stops::StaticLmStops{N, T}[, bound]) -> (x, converged, J, e, rule::StopRule)\n\nThe stack's ONE device-side Levenberg–Marquardt (the spline lenses' inverse at N = 2, any static N): Marquardt-scaled, bounded to the box [lb, ub] (a step is clipped to it), with an unrolled Cholesky-type solve and explicit fused products, so it allocates nothing, never throws, and runs inside a KernelAbstractions kernel with the same bits in every compiled copy. fj(x) returns (r, J, …) (the residual SVector, its SMatrix Jacobian, anything else returned with them as e); stops its stop settings (StaticLmStops); bound a relative step radius (nothing: none). converged is THE success rule of the stack's LMs — the point is STATIONARY: zero to what the stops resolve (the cost below the exact-fit floor, the gradient below its floor, or the undamped Gauss–Newton step from the point below the step tolerance: the device form of the host's exact-fit test), or max_j |Jⱼᵀr| / (‖Jⱼ‖ ‖r‖) ≤ stops.stationarity_tol, the host LM's cosine; on the box the gradient of a coordinate a bound holds (its descent leaves the box) is dropped, the bounded problem's stationarity — and its cost is below stops.acceptance. rule is the stop that fired: ExactFit (the cost floor), ToleranceMet (the gradient floor), IncrementSize (the step), CostDecrease (the relative cost decrease) or IterationCap; static_lm_termination gives the §14 record. NLLSsolver (lmsolve_chart!) is every host fit's LM.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.static_lm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/lm_core/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.static_lm_termination-Tuple{Bool, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StopRule}","page":"Chart LM","text":"static_lm_termination(converged::Bool, rule::StopRule) -> (ReturnCode.T, StopRule)\n\nThe §14 termination record of a static_lm solve: ReturnCode.Success when it converged; otherwise ReturnCode.MaxIters at the iteration cap and ReturnCode.Stalled at any other stop (a step, cost or gradient stop short of the tolerance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.static_lm_termination"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stereo/#Stereo-Rigs-and-Uncertainty","page":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty","text":"Two 0.9 changes share this page because they share a mechanism: the StereoRig became frames-native, and the statistical forms (CovMat/PrecMat) became frame-labeled — both so that a direction (or a transformation law) that used to live in a docstring is now checked by dispatch.","title":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stereo/#The-frames-native-StereoRig","page":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty","text":"A StereoRig is the 2-sensor case of the general Rig — a frame with posed members, whose frame is the gauge member's own. A sensor rig stores ONLY a tuple of cameras, each expressed rig-relative (reference frame = view 1, held at the identity); a rig of calibration targets is the same type with dict storage, PosedTarget members (a target model — PlanarTarget, PointTarget — and its pose) gauged on a board, and a camera rig carrying its marker constellation holds both. The pair's two cameras are the method-form accessors source and destination (no stored fields), and the relative rigid transform is derived from the stored per-camera labeled maps:\n\nextrinsics(rig) :: EuclideanMap{3, …, ViewFrame{id₁}, ViewFrame{id₂}}\n\n(monogram X_{V₂V₁}: consumes source-view coordinates, produces destination-view coordinates, i.e. X₂ = R·X₁ + t). There is no cached inverse and no stored id tuple — the labels are the single source of truth:\n\npose(rig) is the on-demand inv(extrinsics(rig)) (the typed algebra swaps the frames to X_{V₁V₂}).\nids(rig) reads the view-identity symbols off the stored map's frame parameters — an accessor, not a field, resolved at compile time.\nview_frames(rig) / image_frames(rig) expose the minted per-view ViewFrame{id} / ImageFrame{id} tags, ready to attach at a lift.\n\nrig = StereoRig(left_cam, right_cam; ids = (:left, :right))\nids(rig)                      # (:left, :right)\npose(rig)                     # ViewFrame{:right} → ViewFrame{:left}\n\nThe 3-argument constructor validates a caller-supplied transform against extrinsics(destination) ∘ pose(source) computed from the given cameras (check = false for synthetic rigs whose cameras are placeholders).","title":"The frames-native StereoRig"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stereo/#Minting-labeled-epipolar-forms","page":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty","text":"The rig mints the slot-labeled forms of the forms layer in one call:\n\nessential_form(rig) — the calibrated form E = [t]×R, slots labeled by the per-view GnomonicFrame{id} tags (the frame on which EssentialMat-backed forms are honest: gnomonic coordinates x̂ = π(K⁻¹ũ)).\nEpipolarForm(rig) — the pixel-frame fundamental form: essential_form(rig)'s correlation composed with the calibrated → pixel lifts HomographyMap{GnomonicFrame{id}, ImageFrame{id}}(Kᵢ), computing F = K₂⁻ᵀ E K₁⁻¹ with every junction frame-checked.\n\nDepth-driven utilities complete the two-view toolkit: depth_transfer (unit-depth point transfer — a transfer, not a correlation), transfer with depth-attributed points, and the ground-truth machinery validate_gt_metric, validate_gt_inliers that validates correspondences against a rig plus per-view depth maps with a metric (physical-length) tolerance.","title":"Minting labeled epipolar forms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stereo/#Frame-labeled-statistical-forms","page":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty","text":"CovMat{N, T, L, F} and PrecMat{N, T, L, F} carry a trailing F <: Frame{N} parameter naming the frame their coordinates live in — defaulting to UnlabeledFrame{N}, gradual as always. The point of the labels is the law pair:\n\ncovariance is a (2,0) tensor and pushes forward: Σ ↦ J Σ Jᵀ\nprecision is a (0,2) bilinear form and pulls back: Λ ↦ J⁻ᵀ Λ J⁻¹\n\nApplying the covariance law to a precision matrix (or vice versa) produces a plausible-looking, silently wrong matrix — this pair is exactly the bug surface the frame labels exist to check. With the types in place, the law is dispatched, not remembered.","title":"Frame-labeled statistical forms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stereo/#Checked-propagation-through-framed-maps","page":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty","text":"The raw-Jacobian layer propagate(J, Σ) = J Σ Jᵀ stays frame-free (a bare Jacobian carries no frames). The checked layer sits on top: pushing a statistical form through a framed map checks the form's frame against the map's Src (standing wildcard truth table) and stamps the result with the map's Dst:\n\npropagate(m::EuclideanMap, Σ::CovMat)     # J = R, pushforward, result labeled Dst\npropagate(m::EuclideanMap, Λ::PrecMat)    # J = R, pullback, result labeled Dst\npropagate(m::EuclideanMap, u::Uncertain)  # value and uncertainty in one call\npropagate(h::HomographyMap, u::Uncertain) # point-dependent Jacobian at u.value\n\nFor a rigid map the two conjugations coincide numerically (R is orthogonal); the code follows the honest law per type so propagate(m, inv(Σ)) == inv(propagate(m, Σ)) holds by construction. The HomographyMap overloads take the observation (Uncertain) rather than a bare form because the projective point action is nonlinear — the Jacobian must be evaluated at the point.\n\ninv, cov, and prec preserve the frame (inversion swaps the representation, not the frame); relabel/unlabel are the escape hatches, one tag rather than two.\n\nA batched counterpart pushes frame-labeled CovMat stacks through a stage's per-sample Jacobian stack — see Batched Maps.","title":"Checked propagation through framed maps"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/stereo/#See-also","page":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty","text":"Epipolar Forms — the slot-labeled forms the rig mints\nCoordinate Frames — view-identity frames\nImaging Chain and Stereo API","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Visualization","page":"Visualization","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore provides Makie-based visualization through package extensions.","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Setup","page":"Visualization","text":"The plotting functionality is optional and loads automatically when any Makie backend (GLMakie, CairoMakie, WGLMakie) is imported:\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\nusing GLMakie  # Activates the plotting extension","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Image-Display-Functions","page":"Visualization","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore provides two approaches for displaying images:","title":"Image Display Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Axis-based-Display-(imshow)","page":"Visualization","text":"For standard plotting workflows with Axis decorations (ticks, labels, etc.):\n\n# Creates a new Figure with Axis\nfig, ax, plt = imshow(img)\n\n# Add overlays using standard Makie functions\nscatter!(ax, [100, 200], [150, 250]; color=:red, markersize=10)\n\n# Display at a specific position in an existing Figure\nfig = Figure()\nax1, plt1 = imshow(fig[1, 1], img; title=\"Image 1\")\nax2, plt2 = imshow(fig[1, 2], other_img; title=\"Image 2\")\n\n# Add to existing Axis\nax = Axis(fig[2, 1]; aspect=DataAspect(), yreversed=true)\nimshow!(ax, img)","title":"Axis-based Display (imshow)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#LScene-based-Display-(campixel_imshow,-CampixelImshow)","page":"Visualization","text":"For pixel-perfect camera alignment with campixel! camera:\n\n# Non-SpecApi: returns actual Makie objects\nfig, lscene, plt = campixel_imshow(img; scale=0.5)\nscatter!(lscene.scene, [100, 200], [150, 250]; color=:red)\n\n# SpecApi: returns spec for composition\nspec = CampixelImshow(img; scale=0.5)","title":"LScene-based Display (campixel_imshow, CampixelImshow)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Spec-API","page":"Visualization","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore uses Makie's SpecAPI for declarative scene composition:\n\nimport Makie.SpecApi as S","title":"Spec API"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Creating-Scenes","page":"Visualization","text":"# Image display (SpecApi)\nscene = CampixelImshow(img; scale=0.5)\n\n# 3D scene\nscene_3d = LScene3D(width=800, height=600)","title":"Creating Scenes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Plotting-Geometric-Primitives","page":"Visualization","text":"","title":"Plotting Geometric Primitives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Circles-and-Ellipses","page":"Visualization","text":"fig, ax, plt = imshow(img)          # overlays go on the Axis\n\n# Add circles\ncircles = [Circle(Point2(100.0, 150.0), 25.0), ...]\npoly!(ax, circles;\n      strokecolor=:red,\n      strokewidth=2.0,\n      color=:transparent)\n\n# Add ellipses\nellipses = [Ellipse(Point2(200.0, 100.0), 30.0, 20.0, π/4), ...]\npoly!(ax, ellipses; strokecolor=:blue)\n\npoly! forwards to Makie.poly! on an Axis/LScene. On a SpecApi scene from CampixelImshow, overlays are plot specs pushed onto scene.plots:\n\nimport Makie.SpecApi as S\nscene = CampixelImshow(img)\npush!(scene.plots, S.Poly(circles; color=:transparent, strokecolor=:red, strokewidth=2.0))","title":"Circles and Ellipses"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Detections","page":"Visualization","text":"Detections (from VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures) render as circles via the Circle(detection, multiplier) constructor:\n\n# blobs :: Vector{<:AbstractDetection}, e.g. from VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\ncircles = Circle.(blobs, Ref(3.0))  # 3σ radius\nfig, ax, plt = imshow(img)\npoly!(ax, circles; strokecolor=:yellow, strokewidth=2.0)","title":"Detections"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Correspondence-Visualization","page":"Visualization","text":"matches/matches! are the correspondence overlays. VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures extends them by dispatch for its feature types (the old correspondence!/matchlines names are gone from both packages).","title":"Correspondence Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#3D-Visualization","page":"Visualization","text":"","title":"3D Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Camera-Frustums","page":"Visualization","text":"# Create 3D scene (SpecApi)\nscene_3d = LScene3D(width=800, height=600)\n\n# Add camera visualization\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, pose)\nimage_rect = Rect(sensor)\n\n# camera! pushes axes, frustum, and center specs onto the SpecApi scene\ncamera!(scene_3d, camera, image_rect;\n        center_color=:red,\n        center_size=15.0,\n        frustum_color=:orange,\n        frustum_linewidth=2.0)\n\n# Or push the frustum PlotSpec alone\npush!(scene_3d.plots, Frustum(camera, image_rect; color=:blue))\n\nOn a realized LScene (not a spec), use the cameraplot! recipe, which also textures the far plane with frustum_image:\n\ncameraplot!(lscene, camera, image_rect; frustum_image=camera_view)","title":"Camera Frustums"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#3D-Points","page":"Visualization","text":"# Add 3D points\nX = [Point3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), Point3(100.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...]\n\npush!(scene_3d.plots, S.Scatter(X;\n                                color=:black,\n                                markersize=10.0))","title":"3D Points"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Layouts","page":"Visualization","text":"Combine multiple scenes in a grid:\n\n# Create individual scenes\nscene1 = CampixelImshow(img1)\nscene2 = CampixelImshow(img2)\nscene_3d = LScene3D()\n\n# Create grid layout\nlayout = S.GridLayout([\n    (1, 1) => scene1,\n    (1, 2) => scene2,\n    (2, 1:2) => scene_3d  # Span two columns\n])\n\n# Plot and display\nfig = plot(layout)\ndisplay(fig)","title":"Layouts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Advanced-Usage","page":"Visualization","text":"","title":"Advanced Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Custom-Plotting","page":"Visualization","text":"Add custom Makie plots to scenes:\n\nscene = CampixelImshow(img)\n\n# Add scatter points\npoints = [Point2(100.0, 150.0), Point2(200.0, 250.0)]\npush!(scene.plots, S.Scatter(points;\n                            color=:yellow,\n                            markersize=20.0,\n                            marker=:cross))\n\n# Add lines\nline_points = [Point2(0.0, 0.0), Point2(100.0, 100.0)]\npush!(scene.plots, S.Lines(line_points;\n                          color=:green,\n                          linewidth=3.0))","title":"Custom Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Interactive-Displays","page":"Visualization","text":"# Display with GLMakie screen\nfig = plot(layout)\ndisplay(GLMakie.Screen(), fig)\n\n# Save to file\nusing FileIO\nsave(\"output.png\", fig)","title":"Interactive Displays"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Function-Summary","page":"Visualization","text":"Function Returns Use Case\nimshow(img) (Figure, Axis, Plot) Standard plotting with Axis\nimshow(pos, img) (Axis, Plot) Add to existing Figure\nimshow!(ax, img) Plot Add to existing Axis\ncampixel_imshow(img) (Figure, LScene, Plot) LScene with campixel camera\ncampixel_imshow(pos, img) (LScene, Plot) Add to existing Figure\ncampixel_imshow!(ls, img) Plot Add to existing LScene\nCampixelImshow(img) LScene spec SpecApi composition","title":"Function Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#Package-Extension-Details","page":"Visualization","text":"The plotting functionality is provided by two package extensions:\n\nVisualGeometryToolkitCoreMakieExt loads with Makie, i.e. with any backend (GLMakie, CairoMakie, WGLMakie), and implements every plotting function (source in src/plotting/); it has zero overhead when not using visualization\nVisualGeometryToolkitCoreGLMakieExt loads when GLMakie is imported and adds only the GLMakie window helpers window!(fig, title) and closeall()\n\nThe functions both extensions fill are declared as stubs in src/visualization_stubs.jl, so their names resolve without Makie.","title":"Package Extension Details"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visualization/#See-Also","page":"Visualization","text":"Getting Started - Package setup\nCameras Guide - 3D camera visualization","title":"See Also"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#Epipolar-Forms","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"Transformations have endpoints, forms have slots, rank lives on the manifold. The forms layer (since 0.8) applies the frame system to the epipolar matrix — an object that is not a map at all.","title":"Epipolar Forms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#Maps-vs.-forms","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"A HomographyMap{Src, Dst} is a collineation: it consumes Src-frame points and produces Dst-frame points, so its frame tags name the two endpoints of a map. The epipolar matrix is a different citizen — a bilinear form with two contravariant point arguments,\n\nx₂ᵀ M x₁ = 0\n\nIt maps neither argument anywhere; it constrains the pair. Its frame tags therefore label the form's two argument slots (in the tensor sense: the tag names which frame's points an argument position accepts, not an endpoint of a map).","title":"Maps vs. forms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#EpipolarForm","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"EpipolarForm{S1, S2} wraps a frame-free FundMat or EssentialMat and adds only the slot labels — rank and manifold constraints stay with the wrapped matrix type and the solvers that produce it. The slot convention, pinned at the struct and deferred to by every other method: slot 1 takes points of frame S1 (view 1) and is the right argument of the matrix, matching the u₂ᵀ F u₁ convention of the solver layer.\n\nform = EpipolarForm{ImageFrame{:a}, ImageFrame{:b}}(F)   # labeled lift\nform = EpipolarForm(F)                                 # unlabeled (gradual)\nform(x₁, x₂)                                           # x̃₂ᵀ M x̃₁ — 0 for an exact correspondence\n\nAn EpipolarForm is not a Transformation: it does not compose with ∘, and it has no inv (deliberately — below). Its point ↦ line action is epipolar_map, an EpipolarMap — and a frame change is composition on that map.","title":"EpipolarForm"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#Composition-—-the-transformation-law","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"Frame changes act on forms per slot. With slot maps slot1 :: HomographyMap{S1, S1′} and slot2 :: HomographyMap{S2, S2′}, demanding invariance of the constraint gives\n\nM′ = H₂⁻ᵀ · M · H₁⁻¹\n\nEpipolarForm(slot2 ∘ epipolar_map(f) ∘ inv(slot1)) is exactly this: a collineation on the right moves the points fed in, one on the left moves the returned line by the line law ℓ′ = H⁻ᵀℓ. Each junction — a slot frame meeting a map — is checked under the standing wildcard truth table (see Coordinate Frames); a slot not composed keeps its frame and coordinates.\n\nThis is where K-normalization bookkeeping becomes checkable: with the calibrated → pixel collineations Hᵢ = HomographyMap{GnomonicFrame{idᵢ}, ImageFrame{idᵢ}}(Kᵢ), composing an EssentialMat-backed correlation computes exactly F = K₂⁻ᵀ E K₁⁻¹, with the frame labels verifying that each K met the slot it was meant for. The result always wraps a FundMat when a slot moves: an arbitrary collineation preserves rank 2 but not the essential-manifold constraint, so keeping an EssentialMat wrapper through a general frame change would be a silent lie. Rewrap via EssentialMat(F, K₁, K₂) at a trusted boundary.","title":"Composition — the transformation law"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#transpose,-and-the-deliberate-absence-of-inv","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"The epipolar correlation is rank 2: its kernel is the epipole e₁ (M e₁ = 0), every point on one epipolar line maps to the same line in the other view, and the image is not all lines but the one-parameter pencil through the other epipole. A 2-parameter point domain collapsing onto a 1-parameter family of lines has no inverse — inv(::EpipolarForm) is deliberately left undefined (a MethodError, pinned by test).\n\ntranspose (swap the slots; wraps Mᵀ) is the only involution the form supports; adjoint (f') is the same operation. The degenerate directions are exposed by epipoles.","title":"transpose, and the deliberate absence of inv"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#epipolar_map-—-the-honest-correlation","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"epipolar_map(f) returns the typed callable EpipolarMap: point x₁ in frame S1 to its epipolar line ℓ₂ = M x̃₁ in frame S2, as a Line2D — a correlation lands on a line (itself a one-slot form on S2-points), not on a point. epipolar_map(transpose(f)) goes the other way. There is no transfer overload for a form: transfer is the point ↦ point depth-transport verb; the depth-assumed two-view closure is depth_transfer (which epipolar_map was renamed from in 0.8 — the old name promised a correlation and delivered a transfer).","title":"epipolar_map — the honest correlation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#Checked-observation-frames","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"sampson_distance(f, x₁, x₂) delegates to the Sampson machinery on the wrapped matrix, and since 0.9 the delegation is checked where a frame is available: an Uncertain observation whose CovMat/PrecMat carries a frame label must meet the slot it feeds — same wildcard table, MethodError on a labeled mismatch.","title":"Checked observation frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/forms/#See-also","page":"Epipolar Forms","text":"Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty — essential_form(rig) / EpipolarForm(rig) mint the labeled forms\nCoordinate Frames — the tag system the slots reuse\nSolvers API, Epipolar Forms API","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Solvers-API","page":"Solvers","text":"Projective solver kernels (homography, fundamental, essential, P3P), model-validity tests, the zero-allocation SVD workspace, and the total-least-squares line. The slot-labeled epipolar forms have their own page, the Epipolar Forms API. The solve protocol and the solver registry are documented with the Correspondence API; see the Epipolar Forms guide.","title":"Solvers API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Normalization","page":"Solvers","text":"One scheme serves every solver. A normalization STRATEGY is a type named <Method>Normalization, applied as a coordinate map with AffineMap(strategy, points[, mask, weights]):\n\nHartleyNormalization(; center) — translate to the (weighted) centroid, or to a known center, and scale to mean distance √2. Estimation uses it: the centroid for H, F and E; the known distortion centre for the radial models, whose solvers are written about the origin.\nDiagonalNormalization(size; center) — the image centre and the half-diagonal (corners at unit radius, NormalizedImageFrame coordinates). For storage only: the benchmark pools, where one noise level and one threshold must mean the same across resolutions.\n\nA correspondence set's normalization is one value, CorrespondenceNormalization: both sides' maps and their inverses, built from two strategies or by model type (CorrespondenceNormalization(M, cs; center)), with normalize and the side accessors, whose composition moves a model (D ∘ M ∘ inv(S) into the normalized frame, inv(D) ∘ M ∘ S back). An estimator normalizes once at its entry and returns results in its inputs' frame; the DLT kernels take the enclosing normalization as normalization.","title":"Normalization"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Homography-Solvers","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Homography Solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Fundamental-and-Essential-Matrix-Solvers","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Fundamental and Essential Matrix Solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Lines","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Lines"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Circles","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Circles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Ellipses","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Ellipses"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Model-Validity","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Model Validity"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#SVD-Workspace","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"SVD Workspace"},{"category":"section","location":"api/solvers/#Index","page":"Solvers","text":"Pages = [\"solvers.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap","page":"Solvers","text":"AffineMap(s::HartleyNormalization, points, mask = nothing, weights = nothing) -> AffineMap\nAffineMap(s::HartleyNormalization, points3d, weights = nothing) -> AffineMap\nAffineMap(s::DiagonalNormalization) -> AffineMap\n\nApply a normalization strategy: the affine map x ↦ σ·(x − c) it prescribes (a Diagonal linear part, uniform scale). For Hartley the centre and scale come from points — mask (a BitVector) and weights restrict and weight the statistics, an empty selection gives the identity; the diagonal strategy needs no data. Hartley applies to image points (mean distance √2) and to 3-D points (StaticVector{3}: about their centroid, mean distance √3 — the normalization of a least squares over lengths, such as translations); the diagonal strategy is 2-D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization","page":"Solvers","text":"CorrespondenceNormalization(T_src::AffineMap, T_dst::AffineMap)\nCorrespondenceNormalization(src::Normalization, dst::Normalization, cs[, mask, weights])\nCorrespondenceNormalization(M::Type, cs; center)\n\nThe normalization of a correspondence set as a first-class value: the forward homogeneous maps of the source and target sides AND their analytic inverses, computed once at construction (never per candidate model). Build it from two affine maps, from a normalization strategy per side (HartleyNormalization, DiagonalNormalization) applied to the correspondences' sources and targets, or by MODEL TYPE — the strategy each model class needs:\n\nHomographyMat / AbstractFundMat (F and E): Hartley at the centroid on both sides (the classic DLT data normalization), over the mask's correspondences and weighted by the weights column when there is one;\nRadialHomography: Hartley at the centroid on the sources, Hartley about the known distortion center (required) on the targets — the origin the 5-point kernel is written about.\n\nVerbs:\n\nnormalize(normalization, cs) — materialized normalized correspondence set\nsource_map / destination_map — its two sides as affine maps\na model moves by composition with the two sides: destination_map(N) ∘ M ∘ inv(source_map(N)) carries a data-frame model M into the normalized frame and inv(destination_map(N)) ∘ M ∘ source_map(N) back (M a HomographyMat, a FundMat/EssentialMat, a HomographyMap, an EpipolarMap, a RadialHomography, a RadialCamera).\n\nThe pose family builds none (bearings are already metric).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DiagonalNormalization","page":"Solvers","text":"DiagonalNormalization(size; center = (width/2, height/2))\n\nThe image-size normalization as a STRATEGY: translate to center (the image centre by default; size in pixels, plain numbers or px quantities — a length is a DimensionError) and divide by the half-diagonal hypot(width, height)/2, so the image corners land at unit radius — the coordinates of a NormalizedImageFrame. For STORAGE only (the benchmark pools, where one noise level and one threshold must mean the same across resolutions); estimation normalizes by HartleyNormalization. Apply it with AffineMap(DiagonalNormalization(size)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DiagonalNormalization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HartleyNormalization","page":"Solvers","text":"HartleyNormalization(; center = nothing)\n\nHartley's data normalization as a STRATEGY (Hartley & Zisserman §4.4): translate to the (weighted) centroid of the points — or to a KNOWN center — and scale isotropically so that the mean distance from it is √2. Apply it with AffineMap(HartleyNormalization(...), points[, mask, weights]).\n\nThe known centre is the radial models' frame: their distortion centre must land on the origin the minimal solvers are written about, and the isotropic scale keeps the division model in its class (k′ = k/s²); a centroid translation would move the centre off the origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HartleyNormalization"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#CoordinateTransformations.transform_deriv_params-Tuple{HomographyMat, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Solvers","text":"transform_deriv_params(H::HomographyMat, u::StaticVector{2}) -> SMatrix{2,9}\n\nThe derivative of the point a homography transfers, π(H·ũ), along its parameters vec(H) (column-major): Jπ·(ũᵀ ⊗ I₃), Jπ the perspective derivative at H·ũ. The Gθ of the transfer form (`constraintwithjacobians(TransferForm(H), …)); the transferred point's model covariance ispropagate(transformderivparams(H, u), Σθ)for an ambient covarianceΣθ, and a chart'sactionjacobianis this through itsretract_jacobian`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.transform_deriv_params"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{HomographyMat{T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(H::HomographyMat, u₁::SVector{2}, u₂::SVector{2})\n    -> (g::SVector{2}, G_x::SMatrix{2,4}, G_θ::SMatrix{2,9})\n\nCompute the algebraic constraint g and its derivatives for the homography.\n\nThe constraint g = 0 encodes ũ₂ ∝ H·ũ₁ (2 equations):   g₁ = h₁ᵀũ₁ − x'·h₃ᵀũ₁   g₂ = h₂ᵀũ₁ − y'·h₃ᵀũ₁\n\nReturns:\n\ng::SVector{2,T}      — constraint vector\nG_x::SMatrix{2,4,T}  — ∂g/∂(x,y,x',y') (observation Jacobian)\nG_θ::SMatrix{2,9,T}  — ∂g/∂vec(H) (model Jacobian, column-major)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.destination_map-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization}","page":"Solvers","text":"destination_map(f::CorrespondenceNormalization) -> AffineMap\n\nThe forward normalization of the destination side (the correspondences' targets), as a 2D affine map; its inverse is inv of it. The source side is source_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.destination_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_4pt-Union{Tuple{T}, NTuple{8, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"homography_4pt(s₁, s₂, s₃, s₄, d₁, d₂, d₃, d₄) -> SMatrix{3,3} or nothing\n\nCompute a homography from exactly 4 point correspondences using the inhomogeneous method (two 3×3 LU solves, zero heap allocation).\n\nReturns a Frobenius-normalized SMatrix{3,3} with H[3,3] >= 0, or nothing if the points are degenerate (collinear).\n\nAlgorithm\n\nFrom \"An Algebraic Derivation of the Inhomogeneous Homography\":\n\nBuild U3 = [u1|u2|u3], solve U3*c = -u4 for projective depths\nBuild U3' = [u1'|u2'|u3'], solve U3'*c' = -u4' for target depths\nλi = c'i / c_i (projective depth ratios)\nH = U3' * diag(λ) * inv(U3)\n\nExample\n\ns = [SA[0.0,0.0], SA[1.0,0.0], SA[1.0,1.0], SA[0.0,1.0]]\nd = [SA[0.1,0.1], SA[1.1,0.0], SA[1.0,1.1], SA[0.0,1.0]]\nH = homography_4pt(s[1], s[2], s[3], s[4], d[1], d[2], d[3], d[4])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_4pt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_dlt!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization{T}, AbstractVector{<:Pair{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"homography_dlt!(A, N::CorrespondenceNormalization, cs; mask = nothing, svd_ws = nothing) -> HomographyMat or nothing\n\nIn-place N-point DLT of the correspondences cs using the pre-allocated buffer A (2N × 9). Weights are the container's weights column, as for homography_dlt; inside an LO/IRLS loop set them per iteration with withattrs(cs; weights = w) (no allocation beyond the caller's w).\n\nWhen mask::BitVector is provided, only masked points are used and rows are compacted into the top of A. The inner-loop form: N is the enclosing estimator's CorrespondenceNormalization, built once at its entry (the public form builds it from the data); this form applies it row by row and composes the model back into the data frame (inv(D) ∘ H ∘ S), and never normalizes on its own.\n\nIf svd_ws::SvdWorkspace is provided and A is Float64, uses pre-allocated LAPACK workspace for the SVD (zero allocations). Note: A is overwritten when using svd_ws.\n\nNo covariance is returned. The DLT minimizes ALGEBRAIC residuals, whose noise is heteroscedastic under pixel noise, so the SVD's own resolvent covariance is not this estimator's (measured overconfident against Monte Carlo). Its first-order covariance, if ever needed, is implicit_covariance's block form with the DLT's identity weighting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_dlt!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_dlt-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Pair{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"homography_dlt(cs; mask = nothing, svd_ws = nothing) -> HomographyMat or nothing\n\nCompute a homography from N >= 4 correspondences cs (u₁ => u₂ pairs; any sources/targets container, a Correspondences preferred) by the Direct Linear Transform with Hartley normalization.\n\nPer-correspondence weights are the container's weights column (set with withattrs), never a keyword: each weight multiplies its rows' squared algebraic residual and the Hartley normalization. When mask (a BitVector or an index list) is given, only those correspondences are used.\n\nExample\n\ncs = Correspondences(src_pts, dst_pts)\nH = homography_dlt(cs)\nH = homography_dlt(withattrs(cs; weights = [1.0, 0.5, 1.0, 0.8]))\nH = homography_dlt(cs; mask = inlier_mask)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_dlt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_convex_quad-Tuple{NTuple{4, var\"#s549\"} where var\"#s549\"<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T)}","page":"Solvers","text":"is_convex_quad(q::NTuple{4, <:StaticVector{2}}) -> Bool\n\nTrue iff the closed polygon q[1] → q[2] → q[3] → q[4] is a simple, convex, consistently-wound quadrilateral: every edge cross-product around the boundary shares one non-zero sign. Mixed signs (a self-intersecting \"bow-tie\") and the all-zero degenerate case are false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_convex_quad"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_orientation_preserving-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{HomographyMap{3, T, M} where M<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}, HyperRectangle{2}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"is_orientation_preserving(h::HomographyMap{3}, rect::HyperRectangle{2}) -> Bool\n\nTrue iff the four corners of rect map through h to a simple (non-self-intersecting) convex quadrilateral. Catches \"bow-tie\" homographies that the per-correspondence checks (test_cheirality, test_conditioning) miss — these come from H fits that are well-behaved over the inlier cluster but extrapolate to fold the rectangle's corners across each other.\n\nReturns true when consecutive edge cross-products along the projected boundary share a non-zero sign (consistent winding); false when signs are mixed (the projected quad self-intersects). A purely degenerate projection (all crosses zero) also returns false, since it can't be a valid extent map.\n\nThis is an extent-aware check that complements the per-point predicates in RANSAC: caller supplies the rectangle over which the homography is supposed to be valid (e.g. a calibration board's Rect(bundle, :world)).\n\nExample\n\nH_fit = HomographyMap(matrix_from_ransac)\nrect  = Rect2(SA[0.0, 0.0], SA[100.0, 60.0])   # board extent in world coords\nis_orientation_preserving(H_fit, rect) || @warn \"Bow-tie fit\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_orientation_preserving"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sign_normalize-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"sign_normalize(H::StaticMatrix{3,3,T}) -> typeof(H) or nothing\n\nFrobenius-normalize a 3×3 matrix and ensure H[3,3] >= 0. Returns nothing if the matrix norm is negligible. Preserves the input type (HomographyMat, FundMat, SMatrix, etc.).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sign_normalize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.source_map-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization}","page":"Solvers","text":"source_map(f::CorrespondenceNormalization) -> AffineMap\n\nThe forward normalization of the source side, as a 2D affine map (source_map(f)(x) is the normalized source point); its inverse is inv of it. The destination side is destination_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.source_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_cheirality-Tuple{HomographyMat, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_cheirality(H, cs, idx) -> Bool\n\nProjective cheirality check, uniform-sign variant. For each correspondence (uᵢ, u′ᵢ) at the sample indices, compute the projective scale factor λᵢ such that ũ′ᵢ = λᵢ · H · ũᵢ in homogeneous coordinates, where ũ = [u; 1]. Accept iff all λᵢ share a sign.\n\nRationale — H is only defined up to a nonzero scalar (H and −H are the same mapping). Under H → −H, every λᵢ flips sign, so requiring a specific sign (e.g. the original Shekhovtsov rule: sign-normalize to det(H) > 0, then require λ > 0) amounts to picking a matrix representative by convention. That convention matches the image-to-image case (where both frames share 2D handedness) but fails for object-to-image homographies (e.g. a world y-up calibration board mapped to a y-down image), where the \"in front\" representative has det(H) < 0 — the strict rule then rejects every correct fit.\n\nThe mapping-level invariant is uniform sign across the λᵢ: mixed signs signal a bow-tie in projective depth (a real pathology that cannot be repaired by a matrix sign flip), while a shared sign — positive or negative — signals a consistent mapping (with the overall sign absorbed into the free scalar of H).\n\nFor each i: compute Hu = H · ũᵢ, pick the component k with the largest absolute value (numerical robustness), and set λᵢ = ũ′ᵢ[k] / Hu[k] (with ũ′[3] = 1). Reject the sample the moment a sign disagreement is seen. An inner-loop gate: H and cs in the same frame (sign-based, so any positive-scale normalization, or none, gives the same answer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_cheirality"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_conditioning-Union{Tuple{HomographyMat{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"test_conditioning(H; max_cond=100) -> Bool\n\nReject homographies whose SVD condition number κ = σ₁/σ₃ exceeds max_cond.\n\nCatches near-rank-deficient H — the same pathology PoseLib's post-solve |det(H_normalized)| < 1e-8 check rejects (since after Frobenius normalization det(H) = σ₁σ₂σ₃ and one vanishing singular value makes both κ → ∞ and det → 0), plus a broader class of numerically unstable but non-singular H's.\n\nMatches Shekhovtsov's is_homography_ill_conditioned (decompose_homography.py:244) with the same default threshold κ = 100.\n\nκ is a property of the frame H is written in, not of the map: the bound is calibrated for H between Hartley-normalized frames (both sides, as CorrespondenceNormalization builds them), so evaluate it on the normalized H, never on a pixel-frame one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_conditioning"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_normalize-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"unit_normalize(H::StaticMatrix{3,3}) -> SMatrix{3,3}\n\nH / ‖H‖ with NO degenerate guard and NO sign convention — the hot-loop sibling of sign_normalize for chart lifts and retractions, where a Union{Nothing,...} return is unacceptable and the H[3,3] ≥ 0 flip would break retraction continuity mid-iteration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_normalize"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OUT_OF_FIELD_RESIDUAL","page":"Solvers","text":"OUT_OF_FIELD_RESIDUAL = 1.0e6\n\nThe residual of an observation a model cannot place — a ray outside a lens's or division model's field, a point behind the camera — in the caller's units: the ONE constant for such rows. In an LM row it is a CONSTANT (with a zero Jacobian row), so a step that pushes observations out is charged for it and rejected — never a zero residual, which would reward exactly that step; in a scoring residual it is finite (not Inf/NaN), so sorting and squaring stay defined while the point lands among the outliers. 1e6 rather than larger: its square (1e12) leaves Float64 cost sums a resolution of ~1e-4, so the in-field rows stay visible to LM next to a constant out-of-field one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OUT_OF_FIELD_RESIDUAL"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization-Tuple{Type{<:AbstractRadialModel}, Any}","page":"Solvers","text":"CorrespondenceNormalization(::Type{<:AbstractRadialModel}, cs; center, mask = nothing)\n\nThe radial family's normalization of plane_point => pixel correspondences (a RadialHomography, a RadialCamera; the H/F one lives in homography_solvers.jl): Hartley at the centroid on the plane side, Hartley about the REQUIRED center (the distortion centre, the pencil's centre) on the image side — the origin the minimal kernels are written about, with an isotropic scale that keeps a division model in its class (k′ = k/s²) — over mask's correspondences and weighted by the weights column when there is one. A centroid image-side frame would move the centre off the origin (measured: 1.4 px misfit against 4e-13 px).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RadialHomography","page":"Solvers","text":"RadialHomography{T, N}\n\nRadially distorted homography model: a HomographyMat H (plane point → undistorted pixel) and a PROJECTIVE division camera on the TARGET side — the N division coefficients λ = (λ₁, …, λ_N) (in squared pixel units, λₚ multiplying ρ²ᵖ) about the distortion centre c (the camera's principal point, COLMAP pixels). A correspondence (s, d) is explained when the division-model RAY LIFT of the observation is proportional to the recentered H ray:\n\n(d − c ; 1 + Σₚ λₚ‖d − c‖²ᵖ) ≃ λ · T₋c · H s̃\n\nThe camera is CameraModel(m) = CameraModel(ProjectiveIntrinsics(c), Division(λ...)), built on demand: the struct stores its primitives (H, λ, center), so it is isbits and runs on a device. RadialHomography{T} (any N) stays a valid partial type; N = 1 is the one-coefficient model of homography_5pt_radial, for which division_coeff and the property m.k read λ₁ (both throw an ArgumentError for N > 1 — never a silent λ₁).\n\nThe division model is an uncalibrated projection/back-projection model (project/backproject) — the lift's signed third component represents rays beyond 90° (omnidirectional lenses), which no image-space \"undistortion\" can. The solver frame is origin-centered (c = 0 — homography_5pt_radial constructs it that way); composing back through a fixed-center normalization produces the pixel-frame model with the center carried along, so consumers (radial_transfer, quad projection) need no side-channel frame data.\n\nConstructors: RadialHomography(H, camera) (a CameraModel{<:ProjectiveIntrinsics, <:Division}), and for N = 1 RadialHomography(H, k, c), RadialHomography(H, k) and RadialHomography{T}(H, k[, c]) (the centre defaults to the origin). Produced by homography_5pt_radial / Homography5ptRadial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RadialHomography"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#CoordinateTransformations.transform_deriv-Tuple{RadialHomography, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Solvers","text":"transform_deriv(m::RadialHomography, ray::StaticVector{3}) -> SMatrix{2,3}\ntransform_deriv_params(m::RadialHomography{T, N}, ray::StaticVector{3}) -> SMatrix{2,N}\n\nThe derivatives of project(m, ray): along the (centred) ray, and along the division coefficients λ₁ … λ_N (the model's lens parameters; H and the centre act before the ray). Closed form for one coefficient; for N > 1 the Division{N} lens's own (ForwardDiff through its project). NaN where project returns nothing — the same edge rule, as for the lens models beyond their field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.transform_deriv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{RadialHomography{T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"backproject(m::RadialHomography, d) -> SVector{3}\n\nBack-project an observed target point to its (unnormalized) ray in the model's CENTERED frame: (d − c ; 1 + Σₚ λₚ‖d − c‖²ᵖ) — the division-model lift (1 + k‖d − c‖² for one coefficient). Total; the third component's sign carries front/back hemisphere (zero at exactly 90°, negative beyond — valid for omnidirectional lenses). The H part of the model relates source points to these rays: backproject(m, d) ≃ λ · T₋c · H s̃ (with T₋c the recentering translation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.division_coeff-Union{Tuple{RadialHomography{T, 1}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"division_coeff(m::RadialHomography{T, 1}) -> T\n\nThe division coefficient k = λ₁ of a ONE-coefficient RadialHomography's image-side lens, about center (the property m.k is this). Throws an ArgumentError for N > 1: read division_coeffs there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.division_coeff"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.division_coeffs-Union{Tuple{RadialHomography{T, N}}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}} where {T, N}","page":"Solvers","text":"division_coeffs(m::RadialHomography{T, N}) -> NTuple{N, T}\n\nThe N division coefficients (λ₁, …, λ_N) of m's image-side lens, about center, in squared centred-pixel units (λₚ multiplies ρ²ᵖ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.division_coeffs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography-Tuple{RadialHomography}","page":"Solvers","text":"The plane part H of a RadialHomography: plane point → undistorted pixel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_5pt_radial-Union{Tuple{T}, NTuple{10, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"homography_5pt_radial(s₁, …, s₅, d₁, …, d₅; extra_check=false)\n    -> FixedModels{3, RadialHomography}\n\nMinimal 5-point solver for a homography with one-sided one-parameter division-model radial distortion on the TARGET side (Wadenbäck et al. (2026) — port of HomLib's get_one_sided). Sources sᵢ are distortion-free; targets dᵢ are observed (distorted). Each candidate (H, k) satisfies\n\ndᵢ / (1 + k‖dᵢ‖²) ≃ π(H s̃ᵢ)\n\nexactly for i = 1…4, plus ONE scalar equation of the fifth correspondence: the problem is \"4.5-point\" overdetermined (9 DOF vs 10 constraints), so only the true root also satisfies the redundant remaining equation and interpolates all five points. The distortion coefficient solves a cubic, so up to 3 candidates are returned (count == 0 on degenerate samples). With extra_check = true only the root minimizing the redundant-equation residual is kept (at most 1 candidate) — the reference enables this in its RANSAC wrapper.\n\nCoordinates are used raw: supply roughly unit-scale, centered coordinates (k lives in squared target units and rescales as k/s²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.homography_5pt_radial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{RadialHomography, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Solvers","text":"project(m::RadialHomography, ray::StaticVector{3}) -> SVector{2} or nothing\n\nProject a ray (in the model's CENTERED frame, as produced by the H part) to the observed image point whose backproject lift is proportional to it. One coefficient, in closed form: c + μ·(x, y) with μ = 2 / (z + √(z² − 4k ρ²)), ρ² = x² + y² (the branch continuous with k → 0, where it reduces to perspective division μ = 1/z). For k < 0 (fisheye/omni) this is total over front AND back rays; for k > 0 it returns nothing when z² − 4kρ² < 0 (the ray lies outside the model's representable field). N > 1 coefficients: c + project(Division(λ...), ray), the lens's own solve on its monotone branch, nothing where the lens images nothing (past its fold).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_in_front-Tuple{RadialHomography, Any}","page":"Solvers","text":"radial_in_front(m::RadialHomography, s) -> Bool\n\nWhether source point s lands in m's field (project's edge rule) AND in its FRONT hemisphere: the observed point's division lift has a positive third component (one coefficient: μ·z > 0, the ray within 90° of the axis; N > 1: the ray's z > 0, the lift on the lens's branch pointing the ray's way). For k ≥ 0 every in-field ray is in front (the lift's third component 1 + kρ² is positive), so this adds z > 0 for k < 0 only — where project is total over back rays too (omnidirectional lenses). The extent gate of a planar target: a board corner at or past 90° means the hypothesis spans ≥ 180° across a plane, which no camera images.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_in_front"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_outline-Tuple{RadialHomography, HyperRectangle{2}}","page":"Solvers","text":"radial_outline(m::RadialHomography, rect::HyperRectangle{2}; samples = 20) -> Vector{Point2{Float64}}\n\nBoundary of a world-plane rect transferred through the radial model: each edge sampled samples times (_rect_edge_samples) and pushed through radial_transfer; samples whose ray leaves the representable field are DROPPED — the polygon shortens, it never carries a nothing. PER EDGE, not at constant arc length (boundary_point(::Rect, θ)): every corner is sampled exactly, because this outline is a board's image-plane QUAD and a quad whose corners are missed is a different polygon. The one sampler behind a board's image-plane quad and its Makie overlay (hoisted from BlobBoards, 2026-09).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_outline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_transfer-Tuple{RadialHomography, Any}","page":"Solvers","text":"radial_transfer(m::RadialHomography, s) -> SVector{2} or nothing\n\nPredicted OBSERVED target of source s through m: recenter the ray H s̃ and project it. Ray-based — no intermediate perspective point, so it stays defined through and beyond 90° for k < 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_transfer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_transfer_jacobian_rows!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, RadialHomography, Tuple, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Solvers","text":"radial_transfer_jacobian_rows!(J, m::RadialHomography, dH, u₁, sqrtw) -> J\n\nThe Jacobian of radial_transfer_rows!: columns 1:M along the M directions dH of H, columns M + 1 … M + N along the N division coefficients, a zero row for a ray outside the model's field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_transfer_jacobian_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_transfer_rows!-Tuple{AbstractVector, RadialHomography, AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Solvers","text":"radial_transfer_rows!(r, m::RadialHomography, u₁, u₂, sqrtw) -> r\nradial_transfer_jacobian_rows!(J, m::RadialHomography, dH, u₁, sqrtw) -> J\n\nThe √w-weighted observation-space transfer rows of m over u₁[i] => u₂[i], two per correspondence (r[2i-1:2i] = sqrtw[2i-1:2i] .* (radial_transfer(m, u₁[i]) − u₂[i])), and their Jacobian: columns 1:M along the directions dH = (∂H/∂θ₁, …, ∂H/∂θ_M) of H (e.g. the reshaped columns of retract_jacobian(HomographyManifold8(H₀), v)), columns M + 1 … M + N along the division coefficients λ₁ … λ_N (transform_deriv_params). A ray outside the model's field (project's edge rule) is a constant residual with a ZERO Jacobian row, so LM rejects a step that pushes rays out rather than being rewarded for it. One KernelAbstractions kernel each, on every backend (the CPU's included).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radial_transfer_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_model-Tuple{RadialHomography, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_model(m::RadialHomography, cs, idx) -> Bool\n\nFeasibility of a radial-homography candidate: SVD conditioning of the H part plus ray-space cheirality — the recentered H s̃ᵢ must be proportional to backproject(m, dᵢ) with a UNIFORM sign across the sample (m and cs in the same frame). Ray-based, so front/back hemisphere consistency is checked without any perspective division.\n\nThe conditioning bound (test_conditioning, κ ≤ 100) is a property of the frame m is written in, and it is calibrated for Hartley frames — the H/F convention. m and cs are therefore meant to be in the normalization of CorrespondenceNormalization(RadialHomography, cs; center): Hartley on both sides, the target about the distortion centre. (In a smaller-scale frame, e.g. the earlier full-diagonal target frame, κ rejected true-model candidates: measured 1 of 124.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample-Tuple{Type{<:RadialHomography}, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_sample(::Type{<:RadialHomography}, cs, idx; target_tol = 1.0e-3) -> Bool\n\nDegeneracy gate for the 5-point radial sample: the homography checks of the four fully-used correspondences (orientation, coincidence, collinearity — run on the distorted targets, an adequate proxy since the division model is locally orientation-preserving) plus pairwise distinctness of all five. Orientation and collinearity are scale-free; the SOURCE distinctness is a fixed separation 1e-3 in cs's units, and the TARGET distinctness is an absolute separation target_tol in cs's units — a caller holding the normalization converts a pixel separation through its scale (target_tol = τ_px * similarity_scale(destination_map(normalization))), so the gate does not move with the frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractRadialModel","page":"Solvers","text":"AbstractRadialModel\n\nPlane→image models whose image side is a pencil of rays about a known centre: RadialHomography (plane point → pixel, the division lens carried explicitly) and RadialCamera (plane point → the image LINE the pixel lies on, invariant to focal length and to any radially symmetric distortion).\n\nThe family contract is deliberately narrow — center, and movement between coordinate frames by composition (D ∘ m ∘ inv(S) with an image-side D and a plane-side S). project is NOT part of it: the members differ in arity there, which is exactly why both exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractRadialModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.center-Tuple{AbstractRadialModel}","page":"Solvers","text":"center(m::AbstractRadialModel) -> Point2\n\nThe model's centre of radial symmetry — the pencil's vertex, and the principal point when the model is a calibration one. A radial model's matrix predicts a DIRECTION; the centre is what turns that direction into a locus in the image, which is why every member carries one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.center"},{"category":"function","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.direction","page":"Solvers","text":"direction(m, X) -> Vec2\n\nThe bare algebraic prediction of a radial model: the DIRECTION of the image ray a plane point maps to, before the centre turns it into a locus. Defined for RadialCamera in cameras/calibration.jl, where it is P·X̃.\n\nDeclared here so the family owns the name; the method lives with the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.direction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractFundMat-Union{Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"(F::FundMat)(u::SVector{2})    -> Line2D\n(E::EssentialMat)(x̂::SVector{2}) -> Line2D\n\nThe epipolar CORRELATION, applied: the epipolar line in image 2 of the input point in image 1 (ℓ₂ = F ũ₁ — a point-to-hyperplane action, one-way by rank 2; contrast the collineation action of HomographyMap). Pixel input for FundMat, gnomonic (GnomonicFrame-coordinate) input for EssentialMat. Use F'(u) / E'(x̂) for the reverse direction.\n\nThe Mat callable is the frame-free surface; the slot-labeled equivalent is epipolar_map on an EpipolarForm, whose frame tags make the direction checkable. The degenerate directions are epipoles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractFundMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialMat-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Solvers","text":"EssentialMat(R::AbstractMatrix, t::AbstractVector) -> EssentialMat\nEssentialMat(X::EuclideanMap{3})                   -> EssentialMat\n\nConstruct the calibrated epipolar matrix E = [t]× R from the relative rigid transform (R, t) in the EXTRINSIC direction, x₂ = R·x₁ + t (monogram X_{V₂V₁} — view 1 coordinates in, view 2 coordinates out). Returns an EssentialMat{T} (typed wrapper) so that downstream dispatch can distinguish E from F by type, not by comment.\n\nThe EuclideanMap form reads .R/.t directly — labeled maps are welcome (e.g. a CameraExtrinsics or a rig's ViewFrame{id₁} → ViewFrame{id₂} transform); the frame tags are irrelevant to the algebra here and are simply dropped. This is the constructor behind essential_form(rig), which additionally labels the resulting form's slots with the rig's GnomonicFrame tags.\n\nt is treated as a direction (no normalization here — caller can scale for sign or rescale to unit norm if desired).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialMat-Tuple{FundMat, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Solvers","text":"EssentialMat(F::FundMat, K1::AbstractMatrix, K2::AbstractMatrix) -> EssentialMat\nEssentialMat(F::FundMat, K::AbstractMatrix)                      -> EssentialMat\n\nConstruct the calibrated epipolar matrix E = K₂ᵀ F K₁ from a fundamental matrix and per-view intrinsics.  Inverse of FundMat(E, K1, K2).  Replaces the older essential_from_fundamental helper which returned a raw SMatrix and hence lost the F→E type distinction at the call site.\n\nThis is the frame-free algebra; the checked, frame-labeled counterpart is composition on an EpipolarMap — the pixel → gnomonic frame change per argument slot, with the slot labels verifying that each K met the view it was meant for (see GnomonicFrame).\n\nK1, K2 may be supplied as raw SMatrix{3,3}, CameraCalibrationMat (any T<:Real), or any AbstractMatrix of size 3×3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FundMat-Tuple{EssentialMat, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Solvers","text":"FundMat(E::EssentialMat, K1::AbstractMatrix, K2::AbstractMatrix) -> FundMat\nFundMat(E::EssentialMat, K::AbstractMatrix)                      -> FundMat\n\nConstruct the (uncalibrated) epipolar matrix F = K₂⁻ᵀ E K₁⁻¹ from an essential matrix and per-view intrinsic matrices.  The shared-K overload assumes K₁ = K₂ = K (e.g. PhotoTourism scenes captured by one camera).\n\nInverse of EssentialMat(F, K1, K2).  Strongly-typed in both input (EssentialMat) and output (FundMat) so the calibrated / uncalibrated distinction is enforced at compile time.\n\nThis is the frame-free algebra of the gnomonic → pixel frame change; the checked, slot-labeled form of the same law is composition (∘) of an EpipolarMap with the HomographyMap{GnomonicFrame{id}, ImageFrame{id}}(K) lifts, as minted by EpipolarForm(rig) on a StereoRig (see essential_form).\n\nK1, K2 may be supplied as raw SMatrix{3,3}, CameraCalibrationMat (any T<:Real), or any AbstractMatrix of size 3×3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FundMat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cheiral_pose_fast-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Solvers","text":"cheiral_pose_fast(E, f₁, f₂) -> (R::SMatrix{3,3}, t::SVector{3})\n\nThe cheirality vote: of motion(E)'s four (R, t) candidates, the one with the most bearing pairs in front of BOTH cameras by the ray-domain test_cheirality (ties go to the earlier candidate). f₁, f₂ are equal-length vectors of unit bearings — the caller back-projects with its own camera; the z = 1 chart this used to build through K⁻¹ does not exist for a ray at 90°. t is unit (the essential's translation direction).\n\nRay depth is right in meaning and camera-general, not a rate fix: see triangulate's recorded negative result before re-attempting it as one. Moved here from VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's relative-pose bundle, which the evaluation protocol's cheirality also called.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cheiral_pose_fast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_rounding_scale-Tuple{AbstractFundMat, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Solvers","text":"constraint_rounding_scale(F::AbstractFundMat, u₁, u₂)\nconstraint_rounding_scale(E::EssentialMat, f₁, f₂; M₁, M₂)\n\nThe epipolar constraint's rounding scales (see the generic method): the point-gradient bound |F|·|s|, |F|ᵀ·|d| and the value bound |d|ᵀ·|F|·|s| at the homogeneous points, loose by a constant and relative to the model's own entries, so its homogeneous scale never decides. On bearings the gradient carries the backprojection Jacobians into pixels, so its scale carries their 1-norm too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_rounding_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractFundMat{T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(F::FundMat, u₁::SVector{2}, u₂::SVector{2})\nconstraint_with_jacobians(E::EssentialMat, x̂₁::SVector{2}, x̂₂::SVector{2})\n    -> (g::T, G_x::SVector{4,T}, G_θ::SVector{9,T})\n\nCompute the epipolar constraint g and its derivatives.\n\nThe scalar constraint g = ũ₂ᵀ F ũ₁ (one equation, d_g = 1).  Identical algebra for F and E — the wrapper type signals input coordinate space (pixels for FundMat, gnomonic points for EssentialMat).\n\nReturns:\n\ng::T              — epipolar constraint value\nG_x::SVector{4,T} — ∂g/∂(x₁,y₁,x₂,y₂) (observation Jacobian)\nG_θ::SVector{9,T} — ∂g/∂vec(F) (model Jacobian, column-major)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{EssentialMat{T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(E::EssentialMat, f₁, f₂; M₁, M₂) -> (g, G_x, G_θ)\n\nThe epipolar constraint on UNIT BEARINGS and its Jacobians: g = f₂ᵀ E f₁, with\n\nG_x = (f₂ᵀE·M₁, f₁ᵀEᵀ·M₂) — 1×4, in PIXEL coordinates, because M is the backprojection Jacobian ∂f/∂u from backproject_with_jacobian;\nG_θ = vec(f₂ f₁ᵀ) — ∂g/∂vec(E), column-major, unchanged from the plane form and feeding the same covariance machinery.\n\nM is TANGENT (fᵀM = 0), so no explicit projection onto the tangent plane is needed here or downstream — that is the property backproject_with_jacobian is built to guarantee.\n\nWhy the Jacobians are arguments rather than recomputed: they are per-point data, so they ride as attribute COLUMNS on the correspondence set (withattrs(cs; M₁, M₂)), per the correspondence standard. Recomputing them per residual would repeat a camera backprojection in the inner loop.\n\nThe plane sibling constraint_with_jacobians(F, u₁, u₂) is the same question in the z = 1 chart. Use that one for FundMat and this one for EssentialMat: F is a pixel-plane model, E is not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fundamental_7pt-Union{Tuple{T}, NTuple{14, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"fundamental_7pt(s₁..s₇, d₁..d₇) -> FixedModels{3,FundMat{T}} | nothing\n\nCompute fundamental matrices from exactly 7 point correspondences; T is the points' element type.\n\nReturns FixedModels{3,FundMat{T}} with 1–3 Frobenius-normalized solutions (F[3,3] >= 0), or nothing if the system is degenerate. Zero heap allocations.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nBuild 7×9 DLT matrix from Kronecker products\nPartitioned A\\B solve for 2D null space (zero-allocation)\nCubic determinant constraint: det(λ f₁ + f₂) = 0\n1 or 3 real roots → 1 or 3 F matrices\n\nExample\n\nresult = fundamental_7pt(s₁, s₂, s₃, s₄, s₅, s₆, s₇,\n                         d₁, d₂, d₃, d₄, d₅, d₆, d₇)\nif result !== nothing\n    for F in result  # iterates 1–3 FundMat solutions\n        # use F...\n    end\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fundamental_7pt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fundamental_dlt!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CorrespondenceNormalization{T}, AbstractVector{<:Pair{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}}}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"fundamental_dlt!(A, N::CorrespondenceNormalization, cs; mask = nothing, svd_ws = nothing) -> FundMat or nothing\n\nIn-place N-point DLT for the fundamental matrix of the correspondences cs using the pre-allocated buffer A (N × 9). Weights are the container's weights column, as for fundamental_dlt.\n\nWhen mask::BitVector is provided, only masked points are used and rows are compacted into the top of A. The inner-loop form: N is the enclosing estimator's CorrespondenceNormalization, built once at its entry (the public form builds it from the data); this form applies it row by row and composes the model back into the data frame (inv(D) ∘ F ∘ S), and never normalizes on its own.\n\nNo covariance is returned — as for homography_dlt!: the SVD's resolvent covariance is not this estimator's under pixel noise (algebraic rows are heteroscedastic); its first-order covariance, if ever needed, is implicit_covariance's block form with the DLT's identity weighting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fundamental_dlt!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fundamental_dlt-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Pair{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1}}}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"fundamental_dlt(cs; mask = nothing, svd_ws = nothing) -> FundMat or nothing\n\nCompute a fundamental matrix from N >= 8 correspondences cs (u₁ => u₂ pairs; any sources/targets container, a Correspondences preferred) by the Direct Linear Transform with Hartley normalization and rank-2 enforcement.\n\nPer-correspondence weights are the container's weights column (set with withattrs), never a keyword. When mask (a BitVector or an index list) is given, only those correspondences are used.\n\nExample\n\ncs = Correspondences(pts1, pts2)\nF = fundamental_dlt(cs)\nF = fundamental_dlt(cs; mask = inlier_mask)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fundamental_dlt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.motion-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Solvers","text":"motion(E::AbstractMatrix) -> NTuple{4, Tuple{SMatrix{3,3}, Vec3{Float64}}}\n\nExtract candidate (R, t) motion tuples from an essential matrix E.\n\nReturns all 4 combinations of the two possible rotations and two possible translation directions. Only one is physically valid (positive depth for both cameras), but for pose error evaluation we take the minimum over all 4.\n\nParallels motion(::HomographyMat) (Malis-Vargas decomposition for homographies — same shape, multi-candidate).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.motion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.motion-Tuple{HomographyMat}","page":"Solvers","text":"motion(H::HomographyMat) -> Vector{Tuple{RotMatrix{3,Float64}, Vec3{Float64}, Vec3{Float64}}}\n\nMalis–Vargas decomposition (Malis and Vargas, 2007) of a calibrated homography into up to 4 candidate (R, t, n) motions: rotation R, translation t = t_phys / d_phys, and unit plane normal n (sign fixed so n₃ ≥ 0).  Native port of PoseLib's motion_from_homography (single-SVD variant); the candidate order matches PoseLib exactly: (R₁,t₁,n₁), (R₁,−t₁,−n₁), (R₂,t₂,n₂), (R₂,−t₂,−n₂).\n\nFor the degenerate near-equal-outer-singular-value case (planar / pure rotation) it returns the 1-element list [(H̄, 0, 0)] (normalized homography, zero t/n), as PoseLib does.  Parallels motion(E) for essential matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.motion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.observation_gradient_jacobian-Tuple{AbstractFundMat, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Solvers","text":"observation_gradient_jacobian(F::AbstractFundMat, u₁, u₂) -> SMatrix{4, 9}\nobservation_gradient_jacobian(E::EssentialMat, f₁, f₂; M₁, M₂) -> SMatrix{4, 9}\n\n∂G_x/∂vec(F) of the epipolar constraint (column-major vec, the coordinates of its G_θ). The constraint is bilinear, so each entry is one coordinate of a point: on the plane G_x = ((Fᵀd)₁,₂, (Fs)₁,₂) and ∂(Fs)_k/∂F_ij = δ_ki·s_j, ∂(Fᵀd)_k/∂F_ij = δ_jk·d_i; on bearings the two pixel gradients are those carried by M₁, M₂ (per-point data, constant in the model).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.observation_gradient_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_cheirality-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_cheirality(pose::EuclideanMap{3}, f₁, f₂) -> Bool\n\nRay-domain cheirality: triangulate the bearing pair and require BOTH depths positive. The plane-free replacement for X[3] > 0, which assumes a forward hemisphere that a fisheye or omnidirectional camera does not have.\n\nParallel rays (no parallax) are rejected — NaN > 0 is false — which is the honest answer: nothing was triangulated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_cheirality"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_model-Tuple{FundMat, Any, Any}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_model(F::FundMat, u₁_sample, u₂_sample) -> Bool\n\nOriented epipolar constraint (Chum et al., 2004). For an exact correspondence, the epipolar line l₁ = Fᵀũ₂ and the line e₁ × ũ₁ are the same line in image 1 (both go through e₁ and ũ₁), so Fᵀũ₂ = μᵢ(e₁ × ũ₁) for some scalar μᵢ. The signum uses the dot product of all three components:\n\nsᵢ = (Fᵀũ₂ⁱ) · (e₁ × ũ₁ⁱ) = μᵢ · ‖e₁ × ũ₁ⁱ‖²\n\nSince ‖e₁ × ũ₁ⁱ‖² ≥ 0, sign(sᵢ) = sign(μᵢ). The code checks sᵢ · sⱼ > 0 for all pairs, i.e., that all μᵢ share the same sign. Using the full dot product (rather than a single component) avoids numerical instability when one component happens to be near zero.\n\nThis is a chirality parity check. The absolute sign of μᵢ is meaningless (F and e₁ are projective, defined up to scale), but if two correspondences give different signs, they cannot both be in front of both cameras. The test rejects F matrices that are algebraically valid (u₂ᵀFu₁ = 0) but geometrically inconsistent. An inner-loop gate: F and the points in the same frame, the estimator's normalization (sign-based, so any positive-scale normalization gives the same answer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.triangulate-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Solvers","text":"triangulate(pose::EuclideanMap{3}, f₁, f₂) -> (X, λ₁, λ₂)\n\nTriangulate a correspondence of UNIT BEARINGS: the midpoint of the mutual perpendicular between the two rays, in VIEW 1's frame, together with both ray depths. pose maps view 1 to view 2 (X₂ = R·X₁ + t).\n\nThe depths are the point of returning them. Cheirality on rays is `λ₁ > 0 && λ₂\n\n0` — the point is in front of both cameras along their own rays — which is\n\nthe plane-free replacement for testing X[3] > 0. A z-depth test silently assumes a forward hemisphere the ray domain does not have, so it is wrong for any camera that sees past 90°.\n\nReturns λ₁ = λ₂ = NaN for parallel rays (|f₁·R'f₂| → 1), which is degenerate rather than exceptional: it is what zero parallax looks like, and the caller decides. Never throws.\n\nWhy this form, and what it costs\n\nIt is the only form that GENERALISES. The pixel-domain DLT (uᵢ × Pᵢ X̃ = 0) builds P = K[I|0] — pinhole by construction — and calibrated geometry in this stack must work for fisheye and omnidirectional cameras, where that matrix does not exist. That is the deciding argument, and it is not a number.\n\nMEASURED against that DLT rather than assumed better, paired (identical scenes, points and noise draws), binned by parallax, on relative 3-D error. Reprojection error cannot discriminate the two — it sits at the noise level even where the 3-D error reaches 363 — so it is reported only as a sanity check. The trade is real and mixed:\n\napex angle med DLT → ray q99 DLT → ray ray wins (paired)\n[0, 0.1°) 0.488 → 0.567 21.9 → 1.73 43.3%\n[0.1, 0.3°) 0.170 → 0.174 3.69 → 1.38 45.4%\n[0.3, 1°) 0.0592 → 0.0585 0.354 → 0.336 48.0%\n[1, 3°) 0.0202 → 0.0201 0.092 → 0.0925 47.8%\n[3, 90°) 0.00597 → 0.00595 0.0279 → 0.0279 47.6%\n\nSo: it WINS ONLY IN THE TAIL, and decisively there — a q99 of 21.9 is a point placed at 22× its true distance, and those are what corrupt a bundle or a cheirality vote downstream. It is slightly WORSE on the median below 0.1° (43.3% of paired points, i.e. it loses more often than it wins), in a regime where both answers are already about half the true distance. Above 0.3° the two are indistinguishable to three significant figures. Noise-free both are exact to 1e-13–1e-16, so the entire difference is noise propagation near degeneracy, not conditioning.\n\nA NEGATIVE RESULT worth keeping: ray-depth cheirality does NOT reduce the behind-camera rate. Below 0.1° parallax it is 20.1% for the DLT's z-test and 20.4% here; in [0.1, 0.3°) both are 0.23%. That was predicted to improve and measured not to — at that parallax the noisy rays genuinely cross behind a camera, so it is the geometry, not the test. Ray depth is still the right statement (a z-test assumes a forward hemisphere the ray domain lacks), but it buys correctness of MEANING, not accuracy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.triangulate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.essential_5pt","page":"Solvers","text":"essential_5pt(x1, x2) -> FixedModels{10, EssentialMat{Float64}}\n\n5-point essential-matrix solver (Nistér 2004).  The bare form routes to NativeSolver (see essential_5pt_native.jl, a port of PoseLib's relpose_5pt.cc); essential_5pt(PoseLibSolver(), …) dispatches to the compiled PoseLib backend when PoseLib is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.essential_5pt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.essential_dlt","page":"Solvers","text":"essential_dlt(cs) -> EssentialMat{Float64} or nothing\nessential_dlt(backend, cs)\n\n8-point linear DLT for the essential matrix from N >= 8 correspondences cs (x1 => x2 bearing pairs, StaticVector{3} each side; any sources/targets container, a Correspondences preferred), the same correspondence currency as homography_dlt and fundamental_dlt. Only the PoseLib backend exists; load using PoseLib to make this callable (essential_dlt(PoseLibSolver(), cs), and the bare form routes to it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.essential_dlt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.p3p","page":"Solvers","text":"p3p(rays, points_3d) -> FixedModels{4, Extrinsics3}\n\nPerspective-3-Point solver.  The bare form routes to NativeSolver (see p3p_native.jl, a port of PoseLib's p3p.cc); p3p(PoseLibSolver(), …) dispatches to the compiled PoseLib backend when PoseLib is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.p3p"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.essential_5pt-Tuple{NativeSolver, Union{NTuple{5, var\"#s550\"} where var\"#s550\"<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T), StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{5}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, 1}}, Union{NTuple{5, var\"#s550\"} where var\"#s550\"<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T), StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{5}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, 1}}}","page":"Solvers","text":"essential_5pt(::NativeSolver, x1, x2) -> FixedModels{10, EssentialMat{Float64}}\n\nNistér 5-point relative pose (essential matrices only), a native Julia port of PoseLib's relpose_5pt. x1 and x2 hold five calibrated bearings each, as a tuple or a static vector of 3-vectors.\n\nTyped to 3-vectors so a 2-vector (a point on the z = 1 plane) is a MethodError rather than a DimensionMismatch from inside the kernel, the same refusal solve(::Essential5pt, …) and PoseLib's backend give. The type is a necessary condition, not a guarantee: a 3-vector still ON the z = 1 plane is accepted and, the epipolar algebra being scale-invariant per endpoint, even gives the right answer for rays the chart can represent. Bearings must come from the camera's back-projection (backproject_with_jacobian), where they are built.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.essential_5pt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.p3p-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{NativeSolver, AbstractVector{S}, AbstractVector{T}}} where {S<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, Float64, 1}, T<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, Float64, 1}}","page":"Solvers","text":"p3p(::NativeSolver, x, X) -> FixedModels{4, Extrinsics3}\n\nPerspective-3-Point, native Julia (port of PoseLib's p3p.cc). x must be three unit bearing vectors; X the corresponding 3D points. Up to four poses with x_cam = R·X + t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.p3p"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tls_line-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}}","page":"Solvers","text":"tls_line(points) -> Uncertain{Line2D{T}}\ntls_line(points, weights) -> Uncertain{Line2D{T}}\n\nTotal least squares (orthogonal distance regression) line through points (2-vectors): the centroid, and the scatter matrix's smallest-eigenvalue eigenvector as the normal — one closed-form step. The line is in Hesse form n⊤p + d = 0, n = (cos φ, sin φ), and carries the covariance of θ = (φ, d): s² (JᵀWJ)⁻¹, with s² = Σwᵢrᵢ²/(n₊−2) the residual variance, n₊ the number of points with positive weight and J the residual Jacobian — calibrated against Monte Carlo.\n\nweights are FIXED non-negative per-point weights (wᵢ = 1/σᵢ² for known isotropic variances): the weighted centroid, the weighted scatter, and a zero weight drops the point. Without them every point weighs 1. Throws ArgumentError for fewer than 3 points of positive weight, a negative or non-finite weight, or a weights whose length differs from points'.\n\nFor points carrying their own covariances, VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's fit_line (an iterative weighted fit) applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tls_line"},{"category":"function","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tls_line_statistics","page":"Solvers","text":"tls_line_statistics(points, weights[, normal]) -> (line, S, JᵀWJ)\n\nThe weighted total-least-squares line of tls_line(points, weights) with the two statistics its covariance is made of, both at the returned line: the weighted residual sum S = Σwᵢrᵢ² and the (φ, d) information JᵀWJ (J's rows (tᵀpᵢ, 1), t the line's direction). The residual variance is S/(n₊ − 2) and the covariance S/(n₊ − 2)·(JᵀWJ)⁻¹; a caller that owns its own variance model reads the pieces instead.\n\nWith normal given (a 2-vector, taken as is), the step estimates the offset only: the line with that normal through the weighted centroid — the least-squares offset for a known normal.\n\nweights as for tls_line: FIXED and non-negative, a zero weight drops the point; there is no minimum count here (the covariance's is the caller's), but weights that sum to zero throw ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tls_line_statistics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nearest_point-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Pair{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, <:FlexUnits.Quantity, 1}, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T}}}, Tuple{N}} where N","page":"Solvers","text":"nearest_point(lines) -> SVector{N}\n\nThe point x nearest to the lines in the least-squares sense: the minimizer of Σᵢ wᵢ ‖(I − dᵢdᵢᵀ)(x − oᵢ)‖², the weighted sum of squared distances from x to each line, by one closed-form solve of the normal equations (Σ wᵢPᵢ) x = Σ wᵢPᵢoᵢ, Pᵢ = I − dᵢdᵢᵀ (the projector onto the line's normal space).\n\nlines is a vector of origin => direction pairs (a Correspondences by correspondences(origins, directions), or a plain vector of Pairs): oᵢ any point on the line, dᵢ its direction, normalized inside (any nonzero length). N-dimensional: 3-D lines and 2-D lines alike. Per-line weights are the weights attribute column (withattrs(lines; weights = w)): FIXED, non-negative, a zero weight drops the line; without it every line weighs 1.\n\nUnits: origins that are lengths (SVector(1.0, 2.0, 3.0) .* mm) are stripped in metres at entry and the point comes back in metres; plain origins give a plain point in the caller's own unit. The solve runs about the weighted centroid of the origins, so translating every line by t translates the point by t to rounding.\n\nThrows ArgumentError for fewer than two lines of positive weight, a zero or non-finite direction, or lines that do not determine a point — all parallel, the normal matrix singular to 1e-12 of its largest eigenvalue.\n\nlines = correspondences([SVector(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), SVector(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)], [SVector(0.0, 0.0, 1.0), SVector(-1.0, 0.0, 1.0)])\nnearest_point(lines)   # [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]: where the two lines meet\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nearest_point"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Circle3pt","page":"Solvers","text":"Circle3pt()\n\nMinimal 3-point circle solver: the circle through three 2D points (the circumcircle; solve(Circle3pt(), points, idx)), sample_size 3, max_solutions 1. Its data are POINTS on the curve, an AbstractVector of StaticVector{2} — a circle has no second view, so there is no correspondence to form, as for tls_line and direct_ellipse. The solve is closed form and frame-covariant (pixels in, a circle in pixels out), computed about the sample's first point so it needs no normalization.\n\nA (near-)collinear sample is the sample gate's to refuse, before the solve (test_sample(Circle, points, idx), the homography's collinearity ratio). The solver keeps only the last line of defence: exactly collinear points (a zero determinant) or a non-finite circle give count == 0.\n\nDeliberately NOT entered in the minimal_solvers registry: that registry answers correspondence element-type pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Circle3pt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Ellipse5pt","page":"Solvers","text":"Ellipse5pt()\n\nMinimal 5-point ellipse solver: the conic through five 2D points (solve(Ellipse5pt(), points, idx)), sample_size 5, max_solutions 1, returned only when it is a real, non-degenerate ELLIPSE (is_ellipse: a hyperbola, a parabola, a line pair or a point/imaginary ellipse gives count == 0). Its data are POINTS on the curve, an AbstractVector of StaticVector{2}, as for Circle3pt and direct_ellipse. The conic is the null vector of the 4×5 design matrix of the other four points taken about the sample's first point (through which the conic has no constant term), by cofactors: closed form, allocation-free and frame-covariant (pixels in, an ellipse in pixels out), with no normalization.\n\nA sample with three (near-)collinear points — whose conic is a line pair — is the sample gate's to refuse, before the solve (test_sample(Ellipse, points, idx), the homography's collinearity ratio). The solver keeps only the conic classification and a non-finite guard.\n\nDeliberately NOT entered in the minimal_solvers registry: that registry answers correspondence element-type pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Ellipse5pt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.direct_ellipse-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}}","page":"Solvers","text":"direct_ellipse(points) -> Ellipse{T} | nothing\n\nThe ellipse through the 2-D points minimizing the summed squared algebraic residual Σᵢ (xᵢᵀ C xᵢ)² under the ellipse constraint 4AC − B² = 1 (Fitzgibbon, Pilu & Fisher 1999, in the numerically stable form of Halir & Flusser 1998), computed in Hartley-normalized coordinates: one closed-form step whose solution is always an ellipse. nothing when the points admit none (too few in general position, e.g. collinear). Throws ArgumentError for fewer than 5 points.\n\nA start value: the algebraic residual weighs points by where they sit on the curve. VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's refine_ellipse minimizes the orthogonal distance from it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.direct_ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ellipse_from_log_shape-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Solvers","text":"ellipse_from_log_shape(m) -> Ellipse\n\nThe ellipse of the log-shape chart vector m = (cx, cy, S₁₁, S₁₂, S₂₂). Inverse of ellipse_log_shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ellipse_from_log_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ellipse_log_shape-Union{Tuple{Ellipse{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"ellipse_log_shape(e::Ellipse) -> SVector{5}\n\nThe log-shape chart of e: (cx, cy, S₁₁, S₁₂, S₂₂), the centre and the symmetric S = R·diag(log a, log b)·Rᵀ (R the rotation by e.θ), so that the boundary is {c + exp(S)·u : ‖u‖ = 1}. Minimal, unable to leave the ellipse cone (the exponential of every symmetric S is positive definite) and smooth at a circle, where (a, b, θ) swaps its axes. Inverse of ellipse_from_log_shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ellipse_log_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.assert_unit_bearings-Tuple{Any, AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Solvers","text":"assert_unit_bearings(bearings, which, who; remedy) -> nothing\n\nThe entry gate of an estimator that takes BEARINGS (unit rays, StaticVector{3}): every one finite, then every one of unit norm, else an ArgumentError from who (the estimator) naming the first non-finite, or the worst-normed, entry of the which column (\"source\", \"target\") and the remedy (how to back-project).\n\nStaticVector{3} is a necessary condition, not a guarantee: a z = 1 chart point has three coordinates and gives the right answer for every ray the chart can represent, so its exclusion of rays at and past 90° survives the type unseen. The finite check comes first: NaN > worst is false, so a NaN bearing (a pixel the camera cannot back-project, past a fisheye's fold) would get through the norm test.\n\nThe norm tolerance is the gap between the two kinds of input, not a rounding bound: a chart point's norm is sqrt(1 + x² + y²), 1 only on the optical axis (where the two are the same vector) and 1.0185 already at a narrow lens's 11°, while a normalized ray is unit to a few ulps. 1e-6 lies between, with room for a bearing computed in Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.assert_unit_bearings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_consensus-Tuple{HomographyMat, Any, AbstractVector{Bool}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_consensus(model, cs, mask) -> Bool\n\nValidate a model against its consensus (inlier) set mask. For homography this re-runs cheirality on the full inlier set (catches bow-ties that a sample-only check misses). F/E have no consensus-set gate today and return true. An inner-loop gate: model and cs in the same frame (cheirality is sign-based, so any positive-scale normalization gives the same answer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_consensus"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_dlt_conditioning-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SvdWorkspace}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_dlt_conditioning(ws::SvdWorkspace; max_cond = 100.0, null_dim = 1) -> Bool\n\nIs the DLT design matrix of the solve that filled ws well enough conditioned to trust? dlt_conditioning(ws; null_dim) <= max_cond. An inner-loop gate: true succeeds, like every other test_* here.\n\nNOT test_conditioning, and the difference is the point. test_conditioning(H) measures the MODEL matrix; this measures the DESIGN matrix's null-space gap — whether the solution direction is isolated. A degenerate configuration can hand back a perfectly well-conditioned H from a design matrix whose null space is not, which is exactly what a collinear correspondence set does. The two thresholds are both 100.0 by default, on different matrices, so a wrong-matrix gate looks plausibly calibrated: the name has to carry which matrix is meant, because the number does not.\n\nThe defaults (max_cond, null_dim) = (100.0, 1) are the one threshold: VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's DLT refit calls this at them for the relative families. max_cond = Inf never refuses. The refusal is a STRICT >, so a ratio of exactly max_cond succeeds.\n\nThe threshold is only comparable across callers that solve in the same frame — see dlt_conditioning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_dlt_conditioning"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_model-Tuple{HomographyMat, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_model(H::HomographyMat, cs, idx) -> Bool\n\nHomography feasibility: SVD conditioning (κ ≤ 100) AND projective cheirality (uniform-sign λ) over the sample. Composes test_conditioning and test_cheirality. An inner-loop gate: H and cs are both in the estimator's normalization, CorrespondenceNormalization(HomographyMat, cs) — where the κ bound is calibrated.\n\n(test_model(F::FundMat, cs, idx) — the oriented epipolar check — is defined in geometry/solvers/fundmat.jl.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample-Tuple{Type{<:Circle}, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_sample(::Type{<:Circle}, points, idx) -> Bool\n\nReject a degenerate 3-point circle sample: three (near-)collinear points — a repeated point among them — whose circle does not exist or runs off to an unbounded radius. The test is the homography gate's collinearity ratio (thin_ratio(p₁, p₂, p₃) above the same ≈ 0.1% of the longest side), a ratio and so frame-free. An inner-loop gate, run before Circle3pt's solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample-Tuple{Type{<:Ellipse}, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_sample(::Type{<:Ellipse}, points, idx) -> Bool\n\nReject a degenerate 5-point ellipse sample: three (near-)collinear points among the five — a repeated point among them — whose conic is a line pair. The test is the homography and circle gates' collinearity ratio (thin_ratio of every triple above the same ≈ 0.1% of its longest side), a ratio and so frame-free. An inner-loop gate, run before Ellipse5pt's solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample-Tuple{Type{<:EssentialMat}, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_sample(::Type{<:EssentialMat}, cs, idx; tol_px = COINCIDENT_PX) -> Bool\n\nThe same coincidence question for E on UNIT BEARINGS: are any two of the sample's bearings the same detection? Bearings are directions, so the comparison cannot be a distance between them — it is the angle, converted to pixels through each point's backprojection Jacobian.\n\ncs carries M₁/M₂ as attribute COLUMNS (withattrs(cs; M₁, M₂)), the 3×2 Jacobians from backproject_with_jacobian; the pair's separation in pixels is estimated as angle(fₐ, f_b) / σ_max(M). Without those columns (any container without them, a plain Pair vector included) this takes the shared bearing-free path of the F method, because there is then no way to convert an angle into the caller's units — and guessing one would be a threshold with nothing behind it.\n\nThe estimate is CONSERVATIVE, and by how much\n\nM is generally anisotropic: a pixel step produces a different angle along its major and minor singular directions. Dividing by σ_max therefore UNDER-states the separation of a pair displaced along the minor direction, by exactly the factor 1/aniso (aniso = σ_max/σ_min) — measured to five digits across a fisheye field. Rejection happens when the estimate falls below tol_px, so the gate actually rejects true separations up to tol_px × aniso: the effective threshold is tol_px along M's major axis and tol_px × aniso along its minor.\n\nLarger, not smaller — so it never UNDER-rejects, which is the failure a deduplication backstop must not have. It begins over-rejecting genuinely DISTINCT features only when tol_px × aniso > d_min, i.e. aniso > d_min / tol_px — aniso > 10 for the default 0.1 px and features no closer than 1 px. A moderate KannalaBrandt fisheye (f = 300, k1 = −0.02) reaches aniso 1.48 at field radius 386 px, an effective 0.148 px: about 7× below where distinct features live. Stated because this stack exists for strongly distorted lenses, and a field-varying threshold is otherwise the kind of thing that resurfaces as unexplained behaviour at the periphery.\n\nThe tangency contract\n\nM is tangent to its bearing — fᵀM = 0, measured to 4e-20 across the field — which is why no explicit projection onto the tangent plane appears here or in any consumer. It is a CONTRACT, not an observation: a violation would show up as slow drift rather than an error, so it is pinned at backproject_with_jacobian and depended on here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample-Tuple{Type{<:FundMat}, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_sample(::Type{<:FundMat}, cs, idx) -> Bool\n\nReject minimal F samples with coincident points — the only degeneracy gated pre-solve for the epipolar solvers, and unlike homography there is NO other check here, so this one carries the whole gate. Arity-agnostic (a 7- or 8-point sample alike); E has its own method below.\n\ncs is in PIXELS and the tolerance is COINCIDENT_PX (see the note above COINCIDENT_PX). With no collinearity check behind it, a duplicate detection this gate admits goes straight into the solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample-Tuple{Type{<:HomographyMat}, Any, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Solvers","text":"test_sample(::Type{<:HomographyMat}, cs, idx) -> Bool\n\nReject degenerate 4-point homography samples: bow-tie (mixed orientation), coincident points, or any 3-of-4 (near-)collinear. An inner-loop gate.\n\nUNITS ARE MIXED HERE, AND DELIBERATELY SO. The coincidence part takes cs in PIXELS and its tolerance is COINCIDENT_PX. The other two parts are frame-independent and take whatever frame they are given: _orientation_preserving is a SIGN, and _not_collinear is a RATIO (thin_ratio, ≈ 0.1% of the longest side), so neither has a unit to get wrong.\n\n_not_collinear STAYS, although coincidence catches duplicate detections and it can look redundant: two near-identical points make every triangle through them thin, so it also rejects duplicates, incidentally. Genuine collinearity is a DIFFERENT degeneracy, the one it is for: a 3-of-4 collinear sample makes the homography's 3×3 LU systems rank-deficient regardless of how distinct the points are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.test_sample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SvdWorkspace","page":"Solvers","text":"SvdWorkspace{T}(max_rows, ncols)\n\nPre-allocated workspace for in-place SVD via LAPACK gesdd! (divide-and-conquer). Eliminates all heap allocations from repeated SVD calls on matrices up to max_rows × ncols.\n\nFloat64 only: the workspace calls the double-precision dgesdd directly, so SvdWorkspace{Float64} is the one constructible instance (the type parameter stays so that a caller's SvdWorkspace{T} field type still reads).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SvdWorkspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dlt_conditioning-Union{Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SvdWorkspace{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Solvers","text":"dlt_conditioning(ws::SvdWorkspace; null_dim::Int = 1) -> Float64\n\nThe DLT design matrix's null-space gap from the solve that filled ws: S[1] / max(S[n - null_dim], eps()), the largest singular value over the smallest NON-null one (n = length(ws.S); for the 9-column homography and fundamental systems, S[1]/S[8]). The eps() floor is load-bearing: an exactly rank-deficient design matrix returns an enormous ratio rather than dividing by zero, which is how a collinear correspondence set announces itself.\n\nA OBSERVATION, not a policy: it neither rejects nor alters the solve. Pair it with test_dlt_conditioning, whose default 100.0 is the one threshold: VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's refine!(::DltWorkspace, …) refuses a relative-family hypothesis by calling it at that default.\n\nONLY MEANINGFUL IN THE COORDINATES THE SOLVE USED. The ratio is a property of the design matrix as built, so it depends on the normalization AND on the weights column that went into it. VGC's public DLT forms build a HartleyNormalization, which is what makes a threshold of 100.0 mean the same thing here as in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation — a caller that solves in another frame must recalibrate its threshold rather than carry this one across.\n\nREFLECTS THE LAST SOLVE that filled this workspace, and nothing checks that for you: read it immediately after the homography_dlt!/fundamental_dlt! call whose matrix you mean. There is deliberately no staleness flag — adding one means a field on SvdWorkspace, which VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation constructs too, so it would turn a local guard into a cross-repo API change and a lockstep window, to protect against a mistake this sentence prevents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dlt_conditioning"},{"category":"method","location":"api/solvers/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.svd_nullvec!-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SvdWorkspace{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, Int64, Val{N}}} where {T<:Float64, N}","page":"Solvers","text":"svd_nullvec!(ws::SvdWorkspace, A, m, Val(N)) -> SVector{N}\n\nCompute null space vector (last row of Vt) of the first m rows of A (with N columns) using pre-allocated workspace. Zero allocations. A is overwritten by LAPACK. N must be a compile-time constant via Val.\n\nA wide system (m < N, e.g. the 8 × 9 design matrix of a 4-point homography or an 8-point fundamental DLT) is factored in full (JOBZ = 'A'): its thin factorization has only m right singular vectors, none of them the null vector. The singular values it does not have are zeroed, so dlt_conditioning reads such a system as rank-deficient only when it is. A LAPACK failure throws LAPACKException.\n\nExample\n\nws = SvdWorkspace{Float64}(100, 9)\nv = svd_nullvec!(ws, A, size(A,1), Val(9))  # -> SVector{9,Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.svd_nullvec!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#Student-t-Uniform-Scoring-API","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"The scale-free marginal score of \"RANSAC Scoring Done Right\" (moved from RANSACScoringDoneRight, which keeps its research outlier models, grids and loss-width families on these types): the scorer, its label priors and outlier models, its workspace and the one partition sweep. The scoring contract it implements is on the Robust Estimation API page.","title":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#Index","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"Pages = [\"student_t_scoring.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LABEL_PRIOR_TAGS","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"LABEL_PRIOR_TAGS\n\nThe label priors' TagRegistry (family suffix LabelPrior): \"flat\" (FlatLabelPrior), \"profiled\" (ProfiledLabelPrior) and \"beta\" (BetaLabelPrior), the values of a Student-t scorer table's label_prior key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LABEL_PRIOR_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLabelPrior","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"AbstractLabelPrior\n\nThe prior of a partition's labels in StudentTUniformScoring: it adds a term that depends on the partition only through its inlier count k, held in the workspace's label-prior table (label_prior_table), so the sort-and-sweep stays exact. Its TOML tag is LABEL_PRIOR_TAGS'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLabelPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractOutlierModel","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"AbstractOutlierModel\n\nThe outlier component of StudentTUniformScoring: what a partition's complement pays. Core's is the supplied uniform half-width, FixedHalfwidth. A model that ESTIMATES the support from the residuals (RANSACScoringDoneRight's extreme-value and generalized Pareto models) is scored by the estimated-support sweep, model-certain at codimension 1, through its methods of outlier_cost and scored_outlier_halfwidth (and optionally outlier_context!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractOutlierModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BetaLabelPrior","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"BetaLabelPrior(alpha, beta)\n\nThe inlier probability integrated out under its conjugate prior Beta(alpha, beta) (BetaLabelPrior(0.5, 0.5) is Jeffreys'): the partition gains log B(k + alpha, N - k + beta) - log B(alpha, beta). Both parameters are the caller's (a configuration's alpha and beta keys).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BetaLabelPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedHalfwidth","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"FixedHalfwidth(outlier_halfwidth)\n\nThe uniform outlier density at a supplied half-width per axis (the profile-marginal score): the complement pays log(2·outlier_halfwidth) per point and axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedHalfwidth"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FlatLabelPrior","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"FlatLabelPrior()\n\nThe inlier probability fixed at γ = ½: every partition has prior weight 2^{-N}, so the label prior adds nothing (the profile-marginal score's choice).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FlatLabelPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ProfiledLabelPrior","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"ProfiledLabelPrior()\n\nThe inlier probability profiled at γ̂ = k/N: the partition gains k log(k/N) + (N-k) log((N-k)/N).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ProfiledLabelPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RadixPermBuffers","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"RadixPermBuffers{K, I}(n)    # a permutation of integer type I (an Int32 permutation of a device's sort, say)\nRadixPermBuffers{K}(n)       # I = Int\nRadixPermBuffers(n)          # K = UInt32 (Float32 key precision), I = Int — THE default\n\nScratch buffers for radix_sortperm! — the allocation-free stable LSD radix sortperm used by the sweep! single-sort fast path. Owned by ScoringWorkspace; sized to the data count and resized with the other sweep buffers (resize!(sws, n)).\n\nKey width K — the sort-precision knob (2026-07-26)\n\nThe radix does sizeof(K) byte iterations, each scattering a K key plus an Int perm entry, so the key width sets both the iteration count and the scatter bandwidth. K = UInt32 (sort keys at Float32 precision) is the default: measured 1.65× faster than UInt64 on real PhotoTourism residual columns (10.12 → 6.15 µs/hypothesis; ≈1.16× on the whole marginal per-pair scorer), and it loses nothing, because the scored residuals are Float32 to begin with — a pool's residual matrix is a Matrix{Float32}, so the Float64 sweep buffer holds an exactly-representable widening and the round trip back to Float32 is lossless. Verified on that pool: the emitted permutation is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the UInt64 path in 150/150 hypotheses (worst residual delta exactly 0.0).\n\nK = UInt64 remains available and is the right choice for a caller whose residuals carry genuine Float64 information (they are then ordered at full precision). Both widths run the SAME generic code below — the width is a type parameter, not a second implementation.\n\nOrdering semantics at K = UInt32: the key is monotone but not injective on Float64 (values within one Float32 ulp collapse to equal keys). Stability then orders such a tie group by index, exactly as a stable comparison sort under an isless that cannot separate them. Every sweep prefix boundary inside such a group is between residuals equal to Float32 precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RadixPermBuffers"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"ScoringWorkspace\n\nPre-allocated scratch buffers for the scale-free marginal score (Algorithm 1), the scoring contract's scoring_workspace of a StudentTUniformScoring: the scorer carries only the immutable spec, the mutable workspace lives with the caller (a RANSAC's workspace holds one per thread).\n\nPrior consistency\n\nThe log Γ table is built at allocation time with a specific α₀ — once a ScoringWorkspace is constructed it is committed to that scale prior, and to one codimension d_g (the table's log Γ(k·d_g/2 + α₀), recorded as its dg, which every sweep on it reads), and its label-prior table to that label prior (rebuilt for the swept n when it differs from the table's). scale_prior(sws) is the single source of truth for which prior the buffers are configured for; the bare sweep!(sws, …) reads it from here rather than accepting one, which would risk pairing a Jeffreys table with an inverse-gamma score.\n\nRead it through its accessors: sweep_order (the order of the last sweep; its first k* entries are the consensus set), penalties and sort_key (the predictive score's per-point penalty and ordering key, filled before a sweep), lg_table, scale_prior. Build it with scoring_workspace(scoring, n, d_g) (= alloc_scoring_workspace) or default_scoring_workspace(n; dg, scale_prior, label_prior).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#Base.resize!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace, Integer}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"resize!(sws::ScoringWorkspace, n) -> sws\n\nSize every per-point buffer of sws for a problem of n points: the sort permutation and its scratch, the predictive penalties (zeroed, as a fresh workspace has them) and sort_key, the radix perm-sort scratch — keeping the exact-size radix fast path of sweep! reachable — and the launch's sorted column. A label-prior table is REBUILT for n from the workspace's label prior, since its terms depend on N. The estimated-support buffers, when present, are resized with the rest.\n\nThe log Γ table is not resized: its entries depend on k (and the workspace's codimension) alone, so a table of capacity ≥ n serves any n. n > length(lg_table(sws)) throws; build a larger workspace instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.resize!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.default_scoring_workspace-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"default_scoring_workspace(n; dg = 1, scale_prior, label_prior) -> ScoringWorkspace\n\nA scoring workspace for callers that sweep without a StudentTUniformScoring in hand (a per-blob adaptation, a host-side seed loop): length-n buffers for a problem of n points with codimension dg, committed to scale_prior (the log Γ table's α₀) and label_prior (e.g. FlatLabelPrior()), both required — the stack's default priors are a configuration's, not this function's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.default_scoring_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.estimates_outlier_support-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractOutlierModel}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"estimates_outlier_support(model::AbstractOutlierModel) -> Bool\n\nWhether model estimates the outlier support from the residuals (true for every model but the supplied half-width, FixedHalfwidth): its workspace then carries the estimated-support sweep's buffers (the sorted distances and their two suffix sums).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.estimates_outlier_support"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ig_sweep_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"OnBackend(backend; workgroupsize = 1)(ig_sweep_kernel!)(scores_out, sorted_sq_flat, pair_offset, K_per_pair,\n                 M_per_pair, lg_table, log_two_outlier_halfwidth_grid, model_dof, dg, prior, p_offset,\n                 sorted_penalties, prior_table, best_k_out; ndrange = (n, M_max, T))\n\nOne work-item per (pair, model, grid-point) of a flat pool layout (per pair its models' sorted squared residuals, K_per_pair[p] each, from pair_offset[p]; Core's exact_pm_score_kernel! layout): ONE sequential sweep over the model's K sorted squared residuals, writing the best partition score max_k S(k) to scores_out[p_local, m, t] (Float64). S(k) is the one per-partition formula (partition_score, indexed by the partition size) at the grid point's log(2·outlier_halfwidth), less half the running sum of sorted_penalties (per point, in the same order; nothing: none) and plus prior_table[k] (nothing: none). RSS and the penalty sum accumulate in Float64. With best_k_out of nonzero size, the first k at the best score goes to best_k_out[p_local, m, t] (0 when no partition scores above −Inf). This is the stack's ONE prefix sweep: a pool grid (RANSACScoringDoneRight's marginal grid, penalty- and prior-free, no k) and sweep! (one work item on the CPU backend over its sorted column) both launch it. Work-items beyond a pair's model count write nothing. A work item is a loop over the model's residuals: launch it at one item per workgroup through OnBackend, the stack's launch policy (every thread on the CPU, one warp per workgroup on a device).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ig_sweep_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.label_prior_table-Tuple{Int64, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FlatLabelPrior}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"label_prior_table(n, label_prior) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe label prior's term for k = 1..n inliers of n points (a workspace's table; empty for the flat prior, which adds nothing).\n\nThe profiled prior's term is the log of γ̂^{k}(1−γ̂)^{n−k} at γ̂ = k/n, k·log(k/n) + (n−k)·log((n−k)/n) (−n·H(k/n), H the binary entropy; 0·log 0 = 0, so the k = n term is 0); the Beta prior's log B(k + α, n − k + β) − log B(α, β).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.label_prior_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lg_table-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"lg_table(sws::ScoringWorkspace) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe log Γ(k·dg/2 + α₀) table the workspace was built with (log_gamma_table); its length is the largest n the workspace can sweep (see resize!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lg_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.log_gamma_table","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"log_gamma_table(n, d_g = 1; alpha0 = 0.0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nlog Γ(k·d_g/2 + α₀) for k = 1..n (SpecialFunctions' loggamma): the sweep's table, one path for Jeffreys (α₀ = 0) and the inverse-gamma prior (α₀ > 0). A sweep reads entry k − m (the inliers' ν/2 = d_g·(k − m)/2).\n\nCost: ~5000 host-side loggamma calls per probe (~0.5 ms total), read-only on the device for the entire sweep. (A log recurrence specialised on Jeffreys was retired for this one path: uniform Float64 values, no recurrence drift.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.log_gamma_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_context!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractOutlierModel, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"outlier_context!(model, z, T1, T2, n) -> context\n\nWhat an outlier model that estimates the support precomputes once per hypothesis, from the sorted distances z[1:n] and their suffix sums (suffix_sums!: T1, T2, each of length n + 1, T1 free to be overwritten as scratch); handed to every outlier_cost. nothing by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_context!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_cost","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"outlier_cost(model, context, z, T1, T2, k, n) -> Float64\n\nThe complement's cost (-log density) of the partition of the k smallest of the sorted distances z[1:n] (k < n), under an outlier model that estimates the support: the hook the estimated-support sweep calls per prefix. A model implements it (the fallback is an ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_cost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.partition_score-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IGPrior, Any, Any, T, Any}} where T","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"partition_score(prior::IGPrior, lg_half_dof, half_dof, RSS, outlier_cost) -> T\n\nThe marginal score of one inlier/outlier partition: the log evidence of its inliers under the Gaussian with the scale integrated out against prior (the inverse-gamma IGPrior(α₀, β₀); Jeffreys at IGPrior(0, 0)), minus the outliers' cost,\n\nS = lg_half_dof − (ν/2)·log 2π − (ν/2 + α₀)·log(RSS/2 + β₀) − outlier_cost,\n\nwith half_dof = ν/2 (ν = dg·(k − m), the inliers' residual dof), `lghalfdof = log Γ(ν/2 + α₀)(a sweep's log Γ table entry, or a device lgamma's value) andRSSthe inliers' squared residual sum, floored by the caller. Every sweep of the stack scores its partitions with this one formula —sweep!, the parametric and estimated-support sweeps, the device grid kernel (igsweep_kernel!) and a downstream device sweep (BlobBoards' per-blob sweep). Plain arithmetic inRSS's precisionT` (Float32 on a device), callable inside a KernelAbstractions kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.partition_score"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.penalties-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"penalties(sws::ScoringWorkspace) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nPer-point log-determinant penalties ℓᵢ = log|Vᵢ| of predictive scoring (StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive}), filled before a sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.penalties"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.prefix_sweep-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Integer, Integer, Any, Integer, Integer, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IGPrior, Any, Any, Any}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"prefix_sweep(T, sorted_sq, base, K, lg_table, model_dof, dg, prior, log_two_outlier_halfwidth,\n             sorted_penalties, prior_table) -> (S*, k*)\n\nThe loop body of the stack's one prefix sweep (ig_sweep_kernel!), callable inside a KernelAbstractions kernel on every backend: ONE sequential sweep over the K ascending squared residuals sorted_sq[base + 1 : base + K] (a global array, or a kernel's @localmem with base = 0), returning the best partition score S* = max_k S(k) and the first k* at it (k* = 0 when no partition scores above −Inf, as when K ≤ model_dof). S(k) is partition_score under prior at ν = dg·(k − model_dof), with the log Γ entry lg_table[k − model_dof] (log_gamma_table(K, dg; alpha0 = prior.alpha0)), the RSS floored at eps(T) and the K − k outliers paying dg·log(2·outlier_halfwidth) each; less half the running sum of sorted_penalties (in the same order; nothing: none) and plus prior_table[k] (nothing: none). RSS and the penalty sum accumulate in T (Float64 in the grid kernel and sweep!; a device kernel may sweep in its own Float32). Positional arguments only, so it compiles inside a kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.prefix_sweep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radix_key-Tuple{Type{UInt64}, Float64}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"radix_key(K, x) -> K\nradix_key(x) -> UInt64\n\nMonotone key: radix_key(K, a) < radix_key(K, b) ⟹ isless(a, b). For K = UInt64 the converse holds too — radix_key(a) == radix_key(b) ⟺ neither isless(a, b) nor isless(b, a) (every NaN maps to the single maximum key). Total-order equivalent of isless on all Float64 values, including ±0.0 (distinct, -0.0 < +0.0 — exactly isless's ordering) and ±Inf. radix_key(x) is the UInt64 (full-Float64) width.\n\nFor K = UInt32 a Float64 is first rounded to Float32 (the precision the scored residuals actually carry); the map stays monotone non-decreasing, so the emitted order is a valid ascending order, with ties broken by index through LSD stability. A Float32 at UInt32 keys is the identity precision (exact isless order), at UInt64 keys a lossless widening.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radix_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radix_sortperm!-Union{Tuple{I}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{Vector{I}, AbstractVector{<:Union{Float32, Float64}}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RadixPermBuffers{K, I}, Int64}} where {K<:Unsigned, I<:Integer}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"radix_sortperm!(perm, target, rb::RadixPermBuffers, n) -> nothing\n\nperm a Vector{I} of the buffers' integer type I. Fill perm[1:n] with the stable ascending-isless permutation of target[1:n] under the key radix_key(K, ·): bitwise identical to sortperm!(perm, target) for every input when the key is injective on it (K = UInt64, or Float32 target; the stability argument in the header comment). The default K = UInt32 on Float64 input orders values within one Float32 ulp by index, not by value. O(n): one fused histogram scan, then ≤ 8 stable counting iterations over byte digits, skipping digits on which all keys agree (a stable no-op). Allocation-free; buffers may have capacity ≥ n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.radix_sortperm!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_prior-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"scale_prior(sws::ScoringWorkspace) -> IGPrior\n\nThe scale prior the workspace is committed to (that sweep!(sws, …) scores under); a StudentTUniformScoring carries its own (the scoring contract's scale_prior).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_prior"},{"category":"function","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scored_outlier_halfwidth","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"scored_outlier_halfwidth(model, context, z, T1, T2, k, n) -> Float64\n\nThe uniform-equivalent outlier half-width the selected partition (k of n) was scored with, recorded on the workspace (what the scorer's decision_boundary reads); NaN for a model that states none. A model implements it (the fallback is an ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scored_outlier_halfwidth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sort_key-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"sort_key(sws::ScoringWorkspace) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe per-point sweep ordering key of predictive scoring (the unit-scale Mahalanobis q), filled before a sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sort_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.suffix_sums!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"suffix_sums!(T1, T2, z) -> (T1, T2)\n\nThe suffix sums of the sorted values z (length n) into T1 and T2 (length n + 1, T·[n+1] = 0): T1[g] = Σ_{l≥g} z[l], T2[g] = Σ_{l≥g} (n-l+1)·z[l], ranks counted from the top, so a short tail's moments never come from a difference of two large sums.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.suffix_sums!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{Int64}, Vector{Float64}, Int64, Float64, Int64, AbstractVector{T}, Int64}} where T","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"sweep!(perm, lg_table, model_dof, log_two_outlier_halfwidth, dg, scores, n;\n       scratch, penalties, sort_key, prior_table, scale_prior, radix, launch) -> (S*, k*)\n\nThe sort-and-sweep of the scale-free marginal score (Algorithm 1): sorts the points (ascending by scores, or sort_key if given), then sweeps the prefixes k = m+1, …, n scoring each partition by partition_score under scale_prior (required: the stack's default prior is a configuration's). Returns the best score S* and its inlier count k*; perm[1:k*] is the selected support. Mutates perm.\n\nThe per-hypothesis path — the one KernelAbstractions kernel\n\nThe prefix sweep is ONE KernelAbstractions kernel, ig_sweep_kernel!: a pool grid launches it per (pair, model, width), and this function launches it as one work item on the CPU backend over the hypothesis's sorted column (the owner's ruling on the 2026-11-11 twin audit, V2: the host's own scan is gone). This function is the DRIVER around it: the sort (the radix below), the gather of the sorted column and its per-point penalties, the label prior's table, and the exact parametric sweep for heterogeneous gains (a different enumeration, no prefix scan). With the workspace's launch buffers (every sweep! on a ScoringWorkspace) the hypothesis allocates nothing; without them the call allocates its column and launch state.\n\nComplexity — depends on which sort path is taken (see Sizing)\n\nΘ(n) on the exact-size fast path: radix_sortperm! is an LSD radix over monotone unsigned keys — one fused histogram scan plus at most sizeof(K) stable counting iterations (4 for the default UInt32), with trivial digits skipped. The sweep itself is a single Θ(n) scan.\nΘ(n log n) on the partial-buffer path, which falls back to the comparison-based Base.sortperm!.\n\nThis is not a constant-factor distinction: an oversized workspace silently changes the complexity class (measured on PT-E-SIFT-4K, 2026-07-26: 51.5 µs per hypothesis through the partial path, 21.7 through the radix path). resize!(sws, n) resizes every sortable buffer to n for exactly this reason.\n\nSizing\n\nExact-size fast path (length(perm) == n and length(sort_target) == n): sorts via radix_sortperm! when radix buffers of capacity ≥ n are present, else via sortperm! directly.\nPartial-buffer path (length(perm) > n or length(sort_target) > n): sorts views of the first-n prefix through sortperm! (one workspace sized to the largest n, per-call n varying).\n\nHeterogeneous penalties — the parametric sweep\n\nWhen per-point gains g_i = -penalties_i/2 VARY, the optimal support is a top-k set of t_i(μ) = g_i - μ·q_i at the support's own profiled μ_I = ν_k/(2 Q_I) (the generalized sweep lemma), enumerated EXACTLY by the parametric sweep. When all gains are equal (the model-certain score, constant penalties) there are no crossings and the single sort is exact; it is taken untouched, bit-identically.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace, AbstractVector{T}, Int64}} where T","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"sweep!(sws::ScoringWorkspace, sq_distances, n; model_dof, log_two_outlier_halfwidth) -> (S*, k*)\n\nThe sort-and-sweep on a bare ScoringWorkspace, for callers without a StudentTUniformScoring (a per-blob posterior adaptation, a host-side seed loop). The codimension, the scale prior and the label prior are the workspace's, committed at allocation time (default_scoring_workspace(n; dg, scale_prior, label_prior)); the label prior's table is the one for n. The keyword codimension is retired (the workspace's dg is the one its log Γ table was built at). A forwarder: no sweep loop of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep_order-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"sweep_order(sws::ScoringWorkspace) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe order the last sweep! visited the points in: sweep_order(sws)[1:k] is the support of the partition it returned k for (the scoring contract's consensus_order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep_order"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SCORING_TAGS","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"SCORING_TAGS\n\nThe TagRegistry of the scoring families: \"ransac\" (RansacScoring), \"msac\" (MsacScoring) and \"student_t_uniform\" (StudentTUniformScoring), snake_case of the type's name with the family's suffix Scoring stripped. A package that adds families (RANSACScoringDoneRight's MLESAC, MAGSAC++, …) declares its own registry of the same family and suffix, TagRegistry(:scoring, \"Scoring\", …; retired), and its loaders read merge(VgtkCore.SCORING_TAGS, its_tags). VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's scoring_from_toml reads a scorer table through it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SCORING_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Predictive","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"Predictive\n\nType tag for the predictive variant of StudentTUniformScoring: StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive} incorporates the model's estimation uncertainty Σ̃θ (`solvercovariance, of a minimal-sample model or of a refit on its consensus set) into the per-point scores;StudentTUniformScoring{Nothing}` treats the model as exact (Σ_θ = 0). An option, not a default: measured against model-certain scoring it ties or loses on pooled ΔAUC@10° and costs 13–565× per hypothesis (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's docs/src/guide/scoring.md).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Predictive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"StudentTUniformScoring{P, DG, L, O} <: AbstractScoring\n\nThe scale-free marginal score of the Student-t inlier + uniform outlier model (Section 3, Eq. 12 of \"RANSAC Scoring Done Right\") — spec only: the immutable configuration of the score. Its scratch lives on its ScoringWorkspace, the scoring contract's scoring_workspace.\n\nP: Nothing (model-certain) or Predictive (model-uncertain);\nDG::Int: the codimension d_g of the model's constraint;\nL, O: the label prior and the outlier model (type parameters, so the sweep dispatches on them statically).\n\nThe score marginalizes σ² under the scale prior:\n\nS(θ, I) = log Γ(ν/2 + α₀) − (ν/2)·log 2π − (ν/2 + α₀)·log(RSS_I/2 + β₀) − n_o·d_g·log(2h) [+ label prior]\n\nwith ν = d_g·(n_I − m), RSS_I = Σᵢ∈I rᵢ², n_o = N − n_I and h the outlier half-width.\n\nBuild it with\n\nStudentTUniformScoring(problem | model_dof, outlier; scale_prior, label_prior[, codimension])\nStudentTUniformScoring{Predictive}(problem | model_dof, outlier; scale_prior, label_prior[, codimension])\n\nscale_prior (an IGPrior: JeffreysPrior() or IGPrior(α₀, β₀)) and label_prior (FlatLabelPrior, ProfiledLabelPrior, BetaLabelPrior) are REQUIRED: the stack's default is its configuration's, a scorer table read by VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's scoring_from_toml (its defaults in Estimation's configs/default.toml). The model_dof form takes codimension (1 when absent: a scalar constraint).\n\nThe outlier component outlier is a supplied half-width (a number, or FixedHalfwidth: the profile-marginal score) or an outlier model that estimates the support (an AbstractOutlierModel of another package: RANSACScoringDoneRight's extreme-value and generalized Pareto models). Those have no length parameter and take model-certain scalar residuals (d_g = 1), since the scorer receives one distance per point.\n\nAccessors: minimal_sample_size (m), codimension, scale_prior, label_prior, outlier_model, outlier_halfwidth (a supplied width only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.alloc_scoring_workspace-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"alloc_scoring_workspace(problem, scoring::StudentTUniformScoring) -> ScoringWorkspace\nalloc_scoring_workspace(n::Int, dg::Int, scoring::StudentTUniformScoring) -> ScoringWorkspace\n\nThe scratch of the Algorithm 1 sweep for n points (data_size(problem)) of codimension dg (codimension(problem)): the log Γ table at the scorer's scale prior, the label prior's table, the estimated-support buffers when the outlier model estimates the support. The scoring contract's scoring_workspace(scoring, n, dg) is the second form. dg must be the scorer's own codimension, the one its sweep's ν = d_g·(k − m) reads (an ArgumentError otherwise: a scorer built from a bare model DOF has d_g = 1 unless given codimension; one built from the problem has the problem's).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.alloc_scoring_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.label_prior-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"label_prior(scoring::StudentTUniformScoring) -> AbstractLabelPrior\n\nThe scorer's prior of the labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.label_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_halfwidth-Union{Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring{P, DG, L, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedHalfwidth}}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{DG}, Tuple{P}} where {P, DG, L}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"outlier_halfwidth(scoring::StudentTUniformScoring{P, DG, L, FixedHalfwidth}) -> Float64\n\nThe supplied half-width of the uniform outlier support per axis. The sweep charges log(2·outlier_halfwidth) per point and axis. A scorer whose outlier model estimates the support has none: the scoring contract's nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_halfwidth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_model-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"outlier_model(scoring::StudentTUniformScoring) -> AbstractOutlierModel\n\nThe scorer's outlier component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_residuals!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace, AbstractVector{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring, AbstractVector{<:Real}, Int64}} where T","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"score_residuals!(sws, q, scoring, residuals, n; sample_idx = nothing) -> (S, k)\n\nScore from precomputed residuals (model-certain, isotropic noise): squares residuals[1:n] into q, pads q[n+1:end] with typemax, zeros the sws penalties, and sweeps for the optimal partition (overwriting the sws sweep buffers). The sample_idx points get q = 0. Returns (S, k): sweep score and optimal inlier count. The residuals are in the caller's unit, the one scoring's outlier half-width is in.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitCore: StudentTUniformScoring, JeffreysPrior, FlatLabelPrior,\n           alloc_scoring_workspace, score_residuals!\n\njulia> r = [0.010, -0.020, 0.015, 0.005, -0.010, 0.012, -0.008, 0.40, -0.45, 0.30];\n\njulia> scoring = StudentTUniformScoring(2, 0.5; scale_prior = JeffreysPrior(), label_prior = FlatLabelPrior());\n\njulia> sws = alloc_scoring_workspace(length(r), 1, scoring);\n\njulia> S, k = score_residuals!(sws, zeros(length(r)), scoring, r, length(r));\n\njulia> k   # the seven small residuals are the inliers\n7\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_residuals!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"score_sq_residuals!(sws, q, scoring, problem, model, [Σ̃_θ,] [sample_idx,] n) -> (S, k)\n\nThe score of a model from its problem's squared whitened residuals: q[1:n] is filled with them (q[n+1:end] with typemax), the scorer's partition sweep runs over them in the workspace sws, and the result is (S, k), the sweep's score and its optimal inlier count. The generic function of the Student-t + uniform scoring's problem-and-model form; its methods (below) are the plain score, the predictive score of a model with covariance Σ̃_θ, and its minimal-sample form (sample_idx). The residual-buffer form is score_residuals!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace, AbstractVector, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Predictive}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any, AbstractVector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"score_sq_residuals!(sws, q, scoring::StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive}, problem, model, sample_idx, n) -> (S, k)\n\nPredictive variant: the model-uncertain scores qᵢ from the solver covariance solver_covariance(problem, model, sample_idx), then the sort-and-sweep (Algorithm 1, Eq. 20). A DEGENERATE sample — the provider returns nothing — is no hypothesis: (-Inf, 0), with q and sws untouched; it is never scored model-certain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace, AbstractVector{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any, Int64}} where T","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"score_sq_residuals!(sws, q, scoring, problem, model, n) -> (S, k)\n\nScore a model by computing per-point squared whitened residuals and sweeping for the optimal partition (Algorithm 1, Eq. 12). Fills q[1:n] with squared whitened residuals, q[n+1:end] with typemax, and overwrites the sws sweep buffers. Returns (S, k): sweep score and optimal inlier count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace, AbstractVector{T}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Predictive}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any, Any, Union{Nothing, AbstractVector{Int64}}, Int64}} where T","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"score_sq_residuals!(sws, q, scoring::StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive}, problem, model, Σ̃_θ,\n                    sample_idx, n; residuals = nothing) -> (S, k)\n\nPredictive scoring at a GIVEN unit-scale model covariance Σ̃_θ (multiplicative convention, Σ_θ = s²·Σ̃_θ): fills q[i] with the predictive squared residual rᵢᵀ Ṽᵢ⁻¹ rᵢ and the sws penalties with log|Ṽᵢ|, then sort-and-sweeps (Algorithm 1, Eq. 20). The minimal sample's points (sample_idx, or nothing) get zero q and penalty. When residuals is supplied it receives each point's Mahalanobis distance, the norm of its whitened_residual from the same constraint evaluation (≥ 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_sq_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scored_model-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Predictive}, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"scored_model(scoring::StudentTUniformScoring{Predictive}, problem, θ, sample) -> Uncertain | nothing\n\nThe model with the unit-scale covariance of its fit on the points sample (solver_covariance); nothing for a degenerate sample — no hypothesis, skipped like a failed solve, never scored model-certain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scored_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{DG}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoringWorkspace, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring{Nothing, DG}, Vector{T}, Int64}} where {DG, T}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"sweep!(sws::ScoringWorkspace, scoring::StudentTUniformScoring, scores, n) -> (S*, k*)\n\nThe scorer's sort-and-sweep of the per-point scores scores[1:n] on its workspace: the supplied half-width's prefix sweep, the predictive score's (with the workspace's penalties), or the estimated-support sweep of an outlier model that estimates the support. Records the half-width the selected partition was scored with on the workspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sweep!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/student_t_scoring/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_width-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{DG}, Tuple{P}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StudentTUniformScoring{P, DG, L, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedHalfwidth}, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OutlierHalfwidth{Float64}}} where {P, DG, L}","page":"Student-t + Uniform Scoring","text":"with_width(scoring::StudentTUniformScoring, width::OutlierHalfwidth) -> StudentTUniformScoring\n\nThe same scorer at the supplied outlier half-width width (every other field kept); a scorer whose outlier model estimates the support takes none (the contract's ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_width"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"This guide covers installation and the concepts the rest of the documentation assumes.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"Install VisualGeometryToolkitCore from the General registry (Julia 1.13+):\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"VisualGeometryToolkitCore\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#The-Schur-complement-of-the-LM-driver","page":"Getting Started","text":"The stack's Levenberg–Marquardt driver is NLLSsolver. Release 5.3.1 contains our pull requests #61–#65 (allocation-free copies, OptimizeCache, the trial-point reuse, a NaN trial rejected, a non-finite start stopped). One remains open, #60, the Schur-complement linear system for eliminable variables: without it everything is correct, but the relative-pose bundle solves the full system and runs about 2× slower. To use it now, pin our fork once in your environment:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(PackageSpec(url = \"https://github.com/prittjam/NLLSsolver.jl\", rev = \"stack\"))\n\nstack is the latest upstream release plus the unmerged pull requests, rebased onto each new release. Verify with VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nlls_features().schur → true. For a run that feeds a figure, replace rev = \"stack\" by the commit sha so every machine resolves the same tree. When #60 is released, Pkg.free(\"NLLSsolver\") returns the environment to the registry and this note is removed.\n\nFor visualization support, also install GLMakie:\n\nPkg.add(\"GLMakie\")","title":"The Schur complement of the LM driver"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Basic-Usage","page":"Getting Started","text":"using VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\nWith visualization:\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\nusing GLMakie  # Activates the plotting extension","title":"Basic Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Quick-Start","page":"Getting Started","text":"The README's first example, run by the docs build (a doctest):\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> ellipse = Ellipse(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 3.0, 2.0, π/4);\n\njulia> circle = Circle(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 1.5);\n\njulia> line = Line2D(Point2(0.0, 0.0), Point2(1.0, 1.0));\n\njulia> centroid(circle)\n2-element GeometryBasics.Point{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\njulia> signed_distance(line, Point2(0.0, 1.0))   # + on the normal's side\n0.7071067811865475\n\njulia> classify_conic(ellipse)\nEllipseTrait()\n\njulia> VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_boundary(circle)   # public, not exported\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore.HasBoundary()","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Key-Concepts","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Key Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Physical-Units","page":"Getting Started","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore uses FlexUnits.jl for physical quantities; a pixel (px) is a dimensionless count:\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> focal = 35.0mm; pixel_size = 3.45μm;   # lengths\n\njulia> x_coord = 100.0px;                       # pixels (logical)\n\njulia> ustrip(mm, 2.5cm)                        # strip in a named unit\n25.0\n\njulia> ustrip(px, 1inch * 300dpi)               # a length times a density is pixels\n300//1\n\njulia> VisualGeometryToolkitCore.base_quantity(142mm)   # an integer literal is exact; stored as Float64\n0.142 m\n\nValues are stored in base units (metres): strip in a named unit, ustrip(mm, distance) == 50.0. The registry's scales are exact rationals, so 142mm is exactly 71/500 m until a constructor stores its Float64 form (base_quantity). ud\"…\" and uparse parse unit strings, and dimension(q) == dimension(mm) checks a dimension at an API boundary (a pixel is dimensionless, so dimension(px) is also an angle's or a ratio's).","title":"Physical Units"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Coordinate-Conventions","page":"Getting Started","text":"Different vision libraries place the pixel origin differently:\n\nframe first-pixel center\nPixelFrame{:opencv} (0, 0)\nPixelFrame{:colmap} (0.5, 0.5)\nPixelFrame{:julia} (1, 1)\n\nVGC math is COLMAP convention throughout. Origin conversion is a labeled rigid map between pixel frames:\n\nT = EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia})\npt_julia = T(pt_colmap)\n\nSee Coordinate Frames for the frame-tag system this is part of.","title":"Coordinate Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Coordinate-Frames","page":"Getting Started","text":"Maps carry Src/Dst frame tags at the type level; composition and inversion are frame-checked, and unlabeled maps compose with anything (the gradual-typing wildcard). A camera pose and its extrinsics are the same EuclideanMap type under swapped labels — inv(::CameraPose) is a CameraExtrinsics. This system is opt-in and pervades the package; the Coordinate Frames guide is the place to start.","title":"Coordinate Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Module-Structure","page":"Getting Started","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras — camera models, sensors, projection, stereo rigs, the imaging chain, undistortion.\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives — geometric primitives.\nTransforms, frames, epipolar forms, solvers, and robust-estimation primitives are defined in the top-level module.\n\nCommonly-used names are re-exported from the main module:\n\n# These are equivalent:\ncamera = VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera(model, pose)\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, pose)  # Re-exported\n\nFeature detection lives in VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.jl; the estimation engines live in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.jl (see Robust Estimation).","title":"Module Structure"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#The-Attributed{V,A}-Pattern","page":"Getting Started","text":"Values that carry metadata use a single composition type: Attributed{V,A} wraps a value V with attributes A, with property access forwarded transparently. Uncertain{V,M} is the same type with a covariance or precision matrix as the attributes.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> p = Attributed(Point2(320.5, 240.5), 1.35);   # a depth-attributed pixel, as consumed by transfer(rig, p)\n\njulia> p.value                                        # the Point2\n2-element GeometryBasics.Point{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n 320.5\n 240.5\n\njulia> p.attributes                                   # the depth\n1.35\n\njulia> u = Uncertain(Point2(100.0, 200.0), CovMat{2}(σ = 0.5));   # an uncertain point, Σ = σ²·I\n\nSee Design: Attributed{V,A} for the rationale.","title":"The Attributed{V,A} Pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Next-Steps","page":"Getting Started","text":"Design: Attributed{V,A} — the unifying composition pattern\nCoordinate Frames — the frame-tag system\nCameras — camera models and projection\nThe Framed Imaging Chain — frames and stages\nGeometric Primitives — shapes and their transforms\nTransforms — coordinate transformations and warping","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/imaging/#Imaging-Chain-and-Targets-API","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"The framed imaging chain (frame-to-frame stages, ray maps, frame transitions) and calibration targets. See the The Framed Imaging Chain and Ray Maps and Frame Transitions guides; rigs, stereo and captures are on the Rigs, Stereo and Captures API page.","title":"Imaging Chain and Targets API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/imaging/#The-Framed-Imaging-Chain","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"","title":"The Framed Imaging Chain"},{"category":"section","location":"api/imaging/#Targets","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"A calibration target is a MODEL, the analogue of a camera model: AbstractTarget promises points(target) in its own TargetFrame{id}; PointTarget holds explicit (nominal or estimated) points, PlanarTarget a design — its nominal points, or the reference they are resolved from — and a TargetCalibration taking the design to the physical target. A target whose geometry an estimator refines implements the calibratable-target protocol (parameter_vector, with_parameters, minimal_manifold, regularizer, and CoordinateTransformations' transform_deriv_params).\n\nA standard layout is a constructor, an AbstractTargetLayout: Kalibr's AprilGrid is AprilGrid(; tag_rows, tag_cols, tag_size, tag_spacing, apriltag_family, first_id) (lengths, the spacing the gap between two tags), PlanarTarget(grid; calibration, board, canvas, placement) its tag corners in Kalibr's point order and frame, and point_index(grid, tag, corner) the point of a detected corner. Schöps et al.'s tagged star pattern is SchoepsPattern(; squares_x, squares_y, square_length, apriltags, star_segments, apriltag_family), and PlanarTarget(pattern; calibration, board, canvas, placement) its star features in Schöps' feature order (feature id k is point k + 1). The target records its layout, layout(t), so that VisualGeometryToolkitIO writes it as one (its tag in TARGET_LAYOUT_TAGS).","title":"Targets"},{"category":"section","location":"api/imaging/#Target-calibration","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"What a target calibration estimates about the physical target, as a camera calibration estimates intrinsics and a lens: a CoordinateTransformations Transformation of the design, its parameter Jacobian CT's transform_deriv_params and its point Jacobian CT's transform_deriv. The kinds:\n\nkind map parameters Jacobian regularizer free directions\nRigid p ↦ p none 2 × 0 none 0\nSkew (N = 2) p ↦ U p, U unit upper-triangular u12 (U's strictly-upper entries, row by row) 2 × 1 none 1\nAspect (N = 2) p ↦ diag(e^{a}, e^{−a}) p a1 2 × 1 none 1\nScale p ↦ e^{k} p log_scale 2 × 1 none 1\na planar EuclideanMap p ↦ R(ϑ) p + t angle, tx, ty 2 × 3 none 3\nThinPlateSpline p ↦ p + (f_x(p), f_y(p)), no affine term fx1…fxK, fy1…fyK 2 × 2K bending energy, 2K rows 2K − 6\nHeightField p ↦ (p, h(p)), no affine term h1…hK 3 × K bending energy, K rows K − 3\na composition (CT's ∘) the members, the inner first the members', tag-prefixed, the outer member's first CT's chain rule, hcat(p1, m1 * p2) stacked, the outer member's first the members' sum\n\nThe affine print error is its Iwasawa factors. An affine map of the design p ↦ A p + b factors as A = s·R·D·U (the KAN decomposition): a rotation, a scale, a det-1 diagonal (the aspect) and a unit upper-triangular matrix (the skew). The calibration composes them in that canonical order, EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew, and holds only the ones its data observes. With the target's pose estimated jointly, the rotation, the translation and the scale are the pose's — a target and the same target turned, shifted or scaled are the same images — so the print error is Aspect ∘ Skew, two parameters and no gauges; a measured length (a ruler) adds Scale; a known pose (a fixture, motion capture) the rigid member too. The counts agree: an affine map of R^N has N² + N parameters, the pose N(N−1)/2 + N and the scale one, which leaves (N−1) + N(N−1)/2. Aspect, Skew and Scale are N-generic; a planar target takes N = 2.\n\nThe thin-plate kinds are pure bending. An in-plane spline's displacements and a height field's heights have no affine term in their interpolant: the spline through each nodal value vector is Σ wᵢ U(·, pᵢ) alone, its affine coefficients a (tps_coefficients) zero — intrinsic to the spline, whatever the node distribution. The constructor and with_parameters project what they are given onto that subspace, v ↦ v − M a(v) with M = [1 u v] at the nodes, keeping the bending (values whose affine term is within 1e-12 of them are kept bit for bit, so RadiometricCamera's board heights, the bending part of its spline at its chart nodes, are kept unchanged). The condition is defined on the nodes the spline is built on: the radial basis U(r) = r² log r is not scale-invariant, and scaling the nodes moves a constant (a piston) between the affine and the bending parts — build the thin-plate kinds on a dimensionless chart of the board. An affine displacement belongs to the affine factors and a plane of heights to the pose; a spline built from either is the identity. So the canonical print error with a jointly estimated pose is\n\nHeightField(nodes, h) ∘ ThinPlateSpline(nodes, fx, fy) ∘ Aspect(a) ∘ Skew(u)\n\nand no kind has a gauge: every direction a composition's parameters may move in is observable, and the joint pose/calibration Jacobian has full column rank. The stored parameters stay the nodal values (2K, K), with 2K − 6 (K − 3) free directions; a covariance over them has that rank.\n\nA PlanarTarget takes one kind, or kinds in the canonical order HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew, any of them left out, and refuses any other composition with an ArgumentError; it stores a composition right-associated, whatever association ∘ built. A composition's parameters, names, bounds, Jacobian columns and regularizer rows are in CoordinateTransformations' order, the outer member's first. The 0.4 AffineMap kind (tag \"affine\") is retired.\n\nThe thin-plate kinds are splines over K fixed nodes in the target's plane (the numerics' ThinPlateSplineSystem), their parameters the nodal values; a height is tps_evaluate of its spline, bit for bit. Lengths (a rigid member's translation, nodes, displacements, heights) are in the design's form, plain or physical, and move with ustrip/with_unit; Scale, Aspect and Skew are dimensionless. A calibration may be Uncertain(c, CovMat(Σ)), Σ over its parameters, as a pose may be.\n\nA calibration on the board's own chart. \"No affine term\" is a property of the nodes a thin-plate kind is built on (r² log r is not scale-invariant: rescaling the nodes moves a piston into the affine part), so a field computed on a dimensionless chart of the board — RadiometricCamera's page [0, 1]² — stays on that chart, and the target places the chart with a placement, Core's SimilarityMap{3} S₃ of scale side, the identity rotation and the translation (origin, 0) (the design point p = origin + side·c): PlanarTarget(…; calibration, placement) applies its calibration as the composition S₃ ∘ lift ∘ C ∘ S₂⁻¹, a height scaled by side. The calibration is plain chart numbers whatever the points' form; the placement is fixed data, plain numbers in the points' unit (metres for a physical target, rescaled by ustrip/with_unit), and adds no parameters. A page whose chart runs down the design's y is placed at its top-left corner on the reflected chart nodes (u, −v), which keep the bending pure.\n\nnodes = [SVector(n[1], -n[2]) for n in grid_nodes(5; lo = 0.0, hi = 1.0)]   # the page's chart, rows down\nt = PlanarTarget(:page; calibration = HeightField(nodes, heights ./ side),     # heights in chart units\n                 placement = SimilarityMap(side, one(RotMatrix{3, Float64}), SVector(x0, ytop, 0.0)), board, canvas)\n\nPlanarity is in the type. is_planar is true for Rigid, the affine factors and ThinPlateSpline, false once a HeightField is present; points(t) is then SVector{2}s on z = 0 or SVector{3}s, and the result type is inferred. A method that assumes the plane takes t::PlanarTarget{<:PlanarCalibration} and refuses a height field by dispatch.\n\nThe manifold. TargetManifold(t) — minimal_manifold(t) — is linear: the identity basis for the affine factors, the thin-plate kinds' free directions (an orthonormal null-space basis of the affine-coefficient rows, from a pivoted Householder QR: reproducible) for the splines. There is nothing to free or fix: what the data observes is chosen by the composition's members.\n\nt = PlanarTarget(pts; calibration = HeightField(nodes) ∘ ThinPlateSpline(nodes) ∘ Aspect(0.0) ∘ Skew(0.0))\nch = minimal_manifold(t)                                   # (K − 3) + (2K − 6) + 2 directions, all observable\nPlanarTarget(pts; calibration = Scale(0.0) ∘ Aspect(0.0) ∘ Skew(0.0))   # a ruler: the scale is observable","title":"Target calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/imaging/#The-surface-a-ray-meets","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"surface(pt) gives a posed planar target's geometric primitive, in the pose's destination frame (metres when physical), with the DESIGN chart as its chart — an affine calibration (the factors, Aspect ∘ Skew, …) is the primitive's in-plane affine, so a hit's chart coordinates are the nominal ones it shows — bounded by the target's Board (board = … on PlanarTarget, Rect(board) in the design chart) or else by its nominal points: a Quad for a Rigid or affine calibration, a MappedSurface for any other — a HeightField, an in-plane ThinPlateSpline, either after the affine factors — the surface of the composed chart map pose ∘ S₃ ∘ lift ∘ C ∘ S₂⁻¹, built by MappedSurface(pose, calibration, placement, rect) (a device caller builds it from adapted parts). The choice is by type, so a rigid target's ray caster never compiles the root.\n\nOn the device. HeightField, ThinPlateSpline and their ThinPlateSplineSystem take Adapt.adapt (their matrices become device arrays) and convert_eltype (a Float32 form); tps_pure_bending and tps_coefficients give a field's pure-bending values and spline coefficients from nodal values in a kernel (with_parameters on the adapted kind), the same functions the host constructors call. HeightField(nodes, points .=> heights; smoothing) fits a pure-bending field to scattered heights, the plane removed (a one-step algorithm).\n\nNo gauge freedom. The thin-plate kinds' pure bending is the OBLIQUE complement of the pose's directions (values orthogonal to the interpolant's affine rows, not to the plane fields), so no deformation direction is a pose change: the stacked Jacobian of the posed points in the deformation and the pose has full rank (test/mappedsurfacetests.jl pins its condition number and the smallest principal angle between the two spans for each composition). The spans can still come close: with nodes reaching past the detections the nearest bending mode is a nearly constant bowl, almost a piston on the data, about 5° from the pose's span (a variance inflation of about 110× along that mode); a node layout covered by detections and the bending regularizer control it. See The Surface a Ray Meets, where surface is documented with the primitives.","title":"The surface a ray meets"},{"category":"section","location":"api/imaging/#Index","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"Pages = [\"imaging.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedAffine}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"CameraCalibrationMat(m::FramedAffine) -> CameraCalibrationMat\n\nThe intrinsics whose calibration stage is m, K = [A t; 0 0 1] — the inverse of calibration_stage. A K solved in the NormalizedImageFrame comes back in pixels as CameraCalibrationMat(inv(norm) ∘ calibration_stage(DistortedFrame{id}, NormalizedImageFrame{id}, LogicalIntrinsics(K_norm))) (framed affines fuse under ∘, the junction checked). The lens coefficients need no conversion: they live in K⁻¹·pixel, which the normalization leaves where it was. Checked as any K is (positive focals, upper-triangular): a rotated stage is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraCalibrationMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FrameTransition","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FrameTransition{Src <: RetinalFrame, Dst <: RetinalFrame}\n\nThe framed transition map between two retinal frames of the SAME view, {Src → Dst} — a pure radial reparameterization of the viewing sphere, computed once through the sphere as the conjugation\n\nsection(Dst) ∘ lift(Src)\n\nso every pair among the five frames (GnomonicFrame, EquidistantFrame, StereographicFrame, EquisolidFrame, OrthographicFrame) is served by ONE implementation, with no per-pair closed forms to keep consistent. Azimuth is preserved; the radial profile map is profile_Dst(profile_Src⁻¹(r)).\n\nMint with frame_transition; inv is the reverse pair (an honest inverse — on the sphere the frame projections are invertible sections). As a FramedStage it composes junction-checked with the rest of the framed surface, and being isbits it composes into a per-point map that pointwise! runs over a batch.\n\nDomain: the intersection of the two frames' honest domains, enforced — points outside Src's frame radius (equisolid r > 2, orthographic r > 1) and rays outside Dst's domain (gnomonic Z ≤ 0, orthographic Z < 0, the stereographic/equidistant antipode) map to NaN at the edge rather than aliasing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FrameTransition"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedAffine","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedAffine{Src <: Frame{2}, Dst <: Frame{2}, T}\n\nFramed 2D affine stage x ↦ A·x + t — the calibration stage of the imaging chain, {DistortedFrame{id} → ImageFrame{id}} (or {ray frame → ImageFrame{id}} under the no-distortion frame collapse; the identity-K logical case is just A = I, t = 0). Same arithmetic as the AffineMap in the legacy imaging_transform; the frames are the addition. inv swaps the frames around the inverse affine. Mint with calibration_stage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedAffine"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedAffine-Union{Tuple{id}, Tuple{Type{ImageFrame{id}}, Type{NormalizedImageFrame{id}}, Size2}} where id","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedAffine(ImageFrame{id}, NormalizedImageFrame{id}, sz::Size2) -> FramedAffine\nFramedAffine(PixelFrame{O}, NormalizedImageFrame{id}, sz::Size2) -> FramedAffine\n\nThe normalization map of an image of size sz (pixels, plain or px): x ↦ (x − c) / s, c the image centre and s = hypot(width, height) / 2 the half-diagonal — DiagonalNormalization, framed — so the image corners land at unit radius. The storage frame of the benchmark pools, where one noise level and one threshold must mean the same across resolutions; estimation normalizes by HartleyNormalization. The centre is written in the source's convention: (width/2, height/2) for an ImageFrame (always read :colmap), moved by EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{O}) for a PixelFrame{O}, so every convention lands on the same normalized point. inv de-normalizes. The view ids must agree (a MethodError otherwise).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedAffine"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedChain","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedChain{Src <: Frame, Dst <: Frame, T <: Tuple} <: FramedStage{Src, Dst}\nFramedChain(stages::FramedStage...)\n\nComposite of framed stages, applied FIRST-TO-LAST (stages[1] innermost): a FramedChain consumes src_frame(stages[1]) coordinates and produces dst_frame(stages[end]) coordinates, and is itself a FramedStage — so chains compose with stages and with each other under the same checked ∘, flattening (no nested chains from composition).\n\nThe vararg constructor checks EVERY inner junction under the standing wildcard truth table and throws an ArgumentError naming the first mismatched junction; ∘ between stages performs the same check as a MethodError (the house composition idiom). The callable unrolls at compile time. inv inverts and reverses the stages (frames swap) — and is therefore a MethodError for chains containing a ray projection, whose fiber collapse has no inverse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedChain"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedDistort","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedDistort{Src <: Frame{2}, Dst <: Frame{2}, L <: AbstractLensModel}\n\nFramed forward-distortion stage {ray frame → DistortedFrame} wrapping a lens model. The math dispatches on the Src frame KIND:\n\nSrc <: GnomonicFrame: the wrapped lens callable itself — the lens contract already IS a map on the gnomonic frame.\nSrc <: EquidistantFrame: the model's radial map in θ (KannalaBrandt's polynomial; COLMAP's thin-prism fisheye's additive terms evaluated on equidistant-frame coordinates, COLMAP's convention).\n\nMint with distort_stage (which tags the honest frame via distortion_frame); inv yields the matching FramedUndistort with the frames swapped. Direct type-parameter construction is an unchecked expert surface, like relabel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedDistort"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedEquidistantProjection","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"The framed EQUIDISTANT-AZIMUTHAL ray-frame projection θ·(cos ψ, sin ψ) onto EquidistantFrame, defined past 90° too: an alias of FramedProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedEquidistantProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedEquisolidProjection","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"The framed EQUISOLID (equal-area) ray-frame projection 2·sin(θ/2)·(cos ψ, sin ψ) onto EquisolidFrame, the whole sphere: an alias of FramedProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedEquisolidProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedLensProjection","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedLensProjection{Src <: Frame{3}, Dst <: Frame{2}, L <: AbstractLensModel}\nFramedLensProjection(lens; id::Symbol = :_)\n\nThe fused MODEL-CARRYING ray projection of a lens whose projection has no fixed frame (a DoubleSphere, Unified or ExtendedUnified projection): {ViewFrame{id} → DistortedFrame{id}} in ONE stage, project(lens, X) on ray representatives — the lens's whole valid field of view, Z ≤ 0 rays included, NaN outside it. imaging_chain composes it with the framed K-affine:\n\nViewFrame{id} ─ FramedLensProjection(lens) → DistortedFrame{id}\n              ─ calibration_stage(K)       → ImageFrame{id}\n\nLike the other ray projections it is a fiber collapse — no inv (a MethodError by design); the closed-form inverse is backproject.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedLensProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedOrthographicProjection","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"The framed ORTHOGRAPHIC (sine-law) ray-frame projection sin θ·(cos ψ, sin ψ) onto OrthographicFrame, NaN past the fold at 90°: an alias of FramedProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedOrthographicProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedPerspective","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"The framed GNOMONIC ray-frame projection (X, Y, Z) ↦ (X/Z, Y/Z) onto GnomonicFrame: an alias of FramedProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedPerspective"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedProjection","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedProjection{Src <: Frame{3}, Dst <: Frame{2}} <: FramedStage{Src, Dst}\n\nThe framed ray-frame projection (3D → 2D): the view's unit rays onto the retinal frame Dst, the section of the ray sphere that Dst's family defines (_sphere_section, one definition per family, shared with the lens contract in lens_rays.jl). The stage wears the frames; the math is the lens contract's. One type serves the five geometry-defined families, and the five names below are aliases with the Dst family fixed:\n\nalias Dst family image of a ray at polar angle θ, azimuth ψ\nFramedPerspective GnomonicFrame tan θ·(cos ψ, sin ψ) — (X/Z, Y/Z); NaN for Z ≤ 0\nFramedEquidistantProjection EquidistantFrame θ·(cos ψ, sin ψ), θ ∈ [0, π)\nFramedStereographicProjection StereographicFrame 2·tan(θ/2)·(cos ψ, sin ψ); NaN at the antipode\nFramedEquisolidProjection EquisolidFrame 2·sin(θ/2)·(cos ψ, sin ψ), the whole sphere\nFramedOrthographicProjection OrthographicFrame sin θ·(cos ψ, sin ψ); NaN for Z < 0 (the frame folds)\n\nEach alias has the keyword constructor Alias(; id::Symbol = :_), which mints FramedProjection{ViewFrame{id}, Family{id}}() (anonymous view :_ by default — see DistortedFrame); Alias{Src, Dst}() and x isa Alias work as they would on a type of its own. The azimuth is ψ = 0 at the on-axis poles (X = Y = 0). A ray outside a frame's honest domain is answered NaN, never aliased onto an in-frame point.\n\nNo inv — deliberate\n\nA projection collapses each ray (a 1-parameter fiber) to a point: a 3D → 2D fiber collapse has no inverse, so inv is absent by design (a MethodError, pinned by test; the explicit throw is needed because CoordinateTransformations defines a generic inv(::Transformation) fallback, an ErrorException, which would misreport the ABSENCE as a missing definition). The right-inverses — the SECTIONS of the projection, each selecting one point per fiber via a depth — are unproject (pixel + depth → 3D point) and backproject (pixel → unit ray, the norm-one section); use those to go back.\n\nServes the lenses by distortion_frame(lens): the perspective-plane lenses use the gnomonic alias, the omnidirectional ones the others.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedStage","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedStage{Src <: Frame, Dst <: Frame} <: Transformation\n\nAbstract supertype of the framed imaging-chain stages: a map consuming Src-frame coordinates and producing Dst-frame coordinates (same tag semantics as EuclideanMap/HomographyMap). Concrete stages: FramedPerspective, FramedEquidistantProjection, FramedStereographicProjection, FramedEquisolidProjection, FramedOrthographicProjection (3D → 2D ray projections), FramedLensProjection (the fused model-carrying 3D → 2D projection of a lens with a parametric projection), FramedDistort, FramedUndistort, FramedAffine, FrameTransition (2D → 2D), the sphere self-maps RayMap/InverseRayMap (3D → 3D), and the composite FramedChain.\n\n∘ between framed stages is junction-checked under the standing wildcard truth table (see Base.:∘ at EuclideanMap) and produces a flat FramedChain; a labeled junction mismatch is a MethodError. Composing with a foreign (unframed) Transformation falls through to CoordinateTransformations' lazy composition — gradual, as always.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedStage"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedStereographicProjection","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"The framed STEREOGRAPHIC ray-frame projection 2·tan(θ/2)·(cos ψ, sin ψ) onto StereographicFrame, the conformal fisheye: an alias of FramedProjection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedStereographicProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedUndistort","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"FramedUndistort{Src <: Frame{2}, Dst <: Frame{2}, L <: AbstractLensModel}\n\nFramed inverse-distortion stage {DistortedFrame → ray frame} — the frames-swapped inverse of FramedDistort, minted by undistort_stage or inv. Math dispatches on the Dst frame kind: undistort_normalized(lens, ·) toward a GnomonicFrame; the closed-form/Newton θ-inverse toward an EquidistantFrame.\n\nFeeding stages the wrong way around — e.g. composing an undistort stage directly after another undistort stage, or after the ray projection (which lands on the ray frame, not the distorted frame) — is a junction mismatch and throws a MethodError: distort-vs-undistort DIRECTION is what the frame tags check, view identity or not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedUndistort"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.InverseRayMap","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"InverseRayMap{F <: Frame{3}, C <: RetinalFrame, L <: AbstractLensModel}\n\nThe inverse sphere self-map D⁻¹: S² → S² — the undistort conjugation lift ∘ undistort ∘ section, minted by inv of a RayMap (and inverting back). Same frame typing, ray semantics, unit-norm output, and frame-domain behavior as RayMap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.InverseRayMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RayMap","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"RayMap{F <: Frame{3}, C <: RetinalFrame, L <: AbstractLensModel}\n\nThe lens's SPHERE SELF-MAP D: S² → S² as a framed stage {ViewFrame{id} → ViewFrame{id}}: the lens's forward distortion free of any image frame, computed as the conjugation\n\nD = lift ∘ distort ∘ section\n\nwhere section is the projection onto the lens's defining frame (distortion_frame) restricted to the unit sphere and lift is its inverse. C records the defining frame the conjugation runs through; D itself needs no 2D frame tag — its input and output are rays of the view's 3D frame.\n\nActs on RAYS: any 3D representative may be supplied (the input norm is irrelevant — both frame projections are scale-invariant on ray representatives), and the output is always the UNIT bearing vector of the re-oriented ray.\n\nRestricted to the sphere the frame projections are sections — invertible, unlike the fiber-collapsing full projections FramedPerspective / FramedEquidistantProjection — which is why RayMap has an honest inv (the InverseRayMap undistort conjugation) while the projection stages deliberately have none.\n\nDomain: the conjugation is defined exactly where the defining frame is — the forward hemisphere Z > 0 for gnomonic-frame lenses, θ ∈ [0, π) (all but the exact backward ray) for equidistant-frame lenses. Rays outside the frame's domain map to NaN.\n\nMint with ray_map; direct type-parameter construction is an unchecked expert surface, like relabel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RayMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_stage-Union{Tuple{Dst}, Tuple{Src}, Tuple{Type{Src}, Type{Dst}, AbstractIntrinsics}} where {Src<:Frame{2}, Dst<:Frame{2}}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"calibration_stage(intrinsics::AbstractIntrinsics; id::Symbol = :_) -> FramedAffine\ncalibration_stage(Src::Type{<:Frame{2}}, Dst::Type{<:Frame{2}}, intrinsics)\n\nMint the framed calibration stage of intrinsics: the affine action of K (A = K[1:2, 1:2], t = K[1:2, 3] — bit-identical to the legacy imaging_transform's AffineMap), labeled {DistortedFrame{id} → ImageFrame{id}}. The 3-argument form states the frames explicitly — used by imaging_chain for the no-distortion collapse, where K consumes the ray frame directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_stage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distort_stage-Tuple{AbstractLensModel}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"distort_stage(lens::AbstractLensModel; id::Symbol = :_) -> FramedDistort\n\nMint the framed forward-distortion stage of lens, {distortion_frame(lens){id} → DistortedFrame{id}}, with the honest frame tag read from distortion_frame. For COLMAP's thin-prism fisheye the stage's math is its additive bundle, read in the equidistant frame (the equidistant half is the stage-1 ray projection, not distortion). Errors for lenses with no distortion stage: identity-in-frame models (Pinhole, a pure Lens(Equidistant()) — the chain collapses instead, see DistortedFrame) and scoped-out models (a parametric projection, which has no frame split).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distort_stage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distortion_frame-Tuple{Lens}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"distortion_frame(lens::AbstractLensModel) -> Type{<:RetinalFrame} | nothing\n\nThe ray frame a lens model's distortion is DEFINED in — the Src frame kind of its framed distortion stage, and the frame the stage-1 ray projection of imaging_chain must land on. For a Lens it is its projection's frame (projection_frame): GnomonicFrame for the perspective projection, EquidistantFrame for the equidistant one (Kannala–Brandt's θ_d = θ·(1 + k₁θ² + …) is a radial factor there), and so on. A LiftLens is read on the gnomonic frame. nothing for a parametric projection, whose frame its coefficients define — ModelDefined in the FrameDependence vocabulary, which no Symbol tag can carry — so there is no frame + distortion SPLIT; imaging_chain serves such a lens through the fused model-carrying FramedLensProjection onto DistortedFrame{id}.\n\nRelation to the Bool capability traits: has_distortion(lens) == false means the distortion is the identity in this frame, and the chain collapses the ray frame onto DistortedFrame (no distortion stage) — see the collapse rule there. All these frames assume a single center of projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distortion_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.frame_transition-Union{Tuple{C2}, Tuple{C1}, Tuple{id}, Tuple{Type{C1}, Type{C2}}} where {id, C1<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RetinalFrame{id}, C2<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RetinalFrame{id}}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"frame_transition(C1::Type{<:RetinalFrame{id}}, C2::Type{<:RetinalFrame{id}}) -> FrameTransition\n\nMint the framed transition map {C1 → C2} between two retinal frames of view id (the id must MATCH — a transition between frames of different views is a MethodError, the labeled-junction rule):\n\nt = frame_transition(EquidistantFrame{:_}, GnomonicFrame{:_})\nt(Vec2(θ, 0.0))            # == Vec2(tan(θ), 0.0)\ninv(t)                     # the reverse transition {Gnomonic → Equidistant}\n\nThis makes a first-class named object of what production code usually hides in an inline expression: OpenCV's cv::fisheye::undistortPoints ends with the line scale = tan(θ)/θ_d — that line IS frame_transition(EquidistantFrame{id}, GnomonicFrame{id}), the equidistant → gnomonic frame change applied after the polynomial θ-inverse, unnamed and unchecked in the C++. Here the same reparameterization is a framed stage with checked junctions and honest domain edges (see FrameTransition).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.frame_transition"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.imaging_chain-Tuple{AbstractIntrinsics, AbstractLensModel}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"imaging_chain(intrinsics, lens; id::Symbol = :_) -> FramedChain\nimaging_chain(model::CameraModel; id::Symbol = :_)\n\nMint the fully-typed framed imaging composite {ViewFrame{id} → ImageFrame{id}} — the frame-checked counterpart of imaging_transform's frame-free affine_part(K) ∘ lens. Every inner junction is checked at construction:\n\nViewFrame{id} ─ ray-frame projection → distortion_frame(lens){id}\n              ─ distort_stage(lens)  → DistortedFrame{id}\n              ─ calibration_stage(K) → ImageFrame{id}\n\nStage 1 is FramedPerspective for perspective-plane lenses and FramedEquidistantProjection for equidistant-frame (omnidirectional) lenses — see distortion_frame. When the lens is the identity in its defining frame (Pinhole; a pure Lens(Equidistant())), the distortion stage is omitted and K consumes the ray frame directly (frame collapse — see DistortedFrame). A lens with a parametric projection is served by one fused FramedLensProjection onto DistortedFrame{id}.\n\nThe id keyword threads the view identity; the default is the reserved anonymous view :_ (junctions then check by frame KIND — the distort-vs-undistort direction check needs no view identity). Rig-derived chains label from the rig's minted ids:\n\na, b = ids(rig)\nchain₁ = imaging_chain(model(source(rig)); id = a)   # {ViewFrame{a} → ImageFrame{a}}\n\nNumerically the chain matches imaging_transform: bit-identical for perspective-plane lenses (identical operations in identical order); equivalent to floating-point round-off for equidistant-frame lenses, whose framed route works in the model's defining frame instead of hopping through the gnomonic plane (atan/tan cancellation — the legacy callable's route).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.imaging_chain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ray_map-Tuple{AbstractLensModel}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"ray_map(lens::AbstractLensModel; id::Symbol = :_) -> RayMap\n\nMint the lens's sphere self-map D: S² → S² as the framed stage {ViewFrame{id} → ViewFrame{id}} — what a central lens actually does, stated with no image frame: it RE-ORIENTS rays. The familiar 2D distortion polynomial is the conjugation section ∘ D ∘ section⁻¹ in the lens's defining frame (distortion_frame); ray_map returns D itself.\n\nInput is any 3D ray representative (norm irrelevant); output is the unit bearing vector of the re-oriented ray. inv yields the undistort conjugation (InverseRayMap) — honest, because on the sphere the frame projections are invertible sections. Rays outside the defining frame's domain (gnomonic: Z ≤ 0; equidistant: the exact backward ray) map to NaN — see RayMap.\n\nFor lenses whose distortion is the identity in their defining frame (Pinhole, a pure Lens(Equidistant())), D is the identity map — still framed, still returning the unit representative. For COLMAP's thin-prism fisheye the conjugation runs through its equidistant frame, where its distortion is the additive bundle. A lens with a parametric projection has no frame split and is scoped out (ArgumentError), exactly like distort_stage.\n\nD exists only for CENTRAL lenses (single center of projection, which every VisualGeometryToolkitCore lens model satisfies): a finite or thick pupil has no sphere self-map, because its rays do not share a center — that regime is ray tracing, downstream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ray_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.similarity_scale-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FramedAffine}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"similarity_scale(m::FramedAffine) -> Real\nsimilarity_scale(a::AffineMap) -> Real\n\nThe isotropic scale σ of a (framed) similarity x ↦ σ R x + t — a Hartley normalization's side included: lengths in Src are σ times as long in Dst, so a pixel threshold τ is σ τ after the map (and τ / σ back through inv(m)). For the NormalizedImageFrame map σ = 1/s, the inverse half-diagonal. Throws an ArgumentError unless m is a similarity (AᵀA = σ² I to rounding): a sheared or anisotropic map has no single scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.similarity_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort_stage-Tuple{AbstractLensModel}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"undistort_stage(lens::AbstractLensModel; id::Symbol = :_) -> FramedUndistort\n\nMint the framed inverse-distortion stage {DistortedFrame{id} → distortion_frame(lens){id}} — inv of distort_stage. The bare undistort_normalized surface is unchanged; this is its framed, direction-checked wrapper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort_stage"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PlanarCalibration","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"PlanarCalibration\n\nThe planar calibration kinds — Rigid, ThinPlateSpline, the affine factors and their compositions — as one type, for a method that assumes the design stays on z = 0: f(t::PlanarTarget{<:PlanarCalibration}) refuses a target with a HeightField by dispatch (a MethodError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PlanarCalibration"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TARGET_CALIBRATION_ORDER","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"TARGET_CALIBRATION_ORDER\n\nThe canonical order of a target calibration's composition, the outer map first, as the kinds of TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS:\n\n(HeightField, ThinPlateSpline, EuclideanMap, Scale, Aspect, Skew)\n\nA PlanarTarget holds one kind, or two to six of them composed in this order, any left out, each once (Rigid composes with nothing). calibration_tag.(TARGET_CALIBRATION_ORDER) are the tags a composition's record lists its members by, in that order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TARGET_CALIBRATION_ORDER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS\n\nThe TagRegistry of the target calibration kinds — the one map from a kind's tag to its type, read by a composition's parameter names (:aspect_a1) and by VisualGeometryToolkitIO's target record (\"type\": \"aspect\"): \"rigid\", \"euclidean\" (Core's EuclideanMap, the family's suffix Map stripped), \"scale\", \"aspect\", \"skew\", \"thin_plate_spline\", \"height_field\", snake_case of the type's name. A composition has no tag: its record is written by its structure.\n\nRETIRED: \"affine\", CoordinateTransformations' AffineMap as one kind with six parameters (A row by row, then b) and four gauges. Its print error is now the composition of the gauge-free factors, Aspect ∘ Skew (Scale ∘ with a measured length, EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ with a known pose); the registry names its outer member, \"aspect\", as its successor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetCalibration","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"TargetCalibration\n\nWhat a target calibration estimates about a planar target: a CoordinateTransformations Transformation c(p) of its design points to the physical target — one of Rigid, the affine factors (a planar EuclideanMap, Scale, Aspect, Skew), ThinPlateSpline, HeightField, or a composition of them with CT's ∘. The union of those, the one type every calibration-typed method takes. The rigid member's parameters are (:angle, :tx, :ty), and it holds its rotation as a Rotations.jl Angle2d, the angle itself (a member built from another Rotation{2} is converted once, where a target stores it), so its parameters round-trip bit for bit.\n\nA PlanarTarget holds one kind, or kinds composed in the CANONICAL ORDER, any of them left out:\n\nHeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew\n\nThe print error with a jointly estimated pose is HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew — the affine part as the gauge-free factors (see Aspect), the spline and the height field pure bending (no affine term in the interpolant, see ThinPlateSpline); a measured length adds Scale, a known pose the rigid member. No kind has a gauge, so every parameter of HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew is observable with the pose estimated jointly; a rigid member and a Scale duplicate that pose's rotation, translation and scale, so the joint-pose manifold (TargetManifold, minimal_manifold(target; known)) refuses them unless known states the length (:length, Scale) or the pose (:pose, both) — drop them, or absorb them into the pose, for a joint refinement. (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's migration of a retired \"affine\" record writes all four factors EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew: the same map, held exactly.) Any other composition — a Rigid member, members out of order or repeated — is refused with an ArgumentError. A target stores a composition right-associated (a ∘ (b ∘ c)) whatever association ∘ built it in: the members, their order and so the parameters are the same.\n\nThe protocol: parameter_vector, parameter_names, parameter_bounds, with_parameters(c, θ), CoordinateTransformations' transform_deriv_params(c, x) (∂c(x)/∂θ) and transform_deriv(c, x) (∂c(x)/∂x), regularizer(c), length_mask. A composition's are stacked in CT's order, the outer member's first; its names carry each member's tag, :height_field_h1, …, :aspect_a1, :skew_u12. Jacobians are plain numbers; for a physical calibration the point and the length parameters are in metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetCalibration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Aspect","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"Aspect(a::StaticVector{M}) <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation      N = M + 1\nAspect(a::Real)                                                             N = 2\nAspect{N}()                                                                 the identity\n\nThe aspect of an affine print error, x ↦ D·x with D = diag(e^{d₁}, …, e^{d_N}) and Σ dᵢ = 0: a det-1 diagonal, which stretches one axis against the others and changes no area. Its parameters are the N − 1 free log-stretches aᵢ = dᵢ (i < N), named (:a1, …, :a(N-1)), and d_N = −Σ aᵢ: for N = 2, D = diag(e^{a}, e^{−a}) — a = 0.001 stretches x by 0.1 % and shrinks y by as much. det D = 1 exactly in exact arithmetic (Σ dᵢ = 0 holds exactly in floating point too; the product of the e^{dᵢ} is 1 to rounding).\n\nDimensionless (it carries lengths through, so it sits with plain and physical designs alike): no length parameters, no gauges, no regularizer, unbounded. transform_deriv(c, x) = D; transform_deriv_params(c, x) is N × (N − 1), column j is e_j D_jj x_j − e_N D_NN x_N. inv(c) negates a. A kind of the Iwasawa factorization of an affine print error (see Skew, the factor it follows in Aspect ∘ Skew).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Aspect"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.HeightField","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"HeightField(nodes, heights; project = true) <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\nHeightField(nodes)                           flat\n\nAn out-of-plane deformation, p ↦ (p, h(p)): one thin-plate spline (numerics/thinplatespline.jl) over K fixed nodes in the target's plane through the nodal heights, both in the design's length form (plain, or lengths). The height at p is tps_evaluate of the spline, nothing else. Parameters (:h1, …, :hK), unbounded; transform_deriv_params is 3 × K (zero in the plane, the spline's nodal_weights through the pure-bending projection in z: the derivative of with_parameters); regularizer the bending-energy rows (K rows).\n\nA target with a height field is not planar (is_planar): its points are 3-vectors. It composes after an in-plane kind or chain, HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew (the canonical print error), HeightField ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew, …, its nodes then in that chain's output (the calibrated plane, not the design).\n\nIts heights are pure bending: no affine term in the interpolant, a = 0 (the field's a, of tps_coefficients), and the constructor and with_parameters PROJECT the heights they are given onto that subspace, h ↦ h − M a(h) with M = [1 u v] at the nodes — the bending kept, the plane dropped (heights whose affine term is below 1e-12 of them are kept bit for bit, as RadiometricCamera's board heights, the bending part of its spline, are). A plane of heights — a piston and two tilts — is the target pose's, so a height field built from one is flat.\n\n\"No affine term\" is defined ON THE NODES THE SPLINE IS BUILT ON: the radial basis U(r) = r² log r is not scale-invariant (U(s r) = s² U(r) + s² log(s) r², and Σ wᵢ |p − pᵢ|² is a constant under the spline's side conditions), so scaling the nodes moves a constant — a piston — between the affine and the bending parts (translating, rotating or reflecting them does not). Build a height field on a dimensionless chart of the board, not on nodes in physical units, to keep its heights where they were computed: the board in units of ONE length, its width ([0, 1] × [0, H/W], RadiometricCamera's page), a uniform scaling. Never scale x and y apart (a non-square board onto [0, 1]²): a thin-plate spline is the same surface only under a similarity of its domain, so per-axis scaling makes its bending depend on the board's aspect. Place that chart on the target with a placement, a SimilarityMap{3} (PlanarTarget(…; calibration, placement)): the field stays plain, its heights in chart units, and the placement scales them to the target's. A physical field's condition holds in metres, its stored form; a unit move (ustrip(u, c), with_unit) converts the values and does not re-project them (the geometry stays where it is), so in another unit the interpolant may carry the piston that unit's scale adds, and a with_parameters there projects in that unit's numbers. project = false keeps the heights as given, the form a reader restores a field in after checking it was pure bending in the unit it was written in (VisualGeometryToolkitIO). The K stored heights have K − 3 free directions, the TargetManifold's tangent, and no gauges; a covariance over them has rank K − 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.HeightField"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.HeightField-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector{<:Pair}}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"HeightField(nodes, points .=> heights; smoothing = 0) -> HeightField\n\nThe HeightField over nodes that fits the scattered heights at the plane points (point => height pairs, Core's correspondence form) in the least-squares sense, the PLANE REMOVED: the heights are modelled as the pure-bending field plus a plane, zₖ ≈ h(pₖ) + a₁ + a_u uₖ + a_v vₖ, and the plane (the pose's piston and two tilts) is fitted and dropped, so the result is pure bending (is_pure_bending) and a planar input gives a flat field. A one-step algorithm (one pivoted-QR solve, `), the scattered-point counterpart ofHeightField(nodes, heights), which interpolates nodal values. The unknowns are the field'sK − 3free directions (its target manifold's null-space basis of the interpolant's affine rows) and the three plane coefficients; the rows are the spline's [nodalweights](@ref) at each point.smoothing = λ ≥ 0adds the bending energyλ hᵀQh([bendingenergy](@ref)) asKresidual rows (a smoothing thin-plate spline); withλ = 0the fit needs at leastK` points in general position.\n\nnodes, points and heights share one form: plain numbers, or lengths (the field is then physical, as HeightField(nodes, heights) is).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.HeightField"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.HeightField-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem, AbstractVector}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"HeightField(system::ThinPlateSplineSystem, heights; project = true)\n\nThe same field on a solver already built on its nodes — plain numbers, the nodes in metres for heights that are lengths (their form decides the field's): what a caller that has checked is_pure_bending on that system builds, without forming it again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.HeightField"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Rigid","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"Rigid() <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\n\nThe design, exactly: the identity, no parameters, no regularizer, no lengths (so it sits with plain and physical designs alike).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Rigid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Scale","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"Scale(log_scale::Real = 0.0) <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\n\nA uniform scale, x ↦ e^{k}·x with k the log-scale, any dimension: one parameter, named (:log_scale,); transform_deriv(c, x) = e^{k} I, transform_deriv_params(c, x) = e^{k} x (one column). Dimensionless, no gauges, no regularizer, unbounded.\n\nThe scale of an affine print error is the target's pose's when the pose is estimated jointly (the pose's translation scales with it), so it belongs in a calibration only when a LENGTH is measured — a ruler, a known pose: Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew, or EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Scale"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Skew","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"Skew(u::StaticVector{L}) <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation      L = N(N − 1)/2\nSkew(u::Real)                                                             N = 2\nSkew{N}()                                                                 the identity\n\nThe skew of an affine print error, x ↦ U·x with U unit upper-triangular: ones on the diagonal, zeros below it, and its L = N(N − 1)/2 strictly-upper entries the parameters, ROW BY ROW — (U₁₂, U₁₃, …, U₁N, U₂₃, …, U₂N, …), named (:u12, :u13, …, :u23, …); a record persists them in that order. For N = 2, x ↦ (x₁ + u·x₂, x₂): a shear of x along y. det U = 1 exactly.\n\nDimensionless: no length parameters, no gauges, no regularizer, unbounded. transform_deriv(c, x) = U; transform_deriv_params(c, x) is N × L, the column of U_ij being x_j e_i. inv(c) is the skew of U⁻¹ (−u for N = 2). The inner factor of the canonical print error Aspect ∘ Skew.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Skew"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ThinPlateSpline","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"ThinPlateSpline(nodes, fx, fy; project = true) <: CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\nThinPlateSpline(nodes)                       zero displacement\n\nAn in-plane residual, p ↦ p + (f_x(p), f_y(p)): two thin-plate splines (numerics/thinplatespline.jl) over the same K fixed nodes in the target's plane, through the nodal displacements fx and fy. Nodes and displacements are in the design's length form (plain, or lengths). Parameters (:fx1, …, :fxK, :fy1, …, :fyK), unbounded; transform_deriv_params is 2 × 2K (the splines' nodal_weights through the pure-bending projection: the derivative of with_parameters); regularizer the two splines' bending-energy rows (2K rows; a quadratic block, see its docstring).\n\nIt is a pure BENDING residual: no affine term in the interpolant. Each of the two splines, through fx and through fy, has zero affine coefficients (a₁ = a_u = a_v = 0 of tps_coefficients: six conditions), and the constructor and with_parameters PROJECT the displacements they are given onto that subspace, v ↦ v − M a(v) with M = [1 u v] at the nodes — which keeps the bending and drops the affine term (displacements whose affine term is below 1e-12 of them are kept bit for bit). A displacement that is an affine map of the design is not the spline's to carry: four of its six directions are the target pose's, and the other two (and the rest, with a measured length or a known pose) belong to the affine factors composed after it — the canonical print error is HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew. A spline built from an affine displacement is the identity. As for a HeightField, \"no affine term\" is defined on the nodes the spline is built on (scaling them moves a constant between the affine and bending parts): build it on a dimensionless chart. project = false keeps the displacements as given — for a reader restoring values that were pure bending in the unit they were written in (a unit move does not re-project, see HeightField); the caller has checked that condition. The 2K stored displacements have 2K − 6 free directions, the TargetManifold's tangent, and no gauges; a covariance over them has rank 2K − 6. transform_deriv_params is the derivative along that subspace (the nodal weights; the projection is the identity there).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ThinPlateSpline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ThinPlateSpline-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"ThinPlateSpline(system::ThinPlateSplineSystem, fx, fy; project = true)\n\nThe same spline on a solver already built on its nodes — plain numbers, the nodes in metres for displacements that are lengths (their form decides the spline's): what a caller that has checked is_pure_bending on that system builds, without forming it again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ThinPlateSpline"},{"category":"function","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_tag","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"calibration_tag(::Type{C}) -> String\n\nThe tag of the calibration kind C: the prefix of its parameter names in a composition (:aspect_a1) and the type of its record — its TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS tag. A composition has none (an ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_tag"},{"category":"function","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_affine","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"is_affine(::Type{C}) -> Bool\nis_affine(c::TargetCalibration) -> Bool\n\nWhether a calibration's IN-PLANE part — where it takes a design point in the target's plane, a height field's lift aside — is an affine map of the design, p ↦ A p + b: a function of the TYPE, true for Rigid, the affine factors and their compositions, HeightField (it leaves the plane point where it is) and a height field after affine factors; false for a ThinPlateSpline and HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline. A planar target's surface reads its chart's affine off such a calibration (its image of the design origin and its point Jacobian there) and refuses any other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_affine"},{"category":"function","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_planar","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"is_planar(::Type{C}) -> Bool\nis_planar(c::TargetCalibration) / is_planar(t::PlanarTarget) -> Bool\n\nWhether a calibration keeps the design in its plane — a function of the TYPE, so it folds at compile time: true for Rigid, the affine factors and their compositions, and ThinPlateSpline; false for a HeightField and a composition with one. A planar target's points are SVector{2}s (on z = 0 of its frame), a non-planar one's SVector{3}s. An Uncertain calibration is planar when its kind is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_planar"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_pure_bending-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem{K, T} where T<:AbstractFloat, AbstractVector}} where K","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"is_pure_bending(system::ThinPlateSplineSystem{K}, v::AbstractVector) -> Bool\n\nWhether the nodal values v (length K) of a thin-plate spline on system are PURE BENDING, the rule of ThinPlateSpline and HeightField: the affine term of their interpolant, M a(v) with M = [1 u v] at the nodes, is within 1e-12 of them (‖M a(v)‖ ≤ 1e-12 ‖v‖). Exactly the values a thin-plate kind's constructor keeps bit for bit; it projects any other. Defined on the nodes the system is built on (scaling them moves a piston between the affine and the bending parts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_pure_bending"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.iwasawa_parameters-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2, 2}, T, 2} where T}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"iwasawa_parameters(A::StaticMatrix{2, 2}) -> SVector{4}\niwasawa_parameters_with_jacobian(A::StaticMatrix{2, 2}) -> (θ, J)\n\nThe parameters of the Iwasawa factors of a planar linear map A with det A > 0, A = s·R·D·U: θ = (ϑ, log s, a₁, u₁₂) — the angle of R(ϑ) and the parameter vectors of Scale, Aspect and Skew — so that EuclideanMap(Angle2d(ϑ), b) ∘ Scale(θ[2]) ∘ Aspect(θ[3]) ∘ Skew(θ[4]) is the affine map p ↦ A·p + b, held exactly.\n\nIt is the QR factorization A = Q·T (T upper-triangular with a positive diagonal, so Q is a rotation when det A > 0), in closed form — Gram–Schmidt on A's columns: ϑ = atan(a₂₁, a₁₁), s = √(t₁₁ t₂₂) = √det A, a₁ = ½ log(t₁₁/t₂₂), u₁₂ = t₁₂/t₁₁. J is the 4 × 4 Jacobian of θ in A's entries ROW BY ROW, (a₁₁, a₁₂, a₂₁, a₂₂) (ForwardDiff's, in one evaluation). An ArgumentError for det A ≤ 0 (a reflection or a degenerate map, which has no such factorization).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.iwasawa_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.iwasawa_parameters_with_jacobian-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2, 2}, T, 2} where T}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"iwasawa_parameters_with_jacobian(A::StaticMatrix{2, 2}) -> (θ, J)\n\niwasawa_parameters(A) and its 4 × 4 Jacobian J in A's entries row by row, (a₁₁, a₁₂, a₂₁, a₂₂); the same ArgumentError for det A ≤ 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.iwasawa_parameters_with_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.length_mask","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"length_mask(::Type{C}) -> SVector{n, Bool}\nlength_mask(c::TargetCalibration) -> SVector{n, Bool}\n\nWhich entries of parameter_vector(c) are lengths: true for an entry that scales with a unit change (stored in metres for a physical calibration), false for a dimensionless one — a function of the TYPE. Rigid: none; Scale, Aspect, Skew: none (every entry false); a planar EuclideanMap: (false, true, true) (the angle, then the translation); ThinPlateSpline and HeightField: every entry; a composition: its members', the outer member's first. A covariance over θ moves with a unit change by this mask; a record of the calibration states its lengths by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.length_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.members-Tuple{CoordinateTransformations.ComposedTransformation}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"members(c) -> Tuple\n\nThe members of a target calibration's composition, its leaves in CoordinateTransformations' order, the outer map first, whatever association ∘ built it in: members(H ∘ (A ∘ U)) == members((H ∘ A) ∘ U) == (H, A, U). A kind that is not a composition is its own one member, (c,).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.members"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.planar_kind-Union{Tuple{Type{K}}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{Type{K}, Integer}} where K<:Union{Rigid, Scale}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"planar_kind(K::Type, n_nodes::Integer = 0) -> Type\n\nThe type a planar target holds for the calibration kind K (a TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS value): EuclideanMap{2}, Aspect{2} and the planar Skew (N = 2), ThinPlateSpline{n_nodes} and HeightField{n_nodes} over their nodes, Rigid and Scale as they are. Its length_mask is the kind's parameter layout, so length(length_mask(planar_kind(K, n))) is the number of parameters a record of the kind holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.planar_kind"},{"category":"function","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.regularizer","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"regularizer(c | target) -> SVector\n\nThe prior's residual rows r(θ) at the current parameters: ‖r‖² is the penalty a calibrating estimator adds (a thin-plate spline's bending energy, a shrinkage toward the design). Empty for Rigid and the affine factors (Aspect, Skew, Scale, an EuclideanMap).\n\nThe rows are a QUADRATIC BLOCK: only ‖r‖² (equivalently LᵀL) is defined. The thin-plate kinds' rows are L v with L an eigen-factor of the bending form, and an eigenbasis is not unique (a symmetric node grid has repeated eigenvalues; the basis and the row signs follow the LAPACK build). So never compare rows one by one (across machines or against stored values), and never weight them one by one (a robust per-row weight would depend on that basis): weight the block as a whole.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.regularizer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_parameters-Tuple{Uncertain{<:Union{TargetCalibration, TargetCalibration}}, AbstractVector}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"with_parameters(u::Uncertain{<:TargetCalibration}, θ) -> TargetCalibration\n\nThe BARE kind rebuilt from θ: an estimate's covariance describes the parameters it was estimated at and does not survive a change of them, so the result is not Uncertain. (A PlanarTarget's with_parameters does the same to its calibration.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_parameters"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TARGET_LAYOUT_TAGS","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"TARGET_LAYOUT_TAGS\n\nThe TagRegistry of the target layouts (AbstractTargetLayout): \"april_grid\" (AprilGrid) and \"schoeps_pattern\" (SchoepsPattern), snake_case of the type's name — the type of VisualGeometryToolkitIO's target layout record. (Kalibr's own YAML spells its target type aprilgrid; that is the foreign file's word, read by its reader, not a tag.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TARGET_LAYOUT_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractTarget","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"AbstractTarget\n\nA calibration target as a model: points(target) returns its points in its own frame TargetFrame{id} — 2-vectors on the plane z = 0 for a planar target, 3-vectors for a solid one or a planar design lifted off its plane by a HeightField — plain or Uncertain. See the header of targets.jl for the calibratable-target protocol.\n\nPlacement is a trait, is_posed, as for cameras: a target type such as PlanarTarget or PointTarget is unposed, is its own model, and refuses pose and extrinsics with an ArgumentError; a PosedTarget is posed. The calibratable-target protocol and TargetManifold are a target MODEL's, as the lens chart is a camera model's: on a PosedTarget they are an ArgumentError naming model(pt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractTarget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractTargetLayout","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"AbstractTargetLayout\n\nA parametric design of a planar target — AprilGrid or SchoepsPattern: the few numbers its nominal points follow from, and whatever else the printed design states (its tag family and ids). PlanarTarget(layout; …) is the target of its points, and it records the layout (layout(t)) so that a writer can store the design by its layout rather than by its points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractTargetLayout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AprilGrid","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"AprilGrid(; tag_rows, tag_cols, tag_size, tag_spacing, apriltag_family = \"tag36h11\", first_id = 0)\n\nThe layout of an AprilGrid, Kalibr's grid of AprilTags (the aprilgrid target of Kalibr's target YAML: tagRows, tagCols, tagSize, tagSpacing): tag_rows × tag_cols square tags of side tag_size, tag_spacing apart — the gap between two neighbouring tags, a length like the side (Kalibr's tagSpacing is the ratio tag_spacing / tag_size). Both are lengths (90mm, 27mm) or both plain numbers. The tags are of the AprilTag family apriltag_family (Kalibr's grids are tag36h11), with ids from first_id (≥ 0) on. Tag i (0-based: the family id less the grid's first id) sits in row i ÷ tag_cols, column i % tag_cols.\n\nPlanarTarget(grid; calibration = Rigid(), board = nothing, canvas = nothing, placement = nothing) is the target of its tag corners (recording the grid, layout) in Kalibr's point order and frame: the 2tag_rows × 2tag_cols corners row by row, point r·2tag_cols + c (0-based, r and c 0-based) at\n\nx = (c ÷ 2)(tag_size + tag_spacing) + (c % 2)·tag_size\ny = (r ÷ 2)(tag_size + tag_spacing) + (r % 2)·tag_size\n\n— tag 0's first corner at the origin, x along the columns and y along the rows: the design chart (TargetFrame), x right and y up as the print is seen from the front. point_index(grid, tag, corner) is the point of a tag's corner. The points are in the layout's form (lengths, stored in metres, or plain numbers); a board or canvas is in that form too, and a page whose lower-left corner is not tag 0's first corner says so by its origin (Canvas(size, origin)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AprilGrid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PlanarTarget","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"PlanarTarget(nominal; calibration = Rigid(), board = nothing, canvas = nothing, placement = nothing)                  a design given by its points\nPlanarTarget(design::Symbol; nominal = nothing, calibration = Rigid(), board = nothing, canvas = nothing, placement = nothing)   a content-addressed design\n\nA planar target: a DESIGN — its nominal plane points, and/or the reference design they are resolved from (a BlobBoards board uid) — and a TargetCalibration C taking the design to the physical target, points(t) = C.(nominal). An unresolved target (a reference, no nominal points yet) is a pose-only board; resolve supplies its points, and its points is an error until then. VGC never parses a design file: the design's package resolves the reference.\n\nThe calibration may be Uncertain — Uncertain(c, CovMat(Σ)), Σ over parameter_vector(c) — as a PosedTarget's pose may be; the type parameter C is then the calibration kind it wraps. calibration(t) returns it as stored, design(t) the design and reference(t) the reference (or nothing).\n\nThe nominal points are plain numbers (stored as Float64) or lengths (stored in metres), all one form (has_units); a calibration with lengths (a rigid member's translation, a thin-plate spline's nodes and displacements, a height field's nodes and heights) is in the same form, or the constructor raises FlexUnits' DimensionError. ustrip(u, t) and with_unit(t, u) move the points and the calibration together. An unresolved target holds no points: it is neither form, and both move only its calibration.\n\nboard is the board the design is printed on, a Board (its size and its corner in the design chart), or nothing — the target's analogue of a camera's Sensor: Rect(board) is the printed domain in the design chart, and surface bounds the target's primitive by it. The board is in the points' form — lengths for physical points, plain numbers for plain ones (FlexUnits' DimensionError otherwise) — and the points lie inside Rect(board) to within a rounding margin of 16 eps of its coordinates, the margin surface grows the board by (an ArgumentError otherwise). Being in the design chart, the board is the same rectangle under any calibration. board(t) reads it; ustrip and with_unit move it with the points.\n\ncanvas is the printed page the board lies on, a Canvas (its size, its corner in the design chart and optionally the printed image, a CanvasTexture), or nothing: the record of what was printed, which the points, the calibration and surface never read. It is in the points' form like the board, and the board lies on it to within the same rounding margin (an ArgumentError otherwise). canvas(t) reads it; ustrip and with_unit move it with the points.\n\nplacement is a SimilarityMap{3}, or nothing: where the calibration's dimensionless chart lies in the target's frame (scale the chart's side, the identity rotation, the translation (origin, 0); see \"The placement\" in targets.jl). With one, the calibration acts in that chart — a HeightField or ThinPlateSpline built on the chart's nodes (RadiometricCamera's page [0, 1]²), its values in chart units — and is plain numbers whatever the points' form (an ArgumentError for a calibration with units); the placement is plain numbers in the points' unit (metres for a physical target): points(t) = (S₃ ∘ lift ∘ C ∘ S₂⁻¹).(nominal), a height scaled by the placement's side. The calibration's parameters, manifold and regularizer are its own: the placement is fixed data and adds none. A unit move moves the placement and leaves the calibration as it is. placement(t) reads it.\n\nA target built from a parametric design, PlanarTarget(layout; calibration, board, canvas, placement) (an AbstractTargetLayout: AprilGrid, SchoepsPattern), holds the layout's points as its nominal points and records the layout, layout(t); every other construction records none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PlanarTarget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PointTarget","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"PointTarget(points)\n\nA target given by its points, explicitly, in its own frame: 2-vectors (a plane's) or 3-vectors, all of one dimension, plain or Uncertain (an estimated constellation with its covariances). A motion-capture body's marker constellation, or a board whose calibration was resolved to measured points.\n\nA physical target's points are lengths, stored in metres, and an Uncertain physical point's covariance is in m²: values are held in base units, and the covariance is stored as given, never rescaled — supply it in m², not in the squared unit the point was written in. Plain points are stored as Float64. All are one form or the other (has_units); ustrip(u, t) and with_unit(t, u) move between them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PointTarget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SchoepsPattern","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"SchoepsPattern(; squares_x, squares_y, square_length, apriltags, star_segments = 16, apriltag_family = \"tag36h11\")\n\nThe layout of a tagged star pattern of Schöps et al.'s cameracalibration (their `createcalibrationpattern.py; the pattern YAML'ssquaresx,squaresy,squarelengthinmeters,numstarsegmentsandapriltags): asquaresx × squaresygrid of squares of sidesquarelength— a length (8mm) or a plain number — with a star feature ofstarsegmentssegments (even, ≥ 4) at every inner corner, and AprilTags of the familyapriltagfamily, each coveringwidth × heightsquares from square(x, y)(the YAML'stagx,tag_y,width,height: 0-based squares, x to the right and y DOWN as the pattern is printed) and carrying the family idid(the YAML'sindex).apriltagsis a collection of(; x, y, width, height)or(; x, y, width, height, id)— without ids the tags are numbered 0, 1, … in order — stored as(; x, y, width, height, id)`; each tag lies on the pattern, and the ids are distinct and ≥ 0.\n\nPlanarTarget(pattern; calibration = Rigid(), board = nothing, canvas = nothing, placement = nothing) is the target (recording the pattern, layout) of its star features in Schöps' feature order (FeatureDetectorTaggedPattern::GetCorners): the inner corners (gx, gy), gx ∈ 0:squares_x − 2, gy ∈ 0:squares_y − 2, row by row (gy outer), the corners next to a tag left out (IsValidFeatureCoord: x − 1 ≤ gx ≤ x − 1 + width and y − 1 ≤ gy ≤ y − 1 + height for some tag), so for a YAML's first pattern (pattern index 0) feature id k (0-based, Schöps' and a dataset.bin's) is point k + 1. GetCorners numbers the features of a YAML with several patterns across them, in order: pattern i's feature id k is point k + 1 − (n₀ + … + nᵢ₋₁) of its own target, nⱼ the number of points of pattern j's target. Each point is at\n\nsquare_length · (gx + 1, squares_y − 1 − gy)\n\n— the design chart (TargetFrame): the pattern's lower-left corner at the origin, x right and y up as the print is seen from the front. Schöps' own geometry (KnownGeometry: cell_length_in_meters · (gx, gy, 0), y down) is the same points under a half turn about x and a shift of one square, a rigid motion the pose absorbs. The points are in the layout's form (lengths, stored in metres, or plain numbers); a board or canvas is in that form too, and a page whose lower-left corner is not the pattern's says so by its origin (Canvas(size, origin)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SchoepsPattern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetManifold","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"TargetManifold(target; known = ()) <: Manifold\n\nA calibratable target's manifold anchored at target: minimal_manifold(target; known). No calibration kind has a gauge (the affine factors leave the pose's directions out of the composition, the thin-plate kinds hold no affine or plane part), so the manifold is every direction the calibration's parameters may move in, and with the target's pose estimated jointly every one of them is observable.\n\nThat holds for the print error a jointly estimated pose leaves, HeightField ∘ ThinPlateSpline ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew. A rigid member (EuclideanMap) duplicates the pose's rotation and translation, and a Scale the pose's scale (its translation's length): with the pose estimated jointly they are the pose's gauges, and the manifold REFUSES them (ArgumentError) unless the caller states what fixes them — known lists :length (a measured length, a ruler: Scale is observable) or :pose (a known pose, a fixture or motion capture: EuclideanMap and Scale both are). Otherwise drop them from the calibration, or absorb them into the pose, before a joint refinement. (A vgio_migrate of a retired \"affine\" record keeps all four factors EuclideanMap ∘ Scale ∘ Aspect ∘ Skew, the same map; that record takes known = (:pose,) or that reduction.)\n\nThe chart is linear: retract(ch, v) = with_parameters(target, θ₀ + B v), B an n × m matrix with orthonormal columns, so tangent_size(ch) = m and retract_jacobian(ch) = B. For the affine factors B is the identity and the coordinates are the parameters' offsets, named by parameter_names. For a ThinPlateSpline (a HeightField) B spans the 2K − 6 (K − 3) pure-bending directions, the null space of the interpolant's affine-coefficient rows — the complement of an orthonormal basis of those rows' span, from a Householder QR with column pivoting (LAPACK, so one reproducible basis per node set and parameter layout); a composition's the complement of its members' constraints together. action_jacobian(ch, x) = transform_deriv_params(c, x) · B at the design point x; local_coordinates(ch, t) = Bᵀ (θ(t) − θ₀). restrict(ch, names) keeps only the parameters names free, the others held.\n\nNO GAUGE WITH THE POSE, AND AN OBLIQUE COMPLEMENT. The tangent has no direction a change of the jointly estimated pose reproduces: the pose's nodal fields (a piston and two tilts of a height field, an in-plane translation and rotation of a thin-plate spline) are affine, and the thin-plate kinds' free directions are pure bending, their interpolant's affine part zero. That complement of the pose's directions is OBLIQUE, not orthogonal: the basis is orthogonal to the interpolant's affine ROWS (the pose's fields in the metric K⁻¹ of the radial basis), not to the plane fields themselves, so a bending direction correlates with a piston at a set of sampled points while no combination of them is one. The deformation's and the pose's spans intersect trivially — the stacked point Jacobian has full rank (test/mappedsurfacetests.jl pins its condition number and the smallest principal angle between the spans per composition) — which is the gauge-freedom statement; a Euclidean orthogonality in point space is not a property any parametrization can have. The spans can come close: with a 4 × 4 node grid reaching past the detections (nodes at ±0.6, samples to ±0.5 × ±0.4), the nearest bending direction is a nearly constant field of nodal heights — a bowl whose curvature lies outside the data, almost a piston on it — at about 5° (0.095 rad) from the pose's span, which inflates the variance along that one mode about 110×; nodes covered by detections (0.36 rad with the grid inside the samples) and the bending regularizer control it. A Scale and an in-plane EuclideanMap ARE pose directions, hence refused without known.\n\nTHE BASIS IS FIXED AT THE ANCHOR, and recenter keeps it: the constrained directions are constants of the nodes, so the slice is the same wherever it is anchored, and the manifold's type does not change.\n\nB is an SMatrix while n ≤ 18 and a Matrix{Float64} above: a static basis compiles in time that grows steeply with its size (the first call of the manifold's verbs, cold: n = 18, 0.6 s static against 0.8 s as a Matrix; n = 32, 2.3 s against 0.7 s; a 5 × 5 in-plane spline, n = 50, 9.9 s against 0.7 s; 7 × 7, n = 98, 100 s against 2.6 s). In the large case retract's step, action_jacobian and local_coordinates are a Vector or a Matrix, and tangent_size is a run-time value. Zero-dimensional for a Rigid target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetManifold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.board-Tuple{PlanarTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"board(t::PlanarTarget) -> Union{Nothing, Board}\n\nThe board the target's design is printed on (Board), or nothing when the target does not carry one. Rect(board(t)) is its domain in the design chart, plain metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.board"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration-Tuple{PlanarTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"calibration(t::PlanarTarget) -> TargetCalibration or Uncertain\n\nThe target's calibration as stored: a TargetCalibration, or an Uncertain one carrying its covariance (geom unwraps it). VisualGeometryToolkitIO's record of who calibrated a target (the tool, the date, the RMS) is a different thing with another name, the attribute Attributed(t, (; calibration_record = …)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.canvas-Tuple{PlanarTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"canvas(t::PlanarTarget) -> Union{Nothing, Canvas}\n\nThe page the target's board is printed on (Canvas, with its printed image when it carries one), or nothing when the target does not carry one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.canvas"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.design-Tuple{PlanarTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"design(t::PlanarTarget) -> Vector{SVector{2}} or Symbol\n\nThe target's design: its nominal plane points when it is resolved (is_resolved), the reference they are resolved from (a Symbol) when it is not. The physical target is calibration(t) applied to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_resolved-Tuple{AbstractTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"is_resolved(t) -> Bool\n\nWhether a target carries its points (a PlanarTarget holding only a design reference does not).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_resolved"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.layout-Tuple{PlanarTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"layout(t::PlanarTarget) -> Union{Nothing, AbstractTargetLayout}\n\nThe parametric design the target's nominal points were built from — an AprilGrid or a SchoepsPattern, given to PlanarTarget(layout; …) — or nothing for a target given by its points or by a reference. A unit move moves it with the points and a new calibration (with_parameters) keeps it; resolve, which supplies other points, drops it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.layout"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.placement-Tuple{PlanarTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"placement(t::PlanarTarget) -> Union{Nothing, SimilarityMap{3}}\n\nWhere the target's calibration chart lies in its design chart (a SimilarityMap{3}: the chart's side, the identity rotation, the translation (origin, 0), plain numbers in the target's form), or nothing when the calibration acts on the design chart itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.placement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.point_index-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AprilGrid, Integer, Integer}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"point_index(grid::AprilGrid, tag, corner) -> Int\n\nThe 1-based index, in PlanarTarget(grid)'s points, of corner corner of tag tag (both 0-based: Kalibr's tag id less the grid's first id, and its corner order 0 (min column, min row), 1 (max column, min row), 2 (max column, max row), 3 (min column, max row)) — Kalibr's GridCalibrationTargetAprilgrid point of a detected corner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.point_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reference-Tuple{PlanarTarget}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"reference(t::PlanarTarget) -> Union{Nothing, Symbol}\n\nThe content-addressed design the target's points are resolved from (a BlobBoards board uid), or nothing for a design given by its points alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/imaging/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.resolve-Tuple{PlanarTarget, AbstractVector}","page":"Imaging Chain & Targets","text":"resolve(t::PlanarTarget, nominal) -> PlanarTarget\n\nThe target with its design's nominal points supplied — what the design's owner returns for the reference reference(t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.resolve"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#The-Framed-Imaging-Chain","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"The imaging chain of a central camera, as framed stages with checked junctions (since 0.10):\n\nViewFrame{id} ─ ray-frame projection → ray frame (Gnomonic/Equidistant/…)\n              ─ lens distortion      → DistortedFrame{id}\n              ─ framed K-affine      → ImageFrame{id}\n\nimaging_chain mints the fully-typed composite {ViewFrame{id} → ImageFrame{id}}; the frame-free imaging_transform is the same map without frames, affine_part(K) ∘ lens with the lens applied to the ray. The scalar lens math is never reimplemented: the framed stages wrap the lens contract's maps.","title":"The Framed Imaging Chain"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#One-retina,-many-frames","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"Every central camera has a spherical retina — the sphere S² of rays through the center of projection (the manifold-level object of the Geyer–Daniilidis unified imaging model). What differs between camera models is the frame their distortion is expressed in — a 2D coordinate system on the retina, in differential-geometry terms a chart of the sphere — so frames, not retinas, are the type currency of the chain. The perspective plane is not a different manifold: it is the gnomonic chart of the sphere, degenerate at 90° and blind to Z ≤ 0 rays.\n\nThe RetinalFrame{id} family (complete since 0.13) shares the azimuth and differs in the radial profile, each with its own honest domain:\n\nframe radial profile domain note\nGnomonicFrame r = tan θ θ ∈ [0, π/2) gnomonic; calibrated coordinates x̂ = π(K⁻¹ũ)\nEquidistantFrame r = θ θ ∈ [0, π) the omnidirectional models' defining frame\nStereographicFrame r = 2·tan(θ/2) θ ∈ [0, π) conformal — the only angle-preserving member\nEquisolidFrame r = 2·sin(θ/2) θ ∈ [0, π] equal-area; the only frame reaching the antipode\nOrthographicFrame r = sin θ θ ∈ [0, π/2] only folds at the equator\n\nTwo more frames complete the vocabulary: DistortedFrame{id} — the post-lens, pre-K frame forward distortion lands on (defined by the lens map's image, deliberately not a RetinalFrame) — and ImageFrame{id}, the canonical pixel frame.","title":"One retina, many frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#Stage-1:-the-ray-frame-projection-family","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"\"Perspective precedes lens\" is a property of perspective-plane models, not a law of the chain. Stage 1 is therefore a family — one framed projection per ray frame, all one type, FramedProjection{Src, Dst}, named by their Dst family: FramedPerspective, FramedEquidistantProjection, FramedStereographicProjection, FramedEquisolidProjection, FramedOrthographicProjection. Each is a fiber collapse (3D ray → 2D ray-frame point) and deliberately has no inv — the right-inverses are the sections unproject and backproject, which select one point per fiber via a depth.\n\ndistortion_frame(lens) names which frame a lens model's distortion is defined in, and hence which projection stage 1 must be: for a Lens it is its projection's frame (projection_frame) — GnomonicFrame for the perspective projection, EquidistantFrame for the equidistant one (Kannala–Brandt's radial factor, COLMAP's thin-prism fisheye), and so on; a LiftLens is read on the gnomonic frame. A parametric projection (DoubleSphere, Unified, ExtendedUnified) lands on a frame its coefficients define, which no tag can name: imaging_chain serves such a lens through one fused FramedLensProjection, and the split mints refuse it (ArgumentError).","title":"Stage 1: the ray-frame projection family"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#Stages-2–3:-distortion-and-calibration","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"distort_stage(lens) mints the framed forward distortion {distortion_frame(lens){id} → DistortedFrame{id}} as a FramedDistort; undistort_stage is its frames-swapped inv (FramedUndistort). calibration_stage(K) mints the framed affine action of the intrinsics ({DistortedFrame{id} → ImageFrame{id}}) as a FramedAffine.\n\nDirection checking is the point: composing an undistort stage after another undistort stage, or after the ray projection, is a junction mismatch and throws a MethodError — distort-vs-undistort direction is what the frame tags check, view identity or not.","title":"Stages 2–3: distortion and calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#The-collapse-rule","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"When a lens's distortion is the identity in its defining frame (Pinhole on the gnomonic GnomonicFrame; the pure-projection fisheyes on theirs), there is no distortion stage: the chain collapses the ray frame onto the calibration stage — K consumes the ray frame directly — rather than inserting a manufactured identity stage. distort_stage errors for such lenses; the collapse is applied by imaging_chain automatically.","title":"The collapse rule"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#The-anonymous-view-id","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"The id axis is optional. The reserved symbol :_ is the designated anonymous view identity and every stage mint defaults to it — frame kind then still does the junction checking, so a lone CameraModel with no view identity gets direction-checked stages. Rig-derived chains thread real identities:\n\na, b = ids(rig)\nchain₁ = imaging_chain(model(source(rig)); id = a)   # {ViewFrame{a} → ImageFrame{a}}\n\n:_ is a real label, not a wildcard — the UnlabeledFrame rules are unchanged.","title":"The anonymous view id"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#Composition-and-FramedChain","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"∘ between framed stages is junction-checked under the standing wildcard truth table (same five-method statement as EuclideanMap) and produces a flat FramedChain, whose callable unrolls at compile time. The vararg FramedChain(stages...) constructor checks every inner junction and names the first mismatch. inv inverts and reverses the stages — and is therefore a MethodError for chains containing a ray projection.\n\nNumerically, imaging_chain matches imaging_transform: bit-identical for perspective-plane lenses, equal to round-off for the other frames. Both work in the model's defining frame; neither hops through the gnomonic plane.","title":"Composition and FramedChain"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/imaging-chain/#See-also","page":"The Framed Imaging Chain","text":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions — the sphere self-map behind the in-frame distortion polynomial\nBack-projection and Undistortion — the lens contract and inverting the distortion stage\nCoordinate Frames — the tag system\nImaging Chain and Stereo API","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#Robust-Estimation-API","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"note: Division of labor\nThe RANSAC loop, the problems and the core local optimizations live in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.jl. VisualGeometryToolkitCore provides the contracts and shared primitives documented here and on the pages beside it: the problem, scoring and local-optimization contracts with the baseline scorers (this page); the optimization strategies and the local-optimization contract (Optimization Strategies API); the Student-t + uniform scorer (Student-t + Uniform Scoring API); pool selection (Pool Selection API); the robust-loss family and the width axes (Robust Losses API); the robust-statistics toolkit, the generic block-coordinate-descent loop, the chart-rows contract and the chart LM (Chart LM API); the configuration tags, stack contracts and conformance helpers (Configuration Tags and Stack Contracts API). The model manifolds and image-domain confidence regions are on the Model Manifolds and Uncertainty API page.","title":"Robust Estimation API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#Estimation-Interfaces","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"","title":"Estimation Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#Problem-Protocol","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"","title":"Problem Protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#Scoring-Contract-and-Baseline-Scorers","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"","title":"Scoring Contract and Baseline Scorers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#The-Student-t-Uniform-Marginal-Scorer","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The scale-free marginal score of \"RANSAC Scoring Done Right\" is on the Student-t + Uniform Scoring API page.","title":"The Student-t + Uniform Marginal Scorer"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#Optimization-Strategies-and-the-Local-Optimization-Contract","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The optimization strategies (Irls, Triggs and their registry) and the local-optimization and external-refit contracts are on the Optimization Strategies API page.","title":"Optimization Strategies and the Local-Optimization Contract"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#Pool-Selection","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The pool-selection contract and its grid kernels are on the Pool Selection API page.","title":"Pool Selection"},{"category":"section","location":"api/estimators/#Index","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Pages = [\"estimators.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractOptimizationStrategy","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nA local-optimization strategy: refines a seed model on the full observation set. Strategies are routed by RefitFamily; in-loop IRLS-family strategies additionally plug into the solver via a weight policy (AbstractWeightPolicy).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractOptimizationStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractScoring","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"AbstractScoring\n\nA hypothesis score: evaluates a model's residuals and returns a value used for best-so-far gating, the size of the consensus set it implies (for the adaptive stopping rule), and that set's order (for the local optimizer). The contract — scoring_workspace, score!, inlier_count, consensus_order, and the optional verbs with their defaults — is stated in robust/scoring_contract.jl; the RANSAC loop calls only it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractWeightPolicy","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"AbstractWeightPolicy\n\nA reweighting rule: the nuisance and weight blocks of block_coordinate_descent!. The contract is deliberately minimal — a weight policy promises weights, nothing more:\n\nupdate_nuisance!(policy): refresh whatever nuisance parameters the policy carries — ŝ², ν, the mixing weight π, the Barron shape α — at the current residuals (a no-op for policies that carry none).\nupdate_weights!(policy): refresh per-residual weights from the current residuals and those nuisance parameters.\n\nThe two run as a pair, in that order, once per outer iteration at a fixed model — see update_nuisance! for the contract.\n\nA weight policy is NOT a likelihood. M-estimation losses with bounded potentials (Welsch, Huber elbows at frozen scale, Barron shape adaptation) have no normalizable density on an unbounded domain — their \"objective\" is a loss, and only its derivative machinery (ψ(r)/r) is used here. Do not score with a weight policy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractWeightPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusAnchored","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Scale interface: the strategy takes ŝ from the consensus set the score selected (see ScaleInterface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusAnchored"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"ConsensusSet(; count, s2, epoch, score = NaN)\n\nThe consensus set a local optimizer starts from — the inlier set of the most recent score! on a workspace, as consensus_set returns it: its size k* (count), its scale ŝ² (s2, the Student-t scale of the set under the scorer's prior, consensus_s2), the epoch of the score evaluation that produced it and that evaluation's score (NaN when the producer did not carry it). Construct it by keyword; the field order is not API.\n\nA ConsensusSet describes ONE evaluation: it is valid only while it reflects the MOST RECENT score! on the buffers it was built from. Producers stamp epoch with the buffers' evaluation counter; consumers (local optimizers) must establish that the stamp still equals the current epoch before using it, via check_fresh or is_fresh.\n\nRead it through its accessors, never by field name:\n\ninlier_count — count, the set size k*.\nscale_s2 — s2, the consensus scale ŝ².\nconsensus_score — score, the evaluation's score (NaN: not carried).\ncheck_fresh / is_fresh — the epoch stamp, which is only ever meaningful compared against a current epoch, and so is exposed as that comparison rather than as a raw number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ExternalRefit","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"ExternalRefit()\n\nThe RefitFamily of a strategy whose refit is an external refiner called at an absolute width, through external_workspace, prepare_external! and refine_external!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ExternalRefit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedScale","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Scale interface: one absolute loss width for every hypothesis (see ScaleInterface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedScale"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FollowsScorer","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Scale interface: the loss width follows the scorer's decision_boundary (see ScaleInterface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FollowsScorer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrozenNuisance","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"FrozenNuisance() <: AbstractWeightPolicy\n\nThe weight policy that holds nothing: update_nuisance! and update_weights! are no-ops that return the policy. For a block_coordinate_descent! whose loss stays at the deployment it was seeded with (a closed-form lift, a fixed-scale reweighting whose weights the model block computes itself): the nuisance and weight blocks are frozen, and the loop still calls them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrozenNuisance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NativeRefit","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"NativeRefit()\n\nThe RefitFamily of a strategy whose refit runs in the in-process solver loop (locally_optimize!); the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NativeRefit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoScale","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Scale interface: the strategy consumes no scale at all — the seed, ℓ₂ (see ScaleInterface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoScale"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NormalizedWeightPolicy","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"NormalizedWeightPolicy <: AbstractWeightPolicy\n\nA weight policy whose underlying objective IS a proper normalized probability model (e.g. the Student-t/uniform mixture posterior, the Gaussian). Only these support likelihood-grade operations: evaluating a comparable objective value, likelihood-monotonicity statements, and use as evidence in a score. The ρ(∞) = ∞ boundary of the theory, as a type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NormalizedWeightPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RefitFamily","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"RefitFamily(lo::AbstractOptimizationStrategy) -> RefitFamily\n\nHoly trait routing a strategy to its EXECUTION path (where the refit runs — an implementation concern, orthogonal to the paper's two classification axes optimization_path and scale_interface):\n\nNativeRefit()   — the in-process solver loop (weight policy / joint lift / continuation schedule / block-coordinate ascent all run here)\nExternalRefit() — an external refiner called at an absolute width outside the loop (refine_external!: PoseLib LM/LZ and its adaptive-width arm, RansacLib classical LO)\n\nNew strategies declare their family; consumers dispatch on it and never enumerate concrete strategy types.\n\nDESIGN RULE (RVG 2026-07-18 port): all native strategies run through the production locally_optimize port (NativeRefit, the default); the noise scale is ingested ONLY through the strategy's width mode (loss_at) from the scorer's consensus set, never from a deployed plug-in or loss-width-window bootstrap. An external arm that anchors a width (the adaptive PoseLib LM arm) reads its consensus scale through the production scoring machinery too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RefitFamily"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleInterface","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"ScaleInterface\n\nThe paper's scale-interface axis — how a local optimizer obtains the loss_width it needs (§3.6 \"Scale supply to local optimization\"). A LABEL, derived from the strategy, never a stored policy: a width-carrying strategy's interface is read off its width mode (lo_width).\n\nConsensusAnchored — takes ŝ from the consensus set the SCORE selected for the hypothesis being refined and sets loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ (the EfficiencyConstant width mode), cross-validating only the dimensionless efficiency_constant.\nFixedScale — one ABSOLUTE loss width for every hypothesis (the LossWidth width mode; TLS-LM, TLS-LZ, classical TLS-LO⁺).\nFollowsScorer — the loss width follows the scorer's decision point, decision_boundary (a strategy with no width of its own).\nSelfScaling — re-estimates ŝ at each iteration; the consensus set only initializes it (Stu-tU-BCD, Stu-tU-IRLS).\nNoScale — consumes no scale at all (the seed, ℓ₂).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleInterface"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SelfScaling","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"Scale interface: the strategy re-estimates ŝ at each iteration (see ScaleInterface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SelfScaling"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_fresh-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet, Integer}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"check_fresh(cs::ConsensusSet, epoch::Integer) -> nothing\n\nRefuse a stale ConsensusSet with an ArgumentError naming both stamps. epoch is the CURRENT epoch of the buffers that produced cs. Returns nothing when the set is fresh.\n\nThis is the one place the freshness precondition lives. It is a real throw, not an @assert: the failure guarded against is a silent use of an earlier evaluation's set — wrong answers, not a crash — so the check must not be removable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_fresh"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_score-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"consensus_score(cs::ConsensusSet) -> Float64\n\nThe score of the evaluation that produced cs (the value score! returned, so a local optimizer that ascends that score need not re-score its seed), or NaN when the producer did not carry it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_score"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_count-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"inlier_count(cs::ConsensusSet) -> Int\ninlier_count(scoring, W) -> Int\n\nThe size k* of a consensus set; for a scoring and its workspace, the k* of the most recent score! — the statistic that drives the hypergeometric stopping rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_count"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_mask-Tuple{AbstractWeightPolicy}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"inlier_mask(policy) -> BitVector\n\nis_inlier over every residual. Generic on purpose: a policy defines the rule, never the loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_weight","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"inlier_weight(policy) -> AbstractVector\n\nPer-residual inlier weight in [0, 1]: how much each observation belongs to the inlier component.\n\nIts MEANING is the policy's type. Under a NormalizedWeightPolicy it is a posterior membership probability, so it may be summed (an effective inlier count) and thresholded. Under a plain AbstractWeightPolicy it is a loss weight — orderable, but not a probability: summing it means nothing and > 0.5 is not a decision.\n\nReturns a STORED vector or a view of one — an O(1) accessor, never a computation: is_inlier calls it once per residual, so a freshly broadcast result would make inlier_mask quadratic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_weight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_fresh-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet, Integer}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"is_fresh(cs::ConsensusSet, epoch::Integer) -> Bool\n\nWhether cs still reflects the most recent score! on the buffers that produced it, i.e. whether its stamp equals their current epoch.\n\nA stale ConsensusSet describes an earlier evaluation: using it yields a plausible-looking wrong k* and ŝ², not an error — so the stamp must be checked before the set is used. Use check_fresh to enforce it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_fresh"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_inlier","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"is_inlier(policy, i::Integer) -> Bool\n\nWhether residual i is an inlier UNDER THIS POLICY'S OWN RULE. Every policy answers; they disagree about what the question means, which is why this is the policy's method and not a caller's expression.\n\nA NormalizedWeightPolicy takes the default below — the MAP rule is_map_inlier on its membership. A loss policy must state its own rule (typically a residual multiple of its scale): it has no membership to threshold, and 0.5 on a loss weight is arbitrary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_inlier"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_map_inlier-Tuple{Real}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"is_map_inlier(posterior_weight) -> Bool\n\nThe MAP inlier decision on a POSTERIOR inlier probability: inlier iff the posterior exceeds ½, i.e. the inlier label is the more probable one. The ½ is the definition of the MAP decision between two labels, not a tunable.\n\nTHE one statement of that rule: every posterior check in the stack calls it (RANSACScoringDoneRight's posterior weighting, VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation, the BlobBoards pipeline). It applies to a posterior membership only — never to an M-estimation loss weight, whose 0.5 has no probabilistic meaning (see is_inlier).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_map_inlier"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_label-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"lo_label(lo::AbstractOptimizationStrategy) -> String\n\nThe single source of truth for a strategy's figure/table DISPLAY NAME: its LaTeX label string, read by every renderer instead of a hand-maintained lookup table, so legends cannot drift. The marker companion is lo_marker. A concrete lo_label returns the BASE name (e.g. raw\"Welsch-HQ\", i.e. Loss-Path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_marker-Tuple{Union{Nothing, AbstractOptimizationStrategy}}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"lo_marker(lo::AbstractOptimizationStrategy) -> Symbol\n\nThe strategy's PGFPlots marker symbol — the figure grammar's shape = optimization path rule, derived from optimization_path through path_marker. Override only for a strategy that must stand apart from its path (ℓ₂ uses :x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lo_marker"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_family-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"loss_family(lo::AbstractOptimizationStrategy) -> Symbol\n\nThe loss ROW of the design table an arm belongs to (:welsch | :tls | :gm | :cauchy | …; the Student-t/uniform mixture arms report :tls — they share the \"uniform mixtures\" column). Determines the color HUE facet in the paper registry. Default :unspecified so registries can detect a strategy that has not declared its identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_family"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.optimization_path-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"optimization_path(lo::AbstractOptimizationStrategy) -> Symbol\n\nThe paper's optimization-path axis — the column of the design table:\n\n:irls         — static reweighting: the weight function of a FIXED loss is                   applied at every iteration (Welsch-IRLS, GM-IRLS, the hard                   truncated refit TLS-LM, Stu-tU-IRLS)\n:hq           — half-quadratic lift: model and lifted weights optimized                   JOINTLY, no schedule (Welsch-HQ, GM-HQ, TLS-HQ)\n:gnc          — explicit continuation: a scheduled sequence of progressively                   sharper problems (Welsch-GNC, GM-GNC, TLS-GNC, and TLS-LZ —                   Le–Zach's relaxed TLS tightened by a μ-schedule)\n:bcd          — block-coordinate ascent on the scoring objective itself                   over partition, scale and model (Stu-tU-BCD)\n:triggs       — the fixed loss minimized DIRECTLY: one robust                   Levenberg–Marquardt solve on NLLSsolver's robustified                   Hessian, no reweighting loop (Cauchy-Triggs)\n:classical_lo — classical LO-RANSAC inner refinement (TLS-LO, TLS-LO⁺)\n:seed         — no local optimization (the seed hypothesis is returned)\n\nDefault :unspecified, so registries can detect a strategy that has not declared its identity. Drives lo_marker (marker shape = path) and the path half of a composed lo_label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.optimization_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.path_marker-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The PGFPlots marker of an optimization path — the figure grammar's shape = path rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.path_marker"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.path_suffix-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The display suffix of an optimization path (the -IRLS / -HQ / -GNC / -BCD / -LO half of a method name).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.path_suffix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.robust_weight","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"robust_weight(policy) -> AbstractVector\n\nPer-residual factor in the weighted normal equations — psi(r)/r for an M-estimation loss, p_in * u for a Student-t/uniform mixture (membership times the component's own IRLS weight).\n\nEQUALS inlier_weight for a loss policy, where one number does both jobs. The two differ only where the model separates membership from reweighting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.robust_weight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_interface-Tuple{AbstractOptimizationStrategy}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"scale_interface(lo::AbstractOptimizationStrategy) -> ScaleInterface\n\nThe strategy's ScaleInterface label. A width-carrying strategy's is DERIVED from its width mode (lo_width): EfficiencyConstant → ConsensusAnchored(), LossWidth → FixedScale(), nothing → FollowsScorer(). Default UnspecifiedScale() so registries can detect an undeclared strategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_interface"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_s2-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConsensusSet}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"scale_s2(x) -> Float64\n\nThe squared scale ŝ² x carries: a ConsensusSet's consensus scale, a DeployedLoss's noise variance s2, a FixedS2 policy's held value. The robust layer speaks squared scale throughout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_s2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_nuisance!","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"update_nuisance!(policy::AbstractWeightPolicy) -> policy\n\nRefresh the policy's NUISANCE PARAMETERS at the current residuals and a fixed model. One half of the pair block_coordinate_descent! runs per iteration, always in this order:\n\nupdate_model! → residuals → update_nuisance! → update_weights! → check\n\nOne sweep, at a fixed model: update_nuisance! may not touch the model, and update_weights! reads whatever it left behind. A policy that carries no nuisance parameters implements this as a no-op — the loop still calls it.\n\nWhy not \"scale\"\n\nThe parameters this verb moves are, across the implementing policies: nothing at all, ŝ² alone, ν together with ŝ², the pair (ŝ², π), and — on the Barron arm — only the shape α, with no scale involved. \"Scale\" names only one of those.\n\nWhat they share is their ROLE, not their identity. The model (pose, homography, essential matrix) is the parameter of interest; ŝ², ν, π and α exist only because the likelihood cannot be written without them. That is the definition of a nuisance parameter, and it is true of all of them.\n\nNor is it an EM maximization: two implementations are genuine no-ops and the Barron α update is a direct minimization of the negative log-likelihood over a grid. \"Nuisance\" describes every implementation.\n\nSee AbstractWeightPolicy for the full policy contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_nuisance!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_weights!","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"update_weights!(policy::AbstractWeightPolicy) -> policy\n\nRefresh the policy's per-residual WEIGHTS from the current residuals and the nuisance parameters update_nuisance! just set, at a fixed model: the weight block of block_coordinate_descent!, run after the nuisance block in every iteration. It writes the weights and nothing else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.update_weights!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BinaryWeights","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"BinaryWeights()\n\nA WeightKind: weights in {0, 1} — a threshold scorer's consensus decision (RansacScoring, MsacScoring), or an L2 result's all ones. The inlier mask is w > 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BinaryWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IrlsWeights","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"IrlsWeights()\n\nA WeightKind: an M-estimator's loss weights ρ′, normalized to a maximum of 1 (Irls, Triggs). They have no inlier cut of their own: inlier_mask needs one given explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IrlsWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeights","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"PosteriorWeights()\n\nA WeightKind: posterior inlier probabilities — the Student-t + uniform scorer's, or an EM fit's E-step. The inlier mask is the MAP rule, is_map_inlier (posterior > ½).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PosteriorWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WeightKind","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"WeightKind\n\nWhat a robust result's per-datum weights mean, and so where their inlier cut lies: BinaryWeights, PosteriorWeights or IrlsWeights. A trait of the producer's type, read by weight_kind; inlier_mask dispatches on it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WeightKind"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_mask-Tuple{FitResult}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"inlier_mask(result) -> BitVector\ninlier_mask(result, cut::Real) -> BitVector\n\nThe inliers of a robust result, from its weights by their weight_kind: w > 0 for BinaryWeights, the MAP rule is_map_inlier for PosteriorWeights, and an ArgumentError for IrlsWeights, whose loss weights have no cut of their own. With cut, w > cut for any kind — the cut stated by the caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_weights-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Integer}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"unit_weights([T = Float64], n) -> AbstractVector{T}\n\nThe weights of an L2 result — every datum at weight one — as a lazy FillArrays.Fill(one(T), n): no allocation, and BinaryWeights by kind (their inlier_mask is every datum). What an L2 producer stores as its metadata's weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weight_kind","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"weight_kind(result) -> WeightKind\nweight_kind(::Type{P}) -> WeightKind\n\nThe WeightKind of a robust result's weights: a trait of its metadata's TYPE, which answers from its producer's type (a scorer, a local optimization). A contract generic: a producer or metadata type of another package adds its method on the type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weight_kind"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"AbstractConstraintProblem\n\nSupertype of a problem that scoring and local optimization can run on. Implementors provide data_size, codimension, minimal_sample_size, and constraint_with_jacobians; optionally observation_covariance and constraint_rounding_scale (the point's noise and its degeneracy scales), solver_covariance with provides_solver_covariance (predictive scoring), residual_context with mahalanobis_distance (a Jacobian-free distance path), and refine! with refine_workspace (the weighted refit local optimizations call).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.codimension","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"codimension(problem) -> Int\n\nConstraint codimension d_g: the number of independent constraint equations a true datum satisfies (1 for F/E/line scalar residual, 2 for homography / PnP). A scoring reports the codimension it was built for through the same verb. A problem defines it on its type, codimension(::Type{<:P}); the instance form falls back through it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.codimension"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.data_size","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"data_size(problem) -> Int\n\nTotal number of data points the problem scores over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.data_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mahalanobis_distance-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any, Int64}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"mahalanobis_distance(problem, ctx, i) -> Real\n\nThe Mahalanobis distance of data point i, ≥ 0: the norm of its whitened_residual, so that its square is the squared Mahalanobis distance gᵢᵀ Σ̃_{gᵢ}⁻¹ gᵢ (~ χ²(d_g) under H₀). ctx is residual_context(problem, model), the model by default. What every scorer reads per point: scores take its square, which the residual's sign does not move.\n\nA problem overrides it when a cheaper Jacobian-free route to the SAME distance exists (a reprojection error's norm), or to put a gate before it (a point the lens cannot place: OUT_OF_FIELD_RESIDUAL); an override returns a value ≥ 0. mahalanobis_distances! is this at every point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mahalanobis_distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mahalanobis_distances!-Tuple{AbstractVector, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"mahalanobis_distances!(d, problem, model) -> d\n\nFill d[i] with mahalanobis_distance(problem, ctx, i) for every index of d (1:data_size(problem) in the RANSAC loop), ≥ 0, so that d[i]^2 ~ χ²(d_g) under H₀. The per-model work is residual_context, computed once; a problem overrides that hook and mahalanobis_distance, not this loop. The signed rows are whitened_residuals!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mahalanobis_distances!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_sample_size","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"minimal_sample_size(problem) -> Int\n\nModel degrees of freedom m used in the residual DOF ν = (k − m)·d_g.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_sample_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.observation_covariance-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Int64}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"observation_covariance(problem, i) -> Σ̃_x\n\nThe covariance of data point i's observations, over the coordinates its constraint's G_x differentiates (a correspondence's pair block): what whitened_residual whitens by. A problem's observations carry their noise (conventions/observation-noise.md) — I for plain points, the default — and a problem that stores covariances (a correspondence set's Σ₁/Σ₂ columns, a line fit's per-point shapes) returns point i's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.observation_covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.provides_solver_covariance-Tuple{Type{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem}}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"provides_solver_covariance(::Type{<:AbstractConstraintProblem}) -> Bool\n\nWhether problem type P defines solver_covariance, so that it can be scored predictively. Default false; a provider declares provides_solver_covariance(::Type{<:P}) = true beside its method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.provides_solver_covariance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine!","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"refine!(R, problem, θ, w) -> θ′ | nothing\n\nThe weighted refit of problem from θ under per-point weights w (w[1:data_size(problem)]), on the problem's refit workspace R (refine_workspace) — the model block of every IRLS-family local optimization. nothing means the solve failed (an underdetermined or singular system); the caller keeps its incumbent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine_workspace","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"refine_workspace(::Type{P}, n::Int) -> R\n\nThe scratch of refine! for a problem of type P with up to n data points, built once per composed workspace and reused across calls. nothing for a problem whose refit needs none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.refine_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_context-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"residual_context(problem, model) -> ctx\n\nWhat mahalanobis_distances! and whitened_residuals! compute ONCE per model before their per-point loops, and hand to every mahalanobis_distance(problem, ctx, i) and whitened_residual(problem, ctx, i). The default is the model itself, so a problem that defines neither hook scores exactly as before.\n\nA problem overrides it when part of the residual depends on the model alone and is costly: a relative-pose bundle's cheiral pose decomposition and inverse intrinsics, an essential matrix widened to Float64 once rather than per point. It then defines the per-point hooks it answers for its context type C (constraint_with_jacobians(problem, ctx::C, i), which the defaults read, or mahalanobis_distance(problem, ctx::C, i) directly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_context"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.solver_covariance-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any, AbstractVector{Int64}}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"solver_covariance(problem, model, sample_indices) -> SMatrix or nothing\n\nModel parameter covariance Σ̃_θ propagated through the minimal solver, for predictive scoring. A provider returns nothing for a DEGENERATE minimal sample, one whose solver Jacobian does not exist, and predictive scoring then SKIPS that hypothesis like a failed solve.\n\nA provider declares itself with provides_solver_covariance. A problem type that defines no provider cannot be scored predictively: the fallback method throws an ArgumentError naming the problem type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.solver_covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_constraint_rows-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any, Int64}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"whitened_constraint_rows(problem, model, i) -> (r, J_w)\n\nData point i's constraint as a least-squares row: its whitened_residual and its model rows L_Σ⁻¹·G_θ under the SAME factor, held fixed (the Gauss–Newton row of a frozen whitening; L_Σ L_Σᵀ = G_x·Σ̃_x·G_xᵀ). In the constraint's shape: a number and an SVector row for a scalar constraint, an SVector and a matrix for a vector one. Degenerate (the gradient vanishes at the rounding scale, or Σ̃_x is singular along the normal): the rule's residual (0 when the constraint vanishes, else ±Inf) and ZERO rows. What a predictive scorer reads per point: the residual and the rows the model covariance is propagated through.\n\nThe residual is GTSAM's NoiseModelFactor::whitenedError for this point (its unwhitenedError is the constraint g), with the Sampson residual's noise along the constraint's normal; the rows are the frozen-whitening Gauss–Newton rows (the LM's exact rows are whitened_jacobian_rows!'s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_constraint_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_residual-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any, Int64}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"whitened_residual(problem, ctx, i) -> T | SVector{d_g}\n\nData point i's constraint as a whitened residual, SIGNED and in the constraint's shape: a number for a scalar constraint (d_g = 1, the signed distance gᵢ/√Σ̃_{gᵢ}), an SVector of its d_g components for a vector one (L⁻¹gᵢ, LLᵀ = Σ̃_{gᵢ} = ∂ₓgᵢ·Σ̃_x·∂ₓgᵢᵀ), so that ‖rᵢ‖² is the squared Mahalanobis distance gᵢᵀ Σ̃_{gᵢ}⁻¹ gᵢ (~ χ²(d_g) under H₀). ctx is residual_context(problem, model), the model by default. GTSAM's NoiseModelFactor::whitenedError for this point.\n\nThe default is the Sampson residual of constraint_with_jacobians(problem, ctx, i) at the problem's constraint_rounding_scale(problem, ctx, i) (base/sampson.jl's geometry layer, the stack's one Sampson path and its one degeneracy rule: ±Inf for an unsatisfiable degenerate point, 0 for one that vanishes exactly), whitened by the point's observation_covariance (one Cholesky of G_x·Σ̃_x·G_xᵀ). An override returns the same shape: a scalar for a vector constraint is a distance, not a residual, and test_constraint_problem refuses it. The distance is mahalanobis_distance; the rows over every point whitened_residuals!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_residual"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_residuals!-Tuple{AbstractVector, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractConstraintProblem, Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"whitened_residuals!(r, problem, model) -> r\n\nThe whitened_residual of every data point, stacked: point i's d_g signed components in rows d_g(i−1)+1 … d_g·i of r, d_g =codimension(problem), unweighted, at ctx =residual_context(problem, model), computed once. r holds every point, d_g·data_size(problem) rows; another length is a DimensionMismatch, and so is a point whose residual is not d_g components (a scalar override of a vector constraint): it fails loudly rather than reading past the data. The problem-level form of the model-level whitened_residuals!(r, M, xs) (unweighted too); its row norms are mahalanobis_distances!'s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.whitened_residuals!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoreBuffers","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"ScoreBuffers{T, W}(n, scoring_ws)\n\nThe scoring buffers of one composed workspace, built once and handed to a local optimizer by reference: the residuals (residuals(buf), storage precision T), the per-point scores (scores(buf), Float64 — a derived statistic: squaring a Float32 residual into Float64 is exact), the consensus mask (consensus_mask(buf)), the scorer's workspace (scoring_workspace(buf)) and the evaluation epoch that stamps every ConsensusSet built from them (check_fresh(cs, buf)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoreBuffers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_workspace-Tuple{AbstractScoring, Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"check_workspace(scoring, W) -> nothing\n\nRefuse, at entry, a workspace built for a different scorer (ArgumentError). The default refuses a workspace the scorer cannot read — no inlier_count(scoring, W) method but the contract's fallback: another scorer's scoring_workspace built it — and names the scorers it was built for. A scorer whose workspace also depends on its configuration (a prior's tables) adds a method that checks that as well. Called once per entry, never in the inner loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_mask!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoreBuffers, Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"consensus_mask!(buf, scoring) -> buf\n\nSet consensus_mask(buf) to the consensus set of the most recent score!: the first inlier_count entries of consensus_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_mask!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_mask-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoreBuffers}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The consensus mask of a ScoreBuffers: the set of the last scored model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_order","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"consensus_order(scoring, W) -> AbstractVector{Int}\n\nThe order of the points after the most recent score!: its first inlier_count(scoring, W) entries are the consensus set. A view of the scorer's buffer, valid until the next score!. A workspace another scorer built is an ArgumentError (the contract's fallback; see check_workspace).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_s2-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"consensus_s2(scoring, residuals, order, k; model_dof, d_g) -> Float64\n\nThe Student-t scale ŝ² of the consensus set order[1:k] under the scorer's scale_prior: partition_student_t of its RSS (partition_rss, from the plain residuals). model_dof is the caller's — m + 1 for a local optimizer's consensus set (the scale's extra dof), m for a result's σ̂.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_s2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_set-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"consensus_set(scoring, W, residuals; model_dof, d_g, epoch, score = NaN) -> ConsensusSet\n\nThe consensus set of the most recent score!: its size inlier_count, its scale consensus_s2 over consensus_order, stamped with epoch, carrying that evaluation's score when the caller has it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.consensus_set"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_boundary","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"decision_boundary(scoring, buf::ScoreBuffers) -> Float64\n\nThe scorer's decision boundary for the most recent score! on buf: the residual below which a point is an inlier. Two behaviours, by scorer:\n\na threshold scorer (RansacScoring, MsacScoring, the loss-width families): its threshold τ, a CONSTANT of the scorer — the same whatever buf holds;\nthe Student-t + uniform scorer (StudentTUniformScoring): the MAP inlier/outlier crossover of the Gaussian–uniform pair at the CURRENT consensus scale ŝ (the Student-t scale of the last score's consensus set) and its outlier half-width h, decision_boundary(GauInlier(ŝ²; d_g), h) — an ESTIMATE, recomputed with every score.\n\nA local optimizer with no width of its own follows it (loss_at). Only scorers with has_decision_boundary define it. The same generic gives a loss shape's half-weight boundary at a loss width and the Gaussian–uniform pair's at an outlier half-width (decision_boundary(shape, loss_width), decision_boundary(::GauInlier, outlier_halfwidth)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_boundary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_decision_boundary-Tuple{Type{<:AbstractScoring}}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"has_decision_boundary(::Type{S}) -> Bool\n\nWhether scorer type S defines decision_boundary — a static trait, false by default, declared true beside the method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_decision_boundary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_probabilities!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"inlier_probabilities!(w, scoring, scores, order, k; model_dof, d_g) -> w\n\nPer-point inlier probabilities of the consensus set order[1:k]: under a scorer with an outlier model (outlier_halfwidth a number) the Student-t + uniform posterior of the set's Student-t (posterior_inlier_probs! on the squared per-point scores, under the scorer's prior); otherwise 0/1 membership of order[1:k].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.inlier_probabilities!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_predictive-Tuple{AbstractScoring}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"is_predictive(scoring) -> Bool\n\nWhether the scorer scores a model WITH its covariance (θ::Uncertain). Default false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_predictive"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_halfwidth-Tuple{AbstractScoring}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"outlier_halfwidth(scoring) -> Float64 | nothing\n\nThe half-width of the scorer's uniform outlier model, if it has one (the Student-t + uniform posterior weights read it); nothing otherwise (default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_halfwidth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residuals-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoreBuffers}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The residuals of a ScoreBuffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residuals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sample_point_held-Tuple{Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"sample_point_held(x) -> Bool\n\nTHE rule of a minimal sample's points, every scorer's (the threshold families' consensus partition and score correction, the Student-t scorer's zeroed scores, the predictive scorer's scores and penalties — one decision per point, from its score — and the pool staging, through zero_sample!): a sample point is held at zero residual — its model fits it by construction — only where its value x (a residual, its square, a score) is finite. A non-finite one is a point the model does not reach (a lattice detection a pose leaves unassigned, its distance Inf), and it stays an outlier: out of the consensus set, its RSS and its dof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sample_point_held"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_prior-Tuple{AbstractScoring}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"scale_prior(scoring) -> IGPrior\n\nThe scale prior the scorer's consensus scale is fit under. Default JeffreysPrior().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_prior"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score!","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"score!(W, scoring, scores, residuals, n; sample = nothing, bound = -Inf) -> Float64\nscore!(W, scoring, scores, residuals, problem, θ::Uncertain, n; sample = nothing, bound = -Inf) -> Float64\n\nScore a model from its residuals residuals[1:n] (model-certain), or — a predictive scorer only — from the problem and the model with its covariance (filling residuals with the whitened residuals of the same constraint evaluation). Fills scores[1:n] with the per-point score and caches in W what inlier_count and consensus_order read. sample: the minimal sample's indices (or nothing), whose points are exact by construction. Higher is better.\n\nbound: the score a caller must beat (strictly) for the result to matter. A bounded-sum scorer (score_bound) MAY stop early once the candidate provably cannot exceed it; it then returns a value ≤ bound and its cached state is undefined until the next full score!. The default -Inf never stops early.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_bound-Tuple{AbstractScoring}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"score_bound(scoring) -> Float64 | nothing\n\nAn upper bound of one point's contribution to a BOUNDED-SUM score (each point adds a quality in [0, b] to the total, to within a few ulps of b), or nothing (default) for a scorer whose score is not such a sum. A bounded-sum scorer may stop a score! early against its bound keyword. The threshold loop of AbstractThresholdScoring corrects the minimal sample's points after the sum, so its exit needs the lower bound 0 as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_bound"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_model!-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoreBuffers, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"score_model!(buf, scoring, problem, θ; sample = nothing, reuse_residuals = false,\n             mask = true, bound = -Inf) -> (S, k)\n\nTHE one scoring path of a model on a composed workspace: bump the epoch (every ConsensusSet issued before is stale), compute θ's residuals into residuals(buf) (skipped under reuse_residuals — the caller left them there — for a model-certain scorer), score!, and set the consensus mask (mask = false defers it: consensus_mask! sets it when needed). θ is the SCORED form (scored_model): an Uncertain under a predictive scorer, which then fills the residuals itself. Returns the score and inlier_count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.score_model!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scored_model-Tuple{AbstractScoring, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"scored_model(scoring, problem, θ, sample) -> θ′ | nothing\n\nThe form in which a model is scored: the plain model (default), or for a predictive scorer Uncertain(θ, Σ̃θ) with the covariance of the model fit on the points sample; nothing when there is none (a degenerate sample, which is skipped like a failed solve).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scored_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scores-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoreBuffers}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"The per-point scores of a ScoreBuffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scores"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scoring_workspace","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"scoring_workspace(scoring, n::Int, d_g::Int) -> W\n\nThe scorer's own scratch for up to n points of codimension d_g, built ONCE per composed workspace and reused by every score!. scoring_workspace(buf) reads it back from a ScoreBuffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scoring_workspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_width","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"with_width(x, w::WidthAxis) -> typeof(x) with the width `w`\n\nThe width setter shared by scorings and local optimizations: a scoring rebuilt at a native width (with_width(scoring, OutlierHalfwidth(a))), a local optimizer given an explicit width mode (with_width(lo, EfficiencyConstant(m)), with_width(lo, LossWidth(w))). The owner of the type implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.with_width"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractThresholdScoring","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"AbstractThresholdScoring{T} <: AbstractScoring\n\nA scorer whose score is a sum of per-point qualities at one threshold τ (its loss width): S = Σᵢresidual_quality(scoring, dᵢ², τ), higher is better. T is the threshold's type: Float64 at a threshold (a scorer of the scoring contract), Nothing for the width-free form, a grid family that a loss width is swept over and that implements no scoring contract.\n\nA subtype at a threshold implements residual_quality and holds its threshold in a field threshold (or adds an decision_boundary method), and inherits the rest from AbstractThresholdScoring{Float64}:\n\nscore! on a ThresholdScoringWorkspace: the one threshold loop. Σ quality over every point in index order, a non-finite squared residual an outlier (quality 0; a finite d whose square overflows too), then each of the minimal sample's points corrected to zero residual; the consensus set is d² < τ² (strict) with the sample's points in it.\nscoring_workspace, inlier_count, consensus_order, decision_boundary (τ) and has_decision_boundary (true).\nscore_bound: nothing (no early exit) by default. A subtype states a bound b only where it holds 0 ≤ residual_quality(scoring, d², τ) ≤ b for every finite d² ≥ 0, to within a few ulps of b (the exit's margin absorbs those). Both sides matter: the sample's points are corrected after the sum, so a negative quality could let the corrected score exceed the bound the exit tested.\n\nCore's subtypes are RansacScoring and MsacScoring; a downstream package adds its own loss-width families (RANSACScoringDoneRight's paper baselines) the same way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractThresholdScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MsacScoring","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"MsacScoring{DG, T} <: AbstractThresholdScoring{T}\n\nMSAC at codimension DG: the truncated quadratic 1 − ρ(d², τ²)/τ² at the threshold τ, summed over the residuals (see residual_quality). Constructors as RansacScoring: width-free MsacScoring() / MsacScoring(dg) / MsacScoring(problem), or the loop scorer MsacScoring(dg; threshold = τ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MsacScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RansacScoring","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"RansacScoring{DG, T} <: AbstractThresholdScoring{T}\n\nRANSAC inlier counting at codimension DG: a model's score is the number of residuals below the threshold (see residual_quality).\n\nRansacScoring(), RansacScoring(dg), RansacScoring(problem): the width-free grid family (T = Nothing);\nRansacScoring(dg; threshold = τ), RansacScoring(problem; threshold = τ): the loop scorer at threshold τ (in the problem's residual unit — pixels for the pixel-data problems), T = Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RansacScoring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThresholdScoringWorkspace","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"ThresholdScoringWorkspace\n\nThe workspace of a threshold scorer (an AbstractThresholdScoring at a threshold): the consensus order of the last score! (inliers first, each part in index order — a stable partition, no sort) and its size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThresholdScoringWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_quality","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"residual_quality(scoring, d2::Float64, loss_width::Float64) -> Float64\n\nThe score contribution of ONE residual with squared value d2 under a loss-width family scoring at loss_width; a model's score is the sum over its residuals, and higher is better:\n\nRANSAC: the inlier indicator d² < loss_width² (TLS's weight);\nMSAC: 1 − ρ(TLSShape(), d², loss_width²)/loss_width².\n\nRANSACScoringDoneRight adds the methods of its paper baselines (MLESAC, MAGSAC++, the loss costs). Plain Float64 functions, callable inside a kernel. A non-finite d2 is an outlier and is skipped by every caller; it is not an input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.residual_quality"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/estimators/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.@threshold_scoring_constructors-Tuple{Any}","page":"Robust Estimation","text":"@threshold_scoring_constructors S\n\nThe constructors and the width setter of a threshold-scorer family S{DG, T} <:AbstractThresholdScoring{T} (DG its codimension, its threshold in a field threshold, an inner constructor S{DG, T}(threshold)), as RansacScoring and MsacScoring have them: S{DG}() and S() (codimension 1) the width-free form; S(dg; threshold), S(problem; threshold) and S(P; threshold) (a problem, or a problem TYPE, gives the codimension) at a threshold, refused unless positive and finite (ArgumentError), or width-free without one; and with_width(::S{DG}, w::LossWidth), the same family at w's loss width. A downstream family (RANSACScoringDoneRight's loss-width baselines) is given them by this one line after its struct, exactly as Core's two are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.@threshold_scoring_constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Core-Types-API","page":"Core Types","text":"The core composition and uncertainty types (Attributed, Uncertain, CovMat/PrecMat), the conic and epipolar matrix types, interface stubs, and traits. See the Design: Attributed{V,A} and Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty guides.","title":"Core Types API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Core-Types","page":"Core Types","text":"","title":"Core Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Uncertainty-Accessors","page":"Core Types","text":"prec is documented above with the core types; cov extends Statistics.cov (unexported — reachable as Statistics.cov):","title":"Uncertainty Accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Fit-Results","page":"Core Types","text":"Every estimator returns a FitResult: a fixed core — the estimate, why it stopped (its termination record), how many iterations, the residual RMS over n — plus solver-specific metadata selected by type parameter. Read it through the accessors below (estimate(r), retcode(r), …, and the metadata's own such as weights(r)), never by field name. A property spelling still forwards to the metadata for older call sites; a name on neither is an error, never nothing.\n\nA robust result records ONE scale — the one it ran with, or the one it estimated — and where it came from: its metadata's scale and scale_source, read by noise_scale and scale_source. A downstream stage takes the hand-off as scale = result; scale_input parses a stage's scale/width keywords, exactly one of which it requires.\n\nWhy a fit stopped is its termination record, a ReturnCode and a StopRule in the result's core: see the Termination Record page.\n\nIts per-datum weights in [0, 1] are weights(result), a plain vector; what they mean — and so where inlier_mask(result) cuts them — is the producer type's WeightKind, read by weight_kind (Estimation Interfaces).","title":"Fit Results"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Index","page":"Core Types","text":"Pages = [\"types.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection","page":"Core Types","text":"AbstractDetection\n\nType alias for Attributed{<:Union{FeatureFrame, Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}}} — any Attributed value whose wrapped value is a FeatureFrame, or a FeatureFrame with its covariance or precision (Uncertain).\n\nThis replaces the former abstract type. d isa AbstractDetection is true for any Attributed{V,A} where V <: FeatureFrame or V <: Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}. Methods on it read the geometry through feature_frame, never d.value, so both kinds behave alike.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractFundMat","page":"Core Types","text":"Abstract parent for 3×3 epipolar matrices (FundMat, EssentialMat).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractFundMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractHomConicMat","page":"Core Types","text":"Abstract parent for all 3×3 homogeneous conic matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractHomConicMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Attributed","page":"Core Types","text":"Attributed{V,A}\n\nGeneric container composing a value with attributes. Used as the single composition primitive for scored correspondences, detections, deployed losses, uncertain values (Uncertain is the alias Attributed{V, <:Union{CovMat, PrecMat}}) and any \"value with metadata\" pattern.\n\nType Parameters\n\nV: The value (EllipticFrame, Homography, camera pose, correspondence, etc.)\nA: Attributes type (a score Float64, a covariance, a struct of attributes, …)\n\nFields\n\nvalue::V: The wrapped value\nattributes::A: Associated attributes\n\nProperty Access (via @generated getproperty)\n\nr.value, r.attributes → direct field access\nNamed fields of A → forwarded from attributes; when A is a statistical form (CovMat/PrecMat, i.e. r is an Uncertain) its storage is not a record: r.matrix names the form itself and nothing else is forwarded from it\nOther symbols → forwarded to value\n\nExample\n\nsc = Attributed(src => dst, 0.83)   # a scored correspondence\nsc.value            # the Pair\nsc.attributes       # 0.83\nsc.first            # forwarded to value (the Pair)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Attributed"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundedRange","page":"Core Types","text":"BoundedRange{T}\n\nA validated range with min ≤ max constraint. Useful for detector parameters.\n\nExample\n\nscale_range = BoundedRange(1.0, 100.0)\nx in scale_range  # true if 1.0 ≤ x ≤ 100.0\nclamp(x, scale_range)  # clamps to [1.0, 100.0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundedRange"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Conic","page":"Core Types","text":"Conic{T} <: AbstractHomConicMat{T}\n\nA 2D conic section of any class (ellipse, hyperbola, parabola or degenerate), stored and constructed as HomEllipseMat is, with no class check. What a HomographyMap makes of a circle or an ellipse: classify_conic names its class and Ellipse(c) (checked: an ArgumentError for another class) reads the ellipse back; to map a shape and get its ellipse or nothing in one non-throwing call, use transfer_ellipse(H, shape) on the map and the shape instead.\n\nQ = HomographyMap(M)(circle)   # a Conic\nis_ellipse(Q) && Ellipse(Q)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Conic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Correspondence","page":"Core Types","text":"Correspondence{S,T} = Pair{S,T}\n\nA correspondence between a source element of type S and a target of type T. This is a type alias — correspondences are plain Pairs.\n\nCorrespondences are stored in columnar layout via StructArray{Correspondence{S,T}}, accessed as cs.first (sources) and cs.second (targets).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Correspondence"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CovMat","page":"Core Types","text":"CovMat{N,T,M,F} <: StaticMatrix{N,N,T}\n\nN×N covariance matrix Σ = Λ⁻¹ — the covariance counterpart to PrecMat.\n\nUse when the upstream source emits covariance natively (Gaussian process regressors, MVN priors, Kalman-filter outputs).  The two forms are interconvertible: inv(Λ) and inv(Σ) (frame-preserving — the representation swaps, the frame does not).\n\nTensor character and transformation law\n\nCovariance is a (2,0) tensor — both slots contravariant (it PAIRS with two covectors: Σ = E[δx δxᵀ] is built from displacement components). Under a change of coordinates with Jacobian J it pushes forward:\n\nΣ ↦ J Σ Jᵀ\n\nThis is the OPPOSITE variance of PrecMat's pullback Λ ↦ J⁻ᵀ Λ J⁻¹, and this covariant/contravariant law pair is exactly the bug surface the frame label exists to check: conjugating with the wrong factor produces a plausible-looking, silently wrong matrix. With labeled frames, propagate through a framed map dispatches to the correct law per type and records the destination frame on the result.\n\nFrame parameter (0.9)\n\nThe trailing F <: Frame{N} names the frame the matrix's coordinates live in; every historically-shaped constructor defaults it to UnlabeledFrame{N} (the gradual-typing wildcard — see PrecMat). Storage (the held matrix M, parent(Σ): an SMatrix, SDiagonal or ScaledIdentity) and the StaticArrays interface mirror PrecMat.\n\nExample\n\nΣ = CovMat{2,Float32}(4.0f0, 1.0f0, 1.0f0, 9.0f0)   # 2×2 covariance\ne = Ellipse(Uncertain(Point2(100.0, 200.0), Σ); sigma=3)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CovMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DiagonalCovMat","page":"Core Types","text":"FullCovMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalCovMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicCovMat{N, T, C}\nFullPrecMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalPrecMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicPrecMat{N, T, C}\n\nA covariance (precision) by the matrix it holds: an SMatrix (full), an SDiagonal (CovMat(SDiagonal(σ₁², σ₂²))) or a ScaledIdentity (CovMat(ScaledIdentity{2}(σ²)), CovMat{2}(σ = …)). The storages order isotropic ⊂ diagonal ⊂ full. A form keeps its storage under inv and relabel; convert and promote give the coarser of two storages, never a finer one (a container of mixed storage is one concrete type); propagate through a map gives the full form; a unit change gives the diagonal form of an isotropic one (lengths and angles scale apart). Arithmetic on the structure is the matrix's own, parent(Σ); on the form itself it sees the full N × N entries and gives an SMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DiagonalCovMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DiagonalPrecMat","page":"Core Types","text":"FullCovMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalCovMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicCovMat{N, T, C}\nFullPrecMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalPrecMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicPrecMat{N, T, C}\n\nA covariance (precision) by the matrix it holds: an SMatrix (full), an SDiagonal (CovMat(SDiagonal(σ₁², σ₂²))) or a ScaledIdentity (CovMat(ScaledIdentity{2}(σ²)), CovMat{2}(σ = …)). The storages order isotropic ⊂ diagonal ⊂ full. A form keeps its storage under inv and relabel; convert and promote give the coarser of two storages, never a finer one (a container of mixed storage is one concrete type); propagate through a map gives the full form; a unit change gives the diagonal form of an isotropic one (lengths and angles scale apart). Arithmetic on the structure is the matrix's own, parent(Σ); on the form itself it sees the full N × N entries and gives an SMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DiagonalPrecMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialMat","page":"Core Types","text":"EssentialMat{T} <: AbstractFundMat{T}\n\nTyped wrapper for a 3×3 essential matrix encoding the calibrated epipolar constraint x̂₂ᵀ E x̂₁ = 0, where x̂ᵢ = Kᵢ⁻¹ uᵢ.\n\nDistinguished from FundMat by the additional E-manifold constraint (σ₁ = σ₂, σ₃ = 0) and by operating on calibrated (K⁻¹-mapped) points. Minimal sample size: 5 (Nistér 2004).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FeatureFrame","page":"Core Types","text":"FeatureFrame{T}\n\nAbstract type for geometric feature frames.\n\nA feature frame defines a transformation from canonical space (unit circle/square) to image space. The transformation encodes the feature's position, scale, and shape.\n\nConcrete subtypes:\n\nDisc{T}: 3 DOF - isotropic (translation + uniform scale)\nOrientedDisc{T}: 4 DOF - isotropic with orientation\nEllipticFrame{T}: 5 DOF - anisotropic (symmetric shape factor)\nAffineFrame{T}: 6 DOF - full affine (general shape factor)\n\nInterface\n\nAll frames implement:\n\nAffineMap(frame) → AffineMap from canonical to image coordinates\nregion(frame, k=1.0) → unitless Circle or Ellipse at the k·σ contour\ncenter(frame) → Point2{T} center position\nBase.inv(frame) → frame with inverted transformation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FeatureFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedModels","page":"Core Types","text":"FixedModels{N, M}\n\nStack-allocated container for up to N candidate models of type M.\n\nSupports for model in solutions iteration and length(solutions). Used by multi-solution solvers (e.g., 7-point F-matrix) to return 1–3 results without heap allocation.\n\nisbitstype(FixedModels{N,M}) == true when isbitstype(M) == true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FixedModels"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FullCovMat","page":"Core Types","text":"FullCovMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalCovMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicCovMat{N, T, C}\nFullPrecMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalPrecMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicPrecMat{N, T, C}\n\nA covariance (precision) by the matrix it holds: an SMatrix (full), an SDiagonal (CovMat(SDiagonal(σ₁², σ₂²))) or a ScaledIdentity (CovMat(ScaledIdentity{2}(σ²)), CovMat{2}(σ = …)). The storages order isotropic ⊂ diagonal ⊂ full. A form keeps its storage under inv and relabel; convert and promote give the coarser of two storages, never a finer one (a container of mixed storage is one concrete type); propagate through a map gives the full form; a unit change gives the diagonal form of an isotropic one (lengths and angles scale apart). Arithmetic on the structure is the matrix's own, parent(Σ); on the form itself it sees the full N × N entries and gives an SMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FullCovMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FullPrecMat","page":"Core Types","text":"FullCovMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalCovMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicCovMat{N, T, C}\nFullPrecMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalPrecMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicPrecMat{N, T, C}\n\nA covariance (precision) by the matrix it holds: an SMatrix (full), an SDiagonal (CovMat(SDiagonal(σ₁², σ₂²))) or a ScaledIdentity (CovMat(ScaledIdentity{2}(σ²)), CovMat{2}(σ = …)). The storages order isotropic ⊂ diagonal ⊂ full. A form keeps its storage under inv and relabel; convert and promote give the coarser of two storages, never a finer one (a container of mixed storage is one concrete type); propagate through a map gives the full form; a unit change gives the diagonal form of an isotropic one (lengths and angles scale apart). Arithmetic on the structure is the matrix's own, parent(Σ); on the form itself it sees the full N × N entries and gives an SMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FullPrecMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FundMat","page":"Core Types","text":"FundMat{T} <: AbstractFundMat{T}\n\nTyped wrapper for a 3×3 fundamental matrix encoding the epipolar constraint u₂ᵀ F u₁ = 0 between two views.\n\nNot a geometric transform — maps points to epipolar lines, not points to points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FundMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomCircleMat","page":"Core Types","text":"HomCircleMat{T} <: AbstractHomConicMat{T}\n\nThe homogeneous 3×3 matrix of a real circle, stored and constructed as HomEllipseMat is. Its class check adds isotropy: Q₁₁ = Q₂₂ and Q₁₂ = 0 within √eps · ‖A‖.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomCircleMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomEllipseMat","page":"Core Types","text":"HomEllipseMat{T} <: AbstractHomConicMat{T}\n\nThe homogeneous 3×3 matrix of a real, non-degenerate ellipse x̃ᵀ Q x̃ = 0, x̃ = (x, y, 1), stored as its six unique coefficients (Q₁₁, Q₁₂, Q₂₂, Q₁₃, Q₂₃, Q₃₃) — exactly symmetric by storage; indexing returns the full symmetric matrix, and arithmetic on it gives an SMatrix. Built from an Ellipse, a symmetric 3×3 matrix (or its nine entries, column-major) or the six coefficients. A matrix is checked for symmetry (isapprox(Q, Qᵀ; rtol = √eps), its off-diagonal pairs averaged), and check = true checks the class — classify_conic answering EllipseTrait, the same test — with an ArgumentError naming what it is instead; a general conic is a Conic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomEllipseMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IsotropicCovMat","page":"Core Types","text":"FullCovMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalCovMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicCovMat{N, T, C}\nFullPrecMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalPrecMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicPrecMat{N, T, C}\n\nA covariance (precision) by the matrix it holds: an SMatrix (full), an SDiagonal (CovMat(SDiagonal(σ₁², σ₂²))) or a ScaledIdentity (CovMat(ScaledIdentity{2}(σ²)), CovMat{2}(σ = …)). The storages order isotropic ⊂ diagonal ⊂ full. A form keeps its storage under inv and relabel; convert and promote give the coarser of two storages, never a finer one (a container of mixed storage is one concrete type); propagate through a map gives the full form; a unit change gives the diagonal form of an isotropic one (lengths and angles scale apart). Arithmetic on the structure is the matrix's own, parent(Σ); on the form itself it sees the full N × N entries and gives an SMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IsotropicCovMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IsotropicPrecMat","page":"Core Types","text":"FullCovMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalCovMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicCovMat{N, T, C}\nFullPrecMat{N, T, C}, DiagonalPrecMat{N, T, C}, IsotropicPrecMat{N, T, C}\n\nA covariance (precision) by the matrix it holds: an SMatrix (full), an SDiagonal (CovMat(SDiagonal(σ₁², σ₂²))) or a ScaledIdentity (CovMat(ScaledIdentity{2}(σ²)), CovMat{2}(σ = …)). The storages order isotropic ⊂ diagonal ⊂ full. A form keeps its storage under inv and relabel; convert and promote give the coarser of two storages, never a finer one (a container of mixed storage is one concrete type); propagate through a map gives the full form; a unit change gives the diagonal form of an isotropic one (lengths and angles scale apart). Arithmetic on the structure is the matrix's own, parent(Σ); on the form itself it sees the full N × N entries and gives an SMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IsotropicPrecMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PrecMat","page":"Core Types","text":"PrecMat{N,T,M,F} <: StaticMatrix{N,N,T}\n\nN×N information (precision) matrix Λ = Σ⁻¹ for per-observation residual uncertainty. Parametric in N so it sizes to the value dof for any geometric value: 2 for Point2, 3 for Disc, 5 for EllipticFrame, M for Hyperplane{M}, etc. M is the static matrix it holds (parent(Λ)), and its type is the storage: an SMatrix (full), an SDiagonal (diagonal) or a ScaledIdentity (isotropic) — see DiagonalCovMat.\n\nUsed as the storage form for sources that emit precision natively (e.g., RoMa v2 dense matcher, factor-graph SLAM backends, any EIV estimator that propagates observation noise to the constraint).\n\nTensor character and transformation law\n\nPrecision is a (0,2) tensor — a bilinear form with two covariant argument slots (it EATS a pair of displacement vectors: the Mahalanobis quadratic rᵀ Λ r). Under a change of coordinates with Jacobian J it pulls back:\n\nΛ ↦ J⁻ᵀ Λ J⁻¹\n\nThis is the OPPOSITE variance of CovMat's pushforward Σ ↦ JΣJᵀ, and this covariant/contravariant law pair is exactly the bug surface the frame label exists to check: applying the covariance law to a precision matrix (or vice versa) produces a plausible-looking, silently wrong matrix. With labeled frames, propagate through a framed map dispatches to the correct law per type and records the destination frame on the result.\n\nFrame parameter (0.9)\n\nThe trailing F <: Frame{N} names the frame the matrix's coordinates live in (e.g. ImageFrame{:left} for a per-detection pixel precision). Every historically-shaped constructor defaults it to UnlabeledFrame{N} — the gradual-typing wildcard, so pre-0.9 code compiles unchanged and unlabeled matrices propagate uncheckedly. Label with relabel; propagate through a framed map checks the frame against the map's Src and stamps the result with Dst.\n\nPrecision vs. covariance\n\nThis is distinct from Uncertain's model-side covariance role, which stores parameter uncertainty in covariance form because that case is dominated by Jacobian propagation (J Σ Jᵀ). Per-observation uncertainty on the residual side is dominated by Mahalanobis-style weighting (rᵀΛr), so storing Λ directly avoids inversions on the scoring hot path.\n\nExample\n\nΛ = PrecMat{2,Float32}(1.0f0, 0.0f0, 0.0f0, 1.0f0)   # identity precision\nr = SVector(0.3f0, -0.1f0)\nmaha_sq = dot(r, Λ * r)                              # Mahalanobis²\n\nSee also: CovMat, Uncertain, propagate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PrecMat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaledIdentity","page":"Core Types","text":"ScaledIdentity{N}(λ)\n\nThe N × N matrix λ·I, stored as the one number λ: the static, sized counterpart of LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling (which has no size), beside StaticArrays' SDiagonal (one number per axis) and SMatrix (every entry). Isotropic: a rotation leaves it unchanged. Isbits; reads as its full matrix. It stays a ScaledIdentity under inv, a scalar factor and a sum with another; a sum with an SDiagonal is an SDiagonal; every other operation gives an SMatrix. The isotropic storage of CovMat / PrecMat and the round GaussianBlur.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaledIdentity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoredCorrespondence","page":"Core Types","text":"ScoredCorrespondence{S,T} = Attributed{Correspondence{S,T}, Float64}\n\nA correspondence with an associated match score (e.g., SNN ratio).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScoredCorrespondence"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Uncertain","page":"Core Types","text":"Uncertain{V, M} = Attributed{V, M}   (M <: Union{CovMat, PrecMat})\n\nA geometric value V with its 2nd-order uncertainty: an Attributed whose attributes are one of the typed matrix wrappers —\n\nCovMat{N,T,M} — covariance Σ in parameter space\nPrecMat{N,T,M} — precision Λ = Σ⁻¹ in parameter/residual space\n\nUncertain is an alias, not a second struct: one value-with-attributes type carries every value-plus-metadata pattern, and an uncertain value is the one whose attributes are a statistical form. It is constructed ONLY from a CovMat or a PrecMat — a bare matrix or a scalar σ does not say which form is stored, so Uncertain(v, m) with any other m is a MethodError; wrap it as what it is, CovMat(Σ) or PrecMat(Λ). Everything Attributed does it does (u.value, u.attributes, centroid, StructArrays decomposition, index and length forwarding to a vector value), and everything written against Uncertain — cov, prec, geom, propagate, Ellipse — dispatches on exactly these attributes. u.matrix reads the form (getfield(u, :attributes) is the raw field).\n\nThe matrix's nominal type tells consumers which form is stored; accessors cov and prec return whichever form the caller asks for, inverting on demand. Each consumer constructs Uncertain with the form it expects to be queried most so the inverse is paid at most once at the boundary, never in the inner loop.\n\nTwo roles, one wrapper\n\nObservation-side: Uncertain{Point2, PrecMat{2,T,SMatrix{2,2,T,4}}} — a feature detection (e.g. RoMa-v2 dense match) carrying its per-observation precision. Consumed by heteroscedastic Sampson, weighted DLT, IRLS.\nModel-side: Uncertain{HomographyMat{T}, CovMat{8,T,SMatrix{8,8,T,64}}} — a fitted model carrying its parameter covariance. Consumed by predictive scoring and downstream propagation.\n\nType Parameters\n\nV: The wrapped geometric value (Point2, HomographyMat, Disc, …)\nM: The matrix wrapper (CovMat{N,T,M} or PrecMat{N,T,M})\n\nExample\n\n# Covariance-side (e.g. propagated parameter uncertainty)\np = Uncertain(Point2(100.0, 200.0), CovMat{2,Float64}(0.1, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2))\ncov(p)        # CovMat — stored form, no inversion\nprec(p)       # PrecMat — computed via inv(matrix)\n\n# Precision-side (e.g. RoMa per-detection PrecMat)\nΛ = PrecMat{2,Float32}(2.0f0, 0.1f0, 0.1f0, 3.0f0)\nd = Uncertain(Point2(123.4, 56.7), Λ)\nprec(d)       # PrecMat — stored form, no inversion\ncov(d)        # CovMat — computed via inv(matrix)\nd isa Attributed   # true: the same type as Attributed(Point2(123.4, 56.7), Λ)\n\n# A bare matrix states nothing: wrap it as the form it is\np2 = Uncertain(Point2(100.0, 200.0), CovMat(@SMatrix [0.1 0.0; 0.0 0.2]))\n# An isotropic standard deviation σ: the keyword constructor of the form\np3 = Uncertain(Point2(100.0, 200.0), CovMat{2}(σ = 0.5))   # Σ = 0.25·I\nUncertain(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 0.5)   # MethodError: neither a CovMat nor a PrecMat\n\nSee also: CovMat, PrecMat, cov, prec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Uncertain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.classify_conic-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SymmetricConic}","page":"Core Types","text":"classify_conic(Q::Union{Conic, HomEllipseMat, HomCircleMat}) -> ConicTrait\n\nClassify a conic matrix and return the appropriate Holy Trait.\n\nReturns one of:\n\nEllipseTrait(): Valid ellipse (bounded, non-degenerate)\nHyperbolaTrait(): Hyperbola (indefinite quadratic form)\nParabolaTrait(): Parabola (singular quadratic form)\nDegenerateTrait(): a point ellipse, an imaginary ellipse, or a conic with no quadratic part (a line). Intersecting line pairs classify as HyperbolaTrait and parallel pairs as ParabolaTrait (their quadratic forms).\n\nInvariant to the conic's scale and sign (a homogeneous conic is defined only up to a nonzero multiplier). A point ellipse is told from a real one by γ, the constant term at the centre, against the rounding of the terms that produce it (|c| + 2|b||centre| + |λ|·|centre|²) magnified by the quadratic block's condition number κ — never against an absolute threshold, which called large or distant ellipses degenerate. The bound was measured: on 20,000 elongated point ellipses (κ up to 1e6) the computed |γ| stays below 0.55·κ·eps of those terms, and the test allows 2·(1 + κ)·eps. The test runs in at least Float64 on the coefficients divided by the largest, so a Float32 conic is classified as its stored coefficients define it (a 3 px Float32 ellipse at (1000, 800) is an ellipse; in Float32 arithmetic its γ sank below the noise floor), and neither a huge nor a tiny multiple of a conic overflows or underflows. An ellipse whose γ is within that noise — thin and very far along its minor axis — reads degenerate, because the arithmetic cannot tell it from a point. The checked HomEllipseMat constructor runs this same test, and Ellipse(Q) reads the centre and γ in the same precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.classify_conic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.correspondences-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{AbstractVector{S}, AbstractVector{T}}} where {S, T}","page":"Core Types","text":"correspondences(sources, targets) -> StructArray{Correspondence{S,T}}\n\nConstruct a columnar correspondence array from parallel source and target vectors.\n\nExamples\n\nsrc = [SA[1.0, 2.0], SA[3.0, 4.0]]\ndst = [SA[5.0, 6.0], SA[7.0, 8.0]]\ncs = correspondences(src, dst)\ncs.first   # sources\ncs.second  # targets\ncs[1]      # first correspondence as Pair\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.correspondences"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.geom-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core Types","text":"geom(x)\n\nThe geometric value of x: the wrapped value of an Uncertain (its covariance dropped), x itself otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.geom"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_valid_solution-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N, M}, T, 2} where {N, M, T}}","page":"Core Types","text":"is_valid_solution(x) -> Bool\n\nPredicate that decides whether a single solver output should be retained when packing into FixedModels.  Defaults rule out non-finite results (PoseLib emits NaN/Inf for numerically degenerate samples).\n\nAdd a method for any new model type that needs a stricter check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_valid_solution"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.prec-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core Types","text":"prec(u::Uncertain) -> PrecMat\nprec(x) -> LinearAlgebra.I\n\nThe precision (information) form of an observation's uncertainty: returns the stored PrecMat directly, or inv of a stored CovMat (paying the inversion once at the boundary). The fallback for plain values is the identity LinearAlgebra.I — an unweighted observation — so call sites that mix plain points and Uncertain inputs need no special case for the no-weighting branch. Counterpart of cov (the Statistics.cov extension below); see Uncertain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.prec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.value_of-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core Types","text":"value_of(x)\n\nThe value a datum carries: the wrapped value of an Attributed, and a bare value itself. The twin of weight_of, for a stage that takes either.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.value_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weight_of-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core Types","text":"weight_of(x) -> Float64\n\nThe weight a stage reads off a datum: the weight attribute of an Attributed value (Attributed(R, (; weight))), as Float64, and 1.0 for an Attributed without one or a bare value. Weights travel with their data this way, never as a parallel vector (data-arguments.md).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weight_of"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera","page":"Core Types","text":"AbstractCamera\n\nThe one camera type: every camera — an unposed model (CameraModel, a NoncentralSplineModel), a posed camera (PosedCamera) and a bounded one (BoundedCamera) — subtypes it. Whether a camera carries a pose is a trait, is_posed (shared with the targets), not a second type root.\n\nEvery camera implements, in the COLMAP pixel frame:\n\nproject(camera, X) — a 3D point (camera frame for a model, world frame for a posed camera) to its pixel\nbackproject(camera, u) — a pixel to its ray\n\nA posed camera (is_posed(camera) == true) also implements pose(camera) and extrinsics(camera); on an unposed one they are an ArgumentError.\n\nExample\n\n# Code that works with any camera type:\nfunction process(cam::AbstractCamera, points::Vector{Point3})\n    pixels = [project(cam, p) for p in points]\n    # ...\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject","page":"Core Types","text":"backproject(camera_or_model, pixel::StaticVector{2}) -> SVector{3}\n\nBackproject a 2D pixel coordinate to a 3D ray direction. The return is a 1-parameter family of 3D points (depth unknown) represented as a direction vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extent","page":"Core Types","text":"extent(camera) -> Size2{Int}\n\nReturn the pixel-space image extent (width × height) of a camera.\n\nTrait-dispatched via bounds_trait(camera): errors at compile time for cameras that don't carry bounds (default NoBounds()).  Cameras that do carry bounds (e.g. BoundedCamera) opt in via bounds_trait(::Type{...}) = HasBounds().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics","page":"Core Types","text":"extrinsics(camera::AbstractCamera) -> EuclideanMap\n\nReturn the extrinsics (world-to-camera transform) of a camera.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.feature_frame","page":"Core Types","text":"feature_frame(d) -> FeatureFrame\n\nReturn the feature frame (the detected local frame: Disc, EllipticFrame, …) of a detection or a bare FeatureFrame. Named for what it returns, so it cannot be read as \"which coordinate frame\".\n\nUnified accessor: works on both FeatureFrame (returns itself) and any AbstractDetection (returns the wrapped frame; for a detection of an Uncertain frame, the bare frame without its covariance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.feature_frame"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length","page":"Core Types","text":"focal_length(camera_or_model)\n\nReturn the focal length of a camera or camera model.\n\nFor physical cameras, returns a length with units (e.g., mm). For logical cameras, returns focal length in pixels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.intrinsics","page":"Core Types","text":"intrinsics(camera_or_model)\n\nReturn the intrinsics block (e.g. LogicalIntrinsics, PhysicalIntrinsics).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.intrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_posed-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera}","page":"Core Types","text":"is_posed(camera::AbstractCamera) -> Bool\nis_posed(target::AbstractTarget) -> Bool\n\nWhether a rig member kind carries a pose — the one placement trait of both kinds. For a camera: true for a PosedCamera (a model and its extrinsics), false for an unposed model (CameraModel, NoncentralSplineModel), and the wrapped camera's answer for a BoundedCamera. For a target: true for a PosedTarget, false for an unposed one (PlanarTarget, PointTarget). It is decided by the type, so a branch on it compiles away.\n\nThe posed-only verbs — pose, extrinsics, rig membership, the StereoRig constructors that read poses — refuse an unposed camera with an ArgumentError, as pose and extrinsics refuse an unposed target (an unposed member of either kind is its own model), and PosedCamera(model, X) and PosedTarget(target, X) refuse a member that is already posed. A camera or target type of another package that carries a pose adds a method returning true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_posed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lens","page":"Core Types","text":"lens(camera_or_model)\n\nReturn the lens model (e.g. Pinhole, a Lens, or any user-defined <: AbstractLensModel).  Use this for dispatch in downstream packages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lens"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose","page":"Core Types","text":"pose(camera::AbstractCamera) -> EuclideanMap\n\nReturn the pose (position and orientation) of a camera in world coordinates.\n\nThe pose is the inverse of the extrinsics: it transforms from camera frame to world frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer","page":"Core Types","text":"transfer(operator, observation)\n\nTransfer an observation from one view to another via a known multi-view geometry operator (e.g. a StereoRig, a homography, or an explicit (src_model, extrinsics, tgt_model) triple).\n\nThe operator comes first, as in the multi-view analogs project / unproject. Concrete dispatches live with the operator they consume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unproject","page":"Core Types","text":"unproject(camera_or_model, pixel, depth) -> SVector{3}\n\nUnproject a 2D pixel coordinate at the given depth to a 3D point. Unlike backproject (which returns a ray direction), unproject collapses the ray to a specific point using the supplied depth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unproject"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.area","page":"Core Types","text":"area(primitive)\n\nReturn the area of a 2D geometric primitive.\n\nAccessor convention (single argument).\n\nMethods:\n\narea(::Ellipse) → T (π * a * b)\narea(::Circle) → T (π * r²)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.area"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.aspect_ratio","page":"Core Types","text":"aspect_ratio(g)\n\nReturn the aspect ratio (a/b) of an ellipse-like shape.\n\nFor shape-matrix-based types (MvNormal, EllipticFrame): sqrt(λmax / λmin). For geometric types (Ellipse): a/b (semi-major / semi-minor).\n\nA value of 1.0 indicates isotropy (circular shape).\n\nMethods:\n\naspect_ratio(::Ellipse) → T\naspect_ratio(::Circle) → T (always 1.0)\naspect_ratio(::EllipticFrame) → T\naspect_ratio(::AffineFrame) → T\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.aspect_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.boundary","page":"Core Types","text":"boundary(center, Σ, n_sigma=1.0)\n\nConstruct an n-sigma confidence ellipse from a 2×2 covariance matrix. For the detection → primitive gate on feature frames, use region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.boundary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.boundary_point","page":"Core Types","text":"boundary_point(primitive, θ)\n\nReturn a point on the boundary of a primitive at parameter θ.\n\nRequires has_boundary(primitive) == HasBoundary().\n\nMethods:\n\nboundary_point(::Ellipse, θ) → Point2\nboundary_point(::Rect, θ) → Point2 (constant arc length around the perimeter)\nboundary_point(::Ellipse3D, θ) → Point3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.boundary_point"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.boundary_points","page":"Core Types","text":"boundary_points(primitive, n=64)\n\nReturn n points sampled uniformly around the boundary of a primitive.\n\nUnlike GeometryBasics.coordinates (which returns mesh/face vertices for rendering filled shapes), boundary_points samples the outline at equispaced angles θ ∈ [0, 2π). Returns n distinct points (no closing point duplication).\n\nRequires has_boundary(primitive) == HasBoundary() and a corresponding boundary_point(primitive, θ) method.\n\nExample\n\npts = boundary_points(ellipse, 32)  # 32 outline points\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.boundary_points"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.centroid","page":"Core Types","text":"centroid(primitive)\n\nReturn the centroid (geometric center) of a primitive.\n\nNote: Use centroid for the geometric center/midpoint. This is distinct from GeometryBasics.origin which returns the corner/origin of a primitive.\n\nMethods:\n\ncentroid(::Ellipse) → Point2{T}\ncentroid(::Circle) → Point2{T}\ncentroid(::Disc) → Point2{T}\ncentroid(::Ellipse3D) → Point3{T}\ncentroid(::Rect) → Point{N,T} (midpoint, not origin)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.centroid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.characteristic_scale","page":"Core Types","text":"characteristic_scale(g)\n\nReturn the equivalent isotropic scale of a Gaussian: σ = det(Σ)^(1/4).\n\nThis is the geometric mean of the standard deviations along the principal axes.\n\nMethods:\n\ncharacteristic_scale(::EllipticFrame) → T\ncharacteristic_scale(::AffineFrame) → T\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.characteristic_scale"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.closest_point","page":"Core Types","text":"closest_point(geometry, p)\n\nReturn the point on geometry closest to p.\n\nMethods:\n\nclosest_point(hyperplane, point): Orthogonal projection onto hyperplane\n\nSee also: signed_distance (for a hyperplane, p - closest_point(h, p) = signed_distance(h, p) · normal(h)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.closest_point"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.coverage","page":"Core Types","text":"coverage(subject, reference)\n\nFraction of subject's area covered by reference: the measured shape first, the covering shape as its parameter.\n\nDerived from overlap_area: coverage = overlap_area(subject, reference) / area(subject).\n\nReturns a value in [0, 1] where:\n\n0 indicates no overlap\n1 indicates subject is fully covered by reference\n\nExample\n\nsmall = Ellipse(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 1.0, 0.5, 0.0)\nlarge = Ellipse(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 3.0, 2.0, 0.0)\ncoverage(small, large)  # 1.0 (small is inside large)\ncoverage(large, small)  # ~0.08 (only small portion of large is covered)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.coverage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dilate","page":"Core Types","text":"dilate(primitive, factor)\n\nDilate (scale) a geometric primitive by a factor.\n\nMethods:\n\ndilate(::Circle, factor) → Circle with scaled radius\ndilate(::Ellipse, factor) → Ellipse with scaled semi-axes\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dilate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.distance","page":"Core Types","text":"distance(geometry, p)\n\nDistance from the point p to geometry, non-negative. A signed distance is signed_distance, never this name.\n\nMethods: none in VisualGeometryToolkitCore. A hyperplane's distance is signed: signed_distance(hyperplane, point) (abs of it for the unsigned value).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.geom_rank-Tuple{Type}","page":"Core Types","text":"geom_rank(::Type) -> Int\n\nCanonical ordering rank of a geometric primitive type, used to symmetrize binary predicates (intersects, overlap_area, iou): when a pair arrives in non-canonical order (geom_rank(T1) > geom_rank(T2)), the arguments are swapped and re-dispatched, so each unordered pair needs only one method.\n\nUnranked types return typemax(Int); a pair of unranked types is never swapped and falls through to a MethodError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.geom_rank"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.intersects","page":"Core Types","text":"intersects(a, b)\n\nTest whether two geometric primitives intersect.\n\nMethods:\n\nintersects(::Rect, ::Rect) → Bool\nintersects(::Circle, ::Rect) → Bool\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.intersects"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.iou","page":"Core Types","text":"iou(a, b)\n\nCompute Intersection over Union between two geometric primitives.\n\nSymmetric convention (order doesn't matter).\n\nDerived from overlap_area and area: iou = overlap / (area(a) + area(b) - overlap). Returns a value in [0, 1] where 1 indicates identical shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.iou"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.normal","page":"Core Types","text":"normal(primitive)\n\nReturn the normal vector of a geometric primitive.\n\nMethods:\n\nnormal(::Hyperplane) → unit normal vector\nnormal(::Ellipse3D) → unit normal of the ellipse's plane\nnormal(s, x) → unit normal of the surface s at its point x (Quad, MappedSurface)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.normal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.offset","page":"Core Types","text":"offset(hyperplane)\n\nReturn the signed distance from the origin to the hyperplane.\n\nFor a hyperplane with coefficients [n₁, n₂, ..., nₘ, d]:\n\noffset(h) = -d (signed distance along normal)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.offset"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.overlap_area","page":"Core Types","text":"overlap_area(a, b)\n\nCompute the area of intersection between two geometric primitives.\n\nSymmetric convention (order doesn't matter): arguments are put into canonical order via geom_rank before dispatch.\n\nReturns the area in squared units of the input coordinates. Returns 0.0 if the shapes do not intersect.\n\nAnalytic methods (exact):\n\noverlap_area(::Ellipse, ::Ellipse) → Float64 (GTE port)\noverlap_area(::Circle, ::Circle) → Float64 (lens formula)\noverlap_area(::Circle, ::Ellipse) → Float64 (via GTE)\noverlap_area(::Ellipse, ::Rect) → Float64 (circle-polygon)\noverlap_area(::Circle, ::Rect) → Float64 (circle-polygon)\noverlap_area(::Circle/::Ellipse, ::Polygon) → Float64 (convex polygon)\noverlap_area(::Rect, ::Rect) → Float64\n\nPairs without an analytic method fall back to the BoundarySampled overlap method (deterministic boundary polygonization + convex clipping); see overlap_method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.overlap_area"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.overlap_method","page":"Core Types","text":"overlap_method(a, b) -> OverlapMethod\n\nReport how overlap_area computes the intersection area for this pair of primitives: AnalyticOverlap() for pairs with an exact closed-form method, BoundarySampled() for the deterministic polygonization fallback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.overlap_method"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project","page":"Core Types","text":"project(operator, geometry)\n\nProject geometry via an operator.\n\nMethods:\n\nproject(camera, point3d): Camera projection (3D → 2D)\n\nSee also: closest_point(hyperplane, p), the orthogonal projection onto a hyperplane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.region","page":"Core Types","text":"region(frame, k=1.0)\n\nTHE detection → primitive gate: the image region supported by a feature frame at the k·σ contour, returned as a plain UNITLESS primitive (units are stripped here, once, instead of ad-hoc ustrip calls in algorithms).\n\nArguments\n\nframe: A FeatureFrame (Disc, OrientedDisc, EllipticFrame, AffineFrame)\nk::Real=1.0: Sigma multiplier (1.0 = 1σ contour)\n\nReturns\n\nCircle for Disc and OrientedDisc\nEllipse for EllipticFrame and AffineFrame\n\nMethods:\n\nregion(::Disc, k) → Circle\nregion(::EllipticFrame, k) → Ellipse\nregion(::AffineFrame, k) → Ellipse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.region"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale","page":"Core Types","text":"scale(frame)\n\nReturn the characteristic scale of a feature frame.\n\nFor isotropic frames (Disc, OrientedDisc), this is the σ parameter. For anisotropic frames, use characteristic_scale instead.\n\nMethods:\n\nscale(::Disc) → T\nscale(::OrientedDisc) → T\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.shape_factor","page":"Core Types","text":"shape_factor(g)\n\nThe factor F of an elliptical region's matrix M = F·Fᵀ (shape_matrix): the linear part of the affine map that takes the unit disc onto the region, {c + F u : ‖u‖ ≤ 1}.\n\nFor EllipticFrame, the symmetric factor S (M = S²); for AffineFrame, the general factor A (M = A·Aᵀ, A keeping the in-plane rotation that M loses); for Ellipse, R(θ)·diag(a, b).\n\nMethods:\n\nshape_factor(::EllipticFrame) → SMatrix{2,2}\nshape_factor(::AffineFrame) → SMatrix{2,2}\nshape_factor(::Ellipse) → SMatrix{2,2}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.shape_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.shape_matrix","page":"Core Types","text":"shape_matrix(g)\n\nThe shape matrix M of an elliptical region {x : (x-c)ᵀ M⁻¹ (x-c) ≤ 1} (the ellipsoidal-calculus E(c, M)), symmetric positive-definite: M = F·Fᵀ with F its shape_factor (S² for an EllipticFrame, A·Aᵀ for an AffineFrame).\n\nIt is the region's shape, not its second moment: the uniform density on the region has covariance M/4, and in affine feature detection the \"second-moment matrix\" is the structure tensor μ ≈ M⁻¹. A frame built from a Gaussian N(c, Σ) takes its 1σ contour, M = Σ (EllipticFrame of an MvNormal, a Paraboloid3D's equivalent Gaussian).\n\nMethods:\n\nshape_matrix(::EllipticFrame) → SMatrix{2,2,T,4}\nshape_matrix(::AffineFrame) → SMatrix{2,2,T,4}\nshape_matrix(::Ellipse) → SMatrix{2,2,T,4}\nshape_matrix(::Paraboloid3D) → SMatrix{2,2,T,4}\nshape_matrix(::Disc), shape_matrix(::OrientedDisc) → σ² I, SMatrix{2,2,T,4}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.shape_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.signed_distance","page":"Core Types","text":"signed_distance(geometry, p)\n\nSigned distance from the point p to an oriented geometry: its sign says which side p is on.\n\nMethods:\n\nsigned_distance(hyperplane, point): n·p + d, positive on the normal's side\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.signed_distance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractSolverBackend","page":"Core Types","text":"AbstractSolverBackend\n\nHoly trait for selecting which implementation of a minimal solver to use.\n\nConcrete subtypes:\n\nNativeSolver(): Pure-Julia implementations (default, zero-allocation)\nPoseLibSolver(): PoseLib C++ library via ccall\n\nCarried as the backend field of the MinimalSolver verb layer (the public solve protocol) and dispatched on by the positional kernels:\n\nsolve(Homography4pt(), cs, idx)                # default (NativeSolver)\nsolve(Homography4pt(PoseLibSolver()), cs, idx) # PoseLib backend\np3p(PoseLibSolver(), rays, X)                  # kernel-level backend call\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractSolverBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnalyticOverlap","page":"Core Types","text":"Exact closed-form overlap computation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnalyticOverlap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundarySampled","page":"Core Types","text":"Deterministic boundary-polygonization fallback (64 samples per shape).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundarySampled"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundsTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"BoundsTrait\n\nTrait indicating whether a type carries pixel-space image bounds (width × height). Cameras parsed from SfM/MVS exports (COLMAP, ETH3D, ScanNet) typically wrap their intrinsics+lens+pose in a BoundedCamera so downstream geometry code can extent(cam), is_in_image(cam, p), and bound frustum visualizations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundsTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CircleTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating a circle (conic with equal semi-axes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CircleTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConicTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"ConicTrait\n\nTrait type for dispatch on conic sections.\n\nSubtypes (proper conics):\n\nCircleTrait: Circle (equal semi-axes, eccentricity = 0)\nEllipseTrait: Ellipse (eccentricity < 1)\nParabolaTrait: Parabola (eccentricity = 1, tangent to line at infinity)\nHyperbolaTrait: Hyperbola (eccentricity > 1, crosses line at infinity twice)\n\nSpecial:\n\nDegenerateTrait: Degenerate conic (point, line, or two lines)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConicTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DegenerateTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating a degenerate conic (point, line, or two lines).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DegenerateTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EllipseTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating an ellipse (conic with unequal semi-axes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EllipseTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrameScaleTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"FrameScaleTrait\n\nTrait distinguishing feature frames by their local shape, the DOF levels of the FeatureFrame interface (conventions: the FeatureFrame interface table):\n\nPointShape(): a point, no scale and no shape (PointFrame, 2 DOF)\nIsotropicScale(): Frames with a single scale σ (Disc, OrientedDisc)\nShapeFactorScale(): Frames with a 2×2 shape factor (EllipticFrame, AffineFrame)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FrameScaleTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HasBoundary","page":"Core Types","text":"HasBoundary()\n\nThe HasBoundaryTrait value of a shape whose parametric boundary can be sampled (a circle, an ellipse): overlap_area falls back to sampling it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HasBoundary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HasBoundaryTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"HasBoundaryTrait\n\nTrait indicating whether a type has a parametric boundary that can be sampled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HasBoundaryTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HasBounds","page":"Core Types","text":"HasBounds()\n\nThe BoundsTrait value of a type that carries its image extent in pixels (a BoundedCamera, a BoundedMap): extent and is_in_image apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HasBounds"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HyperbolaTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating a hyperbola (crosses line at infinity twice).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HyperbolaTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IsotropicScale","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating an isotropic frame with single scale σ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.IsotropicScale"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NativeSolver","page":"Core Types","text":"Pure-Julia solver implementation (default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NativeSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoBoundary","page":"Core Types","text":"NoBoundary()\n\nThe HasBoundaryTrait value of a type with no parametric boundary to sample; the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoBoundary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoBounds","page":"Core Types","text":"NoBounds()\n\nThe BoundsTrait value of a type with no image extent (a bare camera model); the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoBounds"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OverlapMethod","page":"Core Types","text":"OverlapMethod\n\nTrait describing how overlap_area computes the intersection area for a pair of primitives.\n\nAnalyticOverlap(): an exact closed-form method exists for the pair\nBoundarySampled(): deterministic fallback — both boundaries are polygonized at 64 samples and the convex polygons are clipped exactly\n\nQuery with overlap_method(a, b).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OverlapMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ParabolaTrait","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating a parabola (tangent to line at infinity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ParabolaTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PointShape","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating a point frame: a centre and no local shape (the scaled frames' verbs are absent).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PointShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PoseLibSolver","page":"Core Types","text":"PoseLib C++ solver via ccall.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PoseLibSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ShapeFactorScale","page":"Core Types","text":"Trait indicating a frame with a 2×2 shape factor (shape_factor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ShapeFactorScale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bounds_trait-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core Types","text":"bounds_trait(x) -> BoundsTrait\n\nReturn whether x carries pixel-space image bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bounds_trait"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.classify_conic","page":"Core Types","text":"classify_conic(Q) -> ConicTrait\n\nClassify a conic matrix and return the appropriate trait.\n\nReturns one of:\n\nCircleTrait(): Circle\nEllipseTrait(): Ellipse\nParabolaTrait(): Parabola\nHyperbolaTrait(): Hyperbola\nDegenerateTrait(): Degenerate conic (point, line, or two lines)\n\nImplementations are provided for HomEllipseMat and HomCircleMat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.classify_conic"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.frame_scale","page":"Core Types","text":"frame_scale(x) -> FrameScaleTrait\n\nReturn the frame scale trait for a feature frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.frame_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_boundary-Tuple{Any}","page":"Core Types","text":"has_boundary(x) -> HasBoundaryTrait\n\nReturn whether x has a boundary that can be sampled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_boundary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#Statistics.cov","page":"Core Types","text":"cov(u::Uncertain) -> CovMat\n\nThe covariance form of an observation's uncertainty: returns the stored CovMat directly, or inv of a stored PrecMat (paying the inversion once at the boundary). Extends Statistics.cov for Uncertain only; counterpart of prec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Statistics.cov"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FitResult","page":"Core Types","text":"FitResult{P, M}\n\nResult of a fit: estimate::P, its termination record — retcode::ReturnCode.T (why it stopped, the verdict: ReturnCode) and stop_rule::StopRule (which rule fired: StopRule) — iterations (0 for a closed-form solve), rms over n residuals, and solver-specific metadata::M (nothing for the lean default; see BundleMetadata).\n\nFitResult(estimate, code::ReturnCode.T, rule::StopRule, iterations, rms, n, metadata)\n\nEvery producer records the code and the rule; a loop that reports a legacy Symbol records retcode_from_symbol(retcode, rule)... (its success rule rule), the one map from a Symbol.\n\nFor a RANSAC the loop count iterations is its trials — the hypotheses drawn.\n\nRead a result through its accessors, which are the API: estimate, retcode, stop_rule, converged, iterations, rms, metadata, and the metadata's own (weights, dof, …). Field and property names are an implementation detail.\n\n(A property spelling still resolves, so older call sites keep working: the core fields, the derived converged, and metadata fields forwarded from metadata. A property that exists on none of them is an error; a metadata type with a field named like a core accessor (estimate, retcode, stop_rule, iterations, rms, n, metadata, converged) is rejected at construction.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FitResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BundleMetadata","page":"Core Types","text":"BundleMetadata(weights, residuals, fixed_frames, dof, loss_width, before, after; scale, scale_source)\n\nDiagnostics of a bundle refinement: the final per-observation weights its robust loss left and residuals (each observation's residual NORM, in the bundle's order — what an inlier classification of the refined model reads; a weight is the loss's guide to its optimizer, never inverted back into a residual), the gauge-fixing fixed_frames, the degrees of freedom dof, the robust loss's frozen loss_width (NaN under plain least squares — a LOSS WIDTH, not a noise scale), and before/after residual summaries as concrete (; rms, median, p90) NamedTuples — the FitResult's own rms mirrors after.rms first-class.\n\nThe bundle's scale record (the robust-estimation contract), required: scale, the noise scale it ran with (a LINEAR scale; NaN with ScaleNone, plain least squares), and scale_source (ScaleSource), read by noise_scale and scale_source. Its termination record is the FitResult's core (retcode, stop_rule): build the result as FitResult(estimate, code, rule, iterations, rms, n, metadata).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BundleMetadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.estimate","page":"Core Types","text":"estimate(r::FitResult) -> P\n\nThe fitted value — the model, camera, calibration record, … that the fit produced. The SciML sol.u.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.estimate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retcode","page":"Core Types","text":"retcode(r::FitResult) -> ReturnCode.T\n\nWhy the fit stopped, its ReturnCode — the verdict every producer records. Test success with converged (or successful_retcode), never by comparing the code to ReturnCode.Success; the rule that fired is stop_rule. The legacy Symbol spelling is retcode_symbol(r).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retcode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.converged","page":"Core Types","text":"converged(r::FitResult) -> Bool\n\nWhether the fit succeeded — STRICT: successful_retcode of its retcode, for every producer. A run that hit its iteration (or RANSAC trial) cap, its time cap or a stall did not converge, and a closed-form solve did. For a RANSAC, whether it FOUND a model is found_model. Also reachable as the property r.converged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.converged"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.iterations","page":"Core Types","text":"iterations(r::FitResult) -> Int\n\nIterations (or RANSAC trials) the fit ran; 0 for a closed-form solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.iterations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rms","page":"Core Types","text":"rms(r::FitResult) -> Float64\n\nRoot-mean-square residual over the n residuals the fit reports on (r.n) — for a robust fit, the accepted set, so the number is not outlier-dominated.\n\n\n\n\n\nrms(residuals::AbstractVector, n::Integer = length(residuals)) -> Float64\n\nThe root-mean-square residual √(Σ rᵢ² / n) a fit reports (its rms): over n points, so a point's stacked residual rows (two per pixel point) enter as its squared residual norm — rms(rows, n_points) — and scalar errors as themselves, rms(errors). The one place it is computed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rms"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.metadata","page":"Core Types","text":"metadata(r::FitResult{P, M}) -> M\n\nThe solver-specific diagnostics; nothing for a lean result. Its fields are also reachable directly on r as properties.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weights","page":"Core Types","text":"weights(r::FitResult) -> AbstractVector\n\nPer-datum weights in [0, 1] from the metadata (r.metadata.weights): a plain vector in the data's order (a lazy all-ones unit_weights for an L2 result). What they mean is the producer's weight_kind, and inlier_mask(r) cuts them by it. Errors on a metadata type that carries none — including the lean nothing. A contract generic: a metadata that stores its weights otherwise adds a method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dof","page":"Core Types","text":"dof(r::FitResult) -> Int\n\nResidual degrees of freedom from the metadata (r.metadata.dof). Errors on a metadata type that carries none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nested_metadata","page":"Core Types","text":"nested_metadata(::Type{M}) -> Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}\n\nThe fields of the metadata type M that are themselves metadata records whose fields a FitResult should forward too: for each listed field g, r.f reaches r.metadata.g.f for every field f of g's type, beside the outer fields. Default (). A method is added once per metadata type, next to its definition (nested_metadata(::Type{<:MyMeta}) = (:calibration,)).\n\nThe listed fields stay reachable under their own name. A nested field named like a core field of the result, an outer field or another nested field is refused when the result is built, exactly as an outer one is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nested_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.map_estimate","page":"Core Types","text":"map_estimate(f, r::FitResult) -> FitResult\n\nr with f applied to its estimate, everything else as it was (the termination record, iterations, rms, n, the metadata): the one way a stage hands on a result whose model it re-expresses (a unit attached, frame labels restored). A RANSAC result's estimate is Union{M, Nothing}: f maps a present model, an absent one stays nothing, and the result keeps the union over f's image of M (f needs no method for nothing). Any other result's estimate is concrete and simply mapped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.map_estimate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleSource","page":"Core Types","text":"ScaleSource\n\nWhere a robust result's scale came from — one of:\n\nScaleGiven: a number the caller gave (scale = σ);\nScaleUpstream: an earlier stage's result (scale = result, its noise_scale);\nScaleEstimated: estimated by this stage — a RANSAC scorer's consensus partition, an EM fit's E-step;\nScaleNone: no scale — a plain least-squares stage given none, or a stage given only a width (never backed out into a scale); the scale is NaN.\n\nAn @enum (isbits): a metadata that carries it stays concretely typed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleSource"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleGiven","page":"Core Types","text":"ScaleGiven: a ScaleSource — the scale is a number the caller gave (scale = σ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleGiven"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleUpstream","page":"Core Types","text":"ScaleUpstream: a ScaleSource — the scale is an earlier stage's noise_scale (scale = result).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleUpstream"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleEstimated","page":"Core Types","text":"ScaleEstimated: a ScaleSource — this stage estimated the scale (a scorer's consensus partition, an EM fit's E-step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleEstimated"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleNone","page":"Core Types","text":"ScaleNone: a ScaleSource — no scale was recorded (plain least squares, or a stage given only a width); the scale is NaN and noise_scale refuses it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleNone"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.noise_scale","page":"Core Types","text":"noise_scale(result) -> T\n\nThe scale a robust result recorded — the one it ran with, or the one it estimated (its metadata's scale; scale_source says which). The hand-off: a downstream stage takes it as scale = result. An ArgumentError for a result whose source is ScaleNone (a plain least-squares stage given no scale, or a stage given only a width), so its NaN is never handed on.\n\nA metadata type answers through its fields scale and scale_source; one that records the scale otherwise adds a method on its metadata type. A stage's parsed scale_input answers too: the scale it was given (refused for a width, whose source is ScaleNone).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.noise_scale"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_source","page":"Core Types","text":"scale_source(result) -> ScaleSource\n\nWhere a robust result's noise_scale came from (ScaleSource); of a stage's parsed scale_input, where its scale came from (ScaleNone for a width); of nothing (no input: a least-squares stage), ScaleNone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_source"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleInput","page":"Core Types","text":"ScaleInput\n\nThe scale input of one robust stage, parsed once by scale_input: scale (the noise scale σ, NaN when a width was given) or width (the loss width, NaN when a scale was given) — exactly one is finite, in the caller's float type — and the ScaleSource the stage records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleInput"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_input","page":"Core Types","text":"scale_input(scale, width) -> ScaleInput\n\nThe scale input of a robust estimator, from its two keywords — EXACTLY ONE of:\n\nscale = σ: a positive, finite noise scale (ScaleGiven);\nscale = result: an earlier robust result, read by noise_scale (ScaleUpstream; a result that recorded no scale is refused);\nwidth = w: a positive, finite loss width. The stage records ScaleNone (its scale is NaN) and the width itself: a width may be cross-validated rather than generative, so no scale is ever backed out of it (w / c), and a downstream stage needs its own scale or width.\n\nNeither, or both, is an ArgumentError naming both keywords. A stage with no robust loss (plain least squares) takes neither, and refuses one it would ignore — that check is the stage's, which knows its loss.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_input"},{"category":"section","location":"api/warping/#Warping-API","page":"Warping","text":"Coordinate and region maps (canonical and log-polar), the image_warp adapter and its KernelAbstractions resampler, and the BoundedMap decorator. See the Transforms guide.","title":"Warping API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/warping/#Coordinate-and-Region-Maps","page":"Warping","text":"","title":"Coordinate and Region Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/warping/#Image-Warping","page":"Warping","text":"Two bilinear lookups with two contracts: bilinear_sample reads a texture in its unit-square (half-texel) chart, extends its edges, returns several channels and differentiates in the position (a printed board's albedo under ForwardDiff); bilinear_sample_clamped reads 1-based array positions, rejects one off the image, and is exact at integers (a pixel scan). bilinear_weights is bilinear_sample's texel block and fractions for a caller that spreads a position itself, and is_inside_texel_centres says whether a position needs no edge extension (for a caller that drops it instead). image_sample! is image_warp!'s tap at points the caller holds (the projections of a set of directions), with its boundary.","title":"Image Warping"},{"category":"section","location":"api/warping/#Index","page":"Warping","text":"Pages = [\"warping.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap-Tuple{Circle}","page":"Warping","text":"AffineMap(circle::Circle) -> AffineMap\n\nAffineMap from canonical [-1,1]² to a circle's coordinate frame (isotropic scaling by radius + translation to centre).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap-Tuple{Ellipse}","page":"Warping","text":"AffineMap(ellipse::Ellipse) -> AffineMap\n\nAffineMap from canonical [-1,1]² to an ellipse's coordinate frame (rotation + anisotropic scaling, then translation to centre).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap-Tuple{HyperRectangle{2}}","page":"Warping","text":"AffineMap(rect::HyperRectangle{2}) -> AffineMap\n\nAffineMap from canonical [-1,1]² to a 2D rectangle's coordinate frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap-Union{Tuple{To}, Tuple{From}, Tuple{Type{PixelFrame{From}}, Type{PixelFrame{To}}}} where {From, To}","page":"Warping","text":"EuclideanMap(::Type{PixelFrame{From}}, ::Type{PixelFrame{To}}) -> EuclideanMap{2}\n\nOrigin-convention conversion between two pixel frames, as a LABELED rigid map: identity rotation, constant translation, Src = PixelFrame{From}, Dst = PixelFrame{To} (monogram X_{To,From}). Registered origins: :opencv, :colmap, :julia — see the table in the PixelFrame docstring. This constructor-as-factory on the frame TYPES is the only origin-conversion API (the 0.6-era ImageOrigin value surface is removed in 0.7).\n\nThe returned map is UNITLESS (Vec2{Float64} translation): origin conversion is a compute-side operation and compute runs on stripped values (units are enforced at construction and stripped at the region gate). Apply it to plain-Float64 points; strip united data first (ustrip.(px, ...)).\n\nFor identical frames the translation is zero (≡ identity). inv swaps the frames; composition against other labeled 2D maps is frame-checked, and the map fuses in image_warp chains like any EuclideanMap{2}.\n\nExamples\n\nT = EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia})\nT.t == Vec2(0.5, 0.5)              # true\npt_julia = T(pt_colmap)            # pt_colmap unitless\n\ninv(T) isa EuclideanMap{2, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, PixelFrame{:julia}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.canonical_map-Tuple{Union{Ellipse, Circle}, Integer}","page":"Warping","text":"canonical_map(geom, size::Integer; bound=1.0, align_corners=false) -> AffineMap\n\nSize-form convenience: builds the COLMAP-math patch-domain rect for a size × size patch internally, then delegates to canonical_map(geom, rect; bound).  See the rect-form for full details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.canonical_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.logpolar_map-Tuple{Union{Ellipse, Circle}, HyperRectangle{2}, Real}","page":"Warping","text":"logpolar_map(geometry::Union{Circle, Ellipse}, logpolar_rect::HyperRectangle{2}, inner_radius::Real) -> Function\n\nCreate a composed transformation that maps from log-polar patch coordinates to image coordinates via a circle or ellipse.\n\nThis is a convenience function that composes three transformations:\n\nlogpolar_rect → CANONICAL_SQUARE (normalize patch coordinates)\nCANONICAL_SQUARE → UNIT_CIRCLE via log-polar transform\nUNIT_CIRCLE → geometry (map to target circle/ellipse in image)\n\nThe resulting transformation can be used with warp to extract log-polar patches.\n\nArguments\n\ngeometry::Union{Circle, Ellipse}: Target geometry in image coordinates\nlogpolar_rect::HyperRectangle{2}: Rectangle defining the log-polar patch domain\ninner_radius::Real: Minimum radius for log-polar transform (avoids singularity at origin)\n\nReturns\n\nFunction: Composed transformation logpolar_rect → geometry\n\nBroadcasting\n\nThis function is designed to be broadcastable across multiple geometries:\n\n# Extract patches from multiple circles\ntransforms = logpolar_map.(circles, Ref(logpolar_rect), Ref(inner_radius))\n\nExample\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\nusing GeometryBasics: Circle, Point2\n\n# Define target circle and log-polar patch size\ncircle = Circle(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 50.0)\nix, iy = 1:512, 1:256  # x-axis (scale), y-axis (rotation)\nlogpolar_rect = Rect((ix..., iy...))\ninner_radius = 0.01\n\n# Create transformation and resample through the fused warp\ntransform = logpolar_map(circle, logpolar_rect, inner_radius)\nbm = image_warp(transform, Rect(0, 0, 512, 256))\npatch = Matrix{Float32}(undef, 256, 512)\nimage_warp!(patch, bm, image; fillvalue=0.5f0)\n\nSee Also\n\nAffineMap: canonical → primitive coordinate mapping (constructor)\nlogpolar_to_cartesian: Log-polar to Cartesian transformation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.logpolar_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.logpolar_map-Tuple{Union{Ellipse, Circle}, Integer, Real}","page":"Warping","text":"logpolar_map(geom, size::Integer, inner_radius; align_corners=false) -> ComposedFunction\n\nSize-form convenience: builds the COLMAP-math patch-domain rect for a size × size log-polar patch internally, then delegates to logpolar_map(geom, rect, inner_radius).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.logpolar_map"},{"category":"function","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.logpolar_to_cartesian","page":"Warping","text":"logpolar_to_cartesian(inner_radius=0.01) -> LogPolarToCartesian\n\nConstruct a LogPolarToCartesian transformation. Maps points in unit space [-1,1]² interpreted as (log_r, θ) to Cartesian (x, y) in the unit circle.\n\nCoordinate Convention\n\nx-axis (horizontal/columns) = scale (radius)\ny-axis (vertical/rows) = rotation (angle)\n\nMapping\n\nInput x-coordinate in [-1,1] → radius from inner_radius to 1.0 (logarithmically)\nInput y-coordinate in [-1,1] → angle from 0 to 2π\nOutput: Cartesian (x, y) in unit circle (radius ≤ 1.0)\n\nArguments\n\ninner_radius::Real: Minimum radius (default: 0.01, avoids singularity at origin)\n\nExample\n\ntransform = logpolar_to_cartesian()\ntransform(Point2(0.0, 0.0))   # mid-radius, π angle\ntransform(Point2(-1.0, -1.0)) # min radius, 0\ntransform(Point2(1.0, 1.0))   # max radius (1.0), 2π\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.logpolar_to_cartesian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap-Tuple{Line2D}","page":"Warping","text":"(am::AffineMap)(l::Line2D) -> Line2D\n\nTransform a 2D line through an affine map. Lines stay lines under affine transforms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#CoordinateTransformations.Translation-Tuple{Circle}","page":"Warping","text":"(t::Translation{<:StaticVector{2}})(c::Circle) -> Circle\n\nTranslate a circle by shifting its center. Returns a Circle (translation preserves shape).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.Translation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#CoordinateTransformations.Translation-Tuple{Ellipse}","page":"Warping","text":"(t::Translation{<:StaticVector{2}})(e::Ellipse) -> Ellipse\n\nTranslate an ellipse by shifting its center. Returns an Ellipse (translation preserves shape).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.Translation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#CoordinateTransformations.Translation-Tuple{Line2D}","page":"Warping","text":"(t::Translation{<:StaticVector{2}})(l::Line2D) -> Line2D\n\nTranslate a 2D line. For line ax + by + c = 0, translating by (tx, ty) gives: ax + by + (c - atx - bty) = 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CoordinateTransformations.Translation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap-Tuple{Circle}","page":"Warping","text":"(S::SimilarityMap{2})(x) -> same type as x\n(E::EuclideanMap{2})(x) -> same type as x\n\nThe similarity (or rigid) action on a Circle, an Ellipse, a Line2D or a feature frame (PointFrame, Disc, OrientedDisc, EllipticFrame, AffineFrame), exact in its class: see the class table above. A rotation or a uniform scaling LinearMap acts through its SimilarityMap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap-Tuple{HyperRectangle{2}}","page":"Warping","text":"(S::SimilarityMap{2})(r::Rect{2}) -> Rect{2, Float64}\n(S::SimilarityMap{2})(s::RegionIntersection) -> RegionIntersection\n\nThe similarity's action on a region: a Rect's image is the axis-aligned Rect of its mapped corners, defined when the rotation is exactly a multiple of 90° (its matrix entries are 0 and ±1: the scalings, translations and quarter turns of the pixel-frame maps, a Bayer plane's map among them). Its widths are s times the widths (swapped by a quarter turn), exactly. Any other rotation turns a rectangle off the axes, which a Rect cannot hold: an ArgumentError. An intersection maps part by part (S(a ∩ b) = S(a) ∩ S(b), each part by its own action, so p ∈ s ⟺ S(p) ∈ S(s) up to rounding), and with the Ellipse, Circle and WholePlane actions every region pixel_mask reads maps without testing its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.WholePlane}","page":"Warping","text":"(S::SimilarityMap{2})(w::WholePlane) -> WholePlane\n\nThe whole plane maps to itself under any similarity: the identity on the region a lens without an image circle images (valid_domain), beside the Circle and Ellipse actions, so a region is mapped without testing its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SimilarityMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer-Tuple{Any, FeatureFrame}","page":"Warping","text":"transfer(m, f::FeatureFrame) -> FeatureFrame\n\nA feature frame carried through ANY point map m of the plane (a framed or plain affine, PerspectiveMap() ∘ h of a homography, a camera's lens or plane_map stage — anything callable on a 2-vector): the centre moves by m. A PointFrame is a point, so that is all of it, exact for every map. A scaled frame's shape moves by the map's Jacobian at the centre, its first-order image: an AffineFrame(m(c), J·F) (F its shape_factor) — except through a similarity (SimilarityMap{2}, EuclideanMap{2}, a rotation or uniform-scaling LinearMap), which keeps every frame's class exactly, m(f). A camera is no map of the plane (it images 3-D points): transfer(camera, f) is an ArgumentError naming its plane stages. Plain-number frames; the Jacobian is ForwardDiff's, static and allocation-free. An uncertain frame moves with its covariance by propagate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FramedImage","page":"Warping","text":"FramedImage(data, rect)\n\nA resampled canvas TOGETHER with its placement: rect is the window of the source pixel frame (COLMAP convention — origin at the window's top-left corner, widths its footprint) that data covers; the sampling density is widths(rect) ./ size(data) (reversed: array is row-major). Named rect — not extent — deliberately: VGC's extent(camera) is a SIZE (Size2{Int}), while this is a POSITIONED window. This is the warp layer's currency at the return boundary — a bare matrix loses exactly the information every consumer then has to carry by hand (the ext + σ pair the rect form of undistort used to drop), and misplacing a rectified image by its offset or scale is the half-pixel bug class the frame conventions exist to kill.\n\nimshow(pos, ci::FramedImage) (Makie extension) is self-placing: it draws data over rect, so a rectified view lands in the same axes as the raw frame — principal point, footprint and all — with nothing supplied alongside.\n\nProduced by the rect form of undistort; construct directly for any canvas rendered over a known window of a pixel frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FramedImage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bilinear_sample-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Warping","text":"bilinear_sample(img::AbstractMatrix, uv::StaticVector{2}) -> value\nbilinear_sample(img::AbstractArray{T, 3}, Val(C), uv::StaticVector{2}) -> SVector{C}\n\nBilinear lookup of a texture at uv in its unit-square chart, the half-texel convention: img[i, j] (row i of H, column j of W) is the texel centred at uv = ((j − ½)/W, (i − ½)/H), so (0, 0) is the outer corner of img[1, 1] and (1, 1) that of img[H, W]. A position off the centres' rectangle EXTENDS the edge (clamp to edge, never a rejection), and a non-finite position reads the corner texel img[1, 1]. The image needs at least 2 × 2 texels (an ArgumentError otherwise).\n\nA matrix returns one value (any element type that scales and adds: numbers, SVectors); a 3-D array (H, W, C) returns its first C channels as an SVector{C} — RGB, or a signed distance and its orientation tensor. Allocation-free and generic: under ForwardDiff the texel is chosen on the position's value and the derivative flows through the weights, which are continuous across texels (the value is C⁰).\n\nThe other bilinear lookup, bilinear_sample_clamped, addresses 1-based array positions and rejects positions off the image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bilinear_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bilinear_sample_clamped-Union{Tuple{Ta}, Tuple{Type{Ta}, Any, Any, Any, Int32, Int32}} where Ta","page":"Warping","text":"bilinear_sample_clamped(::Type{Ta}, img, yf, xf, H::Int32, W::Int32) -> (in_bounds::Bool, value::Ta)\n\nBilinear sample of img at the 1-based array position (yf, xf) with the OTHER boundary policy from _bilinear_sample's fill: a position whose floor cell lies outside 1:H × 1:W is rejected ((false, 0)), and the +1 neighbour is clamped to the last row/column, so the border pixel is sampled exactly rather than blended with a fill value. Exact (no interpolation error) at integer positions, which is what keeps an integer-step scan bit-identical to a pixel loop. Device-safe (Int32 indices, no allocation); hoisted verbatim from BlobBoards' adaptation kernels (2026-09).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bilinear_sample_clamped"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bilinear_weights-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, Tuple{Integer, Integer}}","page":"Warping","text":"bilinear_weights(uv::StaticVector{2}, (W, H)) -> (i, j, fx, fy)\n\nThe texel block and blend fractions bilinear_sample reads at uv on a W × H texture (W columns, H rows) in its unit-square, half-texel chart: the 1-based column i and row j of the block's first texel (img[j, i]; its four texels are img[j, i], img[j, i + 1], img[j + 1, i], img[j + 1, i + 1]) and the fractions fx, fy ∈ [0, 1] along x and y, so the four weights are (1 − fx)(1 − fy), fx (1 − fy), (1 − fx) fy and fx fy. The same policy as the lookup: a position off the centres' rectangle extends the edge (its block clamped to the edge, its fraction to 0 or 1), a non-finite coordinate reads the corner, and the texture needs at least 2 × 2 texels (an ArgumentError otherwise; its message is a literal, so it compiles in a GPU kernel). The block is chosen on the position's value under every ForwardDiff Dual layer and the fractions carry the partials. For a caller that spreads one position over the four texels itself (a footprint's rows, a scatter) instead of reading values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bilinear_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fuse-Tuple{CoordinateTransformations.Transformation}","page":"Warping","text":"fuse(t) -> Transformation\n\nFlatten every run of matrix-representable links in a composition chain into a single link.  Translation/LinearMap/AffineMap/EuclideanMap/RCSwap runs merge into one AffineMap; runs containing a HomographyMap merge into one HomographyMap; an affine link immediately left of a PerspectiveMap is pulled through the division into the homography.  Nonlinear links (log-polar, lens models, …) are preserved in place.  Semantically a no-op; structurally the difference between five closure calls per pixel and one matrix multiply.\n\nfuse(bm::BoundedMap) fuses the wrapped map and keeps the rect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.fuse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp!-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, Tuple{Vararg{BoundedMap}}, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}}","page":"Warping","text":"image_warp!(dst::AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, warps::Tuple{Vararg{BoundedMap}}, src::AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}; fillvalue=0) -> dst\n\nA multi-plane image warped plane by plane, each plane through its own warp: plane p of dst is plane p of src sampled through warps[p] — the 2-D image_warp! of each plane, on the backend that owns dst. For an image whose planes are sampled at different points (a Bayer image's R, G and B), each plane's warp composes that plane's own frame map: image_warp(T, inv(plane_map_p) ∘ math, rect). One warp per plane (DimensionMismatch otherwise).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, BoundedMap, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Warping","text":"image_warp!(dst, bm::BoundedMap, src; fillvalue=0) -> dst\n\nResample src into dst through the warp bm (built by image_warp): for each (row, col) of dst, bilinearly sample src at bm((row, col)). Runs the shared KernelAbstractions kernel on the backend that owns dst — supply CuArrays for GPU execution (the warp math evaluates on-device: bm's device form, Adapt.adapt(backend, bm), which moves a link holding arrays — a spline lens's controls — beside dst, is isbits; the workgroup is the backend's).\n\ndst and src may differ in eltype: samples accumulate in float and store back through the destination eltype (UInt8 rounds saturating, colorants clamp to [0, 1]).  fillvalue is the out-of-bounds tap value, in the float accumulation domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Integer}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Warping","text":"image_warp([T=Float64], math, n::Integer) -> BoundedMap\n\nConvenience overload for a square n × n output array.  Equivalent to image_warp(T, math, Rect(0, 0, n, n)).  Typically paired with the size-form math builders (canonical_map(geom, n; …), logpolar_map(geom, n, r; …)) for the common case where the patch domain and output array share n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, CoordinateTransformations.Transformation, HyperRectangle{2}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Warping","text":"image_warp([T=Float64], math, output_rect::Rect{2}) -> BoundedMap\n\nWrap a math-space Transformation into a fused BoundedMap callable from Julia array indices.  Composes (left-to-right when reading a call sequence):\n\n_RC_SWAP ∘ _OUT_BRIDGE ∘ math ∘ _IN_BRIDGE ∘ _RC_SWAP\n\nthen flattens the chain with fuse.  Perspective division is injected automatically when math is projective (is_projective) and doesn't already divide.\n\nThe output BoundedMap consumes (row, col) indices into the OUTPUT array and returns (sr, sc) indices into the SOURCE array — both Julia 1-indexed. The internal bridge pair — the typed frame maps EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:julia}, PixelFrame{:colmap}) and its reverse — converts to/from VGC's package-wide COLMAP math convention.\n\nArguments\n\nT — optional leading element type of the fused warp math (the standard rand(Float32, …)-style convention).  image_warp(Float32, …) keeps the per-pixel math single-precision on GPU backends.\nmath — any CoordinateTransformations.Transformation.  Assumed COLMAP-math.\noutput_rect::Rect{2} — the output region in array coords (e.g., Rect(0, 0, width, height)).\n\nExample\n\nmath = canonical_map(ellipse, 64; bound=1.0)\nbm   = image_warp(math, 64)\npatch = Matrix{Float32}(undef, 64, 64)\nimage_warp!(patch, bm, img_f32; fillvalue=0.5f0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp_batch_to!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 3}, AbstractVector{<:BoundedMap}, AbstractMatrix}} where T","page":"Warping","text":"image_warp_batch_to!(out, warps, src; fillvalue=0) -> out\n\nSample src once per output pixel of every warp in warps, writing into the pre-allocated 3D stack out::AbstractArray{T, 3} of shape (P_r, P_c, N) where N = length(warps).  Each warps[k] is a BoundedMap produced by image_warp.  Same kernel and backend dispatch as image_warp! — supply CuArrays (including the warp vector) for GPU execution.\n\nExample (CPU)\n\nwarps = [image_warp(canonical_map(d, 64; bound=1.0), 64) for d in discs]\nout   = Array{Float32}(undef, 64, 64, length(warps))\nimage_warp_batch_to!(out, warps, src; fillvalue=Float32(0.5))\n\nExample (GPU)\n\nd_warps = CuArray(warps)\nd_src   = CuArray(src_f32)\nd_out   = CUDA.zeros(Float32, 64, 64, length(warps))\nimage_warp_batch_to!(d_out, d_warps, d_src; fillvalue=0.5f0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp_batch_to!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_inside_texel_centres-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, Tuple{Integer, Integer}}","page":"Warping","text":"is_inside_texel_centres(uv::StaticVector{2}, (W, H)) -> Bool\n\nWhether uv lies in the closed rectangle of the texel centres of a W × H texture in bilinear_sample's unit-square, half-texel chart ((½/W, ½/H) to (1 − ½/W, 1 − ½/H), edges and centres included): exactly the positions where bilinear_weights interpolates and does not extend the edge, so its four weights reproduce uv. false off it and for a non-finite coordinate. The same chart and the same check as bilinear_weights (at least 2 × 2 texels, an ArgumentError otherwise), read on the position's value under every ForwardDiff Dual layer. For a caller that drops a position rather than extending the texture (a footprint off the grid).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_inside_texel_centres"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_projective-Tuple{Any}","page":"Warping","text":"is_projective(t) -> Bool\n\ntrue when t contains a projective stage (a HomographyMap) anywhere in its composition tree — i.e. its output is only defined up to scale unless a perspective division is applied somewhere downstream.  Recurses through BOTH sides of Base.ComposedFunction and CT.ComposedTransformation (the predecessor needs_perspective_division only checked the inner side, and missed CT-typed compositions entirely).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.is_projective"},{"category":"method","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_sample!-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractMatrix, AbstractArray{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}}","page":"Warping","text":"image_sample!(dst, src::AbstractMatrix, points; fillvalue = 0) -> dst\n\nBilinearly sample src at points[i] into dst[i], for every index of points (an array of StaticVector{2} of any shape; dst of the same size): points are COLMAP coordinates in src's pixel frame — (x, y), the centre of src[i, j] at (j − ½, i − ½) — the frame every Core map speaks, and the one image_warp's math lands in. The tap and its boundary are image_warp!'s: a tap off the image reads fillvalue, so a point within half a pixel outside the centres' rectangle blends toward it and a point farther out, or a non-finite one (a map's \"not imaged\"), is fillvalue.\n\nRuns one KernelAbstractions kernel on the backend that owns dst: supply device arrays (dst, src and points on one device) for GPU execution. dst and src may differ in eltype, as in image_warp!; fillvalue is in the float accumulation domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_sample!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/warping/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundedMap","page":"Warping","text":"BoundedMap{M<:CT.Transformation, R<:Rect{2,Int}} <: CT.Transformation\n\nDecorator that pairs a CT.Transformation with an output Rect{2,Int}. The rect is the codomain extent — typically the destination region of a warp or the valid pixel area of a homography.\n\nFields\n\nmap::M  — the inner transform (the math)\nrect::R — the output region (decoration)\n\nTrait\n\nbounds_trait(::BoundedMap) = HasBounds(). extent(bm) returns bm.rect.\n\nExample\n\nH  = HomographyMap(K * [r1 r2 t])\nbm = BoundedMap(H, Rect(0, 0, 1920, 1080))\nextent(bm)               # Rect(0, 0, 1920, 1080)\nto_homogeneous(bm) === H.forward   # forwards through\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BoundedMap"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#Raw-images","page":"Raw images","text":"The sensor image of the stack lives here, in Core: the decoded mosaic, its colour phase, its photosite views, its linear values and the exact maps of every sample onto the sensor. Decoding a RAW file is VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' (load_raw_mosaic, LibRaw through LibRaw_jll), and so is the display demosaic (load_raw(path or mosaic; mode = :demosaic)). The names below are exported by Core alone (Features 0.8.0 no longer re-exports them): using VisualGeometryToolkitCore.\n\nJulia has no convention for raw (Bayer) images: JuliaImages reads none, and BayeredImages.jl (unregistered) wraps a plain matrix as a CFAImage <: AbstractMatrix with a periodic colour filter array. The stack keeps its own types as its one raw representation, and takes one idea from BayeredImages: the raw wrapper IS an AbstractMatrix, so an ordinary array or image function works on it unchanged. There is no dependency on BayeredImages. From ImageCore (a dependency of Core) it takes its conventions and, where the concept is the same, its functions: see ImageCore's conventions.","title":"Raw images"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#The-representation","page":"Raw images","text":"A decoded file is a RawMosaic{A} <: AbstractMatrix{UInt16}: the photosite matrix A (an Array, a CuArray, or a view of either), its colour pattern anchored at the full raw frame's origin, the visible area's origin in that frame, the black and white levels and the decoder's metadata.\n\nsize, getindex, IndexStyle and parent are the held array's.\nWhat an array function makes from it is a plain array of the held array's kind: similar, copy, a broadcast (m .- black), a reduction (sum, extrema). The colour pattern and the metadata stay with the mosaic, and nothing pretends a derived array still has them.\nA unit-range index m[rows, columns] (a copy) or view(m, rows, columns) (no copy) is a window: a RawMosaic of the same sensor whose window_origin is its offset into the visible area. Its colours are the parent's at those photosites, whatever the parity of its start (the phase shift BayeredImages makes with a circshift of the pattern is the offset here, added where the pattern is tiled). Any other index is a plain array.\nOn a device: adapt(CuArray, m) or adapt(backend, m) (a KernelAbstractions backend) moves the photosites and keeps the rest. A kernel receives parent(m) or the mosaic's channelview, never the wrapper (its metadata is not a device value).","title":"The representation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#The-photosites-channel-first:-channelview","page":"Raw images","text":"channelview(m) — a method of ImageCore's channelview for a mosaic — is the photosites channel-first, 4 × H/2 × W/2: each 2×2 Bayer cell is a pixel, and its four photosites, spatially interleaved, are its channels. Photosite (2i − 1 + di, 2j − 1 + dj) is v[di + 2dj + 1, i, j], the cell's photosites in column-major order, whose colours are bayer_channels(m) — (:R, :G1, :G2, :B) for an RGGB cell, (:G1, :B, :R, :G2) for GRBG.\n\nIt is built as ImageCore builds its views, with nothing copied: the photosite matrix reshaped H × W → 2 × H/2 × 2 × W/2, its dimensions permuted to 2 × 2 × H/2 × W/2 (a PermutedDimsArray), and reshaped to 4 × H/2 × W/2. A view of a device mosaic is a view of its device array: KernelAbstractions' get_backend is the device's, adapt(Array, v) moves it whole, and a broadcast over a whole mosaic's view runs on the device. A GPU kernel reads the photosites by the view's index rule instead, parent(m)[2i − 1 + di, 2j − 1 + dj], as the cell kernel of LinearRaw does: measured on a B200 (2848 × 4256, the cell kernel, benchmark/raw_channelview.jl),\n\nthe kernel reads whole mosaic window with an odd start\nthe view v[k, i, j] 0.197 ms, 509 GB/s does not compile (InvalidIRError)\nits PermutedDimsArray, parent(v)[di + 1, dj + 1, i, j] 0.073 ms, 1371 GB/s 0.177 ms, 563 GB/s\na packed 4 × h × w copy 0.070 ms, 1426 GB/s 0.072 ms, 1378 GB/s\nthe held matrix by the index rule 0.072 ms, 1391 GB/s 0.074 ms, 1355 GB/s\n\nBase's ReshapedArray indexing splits each index by multiplicative inverses, and over a nested wrapper (a window's SubArray inside) it does not infer in a CUDA kernel. Its inverse, photositeview, turns a 4 × h × w array of cells back into the 2h × 2w photosite matrix — of a mosaic's view, the held array itself.\n\nWhy the channels are in the cell's own order and not always R, G1, G2, B: reordering channels per phase is a gather, not a reshape or a permutation of dimensions, so it cannot be a view. The colour of each channel is one call away, bayer_channels(m), from the same phase rule as photosite_colors.\n\nEach channel is a lattice of its own, sampled at its photosite's position in the cell: its frame is BayerCellFrame{C} (C its colour) and bayer_plane_map(m, k) its isbits SimilarityMap onto the sensor (x ↦ 2x plus the photosite's offset from the cell centre and a window's offset), exact for every Bayer phase and either parity of the visible or the window origin. The photosites' linear values channel-first are channelview(linearview(m)) (next section): the one linearisation, the one the cell kernel applies.\n\nv = channelview(m)                      # 4 × H/2 × W/2, a view of parent(m)\ng1 = findfirst(==(:G1), bayer_channels(m))\nS = bayer_plane_map(m, g1)              # G₁'s lattice → the sensor's PixelFrame{:colmap}\nx = S(pixel_centre(CartesianIndex(i, j)))   # where G₁ of cell (i, j) sits on the sensor\ny = channelview(linearview(m))[g1, i, j]   # its linear value","title":"The photosites channel-first: channelview"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#Linear-values-on-the-raw-grid:-linearview","page":"Raw images","text":"linearview(m) is the mosaic's linear values on the raw grid, lazily, as ImageCore's normedview reads codes as numbers: element (i, j) is (raw − black[c]) / (white − black[c]) of photosite (i, j), c its colour (the black and white levels are the mosaic's fields m.black, per colour index, and m.white). It is the one public spelling of the linearisation. It composes with channelview: channelview(linearview(m)) is the linear values channel-first, and the green-cell image is the mean of its two G channels, bit for bit. A broadcast over it, and copy, run on the mosaic's device, and a kernel reads it as it is. photosite_colors(m) is lazy and isbits (the pattern and its phase, indexed by the one rule), so it fuses into a broadcast with a device mosaic's arrays and runs on the device. saturationview(m) is the linear view's Bool match: the saturation flags on the same grid, lazily, by the one rule raw ≥ white that LinearRaw's flags apply. A photosite mask is a plain broadcast over photosite_colors — the usable greens of a radiometric fit are ((col .== 0x01) .| (col .== 0x03)) .& .!saturationview(m) with col = photosite_colors(m) — so no mask helper exists.","title":"Linear values on the raw grid: linearview"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#Linear-values-on-the-cells","page":"Raw images","text":"A LinearRaw{Mode} (:green_cells, :bin2, :demosaic) stays a container of plain planes, L.image[row, column, plane], beside its saturation flags, geometry, gain and metadata, and is THE public path to the green-cell image: LinearRaw(m; mode = :green_cells) (its image[:, :, 1]), on the mosaic's device. Its cell images are the mosaic's linearview read channel-first: the green-cell value is the mean of the two green channels, times the gain, bit for bit (a test holds it), and :bin2's R and B planes are the R and B channels. The planes are not wrappers that each know their BayerCellFrame{C}, for three reasons:\n\nEvery caller reads the planes as one array: a warp reads all of them in one call (image_warp!(dst, warps, L.image), a tuple of one warp per plane), the decode stage writes L.image as one PNG, a detection image takes a view of one plane, and a quantile reads vec(L.image). A tuple of plane wrappers would split that array or make each caller reassemble it.\nA plane's frame is already one call away and needs nothing new: bayer_plane_map(L, c) and raw_frame_map(L) (the green plane, the cell grid).\nThe saturation flags, the gain and the green code spans belong to the cell, not to one plane; per-plane wrappers would hold copies of them or point back at the container.\n\nThe mosaic, by contrast, is one matrix, and the wrapper costs its callers nothing.","title":"Linear values on the cells"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#One-phase-rule","page":"Raw images","text":"Every colour, offset and map above comes from one rule in src/raw/raw_mosaic.jl: the colour of a photosite is the pattern tiled from the raw origin, shifted by a window's offset (photosite_colors); a cell's four photosites are taken in column-major order; and photosite k sits at its 0-based site's pixel centre from the cell centre, (dj − ½, di − ½) raw pixels. A lattice of samples at offset o from every cell centre maps onto the sensor by the cell grid's x ↦ 2x after its translation by o/2 cell pixels. channelview's channels, bayer_plane_map of a mosaic and of a LinearRaw, green_cell_sites and the cell kernel all read that rule; the R channel's map IS the :bin2 R plane's.","title":"One phase rule"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#Where-an-image's-sensor-pixel-coordinates-come-from","page":"Raw images","text":"ONE accessor: raw_coordinates(img, x), the point x of the image's pixel frame (COLMAP: pixel (row, column) centred at (column − ½, row − ½)) in the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, the frame every calibration of the raw is in. It is raw_frame_map(img)(x):\n\nimage raw_frame_map(img) raw_coordinates(img, x)\na RawMosaic as decoded the identity x\na window of a mosaic its offset x + (columns, rows) of window_origin\na LinearRaw cell image (:green_cells, :bin2) the cell grid's map 2x (then a window's offset)\na cell mode alone, no image: LinearRaw{:green_cells}, LinearRaw{:bin2} the cell grid's map (every whole-mosaic image's, bit for bit) 2x\na :demosaic image refused (ArgumentError) refused\na plain matrix no method no method: it states no frame\n\nA position on another plane of a :bin2 image is bayer_plane_map(L, c)(x), and on a channel of a mosaic's channelview it is bayer_plane_map(m, k)(x): the planes are sampled at different points of the cell, so each has its own frame, and a composition that joins two planes' frames is refused. A mask over a plane's pixels of a region on the sensor is pixel_mask(region, bayer_plane_map(m, k), w, h): each pixel's mapped centre tested in the region's own frame. A region moves between frames by the map itself (S(rect), S(a ∩ b)).","title":"Where an image's sensor-pixel coordinates come from"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#How-detectors-report-positions","page":"Raw images","text":"A detector works in the pixels of the image it is given and returns COLMAP pixel centres of that image. A caller that runs it on a raw image and writes positions as raw pixels maps them through the image first:\n\nL = load_raw(path; mode = :green_cells)        # VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\nxs = detect(L.image[:, :, 1])                  # any detector: COLMAP centres of the cell grid\nraw_xs = raw_coordinates.(Ref(L), xs)          # the sensor's pixels\n\nWriting the cell-grid positions as raw pixels unmapped puts them at half the scale; RadiometricCamera's chrome detection did exactly that with green-cell coordinates. A calibration matrix moves the same way: reframed_calibration(raw_frame_map(L), K_cells).","title":"How detectors report positions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#How-the-frame-survives-a-file-round-trip","page":"Raw images","text":"A file of a decoded image (a PNG of the cells) holds pixels and no frame, and a plain matrix read back from it has no raw_coordinates. The stage that writes it records the frame with it: raw_provenance(L) gives the decoder, the mode, the gain and the frame map as plain data (from, to, scale, translation), for the stage's record (RadiometricCamera's conversion.json). The stage that reads the file rebuilds the map from that record and maps every position it writes through it.","title":"How the frame survives a file round trip"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#raw-imagecore","page":"Raw images","text":"ImageCore is a dependency of Core, and the sensor image follows it where the concept is the same, by its own functions:\n\nImageCore here why\nchannelview, channel-first C × H × W a method, channelview(::RawMosaic), 4 × H/2 × W/2 the same concept: the channels of each pixel, first, as a view\nviews, not copies channelview(m), photositeview, windows by view the same rule\ncolorview (the inverse of channelview) photositeview, a name of its own colorview(C, A) makes colorants of channels; the inverse here makes the photosite matrix of cells, no colorant\nrawview not extended a mosaic IS its raw UInt16 codes: there is nothing to unwrap\nnormedview(N0f16, A) usable on parent(m) as ImageCore defines it it is code / 65535; the linear value subtracts each channel's black level and divides by its span, so it has its own view, linearview\n\nLinearRaw{:bin2}'s image stays H × W × 3 (planes last) in this release: moving it channel-first is a breaking change to a stored layout, left for a breaking release (Core's CHANGELOG, 0.9.2).","title":"ImageCore's conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/manifolds/#Model-Manifolds-and-Uncertainty-API","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"The minimal parameterizations the estimators refine on (the Manifold contract and its instances), first-order covariance transport and solver covariance, and the image-domain confidence regions that display it. A model's ambient_coordinates are what a manifold's default retract_jacobian differentiates; a model type defined outside this package adds its method. Split from the Robust Estimation API page. See the Robust Estimation and Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty guides.","title":"Model Manifolds and Uncertainty API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/manifolds/#Model-Manifolds","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"","title":"Model Manifolds"},{"category":"section","location":"api/manifolds/#Confidence-Regions","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"","title":"Confidence Regions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/manifolds/#Index","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"Pages = [\"manifolds.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Manifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"Manifold\n\nLocal coordinate system anchored at a model iterate. Contract:\n\ntangent_size(ch)::Int — manifold dimension D;\nmodel(ch) — the ambient model at the anchor (= retract(ch, 0));\nretract(ch, v) — ambient model at local coordinates v::SVector{D};\nrecenter(ch, v) — the same manifold family re-anchored at retract(ch, v), with ONE normalization policy: a rotation part re-projected onto SO(3) (reproject_so3), the homography chart's anchor its unit-Frobenius representative of the same model (a first-order retraction lets the scale drift); retract itself never normalizes. Its element type is promote_type of the manifold's and v's, as arithmetic's is: a step of the manifold's own float type returns the manifold's own type (what a solver stepping a manifold variable holds), and a step of ForwardDiff Duals returns a Dual-valued manifold, so automatic differentiation through recenter works (NLLSsolver's, through update). A step that does not widen the manifold's element type keeps its storage types exactly — a rotation's representation and each part's own element type — so the type a solver holds never changes under an update;\nretract_jacobian(ch, v = 0) — T(v) = ∂vec(retract(ch, v))/∂v (9 × D for 3×3 models): at the anchor by default, at any v for an optimizer that iterates in the manifold instead of re-anchoring. Named after the function it differentiates, as every <thing>_jacobian is: it is Ceres' Manifold::PlusJacobian and GTSAM's derivative of retract. The inverse form, if one is ever needed, is local_coordinates_jacobian (Ceres' MinusJacobian);\naction_jacobian(ch, x, v = 0) — ∂(retract(ch, v)(x))/∂v, the manifold's action on a point or ray differentiated (at the anchor by default, at any v for an optimizer that iterates in the manifold): generically the chain rule of the model's own transform_deriv_params through T, closed form where a manifold has one;\nlocal_coordinates(ch, target) — the inverse retraction (closed form for the rigid and lens manifolds, Gauss–Newton for the projective ones);\nambient_coordinates(model) — the model's ambient coordinate vector, which the default retract_jacobian (and so the default action_jacobian and transform_covariance) differentiates; provided for static arrays and tuples of them, and added by every other model a manifold retracts to;\ncovariance transport through the retraction, first order: transform_covariance(ch, EuclideanManifold(ch), Σv) to the ambient space and back, or to another chart of the same model (see header).\n\nNAMES, against the established libraries: retract and local_coordinates are GTSAM's retract / localCoordinates, which are Ceres' Manifold::Plus and Manifold::Minus. action_jacobian is the optional Jacobian GTSAM's transformFrom fills. The two constraint verbs have NO counterpart in either — Ceres and GTSAM both frame a problem as residuals of an explicit cost, while constraint_with_jacobians and constraint_jacobians are the IMPLICIT constraint g(θ, x) = 0 with Förstner's A = ∂g/∂θ and B = ∂g/∂x, which is what an implicit-function covariance and a Gauss-Helmert step need.\n\nA manifold is the MODEL's (minimal_manifold(model)), and the free/fixed choice a fitter makes is a manifold operation (restrict) — so a residual composed with a manifold is differentiated once, here, not per consumer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Manifold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.action_jacobian-Tuple{Manifold, Any}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"action_jacobian(ch::Manifold, x) -> SMatrix\naction_jacobian(ch::Manifold, x, v) -> SMatrix\n\nThe Jacobian of the manifold's ACTION on x — ∂(retract(ch, v)(x))/∂v at v = 0, or at the chart coordinates v — what every residual through a posed or parameterized model needs. Generic: the chain rule of the model's own parameter derivative (transform_deriv_params(retract(ch, v), x), ∂model(x)/∂ambient) through the retraction differential (retract_jacobian(ch, v), ∂ambient/∂v). A manifold with a closed form overrides it (RigidManifold6: [−4·skew(R·x)  I₃] at the anchor); every override is pinned against this default in the manifold tests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.action_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ambient_coordinates","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"ambient_coordinates(model) -> SVector\n\nThe model's AMBIENT coordinates: the vector its manifolds' default retract_jacobian differentiates (so the default action_jacobian and transform_covariance too), and the anchor of its ambient chart EuclideanManifold(ch). A static matrix is its column-major entries, a static vector itself, a tuple the concatenation of its parts'. Every model a manifold retracts to adds its method beside its manifold — a downstream model that is not a static array or a tuple of them adds one too, rather than overriding retract_jacobian.\n\nPublic, not exported; a contract generic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ambient_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.model-Tuple{Manifold}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"model(ch::Manifold)\n\nThe ambient model at the chart's anchor: retract(ch, zero_tangent(ch)). A chart that stores its anchor model overrides it with a read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.recenter","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"recenter(ch::Manifold, v) -> Manifold\n\nThe same manifold family re-anchored at retract(ch, v), with the family's one normalization policy (a rotation re-projected onto SO(3), a homography's unit Frobenius representative). Part of the Manifold contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.recenter"},{"category":"function","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.restrict","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"restrict(ch::Manifold, free::SVector{D, Bool}) -> SubsetManifold\nrestrict(ch::Manifold, idx::SVector{M, Int})\n\nThe manifold ch on the coordinates free marks (or the ascending, distinct indices idx): tangent_size is the number of free coordinates, and retract, recenter, retract_jacobian and action_jacobian act through the parent with the fixed coordinates at zero. restrict(ch, ()) — no coordinate — is the pinned model. See SubsetManifold; a lens manifold also restricts by coefficient name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.restrict"},{"category":"function","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retract","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"retract(ch::Manifold, v::StaticVector{D}) -> model\n\nThe ambient model at local coordinates v (v = 0 is the anchor, model(ch)); it never normalizes. GTSAM's retract, Ceres' Manifold::Plus; its inverse is local_coordinates and its differential retract_jacobian. Part of the Manifold contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retract"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retract_directions-Union{Tuple{Q}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{Manifold, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{D}, T, 1} where T}, Tuple{Manifold, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{D}, T, 1} where T, Val{Q}}} where {D, Q}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"retract_directions(ch::Manifold, v[, Val(Q)]) -> NTuple{Q, SMatrix{3, 3}}\n\nThe first Q (default: all) columns of retract_jacobian(ch, v) — ∂M/∂vq for a chart whose ambient model is a 3×3 matrix (its first nine ambient coordinates, column-major) — each reshaped to a 3×3 matrix: the dM tuple the row Jacobians take (sampson_jacobian_rows!, whitened_jacobian_rows!, radial_transfer_jacobian_rows!). For a radial homography's [vec H; k] chart, Val(8) gives the eight sl(3) directions of H. In the Jacobian's element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retract_directions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retract_jacobian-Tuple{Manifold}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"retract_jacobian(ch::Manifold) -> SMatrix\nretract_jacobian(ch::Manifold, v::AbstractVector) -> SMatrix\n\nThe RETRACTION DIFFERENTIAL: ∂vec(retract(ch, v))/∂v, the ambient model's derivative with respect to the chart coordinates — at the anchor (v = 0) in the one-argument form, at any v for an optimizer that iterates in the manifold instead of re-anchoring.\n\nNamed after the function it differentiates, as every <thing>_jacobian is. Against the established libraries it is Ceres' Manifold::PlusJacobian and GTSAM's derivative of retract; the inverse form, if one is ever needed, is local_coordinates_jacobian (Ceres' MinusJacobian).\n\nThis is the ∂ambient/∂v factor of action_jacobian, which chains it with the model's own transform_deriv_params. The default for a chart that does not override it is _retract_jacobian_fd, ForwardDiff through retract — exact to first order with no hand-derivation risk, and every closed-form override is pinned against it in the manifold contract tests. A linear chart (LensManifold) returns the identity. The default needs only retract and the model's ambient_coordinates. A chart overrides the two-argument form for any AbstractVector v (one method; a constant differential ignores v), and the one-argument form when its anchor's differential is cheaper than evaluating at zero_tangent(ch).\n\nPublic, not exported; VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation builds its LM and covariance steps on it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retract_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tangent_part","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"tangent_part(ch::Manifold, v, offset = 0) -> SVector{tangent_size(ch)}\n\nThe coordinates of ch in a longer tangent vector v: the tangent_size(ch) entries after the first offset, statically sized and in v's element type (ForwardDiff Duals included), since a manifold's tangent_size is a constant of its type. A product manifold cuts its parts' coordinates with it, and so does a solver that stacks several manifolds' coordinates in one step vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tangent_part"},{"category":"function","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tangent_size","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"tangent_size(ch::Manifold) -> Int\n\nThe manifold's dimension D: the length of the local coordinates v that retract takes. Part of the Manifold contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tangent_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transform_covariance-Tuple{Manifold, Manifold, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"transform_covariance(from::Manifold, to::Manifold, Σ) -> Matrix\n\nA covariance in from's coordinates re-expressed in to's, the two charts anchored at the same model and sharing its ambient vectorization, to first order: through the ambient space, T_to⁺·(T_from·Σ·T_fromᵀ)·T_to⁺ᵀ (T the charts' retract_jacobians). The ambient space is a chart as well, EuclideanManifold(ch): TO it the covariance is pushed, T·Σ·Tᵀ; FROM it pulled, T⁺·Σ·T⁺ᵀ (a gauge-deficient chart projects its gauge out). Exact where to is full rank. A rigid pose's covariance between the MRP (RigidManifold6), left-perturbation (LeftRigidManifold6) and decoupled (RigidExpManifold6) charts, for instance — the MRP's factor 4 at the anchor and the translation coupling done by the charts themselves, not by hand. A change of coordinates at one model, not a parallel transport between points.\n\nGTSAM equivalent: TransformCovariance<T> (a group-frame change), and the Local/Retract Jacobians a chart change composes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transform_covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.zero_tangent-Tuple{Manifold}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"zero_tangent(ch::Manifold) -> SVector{tangent_size(ch)}\n\nThe chart coordinates of the anchor, v = 0, in the chart's float type (Float64 unless the chart stores another): what model(ch) and the one-argument retract_jacobian evaluate at. A chart generic in its float type overrides it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.zero_tangent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EssentialManifold{G <: EssentialTranslation, T} <: Manifold\n\nThe essential-matrix chart anchored at (R, t), |t| = 1: R(v) = RotMatrix(MRP(ω))·R, t(v) the translation kind's increment, E(v) = skew(t(v))·R(v), stored in the float type T. The stack's two are aliases: EssentialManifold5 (minimal, production) and EssentialManifold6 (one gauge direction, eval). See Manifold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold-Union{Tuple{EssentialMat}, Tuple{G}} where G<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialTranslation","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EssentialManifold5(E::EssentialMat)\nEssentialManifold6(E::EssentialMat)\n\nThe chart anchored at the essential matrix E: the pose (R, t) of the FIRST of motion(E)'s four candidates, with t normalized (|t| = 1), so model(ch) = skew(t)·R is E up to scale and sign. The Sampson residuals are invariant to both and to the candidate, which is why any one anchors a refit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold5","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EssentialManifold5(R, t) <: Manifold\n\nThe PRODUCTION 5-dof essential-matrix lift chart, EssentialManifold{<:S2TangentT}: so(3) ⊕ T_tS², gauge-fixed. Anchored at (R, t) with |t| = 1 and an orthonormal tangent basis (b1, b2) of S² at t frozen at the anchor; coordinates v = (ω₁, ω₂, ω₃, τ₁, τ₂) act as R(v) = RotMatrix(MRP(ω))·R, t(v) = normalize(t + τ₁·b1 + τ₂·b2), E(v) = skew(t(v))·R(v). Minimal (rank 5, no gauge nullity). See EssentialManifold6 for the deliberately non-minimal eval companion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold5"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold6","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EssentialManifold6(R, t) <: Manifold\n\nThe EVAL 6-dof essential-matrix chart, EssentialManifold{RenormalizedT}: MRP ⊕ unconstrained-t-then-normalize. Coordinates v = (ω₁..₃, τ₁..₃) act as R(v) = RotMatrix(MRP(ω))·R, t(v) = normalize(t + τ), E = skew(t)·R. DELIBERATELY NOT MINIMAL: the radial direction τ ∥ t is a gauge nullity (rank 5) absorbed by the engines' GN damping — the historical eval solver chart kept for solver-parity with the GNC/scheduled arms; a covariance transported into it (pinv) has the gauge projected out. The production minimal chart is EssentialManifold5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialManifold6"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialTranslation","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EssentialTranslation\n\nThe translation increment of an EssentialManifold: S2TangentT (2 dof, minimal) or RenormalizedT (3 dof, one gauge direction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialTranslation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RenormalizedT","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"t(v) = normalize(t + τ), τ ∈ R³: one gauge direction (τ ∥ t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RenormalizedT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.S2TangentT","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"t(v) = normalize(t + τ₁·b1 + τ₂·b2): the tangent basis (b1, b2) of S² at the anchor's t, frozen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.S2TangentT"},{"category":"function","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose_action_jacobian","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"pose_action_jacobian(ch::EssentialManifold, x, v = zero_tangent(ch)) -> SMatrix{3, tangent_size(ch)}\n\nThe Jacobian ∂(R(v)·x + t(v))/∂v of the chart's POSE acting on a point x, with (R(v), t(v)) = recenter_pose(ch, v): 3 × 5 for EssentialManifold5, 3 × 6 for EssentialManifold6. Closed form: the rotation columns are −[R(v)·x]×·L(ω) (L the MRP's left Jacobian, 4·I₃ at ω = 0, as for RigidManifold6), the translation columns (I − t̂t̂ᵀ)/‖s‖ times the increment's basis ([b1 b2], or I₃), s the translation before normalization and t̂ = s/‖s‖. Where action_jacobian differentiates the model E(v) acting on a point, this differentiates the rigid motion the chart carries: a relative pose with unit translation, the two-view bundle's pose block. Neither Ceres nor GTSAM has the counterpart (neither has a gauge-fixed relative-pose chart).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose_action_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.s2_tangent_basis-Tuple{Any}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"Tangent basis of S² at t — the production _setup_tangent_basis code, VERBATIM (PoseLib relative.h port; branch structure and the b2 = normalize(cross(b1, t)) orientation preserved bit-for-bit for the consumer byte-identity gates).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.s2_tangent_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.s2_tangent_basis_matrix-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"s2_tangent_basis_matrix([T,] f) -> SMatrix{3, 2, T}\n\nThe tangent basis of S² at f, s2_tangent_basis's pair (b1, b2), as the columns of one 3 × 2 matrix B (Bᵀ is the 2 × 3 projection onto the tangent plane), its elements converted to T (default: the basis's own type, promote_type(eltype(f), Float64)). The one packing of the basis: a bearing residual's coordinates, a back-projection Jacobian's tangent rows, a transfer form's projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.s2_tangent_basis_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FundMatManifold7-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3, Float64}}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"Manifold from a fundamental matrix anchor via its SVD (the _orthonormal_manifold convention).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FundMatManifold7"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.anchor_svd-Tuple{FundMatManifold7}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"anchor_svd(ch::FundMatManifold7) -> (U, σ, V)\n\nThe anchor of the Bartoli–Sturm chart: the orthonormal factors U, V and the normalized second singular value σ = s₂/s₁ it is centred on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.anchor_svd"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SL3_BASIS","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"SL3_BASIS\n\nThe eight traceless generators G₁…G₈ of sl(3) used by HomographyManifold8: the six off-diagonal elementary matrices plus diag(1, −1, 0) and diag(0, 1, −1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SL3_BASIS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyManifold8","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"HomographyManifold8(H0) <: Manifold\n\nThe minimal 8-dof sl(3) homography manifold about an anchor: H(v) = H₀·(I + Σ vᵢ·Gᵢ) with G₁…G₈ the traceless sl(3) basis (SL3_BASIS). Tracelessness deletes the homogeneity gauge direction H₀·εI, so the manifold is minimal (rank 8) with no ‖H‖ = 1 constraint and no fixed-entry singularity; ∂H/∂vᵢ = H₀·Gᵢ is exact and constant (first-order manifold, no dexp) — the LM refit's Jacobian convention. The anchor is stored in its float type (Float64 from a Float64 or integer H0). See Manifold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HomographyManifold8"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CarriedT","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"t(v) = ΔR(ω)·t + τ: the rotation carries the translation (x ↦ ΔR·(R·x + t) + τ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CarriedT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ExpParam","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"ΔR(ω) = exp_so3(ω): the rotation vector (radians).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ExpParam"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FreeT","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"t(v) = t + τ: the translation is a free R³ update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FreeT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LeftRigidManifold6","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"LeftRigidManifold6(R, t) <: Manifold\nLeftRigidManifold6(pose::EuclideanMap{3})\n\nThe LEFT-perturbation pose chart, RigidChart{ExpParam, CarriedT}: coordinates v = (δω₁..₃, δt₁..₃) act on the whole map, x ↦ exp([δω]×)·(R·x + t) + δt, so retract(ch, v) is (exp_so3(δω)·R, exp_so3(δω)·t + δt).\n\nIt is the perturbation constraint_with_jacobians differentiates for a pose (G_θ = Jπ·[−[X_cam]×  I₃]): action_jacobian(ch, x) = [−[R·x + t]×  I₃], so a PnP refit — and the PnP lift, which is the refit's rows at v = 0 — that solves in these coordinates re-anchors (recenter) with exactly the perturbation its Jacobian assumed. local_coordinates is the closed-form inverse (δω = log_so3(R·R₀ᵀ), δt = t − exp_so3(δω)·t₀). Float-typed storage (SMatrix{3,3,T}, SVector{3,T}). See RigidChart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LeftRigidManifold6"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MRPParam","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"ΔR(ω) = RotMatrix(MRP(ω)): Rotations' modified Rodrigues parameters, 4× the rotation vector at the identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MRPParam"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RigidChart","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"RigidChart{P <: RotationParam, C <: TranslationCoupling, Rot, Tr} <: Manifold\n\nThe 6-dof rigid pose chart family, anchored at (R::Rot, t::Tr): coordinates v = (ω₁..₃, τ₁..₃) act as R(v) = ΔR(ω)·R and t(v) = t + τ (FreeT) or ΔR(ω)·t + τ (CarriedT), ΔR the increment of P (MRPParam, ExpParam). Full rank (no gauge). retract returns a EuclideanMap; the ambient vectorization is [vec(R); t] ∈ R¹²; recenter re-projects the rotation onto SO(3) (the one policy) and keeps the anchor's storage types, in the element type the contract's rule gives (the anchor's for a step of its own float type); local_coordinates is closed form. The stack's named charts are aliases: RigidManifold6, LeftRigidManifold6, RigidExpManifold6.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RigidChart"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RigidExpManifold6","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"RigidExpManifold6(R, t) <: Manifold\nRigidExpManifold6(pose::EuclideanMap{3})\n\nThe DECOUPLED pose chart, RigidChart{ExpParam, FreeT}: R(v) = exp_so3(δω)·R, t(v) = t + δt. Unlike LeftRigidManifold6 the rotation does not carry the translation; unlike RigidManifold6 the rotation coordinate is the rotation vector (radians), not the MRP. The chart of the hand-eye constraint's G_θ: action_jacobian(ch, x) = [−[R·x]×  I₃]. Float-typed storage. Move a covariance between it and the other rigid charts with transform_covariance. See RigidChart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RigidExpManifold6"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RigidManifold6","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"RigidManifold6(R, t) <: Manifold\n\nThe MRP rigid pose chart, RigidChart{MRPParam, FreeT}: R(v) = RotMatrix(MRP(ω))·R, t(v) = t + τ. Parametric in the anchor's storage types (a RotMatrix or an SMatrix, any static 3-vector), which recenter keeps — the rigid-pose retraction of P3P, the relpose-bundle pose block and BlobBoards' bundle. Its action Jacobian at the anchor is [−4·[R·x]×  I₃] (the MRP's factor 4). Distinct from EssentialManifold6: the translation update is free, so there is no radial-t gauge. See RigidChart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RigidManifold6"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RotationChart","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"RotationChart{P <: RotationParam, Rot} <: Manifold\n\nThe 3-dof rotation chart of the rigid family, anchored at R::Rot: coordinates ω act on the left, R(ω) = ΔR(ω)·R, ΔR the increment of P (MRPParam, ExpParam) — the rotation block of RigidChart, written over the same per-parameterization facts (the increment, its logarithm, its left Jacobian, the anchor scale). retract returns a RotMatrix{3}, the ambient vectorization is vec(R) ∈ R⁹, recenter re-projects onto SO(3) and keeps the anchor's storage type, local_coordinates is closed form (ω the coordinates of R·R₀ᵀ), the retraction differential is closed form: ∂vec(R(ω))/∂ω_q = vec([L(ω)e_q]×·R(ω)). Full rank, no gauge. SO3Manifold is the rotation-vector member, minimal_manifold(R) of a rotation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RotationChart"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RotationParam","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"RotationParam\n\nHow a RigidChart parameterizes its rotation increment ΔR(ω): MRPParam or ExpParam.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RotationParam"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SO3Manifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"SO3Manifold(R) <: Manifold\n\nThe rotation-vector chart of SO(3), RotationChart{ExpParam}: R(ω) = exp_so3(ω)·R (the left perturbation, ω in radians) — the rotation block of LeftRigidManifold6 and RigidExpManifold6, and minimal_manifold of a rotation. Float-typed storage (SMatrix{3, 3, T}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SO3Manifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TranslationCoupling","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"TranslationCoupling\n\nWhether a RigidChart's rotation increment carries the translation: FreeT (t(v) = t + τ) or CarriedT (t(v) = ΔR·t + τ, the whole map perturbed on the left).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TranslationCoupling"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.local_coordinates-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RigidChart, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"local_coordinates(ch::RigidChart, pose::EuclideanMap{3}) -> SVector{6}\n\nThe rigid chart's inverse retraction: the v with retract(ch, v) == pose — ω the coordinates of R·R₀ᵀ (its MRP — the principal set, |ω| < 1 for rotations under π — or log_so3), τ = t − t₀ (or t − ΔR(ω)·t₀ when the rotation carries the translation). Closed form and exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.local_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.local_coordinates-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.RotationChart, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"local_coordinates(ch::RotationChart, R) -> SVector{3}\n\nThe rotation chart's inverse retraction: the coordinates of R·R₀ᵀ (its MRP or log_so3). Closed form and exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.local_coordinates"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearSubspaceManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"LinearSubspaceManifold{C, F} <: Manifold\n\nrestrict(ch, Z)'s manifold: the parent manifold::C of tangent size D on the M coordinates y of the subspace spanned by the columns of Z::Matrix{Float64} (D × M), the parent's coordinates v = Z y. At the anchor the model is the parent's, so the parameters move as θ = θ₀ + Z y through the parent's chart (exactly, for an unbounded parent). retract, recenter and the Jacobians are the parent's composed with Z (retract_jacobian(rc) = retract_jacobian(ch) * Z); local_coordinates is the least-squares y of the parent's coordinates, (ZᵀZ)⁻¹ Zᵀ v by the Cholesky factor F of ZᵀZ, computed once at construction; exact for a model in the subspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LinearSubspaceManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SubsetManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"SubsetManifold{C, D, I} <: Manifold\n\nrestrict's manifold: the parent manifold::C of tangent size D on its free coordinates idx::I (ascending), an SVector{M, Int}: a tangent size that is a constant of the type. retract(rc, v) is the parent's at v lifted into D coordinates (zeros elsewhere), the Jacobians are the parent's columns idx, recenter re-anchors the parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SubsetManifold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.restrict-Tuple{Manifold, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"restrict(ch::Manifold, Z::AbstractMatrix) -> LinearSubspaceManifold\n\nThe manifold ch on the subspace of its coordinates spanned by the columns of Z (tangent_size(ch) rows, any number of columns): its coordinates y move the parent's as v = Z y. A fit whose parameters are vec(P) = vec(P₀) + Z y — a vignetting's control points on a regular subspace (a radial profile's pole_regular_basis), a target's deformation basis — is the parameter manifold of P₀ restricted to Z. Z is stored as a plain Matrix{Float64} (a control-point basis has hundreds to thousands of entries) with the Cholesky factor of ZᵀZ; its columns must be linearly independent (ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.restrict"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SphereManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"SphereManifold(q::StaticVector{N}, embed = identity) <: Manifold\n\nThe unit sphere at q (normalized on construction): coordinates v ∈ R^{N−1} along an orthonormal basis B of q's complement — the last N − 1 columns of the Householder reflection taking e₁ to ∓q, built from q + sign(q₁)·e₁ so it is stable for every q — and retract(ch, v) = embed(normalize(q + B·v)).\n\nembed turns the unit vector into the model: the identity for a bare vector, a RadialCamera with its centre for minimal_manifold(::RadialCamera). The ambient space is the vector's: retract_jacobian(ch, v) is (I − ŷŷᵀ)·B/‖q + B·v‖ (N × (N − 1), B at the anchor). local_coordinates(ch, y) is closed form and invariant to the scale and sign of y: Bᵀy/(qᵀy). recenter re-anchors at the retracted point, with a new basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SphereManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EuclideanManifold(x::StaticVector{N}) <: Manifold\n\nRᴺ at x: retract(ch, v) = x + v, the identity differential, local_coordinates(ch, y) = y − x. The chart of a model's plain-vector part — a division coefficient, a 3D point — in a ProductManifold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanManifold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanManifold-Tuple{Manifold}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EuclideanManifold(ch::Manifold)\n\nThe AMBIENT space of the chart ch as a chart: Rᴺ anchored at the ambient coordinates of model(ch) (vec(M) for a 3×3 model, [vec R; t] for a pose, a lens's parameters, …) — where transform_covariance pushes a covariance to, transform_covariance(ch, EuclideanManifold(ch), Σv), and pulls it from, transform_covariance(EuclideanManifold(ch), ch, Σ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EuclideanManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ProductManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"ProductManifold(charts::Manifold...; embed = identity) <: Manifold\n\nThe chart of a model made of independent parts, one chart each: coordinates the concatenation of the parts' (in order), retract(ch, v) = embed(parts) with parts the tuple of the parts' retractions, recenter part by part, retract_jacobian block diagonal over the parts' ambient spaces (an SMatrix when the parts' are static and the whole stays small, a dense Matrix otherwise — products run to many parts: a rig of poses, a pose and its points), and local_coordinates(ch, parts::Tuple) the concatenation. embed assembles the model from its parts: minimal_manifold(::RadialHomography) is sl(3) ⊕ the division lens's chart, assembled into the RadialHomography with its centre.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ProductManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Line2DManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"Line2DManifold(l::Line2D) <: Manifold\n\nThe 2-dof chart of a 2D line n·x + d = 0 (|n| = 1): v = (φ, δd) rotates the normal by φ and moves the offset, retract(ch, v) = Line2D(R(φ)·n, d + δd). The ambient space is the line's coefficients (n₁, n₂, d), retract_jacobian [R(φ)·t  0; 0  1] with t = (−n₂, n₁) the line's direction. A line acts on a point by its signed distance (constraint_with_jacobians(l, x)), so action_jacobian(ch, x) = (t·x, 1) — the point-on-line constraint's chart row. local_coordinates is closed form and reads (n, d) and (−n, −d) as the same line. minimal_manifold(l::Line2D) is this chart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Line2DManifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CircleManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"CircleManifold(c::Circle) <: Manifold\n\nThe 3-dof chart of a 2D circle with centre c and radius r > 0: v = (δc₁, δc₂, δlog r) moves the centre and scales the radius, retract(ch, v) = Circle(c + (δc₁, δc₂), r·exp(δlog r)), so every point of the chart is a circle with a positive radius. The ambient space is (c₁, c₂, r) (ambient_coordinates); retract_jacobian is diag(1, 1, r·exp(δlog r)). A circle acts on a point by the point-on-circle constraint (constraint_with_jacobians(c, x)), so action_jacobian(ch, x) = (−2(x − c)ᵀ, −2r²) is that constraint's chart row. local_coordinates is closed form. minimal_manifold(c::Circle) is this chart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CircleManifold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{Circle, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(c::Circle, x::StaticVector{2}) -> (g, G_x::SVector{2}, G_θ::SVector{3})\n\nThe point-on-circle constraint g = ‖x − c‖² − r² with G_x = 2(x − c) and G_θ = ∂g/∂(c₁, c₂, r) = (−2(x − c), −2r), the circle's ambient coordinates (CircleManifold's). Its Sampson distance g/‖G_x‖ = (d² − r²)/(2d), d = ‖x − c‖, is the geometric distance d − r to first order (the ratio is (d + r)/(2d)). A point at the centre has G_x = 0 — the one Sampson degeneracy (base/sampson.jl), judged at the circle's constraint_rounding_scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EllipseManifold","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"EllipseManifold(e::Ellipse) <: Manifold\n\nThe 5-dof chart of a 2D ellipse: VisualGeometryToolkitCore's log-shape chart (ellipse_log_shape) taken as local coordinates, v = (δc₁, δc₂, δS₁₁, δS₁₂, δS₂₂), retract(ch, v) = ellipse_from_log_shape(ellipse_log_shape(e) + v), so every point of the chart is a real ellipse (the exponential of every symmetric S is positive definite) and the chart is smooth at a circle. The ambient space is the centre and the quadratic form Q = exp(−2S) = R·diag(a⁻², b⁻²)·Rᵀ of the ellipse, (c₁, c₂, Q₁₁, Q₁₂, Q₂₂) (ambient_coordinates), in which the point-on-ellipse constraint (constraint_with_jacobians(e, x)) is polynomial; retract_jacobian is diag(I₂, ∂Q/∂S), the derivative of the symmetric matrix exponential in closed form (Daleckii–Krein). action_jacobian(ch, x) is that constraint's chart row. local_coordinates is closed form. minimal_manifold(e::Ellipse) is this chart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EllipseManifold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{Conic, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(c::Conic, x::StaticVector{2}) -> (g, G_x::SVector{2}, G_θ::SVector{6})\n\nThe point-on-conic constraint of a general conic C (SMatrix{3, 3}(c), symmetric 3×3): g = x̃ᵀ C x̃, x̃ = (x, 1), with G_x = 2(C x̃)₁,₂ and G_θ over the conic's six distinct entries in upper-triangle column-major order, (C₁₁, C₁₂, C₂₂, C₁₃, C₂₃, C₃₃): (x², 2xy, y², 2x, 2y, 1). Homogeneous: a scaled conic is the same curve and has the same sampson_distance (the constraint and its gradient scale together), which is the first-order distance to the curve — for an ellipse's conic, its constraint_with_jacobians(::Ellipse, x)'s. A point where C x̃ has no image part (the centre of a central conic) has G_x = 0: the one Sampson degeneracy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{Ellipse, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(e::Ellipse, x::StaticVector{2}) -> (g, G_x::SVector{2}, G_θ::SVector{5})\n\nThe point-on-ellipse constraint, the implicit conic g = (x − c)ᵀ Q (x − c) − 1 (Q = R·diag(a⁻², b⁻²)·Rᵀ, so g = x̃ᵀ C x̃ for the ellipse's conic C scaled to −1 at the centre), with G_x = 2Q(x − c) and G_θ = ∂g/∂(c₁, c₂, Q₁₁, Q₁₂, Q₂₂) = (−2Q(x − c), d₁², 2d₁d₂, d₂²), d = x − c, the ellipse's ambient coordinates (EllipseManifold's). Its Sampson distance g/‖G_x‖ is the geometric distance to the ellipse to first order, and at a circle of radius r the circle's (‖d‖² − r²)/(2‖d‖) (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's constraint_with_jacobians(::Circle, x)). A point at the centre has G_x = 0 — the one Sampson degeneracy (base/sampson.jl), at the ellipse's constraint_rounding_scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_dim-Tuple{Type{<:AbstractFundMat}}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"constraint_dim(model) -> Int\n\nNumber of scalar algebraic constraints per correspondence for model's class: 1 for the epipolar models (FundMat, EssentialMat), 2 for HomographyMat and for a pose (EuclideanMap{3}, the reprojection constraint). Matches the row count of constraint_with_jacobians.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_dim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_jacobians-Tuple{Manifold, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where {N, T}, AbstractVector}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"constraint_jacobians(ch::Manifold, v, cs) -> (∂_v g, ∂_x g)\n\nThe Jacobians of manifold_constraints at chart coordinates v: with respect to v (G_θ·retract_jacobian(ch, v), dg·k × D) and to the observations (block diagonal, dg·k × nₓk, each pair's G_x over (x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂), or over the image point of a pose correspondence, nₓ = 2). At v = 0 these are the two factors of the implicit-function covariance (implicit_covariance); at v ≠ 0, the Newton matrix of a chart-Newton solve that iterates in the manifold.\n\nNO Ceres or GTSAM counterpart: both frame a problem as residuals of an EXPLICIT cost, whereas this is the IMPLICIT constraint g(θ, x) = 0 in Förstner's form — A = ∂g/∂θ, B = ∂g/∂x — which is what an implicit-function covariance and a Gauss-Helmert step consume. The value-and-Jacobians sibling is constraint_with_jacobians.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians-Tuple{Line2D, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"constraint_with_jacobians(l::Line2D, x::StaticVector{2}) -> (g, G_x::SVector{2}, G_θ::SVector{3})\n\nThe point-on-line constraint g = n·x + d — the point's signed distance (the normal is unit) — with G_x = n and G_θ = ∂g/∂(n₁, n₂, d) = (x₁, x₂, 1), the line's coefficients (Line2DManifold's ambient space). Single-point data: the covariance machinery takes a vector of points for a line chart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.constraint_with_jacobians"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.implicit_covariance-Tuple{Manifold, AbstractVector}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"implicit_covariance(ch::Manifold, cs; Σx = nothing) -> Matrix (D×D) or nothing\nimplicit_covariance(block, indices; noise_weighted = false) -> Matrix or nothing\nimplicit_covariance(A::AbstractMatrix, B::AbstractMatrix; Σx = nothing) -> Matrix or nothing\n\nThe first-order covariance of the solution v(x) of an implicitly defined estimation problem (Haralick's framing): g(v, x) = 0 for a square system (a minimal solve), ∂_v(½‖W₀^{½}·g‖²) = 0 — least-squares stationarity of the whitened constraints — for an overdetermined one. With A = ∂_v g, B = ∂_x g and the estimator's own isotropic whitening W₀ = blockdiag((BᵢBᵢᵀ)⁻¹) over the correspondences' row blocks,\n\nΣ̃_v = (AᵀW₀A)⁻¹ · AᵀW₀·(B·Σx·Bᵀ)·W₀A · (AᵀW₀A)⁻¹,\n\nwhich is A⁻¹·B·Σx·Bᵀ·A⁻ᵀ when A is square (the whitening cancels — the implicit-function theorem proper) and (AᵀW₀A)⁻¹ for unit isotropic Σx. Unit scale: Σx defaults to unit isotropic observation noise; the physical covariance is s²·Σ̃_v with s the consumer's. Invariant to any invertible per-correspondence recombination of the constraint rows (the algebraic and the transfer forms of a homography give the same Σ̃_v).\n\nChart first: the constraints of the correspondences cs (u₁ => u₂ pairs; X => u for a pose chart; single points for a line) differentiated at the anchor of ch (constraint_jacobians), in the coordinates of ch, which must be anchored at the estimate. Σx: one covariance shape per correspondence (4×4 over (x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂); 2×2 for a pose's or a line's point), in the coordinates of cs — data mapped to another frame needs its noise transported there (the map's transform_deriv) before a pixel s² means anything. A minimal sample (k·d_g = D) takes the square solve.\nBlocks, never materializing B (O(D²) memory, for large inlier sets): block(i) returns (Aᵢ, Bᵢ) or (Aᵢ, Bᵢ, Σxᵢ) for each i in indices — the correspondence's rows of ∂_v g and ∂_x g (a StaticVector is one row) and its covariance shape (unit isotropic when absent). Function first, as in map(f, xs). With noise_weighted = true the estimator is instead the one weighted by each block's OWN noise, W₀ᵢ = (BᵢΣxᵢBᵢᵀ)⁻¹ (the Σxᵢ-whitened residuals a scorer uses), whose covariance is (AᵀW₀A)⁻¹ — Σxᵢ folded into Bᵢ as Bᵢ·chol(Σxᵢ).L. The default sandwiches Σxᵢ around the isotropically weighted estimator: a different estimator, a different covariance whenever the Σxᵢ are not isotropic.\nDense square factors: A = ∂_v g (D×D) and B = ∂_x g (D×nₓk) of a square system g(v, x) = 0 whose rows are not per-correspondence constraints — e.g. the stationarity conditions of a staged estimator, one row block per stage — gives A⁻¹·B·Σx·Bᵀ·A⁻ᵀ directly. Σx: one covariance shape per column block of B (nₓ columns each, k blocks); unit when absent. The minimal-sample path of the chart form above is this method.\n\nnothing when the system is singular (A for a square one, AᵀW₀A otherwise — a degenerate sample, the same the solvers' own gates reject, or ill-posed geometry) or when there are no blocks; a block whose Bᵢ is degenerate (whitening_factor's rule) drops out, as it does from the estimator.\n\nGTSAM equivalent: Marginals::jointMarginalCovariance at the optimum, when the factor rows are correctly whitened. This one also covers the square minimal solve and the sandwich for a mismatched weighting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.implicit_covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.local_coordinates-Tuple{Manifold, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"local_coordinates(ch::Manifold, Mtarget; iters=25, tol=1e-13)\n    -> SVector{tangent_size(ch)}\n\nInverse retraction (\"manifold log\"): the local coordinates v such that retract(ch, v) ∝ Mtarget (projective alignment — scale AND sign of the target are matched to the manifold family at each Gauss–Newton step, so any solver normalization convention may be supplied). This is the harness primitive for expressing an estimate IN THE MANIFOLD RECENTERED AT TRUTH.\n\nGauge-deficient manifolds (e.g. EssentialManifold6, near-essential FundMatManifold7 anchors) resolve the step through pinv, projecting the gauge direction out — the returned coordinates are the minimum-norm representative. Converges quadratically for targets in the manifold's injectivity neighborhood (MC-perturbation scale); for far targets check the residual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.local_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.manifold_constraints-Tuple{Manifold, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where {N, T}, AbstractVector}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"manifold_constraints(ch::Manifold, v, cs) -> Vector\n\nThe stacked algebraic constraints g(v, x) of the correspondences cs (u₁ => u₂ pairs; X => u for a pose chart, u calibrated; single points for a line) at chart coordinates v: constraint_with_jacobians' value for retract(ch, v), constraint_dim rows per pair. Zero on the constraint variety; the residual a chart-Newton solve drives to zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.manifold_constraints"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_manifold-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PlainEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"minimal_manifold(model) -> Manifold\n\nThe minimal (gauge-free) manifold anchored at model: FundMat → FundMatManifold7 (via the anchor SVD), HomographyMat → HomographyManifold8, EssentialMat → EssentialManifold5 (via the first motion(E) candidate — the tangent-domain covariance and its ambient push are candidate-invariant, see the header gauge note), EuclideanMap{3} → RigidManifold6, RadialHomography → sl(3) ⊕ its division lens's chart (a ProductManifold), RadialCamera → S⁵ (a SphereManifold, cameras/calibration.jl), a lens → its LensManifold (cameras/lens_chart.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_manifold"},{"category":"type","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LineConfidenceBand","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"LineConfidenceBand\n\nConfidence region of an uncertain homogeneous 2D line: nominal line l, line covariance Σl (3×3, from pushing a model covariance through the line construction), and squared Mahalanobis level k2. Region: {x : (x̃ᵀl)² ≤ k2·x̃ᵀΣl x̃}; boundary conic C = l·lᵀ − k2·Σl. Plot with lines!(ax, band, rect) (Makie layer) or sample the boundary with band_boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LineConfidenceBand"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.band_boundary-Tuple{LineConfidenceBand, Any}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"band_boundary(b::LineConfidenceBand, rect::HyperRectangle{2}; n=201)\n    -> (upper::Vector{Point2{Float64}}, lower::Vector{Point2{Float64}})\n\nSample the two boundary branches of the band inside rect, as offsets along the nominal line's normal over n stations of the clipped nominal segment. Stations where the band is UNBOUNDED (the near-degeneracy blow-up: the normal-section quadratic loses its positive curvature or its real roots) emit (NaN, NaN) so polylines break there instead of lying. Returns empty vectors when the nominal line misses rect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.band_boundary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.band_conic-Tuple{LineConfidenceBand}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"Boundary point conic C = l·lᵀ − k²·Σ_l of the band.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.band_conic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.confidence_ellipse-Tuple{Any, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"confidence_ellipse(μ, Σ; level=0.95) -> Ellipse\n\nThe level confidence ellipse of a 2D Gaussian N(μ, Σ): {x : (x−μ)ᵀ Σ⁻¹ (x−μ) = χ²₂(level)}, as the standard Ellipse primitive (so it plots through the existing poly!/convert_arguments layer).\n\nIt is Ellipse(μ, Symmetric(χ²₂(level)·Σ)), so a Σ that is not positive definite (singular, indefinite, or NaN) is refused with that constructor's ArgumentError, never clamped into a degenerate ellipse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.confidence_ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.epipolar_band-Tuple{AbstractFundMat, AbstractMatrix, Any}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"epipolar_band(F::AbstractFundMat, Σθ::AbstractMatrix(9×9), u₁;\n              level=0.95, dof=2) -> LineConfidenceBand\n\nConfidence band of the epipolar line l = F·ũ₁ in image 2 under the ambient model covariance Σθ (vec(F) column-major). dof=2 gives the whole-line (H–Z envelope) convention, dof=1 the pointwise convention — see the file header. Uncertainty of u₁ itself is not included (model-only band; fold a 4×4 point covariance into Σ_l externally if needed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.epipolar_band"},{"category":"method","location":"api/manifolds/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.in_band-Tuple{LineConfidenceBand, Any}","page":"Manifolds & Uncertainty","text":"Signed band membership: x̃ᵀCx̃ ≤ 0 ⇔ inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.in_band"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_losses/#Robust-Losses-API","page":"Robust Losses","text":"The robust-loss family and the width axes a scorer or a local optimizer is built at. The contracts that use them are on the Robust Estimation API page.","title":"Robust Losses API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_losses/#Robust-Losses","page":"Robust Losses","text":"","title":"Robust Losses"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_losses/#Width-Axes","page":"Robust Losses","text":"","title":"Width Axes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/robust_losses/#Index","page":"Robust Losses","text":"Pages = [\"robust_losses.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnyLoss","page":"Robust Losses","text":"AnyLoss\n\nA loss that KNOWS ITS OWN WIDTH — deployed, or deployed and annealed.\n\nThe one-argument verbs below are written against this, ONCE. Every one of them is <shape math>(loss_shape(loss), …, sq_loss_width(loss)), and both members answer those two accessors, so nothing in a verb body is per-type.\n\nA future wrapper (censored, per-point-scaled) joins the family by defining loss_shape and sq_loss_width, not by copying nine one-liners.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnyLoss"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLossShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"AbstractLossShape\n\nTag-type root of the unified robust-loss family: a normalized scale family ρ_w(r) = w²·ρ₁(r/w) whose scale parameter w is the loss_width, the residual scale at which the loss leaves the shared quadratic regime (see LOSSES.md and the header of robust/losses.jl). A shape carries no width; deploy gives it one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLossShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractMEstimator","page":"Robust Losses","text":"AbstractMEstimator <: AbstractLossShape\n\nA robust loss whose WIDTH is set by asymptotic efficiency at the Gaussian. Subtyping this is a promise, and the promise costs exactly one method.\n\nInterface\n\nA subtype MUST define one method:\n\nefficiency_constant(::Type{MyShape}) -> Float64\n\nits tuning constant at codimension 1 — the solution of eff(m, 1) = 0.95 for that shape's psi. It takes the TYPE, not an instance: the constant is a property of the shape, and a deployment is built FROM it. Failing to define it is a MethodError on the first call, deliberately NOT caught by a catch-all, so hasmethod(efficiency_constant, (Type{MyShape},)) answers honestly.\n\nFrom that single number every other method is inherited and guaranteed:\n\ninherited what it gives\nefficiency_constant(K, d) the constant at codimension d\ncodimension_factor(K, d) its ratio to the d = 1 constant\nsq_loss_width(loss) efficiency_constant^2 * s2, the squared width it runs at\nloss_width(loss) efficiency_constant * sqrt(s2), derived\n\nNote sq_loss_width/loss_width are methods of a DEPLOYMENT (deploy(shape, s2; efficiency_constant)), not of the shape: a bare shape has no width.\n\nThe noise level is an INPUT. This interface turns one into a width; it does not estimate the noise, and nothing here should. How a strategy obtains its scale is a separate axis with its own vocabulary — see ScaleInterface (ConsensusAnchored, FixedScale, FollowsScorer, SelfScaling, NoScale) in robust/estimation.jl — and the estimator is a real choice (MAD, RMS, median-of-norms, Qn, …) that belongs to the caller, not to the loss.\n\nA subtype MAY also define _solved_constants(::Type{MyShape}), a tuple of the constants for d = 1, 2, … (entry 1 equal to its efficiency_constant) at each codimension where eff(m, d) = 0.95 has actually been SOLVED, replacing the generic chi lift sqrt((d+2)/3) with those solved numbers. It is an optimisation of accuracy, never a requirement — see the note under codimension_factor.\n\nWho is deliberately NOT here\n\nLosses that efficiency does not parameterise stay directly under AbstractLossShape, and calling loss_width on one is a MethodError by design: GaussianShape has no width to set, TLSShape is a hard truncation with no influence function, and BarronShape's shape is itself fitted — deriving a fixed constant for it would contradict the fit.\n\nSmoothTLSShape is the one shape that sits outside this hierarchy yet DOES carry constants. It is a truncation, so it ships no default efficiency_constant and subtyping AbstractMEstimator would wrongly promise one — but eff(m, d) = 0.95 is genuinely solvable for its psi, so efficiency_constant(SmoothTLSShape) and its whole solved column exist and are supported. Read the subtype relation as \"has a default efficiency_constant\", NOT as \"has a constant\": a caller wanting the efficiency-matched truncation asks efficiency_constant directly. This is why efficiency_constant(K, d) and codimension_factor(K, d) below take Type{<:AbstractLossShape} and not Type{<:AbstractMEstimator}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractMEstimator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnnealedLoss","page":"Robust Losses","text":"AnnealedLoss\n\nA DeployedLoss wearing its position on the graduation path — what anneal returns. Nests, so target_loss is an unwrap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnnealedLoss"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BandAnnealed","page":"Robust Losses","text":"GNC: Black–Rangarajan band parameter with its own closed form, annealing UP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BandAnnealed"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BarronShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"BarronShape(α) <: AbstractLossShape\n\nBarron's general robust loss at fixed shape α < 2 (facet-B convention above). α is the retirement-steepness dial: −∞ = Welsch, −2 = GM, 0 = Cauchy, 2 = L2 (excluded — not liftable). Bounded (redescending to a floor) iff α < 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.BarronShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CauchyShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"Cauchy shape g(u) = ln(1+u) — unbounded (ρ(∞)=∞) yet liftable (finite w_max).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CauchyShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConvexityAnnealed","page":"Robust Losses","text":"GNC: μ enters as μ·c̄² but denotes CONVEXITY, not a width (Geman–McClure).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ConvexityAnnealed"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DeployedLoss","page":"Robust Losses","text":"DeployedLoss\n\nA shape together with the width it runs at: Attributed(shape, (efficiency_constant, s2)), the multiple of the noise scale and the noise VARIANCE. This is the homotopy's TARGET — anneal wraps one to move along the path, and target_loss unwraps back to it.\n\nisbits and 16 bytes: the shape is a zero-byte tag, so a deployment is two Float64s and costs a stack write.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.DeployedLoss"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GaussianShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"Gaussian/L2 shape g(u) = u — unbounded, the Δ→∞ anchor of the depth sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GaussianShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GemanMcclureShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"Geman–McClure shape g(u) = u/(1+u) — steep core, soft polynomial tail.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GemanMcclureShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GncSurrogate","page":"Robust Losses","text":"GncSurrogate\n\nHow a loss shape graduates under GNC — the trait gnc_weight, gnc_target, gnc_mu0 and gnc_entry_mu0 dispatch on. See gnc_surrogate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GncSurrogate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HuberShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"HuberShape <: AbstractLossShape\n\nHuber tag: quadratic core with a LINEAR tail past the elbow — monotone ψ (influence constant, never vanishing, beyond the elbow), hence unbounded and not redescending; not liftable (w_max is finite but the GR dual adds nothing over plain IRLS). Facet tag only — names the objective of the static-IRLS strategy vocabulary (Irls{HuberShape}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.HuberShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MuScaled","page":"Robust Losses","text":"MuScaled <: GncSurrogate\n\nThe families whose μ multiplies the squared width (c²_μ = μ·c̄²). They differ in what μ MEANS — a width for Welsch, convexity for Geman–McClure — and so in gnc_mu0 and gnc_target, but not in how it enters the weight, so that half of the trait is written once against this supertype.\n\nAn ABSTRACT TYPE, not a Union: a downstream shape whose μ scales the squared width joins by subtyping this, the way a downstream M-estimator joins by subtyping AbstractMEstimator. A Union would have made the new extension point closed — joining would mean editing a Union inside VGC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MuScaled"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoSurrogate","page":"Robust Losses","text":"GNC: this shape has no graduation path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NoSurrogate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SmoothTLSShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"Smooth-TLS shape g(u) = 2u − u² capped at 1 — Gaussian core, hard cutoff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SmoothTLSShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TLSShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"TLSShape <: AbstractLossShape\n\nHARD truncated-least-squares tag: ρ(r) = min(r², c²) — the discontinuous-influence truncation (shape g(u) = min(u, 1)). Facet tag only: NOT liftable (the truncated quadratic has no Geman–Reynolds dual — its graduation is the Black–Rangarajan outer bound, gnc_tls_weight below, not a width anneal), so it ships no lift_* closed forms. Names the objective of the truncated/GNC-TLS strategy vocabulary (e.g. Gnc{Irls{TLSShape}} in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.TLSShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WelschShape","page":"Robust Losses","text":"Welsch shape g(u) = 1 − e^{−u} — Gaussian core, soft exponential tail.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WelschShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WidthAnnealed","page":"Robust Losses","text":"GNC: μ scales the squared width, c²_μ = μ·c̄², annealing down to μ = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WidthAnnealed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.anneal-Tuple{DeployedLoss, Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"anneal(loss, μ) -> AnnealedLoss\n\nThe loss at position μ on its graduation path. μ = 1 is the target for the μ-scaled families; see gnc_surrogate for what μ means to a shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.anneal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.band_signal-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.GncSurrogate, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"band_signal(surrogate, w) -> Int\n\nThe per-residual rung-advance signal a GNC engine counts: 1 when the weight lies in the OPEN band 0 < w < 1, 0 otherwise — and identically 0 for every family whose graduation has no band.\n\nA TRAIT VERB rather than an isa BandAnnealed test inside the accumulation loops (LocalOptimizationDoneRight's lift core). Those loops are generic over the family, so they name no family: a new surrogate family declares its own signal instead of editing the loop.\n\nReturns an Int so the loops accumulate unconditionally; where the answer is the constant 0 the whole term folds away.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.band_signal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bounded-Tuple{AbstractLossShape}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"bounded(shape::AbstractLossShape) -> Bool\n\nWhether the shape is bounded (sup g = 1, so ρ(∞) = Δ): the potential redescends to a positive density floor e^{−Δ} and is therefore NOT normalizable on an unbounded domain — usable as evidence only through a truncated normalizer Z̃, and never likelihood-grade at frozen scale (aligns with the NormalizedWeightPolicy boundary in robust/estimation.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bounded"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.codimension_factor-Tuple{Type{<:AbstractLossShape}, Integer}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"codimension_factor(K::Type{<:AbstractLossShape}, codim::Integer) -> Float64\n\nHow much wider the width must be for a codim-dimensional residual than for a scalar one, at equal efficiency: efficiency_constant(K, d) / efficiency_constant(K), and 1.0 at codimension 1 by construction.\n\nDERIVED, not stored — efficiency_constant(K, d) is the primitive, because the constant is what gets solved. Where eff(m, d) = 0.95 has actually been solved the ratio comes out of that solved number; everywhere else it is the chi-distribution rule sqrt((d+2)/3), which is exact at d = 1, a good approximation elsewhere, and makes a new loss work from its 1-D constant alone. The solved values run 3.8-7.5% under the generic 1.1547 at d = 2 (Huber 1.1109, GM 1.0814, Welsch 1.0893, Cauchy 1.0686).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.codimension_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.conjugate-Tuple{WelschShape, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"conjugate(loss, w, sq_loss_width) -> Float64\n\nGeman–Reynolds penalty conjugate(w) on the UNNORMALIZED weight w ∈ (0, w_max], so that ρ(r) = min_w [w·r² + conjugate(w)] (verified numerically in the RVG tests).\n\nnote: Two psis, and this is not the one\nThe half-quadratic literature (Geman–Reynolds, Black–Rangarajan) writes this penalty ψ; M-estimation writes ψ for the INFLUENCE FUNCTION ρ′(r). This package needs both, so the influence function keeps the symbol — it is the one efficiency_constant is solved against — and the penalty is named for what it IS: the Legendre conjugate of ρ in w.Tell them apart by the argument: conjugate(shape, w, sq_loss_width) takes a WEIGHT, ψ and weight take a residual and a squared residual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.conjugate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.conjugate_curvature-Tuple{WelschShape, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"conjugate_curvature(loss, w, sq_loss_width) -> Float64\n\nw·κ²″(w), the scaled second derivative of the Geman–Reynolds conjugate κ²(w) in the unnormalized weight w — the z-block curvature of a Newton lift in w = z². Since κ²′(w) = −s(w) (s the squared residual whose weight is w), it is −w/ω′(s(w)). Closed forms, each pinned against a central difference of conjugate:\n\nshape κ²(w) w·κ²″(w)\nWelsch c²(w ln w − w + 1) c²\nGeman–McClure c²(√w − 1)² c²/(2√w)\nCauchy c²(w − 1 − ln w) c²/w\nsmooth TLS c²(1 − w/2)² c²w/2\nBarron(α) see BarronShape 2c²·w^{2/(α−2)}\n\nDefined for the liftable shapes only, as conjugate is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.conjugate_curvature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cost-Tuple{AbstractLossShape, Any, Real, Int64}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"cost(loss, r, sq_loss_width, n) -> Float64\n\nLoss M-estimator cost Σᵢ ρ(rᵢ; c) over r[1:n] — the best-tracking objective of the lifted LO, scaled by the shape's cost_scale.\n\nONE loop for every shape; Welsch's ½ comes from cost_scale, so the Welsch ρ closed form is written once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cost_scale-Tuple{AbstractLossShape}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"cost_scale(shape) -> Float64\n\nThe shape's historical COST CONVENTION: the constant its tracked objective is scaled by, 1.0 for everything but Welsch, whose tracker carries a ½.\n\nA pure rescaling — monotone-equivalent, so it cannot change which model wins — and therefore a property of the SHAPE's history, not of its math. It is a trait of its own precisely because it is not deducible from anything else, and it is per-SHAPE so that no graduation family inherits Welsch's ½: a future width-annealed shape carries its own convention, not Welsch's.\n\nMultiplying by ½ is exact in binary floating point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cost_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_boundary-Tuple{WelschShape, Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"decision_boundary(shape::AbstractLossShape, loss_width) -> Float64\n\nThe inverse of decision_width for a loss shape: the residual at which the shape's normalized IRLS weight at loss_width is exactly ½ (its half-weight decision), with the same closed forms read the other way — Welsch t = √(ln 2)·w, Geman–McClure t = √(√2 − 1)·w, Cauchy and TLS t = w, smooth-TLS t = w/√2, Barron t = w·√(b·(2^{2/(2−α)} − 1)). decision_boundary(shape, decision_width(shape, t)) ≈ t. The mixture's method, decision_boundary(::GauInlier, outlier_halfwidth), is the same inverse for the Gaussian-uniform pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_boundary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_width","page":"Robust Losses","text":"decision_width(m, boundary) -> Float64\n\nThe native width of m whose inlier/outlier decision flips at the residual boundary (a decision_boundary) — THE one conversion behind every \"same decision point\" comparison across methods (the evaluation's fairness axis, where boundary = efficiency_constant · ŝ). Each owner defines its own method:\n\na loss shape: the loss_width at which the shape's NORMALIZED IRLS weight at boundary is exactly ½, ω(t²; w²)/w_max = ½ (the half-weight decision of a reweighting loss);\nTLSShape: the identity — a hard truncation decides at its own width;\nRANSACScoringDoneRight's GauInlier: the Student-t marginal's outlier half-width whose MAP boundary sits at boundary.\n\nClosed forms for the shapes (t the decision boundary, w the loss width):\n\nWelsch:        e^{−t²/w²} = ½        ⇒ w = t/√(ln 2)\nGeman–McClure: (1+t²/w²)^{−2} = ½    ⇒ w = t/√(√2 − 1)\nCauchy:        (1+t²/w²)^{−1} = ½    ⇒ w = t\nsmooth-TLS:    1 − t²/w² = ½         ⇒ w = √2·t (hard cutoff at √2·t)\nTLS:           w = t\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.decision_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.deploy-Tuple{AbstractMEstimator, Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"deploy(shape, s2; codim = 1, efficiency_constant = efficiency_constant(shape, codim))\n\nA SHAPE at the width it runs at — the pairing every consumer was assembling by hand. Returns a DeployedLoss whose loss_width is efficiency_constant * sqrt(s2).\n\ns2 is the noise VARIANCE (sigma squared), not sigma. The whole robust layer speaks squared scale — LossWidth(m, s2), FixedS2(s2), scale_s2(cs), and every shape closed form takes a squared width — so storing the square is what makes sq_loss_width exact: efficiency_constant^2 * s2 is the arithmetic the call sites already do, bit for bit. Storing a linear sigma instead would force a sqrt in and a square out, and the two disagree in the last ulps on 61% of inputs.\n\nIt is the CALLER's: this package does not estimate it (see ScaleInterface in robust/estimation.jl for the axis that decides how). From a linear sigma, square it at the call: deploy(shape, mad_scale(residuals)^2; codim = 2).\n\nefficiency_constant is the width as a dimensionless multiple of the noise scale, defaulting to the shape's efficiency constant at codim and overridden by a caller that cross-validates its own operating point.\n\nThe two are kept APART, never multiplied into a stored width: a SelfScaling strategy re-estimates the variance every iteration while efficiency_constant is fixed, so rescale replaces one and leaves the other.\n\ndeploy(WelschShape(), 0.37^2; codim = 2)                         # from efficiency -> 3.2510\ndeploy(WelschShape(), 0.37^2; codim = 2, efficiency_constant = 1.597) # a CV'd operating point\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.deploy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.efficiency_constant","page":"Robust Losses","text":"efficiency_constant(K::Type{<:AbstractMEstimator}) -> Float64\nefficiency_constant(K::Type{<:AbstractLossShape}, codim::Integer) -> Float64\nefficiency_constant(shape::AbstractLossShape, codim::Integer) -> Float64\n\nThe loss's width as a MULTIPLE OF THE NOISE SIGMA. The one-argument form is the shape's own constant at codimension 1 — a property of its psi, like its influence function — and is what a loss must define to join this interface. The two-argument form lifts it to codimension d through codimension_factor.\n\nA shape type defines it; the instance form efficiency_constant(shape, d) — THE entry point every deployment calls, on the SHAPE it deploys (never on a deployed loss, which carries its multiple as a field) — reads the shape's type. A shape whose instance carries a parameter (BarronShape's α) has no constant: a MethodError.\n\nThe DEFAULT for a deployed efficiency_constant, not the only legitimate value. It solves eff(m, d) = 0.95 at the GAUSSIAN — the width that minimises variance inflation on CLEAN data. A stage running under real contamination may legitimately cross-validate its own operating point, and will land tighter: VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation deploys an efficiency constant ≈ 1.6 per model class where this returns ≈ 3.25, trading efficiency for breakdown. Both are right for their stage.\n\nDefined only for losses that efficiency parameterises: GaussianShape has no width and BarronShape's shape is itself fitted, so neither has one; those raise a MethodError by design. TLSShape likewise. SmoothTLSShape is a truncation rather than an influence function and ships no default efficiency_constant in its constructor, but eff(m, d) = 0.95 IS solvable for its psi, so it does carry a tabulated constant for a caller that wants the efficiency-matched truncation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.efficiency_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_entry_mu0-Tuple{DeployedLoss, Float64, Float64}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"gnc_entry_mu0(loss, r2max, entry) -> Float64\n\nEntry-breadth policy (smooth-and-lift dose axis 1): entry = NaN is the seed-extreme Yang–Carlone init (gnc_mu0 — the rmax entry); a numeric entry = m starts the anneal at m× the TARGET WIDTH, i.e. μ₀ = max(m², 1) (μ multiplies the SQUARED scale c²μ = μ·c̄²). m = 1 is the static limit. Only WidthAnnealed consumes numeric entries — for the other families μ is a convexity or band parameter, not a width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_entry_mu0"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_mu0-Tuple{DeployedLoss, Float64}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"gnc_mu0(loss, r2max) -> Float64\n\nYang–Carlone μ₀ from the SEED residual extreme r2max = r²_max: ConvexityAnnealed (GM) μ₀ = max(2r²max/c̄², 1) (convex start; already ≤ 1 ⇒ true GM); WidthAnnealed (Welsch) μ₀ = max(r²max/c̄², 1) (c₀² = r²max ⇒ min weight e^{−1}); BandAnnealed (TLS) μ₀ = c̄²/(2r²max − c̄²), guarded to the μ cap when r²_max ≤ c̄²/2 (no graduation needed — start effectively hard).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_mu0"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_mu_cap-Tuple{}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"gnc_mu_cap() -> Float64\n\nThe μ at which the TLS band surrogate is effectively hard: the cap gnc_mu0 guards to, and the cap of an ascending GNC μ-ladder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_mu_cap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_surrogate-Tuple{AbstractLossShape}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"gnc_surrogate(loss) -> GncSurrogate\n\nThe loss's graduation family. A shape that does not graduate answers NoSurrogate, and the gnc_* verbs then raise a MethodError rather than silently taking a branch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_surrogate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_target-Tuple{DeployedLoss, Float64}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"gnc_target(loss, x) -> Float64\n\nTARGET (non-surrogate) loss the GNC engine best-tracks on: the shape's own ρ at the terminal width c̄², scaled by its cost_scale — which is 1.0 for every shape but Welsch, whose tracker carries a ½ (exact; the ×½ scaling is bitwise).\n\nONE expression for all three families. The ½ is Welsch's cost convention, a property of the shape (cost_scale), not of its graduation family; TLS routes through ρ like every other shape.\n\nThe dispatch exists ONLY to refuse: a NoSurrogate shape gets no method and raises a MethodError, like every other gnc_* verb, rather than best-tracking a graduation it does not have.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_target"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_tls_weight-Tuple{Float64, Float64, Float64}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"gnc_tls_weight(x, sq_loss_width, μ) -> Float64\n\nThe Black–Rangarajan GNC-TLS surrogate weight at squared residual x = r², truncation bound sq_loss_width = c̄², continuation parameter μ:\n\nw = 1                          x ≤ (μ/(μ+1))·c̄²\nw = c̄·√(μ(μ+1))/|r| − μ        in the middle band\nw = 0                          x ≥ ((μ+1)/μ)·c̄²\n\nContinuous across the band edges (w → 1 below, w → 0 above). μ → ∞ recovers hard truncated least squares; the middle band is the graduation. Points with 0 < w < 1 are the OPEN band — a GNC engine keeps graduating only while the band is populated (the open observation of the LoSchedule contract).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_tls_weight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_weight-Tuple{DeployedLoss, Float64, Float64}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"gnc_weight(loss, x, μ) -> Float64\n\nGNC surrogate weight at squared residual x, terminal bound c̄², rung μ: The μ-scaled families are lift_weight at the annealed width μ·c̄²; a band- annealed shape is gnc_tls_weight.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gnc_weight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_conjugate-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnyLoss, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"lift_conjugate(loss, w) -> Float64\n\nGeman–Reynolds penalty conjugate(w) at the loss's own width, so that ρ(r) = min_w [w·r² + conjugate(w)].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_conjugate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_cost-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnyLoss, Any, Int64}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"lift_cost(loss, r, n) -> Float64\n\nTotal lifted cost over n residuals r, at the loss's own width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_cost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_rho-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnyLoss, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"lift_rho(loss, s) -> Float64\n\nLoss value ρ at squared residual s = r², at the loss's own width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_rho"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_weight-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnyLoss, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"lift_weight(loss, s) -> Float64\n\nIRLS weight ω(s) = ρ′(s) at the loss's own width — the factor multiplying a residual in the weighted normal equations, for EVERY shape. For the liftable shapes it is additionally the argmin of the Geman–Reynolds envelope at squared residual s; for Huber and Gaussian it is just the derivative.\n\nThe single deployed-width weight verb.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_weight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_wmax-Tuple{WelschShape}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"lift_wmax(loss) -> Float64\n\nInlier-limit weight w_max = ω(0) of the loss's IRLS weight function — the upper end of the Geman–Reynolds domain w ∈ (0, w_max]. The lifting variable is normalized: w̄ = z² = w/w_max ∈ (0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_wmax"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_zblock-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AnyLoss, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"lift_zblock(loss, w̄) -> (rinv2, hreg)\n\nThe joint-(θ,z) Gauss–Newton z-block at the loss's own width. The one closed form the lift core (LocalOptimizationDoneRight) consumes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift_zblock"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.liftable-Tuple{AbstractLossShape}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"liftable(shape::AbstractLossShape) -> Bool\n\nWhether the loss has a finite inlier-limit weight w_max = ω(0) and ships the closed-form Geman–Reynolds dual (lift_conjugate/lift_zblock) — i.e. the joint-(θ,z) half-quadratic lift machinery applies. NOT the same axis as bounded: Cauchy is liftable but unbounded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.liftable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_shape-Tuple{DeployedLoss}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"The shape a DeployedLoss deploys — g, without a width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_width-Tuple{DeployedLoss}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"loss_width(loss) -> Float64\n\nThe loss's scale parameter in the residual's own units, efficiency_constant * sqrt(s2): the residual scale at which the loss leaves the quadratic regime (see AbstractLossShape). DERIVED; prefer sq_loss_width, which is what every closed form consumes and which needs no square root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mad_scale-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{D}, T, 1} where T}}, Tuple{D}} where D","page":"Robust Losses","text":"mad_scale(residuals) -> Float64\n\nThe robust noise scale σ̂ of residual VECTORS residuals (StaticVector{d}s, d read from their type): the median of their norms over the median of χ_d, median(‖rᵢ‖) / √quantile(Chisq(d), 0.5) (the median of χ²_d, Distributions'). If r ~ N(0, σ²I_d) then ‖r‖/σ ~ χ_d, so dividing the median norm by that distribution's median inverts to σ; the median gives it 50% breakdown, so the gross errors a loss exists to reject do not inflate it as they would an RMS.\n\nIt centres on ZERO — the model — not on the residuals' own median: residuals are already deviations from a fitted model, and a norm has no centre to subtract. So it measures a BIAS AS NOISE (residuals centred at 0.9 with σ = 0.37 give 1.33): feed it residuals of a model that fits.\n\nThe codimension is part of the estimator: a 2-D residual's norm is Rayleigh, its median 1.1774σ, a scalar residual's 0.6745σ; reading 2-D norms as d = 1 returns 1.745× the true σ, and every downstream width inherits it. A scalar residual is a StaticVector{1}.\n\nThere is no public norms form: a reader of mad_scale(norms, d) could not tell whether the norms are re-centred. A kernel that truly holds only the norms (an angle residual, a lift workspace's norms) calls the stack-internal _mad_scale_norms(; norms, codimension) (the owner's ruling, 2026-11-11).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mad_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rescale-Tuple{DeployedLoss, Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"rescale(loss, s2) -> DeployedLoss\n\nThe same shape and efficiency constant at a new noise VARIANCE — the SelfScaling update, and what a moving-scale lift arm calls once per iteration. efficiency_constant is not a function of the data and does not move.\n\nGoes through deploy rather than rebuilding the attribute tuple, so the (efficiency_constant, s2) schema is written in exactly one place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.rescale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retune-Tuple{DeployedLoss, Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"retune(loss, efficiency_constant) -> DeployedLoss\n\nThe same shape and noise variance at a new efficiency constant — a caller replacing the derived efficiency constant with a cross-validated operating point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.retune"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_s2-Tuple{DeployedLoss}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"The noise variance s2 a DeployedLoss runs at (see scale_s2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.scale_s2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sq_loss_width-Tuple{DeployedLoss}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"sq_loss_width(loss) -> Float64\n\nThe squared loss_width, efficiency_constant^2 * s2 — the quantity the shape's closed forms are written in, and the one this type exists to make exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sq_loss_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.target_loss-Tuple{DeployedLoss}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"target_loss(loss) -> DeployedLoss\n\nThe homotopy's endpoint. A deployed loss IS the target; an annealed one unwraps to it. GNC graduates at sq_loss_width but best-tracks against this.\n\nNOT target: a target in this stack is a calibration target (AbstractTarget, PosedTarget) — a different concept that must not share a name with this one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.target_loss"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weight-Tuple{WelschShape, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"weight(loss, s, sq_loss_width) -> Float64\n\nIRLS weight ω(s) = dρ/ds (unnormalized; ω(0) = w_max). TOTAL over the family, like ρ it differentiates. For the LIFTABLE shapes it is additionally the argmin of the Geman–Reynolds envelope at s, and the weight-from-z map of the lift is w = w_max·z²; for the other three there is no envelope, only the derivative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.weight"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.zblock-Tuple{WelschShape, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"zblock(shape, w̄, sq_loss_width) -> (rinv2, hreg)\n\nThe two z-block quantities of the joint-(θ,z) Gauss-Newton step at normalized weight w̄ = z² (the lift keeps w̄ ∈ (0, 1]; the forms are also valid on the continuation w̄ > 1), scale sq_loss_width = c²:\n\nrinv2 = −κ̄²′(w̄) — the inverse-weight map r²(w̄) (≥ 0 on w̄ ≤ 1), where κ̄²(w̄) = conjugate(w_max·w̄)/w_max is the normalized conjugate; enters the z-gradient gz = z(r² − rinv2).\nhreg = w̄·rinv2²/κ̄²(w̄) — the GN z-block Hessian (dκ/dz)²; enters the Schur pivot dᵢ = 1/(r² + hreg + μ).\n\nLosses with a cancelling ratio return the simplified closed form (Geman–McClure: hreg = c² constant; smooth-TLS: hreg = 2c²w̄). For Welsch, Cauchy and Barron both rinv2 and κ̄² vanish at w̄ = 1 while hreg tends to a POSITIVE limit, 2κ̄²″(1) (2c² for Welsch and Cauchy, 4c²·b/(2 − α) for Barron); near w̄ = 1 they evaluate the ratio from its series, so hreg is accurate there and exact at w̄ = 1, where the lift's z clamp puts every confident inlier. That value is Zach & Bourmaud's Taylor limit 2γ″(1) of the lifted Gauss-Newton block (ECCV 2018, Eq. 10; γ = κ̄²/2), the same for every shape, so Barron's anchors (α → −∞ ≡ Welsch, α = −2 ≡ Geman–McClure, α = 0 ≡ Cauchy) hold for zblock at the clamp as everywhere else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.zblock"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ρ-Tuple{WelschShape, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"ρ(loss, s, sq_loss_width) -> Float64\n\nLoss value ρ at squared residual s = r², scale sq_loss_width = c² — the LOSS a residual pays, in the convention ρ′(0) = w_max.\n\nTOTAL over the family. For the liftable shapes ρ is also the Geman–Reynolds envelope min_w [w·s + conjugate(w)], but nothing about a loss REQUIRES a lift — GaussianShape is s, HuberShape is the monotone elbow, TLSShape is the hard truncation min(s, sq_loss_width). Scoping ρ to the liftable ones made ρ(GaussianShape(), …) throw while ψ(GaussianShape(), …) answered, which is incoherent: ψ is ρ′. Only conjugate and zblock stay lift-only, because only they need the auxiliary variable to exist.\n\nThe bounded shapes saturate at sq_loss_width — the well depth — so 1 - ρ/sq_loss_width is a normalised quality in [0, 1]. GaussianShape and HuberShape are unbounded and have no depth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ρ"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ψ-Tuple{AbstractLossShape, Any, Any}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"ψ(loss, r, sq_loss_width) -> Float64\n\nINFLUENCE FUNCTION ρ′(r) at residual r (NOT squared) and scale sq_loss_width = c² — ψ in the M-estimation sense (Huber, Hampel, Maronna–Martin–Yohai).\n\nThis is the function the width contract is defined against: a shape's efficiency_constant is the c solving eff(m, 1) = 0.95 for\n\neff(m, d) = E[ψ′ + (d-1)ψ/r]² / (d · E[ψ²]),   r ~ chi_d\n\nso the solved table (Welsch 2.9846, Geman-McClure 3.7874, Cauchy 2.3849, …) is a claim ABOUT THIS FUNCTION, and exposing it is what makes that claim checkable rather than a comment.\n\nDerived, never a second closed form per shape, via the defining relation of IRLS: the weight IS w(r) = ψ(r)/r, so ψ(r) = r·w(r). Built directly on weight, which covers the whole family.\n\nConstant factors do not matter here: eff is invariant under ψ → αψ (the numerator is squared, the denominator is E[ψ²]), which is why the table holds despite ρ carrying this package's depth convention rather than the classical one.\n\nNot to be confused with conjugate, the half-quadratic penalty that the Geman–Reynolds literature also calls ψ — that one takes a WEIGHT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ψ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EfficiencyConstant","page":"Robust Losses","text":"EfficiencyConstant()\nEfficiencyConstant(m::Real)\n\nA width as a multiple of the estimated scale (see WidthAxis). Bare, it is the AXIS (EfficiencyConstant() === EfficiencyConstant(nothing)); with a value it is a local optimizer's width MODE: the loss width m·ŝ, ŝ the scorer's consensus scale — m the plain multiple for every loss shape. m must be positive and finite: a strategy that follows the scorer takes no width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EfficiencyConstant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LossWidth","page":"Robust Losses","text":"LossWidth()\nLossWidth(w::Real)\nLossWidth(efficiency_constant::Real, s2::Real)\n\nA robust loss's scale parameter, its loss_width (see WidthAxis). Bare, it is the AXIS; with a value it is a local optimizer's ABSOLUTE width mode, stored FACTORED as (efficiency_constant, s2) so that the loss deploys at exactly deploy(shape, s2; efficiency_constant) — LossWidth(w) is (1.0, w²) (sq_loss_width = 1.0²·w² === w²), and LossWidth(m, s2) keeps the two factors apart for a reader of s2 alone (a GNC entry, Barron's α fit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LossWidth"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OutlierHalfwidth","page":"Robust Losses","text":"OutlierHalfwidth()\nOutlierHalfwidth(a::Real)\n\nThe Student-t marginal's uniform outlier half-width (see WidthAxis). Bare, it is the AXIS; with a value it is the width a scoring is built at (with_width(scoring, OutlierHalfwidth(a))). Never a local optimizer's width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OutlierHalfwidth"},{"category":"type","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WidthAxis","page":"Robust Losses","text":"WidthAxis\n\nThe width quantity a value is expressed in — the axis it is tuned on and persisted under:\n\nEfficiencyConstant: a width as a multiple of the estimated scale ŝ, dimensionless (width = efficiency_constant · ŝ), partition-anchored;\nLossWidth: a robust loss's scale parameter (loss_width), the residual scale where the loss leaves its quadratic regime — a threshold score's threshold is its truncated loss's width;\nOutlierHalfwidth: the Student-t marginal's uniform outlier half-width.\n\nwidth_axis reads one from its persisted name and width_axis_name writes it; to_native maps an efficiency constant onto a native axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WidthAxis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_width-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LossWidth{Tuple{Float64, Float64}}}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"loss_width(w::LossWidth) -> Float64\n\nThe absolute loss width a LossWidth value carries, efficiency_constant·√s2 of its factored (efficiency_constant, s2) — w itself for LossWidth(w) (1.0·√(w²) == w in IEEE arithmetic).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_width_to_outlier_halfwidth-Tuple{Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"loss_width_to_outlier_halfwidth(loss_width; operating_efficiency_constant, d_g) -> Float64\n\nInverse of outlier_halfwidth_to_loss_width: the marginal's outlier half-width whose decision boundary matches a truncation at loss_width, at the operating efficiency constant. The literal reciprocal around the same _unit_halfwidth, so the two are inverses by construction. d_g is required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.loss_width_to_outlier_halfwidth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.native_map-Tuple{Union{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LossWidth, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OutlierHalfwidth}}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"native_map(axis::Union{LossWidth, OutlierHalfwidth}; d_g) -> (efficiency_constant, scale) -> Float64\n\nCurried to_native on axis at codimension d_g — THE per-axis map closure every window and coverage grid is cut with. d_g is required for both axes (the loss-width axis ignores it), so a grid and the window cut inside it are always cut at the same codimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.native_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_halfwidth_to_loss_width-Tuple{Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"outlier_halfwidth_to_loss_width(outlier_halfwidth; operating_efficiency_constant, d_g) -> Float64\n\nThe truncation loss width at the SAME effective decision boundary as the Student-t marginal's outlier_halfwidth, holding the operating efficiency constant fixed: ŝ_implied = outlier_halfwidth / _unit_halfwidth(operating_efficiency_constant, d_g), then loss_width = operating_efficiency_constant·ŝ_implied. d_g is required (the crossover moves with it). operating_efficiency_constant is caller policy — a PROTOCOL choice (compare methods, not parameterisations).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.outlier_halfwidth_to_loss_width"},{"category":"function","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_native","page":"Robust Losses","text":"to_native(axis::WidthAxis, efficiency_constant, scale; d_g) -> Float64\n\nAn efficiency constant as a method's NATIVE width at the estimated scale: the width on axis whose inlier/outlier decision flips at the residual efficiency_constant · scale — one conversion, decision_width, of the axis's decision model. Each native axis's method lives with its model:\n\nLossWidth (here): the truncated loss TLSShape(), which flips at its width, so the native width is efficiency_constant · scale itself (MSAC, RANSAC, GaU, MAGSAC++, PoseLib's truncation);\nOutlierHalfwidth: through GauInlier (the Student-t marginal's crossover).\n\nd_g is the residual codimension of the problem (1 for E and F, 2 for H). It has NO default: a native grid and the window cut inside it must be cut at the same d_g, so every caller states it. The loss-width axis does not depend on it; the outlier half-width does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_native"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_native-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OutlierHalfwidth, Real, Real}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"to_native(::OutlierHalfwidth, efficiency_constant, scale; d_g) -> Float64\n\nAn efficiency constant as the Student-t marginal's native width: the outlier half-width whose MAP inlier/outlier decision flips at the residual efficiency_constant · scale — decision_width of the Gaussian-uniform mixture GauInlier(scale²; d_g), the stack-wide definition of the crossover (moved from RANSACScoringDoneRight). scale is the estimated inlier scale (linear, in the units of the residuals), and the result is in those units.\n\nd_g is the residual codimension (1 for E and F, 2 for H) and has no default: it is the EXPONENT of the outlier density, so it moves the outlier half-width by decades (at scale = 1, an efficiency constant of 6 maps to 8.2293e7 at d_g = 1 and to 1.0156e4 at d_g = 2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.to_native"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_axis-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"width_axis(name::Union{AbstractString,Symbol}) -> WidthAxis\n\nThe width axis a persisted name denotes: \"efficiency_constant\" → EfficiencyConstant, \"loss_width\" → LossWidth, \"outlier_halfwidth\" → OutlierHalfwidth (a string, as TOML and CLI values read, or a symbol, as grid keys are). Any other name is an ArgumentError — an abbreviation is never read as a width. The inverse of width_axis_name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_axis_name-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EfficiencyConstant}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"width_axis_name(axis::WidthAxis) -> Symbol\n\nThe persisted name of a width axis — the key of its grid, its sidecar kind, its TOML and CLI value: :efficiency_constant, :loss_width, :outlier_halfwidth (String of it where a string is written), whatever value the width carries. The inverse of width_axis on a bare axis: width_axis(width_axis_name(a)) === a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_axis_name"},{"category":"method","location":"api/robust_losses/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_value-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.WidthAxis}","page":"Robust Losses","text":"The value a width carries (nothing for the bare axis).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.width_value"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/design/#Design:-Attributed{V,A}-Composition","page":"Design: Attributed{V,A}","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore uses a single composition primitive — Attributed{V,A} — to wrap any value with metadata. This replaces deep inheritance hierarchies with a flat, type-stable pattern used uniformly across the toolkit: feature detection (VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures), robust estimation (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation), and the geometric utilities in this package.","title":"Design: Attributed{V,A} Composition"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/design/#The-Pattern","page":"Design: Attributed{V,A}","text":"struct Attributed{V,A}\n    value::V       # The geometric/mathematical object\n    attributes::A  # Metadata about how it was computed\nend\n\nProperty access is forwarded via @generated getproperty:\n\nr.value, r.attributes → direct field access\nNamed fields of A → forwarded from attributes\nOther symbols → forwarded to the value\n\nThis means an Attributed result can be treated as if it were the value itself:\n\np = Attributed(Point2(320.5, 240.5), 1.35)   # a depth-attributed pixel\np.value        # the Point2\np.attributes   # the depth","title":"The Pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/design/#Uses-in-this-package","page":"Design: Attributed{V,A}","text":"Depth-attributed points. Attributed{Point2, T} is the currency of multi-view transfer: transfer(rig, p) consumes a pixel with its depth and returns the target-view pixel with the transported depth (see Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty).\nDetections. AbstractDetection is the alias Attributed{<:Union{FeatureFrame, Uncertain{<:FeatureFrame}}} — a geometric frame (Disc, EllipticFrame, …), bare or with its covariance, with detector metadata; feature_frame reads the bare frame of either kind. The detectors live in VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures; the geometry and the alias live here so downstream packages agree on the type.\nFit results do NOT use Attributed. They return a FitResult: a fixed core (estimate, retcode, stop_rule, iterations, rms, n) plus solver-specific metadata selected by type parameter. It forwards properties into its metadata with the same @generated mechanism described below, so r.weights reads like sc.first does — Attributed with a fixed core, not a second paradigm.\nScored correspondences. ScoredCorrespondence is Attributed{Pair, Float64} — even scalar metadata uses the same pattern.\nPer-datum weights. A value carried between stages carries its weight as Attributed(x, (; weight)) — a homography's vote in zhang_intrinsics, a relative rotation's in spectral_rotation_averaging, a view-graph edge's — and a stage reads it with weight_of (1.0 for a bare value) and the value with value_of: never a parallel weights vector.\n\nDownstream packages use Attributed for their own value-plus-attributes pairs and FitResult for their estimator results (RANSAC estimates, IRLS refinements, calibration fits), supplying their own concrete metadata types; the aliases differ, the mechanism does not.","title":"Uses in this package"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/design/#Uncertain{V,M}-—-an-Attributed-whose-attributes-are-a-statistical-form","page":"Design: Attributed{V,A}","text":"Uncertainty is metadata with laws, but it is still metadata: Uncertain{V,M} is the alias Attributed{V, M} with M <: Union{CovMat, PrecMat} — a geometric value with a typed CovMat or PrecMat as its attributes, not a second wrapper. The matrix's nominal type records which form is stored; the cov/prec accessors return whichever form is asked for, inverting at most once at the boundary, and u.matrix reads the stored form. Since 0.9 the matrices carry a frame label checked by propagate — see Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty.","title":"Uncertain{V,M} — an Attributed whose attributes are a statistical form"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/design/#Why-This-Design?","page":"Design: Attributed{V,A}","text":"Flat over deep. No AbstractResult → ... hierarchy: two parametric types cover every case — Attributed{V, A} for a value with attributes, FitResult{P, M} for an estimate with a fixed diagnostic core — and dispatch on Attributed{ScoredCorrespondence, Float64} or FitResult{CameraModel, M} is exactly as efficient as on a dedicated struct.\n\nZero-cost forwarding. The @generated getproperty compiles to a chain of === :field comparisons — the same as hand-written field access.\n\nUniform interface. Every result has .value and .attributes; code that filters results works the same whether the result came from a detector, a solver, or a fitting loop.\n\nComposable. Attributes can nest — a pipeline result can carry its source detection (itself an Attributed) inside its attributes, and property forwarding handles the chain transparently.","title":"Why This Design?"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sphere/#Ray-Maps-and-Frame-Transitions","page":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions","text":"A central lens does not \"distort 2D coordinates\" — it re-orients rays. The familiar 2D distortion polynomial of a lens model is the conjugation\n\nsection ∘ D ∘ lift\n\nof a self-map D: S² → S² of the view's spherical retina, expressed in the model's defining frame (lift takes a point of that 2D frame onto the sphere, section takes a ray back into it). Since 0.11, ray_map exposes D itself.","title":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sphere/#ray_map-—-the-sphere-self-map","page":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions","text":"D = ray_map(lens)          # RayMap{ViewFrame{:_}, C, typeof(lens)}\nv′ = D(v)                  # any ray representative in; unit bearing vector out\ninv(D)                     # InverseRayMap — the undistort conjugation\n\nRayMap is the framed stage {ViewFrame{id} → ViewFrame{id}}: both endpoints are the view's 3D frame, so D needs no 2D frame tag — the type parameter C only records which defining frame the conjugation runs through (section then the in-frame distortion then lift). Input norm is irrelevant (the frame projections are scale-invariant on ray representatives); output is always the unit bearing vector.\n\nWhy D has an honest inv when the projections do not. Restricted to the sphere, the retinal-frame projections are sections — invertible — unlike the fiber-collapsing full 3D → 2D projections (FramedPerspective and friends), which deliberately have no inv. So InverseRayMap = lift ∘ undistort ∘ section is a genuine inverse, minted by inv and inverting back.\n\nFor identity-in-frame lenses (Pinhole, the pure fisheye projections), D is the identity — still framed, still returning the unit representative. D exists only for central lenses (every VisualGeometryToolkitCore lens model): a finite or thick pupil has no sphere self-map, because its rays do not share a center — that regime is ray tracing, downstream.","title":"ray_map — the sphere self-map"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sphere/#Honest-domains","page":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions","text":"The conjugation is defined exactly on the defining frame's domain. Rays outside it throw a DomainError — not a sentinel, not a silently aliased value:\n\ngnomonic (GnomonicFrame): forward hemisphere Z > 0 only;\nequidistant / stereographic: every ray but the exact backward direction;\nequisolid: the whole sphere;\northographic: θ ∈ [0, π/2] only.","title":"Honest domains"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sphere/#The-orthographic-fold-—-the-cautionary-example","page":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions","text":"The orthographic frame radius r = sin θ rises to 1 at the equator and then decreases: past 90° every radius value is taken twice. The frame folds — a ray with Z < 0 is not \"a large radius\", it is outside the frame's domain, and the section/lift machinery throws rather than aliasing it onto a forward ray (the lift always selects the forward sheet cos θ = +√(1 − r²)). This is the poster child for the honest-domain discipline: the alternative is a silently wrong forward ray.","title":"The orthographic fold — the cautionary example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sphere/#frame_transition-—-first-class-frame-hops","page":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions","text":"The transition between any two retinal frames of a view is a pure radial reparameterization of the sphere. frame_transition(C1, C2) mints it as the framed stage FrameTransition{C1, C2}, implemented once through the sphere (section(Dst) ∘ lift(Src)) — every pair among the five frames is served by one implementation, with no per-pair closed forms to keep consistent:\n\nt = frame_transition(EquidistantFrame{:_}, GnomonicFrame{:_})\nt(Vec2(θ, 0.0))            # == Vec2(tan(θ), 0.0)\ninv(t)                     # the reverse transition\n\nThis names what production code usually hides inline: OpenCV's cv::fisheye::undistortPoints ends with the line scale = tan(θ)/θ_d — that line is frame_transition(EquidistantFrame{id}, GnomonicFrame{id}), the equidistant → gnomonic frame change applied after the polynomial θ-inverse, unnamed and unchecked in the C++. Here it is a framed stage with checked junctions (the view ids must match — a transition between frames of different views is a MethodError) and honest domain edges. Being isbits, a FrameTransition composes into a per-point map that pointwise! runs over a batch, on any backend.","title":"frame_transition — first-class frame hops"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sphere/#See-also","page":"Ray Maps and Frame Transitions","text":"The Framed Imaging Chain — the frame table and stage family\nBack-projection and Undistortion — the lens contract and its inverse maps\nImaging Chain and Stereo API","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"The works cited in these docs and docstrings.\n\nChum, O.; Werner, T. and Matas, J. (2004). Epipolar Geometry Estimation via RANSAC Benefits from the Oriented Epipolar Constraint. In: International Conference on Pattern Recognition (ICPR), Vol. 1; pp. 112–115.\n\n\n\nDevernay, F. and Faugeras, O. (2001). Straight Lines Have to Be Straight. Machine Vision and Applications 13, 14–24.\n\n\n\nHartley, R. and Zisserman, A. (2003). Multiple View Geometry in Computer Vision. 2nd Edition (Cambridge University Press).\n\n\n\nKhomutenko, B.; Garcia, G. and Martinet, P. (2016). An Enhanced Unified Camera Model. IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters 1, 137–144.\n\n\n\nMalis, E. and Vargas, M. (2007). Deeper Understanding of the Homography Decomposition for Vision-Based Control. Research Report RR-6303 (INRIA).\n\n\n\nMei, C. and Rives, P. (2007). Single View Point Omnidirectional Camera Calibration from Planar Grids. In: IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA); pp. 3945–3950.\n\n\n\nUsenko, V.; Demmel, N. and Cremers, D. (2018). The Double Sphere Camera Model. In: International Conference on 3D Vision (3DV); pp. 552–560.\n\n\n\nWadenbäck, M.; Valtonen Örnhag, M. and Edstedt, J. (2026). Radially Distorted Homographies, Revisited. In: International Conference on 3D Vision (3DV); pp. 52–62, arXiv:2508.21190.\n\n\n\nZhang, Z. (2000). A Flexible New Technique for Camera Calibration. IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence 22, 1330–1334.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cameras/#Cameras-API","page":"Cameras","text":"Cameras, camera models, intrinsics, and sensors. The grid cameras (a SplineLens, a NoncentralSplineModel) are on the Spline Lens API page; calibration (the radial camera, intrinsics from homographies, view-angle limits) is on the Calibration API page; lens models and the lens contract with its batched surfaces are on the Lenses API page; the framed imaging chain, ray maps, and stereo rigs are on the Imaging Chain and Stereo API page. See the Cameras guide.","title":"Cameras API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cameras/#Cameras-and-Camera-Models","page":"Cameras","text":"","title":"Cameras and Camera Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cameras/#Intrinsics-and-Sensors","page":"Cameras","text":"The intrinsics and sensor types are on the Intrinsics and Sensors API page.","title":"Intrinsics and Sensors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cameras/#Boards-and-canvases","page":"Cameras","text":"A planar target's Board is the sensor's analogue: the printed board's size (lengths, or plain numbers for a unitless target) and its corner in the design chart; Rect(board) is its domain there, as Rect(sensor) is the image's in pixels. The Canvas is the printed page the board lies on, with the image printed on it (CanvasTexture: a relative path, its SHA-256 and the rectangle it covers) — canvas ⊃ board ⊃ pattern, all rectangles of the one design chart the Canvas docstring states.","title":"Boards and canvases"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cameras/#The-overlap-of-a-rotating-camera's-views","page":"Cameras","text":"view_overlap measures, for every pair of frames of a camera turning about its centre, the solid angle of the directions both image and its share of each frame's footprint: the overlap a panorama's pair selection reads.","title":"The overlap of a rotating camera's views"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cameras/#Index","page":"Cameras","text":"Pages = [\"cameras.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras","page":"Cameras","text":"Cameras\n\nCamera system submodule for VisualGeometryToolkitCore.\n\nProvides complete camera modeling including:\n\nSensors: Physical CMOS sensor specifications\nIntrinsics: Calibration matrices and intrinsic parameters (physical/logical)\nLens Models: forward lenses (Lens: a projection + a distortion in its frame) and lifts (LiftLens)\nCamera Models: Composable camera models (intrinsics + lens)\nCameras: Complete cameras in 3D space (model + pose)\nStereo: Stereo camera rigs and epipolar geometry\nUtilities: Helper functions (focal_length, lookat, P3P solvers)\n\nMain Types\n\nPosedCamera, PosedPinholeCamera\nCameraModel\nLogicalIntrinsics\nLens, LiftLens, Pinhole, KannalaBrandt, Division\nSensor, CMOS_SENSORS\nStereoRig\n\nMain Functions\n\nproject, backproject, unproject\nfocal_length, lookat, pose\np3p, sample_p3p\ndefault_frustum_depth\n\nExample\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras\n\ns = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")\nf = focal_length(s, deg2rad(40.0))\nmodel = CameraModel(f, s.pitch, centroid(Rect(s)) .* px)\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, lookat(...))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundedCamera","page":"Cameras","text":"BoundedCamera{C<:AbstractCamera, B, R} <: AbstractCamera\n\nA wrapper around any AbstractCamera that adds pixel-space image bounds (width × height) without disturbing the underlying camera's intrinsics or units.  Used by SfM/MVS dataset readers (ETH3D, ScanNet, COLMAP exports) where the camera is described by its calibration plus an explicit image extent — information the bare PosedCamera type doesn't retain.\n\nThe wrapped camera may be UNPOSED — a bare model, is_posed(bc) == false — which is COLMAP's \"camera\" row (intrinsics, lens and image size, no pose) and the element of a stored camera collection (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's cameras kind). BoundedCamera(PosedCamera(model(bc), pose), bc.bound; lens_coverage = lens_coverage(bc)) is its posed counterpart; the pose accessors of an unposed one are an ArgumentError. is_posed is the wrapped camera's.\n\nThe bound\n\nBoundedCamera(camera, extent::Size2) bounds by a pixel extent; BoundedCamera(camera, sensor::Sensor) by the physical sensor, whose resolution is the extent and whose pitch (a length per pixel) carries a lens datasheet's millimetres into pixels (image_circle). Either way extent(bc) is the Size2{Int} pixel extent; sensor(bc) is the Sensor, or nothing for the extent form. The bound is ONE field, bc.bound: the extent, or the sensor whose resolution is the extent.\n\nThe lens coverage\n\nBoundedCamera(camera, bound; lens_coverage = region) — where light reaches the sensor, in pixels: a Circle or an Ellipse (the lens's image circle, measured with a detector or from a datasheet), or a Rect2; any real element type, converted to Float64, and kept as the kind given. Omitted, it is the sensor's own Rect: the whole sensor is lit. lens_coverage(bc) reads it, with_lens_coverage replaces it. What the camera images is imaged_region(bc): the sensor ∩ the lens coverage ∩ where the lens model is valid.\n\nField access\n\nbc.camera, bc.bound, bc.lens_coverage — direct fields; read them through model, extent/sensor and lens_coverage.\n\nMethod delegation\n\nA BoundedCamera is THE type for pixel operations: every camera verb a CameraModel answers is forwarded to the wrapped camera (MacroTools.@forward, and @inline project/backproject for device kernels) — the maps and their Jacobians (project, backproject, backproject_with_jacobian, unproject, is_in_front, constraint_with_jacobians), the accessors (intrinsics, lens, pose, extrinsics, focal_length, model, calibration_matrix), the imaging and its domain (imaging_transform, imaging_chain, distortion_map, valid_domain, valid_rect, undistort, …) and the camera-derived solvers (p3p, transfer, radial_homography); so backproject_with_jacobian(bc, u) == backproject_with_jacobian(bc.camera, u). The bound applies to none of them (the extent is image_circle, imaged_region and lens_coverage). The model constructors (pinhole, window, with_parameters) and the parameter protocol are the model's: call them on model(bc), and rebound with with_model.\n\nTrait\n\nbounds_trait(::BoundedCamera) = HasBounds() — methods that need image extent dispatch on BoundsTrait: extent(bc) is the bound's pixel extent.\n\nA device kernel's camera\n\nThe bound's kind and the region's are the type parameters B (Size2{Int}, or the stored Sensor type) and R (Rect2{Float64}, Circle{Float64} or Ellipse{Float64}), so a bounded camera is concrete, and isbits whenever the wrapped camera is (a CameraModel of an isbits lens): it travels into a GPU kernel as it is, its backproject/project the wrapped camera's. convert_eltype(Float32, bc) converts the wrapped camera and keeps the bound and the region.\n\nExample\n\ninner = PosedCamera(CameraModel(K; lens=Pinhole()), pose)\nbc    = BoundedCamera(inner, Size2(6048, 4032))\nintrinsics(bc)     # forwarded → CameraCalibrationMat\nextent(bc)         # Size2(6048, 4032)\nlens_coverage(bc)     # Rect2(0, 0, 6048, 4032): the whole sensor is lit\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BoundedCamera"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.OrientedCamera","page":"Cameras","text":"OrientedCamera{M}\n\nA camera that only turns about its centre: a PosedCamera whose extrinsics are a RotationMap{3}, PosedCamera{M, <:RotationMap{3}} (an alias, not a type of its own). It has an orientation and no position: extrinsics(c) is the world → camera rotation, pose(c) its inverse, and project, backproject and unproject act about the origin. Build it from the rotation of either direction: PosedCamera(model, relabel(CameraFrame, WorldFrame, RotationMap(R))) takes a camera → world orientation and inverts it, an unlabeled or world → camera RotationMap is stored as the extrinsics. What needs a position refuses one by name: a rig member, the plane-induced homography and the verbs on plane coordinates (project of a plane conic or rectangle, transfer_ellipse, HomographyMat, RadialCamera), and a non-central grid model's pose. The frames of a panorama are oriented cameras, as view_overlap takes them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.OrientedCamera"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera","page":"Cameras","text":"PosedCamera{M, Rt} <: AbstractCamera\n\nA camera in 3D space, consisting of a camera model (intrinsics + lens) and extrinsics (pose).\n\nDispatch on the lens through the model parameter: PosedCamera{<:CameraModel{<:Any, <:MyLens}} (the PosedPinholeCamera alias is that form for Pinhole).\n\nUse project(camera, X) to map a 3D world point to 2D pixel coordinates, and backproject(camera, u) / unproject(camera, u, depth) for the inverse directions. The transform is computed on demand and inlined.\n\nType parameters\n\nM <: AbstractCamera: an UNPOSED camera model (CameraModel, or a grid model); PosedCamera(model, X) refuses a posed one (is_posed(model)) and a BoundedCamera — the bound goes outside the pose, BoundedCamera(PosedCamera(model, X), extent)\nRt <: Union{EuclideanMap, RotationMap{3}}: extrinsics type. A RotationMap poses a camera that only turns about its centre, an OrientedCamera; a camera turns in three dimensions, so a RotationMap{2} is a MethodError at construction.\n\nFields\n\nmodel::M: Camera model (intrinsics + lens)\nextrinsics::Rt: Camera extrinsics (world-to-camera transform)\n\nExamples\n\n# Create camera with logical intrinsics\nmodel = CameraModel(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\nextrinsics = EuclideanMap(RotMatrix(I), [0.0, 0.0, 0.0])\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, extrinsics)\n\n# Dispatch on lens (via the model parameter; PosedPinholeCamera is the\n# ready-made alias for the Pinhole case)\nfoo(::PosedPinholeCamera) = \"pinhole path\"\nfoo(::PosedCamera{<:CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:KannalaBrandt}}) = \"fisheye path\"\n\n# Project a world point\nX_world = SVector(1.0mm, 2.0mm, 10.0mm)\nu = project(camera, X_world)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera, CameraPose{T} where T}","page":"Cameras","text":"PosedCamera(model::CameraModel, e::CameraExtrinsics) -> PosedCamera\nPosedCamera(model::CameraModel, p::CameraPose) -> PosedCamera\n\nConstruct a PosedCamera from a frame-labeled map, so the caller states which frame it holds instead of relying on the positional convention.\n\nThe bare positional constructor PosedCamera(model, extrinsics::EuclideanMap) with an UNLABELED map takes the world-to-camera map (documented convention, unchecked); a caller holding a camera-to-world map (a lookat-style pose, a renderer's sensor.to_world) who supplies it positionally stores the WRONG map without any error — every accessor then silently answers for the inverse camera. With frame labels that mistake is unrepresentable: CameraExtrinsics (being the extrinsics-labeled EuclideanMap) stores directly into the extrinsics slot via the default constructor, CameraPose inverts internally, and pose(PosedCamera(model, P::CameraPose)) ≈ P.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}, Union{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:FlexUnits.Quantity, 1}}}} where T","page":"Cameras","text":"PosedCamera(K::StaticMatrix{3,3}, R::StaticMatrix{3,3}, t::StaticVector{3};\n       check=true, project=false) -> PosedCamera\n\nBoundary constructor taking raw matrix data (typical for SfM dataset loaders). R is wrapped via the EuclideanMap(::SMatrix, ...) path which validates (and optionally snaps via project=true) before promoting to RotMatrix. t is plain numbers of K's element type, or lengths (t .* m), which give a physical camera.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedPinholeCamera","page":"Cameras","text":"PosedPinholeCamera\n\nCameras whose lens is exactly Pinhole — the alias for lens dispatch now that PosedCamera carries only {M, Rt} (the former {I, L, Rt, M} repeated what M already determines).  Consumed by the induced-homography constructors in geometry/cameras/homography.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedPinholeCamera"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_matrix-Tuple{PosedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"calibration_matrix(camera::PosedCamera) -> SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}\n\nReturn the 3×3 calibration matrix K as a plain SMatrix. For a SplineLens camera K is the lens's gauge — the pinhole at the pixel imaging ẑ (skew 0) — kept as it is by every fit.\n\nExample\n\ncamera = PosedCamera(CameraModel(; focal=800.0, pp=(320.0, 240.0)), ext)\nK = calibration_matrix(camera)  # [800 0 320; 0 800 240; 0 0 1]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.default_frustum_depth-Tuple{PosedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"default_frustum_depth(camera::PosedCamera) -> Float64\n\nDefault frustum depth for visualization: fx from K, a natural depth scale in the camera's working units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.default_frustum_depth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_in_front-Tuple{PosedCamera, AbstractArray, AbstractArray, AbstractArray}","page":"Cameras","text":"is_in_front(camera, x, y, z) -> AbstractArray{Bool}\n\nBroadcasted component-array (SoA) form of the chirality test: world-frame point components in, camera-frame z > 0 mask out. Per-element semantics match is_in_front(camera, Point3(xᵢ, yᵢ, zᵢ)); the component layout keeps the whole test one fused broadcast on GPU arrays (hoisted from the RayTracedCalibration GPU pipeline).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_in_front"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_in_front-Tuple{PosedCamera, Point3}","page":"Cameras","text":"is_in_front(camera, point::Point3) -> Bool\n\nChirality test: returns true if point is in front of the camera (z > 0 in camera coords).\n\nBroadcasts over points:\n\nmask = is_in_front.(Ref(camera), world_points)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_in_front"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_coverage-Tuple{BoundedCamera, Real}","page":"Cameras","text":"lens_coverage(camera::BoundedCamera, rim_margin::Real) -> Union{Rect2{Float64}, Circle{Float64}, Ellipse{Float64}, Nothing}\n\nThe camera's lens_coverage less rim_margin pixels at the LENS's edge (an image circle's soft rim), or nothing when nothing is left: a Circle of radius r − rim_margin, an Ellipse with both semi-axes less rim_margin, a Rect inset by rim_margin on each side that lies inside the sensor — a side on or past the sensor's edge is the sensor's, not the lens's, and stays, so a camera whose whole sensor is lit loses nothing. The kind it was given; a margin of 0 returns the coverage. Where a consumer counts a pixel as lit away from the rim: imaged_region(with_lens_coverage(camera, c)) is what such a camera images (with with_lens_coverage), and a Circle's c.r the radius it reaches from the circle's centre.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_coverage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_coverage-Tuple{BoundedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"lens_coverage(camera::BoundedCamera) -> Union{Rect2{Float64}, Circle{Float64}, Ellipse{Float64}}\n\nWhere light reaches the camera's sensor, in its pixels: the lens's image circle (a Circle or an Ellipse, measured or from a datasheet), or the sensor's own Rect when the whole sensor is lit — the kind it was given, in Float64. Not where the lens MODEL is valid — that is valid_domain; what the camera images is both, inside the sensor: imaged_region(camera).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lens_coverage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.plane_map-Tuple{PosedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"plane_map(camera::PosedCamera) -> CoordinateTransformations.Transformation\n\nThe board-plane → pixel map of a posed camera, for every camera model: the z = 0 plane of its world (a planar target's design plane, in its units) lifted to 3-D (q ↦ (q, 0), the target surfaces' plane lift), the camera's rigid extrinsics, then its model's projection, project(camera.model, ·) as a map (the forward twin of Backprojection). plane_map(camera)(q) is project(camera, [q; 0]), and transform_deriv(plane_map(camera), q) its 2 × 2 Jacobian with respect to the plane point, the composition's chain rule (the projection's by ForwardDiff through project, a spline model's by the implicit function theorem).\n\nFor a central CameraModel the same map, on the plane points in front of the camera, is the plane-induced homography of its pinhole twin, dehomogenised, then its lens's distortion_map composition (a pinhole: the homography map alone, PerspectiveMap() ∘ HomographyMap(camera)), and plane_map returns that form: it is the composition transfer_ellipse(camera, shape) transfers a plane conic through, stage by stage, so a conic's image is the homography's exact conic action, then the lens's local affinity. A plane point behind the camera (Z ≤ 0) has no image (project is NaN or a ray past 90°), and the homography form does not test it. A SplineLens camera whose lattice does not image ẑ (the off-axis gauge, which distortion_map refuses for an undistortion) takes the same form: the gauge's K cancels between the homography and the lens stage, so the map is the lens's own projection of the plane point.\n\nThe camera's extrinsics are plain numbers in the plane's unit: a physical camera's plain form is ustrip(m, camera).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.plane_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sensor-Tuple{BoundedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"sensor(camera::BoundedCamera) -> Union{Sensor, Nothing}\n\nThe physical Sensor a camera is bounded by — its resolution is the camera's extent, its pitch the length per pixel — or nothing for a camera bounded by a bare pixel extent. The one-argument accessor beside the two-argument database lookup sensor(manufacturer, model): both answer a Sensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sensor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_directions-Tuple{BoundedCamera, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Cameras","text":"with_directions(camera::BoundedCamera, D::AbstractMatrix{<:StaticVector{3}}) -> BoundedCamera\n\nA bounded spline camera (a SplineLens camera or a NoncentralSplineModel, posed or not) with the direction controls D: its model's with_directions, the bound, the lens coverage and a pose kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_directions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_lens_coverage-Tuple{BoundedCamera, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"with_lens_coverage(camera::BoundedCamera, region) -> BoundedCamera\n\nThe same camera and bound with the lens coverage region (a Rect2, Circle or Ellipse of any real element type, converted to Float64; nothing for the whole sensor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_lens_coverage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_present-Tuple{BoundedCamera, AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Cameras","text":"with_present(camera::BoundedCamera, present::AbstractMatrix{Bool}) -> BoundedCamera\n\nA bounded spline camera (a SplineLens camera or a NoncentralSplineModel, posed or not) holding the nodes present: its model's with_present, the bound, the lens coverage and a pose kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_present"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{PosedCamera, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, Float64, 1}}","page":"Cameras","text":"backproject(camera::PosedCamera, u::StaticVector{2, Float64}) -> Vec3\n\nBackproject a 2D pixel to a 3D ray direction in world coordinates (normalized).  Returned as a Vec3 because it represents a direction — not translatable under affine maps.  The ray's origin is the camera center (pose(camera).t); points along it are pose(camera).t + s · dir for s > 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extent-Tuple{Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"extent(cam) -> Size2{Int}\n\nPixel-space image extent (width × height).  Trait-dispatched: errors at compile time for cameras that don't carry bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics-Tuple{PosedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"extrinsics(camera) -> CameraExtrinsics\n\nCamera extrinsics (world-to-camera transform). Returns a frame-labeled map (WorldFrame → CameraFrame): the extrinsics slot is world-to-camera by contract, so the accessor labels it even when the camera was constructed from an unlabeled map (identity when already labeled).\n\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, ext)\nE = extrinsics(camera)  # World-to-camera\nE(world_point)          # Transform point to camera coordinates\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length-Tuple{PosedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"focal_length(camera::PosedCamera)\n\nGet focal length from camera's intrinsics: the (fx, fy) tuple in pixels.\n\nExamples\n\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\nmodel = CameraModel(LogicalIntrinsics(K), Pinhole())\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, extrinsics)\nfx, fy = focal_length(camera)  # Returns (800.0, 800.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.model-Tuple{PosedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"model(camera::PosedCamera) -> CameraModel\n\nReturn the camera's underlying CameraModel (intrinsics + lens), the single object the lower-level project/backproject/transfer kernels take.  Method form so wrappers (e.g. BoundedCamera) can delegate via @forward. A rig's other member kind answers the same accessor: model(::PosedTarget) is its target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose-Tuple{PosedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"pose(camera) -> CameraPose\n\nCamera pose (camera-to-world transform), inverse of extrinsics. Returns a frame-labeled map (CameraFrame → WorldFrame).\n\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, extrinsics)\nP = pose(camera)       # Camera-to-world\nP.t                    # Camera position in world coordinates\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project","page":"Cameras","text":"project(camera::PosedCamera, rect::HyperRectangle{2}, n::Int=20) -> Polygon\n\nProject a 2D rectangle on the world z=0 plane, tessellating each edge into n samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{PosedCamera, AbstractVector}","page":"Cameras","text":"project(camera::PosedCamera, X_world::AbstractVector) -> Point2\n\nProject 3D point from world coordinates to pixel coordinates.\n\nArguments\n\ncamera::PosedCamera: Camera with model and extrinsics\nX_world::AbstractVector: 3D point in world coordinates (with units)\n\nReturns\n\nPoint2 with pixel coordinates (px units)\n\nExample\n\nmodel = CameraModel(16.0mm, Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px), [320.0px, 240.0px])\nextrinsics = lookat(SVector(0.0, 0.0, 10.0), SVector(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), SVector(0.0, 1.0, 0.0))\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, extrinsics)\nX_world = SVector(1.0mm, 2.0mm, 3.0mm)\nu = project(camera, X_world)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{PosedCamera, Circle3D}","page":"Cameras","text":"project(camera::PosedCamera, c::Circle3D) -> Ellipse or Polygon\n\nProject an arbitrarily-placed 3D circle through the camera. For pinhole cameras, the plane homography induced by the circle's local (u, v) frame gives the analytic image conic; other cases fall back to boundary sampling and return a Polygon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{PosedCamera, Point3, Vec3}","page":"Cameras","text":"project(camera::PosedCamera, origin::Point3, dir::Vec3) -> (Point2, Vec2)\n\nProject a 3D origin and displacement through the camera and return the 2D arrow endpoints — the origin's image and the vector from the origin's image to (origin + dir)'s image. Use to plot a 3D normal or direction as an image-space arrow:\n\norigin_2d, vec_2d = project(cam, c.center, c.radius * normal(c))\narrows2d!(ax, origin_2d, vec_2d; color=:magenta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{PosedCamera{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera, <:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.UnitfulEuclideanMap{3, T, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, R<:Rotations.Rotation{3, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:FlexUnits.Quantity, 1}), Src<:Frame{3}, Dst<:Frame{3}}}, Circle3D}","page":"Cameras","text":"project(camera::PosedCamera, shape::Union{Circle, Ellipse}) -> Ellipse or Polygon\n\nProject a 2D circle or ellipse that lies on the world z=0 plane through the camera. For pinhole cameras, the analytic conic projection yields an Ellipse; partially-behind-camera cases or distorting lens models fall back to boundary-point sampling and return a Polygon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unproject-Tuple{PosedCamera, AbstractVector{Float64}, Float64}","page":"Cameras","text":"unproject(camera::PosedCamera, u::AbstractVector{Float64}, depth::Float64) -> Point3\n\nUnproject a 2D pixel at a given camera-space depth (Z along the camera's forward axis) to a 3D point in world coordinates.  Returned as a Point3 because it represents a position — translatable under affine maps.  Works for both PhysicalIntrinsics (metric units; depth in the chosen metric unit, e.g. mm) and LogicalIntrinsics (unitless pixels; depth carries whatever unit the caller picks for their scene).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unproject"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.DEFAULT_MAX_FOV","page":"Cameras","text":"DEFAULT_MAX_FOV\n\nRecommended half-angle cap for flattening a view onto a perspective plane, as the max_fov of undistorted_rect. 70° gives tan θ ≈ 2.75, so the rectified plane stays a small multiple of the frame; past ~80° (tan 5.7) it grows faster than the detail it reveals, and the last degree before 90° is unbounded.\n\nA RECOMMENDATION, not the default: undistorted_rect defaults max_fov to Inf so that it and valid_rect stay bounded by the same rule. The callers that want a viewing cap — plot_rectification — supply this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.DEFAULT_MAX_FOV"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel{I, L} <: AbstractCamera\n\nCamera model combining intrinsics and lens model: an unposed camera (is_posed is false), the parametric intrinsics × lens family. The central grid camera is one of them, a SplineLens; the non-central grid, NoncentralSplineModel, is the other unposed camera.\n\nUse project(model, X) to map a 3D camera-space point to 2D pixel coordinates, and backproject(model, u) / unproject(model, u, depth) for the inverse directions.\n\nFields\n\nintrinsics::I: Camera intrinsics (LogicalIntrinsics)\nlens::L: Lens model (Pinhole, a Lens, a LiftLens, …)\n\nExamples\n\n# Logical camera with pinhole lens\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\nlogical = LogicalIntrinsics(K)\nmodel = CameraModel(logical, Pinhole())\n\n# Physical camera with pinhole lens\nf = 16.0mm\npitch = Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px)\npp = [320.0px, 240.0px]\nphysical = LogicalIntrinsics(f, pitch, pp)\nmodel = CameraModel(physical, Pinhole())\n\n# With Brown-Conrady distortion\nmodel = CameraModel(logical, Lens(Perspective(), Radial((-0.1, 0.01))))\n\n# Project a point\nX = SVector(10.0mm, 5.0mm, 100.0mm)\nu = project(model, X)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{AffineMap}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(map::AffineMap; lens=Pinhole()) -> CameraModel\n\nConstruct a logical camera model from a CoordinateTransformations.AffineMap.\n\nThe 2×2 linear part encodes focal lengths (and optional skew), the 2D translation encodes the principal point. This constructor enables idiomatic composition:\n\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(M)\nmodel = CameraModel(EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:opencv}, PixelFrame{:colmap}) ∘ affine_part(K); lens=lens)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{CameraCalibrationMat}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(K::CameraCalibrationMat; lens=Pinhole(), pixel_frame=:colmap) -> CameraModel\n\nConvenience constructor from a calibration matrix K directly.\n\nWraps K in LogicalIntrinsics internally. The pixel_frame keyword specifies which pixel coordinate convention K is expressed in (:opencv, :colmap, or :julia). The principal point is shifted to COLMAP convention (pixel centers at 0.5) before storage.\n\nExample\n\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\nmodel = CameraModel(K; lens=Lens(Perspective(), Radial((-0.1,))))\n\n# From OpenCV calibration (pixel centers at 0,0)\nK_cv = CameraCalibrationMat(M)\nmodel = CameraModel(K_cv; lens=lens, pixel_frame=:opencv)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics}, CameraCalibrationMat}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(model::CameraModel, K′::CameraCalibrationMat) -> CameraModel\n\nThe same camera with intrinsics K′: the lens re-expressed under the new intrinsics matrix, so the pixel map is unchanged. A parametric lens shares the principal point: normalized′ = normalized / s with s = f′/f, so a scale-covariant radial lens transforms its coefficients by the law aᵢ → aᵢ·s^(2i), bⱼ → bⱼ·s^(2j) (the generalized k′ = k·s²); it throws for a moved principal point (the lens center is data, not a frame choice), for anisotropic rescaling of a radial lens (radial symmetry would be broken), and for families whose coefficients are only meaningful under a metric K. A SplineLens takes any skew-0 K′, a moved principal point included (its K is a gauge): its backward area moves by the change of the normalized plane, x′ = x / s + (c − c′)/f′, and so does a forward's output (its chart is about ẑ, which no K moves), so every projection is kept too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(f::Quantity, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}; lens=Pinhole()) -> CameraModel\n\nConvenience constructor to build a logical camera model from focal length and principal point (both in pixels).\n\nExample\n\ncamera = CameraModel(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{FlexUnits.Quantity, Size2{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(f::Quantity, pitch::Size2{<:Quantity}, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}; lens=Pinhole()) -> CameraModel\n\nConvenience constructor to build a complete physical camera model from parameters.\n\nExample\n\ncamera = CameraModel(16.0mm, Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px), [320.0px, 240.0px])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3, <:Real}}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(K::SMatrix{3,3,<:Real}; lens=Pinhole(), pixel_frame=:colmap) -> CameraModel\n\nA camera model from a plain static 3×3 calibration matrix, as dataset readers and pool records store it: K is validated and wrapped as a CameraCalibrationMat, then built by the method above. With the default Pinhole() lens this is the pinhole camera of K. (A plain Matrix goes through CameraCalibrationMat(K) first.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Tuple{}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(; focal, pp, lens=Pinhole()) -> CameraModel\n\nUnitless convenience constructor for a logical camera model.\n\nAccepts plain Real values (no px units required). Useful for synthetic data generation and quick prototyping.\n\nArguments\n\nfocal: Focal length in pixels — scalar Real for isotropic, or Tuple{Real,Real} for anisotropic\npp::Tuple{Real,Real}: Principal point (cx, cy) in pixels\nlens::AbstractLensModel=Pinhole(): Lens model\n\nExamples\n\nmodel = CameraModel(; focal=800.0, pp=(320.0, 240.0))\nmodel = CameraModel(; focal=(800.0, 805.0), pp=(320.0, 240.0))  # anisotropic\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Union{Tuple{RadialHomography{T, N}}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}} where {T, N}","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(m::RadialHomography{T, N}) -> CameraModel{ProjectiveIntrinsics{T}, Division{N, T}}\n\nThe projective division camera of a radial homography: CameraModel(ProjectiveIntrinsics(center(m)), Division(division_coeffs(m)...)), built from m's stored primitives. Its ray frame is centred pixels, so the lens coefficients ARE the model's pixel-frame λ's; it drives the fused KA undistort! warp directly: undistort(CameraModel(m), img). No metric ray: a field-of-view operation (a finite max_fov of undistorted_rect) refuses it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tuple{T, T}, AbstractVector{<:FlexUnits.Quantity}}} where T<:FlexUnits.Quantity","page":"Cameras","text":"CameraModel(f::Tuple{T,T}, pp::AbstractVector{<:Quantity}; lens=Pinhole()) where T<:Quantity -> CameraModel\n\nConvenience constructor to build a logical camera model with anisotropic focal lengths (fx ≠ fy).\n\nExample\n\ncamera = CameraModel((800.0px, 805.0px), [320.0px, 240.0px])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CameraModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject_with_jacobian-Tuple{CameraModel, AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, <:Real, 1}}}","page":"Cameras","text":"See backproject(model, pixels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject_with_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject_with_jacobian-Tuple{CameraModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Cameras","text":"backproject_with_jacobian(model::CameraModel, u::StaticVector{2}) -> (f, M)\n\nThe unit bearing of pixel u and its 3×2 Jacobian M = ∂f/∂u, in ONE evaluation. f is what backproject returns; M is what propagates a PIXEL covariance onto the sphere, so the pair is the entry point for calibrated two-view geometry on bearings (the tangent Sampson residual and its rows).\n\nM is TANGENT by construction: fᵀM = 0 to rounding — a forward lens's is built in a tangent basis, and a backward lens's is the derivative of its unit bearing. Consumers therefore need no explicit projection onto the tangent plane — which is what keeps an inner loop free of the allocation such a projection would cost.\n\nThe tangency assertion is LOAD-BEARING — do not simplify it away\n\nfᵀM = 0 is not decorative, and it is not merely an internal consistency check. It is the ONLY test in the suite that catches a correspondence whose two views' Jacobians have been SWAPPED — M₂ handed where M₁ belongs, the natural mistake when porting a residual onto bearings.\n\nNothing else sees it, and that is measured rather than assumed (test/essential_tangent_mc_tests.jl). The swap is very nearly norm-preserving: the two halves of the tangent Sampson's G_x are scaled by |Eᵀf₂| and |Ef₁|, whose ratio has median 1.018 and 5–95% range [0.972, 1.066], so ‖G_x‖ barely moves and the residual stays plausible. Every Monte Carlo calibration cell keeps reporting NEES ≈ 1 — the best of them gets to 0.970, inside its own 4 SE band. Tangency, by contrast, fails loudly: fᵀM measures 4.8e-19 with the right Jacobian against 1.0e-3 with the swapped one, a factor of 2e15.\n\nSo a future cleanup that reads the tangency assertion as redundant with the statistical gates would delete the one check that catches a view swap, and the statistical gates would go on succeeding while it did.\n\nConstruction, given that a camera's forward map is the differentiable one: with B an orthonormal basis of T_f S² (s2_tangent_basis, shared with EssentialManifold5 rather than re-derived) and A = ∂project(model, ·)/∂f · B the 2×2 restriction of the projection differential to that plane, M = B·A⁻¹. The projection differential is taken by automatic differentiation through the camera's OWN project, so every forward lens — Kannala-Brandt, double sphere, the OpenCV models — is covered without a per-model derivation, the same derivation-risk-free stance retract_jacobian takes. A BACKWARD lens (a LiftLens — division, Scaramuzza — or a SplineLens), whose back-projection is the closed form and whose projection a solve, instead differentiates backproject itself: M = ∂backproject(lens, x)/∂x · ∂x/∂u at x = K⁻¹u, by the same automatic differentiation, or analytically where the lens has its own (SplineLens).\n\nThis is PoseLib's unproject_with_jac; GTSAM's nearest relative is PinholeBase::backprojectPointAtInfinity(p, Dresult_dpose, Dresult_dpoint), which returns a Unit3. GTSAM spells the pixel → calibrated step calibrate, which collides with THIS stack's task verb calibrate (estimating a camera), so the name here stays backproject.\n\nReturns (NaN bearing, NaN Jacobian) wherever backproject does — outside the lens's domain, past a fold, or where the tangent restriction A is singular (a ray the camera cannot resolve in two directions). Never throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.backproject_with_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.bearing_correspondences-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"bearing_correspondences(camera1, camera2, cs_px) -> Correspondences\n\nPixel correspondences cs_px (u => v, view 1's pixel to view 2's) as bearing correspondences: each endpoint back-projected by its camera (backproject_with_jacobian(camera, u) -> (f, M)), the unit bearings the pair and the 3×2 pixel Jacobians its M₁/M₂ attribute columns, which put a bearing residual back in pixels (bearings.md). One back-projection per endpoint, no filtering: a pixel a camera does not back-project gives its non-finite bearing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.bearing_correspondences"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_matrix-Tuple{CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics}}","page":"Cameras","text":"calibration_matrix(model::CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics}) -> SMatrix{3,3,T,9}\n\nReturn the 3×3 calibration matrix K as a plain SMatrix, preserving the intrinsics' stored element type (Float64 for the default constructors, Float32 for e.g. LogicalIntrinsics(CameraCalibrationMat{Float32}(…))). A projective camera (ProjectiveIntrinsics) has no calibration matrix: there is no method, so the call raises a MethodError. For a SplineLens camera K is the lens's gauge — the pinhole at the pixel imaging ẑ (skew 0) — kept as it is by every fit.\n\nExample\n\nmodel = CameraModel(; focal=800.0, pp=(320.0, 240.0))\nK = calibration_matrix(model)  # [800 0 320; 0 800 240; 0 0 1]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.calibration_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distortion_map-Tuple{CameraModel}","page":"Cameras","text":"distortion_map(model::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:AbstractLensModel}) -> Transformation\n\nMath-space transform mapping undistorted COLMAP pixel coordinates to distorted ones: pixel → K⁻¹ → lens → K → pixel.  Spelled as a plain composition (affine_part(K) ∘ lens ∘ affine_part(K⁻¹)) so image_warp's fuse merges the calibration affines into the COLMAP/Julia index bridges — the fused per-pixel path is one affine, the lens polynomial, one affine. For a SplineLens camera K is the gauge pinhole at the optical-axis pixel, so the undistorted frame is that pinhole's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distortion_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distortion_map-Tuple{RadialHomography}","page":"Cameras","text":"distortion_map(m::RadialHomography) -> Transformation\n\nThe radial model's undistorted → distorted pixel map, c + μ·(u − c) with the division model's μ (project(m, (u − c, 1)) as a map): the camera bridge's distortion_map, distortion_map(CameraModel(m)). Its inverse is the undistortion, inv(distortion_map(m)); NaN where the model's field ends (as its lens does), never nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.distortion_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.division_bounded-Tuple{CameraModel{<:Union{LogicalIntrinsics, ProjectiveIntrinsics}, <:LiftLens}, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"division_bounded(model::CameraModel{<:Union{LogicalIntrinsics, ProjectiveIntrinsics}, <:LiftLens}, sz; margin = HORIZON_MARGIN) -> Bool\n\nDoes the whole image lie inside margin · monotone_radius(lens) (in the lens's normalized distorted radius; a projective camera's is centred pixels)? The general-family form of the coefficient-level predicate above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.division_bounded"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.division_bounded-Tuple{Real, Any, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"division_bounded(k, center, sz; margin = HORIZON_MARGIN) -> Bool\n\nWhether the perspective (undistorted) image of a frame of size sz = (width, height) is BOUNDED under a division model k about center (all in the same pixel units — e.g. a RadialHomography's k/center). For k < 0 the undistorted radius r_u = r_d/(1 + k r_d²) diverges at the 90° circle r_∞ = 1/√(−k): content at or beyond it undistorts to infinity, and no finite canvas holds it. True iff the farthest image corner's distorted radius stays inside margin·r_∞ (k ≥ 0 is always bounded).\n\nWhen true, the corner stretch factor is 1/(1 + k·r_max²) — the canvas scale an undistorted image needs to keep every input pixel in bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.division_bounded"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.fov_radius-Tuple{CameraModel, Real}","page":"Cameras","text":"fov_radius(model, θ) -> Float64\n\nThe UNDISTORTED (perspective-plane) pixel radius of the cone of half-angle θ radians: f·tan(θ). The companion of horizon_radius — one says where the lens stops imaging, this says how big a chosen field of view is once flattened.\n\nA projective camera (ProjectiveIntrinsics) has no focal length, so no field of view: its radius is an ArgumentError (and so is a finite max_fov in undistorted_rect, which reads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.fov_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.horizon_radius-Tuple{CameraModel}","page":"Cameras","text":"horizon_radius(model) -> Float64\n\nThe PIXEL radius of the camera's horizon: the circle at which rays reach the lens's monotone limit, beyond which nothing can be placed on a perspective plane at all. Inf for a lens with no such limit (a pinhole, a mild polynomial). This is the circle plot_rectification draws on the raw panel, and the guide for how much of a frame it is meaningful to rectify.\n\nA projective camera (ProjectiveIntrinsics) has no focal length: its radius is read in its own centred-pixel frame, the unit-focal one its lens coefficients are written in.\n\nIt is NOT a safe rectification bound by itself. The distorted radius is very nearly linear in the view angle, while the perspective magnification is tan θ, so a generous FRACTION of this radius is still a fraction of a degree from the pole: for a 927 px fisheye with a 1419 px horizon, 92% of it is θ ≈ 89.7°, tan θ ≈ 199, and the rectified plane comes out 369 000 px wide. Bound the ANGLE instead — see undistorted_rect's max_fov.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.horizon_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.normalized_point-Tuple{AbstractIntrinsics, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Cameras","text":"normalized_point(intrinsics::AbstractIntrinsics, 𝐩::StaticVector{2}) -> SVector{2}\n\nThe normalized (distorted) image point x = K⁻¹𝐩 of the pixel 𝐩: the first two coordinates of K⁻¹ (𝐩, 1), K the intrinsics' pixel matrix (skew included). It is the pixel-frame half of backproject — a lens's backproject(lens, x) takes it to the bearing — and the point a spline model's lattice is laid over (spline_window(area, grid, normalized_point(K, 𝐩))). The one spelling of that map: the scalar back-projection, the batched kernels and a caller reading a spline lattice at a pixel all call it. Allocation-free, in the element type of K⁻¹ and 𝐩 (Float32, ForwardDiff Duals), callable in a device kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.normalized_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pinhole-Tuple{CameraModel}","page":"Cameras","text":"pinhole(model::CameraModel) -> CameraModel{I, Pinhole}\nimaging_transform(model::CameraModel)\n\nThe lens-stripped pinhole twin (downstream packages hand-rolled CameraModel(x.intrinsics, Pinhole()) in three places) and the composed camera-space → pixel map as a value. intrinsics/lens accessors live in cameras.jl. For a SplineLens camera the twin is the gauge pinhole at the optical-axis pixel (the pixel imaging ẑ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pinhole"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_homography-Tuple{CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics, <:Division}, HomographyMat}","page":"Cameras","text":"radial_homography(camera::CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics, <:Division}, H::HomographyMat) -> RadialHomography\n\nThe radial homography of a plane homography H (plane point → undistorted pixel) seen through a METRIC division camera: RadialHomography(H, λ, c) with λₚ = kₚ / f^{2p}, kₚ the camera's division coefficients in K's normalized chart, f its focal length (pixels) and c its principal point. The projective method's λ's are pixel-frame; this converts the metric camera's to them, so radial_transfer(radial_homography(camera, H), X) is the raw pixel project(camera, K⁻¹·H·[X; 1]) of a plane point X. Throws an ArgumentError for fx ≠ fy or a skewed K, whose lens is not radially symmetric in pixels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_homography"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_homography-Tuple{CameraModel{<:ProjectiveIntrinsics, <:Division}, HomographyMat}","page":"Cameras","text":"radial_homography(camera::CameraModel{<:ProjectiveIntrinsics, <:Division}, H::HomographyMat) -> RadialHomography\n\nThe radial homography of a target's plane homography H seen through a projective division camera: RadialHomography(H, camera). A TargetHomographies entry (a HomographyMap) pairs with the camera that measured it through the HomographyMap method below.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_homography"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_homography-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{CameraModel{<:Union{LogicalIntrinsics, ProjectiveIntrinsics}, <:Division}, HomographyMap{3, T, M} where M<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}}} where T","page":"Cameras","text":"radial_homography(camera::CameraModel{<:Union{ProjectiveIntrinsics, LogicalIntrinsics}, <:Division}, h::HomographyMap{3}) -> RadialHomography\n\nThe radial homography of a plane → pixel HomographyMap (a TargetHomographies entry, labeled or not): radial_homography(camera, H) of its matrix. The map's frames are not checked against the camera, which holds none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_homography"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reframed_calibration-Tuple{SimilarityMap{2, T, S, R, V, Src, Dst} where {T, S<:Real, R<:Rotations.Rotation{2, T}, V<:(StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, <:Real, 1}), Src<:Frame{2}, Dst<:Frame{2}}, CameraCalibrationMat}","page":"Cameras","text":"reframed_calibration(S::SimilarityMap{2}, K::CameraCalibrationMat) -> CameraCalibrationMat{Float64}\nreframed_calibration(from::Type{<:PixelFrame}, to::Type{<:PixelFrame}, K::CameraCalibrationMat) -> CameraCalibrationMat{Float64}\n\nTHE change of image frame of a calibration matrix: K in S's source frame becomes to_homogeneous(S)·K in its destination frame, so a pixel K·x̃ of the source is S(K·x̃) of the destination. In Float64. A similarity of the image plane keeps K's form when its rotation is the identity (a scale s multiplies the focals, the skew and the principal point, then the translation moves the principal point); a rotated S gives no upper-triangular K and is refused by K's own structure check (ArgumentError). inv(S) brings K back.\n\nThe 3-argument form is the pixel-frame change, S = SimilarityMap(EuclideanMap(from, to)) (a translation by the frames' half-pixel offset; the focal lengths and the skew kept): CameraModel(K; pixel_frame) and VisualGeometryToolkitIO's stored K (its readers and writers) all move a K through it. VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures' half-resolution RAW images move K through the map form with their frame map (raw_frame_map). K was checked (or deliberately not, check = false) when it was built; a positive scale keeps its focals positive, so only the structure is re-checked.\n\nK = CameraCalibrationMat(800.0px, [320.0px, 240.0px])            # OpenCV's frame\nreframed_calibration(PixelFrame{:opencv}, PixelFrame{:colmap}, K) # cx, cy = 320.5, 240.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reframed_calibration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reprojection_error-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"reprojection_error(camera, pose, X, u) -> SVector{2}\n\nThe reprojection residual of a scene point, Jacobian-free: project(camera, pose(X)) − u, with pose any map into the camera frame (a world → camera EuclideanMap, X_c = R·X + t; a camera → world pose enters as inv(pose), which is x ↦ Rᵀ(x − t)). NaN where the lens cannot image the point (project answers on the ray domain). Element type follows the inputs, so ForwardDiff duals flow through the pose and the lens.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reprojection_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, CameraModel, AbstractMatrix, HyperRectangle{2}}","page":"Cameras","text":"undistort!(dst, [T = Float64], model, src, rect; fillvalue = zero(eltype(dst))) -> dst\n\nGENERAL form: resample the undistorted-plane window rect (COLMAP pixel coordinates) into dst. dst's size sets the sampling density — supply a smaller dst to downscale; the scale and offset fold into the fused warp affines. The rect-free methods above render Rect(0, 0, w, h) at unit scale (the classic same-size undistortion, which CROPS any content the undistortion pushes outside the frame); pair this form with undistorted_rect to render the full extent instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, CameraModel, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Cameras","text":"undistort!(dst, [T = Float64], model, src; fillvalue = zero(eltype(dst))) -> dst\n\nIn-place undistort: resample src into the pre-allocated dst through the fused lens warp, on the backend that owns dst (any KernelAbstractions backend — supply CuArrays for GPU execution).  dst and src may differ in eltype (see image_warp!).  T sets the warp math's float type: undistort!(dst, Float32, model, src) keeps the per-pixel math single-precision for GPU throughput. The order is Julia Base's: the mutated dst, then the type, then the inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort-Tuple{CameraModel, AbstractMatrix, HyperRectangle{2}}","page":"Cameras","text":"undistort(model, img, rect; scale = 1, fillvalue = zero(eltype(img)), device = default_device()) -> FramedImage\n\nAllocating rect form of undistort!: the canvas is sized ceil.(widths(rect) ./ scale), rect is resampled into it, and the result is returned WITH its placement as a FramedImage — rect is the window of the camera's pixel frame the canvas covers, so imshow(pos, undistort(model, img, ext; scale)) is self-placing and the ext/scale pair never travels beside the pixels by hand. The in-place undistort! forms are unchanged (the caller owns dst and its placement); the rect-free same-size forms return bare matrices (their placement is trivially the frame). device as in undistort: the warp runs there, the canvas comes back in img's array kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort-Tuple{CameraModel, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Cameras","text":"undistort(model::CameraModel{<:AbstractIntrinsics, <:AbstractLensModel}, img)\n\nRemove lens distortion from an image.\n\nFor each output (undistorted) pixel, computes the corresponding source (distorted) pixel through model.lens and resamples. Uses VGC's image_warp builder to bake pixel coordinate conventions into a BoundedMap (K is assumed to be in COLMAP convention: image corner at origin, pixel centers at 0.5).\n\nEach output pixel is sampled through project, which NaNs a ray past a radial factor's fold (the pixel takes the fill value) but does not test the other folds: past a fold of an Additive's deltas or a composed distortion's Tilt the pixel samples a finite source pixel whose backproject is a different ray, so the output repeats content of the imaged branch there. Where the lens overlaps itself over the sensor, no undistortion is one-to-one, and a map built from backproject may jump between preimages (see backproject): injectivity is checked on the lens over the sensor rectangle, not per pixel. A SplineLens camera undistorts onto its gauge pinhole (its K: the pinhole at the pixel imaging ẑ), in every form here and in undistort!.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::CameraModel: Camera model (any lens family)\nimg: Input distorted image (any AbstractMatrix — Gray, RGB, etc.)\ndevice: the KernelAbstractions backend the warp runs on, by default default_device() (an accelerator when one is functional, else KA.CPU()). img is uploaded to it once, and the result comes back in img's array kind: an Array gives an Array, a device array stays on its device with no copy. The in-place undistort! runs on dst's backend.\n\nReturns\n\nUndistorted image of the same size and element type.\n\nExample\n\nmodel = CameraModel(; focal=2458.0, pp=(2099.0, 1494.0),\n                      lens=Lens(Perspective(), Additive(Radial((-0.159, 0.048)), Tangential(0.006, -0.001))))\nimg_clean = undistort(model, img)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistort"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistorted_rect-Tuple{RadialHomography, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"undistorted_rect(m::RadialHomography, (width, height); margin = HORIZON_MARGIN, max_fov = Inf) -> Rect\n\nThe undistorted-plane extent of a width×height frame under m's division model (the walk of its CameraModel(m)): the frame boundary undistorted (u = c + (d − c)/(1 + Σₚ λₚ‖d − c‖²ᵖ)), with boundary radii at or beyond margin·r_∞ (the lens's horizon; r_∞ = 1/√(−k) for one coefficient) clipped to it — the honest cap when division_bounded is false and the true extent is infinite. Feed the rect to the rect form of undistort/undistort!:\n\next = undistorted_rect(m, (w, h))\ncanvas = undistort(CameraModel(m), img, ext;\n                   scale = max(1, GeometryBasics.widths(ext)[1] / 5000))\n\nmax_fov (radians) additionally bounds the extent by FIELD OF VIEW, capping it at the bounding square of that cone about the principal point. Inf, the default, leaves the frame's own field alone; a finite value is what makes a fisheye — whose horizon can sit inside the frame — rectify to a usable plane at all, since tan θ is unbounded in the last degree before the pole. DEFAULT_MAX_FOV is the recommended cap. A field of view needs a metric camera: for a radial homography (a projective camera, ProjectiveIntrinsics) a finite max_fov is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.undistorted_rect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_rect-Tuple{CameraModel{<:Union{LogicalIntrinsics, ProjectiveIntrinsics}}, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"valid_rect(m::RadialHomography, (width, height); margin = HORIZON_MARGIN) -> Rect\nvalid_rect(model::CameraModel{<:Union{LogicalIntrinsics, ProjectiveIntrinsics}, <:AbstractLensModel}, sz;\n           margin = HORIZON_MARGIN) -> Rect\n\nThe INSCRIBED counterpart of undistorted_rect — OpenCV's alpha = 0 endpoint: the largest axis-aligned window of the undistorted plane containing NO invalid pixels, so undistort(model, img, valid_rect(model, size)) renders with no fill — the no-black-borders mode. Same frame-boundary walk as undistorted_rect (one authority, _boundary_undistort), but taking per-edge extrema INWARD — the left constraint is the maximum x over the undistorted left edge, and so on — instead of the global bounding box (sampled, like the outer rect; corners are the edge-sample endpoints, so both bow directions are covered).\n\nBoundary radii at or beyond margin·r_∞ clip to the horizon exactly as in undistorted_rect: when the whole horizon circle lies inside the frame the valid region is unbounded and the clip is the honest cap. Throws when the inscribed window is empty (boundary folded past the principal point). A SplineLens camera's rect is on its gauge pinhole (the pixel imaging ẑ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.valid_rect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{AbstractIntrinsics, AbstractLensModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, <:Real, 1}}","page":"Cameras","text":"backproject(intrinsics::AbstractIntrinsics, lens::AbstractLensModel, u::StaticVector{2,T<:Real}) -> Vec3\n\nThe pixel-frame half of back-projection: K⁻¹ takes the pixel u to the normalized distorted point, and the bearing is the lens's own backproject(lens, xd) — which, not this method, is what a lens family extends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{CameraModel, AbstractVector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, <:Real, 1}}}","page":"Cameras","text":"backproject(model::CameraModel, pixels::AbstractVector{<:StaticVector{2}}; device = get_backend(pixels)) -> rays\nbackproject_with_jacobian(model::CameraModel, pixels::AbstractVector{<:StaticVector{2}}; device = get_backend(pixels))\n    -> Vector{Tuple{SVector{3}, SMatrix{3, 2}}}\n\nThe batched backproject (and backproject_with_jacobian) on the KernelAbstractions backend device (by default the pixels' own, get_backend(pixels): a host vector stays on the host, a device array on its device; give default_device() to send host pixels to the accelerator): the scalar map per pixel, one pointwise! over a device copy of the model and the pixels, each ray an SVector{3}, the result back in the pixels' array kind — bit for bit the scalar value on the CPU. One kernel on every backend: the model goes in its device form (isbits, or its adapt rule); a model with neither fails on an accelerator. A SplineLens camera's batched backproject is its own kernel (spline_lens.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject-Tuple{CameraModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, <:Real, 1}}","page":"Cameras","text":"backproject(model::CameraModel, u::StaticVector{2,T<:Real}) -> Vec3\n\nBackproject 2D pixel coordinates to 3D ray direction.\n\nInputs and outputs are unitless (assumed units: px for input, unitless direction for output). The output eltype follows the usual promotion of the input and the intrinsics: Float64 pipelines are unchanged; a Float32 camera (LogicalIntrinsics{Float32}) with Float32 pixels stays Float32; ForwardDiff Dual pixels give a Dual direction, so the bearing can be differentiated with respect to the pixel; integer pixels give the bearing of their floating-point value, as the lens-level backproject(lens, xd) and the grid models' backproject do.\n\nThe return type and semantics depend on the intrinsics type:\n\nLogicalIntrinsics: Returns normalized unitless direction\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::CameraModel: Camera model with intrinsics and lens\nu::StaticVector{2,T}: 2D pixel coordinates [u, v] (unitless, in px)\n\nReturns\n\nVec3: Normalized ray direction (unitless)\n\nExample\n\ncamera = CameraModel(16.0mm, Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px), [320.0px, 240.0px])\nu = SVector(400.0, 300.0)  # px (unitless)\nray = backproject(camera, u)  # Returns normalized Vec3 direction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.backproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{CameraModel, AbstractVector}","page":"Cameras","text":"project(model::CameraModel, X::AbstractVector) -> Point2\n\nProject 3D point X in camera coordinates to 2D pixel coordinates.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::CameraModel: Camera model\nX::AbstractVector: 3D point in camera coordinates [X, Y, Z] (with units)\n\nReturns\n\nPoint2 with pixel coordinates (including px units)\n\nExample\n\nmodel = CameraModel(16.0mm, Size2(width=5.86μm/px, height=5.86μm/px), [320.0px, 240.0px])\nX = SVector(10.0mm, 5.0mm, 100.0mm)\nu = project(model, X)  # Returns Point2 in pixels\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unproject-Tuple{CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics}, AbstractVector, Real}","page":"Cameras","text":"unproject(model::CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics}, u::AbstractVector, depth::Real) -> Point3\nunproject(intrinsics::LogicalIntrinsics, lens, u, depth) -> Point3\n\nThe camera-frame point on the ray behind pixel u whose Z-DEPTH is depth — the convention of depth images: distance along the optical axis. It is b·(depth / b₃) with b = backproject(intrinsics, lens, u), for every lens, and NaN where the ray has b₃ ≤ 0: a Z-depth names no point on such a ray. A point at distance d ALONG any ray, past 90° included, is d · backproject(model, u). A projective camera has no metric ray, so no Z-depth: there is no method, and the call raises a MethodError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unproject"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.angle_of_view-Tuple{BoundedCamera}","page":"Cameras","text":"angle_of_view(camera::BoundedCamera; dimension = :horizontal) -> θ\n\nThe angle of view of a bounded camera: the FULL angle, in radians, of the scene it IMAGES in dimension — its pixel extent, bounded by its lens_coverage (where the lens covers the sensor) — read through the camera's own backproject (K⁻¹, then the lens), so a distorted or fisheye lens answers its true angle and an anisotropic K uses the focal length of that axis. For a pinhole with a centred principal point it is the inverse of focal_length(resolution, θ; dimension), which takes the same full angle (focal_length(sensor, θ) is its metric form). (The lens's own limit, view_angle_limit, is a HALF angle from the optical axis.) The size this angle spans at a distance is field_of_view.\n\nThe sample points, each moved in along the line from the principal point to where the lens coverage ends when it lies past it (a circular fisheye's dark corners, the 14 mm disc cut by a wide sensor):\n\n:horizontal: the two edge points on the principal point's row, (0, c_y) and (width, c_y);\n:vertical: the two on its column, (c_x, 0) and (c_x, height);\n:diagonal: the four corners of the extent.\n\nThe answer is twice the SMALLEST of those points' view angles — the nearer edge when the principal point is off-centre, so the cone of that full angle about the optical axis is imaged in that dimension. A camera whose whole sensor is covered (the default coverage) reads the sensor's edges. A principal point outside the coverage images nothing about the axis: an ArgumentError. For a lens that is not radially symmetric the valid_domain is inscribed, so a point between it and the true limit counts as the lens's limit: the answer can then overstate the angle slightly.\n\nA SplineLens camera reads about its gauge principal point, the pixel imaging ẑ, so the angles are about the optical axis.\n\nClipping by the model: a point outside the lens model's valid_domain (or one the lens does not image) counts as the lens's own limit, so when the valid domain clips the covered area in that dimension the answer is 2 · view_angle_limit(lens).\n\ncam = BoundedCamera(CameraModel(K; lens = Lens(Equidistant())), sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX250\"))\nangle_of_view(cam)                          # horizontal, full angle in radians\nangle_of_view(cam; dimension = :diagonal)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.angle_of_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.field_of_view-Tuple{BoundedCamera, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"field_of_view(camera::BoundedCamera, distance; dimension = :horizontal) -> size\n\nThe field of view of a bounded camera at distance: the SIZE of the scene it images there, on a plane perpendicular to its optical axis — 2·distance·tan(θ/2) for its angle_of_view θ in dimension, in the unit of distance (a length such as 2.0m, or a plain number). An angle of view of 180° or more spans no finite size on a plane, and a distance must be positive: both an ArgumentError. (Not the field-of-view LENS model FieldOfView, Devernay's distortion — a different thing that shares the words.)\n\nfield_of_view(cam, 3.0m)                         # the width of the scene imaged 3 m away\nfield_of_view(cam, 3.0m; dimension = :vertical)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.field_of_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lookat-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}}","page":"Cameras","text":"lookat(eye::StaticVector{3, <:Real}, target::StaticVector{3, <:Real}, up::StaticVector{3, <:Real};\n       convention::Symbol = :cv)\n\nCreate a camera extrinsics transform that looks from eye position toward target.\n\nUnitless version for internal use and backwards compatibility.\n\nconvention selects the camera-frame convention (same symbol-selector precedent as Rect(sensor, :julia)):\n\n:cv (default) — Computer Vision convention:\n+Z axis points FORWARD (toward target)\n+X axis points RIGHT\n+Y axis points DOWN\nCamera -Y aligns with world up direction (supply up as [0, -1, 0])\n:gfx — graphics convention (Mitsuba/OpenGL-style look_at frame): camera-to-world columns z = normalize(target − eye), x = normalize(cross(up, z)), y = cross(z, x); supply up as the world up direction (typically [0, 1, 0]). The returned map is still the world-to-camera extrinsics — only the frame construction differs.\n\nArguments\n\neye::StaticVector{3}: Camera position in world coordinates (plain numbers, in the caller's unit)\ntarget::StaticVector{3}: Point to look at in world coordinates (same unit)\nup::StaticVector{3}: World up direction (see per-convention notes above)\n\nReturns\n\nCameraExtrinsics: World-to-camera transformation (frame-labeled EuclideanMap)\n\nExample\n\n# Camera at (0, 0, 0) looking toward (0, 0, 1000), with world -Y pointing up\nextrinsics = lookat(SVector(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), SVector(0.0, 0.0, 1000.0), SVector(0.0, -1.0, 0.0))\n\nSee also lookat_pose for the camera-to-world form (bit-exact eye translation, no inversion round-trip).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lookat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lookat-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}}","page":"Cameras","text":"lookat(eye::StaticVector{3, <:Quantity}, target::StaticVector{3, <:Quantity}, up::StaticVector{3, <:Real};\n       convention::Symbol = :cv)\n\nCreate a camera extrinsics transform that looks from eye position toward target.\n\nA PHYSICAL pose: eye and target are lengths, and the extrinsics' translation is a length in metres — the plain lookat of the two points in metres, read back in metres (a plain point beside a length is a DimensionError).\n\nUses Computer Vision convention:\n\n+Z axis points FORWARD (toward target)\n+X axis points RIGHT\n+Y axis points DOWN\nCamera -Y aligns with world up direction\n\nArguments\n\neye::StaticVector{3, <:Quantity}: Camera position in world coordinates (physical lengths)\ntarget::StaticVector{3, <:Quantity}: Point to look at in world coordinates (physical lengths)\nup::StaticVector{3, <:Real}: World up direction, unitless (typically [0, -1, 0] for CV convention where -Y is up)\n\nReturns\n\nCameraExtrinsics: World-to-camera transformation (frame-labeled EuclideanMap, physical)\n\nExample\n\n# Camera at (0, 0, 0) looking toward (0, 0, 1000mm), with world -Y pointing up\nextrinsics = lookat(SVector(0.0mm, 0.0mm, 0.0mm), SVector(0.0mm, 0.0mm, 1000.0mm), SVector(0.0, -1.0, 0.0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lookat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lookat_pose-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Real, 1}}","page":"Cameras","text":"lookat_pose(eye::StaticVector{3, <:Real}, target::StaticVector{3, <:Real}, up::StaticVector{3, <:Real};\n            convention::Symbol = :cv) -> CameraPose\n\nCamera POSE (camera-to-world transform) that looks from eye toward target, as a typed CameraPose — the pose-side companion of lookat, sharing its convention selector (:cv / :gfx).\n\nBuilt directly from the frame columns (rotation = camera basis as camera-to-world columns, translation = eye bit-exactly) rather than as inv(lookat(...)), so no inversion round-trip touches the translation. Feed the result straight to the frame-typed camera constructor PosedCamera(model, lookat_pose(...)), which inverts internally into the extrinsics slot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.lookat_pose"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length-Tuple{Sensor, Real}","page":"Cameras","text":"focal_length(sensor::Sensor, θ::Real; dimension=:horizontal)\n\nThe physical focal length from the FULL field of view angle and the sensor: focal_length(sensor.resolution, θ) pixels times the pitch of that axis.\n\nArguments\n\nsensor::Sensor: the sensor (its resolution and pitch)\nθ::Real: the full angle of view in radians\ndimension::Symbol: Which dimension the angle of view applies to (:horizontal or :vertical)\n\nReturns\n\nPhysical focal length (a length quantity)\n\nExamples\n\ns = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")\nf_mm = focal_length(s, deg2rad(40.0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length-Tuple{Size2, Real}","page":"Cameras","text":"focal_length(resolution::Size2, θ::Real; dimension=:horizontal)\n\nThe focal length an angle of view implies: in pixels from the FULL angle of view (plain radians — angles carry no dimension in the VGC unit system) and resolution. Its inverse, for a camera, is angle_of_view. The other forms: focal_length(sensor::Sensor, θ) (a length) and the accessor focal_length(intrinsics) ((fx, fy) in pixels).\n\nArguments\n\nresolution::Size2: Image resolution (width, height) in pixels: plain numbers or dimensionless quantities (px); a length is a DimensionError\nθ::Real: the full angle of view in radians\ndimension::Symbol: Which dimension the angle of view applies to (:horizontal or :vertical)\n\nReturns\n\nFocal length in pixels (same logical unit as resolution)\n\nExamples\n\nfocal_length(Size2(width=1280px, height=960px), 0.698)\nfocal_length(Size2(width=1280px, height=960px), deg2rad(40.0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.focal_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.p3p-Tuple{CameraModel, Any}","page":"Cameras","text":"p3p(model::CameraModel, correspondences) -> FixedModels{4, Extrinsics3}\n\nSolve the Perspective-3-Point (P3P) problem using correspondences.\n\nThis is a thin wrapper around the core p3p(rays, X) solver that accepts correspondences directly. It extracts the 3D world points and 2D image points from the correspondences, backprojects the image points to rays, and calls the core solver.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::CameraModel: Camera model for backprojecting image points to rays\ncorrespondences: Correspondence container with exactly 3 point pairs (3D world → 2D image); any container satisfying the sources/targets protocol (Correspondences, StructArray of Pairs, plain Pair vector)\n\nReturns\n\nFixedModels{4, Extrinsics3}: Stack-allocated container of 0–4 rigid poses. Empty (length == 0) if no valid solution exists. Iterate with for ext in result ... end or collect(result).\n\nExamples\n\n# Setup camera\ns = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")\nsensor_bounds = Rect(s)\nf = focal_length(s, deg2rad(40.0); dimension=:horizontal)\npp = centroid(sensor_bounds) .* px\nmodel = CameraModel(f, s.pitch, pp)\n\n# Create correspondences (3D world points → 2D image points)\nX3 = [Point3(100.0, 100.0, 0.0), Point3(200.0, 150.0, 0.0), Point3(150.0, 200.0, 0.0)]\nu3 = [Point2(640.0, 480.0), Point2(700.0, 500.0), Point2(680.0, 550.0)]\ncs = correspondences(X3, u3)\n\n# Solve P3P — always a FixedModels; empty (length 0) when no valid pose\nresult = p3p(model, cs)\ncameras = PosedCamera.(Ref(model), collect(result))\n\nSee Also\n\np3p(rays, X): Core P3P solver that works with rays directly\nsample_p3p: Sample valid camera poses by randomly sampling correspondences\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.p3p"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sample_p3p-Tuple{CameraModel, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, <:Point3, 1}, HyperRectangle, Plane3D}","page":"Cameras","text":"sample_p3p(model, X3::SVector{3}, sensor_bounds, plane::Plane3D; max_retries=1000)\n\nSample a P3P configuration using 3 fixed world points with winding-consistent image sampling.\n\nGiven 3 world points (e.g., board corners), samples 3 random image points and sorts them with consistent winding (world CCW, image CW due to y-axis flip). This ensures:\n\nDeterministic scale: World point distances define the scale\nConsistent orientation: Matching winding prevents reflected/flipped solutions\n\nPoints can be supplied in any order - the function sorts world points to CCW and image points to CW (matching winding due to y-axis inversion between coordinate systems).\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::CameraModel: Camera model\nX3::SVector{3,Point3}: 3 world points (any order, will be sorted to CCW)\nsensor_bounds::Rect: Sensor bounds for sampling image points\nplane::Plane3D: Reference plane for chirality test\n\nReturns\n\n(camera, correspondences): Camera and the 3 correspondences used (in CCW order)\n\nExample\n\n# Board corners in world coords (centered, y-up, z=0)\nboard_rect = Rect(bundle, :world)\ncorners = coordinates(board_rect)  # 4 corners\nX3 = SVector{3}(Point3.(vcat.(corners[[1,2,3]], 0.0)))  # any 3 corners\n\ncamera, correspondences = sample_p3p(model, X3, sensor_bounds, Plane3D())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sample_p3p"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sample_p3p-Tuple{CameraModel, Vector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, Float64, 1}}, HyperRectangle, Plane3D}","page":"Cameras","text":"sample_p3p(model, X, sensor_bounds, plane::Plane3D; max_retries=1000)\nsample_p3p(rng, model, X, sensor_bounds, plane::Plane3D; max_retries=1000)\n\nSample a random P3P configuration with chirality filtering.\n\nLike sample_p3p(model, X, sensor_bounds) but additionally filters solutions to only those where the camera is on the positive side of the given plane (i.e., signed_distance(plane, camera_center) > 0).\n\nThis is useful for planar calibration targets where you want to ensure the camera is \"in front of\" the target plane.\n\nArguments\n\nrng=Random.default_rng(): Random number generator (optional first positional argument)\nmodel::CameraModel: Camera model\nX::Vector{<:StaticVector{3,Float64}}: 3D world points on or near the plane\nsensor_bounds::Rect: Sensor bounds for sampling image points (unitless, in pixels)\nplane::Plane3D: Reference plane for chirality test\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nmax_retries=1000: Maximum number of sampling attempts\n\nReturns\n\n(camera, correspondences): Tuple containing:\ncamera::PosedCamera: The camera with valid pose (in front of plane)\ncorrespondences::StructArray{Pair{Point3, Point2}, 1, ...}: The 3 correspondences used\n\nExample\n\n# World coordinate system: origin at board center, z=0 plane, z-axis pointing out\nboard_plane = Plane3D()  # z=0, normal = (0,0,1)\n\n# Sample camera that is in front of the board (z > 0)\ncamera, correspondences = sample_p3p(model, X, sensor_bounds, board_plane)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sample_p3p"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sample_p3p-Tuple{CameraModel, Vector{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, Float64, 1}}, HyperRectangle}","page":"Cameras","text":"sample_p3p(model, X, sensor_bounds; max_retries=1000)\nsample_p3p(rng, model, X, sensor_bounds; max_retries=1000)\n\nSample a random P3P configuration by sampling from world points X and random image points.\n\nCreates 3D-2D correspondences by:\n\nSampling 3 random points from X (world coordinates)\nSampling 3 random 2D image points in sensor bounds\nPairing them as correspondences and solving P3P\n\nTakes a logical camera (CameraModel{<:LogicalIntrinsics}): X and the sensor bounds are unitless (the construction boundary stripped units).\n\nArguments\n\nrng=Random.default_rng(): Random number generator (optional first positional argument)\nmodel::CameraModel: Camera model\nX::Vector{<:StaticVector{3,Float64}}: 3D world points, unitless\nsensor_bounds::Rect: Sensor bounds for sampling image points (unitless, in pixels)\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nmax_retries=1000: Maximum number of sampling attempts\n\nReturns\n\n(extrinsics, correspondences): Tuple containing:\nextrinsics::Vector{Extrinsics3}: Camera extrinsics (world-to-camera transforms) for valid P3P solutions\ncorrespondences::StructArray{Pair{Point3, Point2}, 1, ...}: The 3 correspondences used for P3P, backed by SVector{3} columns\n\nThrows an error if no valid configuration is found after max_retries attempts.\n\nExamples\n\n# Setup camera\ns = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")\nsensor_bounds = Rect(s)\nf = focal_length(s, deg2rad(40.0); dimension=:horizontal)\npp = centroid(sensor_bounds) .* px\nmodel = CameraModel(f, s.pitch, pp)\n\n# 3D points (planar points at z=0)\nX = [Point3(100.0, 100.0, 0.0), Point3(200.0, 150.0, 0.0), ...]\n\n# With default RNG\nextrinsics, correspondences = sample_p3p(model, X, sensor_bounds)\n\n# With explicit RNG for reproducibility\nrng = Random.MersenneTwister(12345)\nextrinsics, correspondences = sample_p3p(rng, model, X, sensor_bounds)\n\n# Access correspondence data (StructArray interface)\nX_used = correspondences.first   # All 3D points\nu_used = correspondences.second  # All 2D image points\n# Or index individual correspondences: correspondences[1].first, correspondences[1].second\n\n# Use best extrinsics (e.g., based on reprojection error)\ncameras = PosedCamera.(Ref(model), extrinsics)\nbest_camera = cameras[1]  # Or select based on reprojection error\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sample_p3p"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Board","page":"Cameras","text":"Board{T}\n\nThe calibration board a planar target's pattern is printed on, the target's analogue of a Sensor: its size and where it lies in the design chart. It follows the target's unit rule: physical (lengths, stored in metres) for a physical target, plain numbers for a unitless one.\n\nFields\n\nsize::Size2{T}: the board proper, width × height — BlobBoards' board_size, the printed board inside the sheet's white margin, frame and corners included; NOT its canvas_size (the sheet) and not the pattern's extent. Lengths (a quantity of any other dimension is a DimensionError) or plain numbers; a mix is an ArgumentError.\norigin::SVector{2, Float64}: the board's lower-left corner in the design chart (the coordinates of the nominal points, stated once at Canvas): plain metres for a physical board, the design's plain numbers for a unitless one.\n\nRead them as properties, board.size and board.origin, as a sensor's resolution and pitch are.\n\nConstructors\n\nBoard(Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm))                              # corner at the design origin\nBoard(Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm), [-10.0mm, -10.0mm])          # the corner as lengths\nBoard(Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm), SVector(-0.01, -0.01))       # or plain metres\nBoard(Size2(width = 297.0, height = 210.0), [-10.0, -10.0])              # a unitless board\n\nRect(board) is the board's domain in the design chart in the board's own numbers (plain metres when physical) — what a target's surface is bounded by (grown by a 16-eps rounding margin), as Rect(sensor) is the image's domain in pixels. ustrip(u, board) and with_unit(board, u) move it between the two forms, as they move a target's points.\n\nSee Also\n\naspect_ratio\nPlanarTarget, board\nCanvas: the printed page the board lies on\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Board"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Canvas","page":"Cameras","text":"Canvas{T}\n\nThe page a planar target is printed on: the sheet that holds the Board (the calibrated rectangle) and around it the margin; the pattern is what is printed on the board. A canvas ⊃ board ⊃ pattern hierarchy, the canvas being the target's record of what was printed, not of what was calibrated.\n\nThe design chart\n\nThe board, the canvas and a canvas texture are axis-aligned rectangles of ONE chart, the design chart: the plane coordinates of a planar target's nominal points, x to the right and y up as the printed side is seen from the front, in metres for a physical target (lengths at the constructors, plain metres stored) and in the design's plain numbers for a unitless one. Each rectangle is its lower-left corner (origin) and its size; the board's placement on the canvas is board.origin − canvas.origin.\n\nFields\n\nsize::Size2{T}: the page, width × height, by the board's length rule (lengths stored in metres, a quantity of any other dimension a DimensionError; or plain numbers; non-negative).\norigin::SVector{2, Float64}: its lower-left corner in the design chart.\ntexture::Union{Nothing, CanvasTexture{T}}: the printed image (CanvasTexture), or nothing; in the canvas's form (FlexUnits' DimensionError otherwise).\n\nConstructors\n\nCanvas(Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm), [-20.0mm, -15.0mm])\nCanvas(Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm), [-20.0mm, -15.0mm];\n       texture = CanvasTexture(\"canvas.png\", sha, Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm), [-20.0mm, -15.0mm]))\nCanvas(Size2(width = 297.0, height = 210.0))                       # a unitless canvas\n\nRect(canvas) is its rectangle in its own numbers. ustrip(u, canvas) and with_unit(canvas, u) move it (and its texture) between the two forms, as they move a board. A PlanarTarget carries it beside its board (canvas = …; canvas(t) reads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Canvas"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CanvasTexture","page":"Cameras","text":"CanvasTexture{T}\n\nThe image printed on a Canvas: a reference to the image file and the rectangle of the design chart it covers. VGC never reads the image; the reference is what a reader resolves and checks.\n\nFields\n\npath::String: the image file, RELATIVE to the file that names it (a rig or board file); an absolute path is an ArgumentError.\nsha256::String: the SHA-256 of the image file's bytes, 64 lowercase hex digits (an ArgumentError otherwise) — the reader's integrity check.\nsize::Size2{T}, origin::SVector{2, Float64}: the rectangle the image covers, by the board's rule (lengths stored in metres or plain numbers; its lower-left corner in the design chart, plain metres when physical). The size is positive.\n\nThe image's first row lies along the rectangle's top edge (largest y) and its first column along the left edge (smallest x); its outer pixel edges lie on the rectangle's edges, so each pixel of a W × H image covers width/W × height/H of the chart.\n\nConstructors\n\nCanvasTexture(\"board.png\", sha, Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm))                    # corner at the design origin\nCanvasTexture(\"board.png\", sha, Size2(width = 297mm, height = 210mm), [-10.0mm, -5.0mm])\nCanvasTexture(\"board.png\", sha, Size2(width = 297.0, height = 210.0), [-10.0, -5.0])     # a unitless one\n\nRect(texture) is the covered rectangle in its own numbers (plain metres when physical).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CanvasTexture"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cameras/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_overlap-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:BoundedCamera}}","page":"Cameras","text":"view_overlap(cameras; boundary_points = 512) -> StructVector\nview_overlap(a, b; boundary_points = 512) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe directions two frames of a camera turning about its centre both image, for every pair i < j of cameras: one row (; i, j, solid_angle, fraction_i, fraction_j) per pair, K(K − 1)/2 rows in the order (1, 2), (1, 3), …, (2, 3), … as a StructVector, so rows.solid_angle is a column. solid_angle is the solid angle (sr) of the intersection of the two frames' footprints on the sphere; fraction_i is it over frame i's own footprint, and fraction_j over frame j's. A pair of cameras a, b gives the one row, i = 1, j = 2. For a panorama's pair selection: a pair worth matching shares a fraction of its view.\n\nEach camera is a BoundedCamera of an OrientedCamera, a frame posed by its orientation alone, BoundedCamera(PosedCamera(model, relabel(CameraFrame, WorldFrame, RotationMap(R_k))), extent) for a camera → world rotation R_k, d_world = R_k · d_camera, whose model is a CameraModel (another model, a grid one, is an ArgumentError). Frames may have different cameras. A camera posed by an EuclideanMap has a position, and an unposed one no orientation: both are an ArgumentError naming the fix. Frame k's footprint is the set of directions R_k · backproject(model, u) over its region Ωk, [`imagedregion](@ref)(camera): the sensor ∩ the camera's lens coverage (its image circle, [lenscoverage](@ref)) ∩ where the lens model is valid ([validdomain`](@ref)). Each frame carries its own lens coverage; a camera whose whole sensor is lit gives the extent where the lens model holds, which is not where light falls for a lens whose image circle lies inside the sensor.\n\nThe pair (i, j) is computed once, in frame i's image:\n\nΩi is a ring of `boundarypoints` pixels along its boundary: the extent's edges (every corner exact), the circle's samples, and their convex intersection by Core's Sutherland–Hodgman.\nPj is frame j's ring drawn into image i: each pixel back-projected by j, rotated by `Riᵀ Rjand projected byi, each edge split into geodesic pieces no longer in imagei` than Ωi's own edges. A direction frame i does not image (behind a perspective camera, past a lens's fold, NaN, or one whose pixel does not back-project to it within 10⁻⁶ rad) has no pixel; the ring is cut there and continued along a far circle about i's principal point, four times Ωi's radius, at the direction's azimuth, so it stays a closed curve that runs outside Ωi wherever i cannot draw it.\nSutherland–Hodgman clips Pj by the convex Ωi. A curve of any shape keeps its winding number inside the clip, so the clipped ring's area is the integral of Pj's winding number over Ωi; pieces a non-convex Pj splits into come back joined by edges along Ωi's boundary, traversed once each way, which add nothing.\nThat area is a solid angle: the clipped ring's vertices are back-projected by i to unit bearings and joined by geodesics, and their fan of signed spherical triangles from i's optical axis is summed (Van Oosterom & Strackee, tan(E/2) = a·(b × c) / (1 + a·b + b·c + c·a)). Along Ω_i's boundary the polygon follows the footprint's own geodesic edges: a vertex there is moved onto its edge's great circle, and a clipped edge along a sensor side gets the ring's vertices between its ends (a lens bends the side).\nThe detours of item 2 are where the winding can be wrong, and only by an integer n constant over Ωi: a detour outside Ωi can only wind around all of it. One interior point of Ωi, the candidate farthest from Pj, fixes n: its winding number against whether frame j images its direction (project by j, in Ωj, back-projected within 10⁻⁶ rad). The overlap is the area less n times frame i's footprint. The same correction covers a footprint j that wraps frame i's backward direction (a wide fisheye behind a narrow lens), whose ring winds the other way round Ωi.\n\nEach footprint's own solid angle is the same fan over its ring from its own optical axis, which needs no hemisphere: the sum is the enclosed area for any apex whose antipode is outside the region, and a footprint never reaches its camera's backward axis. So a lens past 180° (an 8 mm fisheye) is handled; a footprint whose ring comes within 0.05 rad (≈ 3°) of its camera's backward axis is an ArgumentError, as is a region whose boundary the lens cannot back-project and a principal point outside its region.\n\nA perspective camera maps geodesics to lines, so its footprints are exact great-circle polygons and their overlap is exact at any boundary_points. A curved boundary (an image circle, a distorted side) is a polygon of boundary_points vertices, so the error is the chords' sag, O(1/boundary_points²): 1.6 × 10⁻⁵ sr of a 0.77 sr cap at 512 (2 × 10⁻⁵ relative), a quarter of it at 1024. A footprint that coincides with another (the same camera and rotation) clips to itself: fraction 1. A pair whose footprints' bounding caps about the optical axes are disjoint is 0 without clipping.\n\ncam = CameraModel(K; lens = KannalaBrandt(-0.135))\nbare(R) = BoundedCamera(PosedCamera(cam, relabel(CameraFrame, WorldFrame, RotationMap(R))), sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX250\"))\nframe(R) = (b = bare(R); with_lens_coverage(b, image_circle(b, 6.0mm)))   # its lens coverage: the datasheet's 6 mm circle\nframes = [frame(RotY(2π * k / 11)) for k in 0:10]   # 11 frames about the vertical axis\nrows = view_overlap(frames)\nrows[1].fraction_i                                # the share of frame 1's view frame 2 sees\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_overlap"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matching/#Correspondence-API","page":"Correspondences","text":"Correspondence types and the solver layer built on them. Terminology: a correspondence (Correspondence) is a pair; a correspondence set (an n-tuple of mutually corresponding elements, e.g. multiview tracks) lives in VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures as CorrespondenceSet; the columnar collection of pair-correspondences is Correspondences.\n\nDescriptor matching (NNMatch, match, strategies) lives in VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.","title":"Correspondence API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matching/#Correspondences-and-Endpoint-Lifts","page":"Correspondences","text":"","title":"Correspondences and Endpoint Lifts"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matching/#Solver-Layer:-Frames,-Verbs,-Registry","page":"Correspondences","text":"","title":"Solver Layer: Frames, Verbs, Registry"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matching/#Index","page":"Correspondences","text":"Pages = [\"matching.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Correspondences","page":"Correspondences","text":"Correspondences(sources, targets; attrs...)\n\nColumnar correspondence set: parallel sources/targets vectors plus any number of index-aligned attribute columns (score, ratio, weights, covariance, …) that filter and view IN LOCKSTEP with the endpoints.\n\ncs[i] returns the i-th correspondence as a Correspondence (= Pair); cs[mask] / view(cs, idx) subset every column together — no more hand-written scored[keep]; weights[keep] co-filtering.\n\nAttribute columns are reached by property: cs.score, cs.ratio; presence is queried with hasattr. Endpoint columns are reached through the protocol verbs sources / targets (NOT .first/.second — that duck-typing is retired).\n\nExample\n\ncs = Correspondences(src_pts, dst_pts; score = scores, ratio = ratios)\nsources(cs)          # the source column\ncs.score             # the score column\ninliers = cs[mask]   # endpoints AND attributes filtered together\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Correspondences"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MappedColumn","page":"Correspondences","text":"MappedColumn(f, parent::AbstractVector)\n\nLazy map(f, parent) view: m[i] == f(parent[i]), no allocation, no materialization. Element type is inferred via Base.promote_op.\n\nUsed by lift so that endpoint conversions (centroid extraction, z=0 planar lifts, bearing lifts) cost nothing until a solver actually indexes the column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MappedColumn"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PointSet","page":"Correspondences","text":"PointSet(points; attrs...)\n\nA set of single-point observations (a contour's points for a conic, a circle, a line) with index-aligned attribute columns, the one-observation counterpart of Correspondences: xs[i] is the point, xs.Σ (the points' covariances), xs.sqrt_weights and any other column are reached by property, and xs[mask] / view(xs, idx) subset every column together. What the batched Sampson functions (sampson_residuals!) read per element; a plain vector of points is a PointSet with no columns, and withattrs(points; attrs...) makes one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PointSet"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_order-Tuple{AbstractDict, Vararg{AbstractDict}}","page":"Correspondences","text":"block_order(groups::AbstractDict, given::AbstractDict...) -> Vector{K}\n\nThe block order of grouped data: the sorted keys of groups (the order a grouped estimator stacks its per-group unknowns and stores on its result). Every given dict — the per-group models a caller supplies, keyed by the same K — must have exactly the same key set: an ArgumentError otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.block_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.hasattr-Tuple{Correspondences, Symbol}","page":"Correspondences","text":"hasattr(cs::Correspondences, k::Symbol) -> Bool\n\nWhether the correspondence set carries the attribute column k (e.g. hasattr(cs, :score) before reading cs.score).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.hasattr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift-Tuple{Any}","page":"Correspondences","text":"lift(cs; source = identity, target = identity) -> Correspondences\n\nLazy endpoint conversion: returns a Correspondences whose source/target columns apply source/target element-wise on access (no materialization); attribute columns are forwarded untouched. Works on any container that satisfies the sources/targets protocol.\n\nThis replaces eager extraction helpers (point_correspondences) and hand-written z=0 lifts: compose whatever endpoint conversion the solver needs at the call site, pay only when the solver indexes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.planar_lift-Tuple{Any}","page":"Correspondences","text":"planar_lift(cs) -> Correspondences\n\npoints with the source side additionally lifted onto the z=0 plane: 2D source centroids become Point3(x, y, 0) (the planar-board world convention), targets become 2D centroids. The correspondence set is then shaped for absolute-pose estimation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.planar_lift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.points-Tuple{Any}","page":"Correspondences","text":"points(cs) -> Correspondences\n\nlift both endpoints through centroid: detections, frames, discs, Attributed/Uncertain wrappers — anything with a centroid — become bare points, lazily.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sources-Tuple{Correspondences}","page":"Correspondences","text":"sources(cs) -> AbstractVector\n\nThe source column of a correspondence container. Protocol verb — defined for Correspondences, the legacy StructArray{<:Pair} columnar layout, Attributed-wrapped (scored) correspondence vectors, and plain AbstractVector{<:Pair} (lazy view). Solver code MUST go through sources/targets rather than .first/.second.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sources"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.split_blocks-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector{K}, Any}} where K","page":"Correspondences","text":"split_blocks(v::AbstractVector, ids::AbstractVector{K}, counts) -> Dict{K, Vector}\n\nThe inverse of stacking per-group vectors in block order: v's consecutive blocks of counts[j] entries, keyed by ids[j] (a copy per group). A DimensionMismatch unless ids and counts have one entry per group and the counts sum to length(v).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.split_blocks"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.targets-Tuple{Correspondences}","page":"Correspondences","text":"targets(cs) -> AbstractVector\n\nThe target column of a correspondence container. See sources.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.targets"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.withattrs-Tuple{Correspondences}","page":"Correspondences","text":"withattrs(cs::Correspondences; attrs...) -> Correspondences\n\nA copy of cs with the given attribute columns merged in (same-name columns replaced). Endpoint columns are shared, not copied. The idiom for attaching derived per-correspondence data — e.g. IRLS weights after a fit — so it subsets in lockstep from then on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.withattrs"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Essential5pt","page":"Correspondences","text":"Essential5pt(backend = NativeSolver())\n\nMinimal 5-point essential-matrix solver (Nistér; solve verb over the essential_5pt kernel). sample_size 5, max_solutions 10.\n\nCorrespondence endpoints are bearing vectors (StaticVector{3}), and only those. A calibrated 2D point is a MethodError, not an input.\n\nWhy a 2-vector is refused rather than lifted\n\nUntil 0.29 a calibrated 2-vector was accepted and lifted to (x, y, 1). That lift places the ray on the z = 1 plane, which cannot represent a ray at or past 90° from the optical axis at all — so it silently excluded every fisheye and omnidirectional camera, which are the cameras this stack exists for. The failure was invisible: the lift always succeeded and returned a plausible EssentialMat, computed from a chart that could not hold the data.\n\nRefusing by TYPE is the point. A caller holding pixels builds bearings with backproject — or backproject_with_jacobian when the pixel Jacobian is wanted too, which is what keeps a residual threshold in pixels.\n\nThe kernel computes in Float64; returned models are EssentialMat{Float64}. The epipolar constraint is scale-invariant per endpoint, so the algebra does not require unit norm — but unit bearings are the stack's convention and what backproject returns.\n\nWhat the type refusal does NOT catch\n\nIt catches a 2-vector. It cannot catch a 3-vector that is still on the z = 1 plane — (x, y, 1) is a perfectly good StaticVector{3}, and because the epipolar algebra is scale-invariant per endpoint, such a point even produces the right answer for any ray the chart can represent. The exclusion of rays at and past 90° survives that lift unchanged; it has simply stopped being visible in the type.\n\nSo this signature is a NECESSARY condition, not a guarantee, and it should not be read as one. The guarantee has to come from where the vectors are BUILT — backproject(model, u), which is defined for every ray the camera can see — not from where they are consumed. A caller that reaches a 3-vector by dividing through by z has moved the same defect one layer up and past this gate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Essential5pt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Fundamental7pt","page":"Correspondences","text":"Fundamental7pt(backend = NativeSolver())\n\nMinimal 7-point fundamental-matrix solver (solve verb over the fundamental_7pt kernel). sample_size 7, max_solutions 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Fundamental7pt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Homography4pt","page":"Correspondences","text":"Homography4pt(backend = NativeSolver())\n\nMinimal 4-point homography solver (solve verb over the homography_4pt kernel). sample_size 4, max_solutions 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Homography4pt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Homography5ptRadial","page":"Correspondences","text":"Homography5ptRadial(; backend = NativeSolver(), extra_check = false)\n\nMinimal 5-point radially distorted homography solver — one-sided one-parameter division model on the TARGET side (solve verb over the homography_5pt_radial kernel; Wadenbäck et al. (2026)). sample_size 5, max_solutions 3 (at most 1 with extra_check, which keeps only the root most consistent with the redundant equation — the reference enables it inside its RANSAC wrapper).\n\nDeliberately NOT entered in the minimal_solvers registry: the division coefficient k is only meaningful in roughly unit-scale target coordinates, a convention generic pixel correspondences don't carry (cf. the calibrated convention Essential5pt presumes). Opt in explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Homography5ptRadial"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MinimalSolver","page":"Correspondences","text":"MinimalSolver{M}\n\nAbstract parent for exactly-determined minimal solvers producing models of type M. Each algorithm is its own singleton subtype (two solvers may share a model type but require different correspondence types — p3p vs. circle relative pose both yield poses).\n\nInterface (all required):\n\nsolve(s, cs, idx) -> FixedModels{max_solutions(s), M} — ALWAYS a FixedModels; failure is count == 0, never nothing\nsample_size(s) — minimal sample cardinality\nmax_solutions(s) — solution-count bound (the N in FixedModels)\nmodel_type(s) == M\n\nSolvers with pluggable numeric backends carry a backend::AbstractSolverBackend field (default NativeSolver()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.MinimalSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.P3P","page":"Correspondences","text":"P3P(backend = NativeSolver())\n\nPerspective-3-point absolute-pose solver (solve verb over the p3p kernel). sample_size 3, max_solutions 4.\n\nCorrespondence contract: sources are 3D world points, targets are unit bearing vectors (pixel targets are lifted to bearings beforehand, e.g. lift(cs; target = u -> backproject(model, u))). Models are Extrinsics3 with x_cam = R·X + t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.P3P"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Rotation2pt","page":"Correspondences","text":"Rotation2pt()\n\nMinimal 2-point rotation solver: the rotation R with vᵢ ≈ R·uᵢ from two pairs of bearing vectors (StaticVector{3}, any nonzero length — a direction is its unit vector) — the infinite homography of a calibrated camera under a pure rotation, or any two direction pairs. kabsch on the two unit pairs (solve verb); sample_size 2, max_solutions 1.\n\nThe rotation is exact on two consistent pairs (the two bearings of a view span a plane, and their cross product completes the frame). A sample whose two bearings are parallel or antiparallel in either view (sine of their angle below 1e-12) leaves a one-parameter family of rotations and returns count == 0. Two pairs whose angles differ (inconsistent with any rotation) still give the least-squares rotation; the residuals judge it.\n\nDeliberately NOT entered in the minimal_solvers registry: its correspondence type, bearing ↔ bearing, is Essential5pt's too, and the registry answers one model per element-type pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Rotation2pt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.induced_models-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{Type{S}, Type{T}}} where {S, T}","page":"Correspondences","text":"induced_models(S, T)  /  induced_models(cs) -> Tuple{Vararg{Type}}\n\nThe model types estimable from correspondences with element types (S, T) (or from the container cs directly): model_type.(minimal_solvers(S, T)).\n\nAt (3D, 3D) this returns (Extrinsics3, EssentialMat), which are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE — a bare StaticVector{3} is both a world point and a bearing, and no dispatch can tell them apart. That is reported rather than resolved here; minimal_solver disambiguates by model type. See minimal_solvers for why the registry is built this way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.induced_models"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.max_solutions","page":"Correspondences","text":"max_solutions(s::MinimalSolver) -> Int\n\nUpper bound on the number of candidate models one minimal sample can produce (the N of the returned FixedModels{N,M}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.max_solutions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_solver-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{Type{M}, Type{S}, Type{T}}} where {M, S, T}","page":"Correspondences","text":"minimal_solver(::Type{M}, ::Type{S}, ::Type{T}) -> MinimalSolver{<:M}\n\nRegistry lookup: the minimal solver producing models of type M from (S, T) correspondences. Errors if the registry has no such solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_solver"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_solvers-Tuple{Type, Type}","page":"Correspondences","text":"minimal_solvers(::Type{S}, ::Type{T}) -> Tuple\n\nThe minimal solvers applicable to correspondences with source element type S and target element type T. The registry is extended by adding a method — registering a new solver family requires NO edits to the verb layer or any problem struct.\n\nRegistered pairs:\n\n2D ↔ 2D points: Homography4pt, Fundamental7pt. Both are frame-covariant — a homography relates two planes, a fundamental matrix two images, in whatever 2D frame the caller holds — so here the element types really do determine that the solver applies.\n3D ↔ 3D: P3P and Essential5pt.\n\nThe 3D pair is ambiguous, and the registry says so rather than choosing\n\nP3P and Essential5pt are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE and cannot be told apart by element type: P3P's source is a world POINT and its target a unit BEARING, while both of Essential5pt's endpoints are bearings. A bare StaticVector{3} is all three of those things, and no dispatch can separate them. What actually distinguishes them is the frame the caller built the data in, which lives in the frame tags — not in the element type.\n\nSo the two queries answer differently, on purpose:\n\nminimal_solver(M, S, T) disambiguates by MODEL type and is exact: ask for EssentialMat and you get Essential5pt, ask for Extrinsics3 and you get P3P. This is the lookup consumers use.\ninduced_models(S, T) returns BOTH, because \"what is estimable from a pair of 3-vectors\" honestly has two answers and the types do not say which. It is not a claim that both apply to your data at once.\n\nWhy Essential5pt is not at (2D, 2D) any more\n\nUntil 0.29 it was, carrying the note that it \"presumes calibrated coordinates — the element types cannot express that; it is the caller's frame convention\". That was a claim about the CALLER'S DATA written down as a property of the code, and it was false in the way that matters: the lift it relied on sent a calibrated 2-vector to the z = 1 plane, which cannot represent a ray at or past 90°, silently excluding every fisheye and omnidirectional camera. Registering it there also made induced_models OFFER an essential matrix for plane data. The lift is deleted, the registration moved, and a 2-vector endpoint is now a MethodError at the solver.\n\nThe ambiguity that remains at (3D, 3D) is of a different kind: under-determined by the element type, but correct whenever the caller's data matches the solver's stated contract. The old (2D, 2D) entry was not under-determined — it was wrong.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.minimal_solvers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.model_type-Union{Tuple{MinimalSolver{M}}, Tuple{M}} where M","page":"Correspondences","text":"model_type(s) -> Type\n\nThe model type produced by a MinimalSolver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.model_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sample_size","page":"Correspondences","text":"sample_size(s::MinimalSolver) -> Int\n\nNumber of correspondences in the solver's minimal sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sample_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.solve","page":"Correspondences","text":"solve(solver::MinimalSolver, cs, idx) -> FixedModels\n\nRun a minimal solver on the sample idx drawn from the correspondence container cs (any container satisfying the sources/ targets protocol). ALWAYS returns FixedModels; a degenerate sample yields count == 0. This is the single public solve protocol — the positional kernels underneath are implementation detail.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.solve"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#Coordinate-Frames","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore records which coordinate frame a map consumes and produces at the type level (since 0.7). A frame tag is a singleton type — zero fields, zero runtime cost — carried by EuclideanMap and HomographyMap as trailing Src/Dst type parameters. Composition and inversion are then frame-checked by dispatch: the pose-vs-extrinsics direction becomes a dispatch fact instead of a docstring convention.","title":"Coordinate Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#The-Frame{N}-hierarchy","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"Frame{N} is the abstract supertype of frame tags of dimension N. The hierarchy is open: downstream packages declare their own frames\n\nstruct LidarFrame <: Frame{3} end\n\nand participate in frame-checked composition with no changes to VisualGeometryToolkitCore. The tags shipped here:\n\ntag dim meaning\nUnlabeledFrame{N} N \"no frame recorded\" — the gradual-typing wildcard\nWorldFrame 3 the world (scene) frame\nCameraFrame 3 the camera-centered frame (+Z along the optical axis)\nViewFrame{id} 3 the camera frame of a specific view (since 0.8)\nTargetFrame{id} 3 the body frame of a specific calibration target\nBodyFrame{id} 3 the frame of a specific rigid body (a tracked or mounted rig)\nTargetPlaneFrame 2 calibration-target plane frame\nPixelFrame{Origin} 2 pixel frame tagged by origin convention (:colmap, :julia, :opencv)\nImageFrame{id} 2 canonical pixel frame of view id\nRetinalFrame{id} and subtypes 2 ray frames of the viewing sphere (see The Framed Imaging Chain)\nDistortedFrame{id} 2 post-lens, pre-calibration frame","title":"The Frame{N} hierarchy"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#The-monogram-bridge","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"The Drake monogram symbol X_AB — \"pose of frame B expressed in frame A\", which maps B-coordinates to A-coordinates — corresponds to EuclideanMap{..., Src = B, Dst = A}. This bridge is stated once and used throughout: a camera pose is X_WC (Src = CameraFrame, Dst = WorldFrame), camera extrinsics are X_CW, and a two-view relative transform is X_{V₂V₁} (Src = ViewFrame{id₁}, Dst = ViewFrame{id₂}).\n\nThe typed algebra follows: inv swaps the frame tags (X_AB⁻¹ = X_BA), so inv(::CameraPose) is a CameraExtrinsics — the frame-pair aliases CameraPose, CameraExtrinsics, and the concrete solver currency Extrinsics3 are plain EuclideanMaps under labeled frames, not wrappers.","title":"The monogram bridge"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#The-wildcard-truth-table","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"A ∘ B applies B first; the junction is B's Dst meeting A's Src. The result always keeps B's Src and A's Dst. UnlabeledFrame is the composition wildcard:\n\nB.Dst A.Src junction result\nF (labeled) F (same) checked composes\nF (labeled) G ≠ F rejected MethodError\nany UnlabeledFrame unchecked composes\nUnlabeledFrame any unchecked composes\n\nThe same table governs every frame-checked junction in the package: EuclideanMap/HomographyMap composition, the imaging-chain stages (FramedStage), the slots of an EpipolarMap under ∘, and frame-labeled propagate. A labeled-labeled mismatch is always an explicit MethodError — never a silent fall-through to an unchecked composition.\n\nThis rule is deliberately anti-viral: every constructor that does not state frames produces Src = Dst = UnlabeledFrame{N}, so pre-frame code compiles and behaves unchanged, and labels never force themselves onto code that does not opt in.","title":"The wildcard truth table"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#Escape-hatches:-relabel-and-unlabel","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"relabel replaces the frame tags of a map (zero-cost, unchecked — a deliberate override for trust boundaries such as dataset loaders and solver outputs); unlabel erases them, making the map compose with anything under the wildcard rule. Both verbs are house-wide: they apply to maps, epipolar forms (slot tags), statistical forms (CovMat/PrecMat frame tags, one tag rather than two), and batched stages.\n\nE = EuclideanMap(R, t)                          # UnlabeledFrame both ends\nX_CW = relabel(WorldFrame, CameraFrame, E)      # now checked as extrinsics\nX_WC = inv(X_CW)                                # frames swap: a pose\nunlabel(X_WC)                                   # back to the wildcard","title":"Escape hatches: relabel and unlabel"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#View-identity-symbol-frames","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"Since 0.8, Symbol type parameters distinguish instances, not just conventions: ViewFrame{:left} is a different frame than ViewFrame{:right}, so a labeled junction between two cameras' frames is a MethodError instead of a silent CameraFrame collision. The 2D counterpart ImageFrame{id} tags which view an image point belongs to; origin conventions remain the orthogonal PixelFrame{Origin} axis (convert origins first, then label the view — an ImageFrame{id} point is always read in the canonical :colmap convention).\n\nStereoRig mints per-view identities on request (see Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty); the reserved symbol :_ is the designated anonymous view identity used by the imaging-chain mints when no rig is in play — a real label (junctions still check by frame kind), not a wildcard.","title":"View-identity symbol frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#Pixel-origin-conversion","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"Origin conversion is a labeled rigid map between two pixel frames, built by the constructor-as-factory on the frame types:\n\nT = EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia})\nT.t == Vec2(0.5, 0.5)          # true\npt_julia = T(pt_colmap)\n\nThis is the only origin-conversion API; it composes (frame-checked) with other labeled 2D maps and fuses in image_warp chains like any EuclideanMap{2}.","title":"Pixel-origin conversion"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#Frames-and-units","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"A frame tag says which coordinates a map consumes and produces. A unit says what the numbers measure, and the two are independent. A pose between two 3D frames holds its translation in metres (see Coordinate Transforms), while pixel coordinates carry no unit at all: their PixelFrame tag is their unit. A pixel is dimensionless in FlexUnits, so px quantities are used only for sizes and densities. Both leave a value at the same place. An estimator strips the unit and normalizes the frame once at entry, and it restores both once at exit.","title":"Frames and units"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frames/#See-also","page":"Coordinate Frames","text":"Epipolar Forms — frame tags on the slots of a bilinear form\nStereo Rigs and Uncertainty — frames-native rigs, frame-labeled covariance\nThe Framed Imaging Chain — the frame-to-frame stages of one camera\nFrames API","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Batch-API","page":"Batched","text":"Per-point maps over arrays, the B-spline kernels and the thin-plate spline. See the Batches guide; the lens surfaces (project!, …) are documented with the Lenses API.","title":"Batch API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Pointwise-maps","page":"Batched","text":"","title":"Pointwise maps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#B-spline-Kernels","page":"Batched","text":"The B-spline algebra on the CPU is BSplineKit's (knot vectors, evaluation and derivatives, Galerkin matrices, recombined bases). Core keeps only what a GPU kernel needs and BSplineKit cannot give it: the scalar Cox–de Boor basis bspline_basis(knots, Val(p), x) — an SVector of every basis function at x on a static knot vector, isbits and generic in the element type, so it runs in a kernel and under ForwardDiff (hoisted from RadiometricCamera, its arithmetic unchanged; equal to BSplineKit's evaluate_all on the same knots) — its broadcast over a device array, and the tensor-product surface/4D spline kernels built on it; the ChainRulesCore rrules attach via the package extension.\n\nusing BSplineKit: BSplineBasis, BSplineOrder, knots\nk = SVector{11}(knots(BSplineBasis(BSplineOrder(4), range(-0.5, 0.5; length = 5))))   # 7 cubic control points on [-½, ½]\nN = bspline_basis(k, Val(3), 0.12)               # SVector{7}, sum(N) == 1","title":"B-spline Kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#The-bending-energy-of-a-B-spline","page":"Batched","text":"bending_gram is ∫∫ f_xx² + 2 f_xy² + f_yy² of a tensor-product B-spline as a quadratic form in its coefficients, from BSplineKit's Galerkin matrices; bending_rows factors it into a regularizer's rows and bending_stencils gives it cell by cell on a large uniform lattice (cubic_lattice_basis, Core's generic-model lattice). The natural boundary condition f'' = 0 of BSplineKit's recombined basis is natural_extrapolation, the rule BoundaryRule applies to a lattice's nodes outside an imaged region by default (order = 2); its order = 3 is the curvature-preserving Δ³c = 0.","title":"The bending energy of a B-spline"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Penalized-least-squares","page":"Batched","text":"Every penalized solve of the stack is min ‖A x − b‖² + λ‖D (x − c)‖², one owner per piece. The operator D: difference_rows (orders 1–3 on a masked lattice or a texel page, along chosen axes; gradient_pairs its isotropic order-1 pairs) or bending_rows (the bending energy), and row_stencils its rows as a nonlinear solve's residual blocks. The weight's unit: penalty_scale with mean_positive_eigenvalue (m̄). The linear solve on sparse operators, on any KernelAbstractions device: cgls on a PenalizedOperator of CSRMatrixes, or normal_cg on a normal operator AᵀA + DᵀWD that is only applied (its rows never stored): one conjugate-gradient iteration, Jacobi-preconditioned on request, one stopping rule relative to ‖Aᵀb + DᵀWh‖. The weight's choice by generalized cross-validation is VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's (gcv_curve, gcv_select). No penalty fixes a gauge: a direction neither the data nor the penalty holds is held exactly by its caller.","title":"Penalized least squares"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Vignetting","page":"Batched","text":"A camera's relative illumination V on the sensor as a B-spline in V on clamped knots (clamped_knots, bspline_basis), linear in its control points: a tensor-product surface over a sensor box (TensorProductVignetting) or a profile in the radius about a centre (RadialVignetting). A model is callable, v(u), and isbits, so a batch of sensor points goes through pointwise! on a device; coefficient_jacobian is its row of a least squares in the control points, active_coefficients that row's nonzeros, and pole_regular_basis the radial profiles smooth at their centre. The estimators that fit them are their owners': a radiometric calibration's, a panorama's self-calibration. VIGNETTING_TAGS is the fields' tag registry (\"tensor_product\", \"radial\", \"log\"), the type a file or a configuration names them by.\n\nA radial profile reads its normalized radius on a chart of the distance from its centre (RADIAL_CHARTS, radial_chart): linear, ρ = d / radius, constant beyond the radius; or projective (chart = :projective, projective_chart), ρ = d / (radius + d), ½ at the radius and never beyond the knots (projective_knots: uniform in the distance up to the radius, one tail span beyond), so a profile whose centre moves still covers every pixel. with_center moves the centre and center_gradient is ∂V/∂c, for a fit with the centre as a parameter. Ported from RadiometricCamera, bit for bit.\n\nv = RadialVignetting(12, SVector(2128.0, 1424.0), 1700.0)   # control points, centre and radius (camera pixels); V ≡ 1\nw = RadialVignetting(13, SVector(2128.0, 1424.0), 2560.0; chart = :projective)   # ρ = ½ at the farthest corner\nv = with_control_points(v, pole_regular_basis(v) * y)        # a profile with V′(0) = 0\nv(SVector(2500.0, 1300.0))                                   # V at a sensor point","title":"Vignetting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Lighting","page":"Batched","text":"The scene's light as a radiometric camera model sees it: an EnvironmentLight, radiance from every direction given by a directional map on a sphere of finite radius about a centre, looked up where a ray from the shaded point leaves the sphere (exit_direction; the distant light is its limit as the radius grows). The map is a SphericalHarmonicMap: real spherical harmonics per channel (sh_basis, orthonormal, no Condon–Shortley phase, index sh_index(l, m) = l² + l + m + 1), so the radiance is linear in the coefficients and coefficient_jacobian is its row. Everything is isbits and allocation-free, for a kernel; the centre and radius are lengths (ustrip(m, light) for the kernel's plain form). Hoisted from RadiometricCamera, bit for bit.\n\nlight = EnvironmentLight(SphericalHarmonicMap(a, 2), SVector(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), 1.5)   # 3 × 9 coefficients, centre, radius\nradiance(light, x, ω)                                    # per channel, arriving at x from direction ω\ncoefficients(light) * coefficient_jacobian(light, x, ω)  # the same, as a row of a least squares","title":"Lighting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Blur","page":"Batched","text":"One blur of an imaging model — a camera's in-focus blur (diffraction, the sensor's anti-aliasing filter, demosaicing), a print's own blur — as a GaussianBlur. A point spread function is a probability density (where a photon from a point source lands), and a Gaussian blur is the density N(0, Σ), so it is held by its covariance, a CovMat{2} (cov; var and std per axis): round, axis-aligned (stored as an SDiagonal) or general, px² on a sensor, m² (quantities) on a print. Several blurs compose into a point spread: two Gaussians in sequence add their covariances (convolve), and a linear map of the plane pushes the covariance forward, Σ ↦ J Σ Jᵀ (propagate).\n\ncamera = GaussianBlur(0.6)                     # round, σ = 0.6 px\nprint  = GaussianBlur(0.02mm, 0.03mm)          # axis-aligned, on the print: m² quantities\ntotal  = convolve(propagate(J, ustrip(mm, print)), camera)   # in mm², through the pixel footprint J (px/mm), then the camera's\nstd(camera)                                    # (0.6, 0.6) px","title":"Blur"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Thin-plate-spline","page":"Batched","text":"f(𝐩) = a₁ + a_x 𝐩_x + a_y 𝐩_y + Σᵢ wᵢ U(𝐩, 𝐩ᵢ) with U = ½ r² ln r², through nodal values on scattered nodes (hoisted from RadiometricCamera, its arithmetic unchanged). ThinPlateSplineSystem is the host-side solver; the evaluator tps_evaluate and its gradient tps_gradient take isbits (nodes, w, a), allocate nothing and run in a KernelAbstractions kernel. tps_coefficients is a loop (no BLAS), and with the pure-bending projection tps_pure_bending it runs in a kernel on an adapted system (Adapt.adapt(backend, tps), a Float32 one by convert_eltype).\n\ntps = ThinPlateSplineSystem(grid_nodes(5))      # 5 × 5 nodes over [-½, ½]²\nw, a = tps_coefficients(tps, values)            # linear in the 25 nodal values\nw, a = tps_coefficients(tps, tps_pure_bending(tps, values))   # the plane dropped first: a HeightField's (w, a)\nf = tps_evaluate(tps.nodes, w, a, SVector(0.1, -0.2))\n∇f = tps_gradient(tps.nodes, w, a, SVector(0.1, -0.2))\n\nIts analogue on the unit sphere, SphericalSplineSystem (Wahba's m = 2 kernel spherical_spline_kernel, a constant in place of the affine part; also hoisted from RadiometricCamera), has the same shape: spherical_spline_coefficients, the isbits evaluator spherical_spline_evaluate, and the shared nodal_weights, bending_energy and bending_factor.","title":"Thin-plate spline"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Special-functions","page":"Batched","text":"Device-safe scalar functions — pure arithmetic, generic over Real, so they compile in a KernelAbstractions kernel at any float width and ForwardDiff Duals flow through: log Γ and the digamma/trigamma series of the Student-t adaptation kernels, the normal CDF normcdf_as (Abramowitz & Stegun 7.1.26) and the dilogarithm dilog.","title":"Special functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Quadrature","page":"Batched","text":"The probabilists' Gauss–Hermite rule gauss_hermite_prob (an expectation over N(0, 1), exact to degree 2n − 1) and the Hammersley points hammersley (a kernel's own low-discrepancy sample from its index).","title":"Quadrature"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#One-dimensional-minimization","page":"Batched","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.grid_brent_minimum scans an evenly spaced grid and refines its least value by Brent's method: the stack's one 1-D minimizer (public, not exported).","title":"One-dimensional minimization"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Kernel-building-blocks","page":"Batched","text":"An in-register LU solve for fixed-size systems and a KernelAbstractions workgroup reduction, for code that writes its own kernels (public, not exported).","title":"Kernel building blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/batched/#Index","page":"Batched","text":"Pages = [\"batched.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OnBackend","page":"Batched","text":"OnBackend(backend; workgroupsize = nothing)\n(exec::OnBackend)(kernel[, ndrange]) -> KernelAbstractions.Kernel\n\nThe stack's ONE kernel-launch policy. Called on a KernelAbstractions @kernel, it instantiates it on backend's launch value: one per backend type, so a kernel compiles once whichever value of that type the caller holds (Core's CUDA extension compiles every CUDA launch with always_inline = true, the default_device() it registers, and get_backend(::CuArray)'s CUDABackend() launches the same), with the workgroup workgroupsize names, per backend:\n\nnothing: the backend's choice (on the CPU, KernelAbstractions' default: up to 1024 items as one workgroup on the calling task, a larger range in groups of 1024 items over the threads).\none work item (1, (1, 1), (1, 1, 1)): one item per workgroup on the CPU, so a kernel whose work item is a long loop (a pool's (pair, model[, grid point]) item) runs its items on every thread where the default's 1024-item groups leave most threads idle; on a device, one warp (32 items) per workgroup: one item would leave 31 of 32 lanes idle, and the backend's own choice puts these small ranges of long items on few multiprocessors (RANSACScoringDoneRight's grids on a B200: the Student-t grid 30.6 ms at one item, 1.55 at the backend's choice, 1.35 at one warp; its mixture marginal 62.1, 90.4 and 17.7 ms).\nany other size (a kernel's tile, a measured device size): that size, on every backend.\n\nA static ndrange instantiates a statically sized kernel at workgroupsize as given (the CPU's allocation-free one-item launch). Every stack kernel is instantiated here.\n\nAs pointwise!'s exec, it is that one kernel's launch; without it the kernel runs on get_backend(out) with the defaults. A functor whose fields are each one of its type parameters or an isbits value reaches the kernel as its fields, rebuilt on the device: one large argument costs local memory there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OnBackend"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pointwise!-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, AbstractArray, Vararg{AbstractArray}}} where F","page":"Batched","text":"pointwise!(f, out, xs...; exec = OnBackend(get_backend(out))) -> out\n\nout[i] = f(xs[1][i], xs[2][i], …) for every index, as ONE KernelAbstractions kernel on the backend that owns out (every backend, the CPU's included), or on the backend OnBackend exec names. out and xs share one shape (no broadcasting: a DimensionMismatch otherwise). f is any per-point function; for a GPU array it must be isbits and kernel-lawful (no allocation, no throw, no dynamic dispatch), as every lens and camera map is. On the CPU the launch has finished on return; on a device it is asynchronous, as a device broadcast is: KernelAbstractions.synchronize(backend) before out is read on the host.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pointwise!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pointwise_kernel-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, AbstractArray, Vararg{AbstractArray}}} where F","page":"Batched","text":"pointwise_kernel(f, out, xs...; exec = OnBackend(get_backend(out))) -> (kernel, args, ndrange)\n\nThe one launch pointwise!(f, out, xs...; exec) makes, unlaunched: the KernelAbstractions kernel instance, its arguments and its ndrange, so kernel(args...; ndrange) is that call. pointwise! is exactly this followed by the launch (none for an empty out). Its use is to ask about the launch without restating how it is built: device_resident_threads(pointwise_kernel(f, out, xs...)...) counts the threads of the very kernel pointwise! runs (the functor adapted to the backend and given to the kernel as its fields or whole, the kernel compiled for those arguments). The arguments are checked as pointwise! checks them (DimensionMismatch).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pointwise_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_4d_partial-Tuple{AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, Any, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32, 4}, Int64}","page":"Batched","text":"bspline_4d_partial(u1, u2, u3, u4, degrees, k1, k2, k3, k4, control_points, dim)\n\n∂S/∂udim of the 4D spline at the sample points, via the derivative surface (degree − 1 along dim, differenced CPs, trimmed knots). AD-transparent wrt `controlpoints`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_4d_partial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_basis-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SVector, Integer, Any}","page":"Batched","text":"bspline_basis(knots::SVector{K}, Val(p), x) -> SVector{K − p − 1}\nbspline_basis(knots::SVector{K}, p::Integer, x)\n\nEvery degree-p B-spline basis function on knots at x, N_{1,p}(x), …, N_{K−p−1,p}(x) (Cox–de Boor, Piegl & Tiller 1997): 0/0 is 0, and the last non-empty knot span is closed on the right, so the values sum to one on the whole closed interval [knots[1], knots[K]]. x outside it is clamped onto it (constant extrapolation).\n\nThe basis (its knots and degree) leads, the point follows (signatures.md). The degree is a Val so the result's size is static and nothing allocates; the Integer form is the convenience. Scalar, isbits and generic in the element type (promote_type of x's and the knots'): it runs inside a KernelAbstractions kernel, in Float32, and under ForwardDiff. Why it is Core's and not BSplineKit's: a kernel needs an isbits basis on a static knot vector with a static result; on the CPU, BSplineKit's evaluate_all gives the same values (the tests check it). bspline_basis_iterative is its broadcast over an array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_basis_iterative-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Batched","text":"bspline_basis_iterative(u, degree, knots) -> Vector{<:AbstractArray{Float32}}\n\nFor each of the n_basis = length(knots) − degree − 1 basis functions, its values at every point of the device array u: bspline_basis broadcast over u (on u's backend) in Float32, split per basis function — the structure-of-arrays form the tensor-product kernels below consume. u outside the knots is clamped onto them, as by bspline_basis.\n\nKept because the tensor-product kernels below run on the device array's backend; validated against BSplineKit.BSplineBasis on the downstream demo's actual θ knot vector — see test/bspline_tests.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_basis_iterative"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_surface_partial-Tuple{AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, Any, Any, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"bspline_surface_partial(u, v, degree_u, degree_v, knots_u, knots_v, control_points, along)\n\nFirst partial derivative of a B-spline surface along parameter direction along (1 = u, 2 = v), via the derivative-surface trick: difference the control points, drop one knot at each end, evaluate at degree − 1 along the differentiated direction. Shared by the evaluate_bspline_surface rrule (package extension) and the downstream spline-lens Jacobians.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bspline_surface_partial"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.contract_basis_4d_weighted-Tuple{AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, Any, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray}","page":"Batched","text":"contract_basis_4d_weighted(u1, u2, u3, u4, degrees, k1, k2, k3, k4, w)\n\nΣ_s w_s · (4D basis product at sample s), returned in control-point shape — the single memory-critical primitive behind every 4D control-point gradient (the 4D spline rrule and the downstream boundary/render accumulations). Weights are folded to per-sample scalars FIRST, so only one (n1·n2, N)×(N, n3·n4) GEMM remains and the dense per-sample basis tensor is never materialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.contract_basis_4d_weighted"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.evaluate_bspline_4d-Tuple{AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, Any, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32, 4}}","page":"Batched","text":"evaluate_bspline_4d(u1, u2, u3, u4, degrees, k1, k2, k3, k4, control_points)\n    -> AbstractArray{Float32}\n\n4D tensor-product B-spline: S(u) = Σijkl N1i(u1)·N2j(u2)·N3k(u3)·N4_l(u4)·cp[i,j,k,l]. The four inputs must share one shape (callers materialize broadcasts first); the result has that shape. degrees is indexable per dim (NTuple or Vector). Differentiable wrt the inputs and the control points via the ChainRulesCore rrule (package extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.evaluate_bspline_4d"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.evaluate_bspline_surface-Tuple{AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, Any, Any, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}, AbstractArray{Float32}}","page":"Batched","text":"evaluate_bspline_surface(u, v, degree_u, degree_v, knots_u, knots_v, control_points)\n\nTensor-product B-spline surface evaluation on the compute device (kept for the device: BSplineKit evaluates on the CPU; tested equal to its Spline evaluation on the same knots). Inputs u, v are broadcasted device arrays of equal shape; the surface is evaluated point-wise and an array of the same shape is returned. Differentiable wrt u, v, and the control points via the ChainRulesCore rrule (package extension). Periodicity in the second parameter direction is handled by the CALLER tying the wrap control-point columns — no knot wrapping happens here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.evaluate_bspline_surface"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_gram-Tuple{Any}","page":"Batched","text":"bending_gram(B) -> Matrix{Float64}\nbending_gram(Bx, By) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe bending energy of a B-spline as a quadratic form in its coefficients: ∫ f''² of a curve on the BSplineKit basis B; ∫∫ f_xx² + 2 f_xy² + f_yy² of the tensor-product surface on Bx ⊗ By, vec(c)ᵀ G vec(c) with c laid [i, j] (the first index fastest). BSplineKit's Galerkin matrices of the basis's derivatives, over the bases' domains. Positive semidefinite, the affine functions its null space. A regularizer reads it as rows (bending_rows) or, on a large uniform lattice, cell by cell (bending_stencils, and over a mask of held nodes bending_rows(mask)); a lattice of vector-valued controls applies it per component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_gram"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_rows-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Batched","text":"bending_rows(mask::AbstractMatrix{Bool}; spacing = (1, 1)) -> SparseMatrixCSC{Float64, Int}\n\nThe bending energy's rows over the nodes of a uniform cubic lattice that mask holds (true: a node the rows may read), as a sparse operator on the lattice's column-major vector. These are the bending_stencils of the size(mask) lattice (cell size spacing) over the cells whose 16 nodes are all in mask: each cell's rows L in cell order, the first index fastest. It is the masked bending operator beside the masked differences of difference_rows(mask, order). A regularizer of a lattice that holds only the nodes an imaged region reaches reads it as a penalty operator (mean_positive_eigenvalue, row_stencils). A lattice of vector-valued controls applies it per component. A mask that holds no complete cell gives an operator with no rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_rows-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Batched","text":"bending_rows(G) -> Matrix{Float64}\nbending_rows(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem) -> Matrix{Float64}\nbending_rows(sys::SphericalSplineSystem) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe bending energy's rows, the stack's second penalty operator kind beside difference_rows: a factor L of a positive semidefinite bending form — a B-spline lattice's Gram matrix (bending_gram), or a thin-plate or spherical spline system's Q (bending_energy) — with ‖L c‖² = cᵀ G c to rounding, so √λ · L (c − c₀) are a regularizer's residual rows (the chart LM's row protocol) and L a penalty operator for mean_positive_eigenvalue and cgls. L = Λ₊^½ Vᵀ from G = V Λ Vᵀ, one row per positive eigenvalue (the null space — a system's affine or constant values — and its rounding residue have no row; bending_factor is the same factor with a row for every node, the fixed shape a target calibration kind stores); only LᵀL is defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_stencils-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"bending_stencils(nx, ny; spacing = (1, 1)) -> (; windows, L)\n\nThe bending energy of an nx × ny uniform cubic lattice (cubic_lattice_basis per axis, cell size spacing) cell by cell, for a lattice too large for a dense form: E = Σₖ ‖L c[windows[k]]‖² over the (nx − 3)(ny − 3) cells, windows[k] the 16 linear indices of cell k's 4 × 4 window (the first index fastest) and L the factor of one cell's 16 × 16 form — bending_gram of a one-cell lattice, shared by every cell of a uniform lattice. A row involves one window only, so a solve that blocks the lattice keeps its rows local.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_stencils"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.clamped_knots-Tuple{Integer, Integer, Real, Real}","page":"Batched","text":"clamped_knots(n, p, lo, hi) -> SVector{n + p + 1, Float64}\n\nThe clamped (open) uniform knot vector of n basis functions of degree p on [lo, hi]: p + 1 knots at each end and n − p − 1 interior knots spaced evenly, BSplineKit's knots(BSplineBasis(BSplineOrder(p + 1), range(lo, hi; length = n − p + 1))) as the static vector bspline_basis takes (a kernel's knots). The spline interpolates its first and last coefficient at lo and hi. Needs n > p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.clamped_knots"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cubic_lattice_basis-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"cubic_lattice_basis(n; spacing = 1) -> BSplineKit.BSplineBasis\n\nThe uniform cubic B-spline lattice of n nodes (Core's generic-model lattice) as a BSplineKit basis: knots (−3:n) · spacing, so basis j (node j) is centred at (j − 2) · spacing and the spline's domain is the n − 3 cells [0, (n − 3) · spacing] where the basis sums to one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cubic_lattice_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.difference_rows-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}, Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"difference_rows(mask::AbstractMatrix{Bool}, order; axes = (1, 2)) -> SparseMatrixCSC{Float64, Int}\ndifference_rows(dims::Dims{2}, order; axes = (1, 2))\n\nThe forward differences Δᵒʳᵈᵉʳ (difference_stencil, any order ≥ 1: 1 a gradient, 2 the natural rule's, 3 the P-spline's) of a 2-D lattice of coefficients along the lattice axes (a subset of (1, 2), in that order), as a sparse operator on the lattice's column-major vector: one row per window of order + 1 consecutive nodes that lies wholly in mask (true: a node the rows may read), first the windows along the first axis ((i, j) … (i + order, j), j outer), then those along the second. dims is the unmasked lattice (trues(dims)): a full texel page (TW, TH) at order = 1 is its gradient, the horizontal differences first. A lattice of vector-valued controls applies it per component.\n\nDᵀD is the P-spline difference penalty (Eilers & Marx 1996): at order = 3 it annihilates a quadratic along each axis, so only the variation beyond it is penalized. An N × 1 mask is a profile (rows along its one axis only). The isotropic pairing of the order-1 rows is gradient_pairs; a device solve takes the operator as a CSRMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.difference_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.difference_stencil-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"difference_stencil(order) -> NTuple{order + 1, Float64}\n\nThe coefficients of the forward difference Δᵒʳᵈᵉʳ of a lattice's coefficients, Δᵒc₀ = Σₖ sₖ cₖ₋₁ over order + 1 consecutive nodes: the binomial coefficients with alternating sign, (1, −3, 3, −1) for order = 3. The one source of a P-spline's difference penalty (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's fit_camera) and of BoundaryRule's curvature-preserving extrapolation (Δ³c = 0 solved for the outermost coefficient).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.difference_stencil"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gradient_pairs-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Batched","text":"gradient_pairs(mask::AbstractMatrix{Bool}) -> Matrix{NTuple{2, Int}}\ngradient_pairs(dims::Dims{2})\n\nEach node's isotropic gradient pair among the rows of difference_rows(mask, 1): at node (i, j), (r₁, r₂) the rows of its forward differences along the first axis ((i, j) → (i + 1, j)) and along the second ((i, j) → (i, j + 1)), 0 where that difference is not a row (the lattice's last node on the axis, or a neighbour outside mask). A penalty on the gradient's magnitude ((D x)[r₁]² + (D x)[r₂]², an L0 or total-variation step) reads both entries of a pair together.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gradient_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.natural_extrapolation-Tuple{}","page":"Batched","text":"natural_extrapolation() -> (w₁, w₂)\n\nThe natural boundary condition f'' = 0 at a border knot of a uniform cubic B-spline as the outermost coefficient's extrapolation from the two inward, c₀ = w₁ c₁ + w₂ c₂: the relation BSplineKit's RecombinedBSplineBasis(B, Natural()) imposes (its recombination matrix's span), (2, −1) — the rule BoundaryRule applies outward from an imaged region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.natural_extrapolation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CSRMatrix","page":"Batched","text":"CSRMatrix(A::SparseMatrixCSC; device = default_device()) -> CSRMatrix\n\nA sparse operator in compressed sparse rows on a KernelAbstractions device (default_device() when none is supplied: an accelerator when one is functional, else KA.CPU()): mul!(y, C, x) is one work item per row (each row's sum in its stored order: deterministic on every backend, no atomics). Adapt.adapt(backend, C) moves it. The operator a device solve (cgls) applies; its transpose is a second CSRMatrix of transpose(A) (PenalizedOperator holds both).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CSRMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PenalizedOperator","page":"Batched","text":"PenalizedOperator(A::SparseMatrixCSC, D::SparseMatrixCSC = spzeros(0, size(A, 2)); device = default_device())\n\nThe operators of a penalized least squares min ‖A x − b‖² + ‖√w ∘ (D x − h)‖² on a KernelAbstractions device, built once for every solve on them (cgls): A, D and their transposes as CSRMatrixes. D is any of the stack's penalty operators (difference_rows, bending_rows as a sparse matrix), its rows' weights w the solve's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PenalizedOperator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cgls-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.PenalizedOperator, AbstractVector}","page":"Batched","text":"cgls(op::PenalizedOperator, b; w = 1, h = nothing, diagonal = nothing, x0 = nothing, rtol = 1e-6, max_iter = 500)\n    -> (; x, iterations, converged, relres)\n\nThe least-squares solution of min_x ‖A x − b‖² + ‖√w ∘ (D x − h)‖² (op's A and D; w a weight per penalty row, or one for all; h the penalty's target, nothing for 0) by CGLS — conjugate gradients on the normal equations (AᵀA + DᵀWD) x = Aᵀb + DᵀWh applied as the stacked operator [A; √W D], never formed — from x0 (a warm start; nothing: 0), on op's device (the vectors moved there; x returned on it). diagonal, the normal matrix's diagonal diag(AᵀA + DᵀWD) (positive), preconditions the iteration (Jacobi: the unknowns' columns scaled to unit norm), nothing none.\n\nThe stopping rule is relative to the normal equations' right-hand side, ‖sₖ‖ ≤ rtol · ‖Aᵀb + DᵀWh‖, sₖ = Aᵀ(b − A xₖ) + DᵀW(h − D xₖ) the normal-equation residual: a reference fixed by the problem, not by the start, so a warm start that is already close needs few or no iterations (a rule relative to ‖s₀‖ asks a warm start for rtol more digits of improvement than a cold one). converged when the rule is met within max_iter iterations; relres is ‖sₖ‖ / ‖Aᵀb + DᵀWh‖.\n\nThe rows of A stored; a problem whose normal operator is only applied (its rows never formed) solves with normal_cg, the same iteration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.cgls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mean_positive_eigenvalue-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Batched","text":"mean_positive_eigenvalue(D) -> Float64\nmean_positive_eigenvalue(Symmetric(DᵀD)) -> Float64\n\nThe mean of the positive eigenvalues of a penalty operator's normal matrix DᵀD: the trace over the rank, from the operator D itself (‖D‖²_F / rank D; the rank by a sparse QR for a sparse D, so a lattice of 10⁴ nodes needs no dense eigendecomposition), or from the normal matrix given as Symmetric (tr G / rank G). The two forms agree; a plain matrix is read as the operator, so the normal matrix must come wrapped. The m̄ of the penalty weight's normalization (penalty_scale).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.mean_positive_eigenvalue"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.normal_cg-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Batched","text":"normal_cg(N, g; diagonal = nothing, x0 = nothing, rtol = 1e-6, max_iter = 500)\n    -> (; x, iterations, converged, relres)\n\nThe solution of the normal equations N x = g of a penalized least squares, N = AᵀA + DᵀWD and g = Aᵀb + DᵀWh (cgls's problem), by conjugate gradients on N APPLIED: N a function N(y, x) writing y = N x into y, or an operator with mul!(y, N, x) (a matrix, a CSRMatrix). The problem whose rows are never stored — a panorama's pixels accumulated into a lattice stencil and a Schur block — solves here; one whose rows are stored solves with cgls, which applies the rows and so loses no digits to forming N. Both are one iteration with one stopping rule.\n\ndiagonal, N's diagonal (positive), preconditions the iteration (Jacobi), nothing none; x0 is a warm start (nothing: 0). The vectors live on g's KernelAbstractions backend (diagonal and x0 moved there; x returned on it), and N applies there. The stopping rule is cgls's, relative to the right-hand side: ‖g − N xₖ‖ ≤ rtol · ‖g‖, so a warm start at the solution takes 0 iterations. converged when it is met within max_iter iterations; relres is ‖g − N xₖ‖ / ‖g‖.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.normal_cg"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.penalty_scale-Tuple{Real, Integer, Real}","page":"Batched","text":"penalty_scale(energy, N, m̄) -> Float64\n\nThe unit of a penalty weight: λ = β · energy / N / m̄, β relative to the data's energy per parameter (N parameters) and to the penalty operator's mean positive eigenvalue m̄ (mean_positive_eigenvalue), so β means the same for every operator, lattice and region; β = 1 weighs the penalty like the data. The energy is the caller's: a linear solve's data energy (tr AᵀA, or a radiometric Σϕ²), or a nonlinear refine's Gauss–Newton energy tr JᵀJ over its parameters (the residuals of exact data vanish, so they cannot set a scale).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.penalty_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.row_stencils-Tuple{SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC}","page":"Batched","text":"row_stencils(D::SparseMatrixCSC) -> Vector{Tuple{Vector{Int}, Vector{Float64}}}\n\nThe rows of a sparse operator as the columns (nodes) each reads and its coefficients there, in row order, the nodes ascending: the form of a penalty as a nonlinear solve's residual blocks (one block per row on the variables holding its nodes, its residual √λ·Σₘ sₘ xₙₘ, a caller's chart and row weighting applied to it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.row_stencils"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RADIAL_CHARTS","page":"Batched","text":"The ONE registry of a radial profile's charts, the coordinate a profile is a function of at the distance d from its centre: :linear (ρ = d / radius), :projective (ρ = d / (radius + d), projective_chart) — the normalized radii a fitted RadialVignetting is a spline of — and :equisolid (the field angle θ = 2 asin(d / 2f) of an equisolid lens of focal length f pixels, in radians), the coordinate a measured BinnedRadialProfile is binned in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RADIAL_CHARTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.VIGNETTING_TAGS","page":"Batched","text":"VIGNETTING_TAGS\n\nThe TagRegistry of the vignetting fields: \"tensor_product\" (TensorProductVignetting), \"radial\" (RadialVignetting) and \"log\" (LogVignetting), snake_case of the type's name with the family's suffix Vignetting stripped — the type of VisualGeometryToolkitIO's vgio/vignetting/v1 body. RETIRED: \"tensor\", the tensor-product surface's tag before the type was named for its basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.VIGNETTING_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractVignetting","page":"Batched","text":"AbstractVignetting\n\nA vignetting field V on the sensor, linear in its control points: coefficient_jacobian(v, u) ⋅ vec(control_points(v)) == v(u). Callable, v(u) at a sensor point u (COLMAP pixels), and v(u, f) with the point's ray f (not read: the form a panorama's render takes). Implementations: TensorProductVignetting and RadialVignetting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractVignetting"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BinnedRadialProfile","page":"Batched","text":"BinnedRadialProfile(chart, center, radius, edges, values, counts; focal_length = nothing)\n\nA MEASURED radial profile: per channel, a value per bin of a radial chart's coordinate about center, binned rather than fitted (a flat field averaged over rings of field angle). chart is one of RADIAL_CHARTS, its coordinate at the distance d from center (COLMAP pixels): :linear d / radius, :projective d / (radius + d), :equisolid 2 asin(d / 2f) radians (focal_length = f pixels, required for it and refused for the others); radius is the image circle's (pixels, positive). edges are the B + 1 increasing bin edges in the chart's coordinate, values a channels × B matrix (unitless) and counts the B pixel counts (≥ 0); bin b (1-based) is [edges[b], edges[b + 1]). Every argument is checked (ArgumentError, DimensionError for a length where pixels are meant). radial_coordinate(p, u) is the chart coordinate of a sensor point. VisualGeometryToolkitIO's radial_profile kind serializes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BinnedRadialProfile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogVignetting","page":"Batched","text":"LogVignetting(spline::AbstractVignetting)\n\nA vignetting whose B-spline is log V: V(u) = exp(s(u)), s the TensorProductVignetting or RadialVignetting spline read as the logarithm (its control points are log V's). Positive by construction, and linear in its control points in log, so a fit in log V is a linear least squares (PanoramaStitching's self-calibration). Everything but the exponential is the spline's own: its knots, degree, centre, support, pole_regular_basis (V′(0) = 0 exactly when s′(0) = 0) and log_spline. Its coefficient_jacobian is ∂V/∂P = V·∂s/∂P, and active_coefficients that row's nonzeros (log_spline(v)'s are ∂ log V/∂P). Callable, isbits: it runs in pointwise! on a device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LogVignetting"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialVignetting","page":"Batched","text":"RadialVignetting(n, center, radius; degree = 3, T = Float64, chart = :linear)\nRadialVignetting(knots, center, radius, P; degree, chart = :linear)\n\nThe radial B-spline vignetting profile: n control points of degree degree over a normalized radius ρ ∈ [0, 1] of the distance d = ‖u − center‖ (center and radius in COLMAP pixels, plain or px; a length is a DimensionError, a radius that is not positive an ArgumentError), P ≡ 1 (V ≡ 1), V(u) = Σᵢ Nᵢ(ρ(u)) Pᵢ. The chart of ρ (RADIAL_CHARTS):\n\n:linear: ρ = d / radius on clamped uniform knots, V constant beyond radius;\n:projective: ρ = d / (radius + d) (projective_chart), ½ at radius and → 1 at infinity, so no sensor point is ever beyond the knots whatever the centre; on the knots projective_knots(n, degree): uniform in the distance up to radius and one tail span beyond.\n\nRadially symmetric by construction: a field about one centre. pole_regular_basis is the subspace of the profiles that are smooth at the centre (V′(0) = 0 on either chart: dρ/dd = 1 / radius at the centre), and V(center) = P₁ exactly. The second form takes the clamped knot vector itself (checked: n + degree + 1 knots, nondecreasing, clamped, from 0 to 1) and the control points: what a record is rebuilt from. radial_chart is the chart, with_center moves the centre, center_gradient is ∂V/∂center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialVignetting"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TensorProductVignetting","page":"Batched","text":"TensorProductVignetting(n, box::Rect2; degree = 3, T = Float64)\n\nThe tensor-product B-spline vignetting surface of n = (nx, ny) control points over the sensor box box (COLMAP pixels): degree degree along both axes (or a pair), clamped uniform knots (clamped_knots) over the box's two ranges, control points P ≡ 1 (the partition of unity: V ≡ 1). V(u) = Σᵢⱼ Nᵢ(u₁) Mⱼ(u₂) Pᵢⱼ, a point outside the box clamped onto it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TensorProductVignetting"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TensorProductVignetting-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Batched","text":"TensorProductVignetting(knots_x, knots_y, P; degree)\n\nThe surface on the given clamped knot vectors (COLMAP pixels; degree, or a pair, the B-spline degree per axis) with the nx × ny control points P: length(knots_x) == nx + p + 1, the knots nondecreasing and clamped (each end repeated p + 1 times). The knots are taken as they are — what a file's reader needs to rebuild a surface exactly (knot_vectors, spline_degree, control_points give them back).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TensorProductVignetting"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.active_coefficients-Tuple{TensorProductVignetting, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"active_coefficients(v::AbstractVignetting, u) -> (idx::NTuple, w::NTuple)\n\nThe control points whose basis functions can be nonzero at the sensor point u and their values there: (p + 1) of a radial profile, (p + 1)(q + 1) of a tensor-product surface (linear indices of vec(control_points(v))), the entries of coefficient_jacobian(v, u) at idx, every other entry zero. The sparse form a scatter-add over many points reads. Kernel-lawful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.active_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.center_gradient-Tuple{Union{LogVignetting{<:RadialVignetting}, RadialVignetting}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"center_gradient(v, u) -> SVector{2}\n\n∂V(u)/∂c of a radial profile at its centre c (COLMAP pixels): the dipole −V′(ρ) ∂ρ/∂d · (u − c)/d, by a two-partial Dual through with_center and the same evaluation as v(u) (isbits: it runs in a kernel), so a log-domain radial field's is the chain rule V(u)·∂(log V)/∂c; zero at the centre itself (V′(0) = 0 under the pole tie; the distance's derivative has no value there) and for a field without a centre parameter (a tensor-product surface, whose centre is its box's).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.center_gradient"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coefficient_jacobian-Tuple{TensorProductVignetting, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"coefficient_jacobian(v::AbstractVignetting, u) -> SVector\n\n∂V(u)/∂vec(P): the basis functions at the sensor point u, ordered like vec(control_points(v)) (column-major; for a tensor-product surface Nᵢ(u₁) Mⱼ(u₂) at i + (j − 1)·nx), so V(u) = coefficient_jacobian(v, u) ⋅ vec(P). V is linear in P: the Jacobian does not depend on P.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coefficient_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.control_points-Tuple{TensorProductVignetting}","page":"Batched","text":"control_points(v::AbstractVignetting) -> SMatrix\n\nThe control points: nx × ny for a tensor-product surface, N × 1 for a radial profile, so vec(control_points(v)) is the coefficient vector of either field (the order of coefficient_jacobian).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.control_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.field_radius-Tuple{RadialVignetting}","page":"Batched","text":"The radius (COLMAP pixels) at which a radial profile's normalized radius is 1 (linear chart) or ½ (projective chart).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.field_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.knot_vectors-Tuple{TensorProductVignetting}","page":"Batched","text":"knot_vectors(v::AbstractVignetting) -> Tuple\n\nThe clamped knot vectors, one per axis: (knots_x, knots_y) for a tensor surface (camera pixels), (knots,) over [0, 1] for a radial profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.knot_vectors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.log_spline-Tuple{LogVignetting}","page":"Batched","text":"log_spline(v::LogVignetting) -> AbstractVignetting\n\nThe spline of log V: log(v(u)) == log_spline(v)(u).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.log_spline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.n_control_points-Union{Tuple{TensorProductVignetting{T, NX, NY}}, Tuple{NY}, Tuple{NX}, Tuple{T}} where {T, NX, NY}","page":"Batched","text":"n_control_points(v::AbstractVignetting) -> Int\n\nThe number of control points, length(vec(control_points(v))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.n_control_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pole_regular_basis-Tuple{RadialVignetting}","page":"Batched","text":"pole_regular_basis(v::RadialVignetting) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nA basis Z (N × (N − 1)) of the radial profiles that are smooth at the centre, vec(P) = vec(P₀) + Z y: the first two control points tied, P₁ = P₂. A field of revolution V(‖u − c‖) is differentiable at its centre only when its generatrix meets the axis at a right angle, V′(0) = 0 (Piegl & Tiller 1997, §8.5); on clamped knots V′(0) = p/k_{p+2} · (P₂ − P₁) (their eq. 3.7), so the tie is that condition, and the field is then as smooth at the centre as at its knots. Column 1 moves P₁ and P₂ together, column j > 1 moves P_{j+1}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.pole_regular_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projective_chart-Tuple{T} where T","page":"Batched","text":"projective_chart(r) -> Transformation\n\nThe projective chart of the distance d (a 1-vector) at the radius r: ρ = d / (r + d), Core's 1-D HomographyMap([1 0; 1 r]) dehomogenized by PerspectiveMap (ρ(r) = ½, ρ(∞) = 1). Isbits: it runs in a kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projective_chart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projective_knots-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"projective_knots(n, p) -> SVector{n + p + 1, Float64}\n\nThe knots of a radial profile on the projective chart of n control points of degree p: m = n − p − 1 spans uniform in the distance up to the radius — knots at d = k·r/m, which the chart maps to k / (m + k), k = 0 … m (so ½ at the radius) — and one tail span [½, 1) for every point beyond, clamped (p + 1 knots at each end). Needs n > p + 1. With n one more than a linear profile's count, the pixel spacing up to the radius is the linear profile's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.projective_knots"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_chart-Union{Tuple{RadialVignetting{T, N, K, DP, C}}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{DP}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}} where {T, N, K, DP, C<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras._RadialChart}","page":"Batched","text":"radial_chart(v) -> Symbol\n\nThe chart of a radial profile's normalized radius: :linear or :projective (RADIAL_CHARTS); a log field's, its spline's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_chart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_coordinate-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{DP}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{RadialVignetting{T, N, K, DP, C}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}} where {T, N, K, DP, C<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras._RadialChart}","page":"Batched","text":"radial_coordinate(v::RadialVignetting, u) -> ρ\n\nThe normalized radius of the sensor point u (the profile's argument) on the profile's chart: ‖u − center‖ / radius (linear) or ‖u − center‖ / (radius + ‖u − center‖) (projective).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_coordinate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_coordinate-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.BinnedRadialProfile{C}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}} where C","page":"Batched","text":"radial_coordinate(p::BinnedRadialProfile, u) -> Float64\n\nThe chart coordinate of the sensor point u (COLMAP pixels) about the profile's centre: the coordinate its edges bin (searchsortedlast(p.edges, radial_coordinate(p, u)) is the bin, 1-based).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_coordinate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_degree-Union{Tuple{TensorProductVignetting{T, NX, NY, KX, KY, NXY, DP, DQ}}, Tuple{DQ}, Tuple{DP}, Tuple{NXY}, Tuple{KY}, Tuple{KX}, Tuple{NY}, Tuple{NX}, Tuple{T}} where {T, NX, NY, KX, KY, NXY, DP, DQ}","page":"Batched","text":"spline_degree(v::AbstractVignetting) -> Tuple\n\nThe B-spline degree per axis: (p, q) for a tensor-product surface, (p,) for a radial profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.spline_degree"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_center-Union{Tuple{S}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{DP}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{RadialVignetting{T, N, K, DP, C}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, S, 1}}} where {T, N, K, DP, C<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras._RadialChart, S}","page":"Batched","text":"with_center(v::RadialVignetting, center) -> v′\nwith_center(v::LogVignetting{<:RadialVignetting}, center) -> v′\n\nThe same radial profile (chart, knots, degree, radius, control points) about the centre center (COLMAP pixels): what a fit of the centre steps. A log-domain field moves its radial profile's centre. The element type is promote_type of the field's and the centre's (a Dual centre gives a Dual field, its knots exact).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_center"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_control_points-Union{Tuple{S}, Tuple{DQ}, Tuple{DP}, Tuple{NXY}, Tuple{KY}, Tuple{KX}, Tuple{NY}, Tuple{NX}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{TensorProductVignetting{T, NX, NY, KX, KY, NXY, DP, DQ}, AbstractMatrix{S}}} where {T, NX, NY, KX, KY, NXY, DP, DQ, S}","page":"Batched","text":"with_control_points(v::AbstractVignetting, P) -> AbstractVignetting\n\nThe same field (knots, degree, centre) with the control points P: a matrix shaped like control_points(v) or its vec. The element type is promote_type of the field's and P's, so the knots, centre and radius are never narrowed (Int or Float32 control points on a Float64 field give a Float64 field; Duals give a Dual field, its knots exact).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_control_points"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.EnvironmentLight","page":"Batched","text":"EnvironmentLight(map, center, radius)\n\nRadiance from every direction, given by the directional map (a SphericalHarmonicMap) on the sphere of radius about center: a ray from a point x in direction ω reads the map where it leaves that sphere (exit_direction), so nearby points see the light from different directions (the distant, infinite light is its limit as radius → ∞). center and radius are lengths: metres as quantities in a public value, plain numbers in a kernel (ustrip(m, light)); the frame is the owner's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.EnvironmentLight"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SphericalHarmonicMap","page":"Batched","text":"SphericalHarmonicMap(coefficients::AbstractMatrix, l_max)\n\nA radiance map on the sphere of directions: C channels (rows) of real spherical-harmonic coefficients up to degree l_max (columns, in sh_index order; C × (l_max + 1)²). Callable: M(ω) is the SVector{C} of the channels at the unit direction ω.\n\nSphericalHarmonicMap(radiance, l_max; channels = 3)\n\nThe constant map M ≡ radiance in every channel (only the l = 0 term).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.SphericalHarmonicMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coefficient_jacobian-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.EnvironmentLight, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"coefficient_jacobian(light::EnvironmentLight, x, ω) -> SVector{(l_max + 1)²}\n\nThe radiance's linear factor in the coefficients, the same for every channel: coefficients(light) * coefficient_jacobian(light, x, ω) == radiance(light, x, ω).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coefficient_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coefficients-Tuple{SphericalHarmonicMap}","page":"Batched","text":"coefficients(M::SphericalHarmonicMap) -> SMatrix{C, (l_max + 1)²}\ncoefficients(light::EnvironmentLight)\n\nThe spherical-harmonic coefficients, one row per channel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.exit_direction-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.EnvironmentLight, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"exit_direction(light::EnvironmentLight, x, ω) -> SVector{3}\n\nThe unit direction from the light's centre to the point where the ray x + μω (μ > 0, ω a unit vector) leaves the light's sphere. For x outside the sphere (a wide view of a board larger than the sphere): a ray whose line misses the sphere is read at the line's closest point to the centre, μ = −(x − c)·ω, with finite derivatives there (not sqrt(max(Δ, 0)), whose derivative at 0 is Inf · 0); one whose line crosses it but points away is read at the crossing nearer to x, behind it (μ < 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.exit_direction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.max_degree-Union{Tuple{SphericalHarmonicMap{L}}, Tuple{L}} where L","page":"Batched","text":"max_degree(M::SphericalHarmonicMap) -> Int\nmax_degree(light::EnvironmentLight)\n\nThe expansion's highest degree l_max.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.max_degree"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radiance-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.EnvironmentLight, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"radiance(light::EnvironmentLight, x, ω) -> SVector{C}\n\nThe radiance per channel arriving at x from direction ω (a unit vector, pointing from x towards the light): the map at exit_direction(light, x, ω).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radiance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radiance_map-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.EnvironmentLight}","page":"Batched","text":"radiance_map(light::EnvironmentLight)\n\nThe light's directional map (SphericalHarmonicMap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radiance_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sh_basis-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Val{L}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}} where L","page":"Batched","text":"sh_basis(Val(l_max), ω) -> SVector{(l_max + 1)²}\n\nEvery real spherical harmonic y_l^m(ω) of degree l ≤ l_max at the unit direction ω, in the order of sh_index (no Condon–Shortley phase; the convention in lighting.jl's header). Allocation-free and device-lawful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sh_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sh_count-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"sh_count(l_max) -> Int\n\nThe number of real spherical harmonics of degree l ≤ l_max, (l_max + 1)².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sh_count"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sh_index-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"sh_index(l, m) -> Int\n\nThe position of y_l^m in sh_basis: l² + l + m + 1, |m| ≤ l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sh_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_coefficients-Union{Tuple{J}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{L}, Tuple{SphericalHarmonicMap{L, C, J}, AbstractMatrix}} where {L, C, J}","page":"Batched","text":"with_coefficients(M::SphericalHarmonicMap, a) -> SphericalHarmonicMap\nwith_coefficients(light::EnvironmentLight, a) -> EnvironmentLight\n\nThe same map (degree, channels) with the coefficients a, of a's element type (a Dual for a Jacobian); a light keeps its centre and radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_coefficients"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractBlur","page":"Batched","text":"AbstractBlur\n\nA blur kernel of an imaging model on a plane (a sensor, a print). One of several that compose into a point spread; GaussianBlur is the one implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.AbstractBlur"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.GaussianBlur","page":"Batched","text":"GaussianBlur(σ)                       # round: Σ = σ² I\nGaussianBlur(σx, σy)                  # axis-aligned: Σ = diag(σx², σy²)\nGaussianBlur(Σ::CovMat{2})            # by its covariance, its frame kept\nGaussianBlur(Σ::ScaledIdentity{2})    # round\nGaussianBlur(Σ::SDiagonal{2})         # axis-aligned\nGaussianBlur(Σ::SMatrix{2, 2})        # general: symmetric, positive semidefinite\n\nA Gaussian blur: the point spread density N(0, Σ), where a photon from a point source lands relative to its ideal image. Its parameter is the covariance Σ, a CovMat{2} whose frame tag names the plane the blur lives on: px² for a blur on a sensor, m² (FlexUnits quantities) for one on a print. σ, σx, σy are standard deviations. The storage is the structure: a round blur is stored as a ScaledIdentity (one number, unchanged by a rotation), an axis-aligned one as an SDiagonal, a general one as a full symmetric SMatrix. Isbits.\n\nThe accessors are Statistics.jl's: cov(b) is Σ as stored (its frame kept), var(b) the per-axis variances (Σ₁₁, Σ₂₂) and std(b) the per-axis standard deviations (√Σ₁₁, √Σ₂₂). A print's blur holds m² quantities (GaussianBlur(0.02mm)); ustrip(u, b) is its plain form in u² and with_unit(b, u) reads a plain one as in u².\n\nBlurs in sequence add their covariances (convolve); a linear map of the plane pushes the covariance forward (propagate).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.GaussianBlur"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convolve-Tuple{GaussianBlur, GaussianBlur}","page":"Batched","text":"convolve(a::GaussianBlur, b::GaussianBlur) -> GaussianBlur\n\nThe blur of a followed by b (convolution commutes): the sum of two independent displacements, so the covariances add, diagonal when both are. The two must live on one plane (their frames meet; an unlabeled one takes the other's).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convolve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SphericalSplineSystem","page":"Batched","text":"SphericalSplineSystem(directions) -> SphericalSplineSystem{N}\n\nThe solver object of a spherical spline through N unit directions — the ThinPlateSplineSystem's analogue on the sphere, with Wahba's kernel spherical_spline_kernel and a constant in place of the affine part: the directions, the (N + 1) × N matrix coeff that maps nodal values to the weights and the constant, (w; a₁) = coeff · values, and the N × N bending-energy form Q (valuesᵀ Q values = wᵀ K w). coeff is the first N columns of the inverted system matrix [K 1; 1ᵀ 0], formed with inv as RadiometricCamera forms it. The directions are normalized on entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SphericalSplineSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem","page":"Batched","text":"ThinPlateSplineSystem(nodes) -> ThinPlateSplineSystem{N}\n\nThe solver object of a thin-plate spline through N nodes in the plane: the node positions, the (N + 3) × N matrix coeff that maps nodal values to the spline's weights and affine part, (w; a) = coeff · values, and the N × N bending-energy form Q (valuesᵀ Q values = wᵀ K w, K = [U(𝐩ᵢ, 𝐩ⱼ)], see bending_energy). The target calibration kinds' pure-bending rule (is_pure_bending, tps_pure_bending) reads the 3 × N affine rows coeff[N+1:N+3, :] (a = coeff[N+1:N+3, :] · values); the plane fields [1 𝐩_x 𝐩_y] are formed from the nodes.\n\nEverything it holds is in the CALLER's frame (frame in = frame out): the nodes as given, (w, a) of the spline f(𝐩) in those coordinates, and Q the bending form of those coordinates (valuesᵀ Q values = wᵀ K w, K of the given nodes: the values' unit squared over the nodes' unit squared, a fixed multiple of the bending energy J = ∫ f_xx² + 2f_xy² + f_yy² in the given coordinates).\n\nThe system is SOLVED in a conditioned frame, as an estimator's private normalization: the nodes are centred on their centroid and scaled by one isotropic factor σ (HartleyNormalization: mean distance √2 from the centroid), 𝐩′ = σ 𝐩 + 𝐭. An interpolating thin-plate spline is the same surface under a similarity of its domain, so only rounding changes: the weights map back as w = σ² w′, the linear part as (a_x, a_y) = σ (a′_x, a′_y), the constant as a₁ = a′₁ + a′ · 𝐭 + ln σ Σᵢ w′ᵢ |𝐩′ᵢ|² (the kernel's σ² ln σ · r² term, a constant under the side conditions Σ w′ᵢ = 0, Σ w′ᵢ 𝐩′ᵢ = 0), and Q = σ² Q′. (A PER-AXIS scaling is not a similarity: the spline through the same values then bends differently, so it is never used here.) Without it, nodes in pixels (thousands) put r² ln r² near 1e8 beside the unit column of P.\n\ncoeff is mapped back from the first N columns of the inverse of the conditioned system matrix [K′ P′; P′ᵀ 0], solved for those N right-hand sides by one LU factorization. The matrices live on the host; tps_coefficients turns nodal values into the isbits (w, a) that tps_evaluate and tps_gradient take.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_energy-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem, AbstractVector}","page":"Batched","text":"bending_energy(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem, values) -> scalar\nbending_energy(sys::SphericalSplineSystem, values) -> scalar\n\nThe bending energy valuesᵀ Q values of the spline through the nodal values values (= wᵀ K w with K = [U(𝐩ᵢ, 𝐩ⱼ)] the radial basis between the nodes; zero for affine values up to rounding); a quadratic regularizer on the values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_factor-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem}","page":"Batched","text":"bending_factor(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem{N}) -> Matrix{Float64}\nbending_factor(sys::SphericalSplineSystem{N}) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nA factor L (N × N) of the bending-energy form: ‖L · values‖² = bending_energy(tps, values) to rounding, so L · values are a regularizer's residual rows. Q is positive semidefinite with the affine values in its null space, so it has no Cholesky factor: L = Λ₊^½ Vᵀ from the eigendecomposition Q = V Λ Vᵀ, Λ₊ the eigenvalues clamped at zero (the null space's rounding residue may come out negative). An eigenbasis is not unique, so only LᵀL is defined — never compare the rows one by one. The null space's rows (zero) dropped, it is bending_rows(tps), the penalty operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.bending_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.grid_nodes-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"grid_nodes(n; lo = -0.5, hi = 0.5) -> Vector{SVector{2, Float64}}\ngrid_nodes(n, r::Rect) -> Vector{SVector{2, Float64}}\n\nThe n × n equally spaced nodes over the square [lo, hi]², or over the rectangle r (its corners included: a sensor's Rect(sensor) in pixels), column-major (x fastest).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.grid_nodes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nodal_weights-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SphericalSplineSystem{N}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"nodal_weights(sys::SphericalSplineSystem{N}, d) -> SVector{N, Float64}\n\n∂f(d)/∂valuesᵢ of the spherical spline at the direction d: node i's weight Σ_r coeff[r, i] φ_r(d), φ = (R(d · d₁) … R(d · d_N), 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nodal_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nodal_weights-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem{N, T} where T<:AbstractFloat, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"nodal_weights(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem{N}, 𝐩) -> SVector{N, Float64}\n\nThe sensitivity of the spline at 𝐩 to each nodal value, ∂f(𝐩)/∂valuesᵢ. With f(𝐩) = φ(𝐩)ᵀ (coeff · values) and φ = (U(𝐩, 𝐩₁) … U(𝐩, 𝐩_N), 1, 𝐩_x, 𝐩_y), node i's weight is Σ_r coeff[r, i] φ_r(𝐩) — exact, since the spline is linear in its values. Host-side (it reads coeff), in Float64 for a real point (a Float32 point included); a ForwardDiff Dual point gives Dual weights. Allocation-free at every N.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.nodal_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spherical_spline_coefficients-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.SphericalSplineSystem{N}, AbstractVector{T}}} where {N, T}","page":"Batched","text":"spherical_spline_coefficients(sys::SphericalSplineSystem{N}, values) -> (w::SVector{N}, a₁)\n\nThe spline's weights and constant through the nodal values (length N); linear in them and generic in their element type, as tps_coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spherical_spline_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spherical_spline_evaluate-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T, Any, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"spherical_spline_evaluate(nodes, w, a₁, d) -> scalar\nspherical_spline_evaluate(sys::SphericalSplineSystem, w, a₁, d) -> scalar\n\nThe spline at the unit direction d: a₁ + Σᵢ wᵢ R(d · dᵢ). The first form takes isbits inputs (nodes::SVector{N, <:StaticVector{3}}) and allocates nothing; generic in the element type (Float32, ForwardDiff Duals).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spherical_spline_evaluate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spherical_spline_kernel-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"spherical_spline_kernel(d, e) -> scalar\n\nWahba's m = 2 spherical-spline kernel between unit directions d and e, R(z) = 1 − π²/6 + Li₂((1 + z)/2) with z = d · e, evaluated from the chord (1 − (1 + z)/2 = ‖d − e‖²/4) so it stays accurate for nearby directions: R(1) = 1 (with zero partials at coincidence, as the planar tps_radial_basis at r = 0), R(−1) = 1 − π²/6. Isbits, generic over Real (dilog's series).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.spherical_spline_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_coefficients-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem{N, T} where T<:AbstractFloat, AbstractVector}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"tps_coefficients(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem{N}, values) -> (w::SVector{N}, a::SVector{3})\n\nThe spline's weights w and affine part a = (a₁, a_x, a_y) through the nodal values values (length N). Linear in values, and generic in their element type: a ForwardDiff.Dual or a Float32 is kept (the product with the Float64 matrix is formed first, then converted to the values' element type).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_evaluate-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"tps_evaluate(nodes, w, a, 𝐩) -> scalar\ntps_evaluate(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem, w, a, 𝐩) -> scalar\n\nThe spline at 𝐩: a₁ + a_x 𝐩_x + a_y 𝐩_y + Σᵢ wᵢ U(𝐩, 𝐩ᵢ) with (w, a) from tps_coefficients and U the tps_radial_basis. The first form takes isbits inputs only (nodes::SVector{N, <:StaticVector{2}}, w, a, 𝐩): it allocates nothing and runs in a KernelAbstractions kernel on the CPU backend. It is generic in the element type and PROMOTES the nodes with w and 𝐩: a Float32 evaluation with Float64 nodes returns Float64. This differs from tps_gradient, which converts the nodes, as RadiometricCamera's two functions do; supply the nodes in the evaluation's type (Float32 nodes for Float32) and both work in that type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_evaluate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_gradient-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SVector{N, <:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{N}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"tps_gradient(nodes, w, a, 𝐩) -> SVector{2}\ntps_gradient(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem, w, a, 𝐩) -> SVector{2}\n\nThe analytic gradient ∇f(𝐩) of tps_evaluate: (a_x, a_y) + Σᵢ wᵢ (𝐩 − 𝐩ᵢ)(ln r² + 1), since ∇_𝐩 ½ r² ln r² = (𝐩 − 𝐩ᵢ)(ln r² + 1); a node at r = 0 contributes zero. The sum is RadiometricCamera's board_height_grad term for term (its height field is this spline without the affine part, which is added last). Isbits inputs and allocation-free, and it runs in a KernelAbstractions kernel on the CPU backend, as tps_evaluate. Unlike tps_evaluate, which promotes, it CONVERTS the node coordinates to the promoted element type of w and 𝐩 (RadiometricCamera's board_height_grad rule): Float32 w and 𝐩 with Float64 nodes give a Float32 gradient. Supply the nodes in the evaluation's type and the two agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_gradient"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_pure_bending-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ThinPlateSplineSystem{N, T} where T<:AbstractFloat, AbstractVector}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"tps_pure_bending(tps::ThinPlateSplineSystem{N}, values) -> SVector{N}\n\nThe nodal values without the affine term of their interpolant — the pure-bending rule of the thin-plate target calibration kinds (HeightField, ThinPlateSpline): v ↦ v − M a(v), M = [1 u v] the plane fields at the nodes and a(v) the interpolant's affine coefficients (tps_coefficients). The spline through M a is that plane exactly, so the projection keeps the weights w and zeroes a; it is oblique (along the plane fields, not orthogonal to them: orthogonal to the affine rows of coeff) and idempotent. Values whose plane part is within 1e-12 of them (‖M a(v)‖ ≤ 1e-12 ‖v‖, the rule of is_pure_bending) are kept as they are, bit for bit, and a ForwardDiff Dual's partials are projected all the same, so a Dual through it is the derivative of the projection everywhere (the kinds' transform_deriv_params); other values are projected, values and partials. Loops only: allocation-free, generic in the element type and callable in a KernelAbstractions kernel on an adapted system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_pure_bending"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_radial_basis-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Batched","text":"tps_radial_basis(𝐩, 𝐪) -> scalar\n\nThe thin-plate spline's radial basis function U(𝐩, 𝐪) = ½ r² ln r² with r = ‖𝐩 − 𝐪‖ (the same as r² ln r), and U = 0 at r = 0. The zero branch is taken on r² > 0, not by evaluating 0 · log 0, so a ForwardDiff.Dual evaluated on a node gets a zero value with zero partials (the true derivative there) instead of NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tps_radial_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.digamma_stirling-Tuple{T} where T","page":"Batched","text":"digamma_stirling(x)\n\nψ(x) by the Stirling series through the x⁻⁴ term — pure arithmetic, for the Newton step on a Student-t's degrees of freedom inside a device kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.digamma_stirling"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dilog-Tuple{Real}","page":"Batched","text":"dilog(x::Real)\n\nThe dilogarithm Li₂(x) = −∫₀ˣ ln(1 − t)/t dt on [0, 1]: the Bernoulli series in u = −ln(1 − x) (17 terms, by Horner) for x ≤ ½, the reflection Li₂(x) = π²/6 − ln x·ln(1 − x) − Li₂(1 − x) above; x ≥ 1 reads Li₂(1) = π²/6. Accurate to a few eps in Float64; generic over Real (Float32 kernels, ForwardDiff Duals), the constants in the argument's value type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.dilog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lgamma_lanczos-Tuple{T} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Batched","text":"lgamma_lanczos(x::AbstractFloat)\n\nlog Γ(x) by the Lanczos approximation (g = 7, n = 9) — branch-free, only + * / log, so it compiles on every KernelAbstractions backend and every float width. ~1 ulp in Float64 for x ≥ 1; no reflection, so callers keep their argument ≥ 1 (every adaptation call site does).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lgamma_lanczos"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.normcdf_as-Tuple{Real}","page":"Batched","text":"normcdf_as(x::Real)\n\nThe standard normal CDF Φ(x) by Abramowitz & Stegun 7.1.26 on erf (|error| < 1.5e-7): branch-free but for the sign, only + * / exp, so it compiles in any KernelAbstractions kernel at any float width. A Dual flows through the polynomial, so its derivative is that of the approximation (φ(x) to ≈ 1e-6) — the exact derivative of the evaluated objective.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.normcdf_as"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.trigamma_stirling-Tuple{T} where T","page":"Batched","text":"trigamma_stirling(x)\n\nψ′(x) by the Stirling series through the x⁻³ term; companion of digamma_stirling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.trigamma_stirling"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gauss_hermite_prob-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"gauss_hermite_prob(n) -> (nodes::Vector{Float64}, weights::Vector{Float64})\n\nThe n-point Gauss–Hermite rule for the standard normal weight (probabilists' form): Σ wᵢ f(xᵢ) ≈ E[f(X)], X ~ N(0, 1), the weights summing to one, exact for polynomials of degree 2n − 1. Closed forms for n ≤ 3; beyond, Golub–Welsch — the eigen-decomposition of the Jacobi matrix of the monic Hermite polynomials He_k (off-diagonal √k), the weights the squared first components of its eigenvectors. Ascending nodes. An ArgumentError for n < 1.\n\nFor N(μ, σ²), use the nodes μ + σ·xᵢ with the same weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.gauss_hermite_prob"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.hammersley-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"hammersley([T = Float64,] i, N) -> (u₁, u₂)\n\nThe i-th point (1 ≤ i ≤ N) of the N-point 2-D Hammersley set in [0, 1)²: u₁ = (i − ½)/N, u₂ the base-2 van der Corput radical inverse of i − 1. Scalar and isbits-only (a kernel computes its own point from its index); the type argument leads, as in rand(T, …).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.hammersley"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.square_rule-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Batched","text":"square_rule(x, w) -> (nodes::Vector{SVector{2, Float64}}, weights::Vector{Float64})\n\nThe tensor-product rule over a square of a one-dimensional rule (nodes x, weights w, e.g. QuadGK's gauss(n) or gauss_hermite_prob): node (x[i], x[j]) with weight w[i]·w[j], the first coordinate fastest. The weights sum to the square of the 1-D rule's sum; it integrates exactly the products of the polynomials the 1-D rule integrates exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.square_rule"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_disc_rule-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Batched","text":"unit_disc_rule(n_ρ, n_φ) -> (nodes::Vector{SVector{2, Float64}}, weights::Vector{Float64})\n\nThe polar product rule over the unit disc ‖x‖ ≤ 1: Gauss–Legendre in the radius ρ ∈ [0, 1] (n_ρ nodes, QuadGK's gauss mapped from [−1, 1]) times n_φ equally spaced angles φ_j = 2π(j − ½)/n_φ; node (ρ cos φ, ρ sin φ) with weight ρ·w_ρ·2π/n_φ (the polar area element), radius-major (node (k − 1)·n_φ + j). The weights sum to π, the disc's area: Σ wᵢ f(xᵢ) approximates ∫ f over the disc, exactly for ρ^a cos(mφ), ρ^a sin(mφ) with a + 1 ≤ 2n_ρ − 1 and |m| < n_φ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.unit_disc_rule"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.grid_brent_minimum-Tuple{Any, AbstractRange}","page":"Batched","text":"grid_brent_minimum(f, xs::AbstractRange; periodic = false) -> (; x, value, at_end)\n\nThe minimum of f over the evenly spaced grid xs (evaluated in its order), the least value refined by Brent's method (golden-section steps with parabolic interpolation; Brent 1973, ch. 5) between its neighbours: (; x, value, at_end) of the smallest value evaluated. Brent stops at its own floor: x within √eps·(|x| + step(xs)) (a smooth minimum is resolved no better than √eps relative), so the result needs no tolerance. On a periodic grid the neighbours wrap (f periodic). On a linear one a least value at an end of the grid is refined on its one neighbouring interval, and at_end says the result lies at that end: Brent's final bracket still has the end as its edge (every evaluation descended towards it). A value of Inf or NaN is never the minimum. The stack's one 1-D minimizer: Core's azimuthal minimum (the view-angle limit, the inscribed radius) and Estimation's GCV weight search (gcv_select).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.grid_brent_minimum"},{"category":"method","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lu_solve!-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.MMatrix{N, N}, StaticArraysCore.MVector{N}}} where N","page":"Batched","text":"lu_solve!(A::MMatrix{N, N}, b::MVector{N}) -> Int32\n\nSolve A x = b in place by LU with partial pivoting — A is overwritten with its factors and the solution replaces b. Zero-allocation on static mutable arrays (StaticArrays' generic lu falls back to a heap path at these sizes), and pure register arithmetic, so it runs inside a KernelAbstractions kernel on any backend.\n\nReturns 0, or k when the k-th pivot is EXACTLY zero — mirroring LAPACK getrf's info, which a batched driver uses to invalidate degenerate systems rather than propagate Infs. Hoisted verbatim from BlobBoards' 6×6 adaptation solve (2026-09); its op sequence is the one the CPU adaptation path is pinned to.\n\n(_e5_solve10! in the native 5-point essential solver is a multi-right-hand-side elimination that multiplies by the reciprocal pivot on back-substitution, ported from Eigen for fidelity to PoseLib; it stays separate because unifying the two would change one of them at the ulp level.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.lu_solve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/batched/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tree_sum!","page":"Batched","text":"tree_sum!(@context, smem, tid, nt, half = Int32(128))\n\nWorkgroup tree reduction: sums smem[1:nt] into smem[1], thread tid of nt folding smem[tid + s] into smem[tid] for s = half, half/2, …, 1 with a barrier per level (left to right within a level, so the sum is the one every 256-thread reduction of the stack forms). half is HALF the workgroup size (the default serves 256-thread groups). A helper, not a kernel — it takes the kernel's @context so it may @synchronize; every thread in the group must call it, after a barrier that follows its write of smem[tid] (the barriers are convergent only then).\n\nOn KernelAbstractions' CPU backend a barrier must be written in the kernel body itself (its CPU transform splits the body at a syntactic @synchronize; one inside a called function is an error), so a CPU kernel calls this with a group of one, nt == 1 — the stack's CPU group size for its per-blob kernels — where there is nothing to fold and no barrier is taken: smem[1] is the sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.tree_sum!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/features/#Feature-Detection","page":"Features & Detection","text":"note: Module Moved\nFeature detection (scale space, blob detection, affine adaptation, descriptor matching) has been extracted to VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.jl. Import detect_features, the detector types, scale-space machinery, and match from VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures instead of VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Features.\n\nWhat remains in VisualGeometryToolkitCore is the geometry that detections are made of, and the composition types that carry them:\n\nThe feature-frame primitives — Disc, OrientedDisc, EllipticFrame, AffineFrame, and Paraboloid3D — documented in the Primitives guide.\nAttributed{V,A} and AbstractDetection — the value-plus-metadata composition pattern, documented in Design: Attributed{V,A}.\nCorrespondence types (Correspondence, Correspondences, ScoredCorrespondence) and the endpoint-lift protocol — see the Correspondence API.","title":"Feature Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"api/rigs/#Rigs,-Stereo-and-Captures-API","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"Rigs of cameras and targets, stereo rectification, and the per-frame values of a capture. Split from the Imaging Chain and Targets API page, which holds the imaging chain and the targets. See the Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty guide.","title":"Rigs, Stereo and Captures API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/rigs/#Rigs","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"A Rig is members with fixed poses in one frame, each a (model, pose) pair: a camera (its model and rig-relative extrinsics) or a PosedTarget. Sensors only, targets only, or both (a camera rig carrying its marker constellation).","title":"Rigs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/rigs/#Captures","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"The per-frame values of a capture, keyed by frame index: a body's Trajectory, a detector's TargetPoses, the TargetObservations — per frame and target the observations index => point of the target's points in a pixel or 3D frame (pixels, or 3D markers), with its FrameStamps — and convert_frame, the one way to change a pixel frame. The camera's settings per frame are a CaptureSettings: each frame's ExposureSettings and nominal LensSettings, with the DeviceIdentity of the body and the lens beside them (a camera model holds no names) and is_constant, whether every frame was exposed alike.","title":"Captures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/rigs/#Index","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"Pages = [\"rigs.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedTarget","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"PosedTarget(target::AbstractTarget, pose)\n\nA target as a Rig member, the target-side counterpart of a PosedCamera and, like it, of its kind's hierarchy: an AbstractTarget whose is_posed is true. It holds the TARGET — its geometry, an unposed AbstractTarget, independent of where it is mounted, so the same target can sit in several rigs — and its POSE, the rigid map taking target-local coordinates (the pattern plane z = 0 for a planar target, the points a HeightField lifts off it as they are) into the rig frame, stored unlabeled (the rig mints TargetFrame{id} → F on access). The pose may be Uncertain (an estimated pose, its 6×6 covariance in the RigidExpManifold6 chart, rotation first); the rig hands it back uncertain, its covariance unchanged by the labels.\n\nAccessors: model(m) is the target (as model(::PosedCamera) is a camera's model, so code over rig members calls model(member) for either kind), pose(m) the pose, extrinsics(m) its inverse (an ArgumentError for an uncertain pose, whose covariance does not invert by relabeling), and points(m) the target's own points. The target must be unposed: posing a PosedTarget is an ArgumentError, as posing a posed camera is.\n\nA physical pose (a translation in metres) holds a physical target (points in metres; for an unresolved planar target, a calibration or board with lengths in metres), a plain pose a plain one: a unitful pose on plain points, or the reverse, is FlexUnits' DimensionError at construction, never a promotion. ustrip(u, m) and with_unit(m, u) move both together.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedTarget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Rig","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"Rig{F, M}\n\nA rigid assembly: members with FIXED poses in a common frame F, each a (model, pose) pair. Three storages, one interface:\n\nM <: Tuple{Vararg{AbstractCamera}} — a physical multi-sensor rig. Sensor i's extrinsics is the labeled map F → ViewFrame{idᵢ}, stored inside the camera (a camera IS its model and its pose); the ids, the reference frame and every relative transform are read off those labels — there is no cached inverse, no id tuple, no second copy to drift. The tuple makes the sensor count static: StereoRig IS the 2-tuple case, dispatchable with no runtime length check. Many views over time are NOT a rig — they are frames of one (see the header comment) — which is why a Vector of hundreds of cameras is not accepted.\nM <: AbstractDict{Symbol, <:PosedTarget} — a rig of targets: each a PosedTarget keyed by identity, its pose stored unlabeled and minted TargetFrame{id} → F on access.\nM <: NamedTuple{(:sensors, :targets)} — both: a sensor tuple and a target dictionary posed in one frame (a camera rig carrying its motion-capture marker constellation, on BodyFrame{:camera_rig}). Built by Rig{F}(sensors, targets); sensor_rig and target_rig are its two halves as rigs of their own. Ids are distinct across the two.\n\nType parameters\n\nF <: Frame{3}: the frame every member is posed in. THE FRAME IS THE GAUGE: when F is a member's own frame — ViewFrame{id₁} for a stereo rig built by the StereoRig constructors, TargetFrame{uid} for a calibration target gauged on board uid — that member's pose is the identity, checked at construction. A rig posed in an external frame (a body frame, the world, a tracker's room) names that frame instead and has no gauge member.\nM: the storage, above.\n\nAccessors\n\nids, pose(rig, id) / extrinsics(rig, id) (label-minting or label-preserving per storage; sensors also by position), rig[id], length, haskey; view_frames / image_frames on sensor rigs, target_frames and points(rig, id) — the target's points in the rig frame, the WORLD POINTS of the assembly — on target rigs; the stereo surface (source, destination, extrinsics(rig), pose(rig)) on the 2-sensor alias.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Rig"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ids-Union{Tuple{Rig{F, <:Tuple}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"ids(rig::Rig) -> NTuple{N, Symbol} | Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe member identities: a sensor rig's view-identity symbols, read off the frame parameters of each stored sensor's labeled extrinsics (F → ViewFrame{id}) — an accessor, not a field, resolved at compile time from the rig's type; a target rig's keys, sorted for determinism; a rig of both, the sensors' then the targets'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ids"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.image_frames-Union{Tuple{Rig{F, <:Union{Tuple{Vararg{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera}}, NamedTuple{(:sensors, :targets)}}}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"image_frames(rig::Rig) -> (ImageFrame{id1}, ImageFrame{id2}, …)\n\nThe labeled 2D per-view image-frame TAGS of a sensor rig's sensors — ImageFrame{id} types minted from ids(rig), the slot labels for epipolar forms over this rig. See ImageFrame for the convention-vs-view division of labor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.image_frames"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reference_frame-Union{Tuple{Rig{F}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"reference_frame(rig::Rig{F}) -> F\n\nThe rig's frame type — the frame every member is posed in (F → ViewFrame{idᵢ} for sensors, TargetFrame{id} → F for targets). Unexported; resolved from the type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.reference_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sensor_rig-Union{Tuple{Rig{F, <:Tuple{Vararg{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera}}}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"sensor_rig(rig::Rig) -> Rig{F, <:Tuple}\n\nThe sensor half of a rig carrying both sensors and targets, as a rig of its own in the same frame (the identity on a sensors-only rig). The target half is target_rig.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.sensor_rig"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.target_frames-Union{Tuple{Rig{F, <:Union{AbstractDict{Symbol}, NamedTuple{(:sensors, :targets)}}}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"target_frames(rig::Rig) -> Vector{DataType}\n\nThe labeled 3D per-target frame TAGS of a target rig — TargetFrame{id} types minted from ids(rig).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.target_frames"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.target_rig-Union{Tuple{Rig{F, <:AbstractDict{Symbol}}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"target_rig(rig::Rig) -> Rig{F, <:AbstractDict}\n\nThe target half of a rig carrying both sensors and targets, as a rig of its own in the same frame (the identity on a targets-only rig). The sensor half is sensor_rig.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.target_rig"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_frames-Union{Tuple{Rig{F, <:Union{Tuple{Vararg{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera}}, NamedTuple{(:sensors, :targets)}}}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"view_frames(rig::Rig) -> (ViewFrame{id1}, ViewFrame{id2}, …)\n\nThe labeled 3D per-view frame TAGS of a sensor rig's sensors — ViewFrame{id} types minted from ids(rig), ready to be attached at a lift (e.g. relabel(ViewFrame{:left}, WorldFrame, pose)). See ViewFrame for the instance-identity design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.view_frames"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_model-Tuple{PosedCamera, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractCamera}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"with_model(camera, model) -> PosedCamera/BoundedCamera\nwith_model(rig::Rig, i => model) -> Rig\nwith_model(rig::Rig, model) -> Rig            (single-sensor convenience)\n\nRebuild with sensor i's camera MODEL replaced — after a fit_camera regression or a generic-model init — keeping everything geometric: the labeled rig-relative extrinsics, the ids, any BoundedCamera extent. The rig is reconstructed through its validating constructor (never a raw field update, which would bypass the frame invariants); a lens/K change is metric-neutral to the rigid transforms, so the swap is geometrically exact.\n\nNOTE: swapping the model does NOT re-estimate poses. Poses optimized under the old model keep their values; for an APPROXIMATING model (the fit_camera use case) that is the intent — the approximation error is the fit report's — while for a refinement the new model is the START of another bundle run, not a substitute for one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.with_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{Rig{F, <:Tuple}, Integer}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"extrinsics(rig::Rig, i::Integer | id::Symbol) -> EuclideanMap\n\nSensor i's (or view id's) stored rig-relative extrinsics, label-preserving: the map F → ViewFrame{idᵢ} (NOT the WorldFrame → CameraFrame relabel that extrinsics(::PosedCamera) applies — a rig sensor's frame is the rig's, and the labels are kept). For a target rig, F → TargetFrame{id} — common-frame coordinates into target-local coordinates, inv(pose(rig, id)) on demand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{Rig{F, <:Tuple}, Integer}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"pose(rig::Rig, i::Integer | id::Symbol) -> EuclideanMap\n\nA member's pose in the rig frame. For a sensor, inv(extrinsics(rig, i)) computed on demand (ViewFrame{idᵢ} → F — the typed algebra swaps the labels); for a target, the stored map minted with its labels, TargetFrame{id} → F (target-local coordinates into the rig frame).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.StereoRig","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"StereoRig{F, U, V} = Rig{F, Tuple{U, V}}\n\nStereo camera pair — the 2-sensor specialization of Rig: a stereo rig IS a rig whose camera container is a 2-tuple, so the pair's length is in the type and the stereo surface dispatches on it with no runtime check.\n\nThe rig has no source, destination or extrinsics fields (none since 0.16): it stores its two cameras rig-relative (reference frame F = ViewFrame{id₁}; camera 1's stored extrinsics is the identity, camera 2's is the labeled relative transform ViewFrame{id₁} → ViewFrame{id₂}), and the three are method-form accessors:\n\nsource(rig) / destination(rig): the two cameras (rig-relative — the constructors re-express world-posed inputs).\nextrinsics(rig): the relative rigid transform (monogram X_{V₂V₁}), DERIVED from the stored per-camera maps as extrinsics(rig, 2) ∘ pose(rig, 1) — it consumes source-view (ViewFrame{id₁}) coordinates and produces destination-view (ViewFrame{id₂}) coordinates, i.e. X₂ = R·X₁ + t.\npose(rig): inv(extrinsics(rig)) (the typed algebra swaps the frames, X_{V₁V₂}).\nids(rig): the identity symbols, read off the stored labels.\n\nThe labels ARE the single source of truth — the twin-typing and id-duplication policy violations of 0.6–0.8 remain unrepresentable, and the 0.9-era separately-stored relative transform is gone too (one authority: the per-camera rig-relative maps).\n\nConstructors\n\nStereoRig(left_camera, right_camera)   # Computes relative transform\nStereoRig(source, destination, extrinsics)  # Explicit transform, CHECKED against\n                                       # the cameras (check = false to skip)\nStereoRig(left, right; ids = (:left, :right))  # Mint per-view identities\n\nThe ids keyword mints the view identities at construction (label-at- source: the constructor re-expresses the cameras in the rig frame ViewFrame{id₁} and labels the relative transform ViewFrame{id₁} → ViewFrame{id₂}, overriding whatever frames the inputs carried — the rig's ids are authoritative for its own transforms). The labeled per-view tags are exposed by view_frames (3D ViewFrame{id}) and image_frames (2D ImageFrame{id}); essential_form / EpipolarForm(rig) mint the labeled epipolar forms. Purely additive: nothing in the rig forces identities onto callers who ignore them.\n\nThe 3-arg constructor validates the given transform against extrinsics(destination) ∘ pose(source) computed from the GIVEN cameras (check = true by default, atol tolerance on the rotation angle and the relative translation deviation). Supply check = false for synthetic rigs whose cameras are placeholders and do not encode the relative geometry. check = false also admits UNPOSED members (a BoundedCamera with no pose): the rig poses them, so only the given relative transform matters. The 2-argument form computes the transform from the cameras and so requires them posed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.StereoRig"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.StereoRig-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3}, T, 1}}} where T","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"StereoRig(K1, K2, R_rel, t_rel) -> StereoRig\n\nConstruct a stereo rig from two intrinsics matrices plus the relative pose (R_rel, t_rel) from camera 1 to camera 2 (extrinsic convention: X_cam2 = R_rel · X_cam1 + t_rel).  Camera 1 is placed at the world origin with identity rotation; camera 2 gets the given relative pose.\n\nConvenience constructor for benchmarks where ground-truth is expressed as two calibration matrices plus a per-pair relative pose (e.g. HEB's K_<img> and pose_<pair> keys).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.StereoRig"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.depth_transfer-Tuple{PosedCamera, PosedCamera, Any}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"depth_transfer(source::PosedCamera, destination::PosedCamera, extrinsics)\ndepth_transfer(rig::StereoRig)\n\nDepth-ASSUMED point transfer from the source camera to the destination camera: returns a closure mapping a source pixel to a destination pixel by backprojecting to a ray, fixing unit depth in the source camera frame, transforming through extrinsics, and projecting into the destination image.\n\nWARNING: this is a point-to-point transfer under an arbitrary depth assumption — it is NOT the epipolar correlation. Without depth, a pixel backprojects to a ray, not a point; the honest depth-free two-view object is the point-to-LINE map of an EpipolarForm (see epipolar_map, which since 0.8 names only that action). For real transfer with known depth, use transfer with a depth-attributed point; for correspondence search, use triangulation with matched features.\n\nRenamed from epipolar_map in 0.8 — the old name promised a correlation and delivered a transfer.\n\nArguments\n\nsource::PosedCamera: Source camera\ndestination::PosedCamera: destination camera\nextrinsics::EuclideanMap: Relative transform from source to destination\n\nReturns\n\nFunction mapping pixel coordinates from source to destination camera\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.depth_transfer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.destination-Tuple{StereoRig}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"destination(rig::StereoRig) -> AbstractCamera\n\nThe destination (right, view 2) camera — stored rig-relative: its extrinsics is the labeled relative transform ViewFrame{id₁} → ViewFrame{id₂}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.destination"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.essential_form-Tuple{StereoRig}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"essential_form(rig::StereoRig)\n    -> EpipolarForm{GnomonicFrame{id₁}, GnomonicFrame{id₂}, EssentialMat}\n\nThe rig's CALIBRATED epipolar form: E = [t]×R from the labeled relative transform (x̂₂ᵀ E x̂₁ = 0 for gnomonic coordinates x̂ = π(K⁻¹ũ)), with the slots labeled by the per-view GnomonicFrame tags — NOT ImageFrame, which is documented as the canonical-:colmap PIXEL frame and is never overloaded for gnomonic coordinates. Slot 1 is the source view, matching the pinned x₂ᵀMx₁ convention of EpipolarForm.\n\nComposing its epipolar_map with the calibrated → pixel lifts HomographyMap{GnomonicFrame{idᵢ}, ImageFrame{idᵢ}}(Kᵢ) turns this into the pixel-frame fundamental form — which is exactly what EpipolarForm(rig) does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.essential_form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.source-Tuple{StereoRig}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"source(rig::StereoRig) -> AbstractCamera\n\nThe source (left, view 1) camera — stored rig-relative: its extrinsics is the identity map on the rig frame ViewFrame{id₁}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.source"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.validate_gt_inliers-Tuple{StereoRig, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"validate_gt_inliers(rig::StereoRig, correspondences, depth_src, depth_tgt;\n           rel_tol                  = 0.05,\n           metric_tol::Quantity,\n           gross_outlier_pixel_frac = 0.01,\n           bidirectional_depth      = true) -> BitVector\n\nValidate correspondences against GT rig + per-view depth maps, returning a BitVector of inliers. The threshold is a physical metric length at the surface (e.g. 5mm, 50mm); see _gt_inliers_metric for the math.\n\nPixel-based dispatch was removed in favor of metric-only to keep the GT truth process apples-to-apples across datasets at different resolutions/focal lengths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.validate_gt_inliers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.validate_gt_metric-Tuple{StereoRig, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"validate_gt_metric(rig, correspondences, depth_src, depth_tgt;\n                   rel_tol = 0.05, gross_outlier_pixel_frac = 0.01,\n                   bidirectional_depth = true)\n    -> (residuals::Vector{Vec3{Float64}}, valid::BitVector)\n\nGeometric pre-validation in metric space, without any pixel-residual threshold. A correspondence is valid when it satisfies, in order:\n\na gross-outlier gate: its GT-pose Sampson distance is at most gross_outlier_pixel_frac × the destination image diagonal (pose only, no depth);\ndepth_src is finite at p_A and depth_tgt is finite at p_B;\nthe forward warp of p_A lands where depth_tgt is finite, and the predicted and observed destination depths agree within rel_tol (relative);\nwith bidirectional_depth, warping p_B back at its observed depth agrees with depth_src the same way.\n\nThe residual is 3D, in meters: forward, in the destination camera frame,\n\nresiduals[i] = unproject(tgt, p̂_B, d̂_B) − unproject(tgt, p_B, d_obs)\n\nand, with bidirectional_depth, the longer of that and the backward residual (in the source camera frame), so ‖residuals[i]‖ is the worse-direction metric disagreement at the surface.  Used by calibrate_metric_tol to estimate the noise floor without applying a tolerance gate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.validate_gt_metric"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics-Tuple{StereoRig}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"extrinsics(rig::StereoRig) -> EuclideanMap\n\nThe source→destination relative rigid transform (monogram X_{V₂V₁}), DERIVED on demand from the stored per-camera rig-relative maps as extrinsics(rig, 2) ∘ pose(rig, 1) — labeled ViewFrame{id₁} → ViewFrame{id₂}, i.e. X₂ = R·X₁ + t. With the stereo convention (reference = view 1, camera 1's stored map the identity) this is numerically exactly camera 2's stored extrinsics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.extrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose-Tuple{StereoRig}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"pose(rig::StereoRig) -> EuclideanMap\n\nThe destination-to-source relative transform, inv(extrinsics(rig)) computed on demand (monogram X_{V₁V₂}; the typed algebra swaps the frame labels to ViewFrame{id₂} → ViewFrame{id₁}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer-Tuple{CameraModel, EuclideanMap, CameraModel, Attributed{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, <:Real}}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"transfer(src_model::CameraModel, extrinsics::EuclideanMap,\n         tgt_model::CameraModel, p::Attributed{<:StaticVector{2},<:Real})\n    -> Attributed{Point2, T}\n\nPrimitive form: transfer a depth-attributed pixel from one view to another through an explicit (src_model, extrinsics, tgt_model) triple.  Composes unproject(src_model, ·, ·), extrinsics(·), and project(tgt_model, ·), binding the resulting destination-frame depth as the attribute on the returned point.\n\nMulti-view analog of unproject / project (see also depth_transfer, which is the unit-depth-assumed special case).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer-Tuple{StereoRig, Attributed{<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T, <:Real}}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"transfer(rig::StereoRig, p::Attributed{<:StaticVector{2},<:Real})\n    -> Attributed{Point2, T}\n\nConvenience wrapper: transfer through rig in the source→destination direction. For the reverse direction, call the four-arg primitive form directly with destination(rig).model, pose(rig), source(rig).model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.transfer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.rectify-Tuple{StereoRig}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"rectify(rig::StereoRig; K = nothing) -> (; rig, R, K)\nrectify([T = Float64], rig::StereoRig, images::Tuple; K = nothing, out_size = nothing, scale = 1,\n        device = default_device()) -> (; rig, R, K, images::NTuple{2, FramedImage})\n\nEpipolar rectification of a stereo rig.\n\nGeometry form: computes the rectifying rotations R = (Q₁, Q₂) (RotMatrix3s, original view-i orientation → the common rectified orientation, Q₂ = Q₁·R_relᵀ), the shared pinhole calibration K (SMatrix{3,3}; default: mean focal of the two views, mean principal point, zero skew; a SplineLens camera contributes its gauge K, the pinhole at the pixel imaging ẑ), and the RECTIFIED rig — a StereoRig of two pinhole cameras with the same ids whose relative transform is the pure baseline translation. Rectified frames are NEW frames; the returned rig's ViewFrame{id}s label those, not the input views.\n\nImage form: additionally resamples the two source images into the shared rectified pixel frame — for each output pixel, K⁻¹ → rotate the ray by Qᵢᵀ → project through view i's OWN lens model → sample: distortion and rectification in ONE fused warp per view, any AbstractLensModel. Returns two FramedImages over the same window Rect(0, 0, W, H) of the rectified pixel frame (out_size = (W, H), default: the elementwise max of the input sizes; scale downsamples like the rect form of undistort). After rectification, corresponding points share a row and disparity = fx·B/z.\n\nKernelAbstractions end to end, on device (default_device() by default: an accelerator when one is functional, else KA.CPU()): each image is uploaded there once, the fused warp (one bilinear kernel; the isbits chain evaluated per output pixel on the device, no remap grid) runs there, and each canvas comes back in its image's array kind (an Array gives an Array, a device array stays on its device with no copy). rectify(Float32, rig, images) keeps the warp math single-precision for GPU throughput (the type leads, as in Base's zeros(Float32, n)).\n\nThe construction assumes a predominantly HORIZONTAL baseline (the new x-axis is sign-fixed toward +x); a vertical rig wants the transposed convention. Throws for a near-zero baseline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.rectify"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PLANAR_MAP_TAGS","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"PLANAR_MAP_TAGS\n\nThe tags of the planar maps a record embeds by their parameters (the parametric record {\"type\": <tag>, \"parameters\": [θ…]}): \"homography_map\" → HomographyMap. A TagRegistry; the target calibrations, which are also planar maps, keep their own family (TARGET_CALIBRATION_TAGS) — a homography is an observation of a placement, not a calibration of a target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PLANAR_MAP_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CaptureSettings","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"CaptureSettings(frames; body = DeviceIdentity(), lens = DeviceIdentity())\n\nThe camera settings of a capture, per frame index: cs[t] is frame t's FrameSettings, (; exposure, lens_settings) (a frame's value is anything with those two properties). The capture's hardware rides along as cs.body and cs.lens, DeviceIdentitys. The frame index is the join key every per-frame value of one capture shares (Trajectory, TargetObservations); a frame without settings is absent. Otherwise a Dict: keys, pairs, iteration. is_constant says whether every frame was exposed alike.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.CaptureSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.DeviceIdentity","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"DeviceIdentity(make, model, serial)\nDeviceIdentity(; make = nothing, model = nothing, serial = nothing)\n\nWhat a file says a piece of hardware — a camera body, a lens — is: its maker, model and serial number, each a String or nothing where unknown (an empty string is an ArgumentError: state nothing). A CLAIM, not a fact checked here: EXIF names an adapted lens by whatever the adapter reports, so a calibration pipeline may prefer an identity stated in its config. Held beside the values it identifies (CaptureSettings, a stored camera's calibration record), never inside a camera or lens model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.DeviceIdentity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ExposureSettings","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"ExposureSettings(exposure_time, f_number, iso)\n\nThe exposure a frame was taken with: exposure_time a time quantity (0.004s, with s = parse_unit(\"s\"); stored in seconds, FlexUnits' base form), f_number the aperture's f-number and iso the ISO speed, plain numbers stored as Float64. Each is positive and finite (ArgumentError); a plain exposure time is a DimensionError (its unit is not stated), a quantity of another dimension a ConversionError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.ExposureSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FrameSettings","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"FrameSettings = @NamedTuple{exposure::ExposureSettings, lens_settings::LensSettings}\n\nOne frame's camera settings in a CaptureSettings: its ExposureSettings and LensSettings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FrameSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FrameStamps","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"FrameStamps(ts; images = Dict(), sensors = Dict(), times = Dict(), time_origin = nothing)\n\nThe per-frame stamps of a capture value whose frame indices are ts: per index, optionally, images[t] (the image taken then), sensors[t] (what measured it — one name for every frame, or a map) and times[t] (seconds since time_origin, an ISO 8601 date-time, stated iff any time is). Each map names only indices of ts (ArgumentError). Read as fields: s.images, s.sensors, s.times, s.time_origin. Held by TargetObservations, which forwards the four as its own properties.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.FrameStamps"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LensSettings","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"LensSettings(focal_length, focus_distance = nothing)\n\nThe lens settings a frame was taken with: focal_length, the NOMINAL focal length the lens is marked with or reports (a length; a zoom's setting) — not a calibrated focal length, which is the camera model's — and focus_distance, the distance the lens was focused at (a length), or nothing where it is not known. Both are positive and finite (ArgumentError): a lens reporting \"infinity\", or a marker value, is nothing here, never a distance. Lengths are stored in metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.LensSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetHomographies","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"TargetHomographies{F <: PixelFrame}(frames; images, sensors, times, time_origin, targets = String[])\n\nPer frame index, the homography of every target seen, each a HomographyMap taking the target's plane (its chart coordinates, millimetres) to pixels of the pixel frame F, part of the TYPE as in TargetObservations — the projective counterpart of TargetPoses: th[t] is frame t's target id → homography (stored unlabeled, plain or Uncertain, as TargetPoses stores its poses), homography(th, t, id) the labeled map TargetPlaneFrame{id} → F (the target's plane frame, by its id, as TargetFrame{id} is its 3-D frame; the id is the entry's key). A given map is unlabeled, or labeled exactly so; any other labels are an ArgumentError. An H is RadialHomography's H: it acts BEFORE the lens of the camera sensors[t] names, about that camera's centre (radial_homography(camera, H) pairs them). An Uncertain homography's covariance is 8×8, in the HomographyManifold8 chart about its canonical matrix (parameter_vector's: unit Frobenius norm, H[3,3] ≥ 0). targets names the target definitions the ids resolve in by content identity (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's content_id: a rig's or a single target's). images[t] names the image, sensors[t] the camera (one name for every frame, or a map), times[t] seconds since time_origin. The pixel frame is always stated: there is no form without F. Throws ArgumentError for a stamp (images, sensors, times) naming a frame index with no value, times without a time_origin, or a targets naming a definition twice. Otherwise a Dict: keys, pairs, iteration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetHomographies"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetObservations","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"TargetObservations{F <: Frame}(frames; images, sensors, times, time_origin, targets = String[])\nTargetObservations{F <: Frame}(frames; stamps::FrameStamps, targets = String[])\n\nPer frame index, per target, the observations of the target's points (detections associated with the target's design points): td[t] is frame t's target id (Symbol) → correspondence set, and td[t][id] a Correspondences index => observation — index 1-based into points(target) (unique within the set), observation the measured point — so the pairing with the design is points(target)[i] => x. Only detected points are held (sparse, as COLMAP's and Kalibr's detections are); a target not detected in a frame is absent. Built from frames = Dict(t => Dict(id => cs)), each cs any vector of index => point pairs (correspondences(indices, points)), with optional per-detection attribute columns (withattrs(cs; weights = w), a bundle's weights), per the data-arguments convention.\n\nThe dimension is the frame's: F <: PixelFrame holds pixels in that pixel frame (plain SVector{2, Float64} or UncertainPixel, covariances in px²; convert_frame moves them), a named F <: Frame{3} 3D points in it (WorldFrame for a tracker's room; UnlabeledFrame is refused) — plain Float64, or lengths in metres, an Uncertain one's covariance in m², stored as supplied (has_units; ustrip(u, x) and with_unit(x, u) move every point). One form per container: every set holds the same point type and the same attribute columns (ArgumentError), all physical or all plain (DimensionError).\n\nPOINTS only: a detection of a non-point feature (an edge, a conic) is another type. MEASURED only, never computed by posing a design through a pose (that is derived, a function, and not stored). A motion-capture capture's target is the tracked body's marker constellation, never a board mounted on the body. Memory holds metres; a file holds its declared length_unit (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's observations kind).\n\nThe per-frame stamps are a FrameStamps (td.stamps) — given as the four keywords or as one stamps value, not both (ArgumentError) — read as td.images, td.sensors, td.times and td.time_origin; targets names the target definitions the indices resolve in by content identity (VisualGeometryToolkitIO's content_id: a rig's of targets, or a single unposed target's, whose detections are named by its design reference), unique. ==, isequal and hash agree: the frame type, the frame keys, every set with its attribute columns, the stamps and the targets — equal numbers in different frames are different detections. Otherwise a Dict: keys, pairs, iteration.\n\nA 2D bundle's per-point weight is a weights column. The 0.9 containers of 2D and 3D target points, and their element aliases, are folded into this one container (0.10): a file of the old kinds is read by VisualGeometryToolkitIO's version steps into it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetObservations"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetPoses","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"TargetPoses{F}(frames; images = Dict(), sensors = Dict(), times = Dict(), time_origin = nothing)\n\nPer frame index, the pose of every target seen, each mapping TargetFrame{id} → F (F the measuring camera's frame, CameraFrame for a detector's output): tp[t] is frame t's target id → pose (stored unlabeled, plain or Uncertain), pose(tp, t, id) the labeled pose. images[t] names the image, sensors[t] the camera (one name for every frame, or a map), times[t] seconds since time_origin. What a target IS — its points — belongs to its definition (a target Rig), not to every pose of it. Otherwise a Dict: keys, pairs, iteration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.TargetPoses"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Trajectory","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"Trajectory{S, D}(poses; images = Dict(), times = Dict(), time_origin = nothing, recovered = Set{Int}())\nTrajectory(S, D, poses; kwargs...)\n\nThe pose of frame S in frame D (x_D = R x_S + t) at each capture instant, keyed by frame index: tr[t] is a labeled EuclideanMap{…, S, D} — or an Uncertain one, its covariance kept. Both frames are named (a trajectory is the pose of ONE body, BodyFrame{:rig}, a view or a target, in one frame, WorldFrame): an unlabeled pose is labeled S → D, a labeled one must already be. Optional, per index: images[t], times[t] (seconds since time_origin), and recovered, the indices whose pose was substituted under a stated method rather than measured. Otherwise a Dict: keys, pairs, iteration; tr.poses is the underlying Dict.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.Trajectory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.UncertainPixel","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"UncertainPixel = Uncertain{SVector{2, Float64}, CovMat{2, Float64, SMatrix{2, 2, Float64, 4}, UnlabeledFrame{2}}}\n\nA measured pixel with its 2×2 covariance (px²), in the full form. A TargetObservations set keeps the storage its points' covariances hold (isotropic, diagonal or full; a set of mixed storage in the coarser one), so a detector's isotropic noise stays one number per point; a container typed with this alias converts a structured covariance to the full form on the way in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.UncertainPixel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.UncertainPoint3","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"UncertainPoint3 = Uncertain{SVector{3, Float64}, CovMat{3, Float64, SMatrix{3, 3, Float64, 9}, UnlabeledFrame{3}}}\n\nA measured plain 3D point with its 3×3 covariance (in the point's units squared), the full form. A physical one holds metres, its covariance in m² — stored as given, never rescaled from the unit the point was written in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.UncertainPoint3"},{"category":"function","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convert_frame","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"convert_frame(G::Type{<:PixelFrame}, x) -> x in G\n\nThe ONE way to change the pixel frame of frame-typed pixel data (a pixel TargetObservations; a package adds a method for its own frame-typed values): every pixel is moved by EuclideanMap(F, G) and the result is typed G, so a frame is never relabeled without its numbers moving with it. A pixel-frame change is a translation, so a covariance does not move.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convert_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convert_frame-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{G}, Tuple{Type{G}, CameraModel{<:ProjectiveIntrinsics{T}}}} where {G<:PixelFrame, T}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"convert_frame(G::Type{<:PixelFrame}, camera::CameraModel{<:ProjectiveIntrinsics}) -> CameraModel\n\nThe projective camera with its principal point moved from the package's COLMAP pixel frame to G by EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, G), so that project of the result is project of the camera in G's pixels. The lens is unchanged: it acts on centred coordinates, which a translation of the pixel origin does not touch. The result is a CameraModel in G's convention; the camera itself holds no frame tag, so converting it again is the caller's bookkeeping. The element type is preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.convert_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_constant-Tuple{CaptureSettings}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"is_constant(cs::CaptureSettings) -> Bool\n\nWhether every frame of cs has the same exposure time, f-number and ISO (true for none or one frame). The lens settings are not compared: a fixed exposure under a refocused lens is still one exposure. With it, a per-frame brightness gain fitted across the capture is the light changing, not the camera.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.is_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_names-Tuple{HomographyMap{3, T, M} where {T, M<:StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{3, 3}, T, 2}}}","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"parameter_names(::HomographyMap{3}) -> NTuple{9, Symbol}\nparameter_vector(h::HomographyMap{3}) -> SVector{9}\nwith_parameters(h::HomographyMap{3}, θ) -> HomographyMap{3}\n\nThe parameter protocol of a plane homography: its nine entries h11 … h33, row-major, of its CANONICAL matrix — unit Frobenius norm and H[3,3] ≥ 0 (sign_normalize); a matrix already canonical (within 4 ulp) is returned as held, so a round trip is bit for bit. Nine numbers, not eight: a homography has 8 degrees of freedom but no global 8-number chart (fixing h33 = 1 fails at h33 = 0), so the normalization is declared instead. A negligible or non-finite matrix is an ArgumentError. with_parameters rebuilds the map (its frames kept) from nine row-major entries as given; a singular one is an ArgumentError. No entry is a length (length_mask). Its tag is PLANAR_MAP_TAGS' \"homography_map\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"api/rigs/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{TargetPoses{F}, Integer, Symbol}} where F","page":"Rigs, Stereo & Captures","text":"pose(tp::TargetPoses{F}, t, id) -> EuclideanMap{…, TargetFrame{id}, F} | Uncertain\n\nTarget id's pose in frame t, minted with its labels; an Uncertain one keeps its covariance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.pose"},{"category":"section","location":"#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.jl","page":"Home","text":"Computational geometry, camera modeling, and projective solvers for computer vision.\n\nVisualGeometryToolkitCore.jl is the foundation layer of the VisualGeometryToolkit stack:\n\nCoordinate frames — type-level frame tags, 3D and image-plane, on maps, forms, and covariance matrices; composition, inversion, and propagation are frame-checked by dispatch.\nCamera models — pinhole, Brown–Conrady, and the fisheye-projection family (Kannala–Brandt, equidistant, stereographic, equisolid, orthographic), one lens contract on rays (project/backproject, valid past 90°) from which every lens map is derived, and a framed, junction-checked imaging chain.\nTwo-view geometry — stereo rigs, slot-labeled epipolar forms, homography/fundamental/essential/P3P solver kernels, minimal model charts with covariance transport.\nGeometric primitives — circles, ellipses, discs, feature frames, hyperplanes, and their homogeneous matrix representations.\nTransforms — matrix-backed homogeneous transforms, region maps, image warping, and a batched structure-of-arrays protocol with GPU-portable kernels.\nRobust-estimation primitives — the unified robust-loss family, block-coordinate-descent loop, and scoring interfaces shared by the downstream estimation engines.\nPhysical units — mm, μm, px, dpi throughout, via FlexUnits (a pixel is a dimensionless count).","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Design-principles","page":"Home","text":"Labels are opt-in and never viral. Coordinate-frame tags (Src/Dst on maps, slot frames on forms, frame labels on covariance) are singleton types with zero runtime cost. Unlabeled objects compose with anything; labeled junctions are checked, and a labeled mismatch is a MethodError, never a silent fall-through. One wildcard truth table governs every junction in the package. See Coordinate Frames.\n\nTransformations have endpoints, forms have slots, rank lives on the manifold. Maps (EuclideanMap, HomographyMap) carry endpoint frames and compose with ∘; bilinear forms (EpipolarForm, CovMat/PrecMat) carry slot or frame labels and transform by their own laws (the correlation EpipolarMap composes with collineations; propagate) — the rank-2 epipolar correlation deliberately has no inv. See Epipolar Forms.\n\nFrames, not retinas, are the type currency of imaging. Every central camera shares one spherical retina; camera models differ in the 2D frame on it that their distortion is written in. The imaging chain is a family of framed, junction-checked stages over those frames, and a lens's action free of any 2D frame is its sphere self-map ray_map. See The Framed Imaging Chain and Ray Maps and Frame Transitions.\n\nOne lens contract, kernel-lawful numerics. Every lens is two maps on rays — project and backproject, valid past 90° wherever the lens images — and every other lens map (perspective views, Jacobians, undistortion, warps) is derived from them. The maps never throw (NaN where the lens does not image), never allocate, and are precision- and Dual-generic, so the same code runs scalar, batched, and on GPU via KernelAbstractions — backends come from the array type, not from vendor packages. See Back-projection and Undistortion and Batched Maps.\n\nFlat composition over deep hierarchies. A single Attributed{V,A} type carries every value-plus-metadata pattern; Uncertain{V,M} is its alias for a value whose attributes are a covariance or precision. See Design: Attributed{V,A}.","title":"Design principles"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"pkg> add VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\nJulia 1.13 or later; every dependency is in the General registry. Any Makie backend (pkg> add GLMakie) loads the plotting extension.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-example","page":"Home","text":"A camera views a planar board. The example projects the board's points into the image, estimates the board-to-image homography from those correspondences, and maps a circle on the board to the ellipse it images as (the README's example; the test suite runs both).\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\n# A Sony IMX174 sensor behind a lens with a 40° horizontal field of view,\n# 2 m in front of the board and looking at its centre.\ns = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")\nf = focal_length(s, deg2rad(40.0); dimension = :horizontal)\nmodel = CameraModel(f, s.pitch, centroid(Rect(s)) .* px)\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, lookat(Vec3(0.3, 0.2, -2.0) .* m, Vec3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) .* m, Vec3(0.0, -1.0, 0.0)))\n\n# A 5 × 5 grid of board points (metres, on z = 0) and their images (pixels).\nboard = [Point2(x, y) for x in -0.2:0.1:0.2 for y in -0.2:0.1:0.2]\npixels = [project(camera, Point3(p[1], p[2], 0.0) .* m) for p in board]\n\n# The board-to-image homography, and what it does to the board.\nH = HomographyMap(homography_dlt(correspondences(board, pixels)))\nto_euclidean(H(board[1])) ≈ pixels[1]                # true\nimage = transfer_ellipse(H, Circle(Point2(0.0, 0.0), 0.05))   # a 5 cm circle's image: an Ellipse (nothing past the horizon)","title":"A first example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Module-structure","page":"Home","text":"Cameras (submodule) — camera models, sensors, projection, stereo rigs, the imaging chain, undistortion.\nPrimitives (submodule) — geometric primitives.\nTransforms, frames, forms, solvers, robust primitives — defined in the top-level module (not submodules, due to circular dependencies).\n\nCommonly-used names are exported from the main module; the submodule paths (VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.PosedCamera) and the exported names (PosedCamera) are equivalent.","title":"Module structure"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Start here. Getting Started installs the package and introduces units, frames and a first camera.\n\nHow to. Model a camera and its lens (Cameras, Lens Families, Back-projection and Undistortion); work with Geometric Primitives and Transforms; run maps over Batches on the CPU or a GPU; choose an estimator (Robust Estimation); plot (Visualization).\n\nExplanation. Conventions states the stack's rules and the defect each one prevents; Coordinate Frames, Epipolar Forms, The Framed Imaging Chain, Ray Maps and Frame Transitions, Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty and Design: Attributed{V,A} explain the design.\n\nReference. The API Reference pages, from Frames to Synthetic Data, document every public name.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Role-in-the-VisualGeometryToolkit-stack","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore owns the contracts the robust-estimation packages compose through (see The composable RANSAC). It is consumed by VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.jl (the problems, the one RANSAC loop and the core local optimizations), RANSACScoringDoneRight.jl (the Student-t + uniform scorer and the grid selection families, implementing the scoring and pool-selection contracts), LocalOptimizationDoneRight (the paper's local optimizers, implementing the local-optimization contract), VisualGeometryToolkitEval (datasets, pools, tuning and the composition evaluator), VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.jl (feature detection and matching), and BlobBoards.jl (fiducial blob-board targets).\n\nAll toolkit documentation is aggregated at the portal: https://visualgeometrytoolkit.github.io.","title":"Role in the VisualGeometryToolkit stack"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore is part of VisualGeometryToolkit. Cite the toolkit; its CITATION.cff is the source of this entry (GitHub's \"Cite this repository\" button exports it):\n\n@software{pritts2026vgtk,\n  author = {Pritts, James and Sauer, Hendrik},\n  title  = {{VisualGeometryToolkit.jl}: Julia Packages for Multiple-View Geometry},\n  url    = {https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/VisualGeometryToolkit.jl},\n  year   = {2026}\n}\n\nCite also the papers behind the methods you use, listed on the portal.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/calibration/#Calibration-API","page":"Calibration","text":"Camera calibration from planar targets: the 1-D radial camera and its minimal solver, intrinsics from homographies (zhang_intrinsics), and each lens's honest view-angle domain. The iterative calibration built on them — the radial camera's fit, the division-camera focal stack, lens-model regression — lives in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation. Split from the Cameras API page. See the Cameras guide.","title":"Calibration API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/calibration/#Radial-Camera,-Intrinsics","page":"Calibration","text":"RadialCamera(::PosedCamera) is exact only for a radially symmetric lens; which lenses are is Core's camera trait RadialSymmetry (RadiallySymmetric() / RadiallyAsymmetric(), public, not exported: reached as VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialSymmetry). It fails closed: a lens type that has not declared itself symmetric is treated as asymmetric.","title":"Radial Camera, Intrinsics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/calibration/#View-Angle-Limits","page":"Calibration","text":"view_angle_limit and radial_part are documented with the lenses.","title":"View-Angle Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"api/calibration/#Index","page":"Calibration","text":"Pages = [\"calibration.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialCamera","page":"Calibration","text":"RadialCamera{T}\n\nThe 1-D radial camera of one planar board: the 2×3 block P such that the image point of a plane point X̃ lies on the radial line through center in direction P·X̃ (rows 1–2 of the plane homography taken about that centre, up to scale — invariant to f and to any radially symmetric distortion about it).\n\nThe centre is part of the camera, as it is for RadialHomography. A radial camera without one is only half a model: its P predicts a direction, and a direction is not a locus in the image until you know where the pencil is centred. Carrying it here is what lets project return the Line2D the pixel must lie on, and lets constraint_with_jacobians take the RAW pixel — so no caller has to pre-centre its correspondences and keep that convention in its head.\n\nRadialCamera(H) reads it off a HomographyMat (about the origin unless a centre is given); HomographyMat(P) returns the homography with the placeholder third row (0, 0, 1) that the focal solve accepts (it never reads row 3), carrying the centre translation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialCamera"},{"category":"method","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialCamera-Tuple{PosedCamera{<:CameraModel}}","page":"Calibration","text":"RadialCamera(cam::PosedCamera) -> RadialCamera\n\nThe 1-D radial camera of a POSED camera viewing the plane z = 0 of its extrinsics' source frame: rows 1–2 of the induced homography H = K·[r₁ r₂ t], read about K's principal point.\n\nAn IDENTITY, not a fit — no correspondences, no least squares, the pose taken exactly as it stands. That is what makes this the safe way to hand a calibrated arm's Extrinsics3 to VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's division_camera_step: re-estimating a radial camera from the same board's correspondences re-runs the metric upgrade's sign decision on a slightly different matrix, and can flip the board's pose.\n\nThe lens is ignored by construction, not by omission. A radially symmetric distortion about that same principal point moves an image point along its own ray from the centre, so it cannot change the ray — and the ray is all this model claims (see RadialCamera). That invariance is why a camera with ANY radially symmetric lens is admissible here, while HomographyMat(::PosedPinholeCamera) stays Pinhole-only: the plane map of a distorted camera is not a homography, but its radial camera is exact.\n\nA lens that is NOT radially symmetric is an ArgumentError: a decentering piece of the distortion (Tangential, ThinPrism, Tilt, alone or inside an Additive or a ∘ composition) or a SplineLens moves points off their rays from the centre, so the radial camera would not be exact. Which lenses are radially symmetric is the RadialSymmetry trait's verdict, which fails closed: a lens type that has not declared itself RadiallySymmetric() is refused too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialCamera"},{"category":"type","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialCamera5pt","page":"Calibration","text":"RadialCamera5pt(; center = Point2(0, 0))\n\nMinimal 5-point radial-camera solver (solve verb over the radial_camera_5pt kernel) on plane_point => raw_pixel correspondences. The pencil's center belongs to the solver, not the data: it is the problem's geometry, carried onto every returned camera. sample_size 5, max_solutions 1; a degenerate sample (collinear basis, dependent equations) gives count == 0.\n\nDeliberately NOT entered in the minimal_solvers registry: generic Correspondence{Point2, Point2} data carries no pencil centre (the same reason Homography5ptRadial stays out). Construct it where the centre is known.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialCamera5pt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialSymmetry","page":"Calibration","text":"RadialSymmetry(lens) -> RadiallySymmetric() | RadiallyAsymmetric()\nRadialSymmetry(::Type) -> RadiallySymmetric() | RadiallyAsymmetric()\n\nWhether a lens (or a piece of one: a projection, a distortion term, a composition) is radially symmetric about the principal point — a Holy trait of its TYPE, which RadialCamera(::PosedCamera) dispatches on and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's fit_camera reads to warn of non-radial terms it cannot fit.\n\nThe default is RadiallyAsymmetric(), so the trait fails closed: a type that has not declared itself is treated as asymmetric until it declares itself symmetric, by a method RadialSymmetry(::Type{MyLens}) = RadiallySymmetric().\n\nSymmetric: every projection (AbstractProjection), no distortion (nothing), Radial, FieldOfView and LiftLens; an Additive, a ∘ composition and a Lens (its projection and its distortion) when every piece is. Asymmetric: Tangential, ThinPrism, Tilt, a SplineLens (a calibrated grid has no symmetry), and anything else undeclared.\n\nPublic, not exported: VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialSymmetry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadialSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadiallyAsymmetric","page":"Calibration","text":"RadiallyAsymmetric() <: RadialSymmetry\n\nThe RadialSymmetry of a lens not known to be radially symmetric (the default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadiallyAsymmetric"},{"category":"type","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadiallySymmetric","page":"Calibration","text":"RadiallySymmetric() <: RadialSymmetry\n\nThe RadialSymmetry of a lens radially symmetric about the principal point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.RadiallySymmetric"},{"category":"method","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_camera_5pt-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Calibration","text":"radial_camera_5pt(pts; center = Point2(0, 0)) -> RadialCamera{Float64} | nothing\n\nThe exactly determined radial camera from five plane_point => raw_pixel pairs about center — the minimal solver a RANSAC over board correspondences samples. Closed form and homogeneous, the way homography_4pt is: the first three plane points form a projective basis B, so P·B = [λ₁y₁ λ₂y₂ λ₃y₃] with yⱼ the pixels' ray directions and three unknown scales; the fourth and fifth points, written in that basis (c = B⁻¹X̃), each give one linear equation in λ (their pixel must be parallel to Σⱼ cⱼλⱼyⱼ), and λ is the cross product of the two equation rows. Then P = [λⱼyⱼ]·B⁻¹, unit Frobenius norm. Two 3×3 static solves and a cross product — no SVD, no scale fixed by hand, no root to pick. nothing when the basis points are collinear or the two equations are dependent (the configuration does not determine P).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_camera_5pt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_rows-Tuple{RadialCamera}","page":"Calibration","text":"The 2×3 block P of a RadialCamera: rows 1–2 of the plane homography about its center, up to scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.radial_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.zhang_intrinsics-Tuple{AbstractVector, Size2}","page":"Calibration","text":"zhang_intrinsics(Hs, pitch::Size2; center) -> LogicalIntrinsics{Float64}\n\nKnown-sensor variant: with the pixel pitch given, the aspect ratio is fixed (fy/fx = pitch.width / pitch.height) and only a single focal length remains. Each homography's two constraints collapse onto one unknown 1/fx², solved by weighted least squares — this stays well-posed even for view sets that cannot separate fx from fy. The metric focal length is fx * pitch.width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.zhang_intrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.zhang_intrinsics-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Calibration","text":"zhang_intrinsics(Hs; center) -> LogicalIntrinsics{Float64}\n\nThe calibration from plane→image homographies by Zhang's IAC constraints — one linear least-squares step, assuming zero skew and the principal point at the known center (pixels, required). Each H maps its plane to the caller's pixels; they are read about center internally, and the result is in the caller's pixels: K = [fx 0 cx; 0 fy cy; 0 0 1] with (cx, cy) = center. Each homography contributes two linear equations on (1/fx², 1/fy²); the overdetermined system is solved by (optionally weighted) least squares. A homography carries its weight, a non-negative vote strength (e.g. an inlier count), as Attributed(H, (; weight)), read with weight_of; a bare H weighs 1. Throws ArgumentError when the set is degenerate — all views near fronto-parallel leave the IAC unobservable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Cameras.zhang_intrinsics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/calibration/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project-Tuple{RadialCamera, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{2}, T, 1} where T}","page":"Calibration","text":"project(P::RadialCamera, X) -> Line2D\n\nThe 1-D radial camera's image of the plane point X: the radial LINE through the camera's centre in direction P·X̃. The camera knows where a point's ray points, not how far along it the point lands, so a point of the plane images to a line — and the RAW pixel of X lies on it under any radially symmetric distortion about that centre. constraint_with_jacobians is that statement as a residual.\n\ndirection gives the bare P·X̃ for callers working up to scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.project"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pool_selection/#Pool-Selection-API","page":"Pool Selection","text":"The pool-selection contract (best_pool_model, select_on_grid!) and the grid kernels that score a pool's models across a width grid. The scoring and local-optimization contracts it builds on are on the Robust Estimation API page.","title":"Pool Selection API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pool_selection/#Index","page":"Pool Selection","text":"Pages = [\"pool_selection.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.best_pool_model-Tuple{AbstractScoring, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"best_pool_model(scoring, pair::PoolPair, R; ws, scores) -> Int\n\nThe index of the best-scoring stored model of pair under scoring (a scorer at its width), scored on the pair's residual matrix R (num_points(pair) × num_models(pair), the pool frame's units): per model, score! of its residual column with its minimal sample (the model's sample points enter at zero residual, as in every scorer). The FIRST strictly best model wins; a non-finite score never wins; 0 (never a valid index) means no model scores above −Inf. A matrix of another shape is a DimensionMismatch. ws and scores are the scorer's workspace and per-point scratch, allocated for pair when omitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.best_pool_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.select_on_grid!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Int32}, Type, Any, AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"select_on_grid!(idxs::AbstractMatrix{Int32}, P, scoring, grid, staged; kw...) -> idxs\n\nThe selections of scoring at every width of grid (its native axis, width_axis) over a staged batch of pairs: idxs[p, t] the stored model selected for pair p at grid[t] (0: none). P is the pairs' problem type (a family that carries neither its minimal sample size nor its codimension reads them from it). Further keywords are a family's own (a randomized family's per-(pair, column) generator rng_of) and are ignored by the others. The generic method reads the :pool staging: a loss-width family with a per-residual quality (has_residual_quality) is scored by exact_pm_score_kernel! and selected by argmax_models_kernel! on the staging's backend (per pair, every width in one launch, on sorted_squared_residuals); any other scoring per column by with_width(scoring, axis(grid[t])) then best_pool_model. Either way the FIRST strictly best model wins. A grid family implements its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.select_on_grid!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.selection_staging-Tuple{Any}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"selection_staging(scoring) -> key\n\nThe staging a scoring's grid selection reads (stage_selection): scorings with the same key share ONE staged batch of a group of pairs. Default :pool (the pairs and their residual matrices as they are); a grid family with device kernels declares its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.selection_staging"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sorted_squared_residuals-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"sorted_squared_residuals(pair::PoolPair, R) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nPer stored model of pair (a column), its squared residuals from the residual matrix R (num_points(pair) × num_models(pair)) with the model's minimal sample at zero (as every scorer enters it), sorted ascending: what the exact selection kernels read, and RANSACScoringDoneRight's staging. In the residuals' precision (Float32).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.sorted_squared_residuals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stage_selection-Tuple{Symbol, Any, Any}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"stage_selection(key, pairs, residuals; backend) -> staged\n\nThe staged batch of pairs (light pool records) with their residual matrices for the scorings of staging key (selection_staging), on the compute backend (nothing: the staging's default). The :pool staging is (; pairs, residuals, backend), its backend default_device() unless one is supplied: the device its exact kernels run on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.stage_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.argmax_models_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"OnBackend(backend; workgroupsize = 1)(argmax_models_kernel!)(errors_out, idxs_out, scores_pmt, M_per_pair, errors_imc_flat,\n                               errors_offset, p_offset; ndrange = (n, T))\n\nPer (pair, width) of a chunk: the FIRST strictly best model of scores_pmt[p, :, t] over the pair's M_per_pair[p] models (1 when none scores above −Inf), its error errors_imc_flat[errors_offset[p] + m] into errors_out[p, t] and, when idxs_out has rows, its index into idxs_out[p, t]. Padded work items exit untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.argmax_models_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exact_pm_score_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"OnBackend(backend; workgroupsize = 1)(exact_pm_score_kernel!)(scores_out, sorted_sq_flat, pair_offset, K_per_pair, M_per_pair,\n                                loss_widths, T_val, p_offset, scoring, support_sq; ndrange = (n, M_max))\n\nPer (pair, model) of a chunk of n pairs: scores_out[p, m, t] (Float64, (chunk, M_max, T)) the sum of residual_quality(scoring, d², loss_widths[t]) over the model's squared residuals (sorted_sq_flat, ascending, from pair_offset[p], K_per_pair[p] per model), evaluated and accumulated in Float64. A non-finite residual is an outlier (skipped); the loop stops at support_sq (support_sq_loss_width). Chunk row p is pair p + p_offset; a model beyond M_per_pair[p] is left alone. A work item is a loop over the model's residuals: launch it at one item per workgroup through OnBackend, the stack's launch policy (every thread on the CPU, one warp per workgroup on a device).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.exact_pm_score_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_residual_quality-Tuple{Any}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"has_residual_quality(scoring) -> Bool\n\nWhether scoring scores a model by the sum of a per-residual quality, residual_quality(scoring, d², loss_width) (Core's RANSAC and MSAC; RANSACScoringDoneRight's loss-width families add their methods), so its grid selection is exact_pm_score_kernel! then argmax_models_kernel!. false by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.has_residual_quality"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pool_selection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.support_sq_loss_width-Tuple{Any, Float64}","page":"Pool Selection","text":"support_sq_loss_width(scoring, max_loss_width) -> Float64\n\nThe squared residual from which scoring's quality is exactly 0 at every width up to max_loss_width: max_loss_width² for a family of compact support (RANSAC, MSAC; RANSACScoringDoneRight's MAGSAC++ and smooth TLS), Inf for one that reaches 0 only asymptotically (the default). On sorted residuals the exact kernel stops there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.support_sq_loss_width"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Camera-Models","page":"Cameras","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore provides a composable camera system with physical units throughout.","title":"Camera Models"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Camera-Model-Components","page":"Cameras","text":"A camera is assembled from three components:\n\nIntrinsics — the calibration matrix K (LogicalIntrinsics; pixel units)\nLens model — a map between rays and normalized image points (Pinhole, any Lens or LiftLens; see Lens Families)\nExtrinsics — the camera pose in world coordinates, as a frame-labeled EuclideanMap (see Coordinate Frames)\n\nCameraModel pairs intrinsics with a lens; PosedCamera pairs a model with a pose.\n\nEvery camera — a model, a posed camera, a BoundedCamera of either — is an AbstractCamera. Whether it carries a pose is the trait is_posed: false for a model, true for a PosedCamera, the wrapped camera's for a BoundedCamera. The posed-only verbs (pose, extrinsics, rig membership) refuse an unposed camera with an ArgumentError, and PosedCamera(model, X) refuses a model that is already posed or bounded — the bound goes outside the pose, BoundedCamera(PosedCamera(model, X), extent). The trait is shared with the calibration targets: a PosedTarget is posed, a PlanarTarget or PointTarget is not, and model, pose and extrinsics answer on a posed member of either kind.","title":"Camera Model Components"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Quick-Example","page":"Cameras","text":"using VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\n# Select a sensor from the built-in database\ns = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")\n\n# Focal length from field of view\nf = focal_length(s, deg2rad(40.0); dimension=:horizontal)\n\n# Principal point at the image center (Rect(s) is COLMAP convention)\npp = centroid(Rect(s)) .* px\n\n# Camera model (intrinsics + lens)\nmodel = CameraModel(f, s.pitch, pp)\n\n# Posed camera (extrinsics)\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, lookat(SVector(0.0, 0.0, 500.0),\n                              SVector(0.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n                              SVector(0.0, -1.0, 0.0)))","title":"Quick Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Intrinsics","page":"Cameras","text":"LogicalIntrinsics stores the calibration matrix K (and its inverse) in pixel units:\n\nintrinsics = LogicalIntrinsics(1000.0px, Point2(960.0px, 540.0px))\n\nPhysical quantities enter through the constructors — a focal length in mm plus a pixel pitch produce the equivalent logical intrinsics (CameraModel(f, pitch, pp)); physical scale lives with the Sensor, not redundantly in the intrinsics.","title":"Intrinsics"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Built-in-Sensor-Database","page":"Cameras","text":"s = sensor(\"Sony\", \"IMX174\")      # look up by manufacturer and model\ns.resolution                      # Size2 in px\ns.pitch                           # Size2 in μm/px\n\nSee CMOS_SENSORS for the registry.","title":"Built-in Sensor Database"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Lens-Models","page":"Cameras","text":"A lens model is a map between rays and normalized image points: project(lens, X) and backproject(lens, x_d), valid past 90° wherever the lens images. has_distortion classifies the models; distortion_frame names each model's defining ray frame (see The Framed Imaging Chain).\n\nThere are two kinds of lens, built from a handful of parts — see Lens Families:\n\nLens(projection, distortion) — a projection onto a frame (Perspective, Equidistant, Stereographic, Equisolid, Orthographic, or the parametric DoubleSphere, Unified, ExtendedUnified) followed by a distortion in that frame (Radial, Tangential, ThinPrism, Tilt, FieldOfView, combined by Additive and CoordinateTransformations' ∘). Pinhole and KannalaBrandt are named instances.\nLiftLens(radial) — the image point lifts to its ray in closed form: Division, BabelCalib's rational model, Scaramuzza.\n\nEvery other lens map — the perspective views, Jacobians, the radial profile, undistortion — is derived from that pair; see Back-projection and Undistortion.","title":"Lens Models"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Projection-and-Backprojection","page":"Cameras","text":"u = project(camera, X_world)          # 3D world point → pixel\nray = backproject(model, u)           # pixel → unit ray (camera frame)\nX = unproject(model, u, depth)        # pixel + depth → 3D point\n\nproject composes extrinsics with the model's imaging map; backproject and unproject are the sections of the projection, on the ray domain of the lens (no tan θ blow-up at the equator; NaN where the lens does not image, or where a Z-depth names no point). transfer moves depth-attributed points between views (see Stereo Rigs and Uncertainty).\n\nThe camera's ray ↦ pixel map is available in two forms: the frame-free imaging_transform, affine_part(K) ∘ lens with the lens applied to the ray (so it is project(model, X) for every ray), and the fully-typed imaging_chain with every junction frame-checked — see The Framed Imaging Chain.","title":"Projection and Backprojection"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Camera-Pose","page":"Cameras","text":"Camera poses are frame-labeled rigid maps: CameraPose (X_WC, camera-to-world) and CameraExtrinsics (X_CW, world-to-camera) are frame-pair aliases of EuclideanMap, and inv converts between them by swapping the labels. PosedCamera(model, pose) and PosedCamera(model, extrinsics) both work — the constructor normalizes to the extrinsics slot, so handing a pose where extrinsics are expected is unrepresentable.\n\nextrinsics = lookat(eye, target, up)      # world → camera EuclideanMap\np = lookat_pose(eye, target, up)          # the inverse convention\ncamera = PosedCamera(model, extrinsics)\npose(camera)                              # CameraPose (X_WC)","title":"Camera Pose"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#P3P-Pose-Estimation","page":"Cameras","text":"# 3D world points and 2D image points\ncsp = correspondences(X, u)               # StructArray of Pt3-to-Pt2 correspondences\n\nresult = p3p(model, csp)          # FixedModels{4, Extrinsics3}, empty when no solution\nfor ext in result                 # 0–4 Extrinsics3 solutions\n    camera_i = PosedCamera(model, ext)\n    # score by reprojection error ...\nend\n\nThe solver currency is the labeled Extrinsics3 alias (world-to-camera, a dispatch fact). sample_p3p draws minimal samples; the P3P solver type plugs into the solver layer.","title":"P3P Pose Estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#Homographies","page":"Cameras","text":"For a camera viewing the plane z = 0, the plane-induced homography is minted with frame labels (TargetPlaneFrame → PixelFrame{:colmap}):\n\nH = HomographyMap(HomographyMat(camera))\nu = H(Point2(100.0, 50.0))        # board point → pixel\nQ = H(circle_world)               # a Conic: conics transform contravariantly","title":"Homographies"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/cameras/#See-Also","page":"Cameras","text":"Cameras API\nThe Framed Imaging Chain\nBack-projection and Undistortion\nStereo Rigs and Uncertainty","title":"See Also"}]
}
